{"record_id":"D32-B43-R004","dataset_id":"D32","topic":"Go Runtime Performance","subtopic":"Inlining and Compiler Optimization","concept":"Compiler optimization assumptions","knowledge_type":"KNOWLEDGE","language":"Go","version":"Go 1.26","objective":"Avoid encoding assumptions about compiler optimizations into application correctness or performance claims.","requirements":["Treat optimization decisions as compiler-version-sensitive","Measure generated behavior where relevant","Keep correctness independent of optimization"],"input":"Go implementation and compiler behavior","output":"Version-aware performance hypothesis","expected_behavior":"Performance reasoning remains valid even if compiler optimization decisions change.","failure_mode":"Code is structured around an assumed optimization that is not guaranteed across versions or workloads.","root_cause":"Implementation behavior is confused with language-level guarantees.","corrected_approach":"Separate semantic requirements from optimization opportunities and verify performance on supported toolchains.","testing":"Run benchmarks and, where necessary, compiler diagnostics on supported Go versions.","verification":"Record the Go version and evidence used for optimization conclusions.","security_implication":"Optimization must not weaken validation or security boundaries.","performance_implication":"Compiler improvements can change the cost model between Go releases.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"GENERATED","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","source_code":"// No correctness-critical optimization assumption should be encoded here.","corrected_code":"// Preserve simple semantics and verify performance empirically.","test_code":"// Run version-specific benchmark matrix when optimization portability matters.","provenance":{"source_type":"official_release_documentation","source_locator":"go.dev/doc/go1.26","evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH"},"relationships":["compatible_with","tests","v