{"record_id":"D33-B011","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment Topology","concept":"Choosing single-process versus distributed deployment from requirement constraints","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a requirement lead to a single-process deployment rather than a distributed deployment?","answer":"A single-process deployment is appropriate when workload scale, availability, isolation, independent scaling, and fault-domain requirements do not justify distribution. Distribution should be introduced because a concrete requirement demands it, not because it is inherently more advanced.","explanation":"The architecture decision begins with workload, reliability, deployment, operational, and isolation constraints. A distributed design introduces network failure modes, deployment coordination, observability requirements, and operational complexity. A single process may be preferable when those costs do not solve a demonstrated requirement.","principle":"Architectural distribution should be justified by requirements and constraints rather than technological fashion.","objective":"Select a deployment topology that satisfies required scale, reliability, isolation, and operational constraints with appropriate complexity.","requirements":["Support the expected workload.","Meet availability and recovery requirements.","Maintain acceptable operational complexity."],"constraints":["Limited operational capacity may make distributed deployment costly.","Some workloads require independent scaling or fault isolation."],"preconditions":["Workload characteristics and reliability requirements are known."],"input":"Requirements for throughput, availability, scaling, isolation, and deployment operations.","output":"A justified single-process or distributed deployment decision.","expected_behavior":"The selected topology directly traces to measurable requirements and explicit constraints.","incorrect_code":"services = ["api", "worker", "cache", "scheduler", "event_bus"]","corrected_code":"deployment = {"topology":"single_process","reason":"No requirement currently requires independent scaling or fault isolation"}","failure_mode":"Premature distribution or inadequate deployment isolation.","expected_error":"","cause":"Architecture was selected before identifying the requirements that justify deployment boundaries.","root_cause":"Technology topology was treated as the requirement instead of deriving topology from requirements.","solution":"Create an explicit requirement-to-topology trace and compare operational consequences.","edge_case":"A currently small system may still require separate deployment for regulatory, security, or failure-domain reasons.","security_implication":"Separate deployment boundaries can provide isolation, but unnecessary network boundaries also increase configuration and communication attack surface.","performance_implication":"Distribution may enable independent scaling but adds serialization, networking, and coordination overhead.","test_case":"Evaluate workload, failure, deployment, and scaling scenarios against both topology candidates.","test_input":"Expected throughput, recovery target, deployment frequency, isolation requirements.","expected_test_result":"The selected topology satisfies all mandatory requirements without unexplained architectural complexity.","testing_guidance":"Use workload and failure scenarios rather than relying only on component diagrams.","verification_method":"Requirement traceability plus deployment and failure scenario analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify every distributed boundary has an identifiable requirement or operational justification.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Requirement specification","Deployment constraints"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B012"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B011-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Architecture decision analysis based on explicit workload, reliability, scaling, and operational requirements"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B011-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment Topology","concept":"Independent scaling as a deployment boundary requirement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does independent scaling justify separating components into deployable units?","answer":"Independent deployment is justified when components have materially different resource profiles, scaling rates, availability requirements, or release cycles that cannot be efficiently handled by one deployment unit.","explanation":"The decision should compare the operational cost of independent deployment against the measurable benefit of scaling or releasing components independently. Splitting a component solely because it is conceptually separate does not establish a deployment requirement.","principle":"Deployment boundaries should follow independently variable operational requirements.","objective":"Determine whether independent scaling creates a justified deployment boundary.","requirements":["One workload dimension must require materially different scaling or release behavior.","Independent deployment must provide measurable operational value."],"constraints":["Every deployment unit introduces configuration, monitoring, networking, and release overhead."],"preconditions":["Component resource and lifecycle characteristics are understood."],"input":"Component CPU, memory, latency, throughput, release, and availability profiles.","output":"A justified independent deployment boundary or a decision to retain co-deployment.","expected_behavior":"The architecture separates components only where independent operational behavior is required.","incorrect_code":"deploy_separately("every_module")","corrected_code":"deploy_separately("worker") # only when worker scaling differs materially from API scaling","failure_mode":"Over-fragmented deployment architecture.","expected_error":"","cause":"Logical modularity was incorrectly treated as evidence for independent deployment.","root_cause":"No operational requirement justified the additional deployment boundary.","solution":"Evaluate independent scaling, release, availability, and resource requirements before splitting deployments.","edge_case":"A component may require isolation for security or compliance even when its resource profile is similar.","security_implication":"Independent deployment can enforce stronger isolation boundaries but also creates additional trust/configuration surfaces.","performance_implication":"Independent scaling can reduce resource waste when workload profiles differ substantially.","test_case":"Compare resource utilization and scaling behavior under representative workloads.","test_input":"API request load and asynchronous worker load with different growth rates.","expected_test_result":"Independent deployment shows a requirement-backed benefit or is rejected when no material benefit exists.","testing_guidance":"Measure resource profiles before introducing permanent deployment boundaries.","verification_method":"Requirement-to-deployment trace plus load scenario analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify the boundary solves an actual operational constraint.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Workload model","Deployment model"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B011-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B011-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Requirement-driven deployment architecture analysis"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B011-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment Topology","concept":"Failure-domain separation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should failure-domain requirements influence deployment architecture?","answer":"If one component failure must not disable another critical capability, the architecture may require separate failure domains, independent processes, hosts, zones, or services depending on the required isolation level.","explanation":"Failure isolation is stronger than logical separation. The architecture must identify which failures must remain contained and map those requirements to an actual isolation boundary.","principle":"A required failure-isolation property must correspond to a real failure boundary.","objective":"Translate failure-domain requirements into deployment boundaries.","requirements":["Critical failure scenarios must remain within defined blast-radius limits."],"constraints":["Stronger isolation increases deployment and operational complexity."],"preconditions":["Critical failure scenarios and acceptable blast radius are defined."],"input":"Failure scenarios and recovery requirements.","output":"Deployment boundaries aligned with required fault containment.","expected_behavior":"A failure in one domain does not violate explicitly protected availability or integrity requirements in another domain.","incorrect_code":"critical_services = same_process_all_components()","corrected_code":"critical_services = separate_failure_domains(["critical_api","batch_worker"])","failure_mode":"Shared failure domain causes correlated outage.","expected_error":"","cause":"Critical components share a process or infrastructure boundary despite independent failure requirements.","root_cause":"Failure isolation was not represented in the deployment architecture.","solution":"Introduce the minimum isolation boundary required by the failure-domain requirement.","edge_case":"Separate processes on the same host do not provide host-level fault isolation.","security_implication":"Isolation can reduce blast radius for compromised components.","performance_implication":"Cross-boundary communication introduces latency and resource overhead.","test_case":"Terminate or corrupt one deployment unit and verify protected components remain available.","test_input":"Failure injection against one critical component.","expected_test_result":"The protected component remains within its required availability and integrity boundary.","testing_guidance":"Perform controlled failure injection at each claimed isolation boundary.","verification_method":"Fault-injection testing and architecture traceability.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual infrastructure boundary matches the claimed failure boundary.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Failure-injection environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"FAILURE_DOMAIN_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B011-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B011-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Fault-isolation and failure-domain architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B011-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment Topology","concept":"Deployment boundary versus process boundary","knowledge_type":"TRADE_OFF","question":"Why is a deployment boundary not automatically equivalent to a process or host boundary?","answer":"A deployment boundary defines how a component is packaged, released, or operated; a process or host boundary defines a runtime failure and isolation boundary. They can coincide, but they do not have to.","explanation":"Confusing these boundaries can create false assumptions about isolation, scaling, and failure behavior. Architecture documentation should state which property each boundary is intended to provide.","principle":"Architecture boundaries must be interpreted according to the property they are intended to enforce.","objective":"Distinguish release, process, and infrastructure boundaries.","requirements":["Architecture documentation must identify the semantics of each boundary."],"constraints":["Different deployment platforms provide different isolation guarantees."],"preconditions":["The system uses multiple deployment units or packaging boundaries."],"input":"Deployment and runtime topology.","output":"Explicit boundary semantics.","expected_behavior":"No component is assumed isolated unless the actual runtime environment provides that isolation.","incorrect_code":"deployment_unit_isolation = True","corrected_code":"deployment_unit_isolation = {"release_boundary":true,"process_boundary":false,"host_boundary":false}","failure_mode":"False isolation assumption.","expected_error":"","cause":"A packaging boundary was interpreted as a runtime isolation boundary.","root_cause":"Architecture properties were not explicitly mapped to infrastructure semantics.","solution":"Document release, process, host, and network boundaries separately.","edge_case":"A managed platform may provide stronger isolation than the deployment abstraction itself, but that must be verified.","security_implication":"False isolation assumptions can expose sensitive workloads to shared failure or compromise domains.","performance_implication":"Process and host separation affect memory, CPU, and network overhead.","test_case":"Inspect the actual runtime topology and intentionally terminate a deployment unit.","test_input":"Deployment unit restart/failure.","expected_test_result":"Observed isolation matches documented boundary semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test claimed isolation properties rather than trusting deployment names.","verification_method":"Runtime topology inspection and controlled failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual platform behavior against the architecture contract.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Runtime environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B011-R03"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B011-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Deployment and runtime isolation architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B011-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment Topology","concept":"Minimum sufficient distribution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","question":"What does minimum sufficient distribution mean in architecture selection?","answer":"It means introducing only the deployment and runtime distribution necessary to satisfy explicit scale, isolation, availability, compliance, or lifecycle requirements.","explanation":"Distribution is not binary. A system can use one process, multiple processes, or multiple infrastructure domains. The architecture should select the least complex topology that satisfies mandatory constraints.","principle":"Choose the minimum architecture complexity that satisfies the complete requirement set.","objective":"Prevent unjustified distributed-system complexity.","requirements":["All mandatory requirements must remain satisfied.","Every additional distributed boundary must have a documented purpose."],"constraints":["Operational complexity increases as boundaries multiply."],"preconditions":["Alternative topologies are available for comparison."],"input":"Requirements plus topology candidates.","output":"Minimum sufficient topology with documented rationale.","expected_behavior":"No architectural boundary exists without a requirement-backed purpose.","incorrect_code":"add_service_for_each_domain()","corrected_code":"add_boundary_only_when_requirement_requires_it()","failure_mode":"Architecture complexity without corresponding requirement value.","expected_error":"","cause":"Boundaries were added based on conceptual organization rather than system requirements.","root_cause":"Architecture selection optimized for perceived sophistication instead of requirement satisfaction.","solution":"Compare candidate topologies against requirements and operational cost.","edge_case":"Future requirements may justify a boundary later; planned extensibility does not automatically justify immediate distribution.","security_implication":"Fewer boundaries can simplify security configuration, while required boundaries can reduce blast radius.","performance_implication":"Avoiding unnecessary network hops can reduce latency and operational overhead.","test_case":"Review every deployment boundary and require a traceable requirement.","test_input":"Deployment architecture diagram and requirement matrix.","expected_test_result":"Every boundary has a documented justification.","testing_guidance":"Use architecture review as a completeness and complexity control.","verification_method":"Requirement-to-boundary traceability review.","verification_guidance":"Reject unexplained deployment boundaries until their requirement value is established.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Requirement matrix","Architecture diagram"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B011-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B011-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Requirement-driven architecture selection principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B012","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"Stateless versus stateful service selection","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should requirements determine whether a service should be stateless or stateful?","answer":"A service should remain stateless when request processing can be completed from request data and external durable state. Stateful design becomes justified when maintaining local session, cache, stream position, connection state, or other runtime state is itself required for correctness or efficiency.","explanation":"Statelessness simplifies scaling and replacement but may require external state storage. Stateful designs can reduce external coordination for some workloads but create recovery, replication, and routing requirements.","principle":"State placement is an architectural decision driven by correctness, performance, recovery, and scaling requirements.","objective":"Select an appropriate state ownership model.","requirements":["Required state must survive or recover according to its durability requirement.","Scaling must preserve correctness."],"constraints":["Externalizing state introduces network and consistency dependencies.","Local state complicates replacement and recovery."],"preconditions":["The system's state categories and durability requirements are identified."],"input":"State inventory, durability, latency, scaling, and recovery requirements.","output":"Explicit state ownership architecture.","expected_behavior":"Each state category has a defined owner, lifetime, durability, and recovery strategy.","incorrect_code":"service.local_state = business_state","corrected_code":"business_state = durable_store; service.local_state = ephemeral_cache","failure_mode":"Incorrect state placement.","expected_error":"","cause":"State durability and lifecycle requirements were not distinguished.","root_cause":"All application state was treated as having identical architectural requirements.","solution":"Classify state by ownership, durability, scope, lifetime, and recovery requirements.","edge_case":"A local cache may be stateful operationally while remaining non-authoritative for business correctness.","security_implication":"Local state may retain sensitive information longer than intended.","performance_implication":"Local state can reduce network access but may increase memory usage and invalidation complexity.","test_case":"Restart or relocate a service and verify required state remains correct.","test_input":"Service restart during active workload.","expected_test_result":"Authoritative state survives according to its specified durability requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test restart, rescheduling, scaling, and concurrent access scenarios.","verification_method":"State ownership matrix plus restart/failover testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify which state is authoritative and which state is reconstructible.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["State inventory","Failure test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STATE_MANAGEMENT_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B013"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B012-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["State ownership and distributed application architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B012-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"Authoritative versus derived state","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture distinguish authoritative state from derived state?","answer":"Authoritative state is the source of truth for a business fact, while derived state can be reconstructed from authoritative information. Treating derived state as authoritative can create synchronization and recovery problems.","explanation":"The distinction determines persistence, recovery, invalidation, replication, and consistency requirements. A cache, index, materialized view, or computed projection may be safely rebuilt if its source of truth is preserved.","principle":"Durability and correctness requirements belong to authoritative facts, not necessarily every representation of those facts.","objective":"Define state ownership and recovery semantics.","requirements":["Every authoritative fact must have a durable owner.","Derived representations must have a rebuild or repair strategy."],"constraints":["Rebuilding derived state may be expensive.","Multiple derived representations can become temporarily inconsistent."],"preconditions":["The system contains replicated or computed state."],"input":"Business facts and their derived representations.","output":"State classification and recovery model.","expected_behavior":"Recovery restores authoritative state first and reconstructs derived state as required.","incorrect_code":"cache = source_of_truth","corrected_code":"database = source_of_truth; cache = reconstructible_representation","failure_mode":"Recovery or consistency failure caused by ambiguous state authority.","expected_error":"","cause":"A derived representation was treated as the authoritative record.","root_cause":"State ownership was not explicitly defined.","solution":"Assign a single authoritative owner for each business fact and classify all other representations as derived.","edge_case":"Some systems intentionally use multiple authoritative stores, but then explicit consistency and reconciliation requirements are necessary.","security_implication":"Stale derived authorization data can create incorrect access decisions.","performance_implication":"Derived state improves read performance but adds update and invalidation costs.","test_case":"Delete the derived representation and rebuild it from authoritative state.","test_input":"Authoritative dataset plus missing index/cache/projection.","expected_test_result":"Derived state can be reconstructed without losing business facts.","testing_guidance":"Test rebuild, corruption, stale-data, and partial-update scenarios.","verification_method":"State lineage analysis and reconstruction test.","verification_guidance":"Trace every critical output back to an authoritative source.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["State lineage model","Recovery environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STATE_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B012-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B012-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Data lineage and state ownership architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B012-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"Session state placement","knowledge_type":"TRADE_OFF","question":"What architectural trade-off exists between local session state and externally stored session state?","answer":"Local session state can reduce lookup latency and external dependencies but complicates load balancing and recovery. External session state improves replaceability and horizontal scaling but adds access latency and dependency management.","explanation":"The correct choice depends on session size, lifetime, availability requirements, scaling strategy, privacy constraints, and acceptable recovery behavior.","principle":"Session-state placement should follow scaling and recovery requirements rather than convenience alone.","objective":"Evaluate session-state placement alternatives.","requirements":["Session continuity must meet the defined user-experience requirement.","Sensitive session data must meet its protection requirements."],"constraints":["External stores introduce dependency and consistency concerns.","Local state requires affinity or replication when requests move between instances."],"preconditions":["The application maintains state across requests."],"input":"Session lifetime, request routing, scaling, and recovery requirements.","output":"Session state placement decision.","expected_behavior":"A service can be restarted or scaled according to its availability requirements without losing required session semantics.","incorrect_code":"session_store = process_memory_only()","corrected_code":"session_store = external_durable_store()","failure_mode":"Session loss or routing dependency during scaling/restart.","expected_error":"","cause":"Session state exists only in a process that may be replaced.","root_cause":"Session durability and routing requirements were not incorporated into state placement.","solution":"Externalize session state or explicitly adopt and test affinity/replication semantics.","edge_case":"Short-lived non-critical sessions may intentionally be reconstructible or disposable.","security_implication":"External session stores require access control, encryption, expiration, and secure invalidation.","performance_implication":"External session access adds latency but can enable more predictable horizontal scaling.","test_case":"Scale or restart the service while a session remains active.","test_input":"Active session followed by instance replacement.","expected_test_result":"Session behavior matches the specified continuity requirement.","testing_guidance":"Test restart, scale-out, expiration, logout, and concurrent session behavior.","verification_method":"Failure and scaling tests combined with state ownership review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that session behavior is preserved under the deployment topology actually used.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Session store or affinity-capable deployment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STATE_MANAGEMENT_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B012-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B012-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Session architecture and horizontal-scaling principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B012-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"State recovery classification","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_REASONING","question":"How should state recovery requirements influence architecture?","answer":"State should be classified according to whether it must be recovered exactly, reconstructed approximately, recomputed, or intentionally discarded after failure.","explanation":"Recovery requirements determine persistence, replication, checkpointing, rebuild processes, and operational procedures. Without classification, architectures often over-persist low-value state or under-protect critical facts.","principle":"Recovery semantics are a property of the state, not merely the storage technology.","objective":"Map state categories to explicit recovery behavior.","requirements":["Critical state must have a tested recovery mechanism.","Discardable state must be explicitly identified."],"constraints":["Exact recovery can require stronger durability and replication mechanisms."],"preconditions":["State categories and recovery objectives are known."],"input":"State inventory and recovery objectives.","output":"Recovery classification matrix.","expected_behavior":"Every important state item has an explicit recovery outcome.","incorrect_code":"recover_all_state_identically()","corrected_code":"recover(authoritative_state="exact",cache_state="rebuild",ephemeral_state="discard")","failure_mode":"Incorrect recovery behavior.","expected_error":"","cause":"All state was assigned the same recovery semantics.","root_cause":"Recovery requirements were not modeled per state category.","solution":"Classify and test recovery behavior independently for each state category.","edge_case":"A derived state may need checkpointing when rebuilding it within the recovery objective is too slow.","security_implication":"Recovery copies and backups may contain sensitive state and require equivalent protection.","performance_implication":"Aggressive persistence improves recovery but increases write and storage overhead.","test_case":"Simulate failure and execute the documented recovery procedure for each state category.","test_input":"Corrupted or lost state under controlled recovery test.","expected_test_result":"Each state category reaches its documented recovery state within the required objective.","testing_guidance":"Perform restore tests instead of relying solely on successful backup creation.","verification_method":"Recovery exercise and state classification review.","verification_guidance":"Verify both correctness and recovery time requirements.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Recovery environment","State inventory"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RECOVERY_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B012-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B012-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["State recovery and resilience architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B012-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"State ownership contract","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","question":"What information should an architecture record for every important state item?","answer":"It should identify the owner, authority, lifetime, durability, consistency requirement, access pattern, recovery behavior, security classification, and invalidation or update mechanism.","explanation":"A state ownership contract turns an ambiguous architectural concern into explicit machine-readable constraints that can be evaluated during design and implementation.","principle":"Every important state item requires an explicit ownership and lifecycle contract.","objective":"Make state architecture directly usable for design and verification.","requirements":["State ownership and lifecycle must be explicit."],"constraints":["Some state is intentionally ephemeral and does not require durable ownership."],"preconditions":["The system contains persistent or runtime state."],"input":"State inventory.","output":"State ownership contracts.","expected_behavior":"Implementation and deployment decisions can be traced back to state contracts.","incorrect_code":"state = {}","corrected_code":"state_contract = {"owner":"OrderStore","authority":"authoritative","durability":"durable","recovery":"restore"}","failure_mode":"Ambiguous state ownership.","expected_error":"","cause":"State lifecycle properties are implicit.","root_cause":"Architecture lacks a state ownership model.","solution":"Record explicit state contracts and validate implementations against them.","edge_case":"Derived caches may have no independent authority but still require availability and consistency policies.","security_implication":"Security classification should travel with state ownership.","performance_implication":"Access patterns can determine whether state belongs in memory, local storage, or remote storage.","test_case":"Review critical state against the ownership contract.","test_input":"State inventory plus implementation components.","expected_test_result":"Every critical state item maps to one authoritative owner and defined lifecycle.","testing_guidance":"Use automated architecture checks where state metadata is machine-readable.","verification_method":"State-to-component traceability review.","verification_guidance":"Verify implementation ownership against the architecture contract.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["State inventory","Architecture metadata"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B012-R01","D33-B012-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B012-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["State ownership and lifecycle architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B013","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Caching Architecture","concept":"Cache requirement derivation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does a performance requirement justify introducing a cache?","answer":"A cache is justified when measured or strongly evidenced access cost materially affects a requirement and the system can tolerate the cache's consistency, invalidation, memory, and failure semantics.","explanation":"Caching should not be assumed to improve every workload. The architecture must establish what is expensive, how frequently it is accessed, how stale it may become, and what happens when the cache is unavailable.","principle":"Caching is a requirement-driven optimization with correctness consequences.","objective":"Determine whether caching is architecturally justified.","requirements":["The workload contains a demonstrated expensive or high-frequency operation.","Staleness and invalidation semantics are acceptable."],"constraints":["Cache capacity is finite.","Cache misses must have a defined fallback."],"preconditions":["Baseline performance or workload evidence exists."],"input":"Latency, throughput, access frequency, data volatility, and staleness requirements.","output":"Cache/no-cache decision and cache contract.","expected_behavior":"Caching improves the targeted requirement without violating correctness.","incorrect_code":"cache_everything()","corrected_code":"cache_only("read_heavy","reconstructible","staleness<=5s")","failure_mode":"Cache adds complexity without satisfying a real requirement.","expected_error":"","cause":"Caching was introduced without identifying the expensive operation or acceptable staleness.","root_cause":"Performance optimization preceded workload characterization.","solution":"Measure first, then define a cache contract around cost, freshness, capacity, and fallback behavior.","edge_case":"A cache may reduce average latency while worsening tail latency during misses or eviction storms.","security_implication":"Cached sensitive data requires access control, isolation, expiration, and secure invalidation.","performance_implication":"Caches can reduce backend load but introduce memory pressure, serialization cost, and invalidation overhead.","test_case":"Compare representative workload behavior with and without the cache.","test_input":"Measured request workload and cache hit/miss patterns.","expected_test_result":"The cache provides measurable benefit under the requirement's workload without correctness violations.","testing_guidance":"Measure hit rate, latency distribution, backend load, and failure behavior.","verification_method":"Load testing plus cache correctness testing.","verification_guidance":"Do not treat a high hit rate alone as proof of architectural value.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Representative workload","Performance measurement"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B014"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B013-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Cache architecture and performance engineering principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B013-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Caching Architecture","concept":"Cache invalidation contract","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What must be defined before a cache can safely represent changing authoritative data?","answer":"The architecture must define freshness requirements, invalidation triggers, expiration behavior, update ordering, miss behavior, and the consequences of serving stale data.","explanation":"Cache invalidation is part of the correctness contract. A cache that cannot establish acceptable freshness should not silently become authoritative for data whose correctness depends on current state.","principle":"Cache correctness depends on an explicit freshness and invalidation contract.","objective":"Define cache freshness behavior before implementation.","requirements":["Maximum acceptable staleness must be defined where freshness matters.","Invalidation or expiration behavior must be specified."],"constraints":["Distributed invalidation may be delayed or lost.","Strong freshness can reduce cache effectiveness."],"preconditions":["Authoritative data changes over time."],"input":"Data freshness and update requirements.","output":"Cache invalidation contract.","expected_behavior":"Every cached value has a defined validity condition.","incorrect_code":"cache[key] = value","corrected_code":"cache[key] = {"value":value,"expires_at":expiry,"version":source_version}","failure_mode":"Stale data violates business expectations.","expected_error":"","cause":"No explicit validity or invalidation rule exists.","root_cause":"Cache semantics were treated as implementation detail rather than architecture contract.","solution":"Define freshness, invalidation, expiration, and fallback semantics.","edge_case":"Some data may be intentionally eventually consistent and can use bounded staleness.","security_implication":"Stale permissions or identity information can produce incorrect security decisions.","performance_implication":"Short expiration improves freshness but lowers hit rate.","test_case":"Update authoritative data and verify cache behavior under the defined freshness window.","test_input":"Source update followed by cache read.","expected_test_result":"Observed value matches the documented freshness guarantee.","testing_guidance":"Test update races, expiration, invalidation loss, and cache misses.","verification_method":"Consistency and freshness scenario testing.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual stale-read duration under realistic concurrency.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Cache implementation","Authoritative data source"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CACHE_CONSISTENCY_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B013-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B013-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Cache consistency and invalidation architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B013-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Caching Architecture","concept":"Cache-aside versus write-through","knowledge_type":"TRADE_OFF","question":"How should cache-aside and write-through strategies be evaluated?","answer":"Cache-aside keeps the authoritative store independent and populates the cache on reads, while write-through updates the cache as part of writes. The choice depends on read/write patterns, consistency requirements, failure semantics, and cache authority.","explanation":"Neither pattern is universally correct. Cache-aside can be simple and tolerant of cache loss but may produce stale or thundering-miss behavior. Write-through can improve read freshness but couples write latency and cache availability to persistence behavior.","principle":"Cache strategy must follow workload and consistency requirements.","objective":"Compare cache population strategies using explicit architectural criteria.","requirements":["Write and read correctness must be preserved.","Cache failure must have defined semantics."],"constraints":["Different strategies produce different failure and latency behavior."],"preconditions":["A cache is already justified by workload requirements."],"input":"Read/write ratio, freshness requirement, cache failure model.","output":"Selected cache update strategy with rationale.","expected_behavior":"The selected strategy satisfies consistency and performance requirements under failure scenarios.","incorrect_code":"always_write_cache_only()","corrected_code":"write_authoritative_store(); optionally_update_cache()","failure_mode":"Cache becomes an unintended source of truth.","expected_error":"","cause":"Cache update strategy was not aligned with authority semantics.","root_cause":"Persistence and cache responsibilities were not separated.","solution":"Define authoritative store first, then choose cache update semantics.","edge_case":"Write-through may still require invalidation or version handling when multiple writers exist.","security_implication":"Cache writes must enforce the same authorization boundaries as authoritative writes.","performance_implication":"Write-through may increase write latency; cache-aside can create miss bursts.","test_case":"Simulate cache failure during both read and write operations.","test_input":"Cache unavailable during authoritative write.","expected_test_result":"Business correctness remains intact according to the selected failure contract.","testing_guidance":"Test cache outage, stale entry, concurrent writers, and cold-start behavior.","verification_method":"Failure-mode and consistency testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify authoritative state independently of cache state.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Cache","Authoritative store"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CACHE_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B013-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B013-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Cache update strategy architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B013-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Caching Architecture","concept":"Cache stampede control","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should an architecture handle many clients requesting the same expired cache entry simultaneously?","answer":"The architecture should bound concurrent regeneration, use controlled refresh, request coalescing, jittered expiration, or another mechanism appropriate to the workload so that a cache miss does not overload the authoritative dependency.","explanation":"The failure is not simply a cache miss. The architectural risk occurs when many misses synchronize and generate a burst against the backing system.","principle":"Cache failure behavior must consider correlated misses, not only individual misses.","objective":"Prevent cache expiration from causing a synchronized load spike.","requirements":["Backend load must remain within safe limits during cache misses.","Critical requests must have bounded resource consumption."],"constraints":["Coordination itself adds complexity and may become a bottleneck."],"preconditions":["Many callers can request identical or related data concurrently."],"input":"Cache expiration and concurrent request pattern.","output":"Controlled cache regeneration strategy.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent misses do not create an uncontrolled backend request storm.","incorrect_code":"if cache_miss: fetch_backend()","corrected_code":"if cache_miss: coalesce_requests_then_fetch_once()","failure_mode":"Cache stampede.","expected_error":"Backend overload, latency spike, or cascading failure.","cause":"Many clients regenerate the same expired entry concurrently.","root_cause":"Cache miss amplification was not represented in the architecture.","solution":"Introduce request coalescing, bounded refresh, expiration jitter, or another workload-appropriate control.","edge_case":"A single expensive key can require different controls from a large set of independent keys.","security_implication":"Amplified requests can contribute to denial-of-service conditions.","performance_implication":"Coalescing reduces duplicate backend work but can increase waiting for the shared refresh.","test_case":"Expire one high-demand cache entry and generate concurrent requests.","test_input":"High concurrency against one expired key.","expected_test_result":"Backend request amplification remains within the defined limit.","testing_guidance":"Measure concurrent backend calls and tail latency during expiration events.","verification_method":"Load test with synchronized cache expiration.","verification_guidance":"Verify protection under worst-case synchronized misses, not only normal traffic.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Cache system","Load-generation environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CACHE_FAILURE_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B013-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B013-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Cache stampede and load-amplification architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B013-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Caching Architecture","concept":"Cache failure containment","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_DECISION","question":"What should happen when a non-authoritative cache becomes unavailable?","answer":"The architecture should define whether requests fall back to the authoritative source, fail closed, serve bounded stale data, or use another explicitly justified behavior.","explanation":"A cache is often treated as optional infrastructure, but an implementation can accidentally make it mandatory. The architecture must define the intended dependency semantics.","principle":"Optional performance infrastructure should not silently become a correctness dependency.","objective":"Define cache outage behavior.","requirements":["Business correctness must remain within the specified failure contract.","Cache failure must have bounded resource impact."],"constraints":["Fallback to the source may increase load.","Serving stale data may violate freshness requirements."],"preconditions":["Cache is non-authoritative."],"input":"Cache outage scenario.","output":"Explicit fallback or failure policy.","expected_behavior":"Cache failure follows the documented resilience contract.","incorrect_code":"if cache_down: reject_all_requests()","corrected_code":"if cache_down: use_authoritative_source_with_load_control()","failure_mode":"Unnecessary service outage caused by optional cache failure.","expected_error":"","cause":"Cache was accidentally treated as mandatory for normal correctness.","root_cause":"Failure semantics were omitted from the architecture.","solution":"Define cache outage behavior and protect the fallback path from overload.","edge_case":"Some caches may contain required derived computation that cannot be regenerated within the availability target.","security_implication":"Fallback paths must enforce the same authorization and data-protection controls.","performance_implication":"Fallback can increase backend load and latency.","test_case":"Disable the cache during representative traffic.","test_input":"Cache unavailable while source remains healthy.","expected_test_result":"Observed behavior matches the documented cache failure policy.","testing_guidance":"Test cache outage at normal and peak load.","verification_method":"Fault injection and fallback load testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both correctness and backend protection.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Cache","Authoritative source","Fault injection"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B013-R04"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B013-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Cache failure containment architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B014","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Consistency Model","concept":"Strong versus eventual consistency requirement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should a system determine whether a workflow requires strong or eventual consistency?","answer":"The decision should come from the business invariant and the consequences of observing stale or divergent state. Stronger consistency is required when stale observations can violate a mandatory correctness invariant; eventual consistency may be acceptable when bounded delay does not violate the requirement.","explanation":"Consistency is not a generic quality ranking. The architecture must identify which facts must agree at what point in time and which workflows tolerate delayed convergence.","principle":"Consistency strength is a requirement derived from business invariants.","objective":"Select a consistency model from explicit correctness requirements.","requirements":["Critical invariants must not be violated by permitted consistency behavior.","Acceptable propagation delay must be defined where eventual consistency is used."],"constraints":["Stronger consistency can increase latency and coordination.","Eventual consistency requires convergence and conflict handling."],"preconditions":["Business invariants and stale-data consequences are known."],"input":"Business invariant, read/write topology, acceptable staleness.","output":"Consistency requirement and architecture.","expected_behavior":"Observed consistency matches the declared requirement under concurrency and failure.","incorrect_code":"consistency = "strong" # without requirement analysis","corrected_code":"consistency = derive_from_business_invariant(invariant,staleness_tolerance)","failure_mode":"Incorrect consistency model.","expected_error":"","cause":"Consistency strength was selected without identifying business consequences.","root_cause":"Business invariants were not translated into consistency requirements.","solution":"Document the invariant, stale-read tolerance, convergence target, and conflict policy.","edge_case":"Different operations in the same system may legitimately require different consistency levels.","security_implication":"Stale authorization or policy state can create security violations.","performance_implication":"Consistency coordination can increase latency and reduce independent availability.","test_case":"Run concurrent writes and reads under the selected consistency architecture.","test_input":"Conflicting concurrent updates.","expected_test_result":"All declared invariants remain satisfied.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent updates, delayed propagation, retries, and partition scenarios.","verification_method":"Invariant-based concurrency testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify business invariants rather than relying on infrastructure labels such as 'strong' or 'eventual'.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Concurrency test environment","Business invariant specification"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CONSISTENCY_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B015"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B014-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed consistency and business-invariant architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B014-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Consistency Model","concept":"Bounded staleness","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_CONSTRAINT","question":"When is bounded staleness a useful architecture requirement?","answer":"Bounded staleness is useful when a system can tolerate delayed data but must guarantee that divergence does not exceed an explicitly defined limit.","explanation":"Instead of vaguely declaring a system eventually consistent, the architecture can specify a maximum acceptable propagation delay and define what happens when that limit is exceeded.","principle":"Eventual consistency becomes operationally meaningful when convergence bounds are explicit.","objective":"Translate freshness requirements into a measurable consistency constraint.","requirements":["Maximum tolerated staleness must be measurable.","Violation handling must be defined."],"constraints":["Real-world delays may vary during failure conditions."],"preconditions":["Business process tolerates limited stale data."],"input":"Maximum acceptable data age.","output":"Bounded-staleness architecture requirement.","expected_behavior":"Data converges within the specified bound during normal operating conditions and violations are observable.","incorrect_code":"eventual_consistency=true","corrected_code":"freshness_slo = {"max_age_seconds":30,"violation":"alert_and_fallback"}","failure_mode":"Unbounded stale data.","expected_error":"","cause":"Eventual consistency was accepted without a freshness limit.","root_cause":"Freshness was described qualitatively instead of as a requirement.","solution":"Define a measurable freshness target and violation policy.","edge_case":"During declared outages, the system may need a degraded behavior with a different contract.","security_implication":"Stale policy or identity information may become unsafe after the allowed bound.","performance_implication":"Tighter freshness targets can require more frequent synchronization.","test_case":"Delay propagation intentionally and measure observed data age.","test_input":"Propagation delay greater than and less than the allowed bound.","expected_test_result":"Normal operation remains within the defined freshness requirement and violations are detected.","testing_guidance":"Measure freshness distribution rather than only average delay.","verification_method":"Propagation-delay instrumentation and threshold testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify the bound under realistic load and failure conditions.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Timestamped data","Propagation monitoring"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CONSISTENCY_REQUIREMENT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B014-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B014-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Bounded-staleness and data-freshness architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B014-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Consistency Model","concept":"Conflict resolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does a distributed architecture require an explicit conflict-resolution policy?","answer":"Whenever multiple authorities or replicas can accept competing updates before convergence, the architecture must define how conflicts are detected, resolved, rejected, or reconciled.","explanation":"Conflict handling cannot be safely delegated to an unspecified database or messaging layer when the business meaning of competing updates matters.","principle":"Concurrent authority requires explicit conflict semantics.","objective":"Ensure distributed updates have deterministic or business-defined conflict behavior.","requirements":["Conflicts must be detectable.","Resolution must preserve required business invariants."],"constraints":["Some conflicts cannot be resolved automatically without business semantics."],"preconditions":["Multiple writers or replicas can modify related state."],"input":"Concurrent update scenarios.","output":"Conflict detection and resolution model.","expected_behavior":"Conflicting updates follow the documented resolution contract.","incorrect_code":"last_write_wins_everything()","corrected_code":"resolve_conflict_using_business_version_or_explicit_policy()","failure_mode":"Silent data loss or invariant violation.","expected_error":"","cause":"Competing updates were accepted without defined conflict semantics.","root_cause":"Multiple-writer architecture lacked an explicit authority and conflict model.","solution":"Define versioning, rejection, merge, or domain-specific conflict resolution.","edge_case":"Last-write-wins may be valid for some non-critical fields but unsafe for cumulative business facts.","security_implication":"Conflict resolution can affect authorization, ownership, or policy state and must not silently weaken controls.","performance_implication":"Conflict detection and reconciliation add metadata and processing cost.","test_case":"Submit concurrent conflicting updates and inspect the final state.","test_input":"Two writers update the same logical entity concurrently.","expected_test_result":"Final state follows the documented conflict policy without violating invariants.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent writes and delayed replication.","verification_method":"Concurrency and reconciliation testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both final state and preservation of business invariants.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Concurrent-write test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_CONFLICT_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B014-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B014-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed conflict-resolution architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B014-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Consistency Model","concept":"Read-your-writes consistency","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does a user workflow require read-your-writes consistency?","answer":"A workflow requires read-your-writes behavior when a user or caller expects a successful write to be immediately visible to subsequent reads within the defined interaction boundary.","explanation":"This requirement can often be satisfied without globally strong consistency. Architecture may use session affinity, version tokens, routing, or other mechanisms that ensure the relevant caller observes its own committed change.","principle":"Consistency requirements can be scoped to interaction semantics rather than applied globally.","objective":"Identify and implement scoped read-after-write guarantees.","requirements":["A successful write must become visible to subsequent required reads within the defined scope."],"constraints":["Global consistency may be more expensive than scoped consistency."],"preconditions":["The workflow contains a write followed by a dependent read."],"input":"User workflow and visibility expectation.","output":"Scoped consistency requirement.","expected_behavior":"The caller observes its committed state according to the specified guarantee.","incorrect_code":"write(); read_from_any_replica()","corrected_code":"write(); read_using_version_or_consistent_route()","failure_mode":"User observes stale state immediately after a successful update.","expected_error":"","cause":"The subsequent read was routed to a stale replica without a consistency mechanism.","root_cause":"User-visible consistency semantics were not included in architecture requirements.","solution":"Add a scoped read-your-writes mechanism appropriate to the storage architecture.","edge_case":"Background clients may not require the same guarantee.","security_implication":"Stale reads can expose outdated ownership or authorization state.","performance_implication":"Scoped routing can preserve performance better than globally strong coordination.","test_case":"Perform a write followed immediately by a read under replication delay.","test_input":"Write followed by read against potentially stale replica.","expected_test_result":"The caller observes the newly committed value.","testing_guidance":"Inject replication delay and test immediate subsequent reads.","verification_method":"Consistency scenario testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify the guarantee at the caller-visible boundary.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Replicated data store or equivalent consistency test"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CONSISTENCY_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B014-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B014-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Read-your-writes consistency architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B014-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Consistency Model","concept":"Consistency requirement decomposition","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_METHOD","question":"Why should consistency requirements be decomposed per operation instead of declared globally?","answer":"Different operations can have different correctness requirements. A payment authorization, analytics dashboard, search index, and notification feed may legitimately require different consistency semantics.","explanation":"Global consistency declarations often either over-constrain inexpensive workloads or under-specify critical workflows. Decomposing requirements produces more precise architecture choices.","principle":"Consistency should be specified at the business-operation boundary where its consequences are understood.","objective":"Create granular consistency requirements for architecture selection.","requirements":["Each critical operation has an explicit consistency contract."],"constraints":["More granular contracts increase documentation and verification work."],"preconditions":["System operations can be identified separately."],"input":"Operation inventory and business invariants.","output":"Operation-level consistency matrix.","expected_behavior":"Architecture choices can differ where requirements genuinely differ.","incorrect_code":"system_consistency = ONE_GLOBAL_MODE","corrected_code":"consistency = {"payment":"strong","analytics":"eventual","search":"bounded_staleness"}","failure_mode":"Overly strong or insufficient consistency architecture.","expected_error":"","cause":"A single consistency policy was applied to all operations.","root_cause":"Business-level consistency differences were ignored.","solution":"Define consistency guarantees per critical operation.","edge_case":"Operations sharing the same underlying transaction may impose stronger coupled constraints.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive operations should not inherit weak consistency accidentally.","performance_implication":"Granular consistency can reduce unnecessary coordination cost.","test_case":"Map each critical operation to a consistency guarantee and test representative scenarios.","test_input":"Operation-to-consistency matrix.","expected_test_result":"All critical operations have explicit and verified consistency semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use requirement traceability to identify unclassified operations.","verification_method":"Consistency contract review plus scenario tests.","verification_guidance":"Check for implicit dependencies between operations with different guarantees.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Operation inventory","Consistency requirements"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B014-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B014-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Consistency requirement decomposition principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B015","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Messaging Architecture","concept":"Queue requirement derivation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does asynchronous messaging solve a requirement that synchronous communication does not?","answer":"Asynchronous messaging is justified when the producer must decouple its completion from downstream processing, absorb bursts, provide durable work handoff, or allow independent consumer availability.","explanation":"Messaging adds delivery, ordering, duplication, retry, observability, and operational concerns. It should therefore be introduced for a requirement that benefits from those properties.","principle":"Asynchrony is an architectural mechanism for decoupling time, availability, and workload, not merely a performance feature.","objective":"Determine whether a queue or broker is required.","requirements":["Producer and consumer timing or availability must be independently manageable.","Delivery semantics must be defined."],"constraints":["Messaging introduces operational and consistency complexity."],"preconditions":["The business workflow permits asynchronous completion where applicable."],"input":"Latency, availability, burst, delivery, and workflow requirements.","output":"Messaging/no-messaging decision.","expected_behavior":"The selected communication model preserves required user and business semantics.","incorrect_code":"process_everything_synchronously()","corrected_code":"enqueue_work(); acknowledge_when_durable(); process_asynchronously()","failure_mode":"Synchronous coupling creates latency or availability dependency.","expected_error":"","cause":"Downstream work was unnecessarily placed on the synchronous request path.","root_cause":"Temporal decoupling requirement was not represented architecturally.","solution":"Introduce asynchronous messaging when independent completion and durable handoff are required.","edge_case":"Some operations require synchronous confirmation and cannot be safely moved behind a queue.","security_implication":"Message contents, producers, consumers, and replay behavior require authorization and protection.","performance_implication":"Queues can absorb bursts but add latency and processing overhead.","test_case":"Make the consumer unavailable while producers continue submitting work.","test_input":"Consumer outage with continued producer traffic.","expected_test_result":"Producer behavior matches the defined durability and backpressure requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test backlog growth, retry, duplicate delivery, and consumer recovery.","verification_method":"Failure and backlog scenario testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual delivery semantics rather than assuming queue durability implies exactly-once processing.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":["Messaging infrastructure if selected"],"execution_requirements":["Message broker or durable queue"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"MESSAGING_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B016"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B015-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Asynchronous messaging and workload-decoupling architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B015-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Messaging Architecture","concept":"At-least-once delivery and idempotency","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why does an at-least-once message architecture usually require idempotent consumers?","answer":"Because a message may be delivered more than once, a consumer that performs a non-idempotent operation can apply the business effect multiple times. The architecture therefore needs deduplication, idempotency keys, or another mechanism that makes repeated delivery safe.","explanation":"Delivery guarantees and business effect guarantees are different properties. A broker may reliably redeliver a message while the consumer still performs the operation twice unless the business operation is designed for repetition.","principle":"Transport delivery semantics do not automatically provide business-operation exactly-once effects.","objective":"Translate message delivery semantics into safe consumer behavior.","requirements":["Repeated delivery must not violate business invariants."],"constraints":["Idempotency may require persistent operation identifiers or state."],"preconditions":["Messages may be redelivered."],"input":"Message delivery guarantee and business operation semantics.","output":"Consumer idempotency architecture.","expected_behavior":"Duplicate deliveries produce at most the intended business effect.","incorrect_code":"charge_customer(message.amount)","corrected_code":"if not processed(message.id):\n    charge_customer(message.amount)\n    record_processed(message.id)","failure_mode":"Duplicate business effect.","expected_error":"","cause":"Consumer assumes one delivery despite at-least-once transport semantics.","root_cause":"Transport and business-effect guarantees were conflated.","solution":"Use idempotency keys, deduplication records, or an equivalent business-safe mechanism.","edge_case":"The idempotency record and business effect must themselves have a consistency/atomicity strategy.","security_implication":"Replayable messages may cause duplicate privileged operations without idempotency controls.","performance_implication":"Deduplication adds storage and lookup cost but prevents expensive duplicate work.","test_case":"Deliver the same message multiple times and inspect business state.","test_input":"Identical message identifier delivered twice.","expected_test_result":"Only one intended business effect occurs.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate, delayed, reordered, and retry deliveries.","verification_method":"Duplicate-delivery integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify the business effect, not merely message acknowledgement.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Message broker","Consumer","Persistent idempotency mechanism where required"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"MESSAGING_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B015-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B015-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Message delivery semantics and idempotent consumer architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B015-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Messaging Architecture","concept":"Ordering requirement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When must a messaging architecture preserve message ordering?","answer":"Ordering must be preserved when the business meaning of processing one event before another depends on their sequence. If events are independent, global ordering may impose unnecessary throughput and availability costs.","explanation":"The architecture should identify the smallest ordering domain required, such as per account, aggregate, partition, or workflow, rather than automatically requiring global order.","principle":"Ordering guarantees should be scoped to the business invariant that requires them.","objective":"Derive the minimum required message-ordering boundary.","requirements":["Dependent events must be processed in an order that preserves business invariants."],"constraints":["Global ordering can reduce parallelism and increase coordination."],"preconditions":["Events have explicit causal or business dependencies."],"input":"Event dependency graph.","output":"Ordering scope.","expected_behavior":"Required event sequences are preserved while independent events remain parallelizable.","incorrect_code":"global_order_for_all_events()","corrected_code":"order_by_key("account_id")","failure_mode":"Incorrect event ordering or unnecessary global serialization.","expected_error":"","cause":"Ordering requirement was either omitted or applied too broadly.","root_cause":"Event dependency scope was not modeled.","solution":"Define ordering per business key or workflow where necessary.","edge_case":"Retries and redelivery can still affect effective processing order even when transport ordering exists.","security_implication":"Authorization or policy changes may require ordered application to avoid temporary privilege inconsistencies.","performance_implication":"Narrow ordering keys preserve more parallelism than global ordering.","test_case":"Deliver dependent events with controlled reordering.","test_input":"Event A followed by dependent event B, with deliberate reorder attempt.","expected_test_result":"The system preserves or safely handles the required sequence.","testing_guidance":"Test partition movement, retries, duplicates, and concurrent keys.","verification_method":"Ordering scenario tests and event dependency analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual processing order at the business-effect boundary.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":["Messaging infrastructure if selected"],"execution_requirements":["Ordered messaging test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"MESSAGING_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B015-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B015-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Message ordering and event-dependency architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B015-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Messaging Architecture","concept":"Backpressure architecture","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_DECISION","question":"Why must a messaging architecture define what happens when producers outpace consumers?","answer":"Without a backpressure policy, backlog can grow until storage, memory, latency, or downstream dependencies fail. The architecture must define admission control, queue limits, shedding, throttling, or another bounded behavior.","explanation":"A queue absorbs bursts only within finite capacity. Backpressure is therefore a system-level resource and availability requirement, not merely a broker configuration option.","principle":"Every asynchronous buffer requires a bounded overload strategy.","objective":"Prevent unbounded backlog growth.","requirements":["Queue growth must remain within safe operational limits.","Producer behavior under saturation must be defined."],"constraints":["Dropping work may be unacceptable for some business operations."],"preconditions":["Producer rate can exceed consumer capacity."],"input":"Arrival rate, processing rate, queue capacity, and business loss tolerance.","output":"Backpressure policy.","expected_behavior":"System remains within defined resource and correctness limits during sustained overload.","incorrect_code":"queue.unbounded_push(message)","corrected_code":"queue.push_with_limit(message); reject_or_throttle_when_full()","failure_mode":"Queue saturation and cascading resource exhaustion.","expected_error":"","cause":"Producer admission was not bounded by consumer capacity.","root_cause":"No overload contract existed for asynchronous processing.","solution":"Define queue limits and producer behavior under saturation.","edge_case":"Critical messages may require durable retention while low-value work can be shed.","security_implication":"Unbounded queues can be abused for resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Backpressure stabilizes resource usage but may increase producer latency or rejection rates.","test_case":"Run producers faster than consumers for a sustained period.","test_input":"Producer rate greater than consumer rate.","expected_test_result":"The system reaches its defined bounded-overload behavior without uncontrolled resource growth.","testing_guidance":"Measure queue depth, memory, disk, latency, and rejection/throttling behavior.","verification_method":"Overload and soak testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify behavior after the queue reaches each configured threshold.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":["Messaging infrastructure"],"execution_requirements":["Load generator","Queue metrics"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B015-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B015-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Backpressure and bounded-resource architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B015-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Messaging Architecture","concept":"Dead-letter handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why does a retrying message system need a terminal failure path?","answer":"Messages that repeatedly fail may otherwise consume resources indefinitely and prevent healthy work from progressing. A dead-letter or equivalent quarantine path provides explicit handling for messages that cannot currently be processed.","explanation":"Retries are useful only when the failure may recover. Permanent validation errors, incompatible schemas, or exhausted dependencies require a terminal handling strategy.","principle":"Retry policies require an explicit terminal state for non-recoverable work.","objective":"Design bounded message failure handling.","requirements":["Repeatedly failing messages must not retry indefinitely.","Failed work must remain observable and recoverable according to business requirements."],"constraints":["Quarantined messages require operational ownership and replay semantics."],"preconditions":["Consumer processing can fail repeatedly."],"input":"Failure classification and retry policy.","output":"Retry and terminal-failure architecture.","expected_behavior":"Recoverable failures retry within bounds; non-recoverable failures transition to controlled terminal handling.","incorrect_code":"while failed: retry_forever(message)","corrected_code":"retry_with_limit(message); move_to_dead_letter_when_exhausted()","failure_mode":"Infinite retry loop or poisoned-message starvation.","expected_error":"","cause":"No terminal failure state exists.","root_cause":"Retry architecture lacks failure classification and bounded attempts.","solution":"Define retry limits, failure categories, dead-letter handling, and replay controls.","edge_case":"A temporary dependency outage may justify delayed retry while malformed input should normally not retry indefinitely.","security_implication":"Dead-letter stores may contain sensitive or attacker-controlled payloads and require access controls.","performance_implication":"Bounded retries prevent resource starvation.","test_case":"Inject permanent and transient consumer failures.","test_input":"Malformed message and temporary dependency failure.","expected_test_result":"Permanent failures reach terminal handling while transient failures follow retry policy.","testing_guidance":"Test retry exhaustion and safe replay.","verification_method":"Failure classification and message lifecycle testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that terminal messages remain traceable and do not silently disappear.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":["Messaging infrastructure"],"execution_requirements":["Message broker","Failure injection"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"MESSAGING_FAILURE_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B015-R04"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B015-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Retry, dead-letter, and poisoned-message handling architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B016","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Workflow Architecture","concept":"Workflow orchestration versus choreography","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture choose between centralized workflow orchestration and distributed choreography?","answer":"Orchestration is useful when workflow state, sequencing, compensation, and visibility require a central coordinator. Choreography can reduce central coordination when components can react independently to events and the resulting behavior remains understandable and verifiable.","explanation":"The decision depends on workflow complexity, observability, compensation requirements, ownership, and coupling. Neither pattern is universally superior.","principle":"Workflow coordination style should follow the complexity and visibility requirements of the business process.","objective":"Select a workflow coordination architecture.","requirements":["The workflow must remain understandable and recoverable.","Failure and compensation behavior must be defined."],"constraints":["Central orchestration can become a bottleneck or coupling point.","Choreography can make global behavior harder to understand."],"preconditions":["The system contains multi-step workflows."],"input":"Workflow steps, dependencies, compensation rules, ownership.","output":"Orchestration or choreography decision.","expected_behavior":"Workflow state and failure behavior remain observable and controllable.","incorrect_code":"all_workflow_logic_distributed_without_state_model()","corrected_code":"workflow = explicit_coordinator_or_explicit_event_contracts()","failure_mode":"Untraceable distributed workflow behavior.","expected_error":"","cause":"Workflow coordination was distributed without an explicit global behavior model.","root_cause":"The architecture optimized local autonomy without modeling system-level workflow semantics.","solution":"Choose orchestration or choreography based on workflow requirements and document state transitions.","edge_case":"Hybrid designs can use orchestration for critical workflows and event reactions for independent side effects.","security_implication":"Workflow authorization and step ownership must remain explicit across components.","performance_implication":"Central orchestration adds coordination overhead; choreography can reduce central bottlenecks.","test_case":"Simulate failure at every workflow step and inspect recovery behavior.","test_input":"Multi-step workflow with injected failure at each transition.","expected_test_result":"Workflow reaches a defined terminal or recoverable state.","testing_guidance":"Use state-machine and failure-injection testing.","verification_method":"Workflow state model plus failure scenario testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify every state transition and compensation path.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Workflow model","Failure-injection environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"WORKFLOW_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B017"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B016-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Workflow orchestration and choreography architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B016-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Workflow Architecture","concept":"Compensation over rollback","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is compensation required instead of a database rollback?","answer":"Compensation is required when a workflow spans independent resources or external side effects that cannot participate in one atomic transaction.","explanation":"A database rollback can reverse database state but cannot automatically undo an external payment, email, shipment, or third-party API action. The architecture must therefore model compensating actions or accept the irreversible effect explicitly.","principle":"Distributed workflow consistency requires domain-specific compensation when atomic rollback is impossible.","objective":"Identify workflows that need compensation semantics.","requirements":["Irreversible or external steps must have explicit failure semantics.","Compensation must preserve business invariants where possible."],"constraints":["Some external effects cannot be perfectly compensated."],"preconditions":["Workflow spans multiple transactional boundaries."],"input":"Workflow side effects and transactional boundaries.","output":"Compensation model.","expected_behavior":"Failure after a completed external step triggers a defined compensating or reconciliation process.","incorrect_code":"rollback_all_external_effects()","corrected_code":"record_effect(); execute_compensation_or_reconciliation()","failure_mode":"Partial workflow completion.","expected_error":"","cause":"Architecture assumes a single rollback boundary across independent systems.","root_cause":"External side effects were modeled as transactional when they were not.","solution":"Use compensation, reconciliation, or explicit business acceptance of irreversible outcomes.","edge_case":"Compensation can itself fail and therefore requires retry and reconciliation semantics.","security_implication":"Compensating actions must preserve authorization and audit requirements.","performance_implication":"Compensation increases workflow complexity and processing time.","test_case":"Fail after an external side effect has succeeded.","test_input":"Payment succeeds; subsequent workflow step fails.","expected_test_result":"Defined compensation or reconciliation path is initiated and observable.","testing_guidance":"Test compensation failure as well as successful compensation.","verification_method":"Saga/workflow failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify final business state and audit trail.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Workflow test harness","External-effect simulator"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_TRANSACTION_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B016-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B016-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed transaction and compensating workflow architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B016-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Workflow Architecture","concept":"Workflow state persistence","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When must workflow state be persisted independently of the executing process?","answer":"Workflow state should be durably persisted when workflows can outlive a process, require recovery after failure, involve human or long-duration waits, or require auditability of state transitions.","explanation":"Keeping long-lived workflow state only in memory makes process replacement equivalent to workflow loss. Persistence should match the workflow's lifetime and recovery requirements.","principle":"Workflow lifetime determines workflow-state durability requirements.","objective":"Determine workflow state persistence needs.","requirements":["Recoverable workflows must survive process replacement.","State transitions must be auditable when required."],"constraints":["Durable workflow state introduces storage and schema-evolution requirements."],"preconditions":["Workflow duration can exceed process lifetime or includes recovery requirements."],"input":"Workflow lifetime and recovery requirements.","output":"Workflow state persistence architecture.","expected_behavior":"A process failure does not lose required workflow state.","incorrect_code":"workflow_state = process_memory_only()","corrected_code":"workflow_state = durable_state_store()","failure_mode":"Workflow loss after process failure.","expected_error":"","cause":"Workflow state exists only in volatile process memory.","root_cause":"Workflow lifetime exceeded the durability model.","solution":"Persist required state and define recovery semantics.","edge_case":"Very short-lived best-effort workflows may intentionally remain ephemeral.","security_implication":"Persisted workflow state may contain sensitive information and requires appropriate protection.","performance_implication":"Persistence adds I/O and coordination overhead.","test_case":"Terminate the workflow worker while a workflow is active.","test_input":"Process termination during persisted workflow state transition.","expected_test_result":"Workflow resumes or reaches the defined recovery state.","testing_guidance":"Test crash recovery at every major workflow state.","verification_method":"Crash-recovery testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify no required workflow state is silently lost.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Durable workflow store"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"WORKFLOW_RESILIENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B016-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B016-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Durable workflow-state architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B016-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Workflow Architecture","concept":"Human approval as workflow state","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should a workflow architecture model a required human approval step?","answer":"Human approval should be an explicit durable workflow state with authorization, timeout, audit, and resume semantics rather than an implicit blocking call.","explanation":"A human decision can take minutes, hours, or days and may occur after the executing process has restarted. Modeling approval as state makes the workflow recoverable and auditable.","principle":"Long-lived human decisions belong in durable workflow state, not process-local control flow.","objective":"Represent human approval requirements safely in workflow architecture.","requirements":["Approval identity and authority must be explicit.","Approval state must survive process restart.","Approval timeout and rejection behavior must be defined."],"constraints":["Human availability is unpredictable.","Approval interfaces must not expose unauthorized actions."],"preconditions":["Business workflow requires explicit human authorization."],"input":"Approval requirement and authorization policy.","output":"Durable approval workflow state.","expected_behavior":"Workflow pauses safely and resumes only after a valid authorized decision.","incorrect_code":"wait_for_human_inside_process()","corrected_code":"persist_state("AWAITING_APPROVAL"); notify_authorized_approver()","failure_mode":"Lost approval state or unauthorized continuation.","expected_error":"","cause":"Human interaction was implemented as process-local blocking logic.","root_cause":"Human decision latency was not represented as a durable workflow state.","solution":"Persist approval state and enforce authorization at resume time.","edge_case":"Approval may expire or become invalid when the underlying facts change.","security_implication":"Authorization must be revalidated when approval is applied.","performance_implication":"Durable state prevents resource occupation while waiting for human action.","test_case":"Restart the workflow while awaiting approval and then submit approval.","test_input":"Process restart followed by valid and invalid approval attempts.","expected_test_result":"Only authorized valid approval resumes the workflow.","testing_guidance":"Test expiration, revocation, duplicate approval, and changed-context scenarios.","verification_method":"Workflow authorization and restart testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify approval identity, authority, state version, and audit trail.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Durable workflow store","Authorization system"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"HUMAN_IN_THE_LOOP_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B016-R03"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B016-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Human-in-the-loop workflow architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B016-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Workflow Architecture","concept":"Workflow versioning","knowledge_type":"VERSION_ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why does a long-running workflow require workflow-definition versioning?","answer":"A workflow started under one definition may still be executing after a newer definition is deployed. Without version semantics, the running instance can encounter incompatible states or behavior.","explanation":"The architecture must decide whether existing instances remain on their original definition, migrate explicitly, or use compatibility adapters. Deployment version and workflow instance version are separate concepts.","principle":"Long-lived workflow instances require explicit compatibility semantics across definition changes.","objective":"Prevent workflow-version deployment conflicts.","requirements":["Running workflow instances must have deterministic interpretation after deployment changes."],"constraints":["Migration of in-flight workflows can be complex.","Keeping old definitions increases maintenance burden."],"preconditions":["Workflows can outlive application releases."],"input":"Workflow lifecycle and deployment version policy.","output":"Workflow versioning strategy.","expected_behavior":"Existing workflow instances remain valid or are explicitly migrated.","incorrect_code":"execute_with_current_workflow_definition(instance)","corrected_code":"execute_with_definition(instance.definition_version)","failure_mode":"In-flight workflow becomes incompatible after deployment.","expected_error":"","cause":"Runtime selected the latest workflow definition for an older instance.","root_cause":"Workflow instance version was not persisted or respected.","solution":"Persist workflow definition version and define migration/compatibility policy.","edge_case":"A security-critical policy change may require terminating or migrating old instances rather than continuing them.","security_implication":"Old workflow definitions may contain obsolete authorization behavior.","performance_implication":"Supporting multiple versions increases code and testing cost.","test_case":"Start a workflow, deploy a new definition, then resume the old instance.","test_input":"Version 1 instance resumed under version 2 deployment.","expected_test_result":"The instance follows its declared compatibility policy.","testing_guidance":"Test old, current, migrated, and incompatible workflow versions.","verification_method":"Versioned workflow integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both instance version persistence and transition behavior.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Versioned workflow runtime","Deployment test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERSIONED_WORKFLOW_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B016-R03"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B016-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Long-running workflow versioning and migration principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B017","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Read/Write Workload Architecture","concept":"Read-heavy workload architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should a read-heavy workload influence architecture?","answer":"A read-heavy workload may justify read replicas, caching, materialized views, specialized indexes, or other read-optimization techniques when supported by measured access patterns and consistency requirements.","explanation":"The architecture should first establish whether reads dominate and where read latency or backend capacity becomes a requirement. Optimization must preserve required consistency and correctness.","principle":"Workload shape should influence architecture only after it is measured or otherwise evidenced.","objective":"Select architecture for high read volume without compromising correctness.","requirements":["Read capacity and latency targets must be met.","Read optimization must respect consistency requirements."],"constraints":["Replicas and caches can introduce stale data."],"preconditions":["Read/write distribution is known."],"input":"Read/write ratio, latency targets, consistency requirements.","output":"Read-optimized architecture.","expected_behavior":"Read capacity scales without violating defined consistency semantics.","incorrect_code":"scale_writes_and_reads_identically()","corrected_code":"scale_read_path_independently_when_measurements_require_it()","failure_mode":"Read bottleneck limits system capacity.","expected_error":"","cause":"Architecture does not reflect dominant read workload.","root_cause":"Workload characteristics were not considered in capacity architecture.","solution":"Introduce appropriate read scaling after profiling the workload.","edge_case":"A read-heavy workload may still require strongly consistent reads for selected operations.","security_implication":"Replicas and caches must enforce equivalent access controls.","performance_implication":"Read scaling can improve capacity but adds replication and consistency costs.","test_case":"Generate representative read-heavy traffic and measure bottlenecks.","test_input":"High read-to-write workload.","expected_test_result":"The architecture meets read performance and capacity requirements.","testing_guidance":"Measure throughput and tail latency under realistic read distributions.","verification_method":"Load testing and bottleneck analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify consistency during scaling and replication delay.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Load-generation environment","Performance metrics"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"WORKLOAD_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B013","D33-B014"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B017-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Read/write workload architecture and capacity-planning principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B017-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Read/Write Workload Architecture","concept":"Materialized read model","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When can a materialized read model simplify a complex read requirement?","answer":"A materialized read model is useful when read queries are expensive or structurally different from the authoritative write model and the system can tolerate or control the resulting update lag.","explanation":"The architecture separates write authority from read representation. This can improve read performance and simplify query shape but introduces synchronization and rebuild requirements.","principle":"A specialized read model is justified when query requirements materially differ from write-state representation.","objective":"Evaluate materialized read models as a read-optimization architecture.","requirements":["Read model freshness must meet the defined requirement.","Read model can be rebuilt or repaired."],"constraints":["Projection lag and synchronization complexity must be managed."],"preconditions":["Complex or high-volume read workload exists."],"input":"Query workload and freshness requirements.","output":"Materialized read-model decision.","expected_behavior":"Read queries meet requirements while source-of-truth integrity remains independent.","incorrect_code":"run_expensive_join_on_every_request()","corrected_code":"maintain_read_projection_from_authoritative_events()","failure_mode":"Read performance bottleneck.","expected_error":"","cause":"Complex query is executed repeatedly despite stable derived data.","root_cause":"Read model was not separated from authoritative write representation where requirements justified it.","solution":"Introduce a rebuildable projection with explicit freshness semantics.","edge_case":"Projection bugs require replay or rebuild from authoritative data.","security_implication":"Read projections must not accidentally expose fields omitted by the source authorization model.","performance_implication":"Materialization shifts cost from query time to update/storage time.","test_case":"Rebuild projection from authoritative data and compare expected read results.","test_input":"Authoritative records plus projection generation.","expected_test_result":"Projection matches its defined transformation and freshness contract.","testing_guidance":"Test replay, missed events, schema changes, and stale projection behavior.","verification_method":"Projection correctness and rebuild testing.","verification_guidance":"Compare projection against authoritative state rather than only testing sample queries.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Authoritative store","Projection mechanism"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"READ_MODEL_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B017-R01","D33-B015-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B017-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Materialized view and read-model architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B017-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Read/Write Workload Architecture","concept":"Write-path protection under read scaling","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must read scaling architecture protect write capacity?","answer":"Read replicas, caches, or projections can reduce read pressure, but synchronization, replication, and projection work consume resources on the write path. Architecture must account for this secondary load.","explanation":"An architecture that adds many read-side mechanisms can inadvertently increase write latency or replication pressure. The full workload must therefore be evaluated.","principle":"Read optimization changes system-wide resource usage and must be evaluated end-to-end.","objective":"Ensure read optimization does not violate write requirements.","requirements":["Write latency and throughput targets remain satisfied after read scaling is introduced."],"constraints":["Replication and projection consume network, CPU, storage, and write resources."],"preconditions":["Read scaling mechanisms are being considered."],"input":"Read optimization design and write workload.","output":"Balanced read/write capacity architecture.","expected_behavior":"Read improvements do not cause unacceptable write degradation.","incorrect_code":"add_unbounded_read_replicas()","corrected_code":"size_read_scaling_against_replication_and_write_capacity()","failure_mode":"Write degradation after read scaling.","expected_error":"","cause":"Secondary replication/projection load was excluded from capacity planning.","root_cause":"Architecture evaluated only the target read path.","solution":"Model resource impact across both read and write paths.","edge_case":"Read models may be asynchronous and still consume significant event-processing capacity.","security_implication":"Replication expands the number of locations containing sensitive data.","performance_implication":"More replicas can increase replication traffic and storage cost.","test_case":"Run mixed read/write workload before and after read scaling.","test_input":"Representative production-like workload.","expected_test_result":"Both read and write requirements remain within defined limits.","testing_guidance":"Measure resource utilization and tail latency for both paths.","verification_method":"Mixed-workload performance testing.","verification_guidance":"Compare against explicit baseline requirements.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Load-generation environment","Capacity metrics"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CAPACITY_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B017-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B017-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Read/write capacity and replication architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B017-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Read/Write Workload Architecture","concept":"CQRS requirement boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does separating command and query models become a justified architectural decision?","answer":"The separation is justified when read and write models have materially different scalability, consistency, evolution, or domain requirements that cannot be handled cleanly within one model.","explanation":"CQRS is not required merely because a system has reads and writes. Every system has both. The architectural value comes from materially different requirements between command and query processing.","principle":"Architectural separation requires a meaningful difference in requirements, not merely different operation types.","objective":"Prevent unnecessary CQRS complexity.","requirements":["Read and write concerns must have materially different requirements.","The additional synchronization and model complexity must be justified."],"constraints":["Separate models increase code, data flow, and consistency complexity."],"preconditions":["Read/write requirements are independently characterized."],"input":"Command and query workload requirements.","output":"CQRS/no-CQRS decision.","expected_behavior":"Separation is introduced only when it solves a documented architectural problem.","incorrect_code":"use_cqrs_for_every_application()","corrected_code":"use_cqrs_when_read_write_requirements_materially_diverge()","failure_mode":"Unnecessary architectural complexity.","expected_error":"","cause":"Pattern adoption occurred without a requirement difference.","root_cause":"CQRS was treated as an architecture goal rather than a response to constraints.","solution":"Compare unified and separated models against measurable requirements.","edge_case":"A system can use CQRS internally without distributed deployment or event sourcing.","security_implication":"Separate query models require explicit authorization and data-filtering rules.","performance_implication":"Independent scaling may improve performance but synchronization adds cost.","test_case":"Compare architecture candidates against the actual read/write requirement matrix.","test_input":"Command/query workload and consistency requirements.","expected_test_result":"CQRS is selected only if its additional complexity produces required value.","testing_guidance":"Include operational and data-reconciliation costs in evaluation.","verification_method":"Architecture trade-off analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every CQRS boundary maps to a distinct requirement.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Requirement matrix","Architecture alternatives"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_TRADE_OFF","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B017-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B017-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["CQRS architecture decision principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B017-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Read/Write Workload Architecture","concept":"Hot-read and hot-write isolation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when a small subset of records receives disproportionate traffic?","answer":"The architecture may need targeted partitioning, caching, request shaping, replication, or workload isolation rather than uniformly scaling the entire system.","explanation":"Average workload metrics can hide hotspots. Architecture must consider distribution skew because a small number of keys can become bottlenecks even when aggregate capacity appears sufficient.","principle":"Capacity architecture must account for workload distribution, not only aggregate volume.","objective":"Identify and isolate workload hotspots.","requirements":["Hot keys must not violate latency or availability requirements."],"constraints":["Hotspot mitigation can change consistency and routing semantics."],"preconditions":["Traffic distribution is measurable."],"input":"Per-key or per-tenant workload distribution.","output":"Hotspot mitigation architecture.","expected_behavior":"Skewed traffic remains within resource and latency limits.","incorrect_code":"scale_uniformly_without_hotspot_analysis()","corrected_code":"detect_hot_keys(); isolate_or_cache_hot_workloads()","failure_mode":"Hotspot-induced bottleneck.","expected_error":"","cause":"Aggregate capacity hides concentrated load.","root_cause":"Architecture lacks workload-distribution analysis.","solution":"Measure and mitigate hot keys or tenants explicitly.","edge_case":"A single authoritative hot key may have limited legal partitionability.","security_implication":"Tenant-specific hotspots can become denial-of-service vectors.","performance_implication":"Targeted mitigation can improve tail latency without scaling the whole system.","test_case":"Generate highly skewed traffic toward a small key set.","test_input":"90% of requests directed at 1% of keys.","expected_test_result":"Hotspot protection prevents disproportionate resource exhaustion.","testing_guidance":"Test both uniform and highly skewed workloads.","verification_method":"Distribution-aware load testing.","verification_guidance":"Inspect per-key/per-tenant metrics rather than only aggregate throughput.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Load generator","Per-key metrics"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"WORKLOAD_DISTRIBUTION_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B017-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B017-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Hotspot and skew-aware capacity architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B018","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Partitioning Architecture","concept":"Partitioning requirement derivation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does data volume or workload require partitioning?","answer":"Partitioning becomes justified when a single storage or processing domain cannot meet capacity, isolation, locality, or operational requirements and the workload can be divided along a meaningful key.","explanation":"Partitioning introduces routing, rebalancing, cross-partition queries, and failure complexity. The architecture must establish the requirement that makes those costs necessary.","principle":"Partitioning should solve a demonstrated capacity, isolation, locality, or operational constraint.","objective":"Determine whether partitioning is required and identify a suitable partition key.","requirements":["Partitioning must address a measurable constraint.","Partition key must support required access patterns."],"constraints":["Cross-partition operations become more expensive or complex."],"preconditions":["Data distribution and access patterns are known."],"input":"Data size, access patterns, tenant distribution, locality requirements.","output":"Partitioning decision and key.","expected_behavior":"Partitioning distributes workload without violating required correctness and query semantics.","incorrect_code":"partition_randomly()","corrected_code":"partition_by("tenant_id")","failure_mode":"Uneven or unusable partition distribution.","expected_error":"","cause":"Partition key was chosen without workload analysis.","root_cause":"Partitioning architecture ignored access and distribution requirements.","solution":"Choose a key aligned with dominant access and isolation boundaries.","edge_case":"A semantically good key may still create hotspots if distribution is highly skewed.","security_implication":"Partition boundaries can provide isolation but must not be treated as the sole authorization control.","performance_implication":"Good partitioning reduces contention; poor partitioning creates hotspots and cross-partition work.","test_case":"Load test partitions using realistic tenant and key distributions.","test_input":"Representative skewed and uniform datasets.","expected_test_result":"Partition utilization and query behavior meet requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test skew, growth, rebalance, and cross-partition operations.","verification_method":"Partition-distribution and workload testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both data placement and query routing.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Partition-capable storage","Workload model"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PARTITIONING_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B019"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B018-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Data partitioning and workload distribution architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B018-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Partitioning Architecture","concept":"Partition-key selection","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What properties make a partition key suitable for a scalable architecture?","answer":"A useful partition key distributes load adequately, aligns with dominant access patterns, remains sufficiently stable, and supports required isolation and routing semantics.","explanation":"The best partition key is not simply the most unique field. It must minimize hotspots and cross-partition operations while preserving the system's required query patterns.","principle":"Partition-key quality is determined by workload distribution and access locality.","objective":"Evaluate candidate partition keys.","requirements":["The key must distribute workload adequately.","Required queries should usually identify the relevant partition."],"constraints":["Highly correlated keys can create hotspots.","Changing partition keys can be operationally expensive."],"preconditions":["Candidate data fields and query patterns are known."],"input":"Candidate keys, cardinality, workload distribution, queries.","output":"Selected partition key and rationale.","expected_behavior":"The selected key minimizes unnecessary cross-partition work and severe skew.","incorrect_code":"partition_by("timestamp")","corrected_code":"partition_by("tenant_id") # when tenant locality and distribution match requirements","failure_mode":"Hot partition or excessive cross-partition queries.","expected_error":"","cause":"Partition key chosen from data shape without access-pattern analysis.","root_cause":"Routing requirements were not included in key selection.","solution":"Evaluate cardinality, skew, query locality, stability, and isolation.","edge_case":"Time-based partitioning can be appropriate for archival or retention workloads but may create hot current partitions.","security_implication":"Partition key should not be assumed to enforce authorization.","performance_implication":"Good locality reduces cross-partition operations.","test_case":"Replay representative queries against candidate partition keys.","test_input":"Query workload and distribution model.","expected_test_result":"Candidate evaluation identifies a key meeting routing and distribution requirements.","testing_guidance":"Use production-like distribution rather than uniform synthetic data alone.","verification_method":"Workload simulation and partition-skew analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify both read routing and write distribution.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Workload simulator","Partition-capable datastore"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PARTITION_KEY_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B018-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B018-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Partition-key selection and workload-locality principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B018-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Partitioning Architecture","concept":"Cross-partition query cost","knowledge_type":"TRADE_OFF","question":"Why must cross-partition queries be included when evaluating a partitioning architecture?","answer":"A partitioning scheme that makes writes scalable but causes frequent queries to touch many partitions can move the bottleneck from storage writes to query coordination and aggregation.","explanation":"Architecture evaluation must include the dominant read and write patterns. Partitioning is not successful if it merely relocates the scalability problem.","principle":"Partitioning quality depends on end-to-end workload cost, not isolated write scalability.","objective":"Evaluate cross-partition query consequences.","requirements":["Critical query paths must meet latency and resource requirements."],"constraints":["Cross-partition aggregation requires coordination and may increase failure surface."],"preconditions":["Data is partitioned across multiple domains."],"input":"Query patterns and partition layout.","output":"Cross-partition cost assessment.","expected_behavior":"Critical queries avoid unnecessary fan-out or have explicit bounded fan-out semantics.","incorrect_code":"query_all_partitions_for_every_request()","corrected_code":"route_to_required_partition_before_query()","failure_mode":"Fan-out query bottleneck.","expected_error":"","cause":"Partition key does not align with dominant query predicates.","root_cause":"Read access patterns were omitted from partition-key design.","solution":"Align partitioning with query locality or introduce an appropriate derived index/read model.","edge_case":"Administrative queries may legitimately require controlled fan-out.","security_implication":"Fan-out queries must preserve tenant and authorization filtering across every partition.","performance_implication":"Fan-out cost can grow with partition count.","test_case":"Measure critical queries as partition count increases.","test_input":"Same query against 1, 10, and 100 partitions.","expected_test_result":"Critical query latency remains within defined limits or has a documented alternative architecture.","testing_guidance":"Measure p95/p99 latency and resource usage during fan-out.","verification_method":"Partition-count scalability testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify query semantics across every partition.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Partitioned datastore","Load-testing environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PARTITION_QUERY_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B018-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B018-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Partitioned-query architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B018-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Partitioning Architecture","concept":"Rebalancing requirement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why must partition growth and rebalancing be considered before adopting partitioning?","answer":"As data or traffic grows, partitions may become uneven or insufficient. The architecture must define how capacity is added, data is moved, routing is updated, and service continuity is preserved.","explanation":"A partitioned system without a rebalancing strategy can become operationally trapped by its initial partition layout.","principle":"Scalable partitioning requires a lifecycle strategy, not only an initial partition map.","objective":"Design for partition growth and rebalancing.","requirements":["Capacity can increase without unacceptable service interruption.","Routing remains correct during movement."],"constraints":["Rebalancing consumes storage, network, and compute resources."],"preconditions":["Data or workload is expected to grow."],"input":"Growth projections and partition mechanism.","output":"Rebalancing architecture.","expected_behavior":"Partitions can be expanded, split, merged, or migrated according to the defined strategy.","incorrect_code":"fixed_partitions_forever()","corrected_code":"support_partition_migration_and_routing_versioning()","failure_mode":"Capacity exhaustion or migration outage.","expected_error":"","cause":"Initial partition layout has no growth path.","root_cause":"Lifecycle requirements were omitted from partition architecture.","solution":"Define partition expansion and routing migration before production adoption.","edge_case":"Some systems use fixed logical partitions with scalable physical placement underneath.","security_implication":"Data movement must preserve access controls and encryption requirements.","performance_implication":"Rebalancing can temporarily increase resource consumption.","test_case":"Increase data volume beyond the initial capacity plan and perform a controlled rebalance.","test_input":"Partition reaches defined utilization threshold.","expected_test_result":"Rebalancing completes without violating correctness or availability requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test migration interruption and resume behavior.","verification_method":"Capacity-growth and migration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify routing before, during, and after migration.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Partition-capable storage","Migration tooling"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PARTITION_LIFECYCLE_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B018-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B018-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Partition lifecycle and rebalancing architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B018-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Partitioning Architecture","concept":"Tenant partitioning trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADE_OFF","question":"What trade-offs arise when tenants are used as the primary partition boundary?","answer":"Tenant partitioning can provide strong locality and operational isolation, but tenant size skew can create hot partitions and migration complexity.","explanation":"Tenant identity is often an attractive key because it aligns with ownership and access boundaries. It must still be evaluated against distribution, large-tenant behavior, and growth requirements.","principle":"Security or ownership boundaries can inform partitioning but do not automatically guarantee scalable distribution.","objective":"Evaluate tenant-based partitioning.","requirements":["Tenant isolation requirements must be preserved.","Large and small tenants must remain operationally manageable."],"constraints":["Tenant sizes can vary by orders of magnitude.","Moving a tenant may be operationally expensive."],"preconditions":["The system is multi-tenant and tenant identity is stable."],"input":"Tenant distribution and isolation requirements.","output":"Tenant partitioning assessment.","expected_behavior":"Partitioning provides useful locality without creating unacceptable tenant hotspots.","incorrect_code":"partition_by_tenant_without_size_analysis()","corrected_code":"partition_by_tenant_with_hot_tenant_mitigation()","failure_mode":"Large tenant dominates a partition.","expected_error":"","cause":"Tenant size skew was not considered.","root_cause":"Isolation boundary was assumed to equal workload-distribution boundary.","solution":"Model tenant distribution and define a strategy for oversized tenants.","edge_case":"A very large tenant may require sub-partitioning while preserving tenant-level isolation.","security_implication":"Tenant boundaries can reduce accidental cross-tenant access but must not replace application authorization.","performance_implication":"Tenant locality can improve cache and query locality.","test_case":"Simulate highly uneven tenant sizes.","test_input":"One large tenant plus many small tenants.","expected_test_result":"No tenant creates unacceptable partition overload.","testing_guidance":"Include worst-case tenant distribution in capacity tests.","verification_method":"Tenant-distribution simulation and isolation testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both performance and tenant data isolation.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Multi-tenant workload model"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"MULTI_TENANT_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B018-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B018-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Multi-tenant partitioning and workload-isolation principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B019","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Observability requirement derivation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does observability become an architectural requirement rather than an operational convenience?","answer":"Observability becomes architectural when reliability, debugging, compliance, performance, or automated decision requirements depend on being able to reconstruct system behavior and outcomes.","explanation":"Logs, metrics, traces, audit records, and correlation identifiers are different evidence mechanisms. Architecture must define what evidence is required to diagnose failures and verify outcomes.","principle":"A system cannot reliably verify behavior that it cannot observe at the required boundary.","objective":"Derive observability architecture from operational and verification requirements.","requirements":["Critical operations must produce sufficient evidence for diagnosis and verification.","Telemetry must be correlated across relevant boundaries."],"constraints":["Telemetry adds storage, CPU, network, and privacy costs."],"preconditions":["Operational and verification requirements are known."],"input":"Failure scenarios, SLOs, audit requirements, and debugging needs.","output":"Observability architecture.","expected_behavior":"Critical workflows can be traced from input through outcome at the required evidence level.","incorrect_code":"log_everything_without_schema()","corrected_code":"emit_structured_events_with_correlation_id_and_semantic_fields()","failure_mode":"Insufficient evidence for diagnosing production failures.","expected_error":"","cause":"Telemetry was designed after implementation without identifying diagnostic requirements.","root_cause":"Observability was treated as an afterthought rather than an architectural capability.","solution":"Define evidence requirements and telemetry contracts during architecture design.","edge_case":"High-volume low-value telemetry may need sampling while security/audit evidence may require complete retention.","security_implication":"Telemetry can contain sensitive information and must be classified and protected.","performance_implication":"Excessive instrumentation can increase overhead; selective structured telemetry improves value density.","test_case":"Inject a representative failure and attempt to reconstruct the causal path.","test_input":"Controlled failure in a multi-component workflow.","expected_test_result":"Required diagnostic evidence is available and correlated.","testing_guidance":"Perform observability validation as part of failure testing.","verification_method":"Failure reconstruction exercise.","verification_guidance":"Verify that telemetry supports diagnosis rather than merely proving that logs exist.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Telemetry infrastructure","Failure test scenario"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OBSERVABILITY_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B020"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B019-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Observability and diagnosability architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B019-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Correlation identity","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_REQUIREMENT","question":"Why should distributed workflows have explicit correlation identifiers?","answer":"Correlation identifiers allow related operations, messages, logs, traces, and audit records to be connected across component boundaries.","explanation":"Without correlation identity, distributed failures often produce independent fragments of evidence that cannot be reliably associated. The identifier should have defined propagation and trust semantics.","principle":"Cross-boundary diagnostic evidence requires a stable correlation mechanism.","objective":"Define correlation requirements for distributed workflows.","requirements":["Critical cross-component operations must be correlatable.","Correlation data must be propagated consistently."],"constraints":["Correlation identifiers must not be treated as authentication credentials."],"preconditions":["A workflow crosses process or service boundaries."],"input":"Distributed workflow topology.","output":"Correlation propagation contract.","expected_behavior":"Related events can be associated without relying on timestamp coincidence.","incorrect_code":"correlate_by_timestamp_only()","corrected_code":"propagate("correlation_id")","failure_mode":"Fragmented diagnostic evidence.","expected_error":"","cause":"No stable correlation identifier crosses component boundaries.","root_cause":"Observability architecture lacks explicit correlation semantics.","solution":"Generate and propagate a correlation identifier through the workflow.","edge_case":"Nested operations may require separate span/request identifiers while preserving the parent correlation.","security_implication":"Correlation identifiers should not contain secrets or sensitive business information.","performance_implication":"Identifier propagation has negligible cost compared with distributed diagnostic value but still increases telemetry payload size.","test_case":"Send one workflow through multiple components and verify correlation continuity.","test_input":"Multi-component request with generated correlation ID.","expected_test_result":"All required telemetry records reference the same correlation context.","testing_guidance":"Test propagation through synchronous and asynchronous boundaries.","verification_method":"Telemetry correlation test.","verification_guidance":"Verify no unrelated operations are accidentally correlated.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Distributed test environment","Telemetry collection"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OBSERVABILITY_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B019-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B019-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed correlation and observability architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B019-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Audit evidence versus diagnostic telemetry","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DISTINCTION","question":"Why should audit records be architecturally distinguished from ordinary diagnostic logs?","answer":"Audit records represent security, compliance, or accountability evidence with stronger integrity, retention, access, and completeness requirements than ordinary operational logs.","explanation":"Diagnostic logs can often be sampled, rotated, or discarded. Audit evidence may require controlled retention and tamper resistance. Treating both as the same stream can weaken either operational efficiency or accountability requirements.","principle":"Evidence requirements determine telemetry retention and integrity semantics.","objective":"Separate audit evidence from diagnostic telemetry when requirements differ.","requirements":["Audit events must meet defined integrity and retention requirements."],"constraints":["Audit storage and controls can be more expensive than ordinary logs."],"preconditions":["The system has accountability or compliance requirements."],"input":"Audit and operational evidence requirements.","output":"Separated or differentiated telemetry architecture.","expected_behavior":"Audit evidence remains available and protected according to its policy.","incorrect_code":"audit_event = ordinary_log(message)","corrected_code":"audit_event = immutable_audit_record(actor,action,target,outcome)","failure_mode":"Audit evidence incomplete or mutable.","expected_error":"","cause":"Operational logs were assumed sufficient for accountability.","root_cause":"Evidence classes were not distinguished architecturally.","solution":"Define separate audit semantics and controls.","edge_case":"Some systems can use one physical pipeline with different logical retention and access policies.","security_implication":"Audit records can themselves contain sensitive information and require strict access control.","performance_implication":"Strong audit guarantees can add write and storage overhead.","test_case":"Perform a sensitive action and verify required audit evidence.","test_input":"Authorized and unauthorized actions.","expected_test_result":"Required audit records exist with correct actor, action, target, outcome, and timestamp information.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, duplicate, reordered, and unauthorized audit-event scenarios.","verification_method":"Audit completeness and integrity testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify audit requirements independently from ordinary logging requirements.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Audit storage","Security policy"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"AUDIT_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B019-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B019-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Audit logging and accountability architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B019-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Telemetry cardinality control","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should telemetry cardinality be treated as an architectural constraint?","answer":"Unbounded metric or label cardinality can create excessive memory, storage, indexing, and query costs. Architecture must identify which dimensions are safe to aggregate and which belong in logs or traces instead.","explanation":"High-cardinality identifiers are often useful for debugging but can be harmful as metric labels. Observability architecture should place each data dimension in the evidence mechanism appropriate to its cardinality and query needs.","principle":"Telemetry dimensions must be selected according to operational value and resource cost.","objective":"Prevent observability infrastructure from becoming a scalability bottleneck.","requirements":["Telemetry volume and cardinality must remain within defined resource limits."],"constraints":["Debugging needs may require high-cardinality information in another telemetry channel."],"preconditions":["Metrics, logs, or traces contain dynamic dimensions."],"input":"Telemetry schema and expected cardinality.","output":"Cardinality-aware telemetry design.","expected_behavior":"Operational metrics remain bounded while detailed diagnostic context remains available through suitable channels.","incorrect_code":"metric.labels(user_id,request_id,raw_payload)","corrected_code":"metric.labels(region,operation,status); log_context(user_id,request_id)","failure_mode":"Telemetry explosion.","expected_error":"","cause":"High-cardinality fields were placed into aggregation-heavy telemetry.","root_cause":"Telemetry architecture ignored cardinality cost.","solution":"Move high-cardinality context to logs/traces and keep metrics dimensions bounded.","edge_case":"A bounded tenant dimension may still become high cardinality at large scale.","security_implication":"Telemetry identifiers may expose sensitive information if propagated indiscriminately.","performance_implication":"Lower cardinality reduces telemetry storage and query overhead.","test_case":"Generate telemetry with representative cardinality and measure backend resource usage.","test_input":"Large number of unique request/user identifiers.","expected_test_result":"Metric cardinality remains within defined limits.","testing_guidance":"Test cardinality growth before production deployment.","verification_method":"Telemetry load and cardinality analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify both metric cardinality and diagnostic usefulness.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Telemetry backend","Load generator"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OBSERVABILITY_PERFORMANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B019-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B019-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Telemetry cardinality and observability scalability principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B019-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Outcome observability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should architecture capture outcomes rather than only execution events?","answer":"Execution evidence shows that an operation ran, while outcome evidence establishes whether the intended effect actually occurred. Systems requiring verification must represent both.","explanation":"A successful function return, HTTP 200, message acknowledgement, or process completion does not necessarily prove the intended business outcome. Architecture should define outcome evidence where correctness depends on it.","principle":"Execution success and business outcome are distinct architectural evidence types.","objective":"Make outcome verification possible from system evidence.","requirements":["Critical operations must expose evidence sufficient to determine their intended outcome."],"constraints":["Outcome verification may require additional reads, reconciliation, or external confirmation."],"preconditions":["Business operations have measurable intended outcomes."],"input":"Operation intent and expected outcome.","output":"Outcome-evidence architecture.","expected_behavior":"The system can distinguish attempted, executed, and successfully verified operations.","incorrect_code":"success = request_completed()","corrected_code":"execution = request_completed(); outcome = verify_expected_state()","failure_mode":"False success reporting.","expected_error":"","cause":"Execution completion was treated as proof of outcome.","root_cause":"Architecture lacked outcome verification semantics.","solution":"Define outcome evidence and verification boundaries for critical operations.","edge_case":"Some external outcomes cannot be immediately verified and require asynchronous reconciliation.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive changes require stronger evidence that the intended state was actually established.","performance_implication":"Verification can add reads or reconciliation work.","test_case":"Make execution return success while preventing the intended state change.","test_input":"Simulated partial failure after command acceptance.","expected_test_result":"Architecture records execution separately from verified outcome.","testing_guidance":"Test false-positive success scenarios.","verification_method":"Outcome verification scenario testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that reported success cannot be inferred solely from transport completion.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Outcome-verification mechanism"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B019-R01","D33-B016"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B019-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Outcome verification and evidence-driven architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B020","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture Validation","concept":"Architecture fitness functions","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_METHOD","question":"How can architecture requirements be converted into continuously testable constraints?","answer":"Architecture fitness functions express important architectural properties as measurable checks, allowing CI or validation systems to detect violations such as forbidden dependencies, latency thresholds, resource limits, or required isolation.","explanation":"A static architecture document can become outdated. Fitness functions turn selected architecture requirements into executable or mechanically checkable evidence.","principle":"Important architecture constraints should be continuously verifiable where practical.","objective":"Convert architecture requirements into repeatable validation checks.","requirements":["Critical architecture properties must have measurable validation criteria.","Checks must correspond to actual requirements."],"constraints":["Not every architectural property can be fully automated."],"preconditions":["Architecture requirements are explicit and measurable."],"input":"Architecture constraints and quality requirements.","output":"Automated or semi-automated architecture checks.","expected_behavior":"Architecture violations are detected before or during deployment according to the required control point.","incorrect_code":"assert_architecture_is_good()","corrected_code":"assert(no_forbidden_dependency_graph_edges); assert(latency_p99 <= limit)","failure_mode":"Architecture drift.","expected_error":"","cause":"Requirements exist only in documentation and are not continuously checked.","root_cause":"Architecture verification was separated from implementation lifecycle.","solution":"Convert high-value architecture constraints into fitness functions.","edge_case":"A fitness function can itself become obsolete and therefore requires versioned ownership.","security_implication":"Security boundaries and dependency rules are strong candidates for automated architecture checks.","performance_implication":"Automated checks reduce regression detection cost but add CI execution cost.","test_case":"Introduce an intentional architecture violation and run the validation suite.","test_input":"Forbidden dependency or threshold violation.","expected_test_result":"The validation system detects the violation.","testing_guidance":"Test both positive and negative architecture cases.","verification_method":"Architecture fitness-function execution.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each check fails when the corresponding requirement is violated.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":["Architecture validation tooling where available"],"execution_requirements":["CI or validation environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_VERIFICATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B020-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B020-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Architecture fitness-function and continuous architecture verification principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B020-R02","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture Validation","concept":"Architecture threat modeling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should security requirements influence architecture evaluation before implementation?","answer":"Security requirements should be translated into trust boundaries, assets, threats, attack paths, controls, and verification criteria and then evaluated against each candidate architecture.","explanation":"A candidate architecture can satisfy functional requirements while creating unacceptable trust or attack-surface properties. Security must therefore participate in architecture selection rather than being appended afterward.","principle":"Security constraints are architecture-selection inputs, not only implementation tasks.","objective":"Evaluate architecture candidates against explicit security requirements.","requirements":["Critical assets and trust boundaries must be identified.","Security controls must map to threats and requirements."],"constraints":["Additional controls can affect performance and complexity."],"preconditions":["Assets, actors, and security objectives are known."],"input":"Threat model and architecture candidates.","output":"Security-aware architecture evaluation.","expected_behavior":"Selected architecture satisfies mandatory security constraints or documents accepted residual risk.","incorrect_code":"security_after_architecture()","corrected_code":"evaluate_security_constraints_before_selection()","failure_mode":"Architecture creates avoidable security exposure.","expected_error":"","cause":"Security was evaluated only after architectural boundaries were fixed.","root_cause":"Security requirements were excluded from candidate evaluation.","solution":"Include trust boundaries and threat analysis in architecture selection.","edge_case":"Some implementation-level vulnerabilities cannot be known during architecture selection and require later validation.","security_implication":"Security requirements directly define allowed boundaries and controls.","performance_implication":"Encryption, isolation, and validation can add measurable overhead.","test_case":"Perform threat modeling for each candidate architecture.","test_input":"Asset and trust-boundary model.","expected_test_result":"Security-critical differences between candidates are explicitly identified.","testing_guidance":"Trace threats to architectural controls and later verification tests.","verification_method":"Threat-model review plus security architecture tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify every high-impact threat has an architectural control or documented residual risk.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Threat model","Architecture candidates"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B020-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B020-T02"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Threat modeling and secure architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B020-R03","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture Validation","concept":"Architecture performance budget","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should performance requirements be represented during architecture selection?","answer":"Performance requirements should be expressed as measurable budgets for latency, throughput, resource use, payload size, or concurrency and evaluated against candidate architectures.","explanation":"Architecture choices influence performance before code exists. A budget provides a constraint against which topology, communication, storage, serialization, and coordination costs can be reasoned about and later measured.","principle":"Performance must be an explicit architecture constraint rather than an informal expectation.","objective":"Use measurable performance budgets during architecture evaluation.","requirements":["Critical performance targets must be measurable.","Candidate architectures must be evaluated against the same workload assumptions."],"constraints":["Early estimates are uncertain and must not be presented as measurements."],"preconditions":["Representative workload and performance objectives are defined."],"input":"Latency, throughput, concurrency, payload, and resource targets.","output":"Performance budget and architecture assessment.","expected_behavior":"Architecture candidates are rejected or refined when they cannot plausibly satisfy mandatory budgets.","incorrect_code":"performance = "fast"","corrected_code":"performance_budget = {"p99_latency_ms":200,"throughput_rps":1000}","failure_mode":"Architecture misses performance requirement after implementation.","expected_error":"","cause":"Performance was treated as qualitative rather than constrained.","root_cause":"No architecture-level performance budget existed.","solution":"Define measurable budgets and validate assumptions with prototypes or tests.","edge_case":"Early architecture estimates may remain uncertain and should be labeled estimated rather than measured.","security_implication":"Performance pressure must not justify removing mandatory security controls without explicit risk evaluation.","performance_implication":"Budgets provide the basis for capacity and optimization decisions.","test_case":"Prototype critical architecture paths under representative workload.","test_input":"Representative request mix and target concurrency.","expected_test_result":"Measured behavior can be compared with the architecture budget.","testing_guidance":"Record measured versus estimated values separately.","verification_method":"Performance testing and budget comparison.","verification_guidance":"Do not convert estimates into verified benchmark claims.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":["Performance test environment"],"execution_requirements":["Representative workload","Measurement tooling"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_REQUIREMENT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B017-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B020-T03"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Performance-budget and architecture capacity-planning principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B020-R04","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture Validation","concept":"Architecture prototype for uncertainty reduction","knowledge_type":"VALIDATION_METHOD","question":"When should an architecture use a prototype before final selection?","answer":"A prototype is justified when a critical architecture assumption has high uncertainty and cannot be adequately resolved through documentation or static reasoning alone.","explanation":"The prototype should target the uncertain property rather than become an uncontrolled implementation project. Its purpose is evidence generation for architecture selection.","principle":"Prototype only the uncertainty that materially affects the architecture decision.","objective":"Reduce high-impact architecture uncertainty with focused evidence.","requirements":["Prototype scope must map to a specific architecture uncertainty.","Results must be recorded with assumptions and limitations."],"constraints":["Prototype measurements may not represent production behavior.","Prototype work consumes engineering resources."],"preconditions":["A material unresolved architecture assumption exists."],"input":"Architecture uncertainty and candidate designs.","output":"Evidence that updates architecture evaluation.","expected_behavior":"Prototype results reduce uncertainty without being mistaken for full production validation.","incorrect_code":"prototype_everything_before_decision()","corrected_code":"prototype_only(high_risk_uncertain_assumption)","failure_mode":"Architecture decision based on untested critical assumption.","expected_error":"","cause":"Evidence was insufficient for a high-impact unknown.","root_cause":"Architecture uncertainty was not explicitly identified.","solution":"Create a narrow prototype targeted at the uncertain property.","edge_case":"A prototype may disprove feasibility without proving production readiness.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive prototypes must use safe data and environments.","performance_implication":"Prototype results can inform performance feasibility but are not automatically production benchmarks.","test_case":"Define a hypothesis, run a focused prototype, and compare results with the architecture assumption.","test_input":"Critical uncertain architecture property.","expected_test_result":"Prototype evidence changes or confirms the decision with documented confidence and limitations.","testing_guidance":"Record environment, workload, configuration, and measurement method.","verification_method":"Hypothesis-driven architecture prototyping.","verification_guidance":"Separate prototype evidence from production verification.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Prototype environment","Defined hypothesis"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_VALIDATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B020-R03"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B020-T04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Architecture prototyping and uncertainty-reduction principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B020-R05","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Requirement → Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture Validation","concept":"Architecture decision completeness","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_VERIFICATION","question":"How can an architecture decision be verified as complete before implementation proceeds?","answer":"A decision is complete when requirements are traced to constraints, candidate architectures, evaluation criteria, trade-offs, selected architecture, rationale, rejected alternatives, risks, verification methods, and unresolved assumptions.","explanation":"Architecture completeness is not the presence of a diagram. The decision must contain enough evidence for another engineer or automated reasoning system to reconstruct why the architecture was selected and what conditions could invalidate it.","principle":"A high-quality architecture decision is a traceable reasoning artifact, not merely a component diagram.","objective":"Create machine-verifiable architecture decision records.","requirements":["Mandatory requirements must have traceable architectural responses.","Rejected alternatives and unresolved assumptions must be explicit."],"constraints":["Some future conditions cannot be known and must remain explicitly uncertain."],"preconditions":["Candidate architectures and requirements have been evaluated."],"input":"Requirement set and architecture decision evidence.","output":"Complete architecture decision record.","expected_behavior":"An independent reviewer can reconstruct the decision path from requirement to selected architecture.","incorrect_code":"record = {"architecture":"selected"}","corrected_code":"record = {"requirements":requirements,"constraints":constraints,"candidates":candidates,"evaluation":evaluation,"tradeoffs":tradeoffs,"selected":selected,"rationale":rationale,"rejected":rejected,"verification":verification}","failure_mode":"Architecture cannot be independently reviewed or reproduced.","expected_error":"","cause":"Decision rationale and alternatives were not recorded.","root_cause":"Architecture documentation captured the result but not the reasoning chain.","solution":"Persist a structured requirement-to-decision trace with evidence and uncertainty.","edge_case":"An architecture may be intentionally provisional; provisional status and revisit conditions must be explicit.","security_implication":"Security requirements and residual risks must be represented in the decision record.","performance_implication":"Performance assumptions and budgets must be traceable to validation evidence.","test_case":"Give the architecture record to an independent reviewer and ask them to reconstruct the decision.","test_input":"Structured architecture decision record.","expected_test_result":"Reviewer can identify requirements, alternatives, trade-offs, selection rationale, assumptions, and verification conditions.","testing_guidance":"Use structured review checklists and automated completeness checks.","verification_method":"Architecture decision completeness audit.","verification_guidance":"Mark unknown or unverified assumptions explicitly rather than silently treating them as facts.","language":"Agnostic","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Architecture-level","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Architecture decision record schema","Review process"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B020-R01","D33-B020-R02","D33-B020-R03","D33-B020-R04"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D33-B020-T05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Requirement-to-architecture traceability and architecture decision governance principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B021","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"State management","concept":"Choosing explicit state ownership for distributed business workflows","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture be selected when a workflow spans multiple components and each step depends on durable business state?","answer":"Model workflow state explicitly and assign one authoritative owner for each state transition. Avoid allowing multiple components to mutate the same logical state independently.","explanation":"Distributed workflows become difficult to reason about when state is implicit in messages, caches, local variables, or duplicated databases. The architecture should define the authoritative state store, legal transitions, ownership boundaries, idempotency behavior, and recovery semantics before selecting transport or deployment topology.","principle":"Every important business state should have an explicit owner, defined transition rules, and recoverable persistence semantics.","objective":"Select an architecture that makes distributed state ownership deterministic, auditable, and recoverable.","requirements":["Durable workflow state","Explicit state transitions","Recoverability after process failure","Idempotent transition handling","Traceability of state changes"],"constraints":["Multiple components may participate in one workflow","Processes may restart independently","Messages may be duplicated or delayed"],"preconditions":["Business workflow states are identified","State transitions can be enumerated","Failure scenarios are considered"],"input":"Workflow requirements containing states, transitions, participating components, durability requirements, and failure conditions.","output":"Architecture decision specifying authoritative state ownership, transition mechanism, persistence boundary, and recovery strategy.","expected_behavior":"A workflow can resume after component failure without reconstructing authoritative state from unreliable transient data.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"Multiple components independently mutate shared logical state.","expected_error":"STATE_OWNERSHIP_AMBIGUOUS","cause":"No explicit authority exists for state transitions.","root_cause":"Architecture treats distributed state as an implementation detail rather than a first-class design concern.","solution":"Define an authoritative state owner and require all state-changing operations to pass through explicit transition rules.","edge_case":"A delayed message attempts to apply an older transition after the workflow has already advanced.","security_implication":"Unauthorized or stale state transitions can cause privilege or workflow-integrity violations; transition authorization must be evaluated against current state.","performance_implication":"Centralized state ownership can increase contention; partitioning, optimistic concurrency, or appropriate persistence design may be required.","test_case":"Restart the workflow processor between two valid transitions and verify that the next transition uses persisted authoritative state.","test_input":"Workflow state S2 persisted before processor termination; delayed transition referencing S1.","expected_test_result":"The stale transition is rejected or safely handled according to the defined transition policy.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate, delayed, reordered, concurrent, and replayed transitions.","verification_method":"Trace each state transition to the authoritative state store and verify that no independent component can silently create authoritative state.","verification_guidance":"Review state ownership, transition invariants, recovery behavior, and concurrency controls.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Durable persistence","Concurrency control mechanism where required"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B009","D33-B030"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Explicit workflow state model"],"extends":"D33-B004","refines":"D33-B009","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Implicit state distributed across services","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Explicit state ownership architecture","tests":"Workflow recovery and transition tests","verifies":"State transition integrity","derived_from":"Established distributed-systems state-management principles","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture description concepts","Established distributed-systems architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B022","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Introducing caching only when consistency and invalidation requirements are explicit","knowledge_type":"TRADE_OFF","question":"How should architecture handle a requirement for lower read latency without making cached data the accidental source of truth?","answer":"Treat caching as a derived performance mechanism with explicit ownership, freshness rules, invalidation behavior, and fallback behavior. The architecture should preserve correctness when the cache is empty, stale, unavailable, or corrupted.","explanation":"Caching can reduce latency and backend load, but it introduces freshness, invalidation, memory, stampede, and failure concerns. Architecture selection must first establish whether stale data is acceptable and which data can safely be cached.","principle":"A cache should improve performance without becoming an undocumented authority for business correctness.","objective":"Determine whether and where caching belongs in the architecture while preserving correctness.","requirements":["Defined freshness tolerance","Cache failure tolerance","Explicit invalidation or expiration policy","Source-of-truth definition","Load protection where necessary"],"constraints":["Some data may require strong freshness","Cache infrastructure can fail","Invalidation may be distributed"],"preconditions":["Read workload is understood","Data freshness requirements are classified","Source of truth is identified"],"input":"Latency and throughput requirements plus data freshness and consistency constraints.","output":"Caching architecture with source-of-truth, cacheability, freshness, invalidation, and fallback rules.","expected_behavior":"System remains correct when cache entries are missing or unavailable and meets defined latency objectives when caching is available.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"Application treats cached data as authoritative without an explicit consistency contract.","expected_error":"CACHE_SOURCE_OF_TRUTH_UNDEFINED","cause":"Cache was introduced as a performance optimization without architectural ownership rules.","root_cause":"Performance requirement was translated directly into caching rather than evaluated against consistency requirements.","solution":"Define source of truth, acceptable staleness, cache invalidation, fallback, and failure behavior before implementation.","edge_case":"Cache contains an old authorization or pricing decision after the authoritative record changes.","security_implication":"Caching sensitive authorization or tenant data requires strict isolation, key design, expiry, and invalidation controls.","performance_implication":"Poor cache keys, low hit rates, stampedes, or excessive invalidation can increase rather than reduce load.","test_case":"Disable cache access while executing representative reads and verify correctness remains intact.","test_input":"Cache unavailable; authoritative datastore available.","expected_test_result":"Requests remain correct using the defined fallback path.","testing_guidance":"Test cold cache, stale entries, concurrent misses, invalidation failure, cache outage, and tenant/key isolation.","verification_method":"Trace every cached datum to its authoritative source and verify defined freshness/fallback rules.","verification_guidance":"Validate correctness independently of cache availability.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Cache technology if selected","Authoritative datastore"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B002","D33-B008","D33-B023"],"depends_on":["D33-B002"],"requires":["Explicit consistency requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B008","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Cache-as-source-of-truth architecture","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Derived-cache architecture","tests":"Cache failure and consistency tests","verifies":"Cache correctness boundaries","derived_from":"Established caching and distributed-systems architecture practice","provenance":["Established distributed-systems caching practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B023","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Architecture for concurrent updates to shared business resources","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN","question":"How should architecture be selected when multiple actors may update the same logical resource concurrently?","answer":"Define an explicit concurrency-control strategy such as optimistic concurrency, serialized ownership, or transactional locking based on contention, correctness, and latency requirements.","explanation":"Concurrent updates require more than database transactions. The architecture must specify which version of a resource is authoritative, how conflicts are detected, how retries occur, and which operations are safe to repeat.","principle":"Concurrency conflicts must be detected and resolved by an explicit architectural policy rather than accidental last-write-wins behavior.","objective":"Prevent silent data loss and preserve business invariants under concurrent operations.","requirements":["Conflict detection","Invariant preservation","Deterministic retry behavior","Atomic update boundary"],"constraints":["Concurrent writers may operate independently","Retries may occur after ambiguous failures","Contention may vary by resource"],"preconditions":["Shared resource and invariants are identified","Concurrency characteristics are known"],"input":"Resource update requirements and concurrency profile.","output":"Concurrency architecture specifying ownership, versioning, locking, or conflict-resolution policy.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent operations either safely serialize, succeed against the correct version, or produce an explicit conflict outcome.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"Two valid reads are followed by conflicting writes that silently overwrite each other.","expected_error":"CONCURRENT_UPDATE_CONFLICT","cause":"No concurrency invariant was enforced between read and write.","root_cause":"Architecture assumed low concurrency without defining correctness behavior.","solution":"Introduce explicit version checks, transactional locking, serialized ownership, or another justified concurrency strategy.","edge_case":"Client retries the same operation after receiving an uncertain network response.","security_implication":"Concurrency flaws can bypass quotas, authorization-dependent state transitions, or financial/business invariants.","performance_implication":"Heavy locking can reduce throughput; optimistic approaches can increase retry load under contention.","test_case":"Execute concurrent updates against the same versioned resource.","test_input":"Two writers read version 7 and both attempt updates.","expected_test_result":"At most one update commits against version 7; the other receives a defined conflict or retry result.","testing_guidance":"Test high contention, retry storms, transaction timeout, and duplicate operations.","verification_method":"Inspect persistence-level invariants and confirm conflict behavior under concurrent execution.","verification_guidance":"Use deterministic concurrency tests where possible and verify no invariant is violated.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Transactional or version-control mechanism"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B021","D33-B024"],"depends_on":["D33-B021"],"requires":["Explicit business invariants"],"extends":"","refines":"","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Uncontrolled last-write-wins updates","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Explicit concurrency-control architecture","tests":"Concurrent update tests","verifies":"Business invariants under concurrency","derived_from":"Established concurrent-systems and transactional architecture principles","provenance":["Established database and distributed-systems concurrency practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B024","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Designing retry-safe command boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN","question":"When infrastructure or clients can retry requests, what architectural capability prevents duplicate side effects?","answer":"Define idempotent command semantics at the appropriate business boundary and persist enough information to recognize safely repeated operations.","explanation":"Retries are normal in distributed systems because clients, queues, and infrastructure can encounter timeouts or ambiguous outcomes. Architecture must distinguish safe repeated reads from side-effecting commands and define deduplication or idempotency semantics for the latter.","principle":"A retried command must not create an unintended additional business effect.","objective":"Make side-effecting operations safe under legitimate retry behavior.","requirements":["Stable operation identity","Duplicate detection","Defined replay semantics","Atomic relationship between deduplication state and business effect"],"constraints":["Requests may arrive more than once","Network failures can create ambiguous outcomes","Clients may retry independently"],"preconditions":["Command boundary is defined","Side effect is identifiable","Retry policy is understood"],"input":"Command contract and retry scenarios.","output":"Idempotent command architecture with operation identity and duplicate-handling behavior.","expected_behavior":"Repeated delivery of the same logical operation produces the defined single-effect or safely repeatable outcome.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"Retrying a timed-out payment/order/provisioning command creates a second side effect.","expected_error":"DUPLICATE_SIDE_EFFECT","cause":"Command execution has no durable idempotency boundary.","root_cause":"Retry was treated as a transport concern rather than an application architecture concern.","solution":"Introduce an idempotency key or equivalent stable operation identity and atomically associate it with the business effect.","edge_case":"The original operation succeeds but the response is lost before the client receives it.","security_implication":"Predictable or reusable idempotency identifiers must not allow unauthorized users to replay another principal's operation.","performance_implication":"Idempotency records consume storage and require retention/cleanup strategy.","test_case":"Submit the same logical command multiple times with identical operation identity.","test_input":"Three identical command deliveries using operation ID OP-42.","expected_test_result":"Only the defined single business effect occurs and subsequent deliveries return the established result/state.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate, delayed, concurrent, reordered, and post-timeout retries.","verification_method":"Trace operation identity to persisted business effect and verify atomicity.","verification_guidance":"Verify that deduplication cannot succeed while the business effect fails, or vice versa, without a defined recovery protocol.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Durable persistence"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B021","D33-B023","D33-B025"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Stable operation identity"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B021","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Non-idempotent retry architecture","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Retry-safe command boundary","tests":"Duplicate delivery tests","verifies":"Single-effect semantics","derived_from":"Established distributed-systems idempotency practice","provenance":["Established distributed-systems architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B025","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Integration contracts","concept":"Choosing explicit contracts for external and internal integrations","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle integrations when independently evolving systems must exchange structured data and behavior?","answer":"Define explicit versioned contracts and ownership boundaries rather than coupling consumers directly to undocumented internal representations.","explanation":"Integration failures often arise when one component depends on another component's database schema, internal object model, or undocumented behavior. Contract-based architecture makes compatibility assumptions explicit and testable.","principle":"An integration boundary should expose a stable contract, not another component's internal implementation.","objective":"Create evolvable integration boundaries with explicit compatibility expectations.","requirements":["Contract ownership","Versioning strategy","Compatibility policy","Validation at boundaries","Defined error semantics"],"constraints":["Producers and consumers may deploy independently","Contracts may evolve","Legacy consumers may persist"],"preconditions":["Integration participants are identified","Shared data semantics are defined"],"input":"Integration requirements, data structures, compatibility constraints, and deployment independence requirements.","output":"Contract architecture defining interfaces, schemas, versions, compatibility, and ownership.","expected_behavior":"Independent components can evolve without silently breaking existing consumers.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"Consumer reads producer's internal database table or undocumented response field.","expected_error":"INTEGRATION_CONTRACT_BREACH","cause":"No explicit stable integration boundary exists.","root_cause":"Architecture optimized for immediate convenience instead of independent evolution.","solution":"Introduce an explicit API/event/schema contract owned by the producing boundary.","edge_case":"Producer must rename or remove a field while old consumers still depend on it.","security_implication":"Boundary validation reduces exposure of unintended fields and helps enforce authorization and tenant constraints.","performance_implication":"Contract translation can add processing overhead; the trade-off should be measured rather than assumed.","test_case":"Run consumer compatibility tests against current and supported legacy contract versions.","test_input":"Producer version N; consumer requires contract N-1.","expected_test_result":"Compatibility behavior matches the declared versioning policy.","testing_guidance":"Test additive changes, breaking changes, malformed payloads, unknown fields, version negotiation, and backward compatibility.","verification_method":"Validate contract schemas independently and execute consumer-provider compatibility tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no consumer relies on undocumented implementation details.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Contract/schema validation mechanism"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B026","D33-B030"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Defined integration ownership"],"extends":"D33-B009","refines":"","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Shared-internal-schema coupling","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Explicit integration contract architecture","tests":"Contract compatibility tests","verifies":"Interface compatibility","derived_from":"Established API and integration architecture practice","provenance":["Established software architecture and interface-contract practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B026","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Backward compatibility","concept":"Architecture for gradual contract and component migration","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture support migration from an existing interface to a new architecture without requiring an atomic cutover?","answer":"Use an explicitly planned migration boundary such as versioned interfaces, adapters, dual-read/dual-write where justified, or staged traffic migration, with clear ownership and exit criteria.","explanation":"Large architecture changes are safer when the migration path is part of the architecture rather than an afterthought. Transitional mechanisms should have defined lifetimes and removal criteria to prevent permanent compatibility complexity.","principle":"A migration architecture must define both the transition state and the intended final state.","objective":"Enable incremental migration while preserving correctness and operational control.","requirements":["Compatibility during transition","Observable migration state","Rollback strategy","Defined completion criteria"],"constraints":["Legacy consumers cannot migrate simultaneously","Data may require transformation","Two implementations may coexist temporarily"],"preconditions":["Current and target architectures are understood","Migration dependencies are mapped"],"input":"Legacy architecture, target architecture, compatibility constraints, migration risks.","output":"Staged migration architecture with transition mechanisms and exit criteria.","expected_behavior":"Traffic and/or data can move incrementally while correctness and rollback remain observable.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"Temporary adapter or dual-write path becomes permanent and diverges from target architecture.","expected_error":"MIGRATION_STATE_UNBOUNDED","cause":"No explicit exit criteria or migration observability.","root_cause":"Architecture treated migration as deployment work rather than a first-class architectural state.","solution":"Document transitional components, ownership, metrics, rollback conditions, and removal milestones.","edge_case":"Legacy and new systems disagree about a record during a dual-write period.","security_implication":"Migration paths must preserve authorization, tenant isolation, auditability, and data classification controls.","performance_implication":"Temporary duplication may increase storage, latency, and operational cost.","test_case":"Migrate a controlled subset of traffic and compare old/new outcomes before expanding scope.","test_input":"10% controlled traffic routed to target architecture.","expected_test_result":"Defined correctness and operational thresholds are satisfied before progression.","testing_guidance":"Test rollback, divergence, partial migration, data transformation, and compatibility failure.","verification_method":"Compare source and target outcomes using defined invariants and migration metrics.","verification_guidance":"Do not declare migration complete until exit criteria and removal of transitional mechanisms are verified.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Observability","Compatibility mechanism"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B025","D33-B027"],"depends_on":["D33-B025"],"requires":["Defined target architecture"],"extends":"","refines":"","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Big-bang migration","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Staged migration architecture","tests":"Migration validation tests","verifies":"Migration correctness","derived_from":"Established incremental migration and strangler-style architecture practices","provenance":["Established software migration architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B027","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment topology","concept":"Separating logical architecture from deployment architecture","knowledge_type":"TRADE_OFF","question":"When should logical component boundaries be separated from physical deployment boundaries?","answer":"Keep logical boundaries independent from deployment topology unless independent scaling, fault isolation, security, lifecycle, or organizational requirements justify separate deployment units.","explanation":"A system can have strong modular boundaries while remaining one deployable unit. Physical separation introduces network latency, operational complexity, deployment coordination, observability requirements, and failure modes. Deployment topology should therefore follow justified constraints rather than determine logical design prematurely.","principle":"Logical modularity is a design property; physical distribution is a deployment decision requiring additional justification.","objective":"Avoid unnecessary distributed-system complexity while preserving meaningful architectural boundaries.","requirements":["Clear module ownership","Defined dependency direction","Independent testability","Explicit criteria for physical separation"],"constraints":["Network calls introduce additional failure modes","Operational maturity may be limited","Some components require independent scaling"],"preconditions":["Logical boundaries are defined","Deployment constraints are documented"],"input":"Component architecture plus scaling, isolation, security, and operational requirements.","output":"Deployment topology derived from justified physical constraints.","expected_behavior":"Components remain independently understandable even when deployed together or separately.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"Every logical module is deployed as a separate service without independent scaling or isolation requirements.","expected_error":"DISTRIBUTION_WITHOUT_JUSTIFICATION","cause":"Deployment topology was selected before architectural requirements were evaluated.","root_cause":"Microservice count was treated as an architecture objective.","solution":"Retain logical modularity and distribute only components with evidence-backed physical requirements.","edge_case":"A module initially deployed in-process later requires independent scaling.","security_implication":"Physical boundaries can strengthen isolation but also create additional credentials, network paths, and trust relationships.","performance_implication":"Unnecessary network boundaries add latency, serialization cost, retries, and operational overhead.","test_case":"Evaluate whether each deployment boundary has an independently testable scaling, isolation, lifecycle, or failure-domain requirement.","test_input":"Ten logical modules with identical scaling and lifecycle requirements.","expected_test_result":"Modules remain logically separate but do not require ten independent deployments.","testing_guidance":"Test deployment topology against scaling, failure, security, and lifecycle requirements.","verification_method":"Map each physical boundary to at least one explicit requirement.","verification_guidance":"Reject unexplained distribution and document the evidence for every justified boundary.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Deployment platform if physical separation is selected"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B003","D33-B028"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Logical component model"],"extends":"D33-B003","refines":"","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Service-per-module deployment without requirement justification","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Requirement-driven deployment topology","tests":"Architecture-to-deployment traceability tests","verifies":"Deployment-boundary justification","derived_from":"Established modular architecture and distributed-systems practice","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture description principles","Established modularity and deployment architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B028","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Fault isolation","concept":"Selecting failure-domain boundaries based on blast-radius requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when a requirement states that failure in one subsystem must not disable unrelated capabilities?","answer":"Define explicit failure domains and isolate critical dependencies using appropriate process, deployment, resource, queue, or dependency boundaries.","explanation":"Availability requirements are partly architectural because shared processes, resources, dependencies, and databases can create correlated failures. Isolation should be proportional to the required blast radius and supported by actual recovery requirements.","principle":"A failure-isolation boundary is justified when a shared failure domain violates a stated resilience requirement.","objective":"Limit fault propagation to an acceptable architectural blast radius.","requirements":["Defined failure domains","Dependency isolation","Resource isolation where necessary","Graceful degradation behavior"],"constraints":["Isolation introduces operational complexity","Some shared infrastructure may remain unavoidable","Failure modes differ by subsystem"],"preconditions":["Critical capabilities are identified","Failure impact is classified"],"input":"Availability requirements and dependency/failure analysis.","output":"Failure-domain architecture with isolation and degradation strategies.","expected_behavior":"Failure of one noncritical subsystem does not violate the defined availability boundary of unrelated critical capabilities.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"A shared dependency failure cascades through otherwise independent capabilities.","expected_error":"FAILURE_DOMAIN_BREACH","cause":"Critical components share an unbounded failure domain.","root_cause":"Availability requirement was not translated into dependency and resource boundaries.","solution":"Introduce bounded dependencies, isolation, fallback, queueing, or independent deployment where justified.","edge_case":"The supposedly isolated subsystem shares a database or connection pool with the critical subsystem.","security_implication":"Isolation boundaries can also reduce lateral movement but must not be treated as a substitute for authorization.","performance_implication":"Isolation may duplicate resources and reduce utilization efficiency.","test_case":"Intentionally make a noncritical dependency unavailable and verify critical capability behavior.","test_input":"Noncritical service unavailable for defined failure interval.","expected_test_result":"Critical path remains within its documented degradation/availability contract.","testing_guidance":"Test dependency outage, resource exhaustion, process crash, network partition, and recovery.","verification_method":"Execute controlled failure tests against defined failure domains.","verification_guidance":"Verify observed blast radius matches architecture documentation and requirements.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Failure injection or controlled outage testing capability"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESILIENCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B008","D33-B027","D33-B029"],"depends_on":["D33-B008"],"requires":["Availability and failure-impact requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B008","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Shared unbounded failure domain","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Failure-domain isolation","tests":"Fault-injection tests","verifies":"Blast-radius requirements","derived_from":"Established resilience engineering and distributed-systems practice","provenance":["Established resilience engineering practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B029","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Observability architecture","concept":"Designing observability as an architectural capability","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN","question":"How should architecture handle a requirement that failures and important business outcomes must be diagnosable across components?","answer":"Design structured logs, metrics, traces, correlation identifiers, business outcome telemetry, and provenance into the architecture rather than adding them only after deployment.","explanation":"Distributed or asynchronous systems cannot reliably be diagnosed from isolated component logs. Observability architecture must preserve causal context across boundaries and distinguish technical health from business correctness.","principle":"A system cannot be reliably operated or verified beyond the evidence its architecture preserves.","objective":"Make system behavior, failures, dependencies, and business outcomes observable enough for diagnosis and verification.","requirements":["Cross-component correlation","Structured telemetry","Failure visibility","Business outcome visibility","Retention and access policy"],"constraints":["Telemetry volume can be significant","Sensitive data must not leak into telemetry","Sampling can remove diagnostic evidence"],"preconditions":["Critical workflows are identified","Observability objectives are defined"],"input":"Operational, diagnostic, and verification requirements.","output":"Observability architecture covering signals, correlation, retention, access, and business outcome evidence.","expected_behavior":"An operator can trace a representative transaction/workflow across relevant architectural boundaries and determine its outcome.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"A workflow fails but each component contains only isolated local logs with no shared correlation identity.","expected_error":"DIAGNOSTIC_CONTEXT_MISSING","cause":"Correlation and cross-boundary telemetry were not architectural requirements.","root_cause":"Observability was treated as a monitoring add-on.","solution":"Define correlation identifiers, structured events, trace context, business outcome events, and retention/security rules as part of architecture.","edge_case":"A sampled trace omits the exact failing operation needed for investigation.","security_implication":"Telemetry can contain secrets, personal data, tokens, or sensitive business information; collection and access require explicit controls.","performance_implication":"Excessive telemetry can increase CPU, storage, network, and query costs; sampling and aggregation must preserve required evidence.","test_case":"Trace a complete representative workflow across synchronous and asynchronous boundaries.","test_input":"Workflow containing API request, queue operation, worker, datastore write, and business outcome.","expected_test_result":"The workflow can be correlated end-to-end using permitted telemetry.","testing_guidance":"Test missing context, asynchronous propagation, sampling, high-volume conditions, and sensitive-field redaction.","verification_method":"Perform end-to-end traceability tests and inspect telemetry against defined diagnostic requirements.","verification_guidance":"Verify both technical failure evidence and business outcome evidence.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Telemetry infrastructure","Correlation mechanism"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B028","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B004"],"requires":["Defined operational evidence requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Local-log-only architecture","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Cross-boundary observability architecture","tests":"End-to-end observability tests","verifies":"Diagnostic traceability","derived_from":"Established observability and reliability engineering practice","provenance":["Established observability architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B030","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture verification","concept":"Tracing requirements through architecture decisions to verification evidence","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_VERIFICATION","question":"How can an architecture decision be verified rather than merely documented?","answer":"Create explicit traceability from requirements to architectural decisions, architectural mechanisms, measurable acceptance criteria, tests or analysis, and verification evidence.","explanation":"An architecture document can describe an intended system without proving that the design satisfies its requirements. Architecture verification requires a chain of evidence connecting the original requirement to the selected architectural mechanism and a test, analysis, inspection, or measurement capable of evaluating it.","principle":"Every important architectural requirement should have a verifiable acceptance mechanism and evidence path.","objective":"Convert architecture rationale into an auditable and testable requirement-to-evidence chain.","requirements":["Requirement identifiers","Architecture decision records","Acceptance criteria","Verification methods","Evidence retention"],"constraints":["Not every architectural property can be verified through runtime tests alone","Some properties require static analysis, inspection, modeling, or operational evidence"],"preconditions":["Requirements are explicit","Architecture decisions are documented","Verification criteria are defined"],"input":"Requirements, architecture decisions, constraints, and acceptance criteria.","output":"Traceability graph connecting requirement → architecture decision → mechanism → verification method → evidence.","expected_behavior":"A reviewer can identify why an architectural decision exists and how satisfaction of the underlying requirement will be established.","incorrect_code":"","corrected_code":"","failure_mode":"Architecture contains decisions but no evidence path showing whether requirements are satisfied.","expected_error":"ARCHITECTURE_VERIFICATION_GAP","cause":"Documentation stopped at rationale and did not define verification.","root_cause":"Architecture was treated as a static design artifact rather than a hypothesis requiring evidence.","solution":"Attach measurable or inspectable verification criteria to important architecture decisions and maintain traceability.","edge_case":"Requirement is qualitative, such as maintainability or evolvability, and cannot be reduced to one runtime metric.","security_implication":"Security architecture requires explicit verification of trust boundaries, authorization enforcement, data handling, and failure behavior.","performance_implication":"Performance architecture requires measurable workload assumptions and controlled benchmarks or production evidence; unmeasured claims remain unverified.","test_case":"Select a critical requirement and traverse its traceability chain from requirement to verification evidence.","test_input":"Requirement R-017 with architecture decision AD-004 and verification test VT-009.","expected_test_result":"The chain is complete and the evidence status is explicit.","testing_guidance":"Test traceability completeness, orphan requirements, orphan architecture decisions, stale evidence, and contradictory constraints.","verification_method":"Perform architecture traceability analysis plus targeted architecture fitness tests or inspections.","verification_guidance":"Distinguish documented intent from verified satisfaction; preserve evidence provenance and execution status.","language":"","language_version":"","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["Requirements repository","Architecture decision records","Verification/evidence repository"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_STANDARD_AND_ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B001","D33-B010","D33-B029"],"depends_on":["D33-B001"],"requires":["Stable requirement identifiers","Verification criteria"],"extends":"D33-B010","refines":"D33-B001","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"Documentation-only architecture governance","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"Requirement-to-evidence architecture traceability","tests":"Architecture traceability tests","verifies":"Architecture requirement satisfaction evidence","derived_from":"Architecture description and verification principles","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture description concepts","Established requirements traceability and architecture verification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B031","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"API boundary architecture","concept":"Public and internal API boundary selection","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture respond when consumers require a stable API while internal services or modules may evolve independently?","answer":"Define an explicit API boundary that isolates consumers from internal implementation details. Select direct service APIs, a facade, an API gateway, or a backend-for-frontend boundary according to consumer needs, composition requirements, security boundaries, and change independence. Keep the public contract smaller and more stable than the internal model.","explanation":"A requirement for consumer independence is not satisfied merely by exposing an HTTP endpoint. The architecture must control what becomes externally observable. A stable boundary should prevent internal persistence models, implementation-specific errors, and internal service topology from becoming accidental contracts. Where consumers have materially different interaction needs, a facade or backend-for-frontend can compose internal capabilities without forcing clients to understand internal topology.","principle":"Expose intentional contracts, not internal implementation structure.","objective":"Preserve consumer autonomy and compatibility while allowing internal architecture to evolve.","requirements":["Consumers require a stable interaction boundary.","Internal implementation may change independently of consumers.","The architecture must control externally observable behavior.","Contract ownership and compatibility responsibilities must be explicit."],"constraints":["Every additional boundary introduces operational and maintenance cost.","A facade must not become an unbounded business-logic dumping ground.","Internal and external contracts may have different evolution rates.","API compatibility must be evaluated against actual consumer requirements."],"preconditions":["Consumer interactions and ownership are identified.","The capabilities exposed through the boundary are defined.","Compatibility expectations are known."],"input":["Consumer requirements","API capability set","Security requirements","Compatibility requirements","Change-frequency characteristics"],"output":"An explicit API boundary architecture with defined ownership, contract scope, compatibility policy, and composition responsibility.","expected_behavior":"Consumers interact through a deliberate contract while internal modules or services can evolve without requiring unnecessary consumer changes.","incorrect_code":"class UserAPI:\n    def get_user(self, user_id):\n        return database.users.find_one(user_id)\n","corrected_code":"class UserAPI:\n    def get_user(self, user_id):\n        user = user_service.get_user(user_id)\n        return {"id": user.id, "display_name": user.display_name}\n","failure_mode":"The API exposes persistence-shaped objects and accidentally couples consumers to the internal data model.","expected_error":"No runtime exception is required; the architectural failure appears when internal schema changes force consumer changes.","cause":"The API boundary is treated as a transport wrapper instead of an intentional contract.","root_cause":"Internal implementation details were allowed to become externally observable.","solution":"Define a consumer-oriented contract and map internal representations to that contract at the boundary.","edge_case":"An internal field may be useful to one consumer but exposing it can create a long-term compatibility obligation.","security_implication":"A narrow boundary reduces accidental exposure of internal data and capabilities; authorization must be enforced according to the exposed operation rather than assumed from internal reachability.","performance_implication":"A facade or composition layer can add latency; architecture should measure whether aggregation reduces client round trips enough to justify the additional boundary.","test_case":"Change the internal user representation while preserving the public API contract.","test_input":{"internal_change":"Rename database field full_name to legal_name","consumer_contract":{"field":"display_name"}},"expected_test_result":"The consumer contract remains unchanged and the API mapping continues to return display_name.","testing_guidance":"Test contract behavior independently from persistence representation. Include compatibility tests for supported consumer versions.","verification_method":"Contract tests plus architecture review confirming that internal persistence types are not exposed as public contract types.","verification_guidance":"Verify the consumer-facing schema, compatibility policy, ownership, error model, authorization boundary, and dependency direction.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B025","D33-B026","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B025"],"requires":["D33-B025"],"extends":["D33-B025"],"refines":["D33-B025"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture description principles","Established API boundary and information-hiding practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B032","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Event-driven architecture","concept":"Event-driven architecture decision","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a requirement justify event-driven architecture rather than direct synchronous communication?","answer":"Use event-driven architecture when the requirement materially benefits from temporal decoupling, multiple independent consumers, asynchronous processing, durable domain or integration facts, or producer-consumer independence. Do not introduce events merely to make an architecture appear distributed. Define delivery, ordering, duplication, retention, ownership, and failure semantics before selecting an event mechanism.","explanation":"Events introduce useful decoupling but also create new correctness and operational concerns. The architecture decision should begin with the required business behavior rather than with a messaging technology. A domain event can represent a fact that has occurred, while an integration event is shaped for communication across a boundary. Event notification and event-carried state transfer should not be treated as identical patterns because their coupling and data-consistency properties differ.","principle":"Choose asynchronous events because the requirement needs their semantics, not because events are architecturally fashionable.","objective":"Match communication architecture to temporal coupling, consumer independence, and reliability requirements.","requirements":["The requirement must identify a meaningful asynchronous or decoupled interaction.","Event ownership and semantics must be defined.","Delivery and failure behavior must be explicit.","Consumers must tolerate the defined delivery semantics."],"constraints":["Events add operational complexity.","At-least-once delivery can produce duplicates.","Ordering may be local rather than global.","Event schemas become compatibility contracts.","Debugging distributed asynchronous flows requires stronger observability."],"preconditions":["Business facts and commands are distinguished.","Producer and consumer responsibilities are identified.","Required latency and consistency characteristics are known."],"input":["Interaction requirements","Consistency requirements","Latency requirements","Consumer topology","Failure tolerance"],"output":"A decision specifying whether events are required and, if selected, defining event semantics, ownership, delivery assumptions, ordering, retention, and compatibility.","expected_behavior":"The architecture uses asynchronous events only for interactions whose requirements benefit from temporal or dependency decoupling.","incorrect_code":"def create_order(order):\n    database.save(order)\n    event_bus.publish({"order": order})\n","corrected_code":"def create_order(order):\n    database.save(order)\n    outbox.save({"type": "OrderCreated", "order_id": order.id})\n","failure_mode":"A database write and event publication can diverge, causing consumers to miss a business fact or receive an event for data that was not committed.","expected_error":"Potentially no immediate runtime error; the failure manifests as an inconsistent integration state.","cause":"The event is published independently of the transaction that establishes the source fact.","root_cause":"The architecture lacks an explicit consistency mechanism between transactional state and event publication.","solution":"Use a transactional outbox or another explicitly verified atomicity strategy appropriate to the selected infrastructure.","edge_case":"A consumer may receive the same event more than once and must therefore implement idempotent processing when duplicate delivery is possible.","security_implication":"Events can distribute sensitive data beyond the originating boundary; event payloads should contain only data required by authorized consumers.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous delivery can reduce producer blocking but introduces queueing delay, consumer lag, and additional infrastructure overhead.","test_case":"Commit a business transaction and simulate an interruption before event dispatch.","test_input":{"transaction":"OrderCreated","dispatch_state":"interrupted_after_commit"},"expected_test_result":"The architecture preserves the event for eventual dispatch without creating a false successful dispatch state.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate delivery, consumer failure, delayed delivery, schema compatibility, replay behavior, and transaction-to-event consistency.","verification_method":"Architecture review plus integration tests covering delivery and failure semantics.","verification_guidance":"Verify that event usage directly traces to a requirement and that delivery, ordering, duplication, ownership, retention, and recovery are explicitly specified.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B021","D33-B023","D33-B024"],"depends_on":["D33-B021","D33-B024"],"requires":["D33-B021","D33-B024"],"extends":["D33-B004"],"refines":[],"specializes":["D33-B004"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established event-driven architecture and transactional messaging patterns","Distributed systems reliability practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B033","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Transactional and analytical workload separation","concept":"OLTP and analytical architecture boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when analytical workloads compete with transactional workloads for the same data resources?","answer":"Separate analytical access from transactional execution when analytical workload characteristics can materially affect transactional latency, availability, storage behavior, or operational safety. Candidate architectures include read replicas, replicated analytical stores, warehouses, lakes, or purpose-built projections. The selection should follow freshness, query complexity, data volume, isolation, recovery, and governance requirements.","explanation":"A transactional database is optimized around correctness and transactional workload behavior, while analytical workloads may scan large datasets, perform joins or aggregations, and consume substantial I/O and compute. The architecture should therefore treat workload isolation as a requirement-driven boundary rather than automatically assuming that one database must serve every workload.","principle":"Place materially different workload characteristics behind boundaries that protect required system properties.","objective":"Preserve transactional guarantees and operational stability while providing appropriate analytical access.","requirements":["Transactional workload has explicit latency and availability requirements.","Analytical workload characteristics are understood.","Data freshness requirements are defined.","Analytical consumers have a defined data access path."],"constraints":["Replication introduces freshness delay.","Additional stores increase data movement and operational complexity.","Analytical copies create governance and lifecycle responsibilities.","Not every system requires a separate analytical platform."],"preconditions":["Transactional and analytical access patterns are identified.","Expected data volume and query characteristics are understood.","Freshness tolerance is known."],"input":["OLTP workload","Analytical workload","Freshness requirement","Data volume","Query complexity","Availability requirements"],"output":"A workload-separation architecture and data movement strategy appropriate to the stated analytical and transactional requirements.","expected_behavior":"Analytical processing does not materially degrade transactional workloads beyond defined service objectives.","incorrect_code":"def generate_report():\n    return production_db.execute("SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id")\n","corrected_code":"def generate_report():\n    return analytics_store.query("SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) FROM order_facts GROUP BY customer_id")\n","failure_mode":"Large analytical scans compete with transactional operations on the production database.","expected_error":"No specific application exception is required; symptoms may include increased latency, I/O contention, lock contention, or resource exhaustion.","cause":"Analytical and transactional workloads share the same resource boundary despite materially different workload characteristics.","root_cause":"The architecture did not model workload isolation as an explicit requirement.","solution":"Introduce an appropriate analytical projection or replica and define its freshness and correctness semantics.","edge_case":"If analytics require near-real-time data, a replicated or streaming projection may be needed rather than a daily batch copy.","security_implication":"Replicated analytical data can expand the number of locations containing sensitive information and therefore requires equivalent or explicitly scoped access controls and retention policies.","performance_implication":"Workload isolation can protect transactional latency but adds replication, storage, and query-path overhead.","test_case":"Run representative analytical workload while observing transactional latency under defined load.","test_input":{"transactional_operations":"normal workload","analytical_operations":"representative aggregation workload"},"expected_test_result":"Transactional service objectives remain within their defined limits while analytical workload executes on its designated boundary.","testing_guidance":"Use production-like workload characteristics where possible and measure both analytical freshness and transactional impact. Do not treat synthetic results as production benchmarks.","verification_method":"Architecture review plus workload and integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify data lineage, freshness semantics, workload isolation, access control, failure behavior, and recovery procedures.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B008","D33-B009","D33-B021","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B009"],"requires":["D33-B009"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B009"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established OLTP and analytical workload separation practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture concern and stakeholder requirement principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B034","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Batch and streaming processing","concept":"Processing latency versus throughput architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture choose between batch, streaming, and hybrid processing when requirements specify data freshness and processing volume?","answer":"Derive the processing architecture from required freshness, throughput, ordering, replay, processing complexity, and operational constraints. Batch processing is appropriate when bounded processing intervals satisfy the requirement; streaming is appropriate when continuously arriving data must be processed within tighter freshness constraints; hybrid architectures are appropriate when different data products have different latency requirements.","explanation":"Streaming is not automatically superior to batch processing. It introduces state management, recovery, backpressure, ordering, replay, and operational complexity. Batch processing can be simpler and more economical when the business requirement permits delayed processing. The architecture should therefore establish an explicit freshness objective before selecting the processing model.","principle":"Choose processing semantics from required freshness and workload behavior rather than technology preference.","objective":"Satisfy freshness and throughput requirements with the least unnecessary architectural complexity.","requirements":["Freshness target is defined.","Expected event or record volume is understood.","Processing semantics and correctness requirements are defined.","Failure and replay behavior are specified."],"constraints":["Streaming systems require stronger operational handling of continuously evolving state.","Batch systems inherently introduce processing windows.","Hybrid systems increase architectural surface area.","Exactly-once semantics may have infrastructure-specific limitations."],"preconditions":["Data arrival pattern is known.","Processing dependencies are identified.","Consumers' freshness expectations are documented."],"input":["Freshness requirement","Data arrival rate","Processing complexity","Ordering requirement","Replay requirement"],"output":"A selected processing model with explicit latency, throughput, state, failure, and replay semantics.","expected_behavior":"Data is processed within the required freshness boundary without imposing unnecessary continuous-processing complexity where batch satisfies the requirement.","incorrect_code":"while True:\n    record = read_next_record()\n    process(record)\n","corrected_code":"def process_batch(records):\n    for record in records:\n        process(record)\n","failure_mode":"A continuously running processor is introduced despite a requirement that permits periodic processing.","expected_error":"No required runtime exception; the failure is architectural through unnecessary operational complexity and potentially higher resource consumption.","cause":"The architecture equates freshness with real-time processing without establishing an actual freshness requirement.","root_cause":"Processing mode was selected before translating the requirement into measurable latency and throughput characteristics.","solution":"Define the freshness objective and select batch, streaming, or hybrid processing based on that objective and workload behavior.","edge_case":"A system may require streaming ingestion for freshness-sensitive signals while producing some expensive analytical aggregates in periodic batches.","security_implication":"Continuous processing paths can expand the attack surface and require secure handling of long-lived consumers, credentials, offsets, and event payloads.","performance_implication":"Streaming can reduce processing delay but may consume continuously active compute and storage resources; batch can improve resource efficiency when latency tolerance permits.","test_case":"Compare processing behavior against an explicit freshness objective under representative arrival patterns.","test_input":{"freshness_requirement":"less_than_5_minutes","arrival_pattern":"continuous"},"expected_test_result":"The selected architecture produces the required data freshness under the defined workload or identifies that the architecture cannot satisfy the requirement.","testing_guidance":"Measure end-to-end freshness rather than only processor execution time. Include delayed input, consumer restart, backlog, and replay tests.","verification_method":"Requirement traceability review plus representative integration/load testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that latency, throughput, ordering, replay, failure recovery, and resource requirements are explicitly documented.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_PROCESSING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B032","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B030"],"requires":["D33-B030"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B004"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established batch and stream processing architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B035","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Configuration and secrets architecture","concept":"Separation of configuration, secrets, and immutable application artifacts","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle runtime configuration and secrets without coupling sensitive values to application code or deployment artifacts?","answer":"Separate ordinary configuration from secrets and from immutable application artifacts. Configuration should be supplied through an explicit runtime mechanism, while secrets should use a protected secret-management mechanism appropriate to the deployment environment. The architecture should define ownership, rotation, scope, precedence, validation, and failure behavior for both.","explanation":"Configuration and secrets have different security and lifecycle properties. Treating both as source code constants, embedding them in images, or scattering environment-variable reads throughout business logic makes rotation and governance difficult. A centralized configuration boundary can validate required settings and provide controlled access while keeping application behavior deterministic.","principle":"Sensitive runtime values require a controlled lifecycle and access boundary distinct from application source code.","objective":"Provide reliable runtime configuration while minimizing secret exposure and simplifying rotation.","requirements":["Secrets must not be hard-coded into source.","Configuration ownership and precedence are defined.","Secret access is restricted to required components.","Missing or invalid configuration fails predictably."],"constraints":["Secret-management mechanisms vary by deployment environment.","Some local development environments may require simpler adapters.","Rotation may require application reload or dynamic refresh support.","Configuration values can still contain sensitive information even when not technically classified as secrets."],"preconditions":["Required configuration keys are enumerated.","Secret and non-secret values are classified.","Runtime deployment model is known."],"input":["Application settings","Secret requirements","Deployment environment","Rotation requirements","Access-control requirements"],"output":"A configuration architecture defining storage, loading, validation, access control, rotation, and failure behavior.","expected_behavior":"Application artifacts remain free of deployment-specific secrets and configuration is loaded through an explicit validated boundary.","incorrect_code":"DATABASE_PASSWORD = "production-password-123"\n","corrected_code":"import os\n\nDATABASE_PASSWORD = os.environ["DATABASE_PASSWORD"]\n","failure_mode":"A secret is embedded directly in application source code.","expected_error":"No runtime error is required; the security failure occurs through source exposure and difficult secret rotation.","cause":"Secret lifecycle is coupled to source code.","root_cause":"Configuration and secret management were not treated as architecture concerns.","solution":"Externalize secrets and establish an explicit protected runtime injection and rotation mechanism.","edge_case":"An environment variable can reduce source-code exposure but may still be observable through process inspection or deployment configuration; stronger secret management may therefore be required.","security_implication":"Secrets require least-privilege access, controlled storage, rotation, auditability, and protection against accidental logging or exposure.","performance_implication":"Centralized secret retrieval may add startup or refresh overhead; caching secrets in memory must be balanced against rotation requirements.","test_case":"Start the application with a required secret missing and verify deterministic failure without logging the secret.","test_input":{"DATABASE_PASSWORD":"missing"},"expected_test_result":"Startup or initialization fails with a non-sensitive configuration error and does not expose secret material.","testing_guidance":"Test missing values, invalid values, rotation, permission denial, logging redaction, and configuration precedence.","verification_method":"Static secret scanning, configuration tests, deployment review, and access-control verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify no secrets are committed to source or artifacts and confirm that runtime access is restricted to required components.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B027","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B030"],"requires":["D33-B030"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established secure configuration and secret-management practice","OWASP secure configuration principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B036","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Dynamic business policy architecture","concept":"Rules and policy engine boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should frequently changing business rules become an explicit policy or rules-engine boundary rather than remain embedded in application code?","answer":"Introduce an explicit policy or rules boundary when rule ownership, change frequency, auditability, versioning, or runtime selection requirements materially differ from normal application deployment. Keep stable and tightly coupled invariants in application code when externalizing them would add unnecessary complexity. The architecture must preserve deterministic evaluation, version traceability, authorization, and rollback behavior.","explanation":"A rules engine is not inherently better than ordinary code. Externalizing rules becomes valuable when authorized non-developer policy owners or independently versioned decision logic require controlled change without changing the surrounding application. Conversely, simple invariants can become harder to reason about if moved into a generic rules layer. The architecture should therefore distinguish stable domain invariants from independently governed policies.","principle":"Externalize decision logic only when its lifecycle and governance differ materially from application code.","objective":"Support controlled policy evolution without sacrificing determinism, traceability, or correctness.","requirements":["Rules have identifiable ownership.","Rule changes require independent lifecycle or governance.","Rule versions must be traceable.","Evaluation behavior must be deterministic where the requirement demands it."],"constraints":["A generic rules engine can increase complexity.","Dynamic rule changes can introduce production risk.","Rule dependencies can become difficult to discover.","Policy evaluation must remain auditable."],"preconditions":["Rules are catalogued and classified.","Change frequency and ownership are understood.","Required audit and rollback behavior is defined."],"input":["Business rules","Change frequency","Ownership model","Versioning requirements","Audit requirements"],"output":"A decision on embedded versus externalized policy evaluation with explicit lifecycle and governance semantics.","expected_behavior":"Rules whose lifecycle requires independent governance can be changed and audited without modifying unrelated application behavior.","incorrect_code":"def approve_order(order):\n    if order.total < 1000 and order.customer_age >= 18:\n        return True\n    return False\n","corrected_code":"def approve_order(order, policy):\n    decision = policy.evaluate({"total": order.total, "customer_age": order.customer_age})\n    return decision.allowed\n","failure_mode":"Business policy is scattered through application code and changes require finding and modifying multiple unrelated implementation paths.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime exception; the failure is governance and change-control related.","cause":"Policy lifecycle was not separated from application implementation.","root_cause":"Architecture did not model independently governed rules as an explicit concern.","solution":"Create a versioned policy boundary only when independent lifecycle, audit, and ownership requirements justify it.","edge_case":"A rule may look dynamic but still depend on internal invariants that must remain enforced in code regardless of external policy decisions.","security_implication":"Policy evaluation must be protected from unauthorized rule changes and must preserve an auditable relationship between decision, policy version, inputs, and outcome.","performance_implication":"External policy evaluation may add latency; local compiled or cached policies can reduce overhead but must preserve version and invalidation semantics.","test_case":"Evaluate the same input against two explicitly versioned policy definitions and verify deterministic version-specific results.","test_input":{"policy_versions":["v1","v2"],"input":{"total":500,"customer_age":21}},"expected_test_result":"Each evaluation is attributable to its declared policy version and produces the behavior defined by that version.","testing_guidance":"Test rule precedence, conflicting rules, missing inputs, policy version selection, rollback, authorization, deterministic evaluation, and audit records.","verification_method":"Policy unit tests, integration tests, version traceability review, and authorization verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every production decision can identify the policy version and relevant inputs without relying on mutable undocumented state.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_POLICY_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B006","D33-B025","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B030"],"requires":["D33-B030"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established policy and rules-engine architecture practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture concern and decision principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B037","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Extension security architecture","concept":"Trust boundaries for untrusted plugins and extensions","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture change when third-party or untrusted extensions must be supported?","answer":"Treat an untrusted extension as a separate trust boundary and select an isolation model based on its required privileges and threat model. In-process plugins are appropriate only when the extension is trusted to share the host process boundary. Untrusted extensions generally require stronger isolation such as separate processes or other sandboxing mechanisms whose security properties are explicitly verified.","explanation":"A plugin interface alone does not provide security isolation. An extension executing inside the host process can potentially access memory, credentials, filesystem resources, or internal APIs available to the host. Therefore the requirement for third-party extensibility must be decomposed into trust, capability, isolation, communication, resource, update, and revocation requirements.","principle":"Extensibility does not remove the trust boundary; architecture must make the boundary explicit.","objective":"Enable controlled extensibility while limiting the authority and blast radius of extensions.","requirements":["Extension trust level is defined.","Required capabilities are enumerated.","Isolation strength is matched to the threat model.","Extension lifecycle and revocation are defined."],"constraints":["Strong isolation adds IPC and deployment complexity.","Language-level sandboxing may not provide the security properties assumed by the threat model.","Extensions may require controlled access to host capabilities.","Security depends on the actual isolation mechanism and configuration."],"preconditions":["Extension threat model exists.","Extension capabilities are classified.","Host assets requiring protection are identified."],"input":["Extension trust level","Required capabilities","Threat model","Host resources","Availability requirements"],"output":"An extension architecture with explicit trust boundaries, capabilities, isolation mechanism, communication model, and revocation strategy.","expected_behavior":"An extension can access only explicitly authorized capabilities and cannot automatically inherit unrestricted host authority.","incorrect_code":"def load_plugin(path):\n    module = import(path)\n    return module.run()\n","corrected_code":"def invoke_plugin(plugin_client, request):\n    return plugin_client.invoke(request, allowed_capabilities={"read_catalog"})\n","failure_mode":"Arbitrary extension code executes inside the host process with inherited privileges.","expected_error":"No specific exception is required; the security failure may occur through unauthorized resource access.","cause":"Plugin execution and host authority share the same trust boundary.","root_cause":"The architecture treated extensibility as an API design problem rather than a trust-boundary problem.","solution":"Isolate untrusted extensions and expose only explicitly authorized capabilities through a controlled interface.","edge_case":"A plugin may require filesystem or network access that cannot be safely represented by a narrow API; this should trigger an explicit threat-model review rather than silently granting broad privileges.","security_implication":"Isolation, least privilege, capability control, update provenance, revocation, and resource limits are core architectural requirements.","performance_implication":"Process isolation and IPC add overhead; the acceptable cost depends on extension call frequency and security requirements.","test_case":"Attempt an unauthorized extension operation and verify that the host denies it without exposing broader capabilities.","test_input":{"requested_capability":"read_host_private_file","authorized_capabilities":["read_catalog"]},"expected_test_result":"The request is denied and the extension receives only an appropriately scoped failure response.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized capability access, process termination, malformed requests, resource exhaustion, extension update, revocation, and host recovery.","verification_method":"Threat-model review, isolation tests, capability authorization tests, and deployment verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual isolation mechanism rather than trusting a configuration label. Confirm that host secrets and privileged resources are inaccessible through unauthorized paths.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B006","D33-B028","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B006"],"requires":["D33-B006"],"extends":["D33-B006"],"refines":["D33-B006"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established sandboxing and trust-boundary architecture practice","OWASP secure design principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B038","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Multi-region resilience","concept":"RTO/RPO-driven disaster recovery architecture","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should recovery requirements determine whether a system uses backup restore, active-passive, or active-active regional architecture?","answer":"Start with explicit recovery time objective and recovery point objective requirements, then evaluate architecture options against those objectives, data consistency needs, dependency recovery, operational complexity, and cost. Backup restore, active-passive, and active-active are architectural patterns with different recovery characteristics; none should be selected without evidence that its complete dependency chain can satisfy the required recovery objectives.","explanation":"Disaster recovery is a system property rather than a database-only feature. A design that replicates application data but cannot recover identity, configuration, external dependencies, networking, or deployment artifacts may fail to meet the actual recovery requirement. Architecture must therefore model the complete recovery path and validate it through exercises or tests.","principle":"Recovery objectives apply to the complete service dependency graph, not only to primary data storage.","objective":"Design a recovery architecture whose documented mechanisms can satisfy defined RTO and RPO requirements.","requirements":["RTO is explicitly defined.","RPO is explicitly defined.","Critical dependencies are identified.","Recovery procedures and ownership are defined.","Recovery verification is planned."],"constraints":["Lower recovery objectives generally require greater architectural complexity and operational readiness.","Cross-region consistency may involve latency or availability trade-offs.","External dependencies can dominate actual recovery time.","Recovery mechanisms require testing to establish confidence."],"preconditions":["Business impact and service criticality are known.","Dependencies are inventoried.","Data durability requirements are defined."],"input":["RTO","RPO","Service dependency graph","Consistency requirements","Operational capabilities"],"output":"A recovery architecture with documented recovery paths, dependency handling, objectives, and verification procedures.","expected_behavior":"The complete service can be restored within the defined recovery objectives under the tested failure scenarios or the architecture explicitly records unmet requirements.","incorrect_code":"def disaster_recovery():\n    backup_database()\n    return True\n","corrected_code":"def recovery_plan():\n    return {\n        "rto": "defined_and_tested",\n        "rpo": "defined_and_tested",\n        "dependencies": "included",\n        "verification": "scheduled"\n    }\n","failure_mode":"A database backup is treated as proof that the entire service can recover within required objectives.","expected_error":"No runtime exception is required; the failure is an unverified recovery assumption.","cause":"Recovery architecture covers storage but not application, identity, configuration, external dependencies, or operational procedures.","root_cause":"RTO and RPO were not translated into an end-to-end recovery architecture.","solution":"Model the complete recovery dependency graph and verify restoration through controlled recovery exercises.","edge_case":"A dependency outside organizational control may have a recovery objective incompatible with the primary service objective and must therefore be explicitly represented.","security_implication":"Recovery environments and backups can contain sensitive production data and require equivalent or explicitly controlled security protections.","performance_implication":"Cross-region replication and active-active architectures can add network, storage, and operational overhead; actual impact must be measured for the workload.","test_case":"Perform a controlled regional recovery exercise and measure actual service restoration time and recovered data point.","test_input":{"failure_scenario":"primary_region_unavailable","required_rto":"defined_target","required_rpo":"defined_target"},"expected_test_result":"Measured recovery behavior is recorded against the stated RTO/RPO without treating an unexecuted plan as proof.","testing_guidance":"Run documented recovery exercises and record observed RTO/RPO, missing dependencies, operator actions, data integrity, and post-recovery convergence.","verification_method":"Disaster recovery exercise with measured evidence and architecture review.","verification_guidance":"Verify the entire dependency chain, not only storage replication. Mark objectives as unverified until exercised.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESILIENCE_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B008","D33-B028","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B008","D33-B028"],"requires":["D33-B008"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B008"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established disaster recovery and resilience engineering practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture risk and concern principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B039","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Operational and cost constraints","concept":"Architecture selection under resource and operational constraints","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_TRADEOFF","question":"How should cost, operational capacity, and organizational constraints influence architecture selection without becoming an unstructured preference?","answer":"Represent cost and operational constraints as explicit architecture requirements or evaluation criteria. Compare candidate architectures using total resource demands, operational responsibilities, failure-management burden, migration cost, vendor dependence, required expertise, and lifecycle obligations. Avoid assuming that managed or self-hosted infrastructure is universally preferable; select according to the documented constraints and evidence.","explanation":"Architecture decisions can fail when technical requirements are evaluated while operational constraints remain implicit. A technically capable design may be unsuitable if the organization cannot operate its dependencies, support required expertise, or absorb migration and lifecycle costs. Conversely, minimizing infrastructure at all costs can create unacceptable reliability or security gaps. The decision record should expose these trade-offs rather than hide them.","principle":"Operational capacity and lifecycle constraints are first-class architecture requirements.","objective":"Select an architecture that satisfies technical requirements within explicitly defined operational and resource constraints.","requirements":["Resource constraints are documented.","Operational ownership is identified.","Lifecycle and migration responsibilities are understood.","Candidate architectures are compared against the same criteria."],"constraints":["Costs change over time and depend on workload.","Vendor pricing and capabilities can change.","Operational maturity varies between organizations.","A low direct infrastructure cost can still create high labor or risk costs."],"preconditions":["Candidate architectures exist.","Technical requirements are defined.","Operational ownership and resource limits are known."],"input":["Technical requirements","Infrastructure constraints","Operational skills","Lifecycle requirements","Vendor dependency requirements"],"output":"A documented architecture decision that incorporates technical, operational, lifecycle, and resource constraints.","expected_behavior":"Architecture selection makes operational responsibilities and resource constraints explicit rather than treating them as post-deployment concerns.","incorrect_code":"def choose_database():\n    return "managed"\n","corrected_code":"def choose_database(requirements):\n    candidates = ["managed", "self_hosted"]\n    return evaluate_candidates(candidates, requirements)\n","failure_mode":"Infrastructure choice is made from a generic preference without evaluating actual workload, ownership, or lifecycle constraints.","expected_error":"No required runtime exception; the failure appears as an architecture mismatch during operation or maintenance.","cause":"Operational requirements were omitted from architecture evaluation.","root_cause":"Architecture selection treated implementation preference as a substitute for documented decision criteria.","solution":"Define comparable evaluation criteria and record evidence, assumptions, uncertainty, and lifecycle responsibilities for each candidate.","edge_case":"A candidate with lower direct infrastructure cost may require substantially more internal operational effort, which should be represented explicitly rather than ignored.","security_implication":"Operational ownership affects patching, access management, backup security, incident response, and configuration control; these responsibilities must be assigned.","performance_implication":"Resource constraints can affect capacity headroom and scaling behavior; capacity assumptions should be validated under representative workloads.","test_case":"Evaluate two architecture candidates using the same technical and operational criteria and inspect whether every criterion has an explicit rationale.","test_input":{"criteria":["reliability","security","operational_capacity","resource_constraints","migration_cost"]},"expected_test_result":"The decision record contains evidence or explicit uncertainty for each criterion and does not rely on an undocumented preference.","testing_guidance":"Perform sensitivity analysis where uncertain assumptions could materially change the architecture decision.","verification_method":"Architecture decision review and constraint traceability audit.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every stated constraint is represented in candidate evaluation and that assumptions are clearly separated from measured facts.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_TRADEOFF_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B001","D33-B010","D33-B030","D33-B040"],"depends_on":["D33-B001"],"requires":["D33-B001"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030","D33-B040"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture trade-off and operational engineering practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture stakeholder concern principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B040","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture decision closure","concept":"Evidence-based candidate evaluation and architecture decision record","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION_METHOD","question":"How should an architecture decision be formally closed when multiple candidates satisfy the basic requirements but differ in trade-offs?","answer":"Close the decision by defining explicit evaluation criteria, recording candidate evidence and assumptions, identifying uncertainties, evaluating trade-offs, documenting the selected architecture and rejected alternatives, and defining review or trigger conditions that could reopen the decision. If criteria have different importance, their weighting must be explicitly justified rather than hidden. Sensitivity analysis should be used when uncertain assumptions could materially change the result.","explanation":"Architecture selection is not complete when a candidate is merely named. A defensible decision explains how requirements became criteria, how candidates were evaluated, which evidence was available, which assumptions remain uncertain, and why alternatives were not selected under the stated context. The decision should also define when changed requirements or evidence require reevaluation.","principle":"An architecture decision is complete only when its rationale, evidence, uncertainty, alternatives, and revisit conditions are explicit.","objective":"Create an auditable architecture selection record that can be understood and reassessed as requirements or evidence change.","requirements":["Requirements are traceable to evaluation criteria.","Candidates are explicitly identified.","Evidence and assumptions are distinguished.","Trade-offs are documented.","Rejected alternatives have recorded reasons.","Revisit triggers are defined."],"constraints":["Incomplete evidence can prevent definitive comparison.","Criteria may conflict.","Weights can introduce stakeholder judgment and must be transparent.","Architecture decisions can become stale as requirements and technology change."],"preconditions":["Requirement baseline exists.","Candidate architectures have been described consistently.","Evaluation criteria are agreed or explicitly documented."],"input":["Requirement set","Candidate architectures","Evaluation criteria","Evidence","Constraints","Risk and uncertainty information"],"output":"A complete architecture decision record containing rationale, evidence, trade-offs, selected architecture, rejected alternatives, uncertainties, and revisit triggers.","expected_behavior":"Another engineer can reconstruct how the architecture decision was reached and determine what new evidence would justify reopening it.","incorrect_code":"def select_architecture(candidates):\n    return candidates[0]\n","corrected_code":"def select_architecture(candidates, criteria):\n    evaluations = [evaluate(candidate, criteria) for candidate in candidates]\n    return choose_by_documented_criteria(evaluations)\n","failure_mode":"The first plausible architecture is selected without systematic comparison or documented rationale.","expected_error":"No required runtime exception; the failure is loss of decision traceability and inability to reassess the architecture rationally.","cause":"Candidate evaluation is implicit or undocumented.","root_cause":"Architecture selection was treated as implementation preference instead of an evidence-backed decision process.","solution":"Create an architecture decision record linking requirements, criteria, candidate evidence, trade-offs, uncertainty, alternatives, and revisit triggers.","edge_case":"If evidence is insufficient to distinguish candidates, the decision should preserve the uncertainty and define what evidence is required rather than inventing precision.","security_implication":"Security criteria must be evaluated explicitly; an architecture decision should not hide unresolved security assumptions behind a general technical score.","performance_implication":"Performance criteria should distinguish measured evidence from estimates and should specify workload, environment, and measurement conditions where evidence exists.","test_case":"Remove or alter a major requirement and verify that the decision process identifies whether the architecture must be reconsidered.","test_input":{"changed_requirement":"availability_target_changed","original_decision":"candidate_A","revisit_trigger":"availability_requirement_change"},"expected_test_result":"The architecture decision is flagged for review when the changed requirement crosses a documented revisit trigger.","testing_guidance":"Test decision records for completeness, traceability, assumption visibility, candidate coverage, sensitivity to changed requirements, and consistency between rationale and selected architecture.","verification_method":"Architecture review, requirement traceability audit, and decision-record completeness validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify requirement-to-criterion-to-candidate-to-rationale traceability and confirm that rejected alternatives and uncertainty are represented without fabricated evidence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_STANDARD_AND_ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B001","D33-B010","D33-B030","D33-B039"],"depends_on":["D33-B001","D33-B030"],"requires":["D33-B001","D33-B010"],"extends":["D33-B030"],"refines":["D33-B010"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture description and stakeholder concern principles","Established Architecture Decision Record practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B041","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Dependency architecture","concept":"Dependency direction and inversion at architecture boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when high-level business capabilities must remain independent from infrastructure or vendor-specific implementations?","answer":"Define dependency direction so that stable business capabilities do not become structurally dependent on volatile infrastructure details. Introduce explicit interfaces or ports at appropriate boundaries and place infrastructure-specific adapters behind them when the requirement for replaceability, testability, or policy independence justifies the abstraction.","explanation":"Dependency direction determines how architectural change propagates. If core business behavior directly imports database, messaging, vendor SDK, or transport details, infrastructure changes can force changes throughout the system. Dependency inversion can reduce this coupling, but unnecessary abstractions can also increase complexity. The architecture should therefore introduce boundaries where dependency volatility and business stability justify them.","principle":"Stable policy should not depend unnecessarily on volatile implementation details.","objective":"Control architectural change propagation and preserve independence of core business capabilities from infrastructure mechanisms.","requirements":["Business capabilities have identifiable ownership.","Infrastructure dependencies have identifiable volatility.","Dependency direction is explicitly defined.","Replacement or testing requirements justify any abstraction introduced."],"constraints":["Abstractions have maintenance cost.","Not every dependency requires an interface.","Leaky abstractions can preserve coupling despite nominal separation.","Dependency inversion does not remove the underlying infrastructure dependency."],"preconditions":["Business and infrastructure responsibilities are identified.","Dependency graph is available.","Change drivers are understood."],"input":["Business capabilities","Infrastructure dependencies","Change-frequency information","Testability requirements","Replaceability requirements"],"output":"A dependency architecture showing stable core responsibilities and controlled infrastructure dependencies.","expected_behavior":"Changes in infrastructure implementations do not unnecessarily propagate into stable business policy code.","incorrect_code":"class OrderService:\n    def init(self):\n        self.db = PostgresClient()\n","corrected_code":"class OrderService:\n    def init(self, orders):\n        self.orders = orders\n","failure_mode":"Business logic directly constructs and controls a concrete infrastructure implementation.","expected_error":"No required runtime exception; the architectural failure appears through high coupling, difficult testing, and costly infrastructure replacement.","cause":"Dependency direction follows implementation convenience rather than architectural stability.","root_cause":"Infrastructure concerns were allowed to become compile-time or structural dependencies of core business behavior.","solution":"Define a focused boundary owned by the stable capability and implement infrastructure-specific adapters behind it when justified.","edge_case":"An abstraction that mirrors every method of a concrete infrastructure library may preserve coupling instead of reducing it.","security_implication":"Dependency boundaries must preserve security invariants; adapters must not silently bypass authorization, validation, or audit requirements.","performance_implication":"Additional abstraction layers normally have low direct cost, but remote adapters, serialization, or unnecessary indirection can materially affect performance.","test_case":"Replace the concrete persistence adapter with a deterministic test implementation without modifying business policy code.","test_input":{"adapter":"in_memory_orders","business_service":"OrderService"},"expected_test_result":"Business behavior remains testable without requiring the production infrastructure implementation.","testing_guidance":"Test dependency substitution, failure propagation, transaction semantics, authorization preservation, and adapter contract compatibility.","verification_method":"Static dependency analysis, architecture review, and contract tests for infrastructure adapters.","verification_guidance":"Verify that dependency direction matches documented architecture and that abstractions correspond to real change or ownership boundaries.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B025","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B009"],"requires":["D33-B009"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B009"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established dependency inversion and hexagonal architecture practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture concern principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B042","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Consistency architecture","concept":"Explicit consistency boundary selection","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when a business process spans multiple independently owned data boundaries but requires a defined consistency outcome?","answer":"Identify the business consistency requirement first, then choose an architecture such as a single transactional boundary, coordinated workflow, saga-style process, compensating action, or explicitly eventual consistency. The architecture must define which invariants are immediate, which can become temporarily inconsistent, and how failure and recovery restore the required business state.","explanation":"Distributed data ownership makes a single atomic transaction difficult or undesirable. Treating every cross-boundary operation as eventually consistent without identifying business invariants can produce incorrect outcomes. Conversely, forcing a distributed transaction where independent ownership is required can create tight coupling. The architecture should therefore separate hard invariants from process-level convergence.","principle":"Consistency requirements should determine transaction boundaries and recovery semantics.","objective":"Preserve business correctness while respecting explicit ownership and distribution boundaries.","requirements":["Business invariants are identified.","Consistency timing is specified.","Failure states are modeled.","Recovery or compensation behavior is defined."],"constraints":["Distributed coordination introduces latency and failure modes.","Compensation may not perfectly reverse every external effect.","Eventual consistency requires consumers to tolerate intermediate states.","Some business invariants cannot safely be deferred."],"preconditions":["Data ownership boundaries are known.","Cross-boundary operations are identified.","Business invariants are documented."],"input":["Business transaction","Data ownership boundaries","Consistency requirements","Failure scenarios","Compensation capabilities"],"output":"A consistency architecture specifying transaction scope, intermediate states, failure handling, and convergence behavior.","expected_behavior":"Cross-boundary workflows preserve documented invariants and expose temporary states explicitly where immediate consistency is not required.","incorrect_code":"def transfer():\n    debit_account()\n    credit_account()\n","corrected_code":"def transfer():\n    workflow.start("transfer")\n    workflow.execute_step("debit")\n    workflow.execute_step("credit")\n    workflow.verify_or_compensate()\n","failure_mode":"A multi-step operation assumes all remote effects succeed even though failures can occur between steps.","expected_error":"The system may end in a partially completed business operation without an explicit recovery state.","cause":"Distributed operations were modeled as if they were one local atomic transaction.","root_cause":"Business consistency and failure semantics were not translated into architecture.","solution":"Define explicit transaction boundaries and a recovery strategy such as coordination, compensation, or controlled eventual consistency.","edge_case":"Some external actions are irreversible, so compensation may require a corrective business action rather than a literal technical rollback.","security_implication":"Compensating workflows must preserve authorization and auditability for every forward and corrective action.","performance_implication":"Cross-boundary coordination can increase latency and resource consumption; consistency requirements should justify the coordination cost.","test_case":"Fail the workflow after the first successful cross-boundary step and verify the defined recovery behavior.","test_input":{"failure_point":"after_debit_before_credit"},"expected_test_result":"The workflow enters a known recoverable state and executes or schedules the documented recovery path.","testing_guidance":"Test partial completion, retries, duplicate messages, timeout, compensation failure, operator intervention, and final convergence.","verification_method":"Workflow integration tests and state-machine verification against documented business invariants.","verification_guidance":"Verify every failure transition and confirm that no undocumented partial state can violate a mandatory invariant.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B021","D33-B023","D33-B024","D33-B032"],"depends_on":["D33-B021","D33-B023","D33-B024"],"requires":["D33-B021"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B021"],"specializes":["D33-B004"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed consistency and workflow architecture practice","Distributed systems transaction and compensation patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B043","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Workflow architecture","concept":"Explicit state-machine architecture for long-lived processes","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a business process be modeled as an explicit state machine rather than implicit control flow?","answer":"Use an explicit state-machine representation when a workflow is long-lived, interruptible, retryable, externally observable, subject to authorization gates, or capable of entering meaningful failure and recovery states. Define valid states, transitions, transition guards, side effects, persistence, and recovery behavior explicitly.","explanation":"Implicit workflow state scattered across flags, timestamps, database fields, and conditional branches becomes difficult to reason about as process complexity grows. An explicit state model makes legal transitions and recovery paths inspectable. It should be introduced when the workflow's state is itself a meaningful architectural concern, not for every short-lived function.","principle":"When workflow state affects correctness or recovery, make that state explicit.","objective":"Make long-running process behavior deterministic, recoverable, and verifiable.","requirements":["Workflow states are meaningful.","Transitions can be enumerated.","Invalid transitions must be rejected.","State persistence requirements are defined."],"constraints":["State machines add modeling and migration overhead.","Real-world workflows may require asynchronous external events.","State definitions must evolve compatibly.","Side effects must not be assumed atomic with state transitions without evidence."],"preconditions":["Workflow lifecycle is understood.","Failure and recovery scenarios are identified.","State ownership is defined."],"input":["Workflow states","Transition events","Guards","Side effects","Recovery requirements"],"output":"An explicit workflow state model with transition and recovery semantics.","expected_behavior":"Only documented transitions are accepted and workflow recovery can determine the current state without reconstructing it from unrelated side effects.","incorrect_code":"def process(order):\n    if order.flag:\n        do_next_step(order)\n","corrected_code":"ALLOWED = {"PENDING": {"APPROVED"}, "APPROVED": {"FULFILLED"}}\n\ndef transition(state, target):\n    if target not in ALLOWED.get(state, set()):\n        raise ValueError("invalid transition")\n    return target\n","failure_mode":"Workflow state is inferred from loosely related flags and side effects.","expected_error":"Invalid or contradictory workflow states can be accepted without a deterministic transition error.","cause":"The lifecycle has no explicit transition model.","root_cause":"Workflow state was treated as incidental data instead of a correctness boundary.","solution":"Model states and legal transitions explicitly when workflow complexity justifies it.","edge_case":"A workflow may receive a duplicate or delayed external event after it has already transitioned; the state model must define whether the event is ignored, rejected, or handled idempotently.","security_implication":"Authorization should be evaluated at security-sensitive transitions rather than inferred solely from the current state.","performance_implication":"Persisting explicit workflow state adds storage operations but can reduce expensive reconstruction and improve recovery determinism.","test_case":"Attempt an invalid state transition and verify rejection without modifying persisted state.","test_input":{"current_state":"PENDING","target_state":"FULFILLED"},"expected_test_result":"The transition is rejected because FULFILLED is not a legal direct transition from PENDING.","testing_guidance":"Test every valid transition, invalid transition, duplicate event, restart recovery, concurrent transition, and migration of persisted state.","verification_method":"State-transition tests and formal or model-based verification where workflow criticality justifies it.","verification_guidance":"Verify transition completeness, illegal-transition rejection, persistence semantics, recovery behavior, and authorization at sensitive transitions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_WORKFLOW_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B021","D33-B024","D33-B042"],"depends_on":["D33-B021","D33-B024"],"requires":["D33-B021"],"extends":["D33-B004"],"refines":["D33-B021"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established state-machine and workflow architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B044","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Resource lifecycle architecture","concept":"Explicit ownership and lifecycle of scarce resources","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle resources whose creation, allocation, expiration, and cleanup affect system correctness?","answer":"Model scarce or externally allocated resources with explicit ownership, lifecycle, expiration, cleanup, and recovery semantics. The architecture should define who acquires the resource, who owns it while active, what happens when ownership is lost, and how stale allocations are detected and safely reclaimed.","explanation":"Connections, leases, reservations, worker slots, locks, temporary storage, and external allocations can all become architectural resources. If lifecycle is implicit, failures can create leaks, stale ownership, or indefinite blocking. Explicit lifecycle semantics allow recovery mechanisms to distinguish active ownership from abandoned state.","principle":"Every scarce resource needs an explicit lifecycle and failure-recovery model.","objective":"Prevent resource leakage, stale ownership, and indefinite blocking across normal and failure paths.","requirements":["Resource ownership is identifiable.","Acquisition and release semantics are defined.","Expiration or lease behavior is defined where required.","Recovery from owner failure is specified."],"constraints":["Automatic reclamation can race with legitimate long-running work.","Lease duration must account for workload variability.","External resources may have provider-specific lifecycle semantics.","Cleanup operations must be safe to retry."],"preconditions":["Resource type and scarcity are known.","Failure modes affecting ownership are identified.","Cleanup authority is defined."],"input":["Resource definition","Owner identity","Lease duration","Cleanup policy","Failure scenarios"],"output":"A resource lifecycle architecture with acquisition, ownership, renewal, expiration, release, and recovery semantics.","expected_behavior":"Resources are released or safely reclaimed according to explicit lifecycle rules even when the original owner fails.","incorrect_code":"lock.acquire()\ndo_work()\nlock.release()\n","corrected_code":"lease = lock.acquire_with_lease()\ntry:\n    do_work()\nfinally:\n    lease.release()\n","failure_mode":"A process terminates before releasing a resource and no recovery mechanism exists.","expected_error":"Other workers may block indefinitely or resource capacity may remain unavailable.","cause":"Resource release depends exclusively on normal process completion.","root_cause":"Failure-path ownership and reclamation were omitted from the architecture.","solution":"Use explicit lifecycle semantics such as leases, timeouts, ownership records, or recovery processes appropriate to the resource.","edge_case":"A lease can expire while work is still executing, causing concurrent owners unless renewal and fencing semantics are correctly designed.","security_implication":"Resource ownership must be authenticated and authorization must prevent one actor from releasing or modifying another actor's allocation without permission.","performance_implication":"Lease renewal and ownership checks add overhead but may be necessary to prevent stale resource allocation.","test_case":"Terminate the resource owner before release and verify that the resource becomes safely reclaimable.","test_input":{"owner":"worker-A","failure":"owner_terminated"},"expected_test_result":"The resource enters a defined recovery or expiration state and can be safely reclaimed without corrupting active work.","testing_guidance":"Test owner failure, timeout, renewal race, duplicate release, cleanup failure, restart, and concurrent acquisition.","verification_method":"Lifecycle tests plus concurrency and failure-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every resource lifecycle state and abnormal termination path has a defined outcome.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_MANAGEMENT_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B023","D33-B024","D33-B028"],"depends_on":["D33-B023"],"requires":["D33-B023"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B023"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established concurrency, lease, and resource lifecycle architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B045","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Migration architecture","concept":"Expand-migrate-contract database and interface evolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_EVOLUTION_DECISION","question":"How should architecture support schema or contract changes when old and new versions must coexist temporarily?","answer":"Use a staged compatibility architecture when migration cannot occur atomically. Introduce the new representation while preserving the old path, migrate or dual-write carefully where justified, switch consumers, verify convergence, and remove the legacy path only after explicit exit criteria are satisfied.","explanation":"Large systems often cannot update every consumer and data record simultaneously. A staged migration reduces coordination requirements but creates temporary complexity. The architecture must define compatibility direction, migration ownership, consistency checks, rollback behavior, and the conditions under which transitional code can be removed.","principle":"Temporary compatibility must have explicit entry and exit conditions.","objective":"Enable safe incremental evolution without leaving permanent ambiguous dual representations.","requirements":["Old and new representations are identified.","Compatibility behavior is explicit.","Migration progress can be measured.","Rollback and exit criteria are defined."],"constraints":["Dual-read or dual-write strategies can create divergence.","Migration code increases temporary complexity.","Rollback may become impossible after irreversible data transformation.","Long-lived transitional states create maintenance risk."],"preconditions":["Current schema or contract is known.","Target representation is defined.","Migration dependencies are identified."],"input":["Current representation","Target representation","Consumer versions","Migration strategy","Rollback requirements"],"output":"A staged migration architecture with compatibility, migration, verification, cutover, rollback, and cleanup stages.","expected_behavior":"Old and new consumers can coexist only for the explicitly defined migration period, and the system has evidence that the target representation is complete before legacy removal.","incorrect_code":"def read_customer(data):\n    return data["name"]\n","corrected_code":"def read_customer(data):\n    if "display_name" in data:\n        return data["display_name"]\n    return data["name"]\n","failure_mode":"A new schema is deployed without a compatibility path for still-active consumers or records.","expected_error":"Older consumers or unmigrated records can fail when the old field or contract disappears.","cause":"Migration was treated as a single deployment event.","root_cause":"The architecture did not model coexistence as a temporary system state.","solution":"Define staged compatibility, migration verification, cutover, and explicit legacy-removal criteria.","edge_case":"A fallback reader can hide incomplete migration indefinitely; migration completeness must therefore be measured independently.","security_implication":"Temporary compatibility paths must preserve authorization, validation, and data-protection semantics and should be removed when no longer required.","performance_implication":"Dual reads, writes, or transformations can increase latency and storage cost during migration.","test_case":"Run old and new consumer versions concurrently during the migration window and verify both supported paths.","test_input":{"consumer_versions":["v1","v2"],"data_versions":["legacy","target"]},"expected_test_result":"All explicitly supported combinations behave according to the migration compatibility contract.","testing_guidance":"Test mixed-version deployment, partial migration, rollback, duplicate migration execution, data divergence, and legacy cleanup.","verification_method":"Migration integration tests, data reconciliation, compatibility tests, and deployment verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify migration completeness before disabling compatibility and verify that legacy code is actually removed after exit criteria are met.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_EVOLUTION_AND_MIGRATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B025","D33-B026","D33-B030"],"depends_on":["D33-B025","D33-B026"],"requires":["D33-B026"],"extends":["D33-B026"],"refines":["D33-B026"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established expand-migrate-contract and compatibility migration practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B046","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Capability composition","concept":"Composition boundary for independently owned capabilities","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture compose multiple capabilities when no single component should own all of their internal business logic?","answer":"Create an explicit composition boundary when a use case requires coordination across independently owned capabilities. The composition layer should orchestrate interactions, enforce workflow-level policies, and avoid duplicating the internal domain rules owned by participating capabilities.","explanation":"A composition layer can provide a coherent use-case interface without collapsing independent capabilities into one implementation. However, it becomes harmful when it absorbs domain logic that should remain with the owning capability. The architecture should distinguish orchestration from domain ownership.","principle":"Compose capabilities at their boundaries without transferring ownership of their internal rules.","objective":"Provide coherent cross-capability workflows while preserving clear ownership and reducing domain duplication.","requirements":["Capability ownership is explicit.","Cross-capability use cases are identified.","Composition responsibilities are defined.","Domain ownership remains unambiguous."],"constraints":["Composition can become a bottleneck or orchestration monolith.","Cross-capability calls increase failure surface.","Duplicated rules can diverge.","The composition layer may require distributed consistency handling."],"preconditions":["Capabilities have stable boundaries.","Use-case dependencies are documented.","Ownership rules are defined."],"input":["Use cases","Capability boundaries","Interaction requirements","Failure semantics","Authorization requirements"],"output":"A composition architecture specifying orchestration responsibility and preserving domain ownership.","expected_behavior":"Cross-capability workflows are coordinated without duplicating or silently overriding capability-owned business rules.","incorrect_code":"def process_order(order):\n    validate_customer(order)\n    calculate_inventory_rules(order)\n    apply_billing_rules(order)\n","corrected_code":"def process_order(order):\n    customer = customer_service.validate(order.customer_id)\n    inventory = inventory_service.reserve(order.items)\n    billing = billing_service.authorize(order.amount)\n    return workflow_result(customer, inventory, billing)\n","failure_mode":"A central orchestrator duplicates rules from several domains and becomes responsible for their internal semantics.","expected_error":"No required runtime exception; behavior diverges when individual capability rules evolve independently.","cause":"Composition and domain ownership were conflated.","root_cause":"The architecture lacked an explicit distinction between orchestration and capability-owned policy.","solution":"Keep domain decisions within their owning capabilities and use the composition layer for coordination and workflow concerns.","edge_case":"A cross-capability policy may genuinely belong to the workflow boundary; ownership should be decided explicitly rather than assumed from where code happens to execute.","security_implication":"The composition layer must not become a privilege escalation path; each capability should authorize sensitive operations according to its own ownership boundary.","performance_implication":"Sequential composition can increase latency; parallelization is possible only where dependency and consistency requirements permit it.","test_case":"Change a capability-owned rule and verify that the composition workflow automatically consumes the updated behavior without duplicated rule changes.","test_input":{"changed_capability":"billing","changed_rule":"authorization_policy"},"expected_test_result":"The workflow reflects the capability's updated policy without requiring duplicated orchestration logic.","testing_guidance":"Test capability failures, partial success, authorization, ordering, parallel execution where applicable, and rule ownership boundaries.","verification_method":"Architecture dependency review plus integration tests across capability boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each decision is owned by exactly one appropriate capability unless shared ownership is explicitly designed.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MODULAR_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B003","D33-B009","D33-B041","D33-B042"],"depends_on":["D33-B003","D33-B009"],"requires":["D33-B009"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B003"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established modular and domain-oriented architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B047","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Read/write path architecture","concept":"Separate read optimization from authoritative write ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when read requirements differ substantially from write requirements while the system still needs authoritative write ownership?","answer":"Separate read optimization from authoritative write ownership when read patterns, scale, representation, or latency requirements materially differ from transactional write behavior. Read models, projections, caches, or query-specific representations may be introduced, but the architecture must define source-of-truth ownership, freshness, rebuild behavior, and consistency expectations.","explanation":"Read optimization does not require duplicating business ownership. A specialized read model can serve complex queries while the authoritative write model remains responsible for transactional invariants. The architecture must make the relationship explicit so that consumers do not accidentally treat derived read data as authoritative state.","principle":"Optimize read representation without creating ambiguous ownership of authoritative state.","objective":"Support specialized read workloads while preserving a single explicit authority for mutable business state.","requirements":["Authoritative write ownership is defined.","Read workload characteristics justify specialization.","Read freshness is specified.","Read-model rebuild and failure behavior are defined."],"constraints":["Derived read models can become stale.","Additional projections increase operational complexity.","Rebuild processes must be correct and repeatable.","Consumers must understand consistency semantics."],"preconditions":["Write source of truth exists.","Read workload characteristics are known.","Data derivation relationship is defined."],"input":["Write model","Read patterns","Freshness requirements","Query complexity","Scaling requirements"],"output":"An architecture separating authoritative write ownership from optimized read representations with explicit synchronization semantics.","expected_behavior":"Read consumers receive optimized representations without treating derived data as authoritative mutable state.","incorrect_code":"def update_order(order):\n    cache[order.id] = order\n    return database.update(order)\n","corrected_code":"def update_order(order):\n    result = database.update(order)\n    publish_order_changed(result.id)\n    return result\n","failure_mode":"A cache or read representation is updated as if it were authoritative state before the source of truth is successfully changed.","expected_error":"Readers can observe state that never became authoritative.","cause":"Read optimization was incorrectly treated as part of write ownership.","root_cause":"The architecture did not distinguish source-of-truth state from derived state.","solution":"Make the authoritative write path primary and derive read representations through explicitly defined synchronization semantics.","edge_case":"A stale read may be acceptable for one consumer but unacceptable for another; freshness must therefore be part of the consumer requirement rather than globally assumed.","security_implication":"Derived read stores must enforce access controls appropriate to their data even if they are not authoritative.","performance_implication":"Read models can reduce expensive query work but introduce synchronization and storage overhead.","test_case":"Fail the authoritative write and verify that no derived representation claims a successful state change.","test_input":{"write_result":"FAILED","projection_update":"ATTEMPTED"},"expected_test_result":"The architecture prevents an unsuccessful authoritative write from being represented as a successful business state.","testing_guidance":"Test stale reads, projection delay, rebuild, duplicate updates, source-of-truth failure, and authorization consistency.","verification_method":"Integration tests and source-of-truth/read-model traceability review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every mutable business fact has an explicit authority and every derived representation can be rebuilt or reconciled where required.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_READ_MODEL_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B005","D33-B022","D33-B033"],"depends_on":["D33-B005"],"requires":["D33-B005"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B022"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established CQRS and derived read-model architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B048","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"External dependency architecture","concept":"Anti-corruption boundary for incompatible external models","knowledge_type":"INTEGRATION_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture protect internal domain concepts when an external system exposes a materially different data or behavioral model?","answer":"Introduce an explicit translation or anti-corruption boundary when an external model should not become the internal domain model. The boundary maps external concepts, validation, errors, and lifecycle semantics into internally owned concepts while preventing external naming and behavior from leaking throughout the system.","explanation":"External systems frequently encode concepts for their own purposes. Directly adopting those concepts internally can make the architecture dependent on vendor semantics and make future replacement difficult. A translation boundary is justified when semantic mismatch or ownership differences are substantial enough to create ongoing coupling.","principle":"Translate across semantic boundaries rather than allowing incompatible external models to become internal architecture.","objective":"Preserve internal domain ownership while integrating with externally governed systems.","requirements":["External and internal models are compared.","Ownership differences are identified.","Translation rules are explicit.","External failure semantics are mapped deliberately."],"constraints":["Translation adds implementation and testing cost.","Some external semantics cannot be perfectly represented internally.","Mappings must evolve when the external contract changes.","Over-translation can create unnecessary abstraction."],"preconditions":["External contract is known.","Internal domain model is defined.","Semantic mismatches are identified."],"input":["External model","Internal model","Integration contract","Failure semantics","Ownership boundaries"],"output":"An integration boundary with explicit mapping, validation, error translation, and lifecycle semantics.","expected_behavior":"External model changes remain localized to the integration boundary unless the internal business requirement itself changes.","incorrect_code":"def create_order(external_order):\n    return internal_order_service.create(external_order)\n","corrected_code":"def create_order(external_order):\n    internal = external_order_mapper.to_internal(external_order)\n    return internal_order_service.create(internal)\n","failure_mode":"External data structures are passed directly into internal domain operations.","expected_error":"No required immediate exception; external semantics can leak into business logic and create long-term coupling.","cause":"Integration boundary performs transport without semantic translation.","root_cause":"External ownership and internal ownership were not modeled as distinct architecture concerns.","solution":"Introduce a focused translation boundary where semantic mismatch justifies it.","edge_case":"A trivial external representation may be compatible enough to map directly; introducing a large translation layer in that case adds unnecessary complexity.","security_implication":"The boundary should validate external input and prevent external data from bypassing internal authorization and integrity rules.","performance_implication":"Mapping introduces processing overhead but can reduce the long-term cost of external-model coupling.","test_case":"Change an external field representation while preserving the internal business concept and verify only the adapter requires modification.","test_input":{"external_change":"customerName_renamed","internal_field":"customer_name"},"expected_test_result":"The integration adapter absorbs the external change while internal domain behavior remains unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Test malformed external data, unknown fields, version changes, semantic mismatches, external failures, and mapping completeness.","verification_method":"Contract tests against the external boundary plus internal domain tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that internal code does not depend directly on external model types where the architecture requires semantic isolation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_INTEGRATION_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B025","D33-B041"],"depends_on":["D33-B009"],"requires":["D33-B025"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B009"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established anti-corruption layer and integration-boundary practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B049","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture evolution","concept":"Fitness functions and architecture guardrails","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How should architecture requirements that must remain true over time be enforced after the initial architecture decision?","answer":"Translate durable architecture requirements into automated or reviewable architecture guardrails where practical. Examples include dependency-direction rules, forbidden package dependencies, API compatibility checks, security invariants, performance budgets, or deployment constraints. Guardrails should verify explicit requirements rather than enforce arbitrary structural preferences.","explanation":"Architecture can degrade after the initial design through incremental changes. Documentation alone does not prevent drift. Architecture fitness functions and guardrails provide recurring evidence that important properties remain true. They should be tied to requirements and designed to avoid blocking legitimate evolution without justification.","principle":"Important architectural properties should be continuously verifiable rather than remembered only through documentation.","objective":"Detect architectural drift before it becomes embedded in implementation or deployment behavior.","requirements":["Architectural properties are measurable or mechanically checkable.","Each guardrail has an identified requirement or risk.","Violations have an explicit response process.","Guardrails can evolve with the architecture."],"constraints":["Not every architecture property is mechanically measurable.","Overly rigid rules can prevent legitimate design evolution.","Checks must remain maintainable.","Passing a structural check does not prove complete architectural correctness."],"preconditions":["Critical architecture properties are identified.","Verification mechanisms are available or can be developed.","Ownership of violations is defined."],"input":["Architecture requirements","Dependency rules","Security constraints","Performance budgets","Compatibility rules"],"output":"A set of architecture guardrails connected to explicit requirements and integrated into appropriate validation workflows.","expected_behavior":"Relevant architectural regressions are detected automatically or through defined review gates before they become accepted system behavior.","incorrect_code":"from forbidden_internal_module import implementation\n","corrected_code":"from approved_public_interface import capability\n","failure_mode":"A module introduces a dependency that violates the intended architectural boundary.","expected_error":"The architecture test or static dependency check should identify the violation before acceptance.","cause":"Architecture boundary exists only as documentation.","root_cause":"Critical architectural constraints were not translated into recurring verification mechanisms.","solution":"Implement a focused guardrail tied to the documented architectural requirement.","edge_case":"A legitimate exception may exist; exceptions should be explicit, reviewed, and narrowly scoped rather than silently disabling the guardrail.","security_implication":"Security architecture guardrails can detect prohibited dependencies or unsafe configuration paths but should complement, not replace, security testing.","performance_implication":"Guardrails add CI or validation cost; prioritize checks that protect materially important architectural properties.","test_case":"Introduce a known forbidden dependency and run the architecture validation rule.","test_input":{"dependency":"forbidden_internal_module"},"expected_test_result":"Validation identifies the dependency violation and prevents silent architectural drift.","testing_guidance":"Test both positive and negative cases and verify that legitimate exceptions are explicit and auditable.","verification_method":"Static architecture tests, dependency graph analysis, contract validation, and review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every high-value guardrail traces to a requirement and that passing the guardrail is not incorrectly interpreted as complete system verification.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B030","D33-B040"],"depends_on":["D33-B030"],"requires":["D33-B030"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B030"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture fitness function and architecture governance practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B050","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture closure and change control","concept":"Architecture baseline, decision ownership, and controlled evolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture remain changeable without allowing uncontrolled structural drift after its initial selection?","answer":"Establish an explicit architecture baseline containing accepted decisions, boundaries, assumptions, known limitations, and verification status. Define ownership for architecture changes and require significant changes to identify affected requirements, dependencies, risks, and verification evidence. Architecture should evolve through controlled decisions rather than becoming frozen or changing implicitly through implementation commits.","explanation":"Architecture is neither permanently fixed nor safely changed without governance. A useful baseline records what is intentionally true at a point in time and makes change impact visible. Change control should be proportional to risk: small local changes may need lightweight review, while changes to trust, data ownership, consistency, deployment, or public contracts may require formal architectural review.","principle":"Architecture should be governed as an evolving evidence-backed system of decisions.","objective":"Preserve architectural integrity while allowing justified evolution as requirements and evidence change.","requirements":["Architecture baseline is identifiable.","Decision ownership is defined.","Significant change categories are defined.","Affected requirements and verification evidence are traceable.","Superseded decisions remain historically recoverable."],"constraints":["Excessive governance can slow legitimate engineering work.","Insufficient governance allows structural drift.","Architecture documentation can become stale without ownership.","Not every implementation change requires an architecture decision."],"preconditions":["Current architecture decisions are documented.","Requirement relationships exist.","Architecture verification status is known."],"input":["Architecture baseline","Change proposal","Affected requirements","Dependency graph","Verification evidence"],"output":"A controlled architecture evolution process with baseline, ownership, impact analysis, decision records, and verification updates.","expected_behavior":"Significant architecture changes update the appropriate decision records and verification evidence rather than silently changing architectural assumptions.","incorrect_code":"def deploy_change(change):\n    deploy(change)\n","corrected_code":"def deploy_change(change, architecture_review):\n    architecture_review.verify_impact(change)\n    deploy(change)\n","failure_mode":"A structurally significant change is deployed without evaluating its impact on architecture requirements or boundaries.","expected_error":"No required immediate exception; architectural drift accumulates until a later failure exposes the inconsistency.","cause":"Architecture change governance is disconnected from implementation change.","root_cause":"The architecture baseline has no controlled relationship to change management.","solution":"Define change thresholds, ownership, impact analysis, and verification updates for architecture-significant changes.","edge_case":"An emergency production change may require expedited approval; it should still produce a retrospective architecture and verification record.","security_implication":"Changes to trust boundaries, authorization architecture, secrets handling, or external exposure require explicit security impact assessment.","performance_implication":"Architecture changes affecting scaling, data paths, or resource boundaries require performance-impact analysis rather than assumption-based approval.","test_case":"Submit a change that crosses a documented architecture boundary and verify that the change process requires impact and verification evidence.","test_input":{"change_type":"new_external_integration","crosses_boundary":true},"expected_test_result":"The change is classified as architecture-significant and requires the defined review and verification path.","testing_guidance":"Test normal, architecture-significant, emergency, rollback, and superseding-decision workflows.","verification_method":"Architecture governance audit and change-control integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify baseline versioning, decision ownership, supersession links, requirement impact, security/performance review, and updated verification evidence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_AND_ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B010","D33-B030","D33-B040","D33-B049"],"depends_on":["D33-B030","D33-B040"],"requires":["D33-B030"],"extends":["D33-B040"],"refines":["D33-B049"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030","D33-B049"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture description and evolution principles","Established architecture governance and Architecture Decision Record practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B051","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Security boundary architecture","concept":"Trust-zone decomposition from security requirements","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture translate security requirements into explicit trust boundaries rather than relying only on application-level checks?","answer":"Decompose the system into trust zones according to asset sensitivity, actor trust, administrative authority, network exposure, and required controls. Define which components may communicate across each boundary, what authentication and authorization are required, and what data or capabilities may cross the boundary.","explanation":"Security requirements become stronger architectural constraints when trust boundaries are explicit. A system that treats all internal components as equally trusted can allow compromise or misconfiguration in one component to spread across the system. Trust zones should therefore reflect actual authority and asset sensitivity rather than network location alone.","principle":"Security boundaries should follow trust and asset sensitivity, not merely deployment topology.","objective":"Limit unauthorized access and reduce the blast radius of compromised or misbehaving components.","requirements":["Sensitive assets are identified.","Actors and component trust levels are classified.","Trust boundaries are explicit.","Cross-boundary authentication and authorization are defined."],"constraints":["More trust boundaries increase operational and communication complexity.","Internal network location must not automatically imply trust.","Some legacy systems may not support required controls.","Security boundaries require continuous validation as architecture evolves."],"preconditions":["Assets and actors are identified.","Security requirements are documented.","Component communication paths are known."],"input":["Asset classification","Actor identities","Trust levels","Communication paths","Security controls"],"output":"A trust-zone architecture defining boundaries, allowed flows, authentication, authorization, and containment expectations.","expected_behavior":"Cross-boundary access is explicitly authorized and unauthorized flows are denied or detected according to the security requirement.","incorrect_code":"def internal_request(service):\n    return service.execute()\n","corrected_code":"def internal_request(service, identity, policy):\n    policy.authorize(identity, service.required_capability)\n    return service.execute()\n","failure_mode":"Internal reachability is treated as sufficient authorization.","expected_error":"Unauthorized internal callers may access capabilities that should be restricted by trust or role.","cause":"Network or process location was used as a proxy for trust.","root_cause":"Security requirements were not translated into explicit architectural trust boundaries.","solution":"Define trust zones and enforce authentication, authorization, and capability restrictions at the relevant boundaries.","edge_case":"A component may operate in multiple trust contexts depending on the caller; authorization must therefore be evaluated per request rather than inferred solely from component identity.","security_implication":"This record directly defines security architecture: least privilege, explicit trust boundaries, authenticated communication, authorization, and containment are required.","performance_implication":"Additional authentication and authorization checks add overhead but can be reduced through appropriate caching without weakening revocation semantics.","test_case":"Attempt a cross-zone operation without the required capability and verify denial.","test_input":{"caller_zone":"standard_application","target_zone":"restricted_service","capability":"restricted_operation","authorized":false},"expected_test_result":"The request is denied and an auditable security event is produced where required.","testing_guidance":"Test allowed and denied flows, identity spoofing attempts, privilege changes, service compromise assumptions, and boundary bypass paths.","verification_method":"Threat-model review, access-control tests, architecture flow analysis, and security validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify every cross-zone flow has an explicit owner, authentication mechanism, authorization rule, and data/capability classification.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B028","D33-B037","D33-B050"],"depends_on":["D33-B028"],"requires":["D33-B028"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B028"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B050"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established zero-trust and trust-boundary architecture practice","OWASP secure architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B052","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Privacy architecture","concept":"Data minimization and purpose-bound data flow","knowledge_type":"PRIVACY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when privacy requirements restrict what personal or sensitive data may be collected, retained, or propagated?","answer":"Model privacy requirements as explicit data-flow and lifecycle constraints. Collect only data necessary for defined purposes, minimize propagation across boundaries, define retention and deletion behavior, and ensure derived or replicated stores inherit appropriate privacy controls. Architecture should distinguish operational necessity from convenience-driven data collection.","explanation":"Privacy can be undermined even when individual components are secure if the architecture unnecessarily copies sensitive data across systems. Data minimization therefore belongs in architecture: the system should identify where sensitive data originates, where it flows, why each copy exists, how long it remains, and how deletion or correction propagates.","principle":"Data should cross architectural boundaries only when its purpose and lifecycle justify the transfer.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary sensitive-data exposure while preserving required business functionality.","requirements":["Sensitive data categories are identified.","Purpose of each collection and transfer is defined.","Retention and deletion requirements are explicit.","Derived and replicated data paths are included."],"constraints":["Some business processes require retained historical records.","Deletion can be difficult across asynchronous or replicated stores.","Legal and organizational requirements vary by jurisdiction and system context.","Data minimization must not break required business invariants."],"preconditions":["Data inventory exists.","Data purposes are documented.","Retention requirements are known."],"input":["Data classifications","Business purposes","Data flows","Retention requirements","Deletion requirements"],"output":"A privacy-aware data architecture specifying collection, propagation, retention, deletion, and access boundaries.","expected_behavior":"Sensitive data is collected and propagated only where required by an identified purpose and is subject to explicit lifecycle controls.","incorrect_code":"def create_customer(request):\n    customer = request.json\n    database.save(customer)\n","corrected_code":"def create_customer(request):\n    customer = {\n        "name": request.json["name"],\n        "contact": request.json["contact"]\n    }\n    database.save(customer)\n","failure_mode":"The system persists the entire incoming request even when only a subset is required.","expected_error":"No runtime exception is required; the architectural failure is unnecessary sensitive-data retention.","cause":"Input payload and persistence model are treated as identical.","root_cause":"Data minimization was not represented as an architectural requirement.","solution":"Define an explicit data mapping that persists only required attributes and document their purpose and lifecycle.","edge_case":"Operational debugging may tempt teams to retain full payloads; sensitive logging and diagnostic storage must follow the same minimization requirements.","security_implication":"Reducing data copies lowers exposure, but every retained copy still requires appropriate access control, encryption, auditability, and deletion behavior.","performance_implication":"Data minimization can reduce storage, serialization, replication, and query costs, although transformation and filtering add small processing overhead.","test_case":"Submit an input containing an unnecessary sensitive field and verify that the field is not persisted or propagated unless explicitly required.","test_input":{"input":{"name":"Example","contact":"example@example.invalid","unnecessary_sensitive_field":"restricted"},"required_fields":["name","contact"]},"expected_test_result":"Only required fields are persisted and the unnecessary sensitive field is absent from downstream representations.","testing_guidance":"Test collection, storage, replication, logs, caches, backups, derived stores, deletion, and correction propagation.","verification_method":"Data-flow review, privacy impact analysis, storage inspection, and lifecycle tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every sensitive-data copy has a documented purpose, owner, retention policy, and access-control model.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRIVACY_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B033","D33-B051"],"depends_on":["D33-B009"],"requires":["D33-B009"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B009"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B051"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established data minimization and privacy-by-design practice","Privacy engineering principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B053","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Audit architecture","concept":"Tamper-aware audit trail for security and business decisions","knowledge_type":"AUDIT_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When requirements demand accountability for important actions, how should audit architecture be separated from ordinary application logging?","answer":"Define an explicit audit trail for actions whose accountability, compliance, security, or business significance requires durable evidence. Audit records should identify actor or service identity, action, target, relevant context, time, outcome, and integrity requirements. Ordinary diagnostic logs should remain distinct because their lifecycle and evidentiary requirements differ.","explanation":"Operational logs are often optimized for troubleshooting and retention flexibility, while audit records may need stronger integrity, access control, retention, and traceability. Mixing them can result in either excessive retention of diagnostics or insufficient protection of important audit evidence. Architecture should therefore define the purpose and lifecycle of each record type.","principle":"Evidence of consequential actions requires stronger ownership and integrity semantics than ordinary diagnostics.","objective":"Provide trustworthy accountability for security-sensitive, administrative, financial, or otherwise consequential actions.","requirements":["Auditable actions are explicitly classified.","Required audit fields are defined.","Audit integrity and access controls are specified.","Retention and review requirements are documented."],"constraints":["Audit volume can become substantial.","Audit records may contain sensitive information.","Strong immutability mechanisms can add storage and operational complexity.","Audit evidence must remain useful without becoming an unrestricted data dump."],"preconditions":["Actions requiring accountability are identified.","Actor identity semantics are defined.","Retention requirements are known."],"input":["Action taxonomy","Actor identity","Target resource","Outcome","Context requirements"],"output":"An audit architecture with defined event schema, storage, integrity, access, retention, and review mechanisms.","expected_behavior":"Consequential actions produce durable audit evidence sufficient to reconstruct who or what acted, on what, when, and with what outcome.","incorrect_code":"logger.info("admin changed permissions")\n","corrected_code":"audit.record({\n    "actor_id": actor.id,\n    "action": "permission_change",\n    "target_id": target.id,\n    "outcome": "success"\n})\n","failure_mode":"A security-significant action is represented only by an unstructured diagnostic message.","expected_error":"No required runtime exception; the failure is loss of reliable accountability and insufficient audit context.","cause":"Diagnostic logging was used as a substitute for an audit architecture.","root_cause":"Auditability was not modeled as a distinct system requirement.","solution":"Create an explicit audit record with controlled schema, integrity, access, retention, and review semantics.","edge_case":"An action may partially succeed or trigger downstream effects; the audit model should distinguish requested, executed, and verified outcomes where the requirement requires it.","security_implication":"Audit records themselves are security-sensitive and require restricted write/read access, integrity protection, and protection against unauthorized alteration or deletion.","performance_implication":"Synchronous durable audit writes can add latency; asynchronous mechanisms may be appropriate only if the audit requirement permits temporary buffering and defined failure behavior.","test_case":"Execute an auditable action and verify that the audit record contains the required actor, action, target, time, and outcome information.","test_input":{"action":"permission_change","actor":"admin-1","target":"resource-1","outcome":"success"},"expected_test_result":"A complete audit record is created according to the defined audit schema and lifecycle.","testing_guidance":"Test successful actions, denied actions, partial failures, audit-store unavailability, duplicate records, clock issues, access restrictions, and retention behavior.","verification_method":"Audit schema tests, access-control tests, integrity validation, and end-to-end action tracing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that audit evidence can reconstruct consequential actions without relying solely on mutable diagnostic logs.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_AUDIT_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B030","D33-B050","D33-B051"],"depends_on":["D33-B030"],"requires":["D33-B030"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B030"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established security audit and accountability architecture practice","OWASP logging and monitoring principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B054","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Observability architecture","concept":"Cross-boundary correlation and causal telemetry","knowledge_type":"OBSERVABILITY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture provide observability when one business operation crosses multiple components or asynchronous boundaries?","answer":"Define a telemetry architecture that preserves correlation across synchronous and asynchronous boundaries and connects technical events to business operations. Correlation identifiers, structured logs, metrics, traces, and outcome information should be designed as related evidence rather than independent streams.","explanation":"Distributed systems are difficult to diagnose when individual components produce isolated telemetry. Architecture should make it possible to reconstruct an operation across boundaries and distinguish symptoms from the originating cause. Business context is important because infrastructure health alone may not show that a business operation failed or degraded.","principle":"Observability should preserve causal and business context across architectural boundaries.","objective":"Enable reliable diagnosis of cross-component behavior and distinguish infrastructure symptoms from business outcomes.","requirements":["Cross-boundary operations have correlation semantics.","Telemetry is structured.","Important business outcomes are observable.","Asynchronous boundaries preserve sufficient correlation context."],"constraints":["High-cardinality telemetry can increase cost.","Correlation metadata must not expose sensitive information.","Sampling can omit evidence needed for rare failures.","Telemetry systems themselves can fail or become overloaded."],"preconditions":["Operationally important workflows are identified.","Component boundaries are known.","Required diagnostic outcomes are defined."],"input":["Business operation identifiers","Component events","Metrics","Traces","Logs","Outcome data"],"output":"An observability architecture linking distributed technical telemetry to business operations and outcomes.","expected_behavior":"An operator can trace a significant operation across relevant boundaries and identify where behavior diverged from expectations.","incorrect_code":"logger.info("payment failed")\n","corrected_code":"logger.info("payment failed", extra={"operation_id": operation_id, "payment_id": payment_id, "outcome": "failed"})\n","failure_mode":"A diagnostic event lacks enough context to associate it with the originating operation or related component activity.","expected_error":"No application exception is required; diagnosis becomes ambiguous or excessively manual.","cause":"Telemetry was designed per component rather than per end-to-end operation.","root_cause":"Observability requirements were not included in architecture boundary design.","solution":"Define correlation propagation and structured telemetry semantics across the complete workflow.","edge_case":"Correlation identifiers can cross trust or privacy boundaries; propagation rules must define what identifiers are safe to expose.","security_implication":"Telemetry may contain sensitive identifiers or data and therefore requires access controls, minimization, retention policies, and redaction.","performance_implication":"Tracing and detailed telemetry add CPU, storage, and network overhead; sampling and aggregation should be designed around diagnostic requirements.","test_case":"Execute a multi-component workflow and verify that telemetry from each component can be correlated to the same operation.","test_input":{"operation_id":"op-001","components":["api","service","worker"]},"expected_test_result":"Relevant telemetry contains compatible correlation information and can reconstruct the operation path.","testing_guidance":"Test synchronous calls, asynchronous messages, retries, failures, sampled traces, missing telemetry, and correlation across deployment boundaries.","verification_method":"End-to-end observability tests and telemetry schema validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify correlation continuity, sensitive-data handling, retention, and linkage between technical signals and business outcomes.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B029","D33-B032","D33-B043"],"depends_on":["D33-B029"],"requires":["D33-B029"],"extends":["D33-B029"],"refines":["D33-B029"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed observability and telemetry architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B055","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Failure containment","concept":"Graceful degradation and dependency isolation","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when a non-critical dependency fails but the primary business capability should remain partially available?","answer":"Identify which capabilities can degrade independently and define explicit fallback, reduced-functionality, timeout, isolation, and recovery behavior. Use bounded dependency failures, circuit-breaking or equivalent containment mechanisms where appropriate, and ensure that degraded behavior does not silently violate critical business or security invariants.","explanation":"A resilient system does not necessarily keep every feature working during every dependency failure. Instead, it distinguishes critical from non-critical capabilities and defines acceptable degraded modes. The architecture should make this behavior intentional rather than allowing accidental timeouts or cascading failures to determine user-visible behavior.","principle":"Graceful degradation requires explicit acceptable behavior under dependency failure.","objective":"Preserve critical capabilities while containing non-critical dependency failures and avoiding cascading outages.","requirements":["Critical and non-critical capabilities are identified.","Dependency failure behavior is specified.","Fallback semantics are explicit.","Critical invariants remain enforced during degradation."],"constraints":["Fallback behavior may provide less functionality or lower freshness.","Silent fallback can conceal important failures.","Circuit breakers and timeouts require careful tuning.","Some capabilities cannot safely degrade."],"preconditions":["Dependency graph is known.","Business criticality is classified.","Failure modes are modeled."],"input":["Dependency graph","Capability criticality","Failure modes","Fallback options","Service objectives"],"output":"A degradation architecture defining isolation, fallback, timeout, user-visible behavior, and recovery semantics.","expected_behavior":"Failure of a non-critical dependency does not unnecessarily disable independent critical capabilities and does not silently violate required invariants.","incorrect_code":"def get_recommendations(user):\n    return recommendation_service.get(user)\n","corrected_code":"def get_recommendations(user):\n    try:\n        return recommendation_service.get(user, timeout=1.0)\n    except TimeoutError:\n        return []\n","failure_mode":"A non-critical dependency blocks the primary request indefinitely or causes the entire capability to fail.","expected_error":"Requests may accumulate until thread, worker, or connection resources are exhausted.","cause":"Dependency failure has no bounded timeout or degradation behavior.","root_cause":"Failure containment was not represented as an architectural requirement.","solution":"Define timeout, isolation, and acceptable degraded behavior for the dependency.","edge_case":"An empty fallback can be misleading if the consumer interprets it as authoritative absence; degraded responses must be distinguishable where required.","security_implication":"Fallback paths must enforce the same authorization and security invariants as primary paths.","performance_implication":"Timeouts and circuit breakers can protect resources but may temporarily reduce functionality while the dependency recovers.","test_case":"Make the non-critical dependency unavailable and verify that the primary capability remains within its defined degraded behavior.","test_input":{"dependency":"recommendation_service","availability":"unavailable"},"expected_test_result":"The primary operation completes within the defined timeout and returns the documented degraded result without violating security or business invariants.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, repeated failure, recovery, fallback correctness, dependency overload, partial responses, and circuit reset behavior.","verification_method":"Failure-injection testing and resilience architecture review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every fallback is intentional, safe, observable, and distinguishable from a normal authoritative result when necessary.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESILIENCE_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B008","D33-B028","D33-B038","D33-B054"],"depends_on":["D33-B028"],"requires":["D33-B028"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B028"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established resilience and fault-containment architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B056","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment independence","concept":"Independent deployability as an explicit architecture requirement","knowledge_type":"DEPLOYMENT_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does a requirement for independent deployment justify separating components into independently deployable units?","answer":"Separate deployment units when independent release cadence, ownership, scaling, isolation, or failure containment provides measurable value that outweighs the additional operational complexity. Do not equate logical modularity with separate deployment; a modular monolith can provide strong boundaries without distributed deployment when independent deployment is not a real requirement.","explanation":"Independent deployment is a specific capability, not a synonym for good architecture. Separate deployables introduce network boundaries, version compatibility, deployment coordination, observability, and operational overhead. The requirement should identify why independent deployment matters and what organizational or runtime benefit it provides.","principle":"Create deployment boundaries only when independent deployment is itself a meaningful requirement.","objective":"Align physical deployment boundaries with genuine ownership, release, scaling, or isolation requirements.","requirements":["Independent deployment need is explicit.","Component ownership is clear.","Compatibility between independently deployed units is defined.","Operational capability supports the deployment model."],"constraints":["More deployables increase operational surface area.","Network failures become part of normal execution.","Version skew must be supported.","Deployment and observability complexity increases."],"preconditions":["Logical module boundaries are known.","Release and ownership patterns are understood.","Runtime dependencies are identified."],"input":["Release cadence","Ownership model","Scaling requirements","Failure isolation requirements","Operational maturity"],"output":"A deployment architecture distinguishing logical modularity from physical deployment independence.","expected_behavior":"Only components with a justified independent-deployment requirement become separately deployable units.","incorrect_code":"class ServiceA:\n    pass\n\nclass ServiceB:\n    pass\n","corrected_code":"# Logical modules can remain in one deployable when independent deployment is not required.\nclass ServiceA:\n    pass\n\nclass ServiceB:\n    pass\n","failure_mode":"Separate deployment is introduced solely because modules have been identified as conceptually distinct.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime error; unnecessary distribution creates additional operational failure modes and compatibility burden.","cause":"Logical separation was incorrectly treated as evidence for physical separation.","root_cause":"Deployment topology was selected without a concrete independent-deployment requirement.","solution":"Evaluate release, ownership, scaling, and failure-isolation requirements before creating separate deployment units.","edge_case":"A component may initially remain in one deployment and later be extracted when a measurable requirement emerges; the architecture should preserve that evolution path where practical.","security_implication":"Separate deployments can create new network and identity boundaries that must be explicitly secured.","performance_implication":"Remote calls introduce network latency and serialization overhead compared with in-process calls.","test_case":"Compare a modular single-deployment architecture with separate deployments against the actual independent-release requirement.","test_input":{"independent_release_required":false,"modules":["A","B"]},"expected_test_result":"The architecture does not introduce separate deployment solely from logical modularity.","testing_guidance":"Test deployment compatibility, version skew, network failure, rollback, observability, and release independence when separate deployment is selected.","verification_method":"Architecture decision review and deployment integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every physical boundary traces to an explicit requirement and that operational costs are represented.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DEPLOYMENT_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B003","D33-B027","D33-B039"],"depends_on":["D33-B027"],"requires":["D33-B027"],"extends":["D33-B027"],"refines":["D33-B003"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established modular monolith and independently deployable component architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B057","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Asynchronous workflow recovery","concept":"Durable workflow checkpoints and resumability","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture support workflows that may run for a long time and must resume after process or infrastructure failure?","answer":"Persist explicit workflow checkpoints or durable state when a workflow's lifetime exceeds the reliability of a single process execution. The architecture should define checkpoint boundaries, idempotent recovery behavior, external side-effect handling, version compatibility, and what constitutes a resumable versus terminal state.","explanation":"Long-running workflows cannot safely depend on in-memory execution state. A restart must be able to determine what work was completed, what remains, and whether external side effects can be retried. Durable workflow state should therefore be designed together with idempotency and consistency boundaries rather than added after failures occur.","principle":"A workflow that must survive process failure needs durable state sufficient to resume safely.","objective":"Provide deterministic recovery for long-lived workflows without duplicating or losing externally visible effects.","requirements":["Workflow state survives process failure.","Checkpoint semantics are explicit.","External side effects have retry or reconciliation behavior.","Workflow versions are compatible with persisted state."],"constraints":["Persisted state creates schema-evolution obligations.","Checkpointing too frequently can increase overhead.","Some external effects cannot be atomically coordinated with checkpoints.","Recovery may require operator intervention for irrecoverable states."],"preconditions":["Workflow lifecycle is explicit.","State ownership is defined.","External side effects are inventoried."],"input":["Workflow state","Checkpoint boundaries","External effects","Recovery rules","Versioning requirements"],"output":"A durable workflow architecture with resumable checkpoints and defined side-effect recovery semantics.","expected_behavior":"After process failure, the workflow resumes from a valid durable state without repeating non-idempotent effects incorrectly.","incorrect_code":"def run_workflow(items):\n    for item in items:\n        process(item)\n","corrected_code":"def run_workflow(state):\n    for item in state.pending_items:\n        process_idempotently(item)\n        state.mark_complete(item)\n        state.persist()\n","failure_mode":"A process terminates after an external side effect but before the in-memory workflow state records completion.","expected_error":"Restart can repeat the side effect or incorrectly skip it because execution state was lost.","cause":"Workflow progress and side-effect state were not durably coordinated.","root_cause":"Long-running workflow recovery was not treated as an architectural requirement.","solution":"Define durable checkpoints together with idempotent side-effect handling or reconciliation mechanisms.","edge_case":"Persisting a completion marker before an external side effect can cause the opposite failure: the system may permanently skip an effect that never occurred.","security_implication":"Persisted workflow state may contain sensitive business context and must follow appropriate access, encryption, retention, and tenant-isolation requirements.","performance_implication":"Checkpoint frequency trades recovery precision against storage and I/O overhead.","test_case":"Terminate the workflow at multiple points around an external side effect and restart it from durable state.","test_input":{"failure_points":["before_effect","after_effect_before_checkpoint","after_checkpoint"]},"expected_test_result":"Each recovery scenario produces a defined and non-duplicating outcome according to the workflow's recovery semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use failure injection around state persistence and external effects; test restart, duplicate events, version changes, and corrupted or missing checkpoints.","verification_method":"Failure-injection integration testing and workflow-state verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every checkpoint has a clear meaning and that recovery semantics cover both before-effect and after-effect failures.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DURABLE_WORKFLOW_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B024","D33-B043","D33-B044"],"depends_on":["D33-B043","D33-B024"],"requires":["D33-B024"],"extends":["D33-B043"],"refines":["D33-B043"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established durable workflow and failure-recovery architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B058","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture risk analysis","concept":"Risk-driven architecture decomposition","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_RISK_DECISION","question":"How should architecture decisions prioritize analysis when not every requirement or component carries equal risk?","answer":"Identify architectural risks by considering uncertainty, impact, exposure, change cost, failure severity, and dependency concentration. Focus deeper analysis and verification on decisions whose failure could materially affect security, correctness, availability, data integrity, or irreversible cost. Low-risk decisions can use lighter-weight evaluation.","explanation":"Architecture analysis is resource-constrained. Treating every decision with identical depth can either waste effort or leave critical risks under-analyzed. A risk-driven method concentrates evidence and verification where architectural uncertainty and consequence are greatest while preserving traceability for lower-risk decisions.","principle":"Verification depth should reflect architectural risk and uncertainty.","objective":"Allocate architecture analysis effort toward decisions with the greatest potential consequence and uncertainty.","requirements":["Architectural risks are identified.","Impact and uncertainty are distinguishable.","High-risk decisions receive stronger verification.","Risk assumptions are revisited as evidence changes."],"constraints":["Risk assessment contains judgment and must expose assumptions.","Low-probability high-impact events can be difficult to quantify.","Risk prioritization should not eliminate baseline security or correctness requirements.","Risk status can change as architecture evolves."],"preconditions":["Architecture candidates and boundaries are known.","Important assets and business consequences are identified.","Evidence gaps can be documented."],"input":["Architecture decisions","Failure consequences","Uncertainty","Exposure","Dependency concentration"],"output":"A risk-driven architecture analysis plan linking important decisions to stronger evidence and verification.","expected_behavior":"High-consequence uncertain decisions receive explicit analysis and verification rather than being treated identically to low-impact implementation details.","incorrect_code":"def review(decisions):\n    return all(decisions)\n","corrected_code":"def prioritize_reviews(decisions):\n    return sorted(decisions, key=lambda d: (d.impact, d.uncertainty), reverse=True)\n","failure_mode":"Architecture review treats every decision as equally important and can therefore miss high-risk unknowns.","expected_error":"No runtime exception is required; the failure is insufficient analysis of consequential architectural assumptions.","cause":"Risk and uncertainty are not represented in the decision process.","root_cause":"Architecture governance lacks risk-driven prioritization.","solution":"Record impact and uncertainty explicitly and allocate verification depth accordingly.","edge_case":"A low-impact component can become high-risk when it crosses a security boundary or becomes a single point of failure; risk must therefore be reassessed when context changes.","security_implication":"Security-critical boundaries should receive explicit threat modeling and verification regardless of apparent implementation simplicity.","performance_implication":"Risk-driven performance analysis should focus on architectural bottlenecks and requirements rather than optimizing every component prematurely.","test_case":"Introduce a highly consequential but uncertain architecture decision and verify that it receives stronger review criteria than a low-impact known decision.","test_input":{"high_risk":{"impact":"high","uncertainty":"high"},"low_risk":{"impact":"low","uncertainty":"low"}},"expected_test_result":"The high-risk decision is prioritized for deeper evidence and verification.","testing_guidance":"Test risk classification changes, uncertainty reduction, new evidence, dependency changes, and escalation thresholds.","verification_method":"Architecture risk review and traceability audit.","verification_guidance":"Verify that risk scores or categories are supported by explicit criteria and are not treated as objective facts when they represent judgment.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RISK_DRIVEN_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B030","D33-B039","D33-B040","D33-B050"],"depends_on":["D33-B040"],"requires":["D33-B040"],"extends":["D33-B040"],"refines":["D33-B040"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B040"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established risk-driven architecture and engineering assurance practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture concern principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B059","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"External service dependency architecture","concept":"Dependency abstraction and replacement strategy for external providers","knowledge_type":"INTEGRATION_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture respond when a critical capability depends on an external provider that may change, fail, or need replacement?","answer":"Define the external dependency's required capability separately from provider-specific implementation where replacement, resilience, or contractual independence is a meaningful requirement. Establish provider ownership, failure semantics, compatibility expectations, data portability, and replacement or fallback strategy before introducing abstraction solely for hypothetical future changes.","explanation":"Provider abstraction is useful when the system genuinely requires replacement, portability, resilience, or multiple implementations. An abstraction that merely mirrors a single vendor SDK without a concrete requirement can add complexity without reducing coupling. The architecture should therefore distinguish capability-level contracts from speculative generalization.","principle":"Abstract external dependencies around real capability and lifecycle requirements, not hypothetical substitution.","objective":"Control external-provider coupling while preserving practical implementation simplicity.","requirements":["External provider responsibilities are defined.","Required provider capabilities are identified.","Failure behavior is explicit.","Replacement or portability requirements are explicit if abstraction is introduced."],"constraints":["Provider-specific features may not map cleanly to a generic interface.","Multiple providers can have different consistency or operational semantics.","Fallback providers may require data synchronization.","Abstraction increases testing and maintenance requirements."],"preconditions":["Provider dependency is known.","Business capability supported by the provider is identified.","Portability or resilience requirements are assessed."],"input":["Provider capabilities","Required business capability","Failure requirements","Portability requirements","Data portability constraints"],"output":"An external dependency architecture defining capability boundary, provider coupling, failure behavior, and replacement strategy.","expected_behavior":"Provider-specific behavior remains localized where architectural independence is required, while necessary provider capabilities remain explicit.","incorrect_code":"def send_message(message):\n    return vendor_sdk.send(message)\n","corrected_code":"def send_message(message, provider):\n    return provider.send(message)\n","failure_mode":"Vendor-specific API behavior spreads through the application and makes replacement or testing difficult.","expected_error":"No required immediate runtime exception; replacement requires widespread changes or provider behavior leaks into business logic.","cause":"Provider implementation is directly embedded in business-facing code.","root_cause":"External dependency lifecycle requirements were not translated into a capability boundary.","solution":"Define a focused provider interface only around capabilities the application actually requires.","edge_case":"Provider-specific features may be intentionally required; hiding them behind an artificial generic abstraction can make the design less truthful.","security_implication":"Provider access requires controlled credentials, least privilege, data-minimization, and explicit trust-boundary analysis.","performance_implication":"An abstraction layer usually adds little direct overhead, but provider switching or fallback can introduce latency, synchronization, or duplicate work.","test_case":"Run the capability against a deterministic test provider and verify business behavior does not require the vendor SDK directly.","test_input":{"provider":"test_provider","operation":"send_message"},"expected_test_result":"The business-facing capability operates through the defined provider contract.","testing_guidance":"Test provider failure, timeout, contract incompatibility, provider-specific errors, credential failure, and replacement scenarios.","verification_method":"Provider contract tests and dependency graph inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the abstraction represents actual requirements and that provider-specific semantics are not falsely erased.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_INTEGRATION_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B025","D33-B041","D33-B048"],"depends_on":["D33-B041"],"requires":["D33-B041"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B041"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established dependency abstraction and integration architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B060","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture assurance","concept":"Architecture proof obligations and verification evidence","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_VERIFICATION","question":"How should an architecture demonstrate that its selected mechanisms actually satisfy the original requirements?","answer":"Convert important architecture requirements into explicit proof obligations and connect each obligation to an architectural mechanism and verification method. Verification evidence should distinguish documented design intent from implemented behavior, executed tests, measured results, and independently reviewed evidence.","explanation":"Architecture diagrams and decision records describe intent but do not prove that the implementation satisfies the intent. A requirement such as availability, isolation, compatibility, or latency must have an associated mechanism and evidence path. The strength of the evidence should match the consequence of failure and should never be represented as verified when it has only been designed or reasoned about.","principle":"Architecture claims require traceable evidence proportional to their importance.","objective":"Close the requirement-to-architecture loop by proving, rather than merely asserting, that selected mechanisms satisfy requirements.","requirements":["Requirements are uniquely identifiable.","Architectural mechanisms are mapped to requirements.","Verification methods are defined.","Evidence status is explicit.","Unverified assumptions remain visible."],"constraints":["Some architecture properties cannot be fully proven through testing alone.","Production behavior may differ from controlled test environments.","Verification evidence can become stale after architectural changes.","Formal verification may be expensive and should be targeted appropriately."],"preconditions":["Architecture baseline exists.","Requirements and decisions are traceable.","Verification methods are available or defined."],"input":["Requirement set","Architecture mechanisms","Implementation status","Test evidence","Operational measurements","Review evidence"],"output":"A requirement-to-mechanism-to-verification evidence graph showing satisfied, partially satisfied, unverified, and unresolved obligations.","expected_behavior":"Every important architecture requirement has a defined verification path and its status reflects actual available evidence rather than design intent.","incorrect_code":"def verify_architecture(requirements):\n    return "architecture looks correct"\n","corrected_code":"def verify_requirement(requirement, evidence):\n    return {\n        "requirement_id": requirement.id,\n        "status": evidence.status,\n        "evidence": evidence.references\n    }\n","failure_mode":"Architecture is marked verified because a design document describes the intended mechanism even though implementation or execution evidence is absent.","expected_error":"No runtime exception is required; the failure is false verification status.","cause":"Design intent and verification evidence are conflated.","root_cause":"The architecture assurance model does not distinguish planned, implemented, tested, and verified states.","solution":"Track explicit evidence states and require appropriate evidence before declaring a requirement satisfied.","edge_case":"A requirement may be logically demonstrated but not operationally measured; the evidence type should be recorded rather than collapsed into a single verified flag.","security_implication":"Security requirements require evidence appropriate to their threat model; architecture documentation alone should not be treated as proof of security.","performance_implication":"Performance requirements require workload-specific measurements or clearly labeled estimates; architecture selection alone does not prove latency or throughput.","test_case":"Mark a requirement as designed but provide no execution evidence and verify that the assurance system does not report it as fully verified.","test_input":{"requirement_status":"DESIGNED","execution_evidence":false},"expected_test_result":"The requirement remains unverified or partially verified according to the defined evidence model.","testing_guidance":"Test transitions among designed, implemented, executed, tested, passed, and verified states and ensure that evidence cannot be promoted without the required basis.","verification_method":"Traceability audit plus evidence-state validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every claimed architecture property links to concrete evidence and that stale evidence is invalidated when relevant architecture changes occur.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_ASSURANCE_AND_ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B010","D33-B030","D33-B049","D33-B050"],"depends_on":["D33-B030","D33-B040"],"requires":["D33-B030"],"extends":["D33-B030"],"refines":["D33-B030"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030","D33-B049"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture description principles","Established requirements traceability and verification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B061","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Authorization architecture","concept":"Centralized versus distributed authorization decision boundaries","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture determine where authorization decisions belong when multiple components enforce access to the same business capability?","answer":"Define an explicit authorization decision boundary and distinguish policy decision from policy enforcement. Centralized policy evaluation can improve consistency, while local enforcement can reduce latency and dependency on a central service. The architecture should select the boundary according to consistency, availability, latency, policy complexity, and failure requirements rather than assuming that all authorization must be centralized.","explanation":"Authorization becomes difficult to reason about when each service independently implements slightly different rules. Conversely, a single mandatory authorization service can become a dependency or availability bottleneck. Architecture should define which policies require centralized consistency and which can safely be evaluated locally from trusted policy state.","principle":"Authorization architecture should make policy ownership, decision authority, and enforcement responsibility explicit.","objective":"Provide consistent and auditable access-control behavior without introducing unnecessary availability or latency dependencies.","requirements":["Policy ownership is explicit.","Decision and enforcement responsibilities are defined.","Policy freshness requirements are known.","Failure behavior is defined."],"constraints":["Centralized policy services introduce runtime dependencies.","Local policy evaluation requires reliable policy distribution and freshness semantics.","Authorization decisions may depend on rapidly changing resource state.","Different tenants or domains may require different policy scopes."],"preconditions":["Actors, resources, and capabilities are identified.","Authorization requirements are documented.","Trust boundaries are defined."],"input":["Identity","Resource","Requested capability","Policy","Resource context","Policy freshness requirement"],"output":"An authorization architecture specifying policy ownership, decision location, enforcement point, and failure behavior.","expected_behavior":"Every protected capability reaches an explicit authorization decision and uses policy state consistent with the documented freshness requirement.","incorrect_code":"def delete_document(user, document):\n    if user:\n        return document.delete()\n","corrected_code":"def delete_document(user, document, authorizer):\n    authorizer.require(user, "document.delete", document.id)\n    return document.delete()\n","failure_mode":"Authentication is incorrectly treated as sufficient authorization.","expected_error":"A valid identity may perform an operation outside its permitted capability or resource scope.","cause":"Authorization responsibility was not explicitly modeled.","root_cause":"Identity verification and authorization decision boundaries were conflated.","solution":"Define authorization policy ownership and enforce capability/resource permissions at the protected operation boundary.","edge_case":"A cached authorization decision may become stale after role or resource changes; cache lifetime must follow the security requirement.","security_implication":"Incorrect authorization architecture can create privilege escalation or cross-tenant access paths.","performance_implication":"Central policy calls add latency and availability dependency; local evaluation reduces network overhead but requires policy distribution and freshness controls.","test_case":"Attempt the same operation with an authenticated identity that lacks the required capability.","test_input":{"authenticated":true,"capability":"document.delete","authorized":false},"expected_test_result":"The operation is denied regardless of successful authentication.","testing_guidance":"Test allow, deny, role changes, resource ownership changes, stale policy state, policy-service failure, and cross-tenant access.","verification_method":"Authorization integration tests, policy review, and boundary analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every sensitive operation has an identifiable enforcement point and that failure behavior cannot accidentally fail open.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B051","D33-B037","D33-B050"],"depends_on":["D33-B051"],"requires":["D33-B051"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B051"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B050"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established authorization architecture and least-privilege practice","OWASP access-control principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B062","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Consistency architecture","concept":"Consistency scope and invariant ownership","knowledge_type":"DATA_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture determine where strong consistency is actually required instead of imposing one consistency model on the entire system?","answer":"Identify business invariants and assign each invariant to an authoritative consistency boundary. Use strong consistency where violating the invariant is unacceptable, while allowing weaker or eventual consistency for derived, informational, or independently repairable views. The architecture should explicitly document which data can lag and which cannot.","explanation":"Distributed systems often become unnecessarily complex when every read and write is treated as requiring globally strong consistency. The more useful architectural question is which business invariants require atomic enforcement and within what scope. Consistency should therefore follow invariant ownership rather than infrastructure preference.","principle":"Consistency requirements should be derived from business invariants and their authoritative ownership boundaries.","objective":"Preserve critical correctness while avoiding unnecessary global coordination.","requirements":["Business invariants are identified.","Authoritative owners are defined.","Acceptable staleness is explicit.","Cross-boundary consistency mechanisms are documented."],"constraints":["Strong consistency can require coordination.","Eventual consistency requires reconciliation or tolerance for temporary divergence.","Some business rules span multiple bounded contexts.","Users may observe intermediate states."],"preconditions":["Data ownership is defined.","Business invariants are documented.","Read and write paths are known."],"input":["Business invariants","Data ownership","Consistency requirements","Allowed staleness"],"output":"A consistency architecture mapping each important invariant to its authoritative boundary and permitted consistency model.","expected_behavior":"Critical invariants are enforced at their authoritative boundary while derived data may use explicitly permitted weaker consistency.","incorrect_code":"def transfer(source, target, amount):\n    source.balance -= amount\n    target.balance += amount\n","corrected_code":"def transfer(source, target, amount, transaction):\n    with transaction:\n        source.balance -= amount\n        target.balance += amount\n","failure_mode":"Related state is changed without an atomic consistency boundary even though the business invariant requires both changes to succeed together.","expected_error":"A failure between updates can create an invalid aggregate state.","cause":"Consistency scope was not defined from the business invariant.","root_cause":"The architecture treated storage operations independently instead of identifying the invariant owner.","solution":"Place the invariant inside an appropriate atomic consistency boundary or explicitly redesign the business process around asynchronous reconciliation.","edge_case":"A distributed transfer across independent ownership boundaries may require a workflow or compensating process rather than a single database transaction.","security_implication":"Consistency failures can become security issues when authorization, balances, quotas, or entitlements depend on authoritative state.","performance_implication":"Broader consistency boundaries can increase locking, coordination, and latency; narrower boundaries can improve scalability when the business invariant permits them.","test_case":"Inject failure between two state updates and verify that the selected consistency model preserves or explicitly reconciles the invariant.","test_input":{"failure_point":"between_updates","invariant":"total_value_preserved"},"expected_test_result":"The invariant remains valid or the architecture enters a documented recoverable state with reconciliation semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test transaction rollback, partial failure, concurrent updates, retries, stale reads, and reconciliation paths.","verification_method":"Invariant-based testing and architecture consistency review.","verification_guidance":"Verify every critical invariant has a named owner and an explicit consistency mechanism.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_DATA_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B002","D33-B021","D33-B023","D33-B033"],"depends_on":["D33-B021"],"requires":["D33-B021"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B002"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B030"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established consistency and invariant-driven data architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B063","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Schema evolution","concept":"Backward-compatible data schema evolution","knowledge_type":"DATA_EVOLUTION_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle schema changes when old and new application versions may coexist?","answer":"Treat schema evolution as a compatibility problem between producers, consumers, persisted data, and deployment versions. Prefer additive or staged changes when compatibility is required, define migration and rollback behavior, and establish when obsolete fields or structures can be removed.","explanation":"Application deployment is often not instantaneous. During rolling deployments, backups, queued messages, and independently deployed consumers may contain older schema assumptions. Architecture therefore needs explicit compatibility windows and migration stages rather than assuming every component changes simultaneously.","principle":"Schema evolution must account for coexistence of versions across code, data, and messages.","objective":"Allow controlled system evolution without breaking active producers, consumers, or persisted state.","requirements":["Schema owners are defined.","Compatibility expectations are explicit.","Migration sequencing is documented.","Rollback and cleanup behavior are defined."],"constraints":["Some schema changes are inherently incompatible.","Long compatibility windows increase maintenance burden.","Large migrations may require background processing.","Old consumers may persist longer than expected."],"preconditions":["Data contracts are identified.","Deployment topology is known.","Consumers and producers are inventoried."],"input":["Current schema","Target schema","Consumers","Producers","Deployment sequence","Compatibility requirements"],"output":"A schema evolution plan covering compatibility, migration, deployment sequencing, and removal of obsolete structures.","expected_behavior":"Old and new participants coexist only within explicitly supported compatibility boundaries.","incorrect_code":"ALTER TABLE customer RENAME COLUMN name TO legal_name;\n","corrected_code":"ALTER TABLE customer ADD COLUMN legal_name TEXT;\n-- Backfill and migrate consumers before removing the legacy column.\n","failure_mode":"A schema is changed incompatibly before all consumers have migrated.","expected_error":"Older consumers may fail because an expected field or structure no longer exists.","cause":"Schema migration assumed atomic application deployment.","root_cause":"Version coexistence was omitted from the architecture model.","solution":"Use staged compatibility, migration, consumer rollout, validation, and eventual cleanup.","edge_case":"A new field may be present but semantically incompatible despite being syntactically backward compatible; compatibility must therefore include meaning, not only structure.","security_implication":"Schema changes can expose previously restricted fields or alter authorization assumptions and therefore require security review.","performance_implication":"Dual-read or dual-write migration strategies can temporarily increase storage, I/O, and processing costs.","test_case":"Run old and new consumers against the transition schema and verify that supported interactions remain valid.","test_input":{"old_consumer":true,"new_consumer":true,"schema_stage":"transition"},"expected_test_result":"Both supported versions operate correctly within the declared compatibility window.","testing_guidance":"Test rolling deployment, rollback, partial migration, stale messages, backup restoration, and cleanup after migration.","verification_method":"Compatibility integration testing and migration rehearsal.","verification_guidance":"Verify that removal criteria are evidence-based and that migration completion is measurable.","language":"SQL","language_version":"SQL-92+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Database-dependent","dependencies":["Relational database"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SCHEMA_EVOLUTION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B025","D33-B026","D33-B048"],"depends_on":["D33-B026"],"requires":["D33-B025"],"extends":["D33-B026"],"refines":["D33-B025"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established database schema evolution and backward-compatibility practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B064","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Concurrency architecture","concept":"Optimistic versus pessimistic concurrency control","knowledge_type":"CONCURRENCY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture select a concurrency-control strategy when multiple actors can modify the same resource?","answer":"Choose concurrency control according to conflict frequency, conflict cost, transaction duration, contention, and correctness requirements. Optimistic approaches detect conflicting updates and can work well when conflicts are uncommon, while pessimistic approaches coordinate access before modification and can be appropriate when conflicts are frequent or incorrect concurrent changes are costly.","explanation":"Concurrency control is a requirement-driven choice. Neither optimistic nor pessimistic coordination is universally correct. The architecture should identify the invariant being protected, the expected contention pattern, and the acceptable behavior when concurrent updates conflict.","principle":"Concurrency strategy should follow conflict characteristics and correctness requirements.","objective":"Prevent unacceptable lost updates or invariant violations while maintaining appropriate throughput.","requirements":["Concurrent modification risks are identified.","Conflict semantics are explicit.","Conflict resolution behavior is defined.","The protected invariant is documented."],"constraints":["Pessimistic locking can reduce concurrency.","Optimistic control can increase retries under contention.","Long transactions increase contention or stale-version probability.","Distributed resources may require coordination beyond a single process."],"preconditions":["Shared resources are identified.","Update semantics are defined.","Conflict consequences are understood."],"input":["Resource","Current version","Proposed update","Concurrency policy"],"output":"A concurrency architecture specifying conflict detection or exclusion behavior and recovery from conflicts.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent updates either serialize safely or one is rejected/reconciled according to the defined policy.","incorrect_code":"def update_account(account, value):\n    account.balance = value\n    save(account)\n","corrected_code":"def update_account(account, value, expected_version):\n    if account.version != expected_version:\n        raise ConflictError()\n    account.balance = value\n    account.version += 1\n    save(account)\n","failure_mode":"A stale writer overwrites a newer update without detecting the conflict.","expected_error":"Lost update or invariant violation can occur without an explicit runtime error.","cause":"The update lacks version or locking semantics.","root_cause":"Concurrent modification was not modeled as an architectural requirement.","solution":"Use an appropriate concurrency-control mechanism and define conflict behavior.","edge_case":"High contention can make optimistic retries effectively unavailable; the architecture must define a contention threshold or alternative strategy.","security_implication":"Authorization checks should be evaluated against sufficiently current state when concurrent changes can alter permissions or resource ownership.","performance_implication":"Optimistic control avoids unnecessary locking under low contention but can waste work under frequent conflicts; pessimistic control can reduce retries while increasing lock contention.","test_case":"Execute concurrent updates against the same versioned resource and verify that conflicting updates are detected.","test_input":{"initial_version":5,"writers":2,"expected_version":5},"expected_test_result":"At most one update succeeds for the same version unless the architecture explicitly supports safe merging.","testing_guidance":"Test low and high contention, retry storms, transaction timeout, stale versions, lock failure, and conflict resolution.","verification_method":"Concurrency integration tests and invariant validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that conflict handling is explicit and that tests exercise realistic contention patterns.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_CONTROL_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B023","D33-B062","D33-B024"],"depends_on":["D33-B023"],"requires":["D33-B023"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B023"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established concurrency-control and transactional consistency practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B065","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Rate control architecture","concept":"Rate limiting as a requirement-driven protection boundary","knowledge_type":"TRAFFIC_CONTROL_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture introduce rate limiting when requirements include abuse prevention, resource protection, or fair-use constraints?","answer":"Define rate limits around the resource or capability being protected and choose limits according to capacity, fairness, identity scope, and business requirements. Specify whether limits apply per user, tenant, credential, resource, or system and define behavior when limits are exceeded.","explanation":"A global request limit may protect infrastructure while failing to protect a particular expensive capability. Conversely, overly granular limits can create operational complexity. Architecture should therefore identify the scarce resource and place rate-control boundaries close enough to that resource to enforce the intended protection.","principle":"Rate limits should protect explicitly identified scarce resources and fairness requirements.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled demand from exhausting resources or violating defined usage policies.","requirements":["Protected resource is identified.","Rate scope is explicit.","Exceeded-limit behavior is defined.","Capacity assumptions are documented."],"constraints":["Limits can reject legitimate bursts.","Distributed rate limiting requires coordination or carefully bounded approximation.","Identity-based limits require trustworthy identity signals.","Different operations may have substantially different costs."],"preconditions":["Resource capacity is understood.","Traffic sources are identifiable.","Abuse or fairness requirements are documented."],"input":["Identity","Tenant","Operation","Resource cost","Configured limit"],"output":"A rate-control architecture defining scope, limit semantics, enforcement location, and overload behavior.","expected_behavior":"Requests exceeding the defined policy are rejected or delayed without allowing uncontrolled resource consumption.","incorrect_code":"def handle_request(request):\n    return expensive_operation(request)\n","corrected_code":"def handle_request(request):\n    limiter.require(request.identity, "expensive_operation")\n    return expensive_operation(request)\n","failure_mode":"An expensive operation has no demand-control boundary and can consume disproportionate resources under burst or abuse conditions.","expected_error":"Resource exhaustion, queue growth, latency increase, or service unavailability may occur.","cause":"Rate control was applied only at a generic gateway or omitted entirely.","root_cause":"The protected resource and its demand characteristics were not modeled architecturally.","solution":"Define rate limits at the appropriate resource or capability boundary and document their scope and failure behavior.","edge_case":"A legitimate batch operation may need a different quota or scheduling policy than interactive traffic; rate limiting should not conflate fundamentally different workload classes.","security_implication":"Rate limiting can reduce abuse and denial-of-service impact but must not be treated as the sole security control.","performance_implication":"Rate-limit checks add small overhead but can protect much larger downstream resource consumption.","test_case":"Generate requests above the configured limit and verify that excess traffic is controlled without bypass through alternate identities or paths.","test_input":{"limit":10,"requests":20,"scope":"user"},"expected_test_result":"Only the permitted request volume is accepted within the defined interval, and excess requests follow the documented policy.","testing_guidance":"Test bursts, distributed callers, boundary conditions, clock/window behavior, trusted identities, tenant isolation, and recovery after limits expire.","verification_method":"Load testing, policy tests, and resource-consumption monitoring.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the enforcement point actually protects the intended scarce resource rather than only a superficial API endpoint.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TRAFFIC_CONTROL_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B008","D33-B028","D33-B065"],"depends_on":["D33-B028"],"requires":["D33-B028"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B028"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established rate-limiting and resource-protection architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B066","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Resource isolation architecture","concept":"Tenant and workload resource isolation","knowledge_type":"MULTI_TENANT_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture prevent one tenant or workload from consuming shared resources needed by others?","answer":"Identify shared resource pools and define isolation boundaries, quotas, scheduling, or dedicated capacity according to the required fairness and blast-radius properties. Logical data isolation alone does not guarantee resource isolation; CPU, memory, connections, queues, storage, and concurrency may also require controls.","explanation":"A multi-tenant system can maintain correct data authorization while still allowing one tenant to degrade service for others through resource exhaustion. Resource isolation therefore belongs alongside data and authorization architecture. The required mechanism depends on whether fairness, strict isolation, or best-effort sharing is required.","principle":"Tenant isolation includes resource behavior, not only data access.","objective":"Prevent noisy-neighbor behavior from violating availability or service requirements for other tenants.","requirements":["Shared resources are inventoried.","Tenant fairness requirements are defined.","Resource limits or scheduling behavior are explicit.","Exhaustion behavior is documented."],"constraints":["Strict isolation can increase infrastructure cost.","Quotas can reduce utilization if poorly calibrated.","Some resources are difficult to isolate precisely.","Tenant workload patterns can change over time."],"preconditions":["Multi-tenant boundaries are defined.","Shared resource pools are known.","Service objectives are documented."],"input":["Tenant identity","Resource usage","Quota","Priority","Capacity"],"output":"A tenant resource-isolation architecture defining quotas, scheduling, dedicated resources, and overload behavior.","expected_behavior":"One tenant's workload cannot consume resources beyond its permitted boundary in a way that violates the documented service requirements of others.","incorrect_code":"def submit_job(tenant, job):\n    global_queue.append(job)\n","corrected_code":"def submit_job(tenant, job):\n    quota.require_capacity(tenant, job.cost)\n    tenant_queue[tenant].append(job)\n","failure_mode":"All tenants share an unconstrained resource queue.","expected_error":"A high-volume tenant can consume queue capacity and delay or starve other tenants.","cause":"Resource ownership and scheduling were not modeled.","root_cause":"Tenant isolation was treated solely as a data-access concern.","solution":"Introduce tenant-aware quotas, queues, scheduling, or capacity boundaries according to service requirements.","edge_case":"A single tenant may legitimately need temporary bursts; burst capacity and fairness rules should be explicit rather than relying on accidental queue behavior.","security_implication":"Resource exhaustion can become a denial-of-service or availability issue even when authorization remains correct.","performance_implication":"Isolation can reduce peak shared-resource utilization but provides more predictable tail behavior under contention.","test_case":"Generate disproportionate workload from one tenant and measure resource consumption and service behavior for other tenants.","test_input":{"tenant_a_load":"high","tenant_b_load":"normal","shared_resource":"worker_queue"},"expected_test_result":"Tenant A cannot consume resources beyond its permitted policy in a way that violates tenant B's documented service objective.","testing_guidance":"Test burst traffic, sustained overload, quota boundaries, tenant creation, priority changes, and resource recovery.","verification_method":"Load isolation testing and resource-usage monitoring.","verification_guidance":"Verify data isolation and resource isolation independently; passing one does not prove the other.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MULTI_TENANT_RESOURCE_ISOLATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B007","D33-B028","D33-B065"],"depends_on":["D33-B007"],"requires":["D33-B007"],"extends":["D33-B007"],"refines":["D33-B028"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established multi-tenant resource isolation and capacity-management practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B067","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Messaging architecture","concept":"Dead-letter and poison-message handling","knowledge_type":"MESSAGING_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle messages that repeatedly fail processing without blocking healthy messages?","answer":"Define a failure-handling path for messages that cannot be successfully processed after the permitted retry policy. A dead-letter or equivalent quarantine mechanism should preserve sufficient context for diagnosis and controlled replay while preventing a poison message from causing an endless retry loop.","explanation":"Retry mechanisms address transient failures but cannot guarantee that every message will eventually succeed. Permanent schema errors, invalid data, unsupported versions, or deterministic processing bugs can create poison messages. Architecture therefore needs an explicit terminal or quarantine state and an operator or automated remediation path.","principle":"Retries require a defined terminal failure path for messages that cannot safely progress.","objective":"Prevent poison messages from causing endless retries while preserving recoverability and evidence.","requirements":["Retry policy is defined.","Terminal failure condition is defined.","Failed messages retain diagnostic context.","Replay and remediation behavior are controlled."],"constraints":["Quarantine storage increases operational state.","Replay can duplicate side effects if processing is not idempotent.","Large failed-message payloads may contain sensitive information.","Some failures should trigger broader incident handling."],"preconditions":["Message processing semantics are defined.","Retry behavior is known.","Message identity and idempotency strategy exist."],"input":["Message","Attempt count","Failure reason","Retry policy","Message identity"],"output":"A messaging failure architecture with bounded retries, quarantine, diagnosis, and controlled replay.","expected_behavior":"Messages that cannot be processed within the retry policy are removed from the normal retry path and become available for controlled diagnosis or replay.","incorrect_code":"def consume(message):\n    while True:\n        try:\n            process(message)\n            break\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n","corrected_code":"def consume(message, attempts):\n    try:\n        process(message)\n    except TransientError:\n        if attempts < 3:\n            raise RetryMessage()\n        dead_letter.store(message)\n","failure_mode":"A permanently failing message is retried indefinitely.","expected_error":"The same message consumes processing capacity continuously and can prevent other work from progressing.","cause":"No bounded retry or terminal failure state exists.","root_cause":"Message failure lifecycle was not included in architecture design.","solution":"Define retry classes, maximum attempts, quarantine behavior, observability, and controlled replay.","edge_case":"A transient dependency outage can cause many messages to reach retry limits simultaneously; the architecture should avoid turning a temporary outage into unnecessary permanent quarantine where possible.","security_implication":"Dead-letter stores can contain sensitive payloads and require access control, retention, encryption, and tenant isolation.","performance_implication":"Bounded retries protect worker capacity; excessive retry attempts can amplify load during dependency failures.","test_case":"Submit a deterministically invalid message and verify that it reaches the terminal failure path after the defined retry limit.","test_input":{"message_id":"m-001","failure":"deterministic_validation_error","max_attempts":3},"expected_test_result":"The message is quarantined after the permitted attempts and is no longer retried indefinitely.","testing_guidance":"Test transient failures, permanent failures, poison messages, replay, duplicate delivery, storage failure, and retry storms.","verification_method":"Messaging integration tests and failure-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify retry classification, bounded attempts, quarantine integrity, replay authorization, and observability.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MESSAGING_RELIABILITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B032","D33-B024","D33-B043"],"depends_on":["D33-B032","D33-B024"],"requires":["D33-B024"],"extends":["D33-B032"],"refines":["D33-B043"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established message-processing reliability and dead-letter architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B068","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Dependency lifecycle","concept":"Dependency freshness and compatibility architecture","knowledge_type":"DEPENDENCY_MANAGEMENT_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle requirements around third-party library freshness, compatibility, and security without making upgrades unsafe?","answer":"Treat external dependencies as versioned architectural inputs with ownership, compatibility constraints, update policy, vulnerability response, and verification requirements. Separate routine upgrades from major compatibility changes and maintain reproducible dependency resolution so the system can be rebuilt and tested against known versions.","explanation":"Third-party dependencies affect runtime behavior, security, licensing, compatibility, and build reproducibility. Automatically accepting arbitrary latest versions can introduce unexpected changes, while never updating can leave known defects or vulnerabilities unresolved. Architecture should therefore define controlled dependency lifecycle management.","principle":"Dependencies are part of the system boundary and require explicit version and lifecycle governance.","objective":"Balance dependency security, freshness, reproducibility, and compatibility.","requirements":["Dependencies are inventoried.","Supported version ranges are known.","Upgrade ownership is defined.","Security and compatibility checks exist.","Builds are reproducible."],"constraints":["Dependency ecosystems evolve independently.","Transitive dependencies can change indirectly.","Security urgency can conflict with normal release cadence.","Strict pinning can increase upgrade burden."],"preconditions":["Dependency graph can be inspected.","Build process is defined.","Compatibility requirements are known."],"input":["Dependency manifest","Version constraints","Security advisories","Compatibility tests","Build environment"],"output":"A dependency lifecycle architecture covering version constraints, update policy, verification, and emergency response.","expected_behavior":"Dependency changes are introduced through controlled compatibility and verification processes rather than arbitrary unreviewed version changes.","incorrect_code":"requirements = ["example-package"]\n","corrected_code":"requirements = ["example-package>=1.4,<2.0"]\n","failure_mode":"An unconstrained dependency can change between builds and introduce behavior or compatibility changes without an explicit architectural decision.","expected_error":"Builds or runtime behavior may differ despite unchanged application source code.","cause":"Dependency version constraints were omitted.","root_cause":"External dependency lifecycle was not treated as part of architecture governance.","solution":"Define explicit version policy, reproducibility, compatibility testing, and update procedures.","edge_case":"A security fix may require an otherwise incompatible major upgrade; emergency architecture should define accelerated verification rather than ignoring compatibility requirements.","security_implication":"Dependency governance is an important supply-chain security boundary; known vulnerable versions require risk assessment and appropriate remediation.","performance_implication":"Dependency upgrades can change runtime characteristics; performance-sensitive components should be included in regression testing where relevant.","test_case":"Resolve the dependency environment twice from the same declared constraints and verify reproducibility, then test a proposed upgrade against the compatibility suite.","test_input":{"constraint":">=1.4,<2.0","upgrade":"1.5"},"expected_test_result":"The build resolves deterministically within the supported range and the proposed upgrade passes defined compatibility checks.","testing_guidance":"Test clean builds, lock or resolution behavior, transitive changes, major upgrades, rollback, and security-driven emergency updates.","verification_method":"Reproducible-build testing and dependency compatibility validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify both direct and transitive dependencies and record the evidence used to approve upgrades.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Third-party package manager"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SOFTWARE_SUPPLY_CHAIN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B039","D33-B048","D33-B050"],"depends_on":["D33-B048"],"requires":["D33-B048"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B048"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established software dependency management and supply-chain security practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B069","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture migration","concept":"Strangler-style incremental replacement boundary","knowledge_type":"MIGRATION_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture support incremental replacement of a legacy subsystem when an immediate full rewrite is too risky?","answer":"Define a controlled boundary around the legacy capability and incrementally redirect selected responsibilities to the replacement while preserving required behavior during transition. The architecture should specify routing, data ownership, compatibility, coexistence duration, rollback, and explicit exit criteria rather than allowing the legacy and replacement systems to coexist indefinitely without governance.","explanation":"Legacy replacement is often constrained by business continuity, data migration, and risk. Incremental replacement can reduce the size of each change but introduces temporary duplication and coexistence complexity. The architecture must therefore treat the migration state as intentional and bounded.","principle":"Incremental replacement requires explicit transition boundaries and exit criteria.","objective":"Reduce migration risk while preventing permanent architectural ambiguity between legacy and replacement components.","requirements":["Legacy capability boundary is identified.","Replacement responsibility is explicit.","Traffic or responsibility transition is controllable.","Data migration and compatibility are defined.","Exit criteria exist."],"constraints":["Dual systems can create data divergence.","Transition routing increases complexity.","Some legacy behavior may be poorly understood.","Migration can take longer than expected."],"preconditions":["Legacy behavior is sufficiently understood.","Replacement capability is defined.","Transition ownership is assigned."],"input":["Legacy capability","Replacement capability","Traffic routing","Data ownership","Migration state"],"output":"A staged migration architecture with controlled coexistence and explicit completion criteria.","expected_behavior":"Responsibility moves incrementally from legacy to replacement while preserving required behavior and maintaining a measurable migration state.","incorrect_code":"def process(request):\n    return legacy_system.process(request)\n","corrected_code":"def process(request, migration_router):\n    target = migration_router.select(request)\n    return target.process(request)\n","failure_mode":"Migration routing has no explicit ownership or completion criteria, leaving both systems permanently active.","expected_error":"No immediate runtime exception; long-term behavior becomes difficult to reason about and data divergence can persist.","cause":"Transition architecture was treated as a temporary implementation detail rather than a governed architecture state.","root_cause":"Migration requirements did not define exit conditions and ownership.","solution":"Define migration stages, routing rules, verification gates, rollback behavior, and final legacy retirement criteria.","edge_case":"A small percentage of legacy-only behavior may remain for an extended period; the architecture should explicitly classify such exceptions rather than falsely declaring migration complete.","security_implication":"Both legacy and replacement paths must maintain equivalent security controls during coexistence.","performance_implication":"Dual-path routing and data synchronization can increase latency and resource consumption during migration.","test_case":"Route a controlled subset of requests to the replacement and compare behavior with the legacy path before increasing migration scope.","test_input":{"migration_percentage":10,"comparison_enabled":true},"expected_test_result":"The selected traffic follows the replacement path while required correctness and security invariants remain satisfied.","testing_guidance":"Test shadow traffic, controlled routing, rollback, data reconciliation, divergent behavior, legacy-only cases, and final retirement.","verification_method":"Incremental migration testing, differential validation, and architecture gate review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that migration completion is based on explicit evidence rather than elapsed time or subjective confidence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LEGACY_MIGRATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B026","D33-B027","D33-B063"],"depends_on":["D33-B026"],"requires":["D33-B026"],"extends":["D33-B026"],"refines":["D33-B027"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established incremental legacy migration and strangler-style architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B070","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture evolution governance","concept":"Architecture fitness functions and continuous architectural verification","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture remain aligned with requirements after the initial architecture decision has been approved?","answer":"Translate important architectural requirements into continuously checkable constraints, tests, measurements, or review gates. These architecture fitness functions should detect erosion such as forbidden dependencies, boundary violations, unacceptable latency, loss of isolation, or unsupported compatibility changes. They complement rather than replace design review and system tests.","explanation":"Architecture can degrade after the original decision through incremental changes, shortcuts, dependency additions, or organizational changes. One-time approval cannot guarantee long-term conformance. Continuous architectural verification makes important constraints executable or routinely reviewable so that erosion becomes visible earlier.","principle":"Important architectural constraints should remain observable and verifiable throughout system evolution.","objective":"Prevent architecture erosion by continuously checking requirements and structural constraints after initial selection.","requirements":["Critical architecture properties are identified.","Properties have measurable or reviewable checks.","Violations produce actionable evidence.","Checks evolve when architecture requirements change."],"constraints":["Not every architecture property can be automated.","Overly rigid rules can block legitimate evolution.","Architecture checks can become stale if requirements change.","False positives reduce trust in governance mechanisms."],"preconditions":["Architecture baseline is defined.","Important constraints are known.","Verification ownership exists."],"input":["Architecture constraints","Dependency graph","Runtime metrics","Test results","Requirement changes"],"output":"A continuous architecture assurance mechanism linking important constraints to automated checks, measurements, or review gates.","expected_behavior":"Relevant architectural violations are detected after changes rather than relying exclusively on the original architecture approval.","incorrect_code":"def validate_architecture():\n    return True\n","corrected_code":"def validate_architecture(dependency_graph):\n    forbidden = {("billing", "ui")}\n    return not any(edge in forbidden for edge in dependency_graph.edges)\n","failure_mode":"Architecture validation always returns success or checks only superficial properties unrelated to actual architectural constraints.","expected_error":"Architecture erosion can enter the system without detection despite an apparently passing validation stage.","cause":"Architectural requirements were not translated into meaningful checks.","root_cause":"Architecture governance ended after initial design approval.","solution":"Define fitness functions or equivalent recurring checks for important structural and behavioral architecture constraints.","edge_case":"A legitimate architecture change may intentionally violate an old rule; the governance process must support versioned constraint changes rather than silently weakening checks.","security_implication":"Security boundaries and dependency constraints can be encoded as continuous architecture checks where practical, reducing regression risk.","performance_implication":"Automated structural checks are generally cheaper than repeated full-system tests, while runtime fitness functions can add monitoring overhead.","test_case":"Introduce a forbidden dependency into the architecture graph and verify that the architecture check detects the violation.","test_input":{"dependency_graph":[["billing","ui"],["api","billing"]],"forbidden":[["billing","ui"]]},"expected_test_result":"The architecture validation reports a violation rather than returning an unconditional success state.","testing_guidance":"Test allowed and forbidden dependencies, boundary changes, renamed components, requirement-version changes, and false-positive scenarios.","verification_method":"Automated architecture tests plus periodic architecture review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each fitness function maps to a documented requirement and that obsolete checks are removed or versioned when requirements change.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B030","D33-B040","D33-B058","D33-B060"],"depends_on":["D33-B060"],"requires":["D33-B060"],"extends":["D33-B060"],"refines":["D33-B030"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B060"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture fitness function and continuous architecture governance practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B071","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Release architecture","concept":"Safe rollout strategy selection","knowledge_type":"DEPLOYMENT_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture select a rollout strategy when a change must be introduced without exposing the entire workload to a new version immediately?","answer":"Select a rollout mechanism based on rollback speed, traffic segmentation, state compatibility, observability, and risk tolerance. Progressive rollout, canary deployment, blue-green deployment, or controlled replacement are architectural options whose suitability depends on whether old and new versions can safely coexist and whether traffic can be shifted independently.","explanation":"Deployment strategy is part of architecture when release risk depends on runtime topology, compatibility, state migration, or blast radius. A rollout mechanism should not be selected only from operational preference. The architecture must first establish whether versions can coexist and what evidence is required before increasing exposure.","principle":"Deployment exposure should be controllable and proportional to the risk of the change.","objective":"Reduce change blast radius while providing observable evidence before full rollout.","requirements":["Traffic exposure can be controlled.","Old and new versions have defined compatibility behavior.","Rollback or forward-recovery behavior is defined.","Release health criteria are measurable."],"constraints":["Some state changes cannot be rolled back safely.","Long-lived connections may complicate traffic switching.","Database compatibility can constrain deployment order.","Progressive rollout requires meaningful observability."],"preconditions":["Deployment topology is known.","Compatibility requirements are documented.","Health indicators are defined."],"input":["New version","Current version","Traffic allocation","Health criteria","Rollback policy"],"output":"A controlled rollout architecture with exposure stages, verification gates, and recovery behavior.","expected_behavior":"The new version receives only the permitted exposure until predefined health and compatibility conditions are satisfied.","incorrect_code":"def deploy(new_version):\n    production.replace_all(new_version)\n","corrected_code":"def deploy(new_version, rollout_controller):\n    rollout_controller.expose(new_version, percentage=10)\n    rollout_controller.verify_health()\n","failure_mode":"A new release is exposed to the entire workload before sufficient evidence exists.","expected_error":"A defective release can affect the full production workload before detection and recovery.","cause":"Deployment was modeled as a binary replacement.","root_cause":"Architecture did not treat exposure control as a requirement.","solution":"Introduce staged exposure with explicit health gates and recovery semantics where the workload permits it.","edge_case":"A schema or protocol change may require temporary compatibility with both versions; rollout sequencing must respect that dependency.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive changes require verification that the rollout does not create inconsistent policy enforcement across versions.","performance_implication":"Progressive rollout adds operational complexity but can reduce the performance impact of defective changes by limiting exposure.","test_case":"Expose a new version to a controlled subset and inject a known failure condition to verify that rollout stops or reverses according to policy.","test_input":{"initial_exposure":10,"failure_detected":true},"expected_test_result":"Exposure does not automatically progress to the full workload after the failure condition is detected.","testing_guidance":"Test successful progression, failed health gates, rollback, partial rollout, connection draining, schema compatibility, and controller failure.","verification_method":"Deployment rehearsal and automated rollout-gate testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that progression criteria are measurable and that a failed gate cannot silently advance exposure.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DEPLOYMENT_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B026","D33-B027","D33-B056","D33-B063"],"depends_on":["D33-B026","D33-B056"],"requires":["D33-B026"],"extends":["D33-B056"],"refines":["D33-B027"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B060"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established progressive delivery and deployment architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B072","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment recovery","concept":"Rollback versus forward-fix architecture","knowledge_type":"DEPLOYMENT_RECOVERY_DECISION","question":"How should architecture determine whether a failed release can be rolled back or must be corrected forward?","answer":"Define rollback feasibility separately from application version rollback. A release can only be safely rolled back when code, schema, state, external contracts, and side effects remain compatible with the previous version. When irreversible state changes or external effects prevent safe rollback, architecture should use controlled forward correction and containment.","explanation":"Rollback is often described as replacing the new binary with the previous one, but persistent data and external side effects may already have changed. Architecture must therefore evaluate the complete system state before treating rollback as a recovery mechanism.","principle":"Rollback is a system-state compatibility property, not merely a deployment command.","objective":"Prevent recovery actions from creating additional corruption or incompatibility.","requirements":["Rollback compatibility is assessed.","Persistent state compatibility is defined.","External side effects are considered.","Forward-fix behavior exists when rollback is unsafe."],"constraints":["Some migrations are irreversible.","External systems may retain effects.","Queued work may contain new-version assumptions.","Rollback windows may be time-limited."],"preconditions":["Deployment history is available.","Schema and state changes are documented.","External dependencies are identified."],"input":["Current version","Previous version","Schema state","External side effects","Recovery policy"],"output":"A recovery architecture specifying safe rollback conditions and forward-fix procedures.","expected_behavior":"The system uses rollback only when compatibility evidence supports it; otherwise it follows the defined containment and forward-correction path.","incorrect_code":"def recover():\n    deploy(previous_version)\n","corrected_code":"def recover(change):\n    if change.rollback_safe:\n        deploy(change.previous_version)\n    else:\n        deploy(change.forward_fix_version)\n","failure_mode":"A previous binary is restored even though the current state is incompatible with it.","expected_error":"The application may fail against changed data or produce additional inconsistent state.","cause":"Rollback was treated as automatically reversible.","root_cause":"State and side-effect compatibility were omitted from recovery architecture.","solution":"Define explicit rollback proof conditions and a forward-fix path for irreversible changes.","edge_case":"A rollback may be technically executable but operationally unsafe because external side effects cannot be undone; architectural recovery must account for both.","security_implication":"A rollback can reintroduce a previously fixed security defect; recovery architecture must include security-version constraints.","performance_implication":"Forward fixes may temporarily require additional capacity for migration or reconciliation.","test_case":"Perform a release with a non-reversible state change and verify that the recovery mechanism does not attempt an incompatible rollback.","test_input":{"schema_change":"irreversible","rollback_requested":true},"expected_test_result":"The system rejects unsafe rollback and selects the documented forward-recovery path.","testing_guidance":"Test reversible and irreversible migrations, external side effects, queued messages, rollback windows, and security-fixed versions.","verification_method":"Recovery rehearsal and state-compatibility analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify rollback claims against actual state and contract compatibility rather than deployment tooling capability.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DEPLOYMENT_RECOVERY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B063","D33-B071","D33-B026"],"depends_on":["D33-B063"],"requires":["D33-B063"],"extends":["D33-B071"],"refines":["D33-B026"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established deployment rollback and forward-fix engineering practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B073","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Legacy integration","concept":"Anti-corruption layer for incompatible legacy models","knowledge_type":"INTEGRATION_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should architecture introduce an anti-corruption layer between a new domain model and a legacy system?","answer":"Use an anti-corruption layer when a legacy model, protocol, terminology, or behavior would otherwise leak into the new domain boundary and distort its design. The layer translates concepts, data, errors, and interaction semantics while preserving explicit ownership and preventing accidental coupling.","explanation":"Directly sharing legacy representations can make the new system dependent on assumptions it does not own. An anti-corruption layer creates a deliberate translation boundary. It should not be introduced merely for abstraction aesthetics; the architectural requirement is protection of domain independence from incompatible external semantics.","principle":"Integration boundaries should translate external models rather than allowing incompatible semantics to become internal domain rules.","objective":"Preserve internal domain integrity while integrating with legacy or externally owned systems.","requirements":["Legacy semantics are identified.","Translation responsibility is explicit.","Error and lifecycle mapping are defined.","Internal domain ownership remains clear."],"constraints":["Translation introduces code and maintenance overhead.","Some semantics cannot be perfectly mapped.","Dual representations may require synchronization.","Legacy behavior may be undocumented."],"preconditions":["Internal domain model is defined.","Legacy interface and semantics are sufficiently understood.","Integration ownership is established."],"input":["Internal model","Legacy model","Mapping rules","Error semantics","Lifecycle semantics"],"output":"A translation boundary isolating the internal model from legacy-specific representations.","expected_behavior":"Legacy-specific concepts remain at the integration boundary while the internal domain uses its own stable abstractions.","incorrect_code":"def create_order(legacy_customer):\n    return Order(customer=legacy_customer)\n","corrected_code":"def create_order(legacy_customer):\n    customer = LegacyCustomerMapper.to_domain(legacy_customer)\n    return Order(customer=customer)\n","failure_mode":"Legacy representations become direct internal domain dependencies.","expected_error":"Future legacy changes propagate into unrelated domain code and create widespread coupling.","cause":"Integration mapping was omitted.","root_cause":"The external system's model was incorrectly treated as the internal model.","solution":"Introduce a deliberate translation boundary when semantic incompatibility or ownership differences justify it.","edge_case":"If the external and internal models genuinely share stable semantics and ownership constraints are acceptable, a translation layer may add unnecessary complexity.","security_implication":"Translation boundaries should preserve authorization-relevant identity, ownership, and classification semantics without silently dropping security attributes.","performance_implication":"Mapping adds processing overhead but can reduce long-term change propagation and integration coupling.","test_case":"Change a legacy representation while keeping the internal model stable and verify that only the integration mapping requires modification.","test_input":{"legacy_field":"customer_code","new_legacy_field":"account_code"},"expected_test_result":"The translation boundary adapts the change without requiring unrelated domain components to understand the legacy representation.","testing_guidance":"Test field mapping, missing values, semantic mismatches, error translation, version changes, and security attributes.","verification_method":"Contract tests and architecture dependency analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify that internal domain packages do not import or expose legacy-specific types unless explicitly permitted.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_INTEGRATION_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B025","D33-B041","D33-B069"],"depends_on":["D33-B009"],"requires":["D33-B009"],"extends":["D33-B025"],"refines":["D33-B041"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established domain integration and anti-corruption-layer architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B074","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Network architecture","concept":"Service discovery and endpoint ownership","knowledge_type":"NETWORK_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture determine how components discover the network endpoints of independently deployed capabilities?","answer":"Choose endpoint discovery based on deployment topology, address stability, scaling behavior, failure requirements, and operational control. Static configuration can be sufficient for stable environments, while dynamic service discovery is useful when instances change frequently. The architecture should make ownership and failure behavior explicit rather than introducing discovery infrastructure without a requirement.","explanation":"Independent deployment creates a need to locate service instances or endpoints. Hard-coded addresses become fragile when infrastructure changes, while a discovery system introduces another dependency and failure mode. Discovery should therefore be selected from actual topology and lifecycle requirements.","principle":"Endpoint discovery should match the volatility and ownership of deployment topology.","objective":"Provide reliable endpoint resolution without unnecessary infrastructure complexity.","requirements":["Endpoint ownership is explicit.","Address lifecycle is understood.","Failure behavior is defined.","Discovery freshness requirements are documented."],"constraints":["Discovery systems introduce control-plane dependencies.","DNS or registry information may become stale.","Direct addressing may be adequate for stable deployments.","Dynamic environments may require health-aware resolution."],"preconditions":["Deployment topology is defined.","Service boundaries are known.","Endpoint lifecycle is understood."],"input":["Service identity","Endpoint set","Health state","Deployment topology"],"output":"A service endpoint discovery architecture with ownership, freshness, and failure behavior.","expected_behavior":"Consumers resolve supported endpoints according to the declared discovery mechanism and handle unavailable or stale endpoints safely.","incorrect_code":"SERVICE_URL = "http://10.0.0.42:8080"\n","corrected_code":"SERVICE_NAME = "orders"\nendpoint = service_discovery.resolve(SERVICE_NAME)\n","failure_mode":"A deployment-dependent endpoint is embedded directly into application code.","expected_error":"The consumer can fail when the service moves, scales, or changes network topology.","cause":"Endpoint lifecycle was not modeled.","root_cause":"Deployment topology and service ownership were separated from application architecture.","solution":"Use a discovery mechanism appropriate to the environment or explicitly manage stable endpoint configuration.","edge_case":"Introducing dynamic discovery for a small static deployment can create unnecessary operational dependency; simpler configuration may be architecturally preferable.","security_implication":"Discovery responses must be authenticated or otherwise trusted when endpoint selection affects security boundaries.","performance_implication":"Discovery adds lookup overhead but can reduce repeated configuration changes and improve adaptation to dynamic topology.","test_case":"Change the service endpoint while retaining the logical service identity and verify that consumers resolve the new endpoint according to the supported mechanism.","test_input":{"service":"orders","endpoint_before":"A","endpoint_after":"B"},"expected_test_result":"Consumers can reach the supported endpoint without requiring application source changes.","testing_guidance":"Test endpoint changes, stale discovery, unavailable registry, multiple instances, health changes, and startup dependency failures.","verification_method":"Integration testing and deployment topology validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that discovery failure has an explicit fallback or fail-closed behavior appropriate to the capability.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B027","D33-B039","D33-B055"],"depends_on":["D33-B027"],"requires":["D33-B027"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B027"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established service discovery and deployment topology practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B075","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Network security architecture","concept":"Egress control and outbound dependency boundaries","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_NETWORK_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should architecture restrict outbound network access from application components?","answer":"Restrict outbound access when components have sensitive privileges, handle untrusted input, operate in regulated environments, or have a meaningful requirement to limit unintended external communication. Define explicit destination classes, protocols, identities, and failure behavior rather than relying on unrestricted outbound connectivity.","explanation":"Outbound access is an architectural trust boundary because a compromised or misconfigured component may otherwise communicate with arbitrary destinations. Egress control should be designed around actual communication requirements, with explicit exceptions and observability.","principle":"Outbound communication should be permitted according to documented capability requirements rather than assumed by default.","objective":"Reduce unauthorized data exfiltration, unintended dependency access, and lateral movement opportunities.","requirements":["Required outbound destinations are identified.","Protocol and port requirements are explicit.","Exception ownership is defined.","Denied traffic is observable."],"constraints":["Strict egress policies can break legitimate integrations.","Dynamic third-party endpoints may complicate allowlists.","Some applications require broad external connectivity.","Network policy failures can affect availability."],"preconditions":["External dependencies are inventoried.","Trust boundaries are known.","Data sensitivity is classified."],"input":["Component identity","Destination","Protocol","Port","Data classification"],"output":"An egress architecture defining permitted outbound communication and denied-path behavior.","expected_behavior":"Components can communicate with required destinations while unauthorized outbound paths are denied and observable.","incorrect_code":"requests.get(user_supplied_url)\n","corrected_code":"if destination not in ALLOWED_DESTINATIONS:\n    raise PermissionError("Outbound destination denied")\nrequests.get(destination)\n","failure_mode":"Application-controlled destinations are allowed without architectural restriction.","expected_error":"An attacker may abuse the component to contact unauthorized systems or exfiltrate data.","cause":"Outbound communication was treated as an implementation detail.","root_cause":"Egress trust boundaries were not modeled.","solution":"Define destination policy, enforcement location, exceptions, and monitoring for outbound traffic.","edge_case":"A service may legitimately need dynamic destinations; architecture should distinguish trusted discovery mechanisms from arbitrary user-controlled destinations.","security_implication":"Egress control can limit exfiltration and unauthorized lateral communication but must be combined with application-layer validation and authorization.","performance_implication":"Network policy evaluation may add negligible per-connection overhead but restrictive routing can increase architectural complexity.","test_case":"Attempt outbound communication to an allowed destination and an unauthorized destination and verify distinct outcomes.","test_input":{"allowed":"api.example.internal","denied":"unapproved.example"},"expected_test_result":"Allowed communication succeeds according to policy while unauthorized communication is denied and recorded.","testing_guidance":"Test policy boundaries, DNS changes, redirects, proxy paths, IPv4/IPv6 paths, service identity, and policy failures.","verification_method":"Network policy tests, integration tests, and controlled egress validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that alternate network paths cannot bypass the intended egress boundary.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B051","D33-B037","D33-B055"],"depends_on":["D33-B051"],"requires":["D33-B051"],"extends":["D33-B051"],"refines":["D33-B055"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established network segmentation and egress-control security practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B076","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Runtime architecture","concept":"CPU-bound versus I/O-bound execution model selection","knowledge_type":"RUNTIME_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture choose between concurrency mechanisms when workloads contain CPU-bound and I/O-bound operations?","answer":"Classify workload characteristics before selecting the execution model. I/O-bound work benefits from concurrency that allows other work to progress while operations wait, whereas CPU-bound work generally requires parallel execution across available compute resources or algorithmic optimization. The architecture should also account for runtime-specific scheduling and resource limits.","explanation":"Choosing an asynchronous or threaded architecture solely because a workload is described as concurrent can produce poor results when the dominant work is CPU-bound. Conversely, using process-based parallelism for primarily I/O-bound work can introduce unnecessary overhead. Workload classification should drive the architecture.","principle":"Execution architecture should match the dominant resource that limits progress.","objective":"Select a runtime execution model that satisfies throughput, latency, and resource requirements.","requirements":["Workload type is characterized.","Concurrency model is explicit.","Resource limits are known.","Blocking operations are identified."],"constraints":["Language runtimes differ in scheduling semantics.","Parallel execution has memory and coordination overhead.","External dependencies may remain blocking.","Workloads can change over time."],"preconditions":["Representative workload is understood.","Latency and throughput requirements exist.","Runtime environment is known."],"input":["Workload profile","CPU cost","I/O wait","Concurrency target","Runtime characteristics"],"output":"An execution architecture matching workload characteristics to concurrency or parallelism mechanisms.","expected_behavior":"The selected execution model prevents avoidable blocking and provides sufficient compute or I/O concurrency for the documented workload.","incorrect_code":"async def process(items):\n    for item in items:\n        cpu_heavy_operation(item)\n","corrected_code":"async def process(items):\n    results = await run_cpu_work_in_process_pool(items)\n    return results\n","failure_mode":"CPU-heavy work executes directly on an event-loop thread and prevents other asynchronous work from progressing.","expected_error":"Latency increases and unrelated asynchronous tasks become starved under CPU load.","cause":"Workload characteristics were not considered when selecting the execution model.","root_cause":"Concurrency and parallelism were treated as interchangeable concepts.","solution":"Separate I/O concurrency from CPU parallelism and choose execution boundaries accordingly.","edge_case":"A workload may contain mixed CPU and I/O stages; the architecture may need explicit stage boundaries rather than one global execution model.","security_implication":"Isolation boundaries may be required when executing untrusted or resource-intensive workloads.","performance_implication":"The correct model can reduce blocking and improve throughput, but process or thread coordination introduces overhead that must be measured.","test_case":"Run representative CPU-heavy and I/O-heavy workloads through the selected execution model and measure blocking behavior and throughput.","test_input":{"workload_types":["cpu_bound","io_bound"]},"expected_test_result":"CPU-heavy work does not block required I/O concurrency, and I/O-heavy work does not incur unnecessary parallel execution overhead beyond documented requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test saturation, cancellation, queue growth, process/thread limits, blocking calls, and mixed workloads.","verification_method":"Workload testing and runtime profiling.","verification_guidance":"Use measured workload evidence rather than assuming that a particular concurrency model is faster.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RUNTIME_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B039","D33-B055","D33-B065"],"depends_on":["D33-B039"],"requires":["D33-B039"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B055"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established concurrency and runtime workload-classification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B077","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Backpressure architecture","concept":"Queue backpressure and load-shedding boundaries","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture behave when producers can generate work faster than consumers can process it?","answer":"Define an explicit backpressure or load-shedding strategy based on queue capacity, workload criticality, latency requirements, and recovery behavior. Options include bounded queues, producer throttling, priority scheduling, rejection, sampling, or dropping work that is explicitly classified as non-critical.","explanation":"An unbounded queue can transform overload into memory growth and increasing latency. Immediate rejection can preserve system stability but may violate business requirements. Architecture should therefore classify work and define what happens when demand exceeds sustainable capacity.","principle":"Overload behavior must be designed rather than delegated to uncontrolled queue growth.","objective":"Keep resource usage bounded while preserving the most important work during overload.","requirements":["Queue capacity is bounded or its growth behavior is justified.","Work criticality is classified.","Overload behavior is explicit.","Recovery behavior is defined."],"constraints":["Dropping work can have business consequences.","Producer throttling may not be possible for external clients.","Priority policies can starve lower-priority work.","Backpressure propagation can spread across dependencies."],"preconditions":["Workload classes are identified.","Capacity characteristics are understood.","Queue ownership is defined."],"input":["Incoming rate","Processing rate","Queue depth","Work priority","Capacity"],"output":"A bounded overload architecture defining throttling, rejection, prioritization, or load shedding.","expected_behavior":"When demand exceeds sustainable capacity, the system follows a documented protection policy instead of allowing uncontrolled resource growth.","incorrect_code":"queue = []\ndef enqueue(item):\n    queue.append(item)\n","corrected_code":"queue = BoundedQueue(max_size=1000)\ndef enqueue(item):\n    if not queue.try_put(item):\n        raise OverloadedError()\n","failure_mode":"An unbounded queue absorbs overload until memory, latency, or downstream capacity is exhausted.","expected_error":"Memory growth, excessive latency, cascading failure, or process termination may occur.","cause":"Queue capacity and overload behavior were unspecified.","root_cause":"Architecture assumed consumers would always keep pace with producers.","solution":"Introduce bounded capacity and define backpressure or load-shedding semantics.","edge_case":"Critical work may require persistence rather than dropping; architecture should separate critical durable work from disposable or deferrable work.","security_implication":"Attackers can exploit unbounded work submission to exhaust resources; admission control reduces this risk.","performance_implication":"Backpressure protects tail latency and memory but may reduce accepted throughput during sustained overload.","test_case":"Increase producer rate beyond consumer capacity and verify bounded resource use and documented overload behavior.","test_input":{"producer_rate":"10x_consumer_rate","queue_limit":1000},"expected_test_result":"Queue growth remains bounded and excess work follows the documented policy.","testing_guidance":"Test bursts, sustained overload, recovery, priority starvation, producer throttling, and queue persistence.","verification_method":"Load testing and controlled overload experiments.","verification_guidance":"Record measured capacity and validate that overload behavior remains within resource limits.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],