{"record_id":"D33-B078","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Lifecycle architecture","concept":"Graceful startup, readiness, draining, and shutdown","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture define component lifecycle behavior so that startup, traffic acceptance, maintenance, and shutdown do not create avoidable failures?","answer":"Model startup, readiness, serving, draining, and shutdown as distinct lifecycle states. A component should not receive traffic until required dependencies and internal state are ready, and it should stop accepting new work before terminating while allowing permitted in-flight work to complete or be safely cancelled.","explanation":"Treating process existence as equivalent to service readiness can route traffic to components that cannot yet serve correctly. Abrupt shutdown can also terminate work or create duplicate processing. Explicit lifecycle states allow deployment and failure-management systems to coordinate safely with application behavior.","principle":"Process lifecycle and service readiness are distinct architectural states.","objective":"Provide predictable behavior during startup, deployment, maintenance, scaling, and shutdown.","requirements":["Readiness is distinct from liveness.","Startup dependencies are defined.","Drain behavior is defined.","Shutdown timeout and cancellation semantics are documented."],"constraints":["Some initialization is expensive or asynchronous.","Long-running operations may exceed normal shutdown windows.","Dependency failures can prevent readiness.","Different workloads require different drain semantics."],"preconditions":["Component dependencies are known.","Work lifecycle is defined.","Deployment environment can observe lifecycle state."],"input":["Startup state","Dependency health","In-flight work","Shutdown signal"],"output":"A component lifecycle architecture with readiness, draining, and shutdown behavior.","expected_behavior":"Traffic is accepted only when the component is ready, new work stops during draining, and in-flight work follows documented completion or cancellation rules.","incorrect_code":"def start():\n    server.listen()\n    initialize_database()\n","corrected_code":"def start():\n    initialize_database()\n    readiness.set_ready()\n    server.listen()\n","failure_mode":"Traffic is accepted before required initialization completes.","expected_error":"Requests may fail because required resources or state are unavailable during startup.","cause":"Process startup and service readiness were treated as the same event.","root_cause":"Lifecycle state transitions were not architecturally defined.","solution":"Separate initialization, readiness, serving, draining, and shutdown states.","edge_case":"A component may remain alive but intentionally unready during dependency degradation; liveness should not automatically imply readiness.","security_implication":"Sensitive components should not expose partially initialized endpoints or administrative paths during startup.","performance_implication":"Graceful draining may temporarily reduce capacity but avoids retry amplification and incomplete work.","test_case":"Start a component with a delayed dependency and verify that it remains unready until required initialization succeeds.","test_input":{"dependency_delay_seconds":5,"readiness_checked_during_delay":true},"expected_test_result":"The component does not accept normal traffic before readiness criteria are satisfied.","testing_guidance":"Test delayed startup, dependency failure, readiness transitions, shutdown during work, timeout, cancellation, and restart.","verification_method":"Lifecycle integration tests and deployment orchestration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that readiness reflects actual service capability rather than process existence alone.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SERVICE_LIFECYCLE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B055","D33-B071","D33-B076"],"depends_on":["D33-B055"],"requires":["D33-B055"],"extends":["D33-B071"],"refines":["D33-B027"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established service readiness and graceful lifecycle management practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B079","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Data portability architecture","concept":"Import, export, and portability boundary","knowledge_type":"PORTABILITY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should architecture treat data export and import as first-class capabilities rather than administrative utilities?","answer":"Treat portability as an architectural requirement when users, tenants, integrations, regulatory obligations, migration plans, or lock-in concerns require controlled movement of data. Define export scope, format, consistency point, authorization, sensitive-data handling, integrity validation, and import semantics explicitly.","explanation":"Data portability becomes difficult when the system's data model is inseparable from internal storage structures or when exports omit derived or relationship information. A first-class portability boundary defines what constitutes transferable business data and how its integrity and security are preserved.","principle":"Portable data should be defined by business ownership and semantics, not by accidental database representation.","objective":"Enable controlled migration, backup, interoperability, or user/tenant data portability without exposing internal storage coupling.","requirements":["Export scope is defined.","Portable representation is documented.","Authorization is enforced.","Integrity and completeness are verifiable.","Import compatibility is defined when import is supported."],"constraints":["Derived data may require reconstruction.","Large exports require asynchronous processing.","Sensitive data requires strict access controls.","Internal schema should not become the public portability contract accidentally."],"preconditions":["Data ownership is defined.","Data classification exists.","Portability requirements are known."],"input":["Tenant or user scope","Business data","Export version","Integrity metadata"],"output":"A portability architecture defining export/import boundaries, formats, security, integrity, and compatibility.","expected_behavior":"Authorized users can obtain the defined portable dataset without exposing unrelated tenant data or relying on internal storage layout.","incorrect_code":"def export_tenant(tenant_id):\n    return database.dump_all_tables()\n","corrected_code":"def export_tenant(tenant_id):\n    records = business_exporter.collect_owned_data(tenant_id)\n    return exporter.write_versioned_package(records)\n","failure_mode":"A raw database dump is treated as a portable business-data export.","expected_error":"The export may contain unrelated tenants, internal metadata, secrets, incompatible storage structures, or incomplete derived relationships.","cause":"Portability boundary was not defined.","root_cause":"Database representation was incorrectly treated as the business portability model.","solution":"Define a versioned business-level export contract with authorization, integrity, and completeness rules.","edge_case":"Large or continuously changing datasets may require snapshot semantics or asynchronous export jobs to define a consistent export point.","security_implication":"Exports can concentrate sensitive information and require authorization, encryption, retention, and audit controls.","performance_implication":"Large exports can consume substantial I/O and CPU; asynchronous generation and bounded resource use may be required.","test_case":"Export one tenant and verify that only authorized business data for that tenant is present and integrity metadata validates the package.","test_input":{"tenant":"tenant-A","records_owned":100,"records_other_tenants":100},"expected_test_result":"The export contains only the defined tenant-owned portable data and passes integrity validation.","testing_guidance":"Test tenant isolation, authorization, large datasets, concurrent changes, export versioning, corruption, import compatibility, and deletion requirements.","verification_method":"Portability integration tests and data-integrity validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify completeness against the documented export contract rather than comparing only row counts.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_PORTABILITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B052","D33-B063"],"depends_on":["D33-B009"],"requires":["D33-B009"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B052"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established data portability and business-level export architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B080","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture evolution","concept":"Architecture drift detection and conformance boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_CONFORMANCE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture detect when implementation has diverged from approved architectural boundaries?","answer":"Define a small set of enforceable architectural boundaries and continuously compare implementation structure or runtime behavior against them. Useful checks include dependency direction, forbidden imports, component ownership, interface usage, deployment boundaries, and security-zone transitions. Drift should produce evidence and a reviewable remediation path rather than merely generating warnings with no owner.","explanation":"Architecture documentation can remain correct while implementation gradually diverges. Drift detection converts important architecture constraints into observable checks. The architecture should focus on constraints that materially affect maintainability, security, deployment independence, or correctness rather than attempting to encode every design detail.","principle":"Architectural boundaries remain useful only when important deviations can be detected and governed.","objective":"Detect meaningful architecture erosion before it becomes embedded technical debt or a security/reliability problem.","requirements":["Approved boundaries are documented.","Boundary violations are detectable.","Ownership of remediation is defined.","Intentional exceptions are recorded."],"constraints":["Static analysis cannot observe every runtime property.","Overly broad rules create noise.","Architecture changes require corresponding rule updates.","Some boundaries depend on deployment or operational state."],"preconditions":["Architecture baseline exists.","Components and ownership are identifiable.","Important constraints are selected."],"input":["Architecture rules","Source dependency graph","Deployment topology","Approved exceptions"],"output":"An architecture conformance mechanism that identifies meaningful drift and distinguishes approved exceptions from violations.","expected_behavior":"A change that violates a protected architecture boundary is detected or explicitly reviewed before being accepted as normal architecture.","incorrect_code":"def check_architecture():\n    return True\n","corrected_code":"def check_architecture(graph, forbidden_edges):\n    violations = [edge for edge in graph.edges if edge in forbidden_edges]\n    return violations\n","failure_mode":"Architecture conformance tooling reports success without checking actual constraints.","expected_error":"Architectural drift remains undetected while governance appears operational.","cause":"Verification was disconnected from the architecture model.","root_cause":"The architecture baseline was not translated into executable or reviewable conformance rules.","solution":"Define evidence-producing architecture checks tied directly to approved constraints and exceptions.","edge_case":"A legitimate architecture evolution can invalidate an old constraint; rules must be versioned with the architecture rather than permanently enforcing obsolete decisions.","security_implication":"Drift detection can identify unauthorized dependency paths or trust-boundary violations before they become exploitable runtime behavior.","performance_implication":"Static conformance checks are generally cheaper than runtime validation but should be scoped to meaningful architectural properties.","test_case":"Introduce a known forbidden dependency and verify that the conformance mechanism reports the exact violating boundary.","test_input":{"forbidden_edge":["domain","infrastructure"]},"expected_test_result":"The violation is identified with enough context for remediation or explicit architectural exception review.","testing_guidance":"Test valid dependencies, forbidden dependencies, approved exceptions, renamed components, architecture version changes, and false-positive handling.","verification_method":"Architecture conformance tests and periodic architecture review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every protected rule maps to an approved architectural requirement and that detected exceptions have explicit ownership and expiry where appropriate.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_CONFORMANCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B030","D33-B040","D33-B060","D33-B070"],"depends_on":["D33-B070"],"requires":["D33-B060"],"extends":["D33-B070"],"refines":["D33-B030"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D33-B070"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture conformance and drift-detection practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B081","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Identity architecture","concept":"Authentication session and token lifecycle","knowledge_type":"IDENTITY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture define authentication session or token lifecycle requirements for applications with protected resources?","answer":"Define authentication state, credential lifetime, renewal, revocation, logout behavior, audience or scope, and failure semantics explicitly. The architecture should distinguish authentication proof from authorization and should avoid treating a long-lived credential as equivalent to continuously valid access.","explanation":"Authentication architecture includes more than the initial login. Sessions and tokens persist across time, devices, services, and privilege changes. Their lifecycle therefore affects security, availability, user experience, and incident response. Architecture should define how credentials are issued, refreshed, revoked, expired, and invalidated when security state changes.","principle":"Authentication credentials require an explicit lifecycle and must not be treated as permanently valid identity proofs.","objective":"Provide controlled, auditable authentication state while supporting appropriate session continuity.","requirements":["Credential issuance is defined.","Expiration and renewal semantics are explicit.","Revocation behavior is defined.","Audience and scope are constrained.","Logout and compromise response are defined."],"constraints":["Immediate revocation can require centralized state or short credential lifetimes.","Long-lived credentials improve continuity but increase compromise exposure.","Distributed systems may observe revocation at different times.","Different clients may require different lifecycle policies."],"preconditions":["Identity model is defined.","Protected resources and trust boundaries are known.","Threat and session requirements are documented."],"input":["Identity","Credential","Audience","Scope","Expiration","Revocation state"],"output":"An authentication lifecycle architecture covering issuance, validation, renewal, expiration, and revocation.","expected_behavior":"Credentials are accepted only within their documented lifecycle and scope, with explicit handling for expired or revoked authentication state.","incorrect_code":"def authenticate(token):\n    return token is not None\n","corrected_code":"def authenticate(token, verifier):\n    claims = verifier.verify(token)\n    if claims.expired or claims.revoked:\n        raise AuthenticationError()\n    return claims\n","failure_mode":"Credential presence is treated as proof of valid authentication.","expected_error":"Expired, forged, revoked, or incorrectly scoped credentials may be accepted.","cause":"Credential lifecycle validation was omitted.","root_cause":"Authentication was modeled as a one-time login event rather than a continuing security state.","solution":"Define credential validation, expiration, renewal, scope, and revocation semantics.","edge_case":"A user may lose authorization while an authentication credential remains valid; authorization must therefore be evaluated separately where required.","security_implication":"Credential lifecycle design directly affects replay, session hijacking, revocation, and compromise containment.","performance_implication":"Frequent validation or introspection can add latency and dependency overhead; caching must respect security freshness requirements.","test_case":"Attempt access with a valid, expired, revoked, and incorrectly scoped credential.","test_input":{"credential_states":["valid","expired","revoked","wrong_scope"]},"expected_test_result":"Only credentials satisfying the documented lifecycle and scope requirements are accepted.","testing_guidance":"Test expiration boundaries, renewal, revocation, logout, clock skew, replay, credential rotation, and compromised-session response.","verification_method":"Identity integration tests and security review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that authentication lifecycle rules are enforced at all relevant trust boundaries.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_IDENTITY_SECURITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B051","D33-B061","D33-B035"],"depends_on":["D33-B051"],"requires":["D33-B051"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B061"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established authentication and session lifecycle security practice","OWASP authentication guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B082","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Authorization architecture","concept":"Administrative control plane separation","knowledge_type":"CONTROL_PLANE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should administrative operations be architecturally separated from normal application data-plane operations?","answer":"Separate control-plane operations when administrative capabilities have substantially greater authority, different availability or audit requirements, distinct trust boundaries, or different operational lifecycles from ordinary business traffic. The separation may be logical, network-based, process-based, or deployment-based depending on the requirements.","explanation":"Administrative functions can alter policies, credentials, configuration, tenants, or system behavior. Treating them as ordinary application operations can enlarge the attack surface and make high-impact actions difficult to distinguish. A control-plane boundary makes authority and operational responsibility explicit.","principle":"High-authority administrative operations should have an explicit control boundary distinct from ordinary data-plane execution where risk or lifecycle requirements justify it.","objective":"Reduce exposure and improve governance of high-impact administrative capabilities.","requirements":["Administrative capabilities are identified.","Authority levels are explicit.","Administrative access is separately governed.","Control-plane actions are auditable.","Failure behavior is defined."],"constraints":["Separate control planes add operational components.","Some administrative actions require data-plane coordination.","Strict separation can complicate emergency operations.","Availability requirements may differ between planes."],"preconditions":["Administrative operations are inventoried.","Trust boundaries are defined.","Authorization policy is established."],"input":["Administrative operation","Actor","Target resource","Policy","Control-plane state"],"output":"A control-plane/data-plane architecture with explicit authority and audit boundaries.","expected_behavior":"Administrative operations cross an explicit high-authority boundary and cannot be accidentally exposed through ordinary data-plane paths.","incorrect_code":"def update_tenant(request):\n    return database.update(request.json)\n","corrected_code":"def update_tenant(admin_context, change):\n    admin_authorizer.require(admin_context, "tenant.update")\n    audit.record(admin_context, change)\n    return control_plane.apply(change)\n","failure_mode":"A privileged administrative operation is exposed through a normal business endpoint without additional governance.","expected_error":"A lower-trust path may gain access to a high-impact capability.","cause":"Administrative and business operations share the same architectural boundary.","root_cause":"Authority differences were not reflected in the architecture.","solution":"Define an explicit control-plane boundary with stronger authorization, audit, and operational controls.","edge_case":"Emergency operations may require controlled break-glass access; this should be explicitly designed rather than bypassing the control plane informally.","security_implication":"Control-plane separation reduces accidental exposure of high-impact operations and supports stronger monitoring and authorization.","performance_implication":"Administrative operations are usually low-volume, so stronger controls can often be applied without significant business-path latency impact.","test_case":"Attempt an administrative operation through a normal data-plane identity and through an authorized administrative identity.","test_input":{"normal_identity":true,"admin_identity":true,"operation":"tenant.update"},"expected_test_result":"The normal identity is denied and the authorized administrative path succeeds subject to governance controls.","testing_guidance":"Test privilege separation, audit generation, emergency access, network boundaries, and attempted data-plane bypass.","verification_method":"Authorization tests, architecture boundary review, and audit verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no ordinary data-plane route can invoke the protected administrative capability without the required control-plane checks.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONTROL_PLANE_SECURITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B051","D33-B053","D33-B061"],"depends_on":["D33-B061"],"requires":["D33-B051"],"extends":["D33-B061"],"refines":["D33-B053"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established control-plane/data-plane separation and privileged-operation architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B083","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Authorization architecture","concept":"Break-glass administrative access","knowledge_type":"EMERGENCY_ACCESS_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture support emergency administrative access without turning the emergency path into an unrestricted permanent bypass?","answer":"Define emergency access as a separately governed capability with explicit eligibility, authentication strength, limited scope, time bounds, approval or dual-control requirements where appropriate, detailed audit, and automatic expiration. Emergency access should preserve accountability even when normal administrative paths are unavailable.","explanation":"Operational emergencies can require access that is intentionally outside normal workflows. A permanently enabled bypass creates a hidden privileged path. Architecture should instead model emergency access as a controlled state with explicit activation, monitoring, and termination.","principle":"Emergency access should be exceptional, bounded, observable, and automatically constrained.","objective":"Provide recoverability during exceptional conditions without creating an uncontrolled privileged backdoor.","requirements":["Emergency activation is explicit.","Scope and duration are bounded.","Activation is audited.","Normal controls are bypassed only to the minimum necessary extent.","Automatic expiration exists."],"constraints":["Emergency access may be required when normal identity systems are degraded.","Strong controls can slow urgent response.","Emergency credentials are highly sensitive.","Some environments require independent approval."],"preconditions":["Administrative capabilities are identified.","Normal access path exists.","Emergency scenarios are documented."],"input":["Emergency actor","Reason","Requested capability","Activation time","Expiration time"],"output":"A governed emergency-access architecture with bounded authority and evidence.","expected_behavior":"Emergency access cannot remain active indefinitely and every activation produces an attributable audit record.","incorrect_code":"def emergency_access(user):\n    return True\n","corrected_code":"def emergency_access(context):\n    require_strong_authentication(context)\n    require_reason(context)\n    grant_temporary_scope(context, expires_at=context.expiry)\n    audit.record(context)\n","failure_mode":"An emergency flag grants unrestricted access without expiration or audit.","expected_error":"A temporary recovery mechanism becomes a persistent privileged bypass.","cause":"Emergency access was implemented as an unconditional boolean privilege.","root_cause":"Exceptional access was not modeled as a governed lifecycle.","solution":"Define temporary, scoped, auditable emergency authorization with explicit activation and expiry.","edge_case":"The identity provider itself may be unavailable; the architecture must define how emergency authentication remains trustworthy without relying on the failed dependency.","security_implication":"Break-glass mechanisms are high-value attack targets and require stronger monitoring and credential protection than ordinary access.","performance_implication":"Emergency access is generally low-volume; stronger controls have limited normal-path performance impact.","test_case":"Activate emergency access, verify the intended scope, and attempt access after its expiration time.","test_input":{"duration_minutes":30,"requested_scope":"admin.recovery"},"expected_test_result":"Access is permitted only during the defined emergency window and scope.","testing_guidance":"Test activation, expiry, scope boundaries, failed activation, repeated activation, audit integrity, and compromised emergency credentials.","verification_method":"Security integration tests and emergency-access rehearsal.","verification_guidance":"Verify that emergency access remains attributable and automatically expires even if the initiating workflow fails.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRIVILEGED_ACCESS_SECURITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B053","D33-B061","D33-B082"],"depends_on":["D33-B082"],"requires":["D33-B053","D33-B061"],"extends":["D33-B082"],"refines":["D33-B053"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established break-glass and emergency privileged-access architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B084","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Data residency architecture","concept":"Regional data residency and processing boundaries","knowledge_type":"DATA_RESIDENCY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle requirements that specific data must remain stored or processed within defined geographic or legal regions?","answer":"Classify data and processing activities by residency requirement, then define storage, backup, replication, processing, support, and telemetry boundaries accordingly. Architecture should distinguish where primary data resides from where derived, replicated, cached, logged, or backed-up information may travel.","explanation":"Data residency is broader than selecting a database region. Replicas, backups, logs, analytics pipelines, support systems, and third-party processors can move information outside the intended boundary. Architecture therefore needs a complete data-flow view and explicit regional constraints.","principle":"Residency requirements apply to the complete lifecycle and propagation of governed data, not only its primary storage location.","objective":"Keep regulated or contractually restricted data within defined geographic or legal boundaries.","requirements":["Residency classes are defined.","Cross-region flows are identified.","Backup and replication behavior is defined.","Third-party processing is accounted for.","Violation behavior is explicit."],"constraints":["Global availability can conflict with strict residency.","Some services may not support regional isolation.","Operational telemetry can unintentionally contain sensitive data.","Regional failure can affect availability."],"preconditions":["Data classification exists.","Processing flows are mapped.","Applicable residency requirements are known."],"input":["Data class","Region","Processing location","Replication policy","Backup policy"],"output":"A regional data architecture covering storage, processing, replication, backup, and telemetry boundaries.","expected_behavior":"Data subject to residency restrictions remains within permitted locations throughout its defined lifecycle.","incorrect_code":"def replicate(data):\n    send_to_global_cluster(data)\n","corrected_code":"def replicate(data, residency_policy):\n    destination = residency_policy.allowed_replica_region(data.classification)\n    send_to_region(data, destination)\n","failure_mode":"Data is replicated globally without considering its residency classification.","expected_error":"Restricted data may cross a prohibited geographic or legal boundary.","cause":"Residency was treated as a database-location property.","root_cause":"The complete data propagation lifecycle was not modeled.","solution":"Apply residency policy across storage, processing, backup, replication, logging, and external processing.","edge_case":"Derived data may contain enough information to remain subject to the same restriction even when direct identifiers are removed; classification must consider actual policy requirements.","security_implication":"Cross-region propagation can create unauthorized disclosure or jurisdictional exposure.","performance_implication":"Regional isolation can increase latency for globally distributed users and may require regional processing or carefully designed routing.","test_case":"Attempt to replicate restricted data to a prohibited region and verify that the architecture blocks the flow.","test_input":{"classification":"restricted-region-A","destination":"region-B","allowed":false},"expected_test_result":"The transfer is denied and an auditable policy decision is recorded.","testing_guidance":"Test primary storage, replicas, backups, caches, logs, analytics, support tools, failover, and disaster-recovery paths.","verification_method":"Data-flow analysis, policy tests, and regional deployment validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify all propagation paths, including indirect telemetry and backups, not just the primary datastore.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_GOVERNANCE_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B052","D33-B033","D33-B038"],"depends_on":["D33-B052"],"requires":["D33-B052"],"extends":["D33-B038"],"refines":["D33-B033"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established data residency and data-flow governance practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B085","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Local-first architecture","concept":"Offline-first state synchronization and conflict resolution","knowledge_type":"DISTRIBUTED_STATE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture support clients that must continue operating when network connectivity is intermittent or unavailable?","answer":"Define which operations can be performed locally, which state is authoritative, how local changes are recorded, how synchronization occurs, and how conflicts are resolved. Offline-first architecture requires explicit reconciliation semantics rather than simply caching the last server response.","explanation":"Disconnected operation changes the consistency model because local state can diverge from shared authoritative state. The architecture must define whether operations are queued, merged, rejected, or transformed after reconnection. User-visible conflict behavior should follow business semantics.","principle":"Offline capability requires explicit ownership, synchronization, and conflict-resolution semantics.","objective":"Maintain useful operation during connectivity loss without silently corrupting shared business state.","requirements":["Local state ownership is defined.","Offline-capable operations are classified.","Synchronization protocol is defined.","Conflict semantics are explicit.","Recovery from failed synchronization is defined."],"constraints":["Local changes may become stale.","Some operations cannot safely execute offline.","Conflict resolution can require business-specific rules.","Local storage may contain sensitive data."],"preconditions":["Authoritative state is identified.","Client capabilities are classified.","Synchronization requirements are known."],"input":["Local state","Local changes","Server state","Operation identity","Conflict policy"],"output":"An offline-first architecture defining local execution, synchronization, reconciliation, and conflict behavior.","expected_behavior":"Offline-capable operations are recorded locally and reconciled according to explicit rules when connectivity returns.","incorrect_code":"def save_change(change):\n    server.save(change)\n","corrected_code":"def save_change(change):\n    if network.available():\n        return server.save(change)\n    local_queue.append(change)\n    return "PENDING_SYNC"\n","failure_mode":"The application assumes immediate network availability for all writes.","expected_error":"Users cannot continue working offline or may lose changes when connectivity disappears.","cause":"Network availability was treated as guaranteed.","root_cause":"Offline state and synchronization were not modeled architecturally.","solution":"Define local durable state, synchronization, conflict handling, and operation status explicitly.","edge_case":"Two clients may modify the same business object while offline; the architecture must define whether changes can merge or whether one operation must be rejected.","security_implication":"Offline data requires local encryption, access control, secure deletion, and careful handling of queued sensitive operations.","performance_implication":"Local writes can reduce network latency but synchronization may create bursts after reconnection.","test_case":"Perform an offline-supported operation, restore connectivity, and verify that the change synchronizes exactly once or follows the documented reconciliation policy.","test_input":{"network":"offline","operation":"supported_write","reconnect":true},"expected_test_result":"The operation remains durable locally and reaches the authoritative state according to the synchronization contract.","testing_guidance":"Test offline writes, reconnect storms, duplicate synchronization, conflicts, client restarts, stale state, and queue corruption.","verification_method":"Offline integration testing and conflict-reconciliation testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify business invariants after reconciliation rather than merely checking that synchronization completed.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYNC_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B021","D33-B062","D33-B024","D33-B069"],"depends_on":["D33-B021","D33-B024"],"requires":["D33-B021"],"extends":["D33-B062"],"refines":["D33-B024"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established offline-first and distributed synchronization architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B086","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Caching architecture","concept":"Cache invalidation and freshness ownership","knowledge_type":"CACHE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture define cache invalidation when stale data can affect correctness or user-visible behavior?","answer":"Identify the authoritative source, acceptable staleness, invalidation trigger, freshness guarantee, and behavior when invalidation fails. Cache entries should be treated as derived state unless the architecture explicitly defines another ownership model. Critical correctness decisions should not depend on stale cache state without an appropriate consistency mechanism.","explanation":"Caching becomes dangerous when its freshness semantics are implicit. Time-to-live alone does not guarantee correctness, while synchronous invalidation can add coupling. Architecture should select invalidation and refresh behavior based on the consequence of stale data.","principle":"Cache freshness must be an explicit architectural property tied to the consequences of stale information.","objective":"Improve performance without allowing stale derived state to violate correctness requirements.","requirements":["Source of truth is identified.","Freshness requirement is defined.","Invalidation or refresh mechanism is documented.","Cache failure behavior is explicit."],"constraints":["Invalidation can be difficult across distributed components.","Long TTL improves cache efficiency but increases staleness.","Synchronous invalidation adds dependency coupling.","Cache rebuilds can create load spikes."],"preconditions":["Authoritative data owner is known.","Read workload is characterized.","Staleness consequences are understood."],"input":["Source data","Cache key","Freshness policy","Invalidation event"],"output":"A cache architecture with explicit ownership, freshness, invalidation, and fallback behavior.","expected_behavior":"Cache reads conform to the declared freshness requirement and fall back safely when cached state is unavailable or invalid.","incorrect_code":"def get_user(user_id):\n    return cache.get(user_id)\n","corrected_code":"def get_user(user_id):\n    value = cache.get(user_id)\n    if value is not None and value.is_fresh():\n        return value\n    return authoritative_store.get(user_id)\n","failure_mode":"Cached data is treated as authoritative without a documented freshness guarantee.","expected_error":"Users or business logic may act on stale state.","cause":"Cache ownership and freshness semantics were undefined.","root_cause":"Performance optimization was allowed to become an implicit consistency mechanism.","solution":"Define cache derivation, freshness, invalidation, fallback, and correctness boundaries.","edge_case":"An invalidation event may be lost; periodic reconciliation or bounded TTL may be required when stale data has material consequences.","security_implication":"Stale authorization, entitlement, or tenant metadata can create security failures; such data may require stronger freshness guarantees than ordinary display data.","performance_implication":"Shorter freshness windows can increase backend load; longer windows improve hit rates but increase staleness risk.","test_case":"Change authoritative data while leaving a stale cache entry and verify behavior according to the declared freshness policy.","test_input":{"cache_state":"stale","authoritative_state":"new","freshness_required":"strong"},"expected_test_result":"The system does not use stale cache data where the architecture requires current authoritative state.","testing_guidance":"Test cache miss, stale entry, invalidation failure, backend failure, rebuild, concurrent updates, and cache stampede conditions.","verification_method":"Cache consistency tests and load testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every correctness-sensitive cache has an explicit freshness contract.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CACHE_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B022","D33-B062","D33-B085"],"depends_on":["D33-B022"],"requires":["D33-B022"],"extends":["D33-B022"],"refines":["D33-B062"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established caching, freshness, and consistency architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B087","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Search architecture","concept":"Authoritative store versus derived search index","knowledge_type":"SEARCH_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture treat a search index when the indexed data originates from an authoritative transactional system?","answer":"Treat the search index as derived state unless the requirements explicitly make it authoritative. Define synchronization, freshness, rebuildability, failure fallback, and index schema ownership. The authoritative store should remain the source of truth when search data can be reconstructed from it.","explanation":"Search indexes optimize retrieval but can become inconsistent, corrupted, or unavailable. If the architecture treats an index as the only copy of business data without an explicit requirement, recovery becomes difficult. Separating source of truth from derived search state makes rebuilding and failure handling explicit.","principle":"Derived indexes should be rebuildable from authoritative data whenever the business model permits.","objective":"Provide efficient search without making derived indexing state an accidental source of truth.","requirements":["Authoritative source is defined.","Index synchronization is defined.","Freshness requirements are explicit.","Rebuild procedure exists.","Search failure behavior is defined."],"constraints":["Index rebuilds can be expensive.","Eventual indexing can produce temporary search gaps.","Some search features require denormalized or enriched data.","Authoritative data may itself be unavailable during rebuild."],"preconditions":["Business data owner is known.","Search requirements are documented.","Indexable fields are identified."],"input":["Authoritative records","Index schema","Synchronization events","Freshness requirement"],"output":"A search architecture separating authoritative business state from derived retrieval state.","expected_behavior":"The index can be regenerated or repaired without requiring the index itself to become the sole source of truth.","incorrect_code":"def update_product(product):\n    search_index.save(product)\n","corrected_code":"def update_product(product):\n    authoritative_store.save(product)\n    indexing_queue.publish(product.id)\n","failure_mode":"The search index is updated as though it were the authoritative business store.","expected_error":"A failed or corrupted index can become indistinguishable from lost business data.","cause":"Authority and derivation responsibilities were not separated.","root_cause":"Search architecture was coupled directly to persistence ownership.","solution":"Persist authoritative state first and treat indexing as a derived synchronization process with rebuild capability.","edge_case":"Search freshness may be required within a strict window; architecture must define whether temporary indexing delay is acceptable.","security_implication":"Indexes can contain sensitive fields and must enforce equivalent access-control and tenant-isolation requirements.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous indexing reduces write-path coupling but creates indexing lag and additional processing load.","test_case":"Delete or corrupt the search index and rebuild it from authoritative data, then compare required search results.","test_input":{"authoritative_records":1000,"index_available":false},"expected_test_result":"The index can be reconstructed from authoritative data and returns the required results after rebuild.","testing_guidance":"Test indexing lag, missed events, duplicate events, rebuilds, partial index failure, authorization filtering, and large datasets.","verification_method":"Index rebuild testing and consistency comparison.","verification_guidance":"Measure completeness and freshness against explicit search requirements rather than assuming immediate consistency.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SEARCH_AND_DERIVED_DATA_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B033","D33-B067","D33-B086"],"depends_on":["D33-B033"],"requires":["D33-B033"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B022"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established search-index and derived-data architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B088","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Object storage architecture","concept":"Binary object storage versus transactional metadata storage","knowledge_type":"STORAGE_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture separate large binary objects from transactional metadata?","answer":"Store large binary content in a storage mechanism designed for object or blob workloads while keeping authoritative transactional metadata in the system responsible for business state. Define object identity, integrity checks, lifecycle, access control, upload/download semantics, and recovery relationships between metadata and content.","explanation":"Large files can impose inefficient storage and transactional characteristics when embedded directly in an operational database. Separating content from metadata can improve scalability and lifecycle control, but it creates a consistency relationship that architecture must explicitly manage.","principle":"Binary content and transactional metadata should have explicit ownership and lifecycle relationships.","objective":"Handle large objects efficiently without losing business-level integrity or access control.","requirements":["Object ownership is defined.","Metadata relationship is explicit.","Integrity verification exists.","Lifecycle and deletion behavior are defined.","Access control covers both metadata and content."],"constraints":["Object storage introduces a second persistence boundary.","Metadata and object operations may not be atomic together.","Large objects require streaming or bounded transfer.","Deletion and retention policies may differ."],"preconditions":["File or object requirements are known.","Data classification exists.","Business metadata ownership is defined."],"input":["Object content","Object identifier","Metadata","Owner","Integrity hash"],"output":"A storage architecture separating object content from transactional metadata with explicit consistency and lifecycle behavior.","expected_behavior":"Metadata and object content remain associated through stable identity and integrity validation while each storage system retains its appropriate responsibility.","incorrect_code":"def upload(file_bytes):\n    database.insert_blob(file_bytes)\n","corrected_code":"def upload(file_bytes, metadata):\n    object_id = object_store.put(file_bytes)\n    database.insert_metadata(object_id, metadata)\n    return object_id\n","failure_mode":"Large binary content is stored directly in a transactional path without considering object lifecycle or transfer characteristics.","expected_error":"Large uploads can increase transaction duration, storage pressure, and operational coupling.","cause":"Binary workload characteristics were treated like ordinary transactional records.","root_cause":"Storage requirements were not decomposed by data type and access pattern.","solution":"Separate object content from business metadata and define reconciliation behavior for partial failures.","edge_case":"Metadata creation can fail after object upload, leaving an orphaned object; cleanup or reconciliation must be part of the architecture.","security_implication":"Object URLs or identifiers must not bypass authorization applied to business metadata.","performance_implication":"Object storage and streaming can reduce transactional database load but introduce additional network and storage operations.","test_case":"Complete an upload where metadata persistence fails and verify that the architecture can identify and clean up the orphaned object.","test_input":{"object_upload":true,"metadata_commit":false},"expected_test_result":"The orphaned object is detectable and follows the documented reconciliation or cleanup policy.","testing_guidance":"Test partial upload, metadata failure, duplicate upload, checksum mismatch, unauthorized download, deletion, retention, and large-object streaming.","verification_method":"Storage integration tests and reconciliation testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both object integrity and business-level authorization independently.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBJECT_STORAGE_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B052","D33-B067"],"depends_on":["D33-B009"],"requires":["D33-B052"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B033"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established object-storage and transactional-metadata architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B089","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Cryptographic architecture","concept":"Cryptographic key lifecycle and separation of key material from encrypted data","knowledge_type":"CRYPTOGRAPHIC_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture manage encryption keys across storage, rotation, access, and recovery requirements?","answer":"Separate cryptographic key management from application data storage where appropriate and define key generation, access, rotation, versioning, revocation, backup, and recovery requirements. Encrypted data should retain enough metadata to identify the required key version without exposing key material.","explanation":"Encryption at rest is incomplete if key lifecycle is undefined. Keys require stronger access controls and different lifecycle semantics from ordinary application data. Architecture should therefore establish ownership, rotation, recovery, and separation boundaries before selecting implementation details.","principle":"Cryptographic key material requires a distinct lifecycle and trust boundary from the data it protects.","objective":"Protect encrypted information while maintaining controlled key access and recoverability.","requirements":["Key ownership is defined.","Key versions are identifiable.","Rotation and revocation are defined.","Key access is restricted.","Recovery requirements are documented."],"constraints":["Key loss can make encrypted data unrecoverable.","Rotation can require re-encryption or envelope-key changes.","Application availability may depend on key-management availability.","Backup of keys creates additional security requirements."],"preconditions":["Data requiring encryption is classified.","Threat model is known.","Recovery requirements exist."],"input":["Plaintext data","Data-encryption key","Key-encryption key","Key version","Ciphertext metadata"],"output":"A cryptographic key lifecycle architecture separating key management from protected data and defining rotation and recovery.","expected_behavior":"Encrypted data can be decrypted only through authorized access to the appropriate key version and remains recoverable according to the documented key lifecycle.","incorrect_code":"ENCRYPTION_KEY = "hard-coded-secret"\n","corrected_code":"key_version = key_manager.current_version()\nciphertext = encrypt(data, key_manager.get_key(key_version))\n","failure_mode":"Long-lived key material is embedded directly in application source code.","expected_error":"Source disclosure or repository compromise can expose encryption keys and all data protected by them.","cause":"Key lifecycle and secret boundary were not modeled.","root_cause":"Encryption was implemented without separating cryptographic key management from application code.","solution":"Use an appropriate key-management boundary, version keys, restrict access, and define rotation and recovery.","edge_case":"Emergency recovery may require access to historical key versions; destroying old keys too early can make retained data unrecoverable.","security_implication":"Key compromise can expose large amounts of protected data; separation and least-privilege access reduce blast radius.","performance_implication":"Key-management operations can add latency; envelope encryption and controlled key caching can reduce repeated key-service calls while respecting security requirements.","test_case":"Encrypt data under one key version, rotate to a new version, and verify that supported old data remains decryptable according to the retention policy.","test_input":{"old_key_version":"v1","new_key_version":"v2","retained_ciphertext":true},"expected_test_result":"Data remains accessible only through authorized key versions and the rotation policy is correctly enforced.","testing_guidance":"Test rotation, revoked keys, historical versions, recovery, unauthorized key access, backup restoration, and application startup when key management is unavailable.","verification_method":"Cryptographic integration tests and key-management security review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no production key material is embedded in source, logs, configuration repositories, or test fixtures.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CRYPTOGRAPHIC_KEY_MANAGEMENT_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B035","D33-B052","D33-B053"],"depends_on":["D33-B035"],"requires":["D33-B035"],"extends":["D33-B035"],"refines":["D33-B052"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established cryptographic key-management and encryption architecture practice","NIST key-management guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B090","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Time architecture","concept":"Wall-clock time versus monotonic time and event-time semantics","knowledge_type":"DISTRIBUTED_TIME_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture distinguish timestamps used for human meaning, event ordering, expiration, and duration measurement?","answer":"Use wall-clock time for real-world timestamps and business dates when appropriate, but use a monotonic clock for measuring elapsed durations within a process. In distributed systems, distinguish event time from processing time and define ordering or expiry semantics explicitly because clocks can drift, adjust, or disagree across machines.","explanation":"Time appears simple but creates subtle architectural failures. Wall clocks can move forward or backward, while monotonic clocks are suitable for elapsed-time measurement but are not meaningful global timestamps. Distributed events can also arrive out of order. Architecture must therefore assign each time requirement to the correct semantic model.","principle":"Time semantics should be chosen according to whether the requirement concerns human chronology, elapsed duration, expiration, or distributed event ordering.","objective":"Prevent incorrect timeout, ordering, expiry, and audit behavior caused by ambiguous time semantics.","requirements":["Timestamp meaning is explicit.","Duration measurement uses appropriate clock semantics.","Event-time and processing-time distinctions are documented where relevant.","Clock synchronization assumptions are explicit."],"constraints":["Wall clocks can drift or change.","Distributed event arrival order can differ from event occurrence order.","Some environments have limited clock precision.","Cross-system timestamps may use different time sources."],"preconditions":["Temporal requirements are identified.","Distributed boundaries are known.","Expiry and ordering semantics are documented."],"input":["Event timestamp","Processing timestamp","Current wall time","Monotonic start time","Clock assumptions"],"output":"A temporal architecture specifying timestamp, duration, expiry, and ordering semantics.","expected_behavior":"Duration calculations are not corrupted by wall-clock adjustments, and distributed event ordering follows an explicit documented rule.","incorrect_code":"start = time.time()\ndo_work()\nelapsed = time.time() - start\n","corrected_code":"start = time.monotonic()\ndo_work()\nelapsed = time.monotonic() - start\n","failure_mode":"Wall-clock changes affect elapsed-duration measurement.","expected_error":"Timeout or performance calculations can become negative, unexpectedly long, or otherwise incorrect.","cause":"Wall-clock time was used for a monotonic duration requirement.","root_cause":"Temporal semantics were not distinguished architecturally.","solution":"Use monotonic time for elapsed durations and explicitly define wall-clock/event-time semantics for timestamps and ordering.","edge_case":"A distributed workflow may receive an event with a timestamp later than another event but process it first; event ordering must not be inferred solely from local processing time.","security_implication":"Incorrect expiry or timestamp validation can weaken authentication, authorization, replay protection, or audit interpretation.","performance_implication":"Clock retrieval overhead is generally small; the major performance concern is incorrect timeout behavior causing unnecessary retries or resource retention.","test_case":"Simulate a wall-clock adjustment during a duration measurement and verify that elapsed-time calculation remains monotonic.","test_input":{"clock_adjustment":"backward","operation":"duration_measurement"},"expected_test_result":"Elapsed duration remains non-negative and follows monotonic progression independent of wall-clock adjustment.","testing_guidance":"Test clock skew, daylight-saving changes where applicable to local representations, expiration boundaries, event reordering, duplicate timestamps, and distributed clock differences.","verification_method":"Time-semantics unit tests, distributed integration tests, and architecture review.","verification_guidance":"Verify every time-dependent mechanism identifies whether it requires wall-clock, monotonic, event-time, or processing-time semantics.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TIME_AND_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B057","D33-B063","D33-B067"],"depends_on":["D33-B057"],"requires":["D33-B057"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B063"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed-systems time semantics practice","Python time API semantics"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D33-B091","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment architecture","concept":"Feature flags and controlled capability rollout","knowledge_type":"ROLLOUT_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should architecture use feature flags to control release or activation of a capability?","answer":"Use feature flags when requirements include controlled activation, staged exposure, rapid disablement, tenant-specific rollout, experimentation, or operational risk reduction. Define flag ownership, evaluation scope, default behavior, lifecycle, removal criteria, and failure behavior so flags do not become permanent undocumented architecture state.","explanation":"A feature flag introduces runtime variability into system behavior. It can reduce deployment risk when the architecture requires independent deployment from activation, but it also creates additional states that must be tested and governed. Flags should therefore be treated as temporary or explicitly managed configuration rather than invisible conditional code.","principle":"Runtime-controlled capabilities require explicit lifecycle, ownership, safe defaults, and removal criteria.","objective":"Separate deployment from activation when controlled rollout materially reduces operational risk.","requirements":["Flag ownership is defined.","Default behavior is safe.","Evaluation scope is explicit.","Rollout and rollback behavior are defined.","Flag removal criteria exist."],"constraints":["Every flag can create additional behavioral states.","Flag systems become dependencies if unavailable.","Stale flags increase architectural complexity.","Security-sensitive flags require stronger governance."],"preconditions":["Capability rollout requirements are known.","Deployment and activation are separable.","Operational ownership exists."],"input":["Capability","Flag state","Evaluation context","Rollout policy"],"output":"A governed feature-flag architecture with controlled activation and removal.","expected_behavior":"A capability can be activated or disabled according to explicit policy without requiring an unsafe emergency code change.","incorrect_code":"if ENABLE_NEW_FEATURE:\n    execute_new_path()\n","corrected_code":"flag = feature_flags.evaluate("new_feature", context)\nif flag.enabled:\n    execute_new_path()\nelse:\n    execute_existing_path()\n","failure_mode":"A permanent or undocumented flag changes system behavior without defined ownership or removal.","expected_error":"Architecture accumulates hidden behavioral states and becomes difficult to reason about or test.","cause":"Feature flags were treated as ordinary conditionals rather than controlled deployment architecture.","root_cause":"Rollout requirements were not translated into a lifecycle-managed architecture.","solution":"Define flag ownership, scope, defaults, observability, rollout, rollback, expiry, and removal.","edge_case":"A flag service may be unavailable; critical capabilities require a deterministic local fallback rather than an undefined dependency.","security_implication":"Security controls implemented through remotely changeable flags require strong authorization, audit, and safe defaults.","performance_implication":"Flag evaluation adds runtime overhead; local or cached evaluation may be appropriate where consistency requirements permit it.","test_case":"Disable a newly deployed capability through its feature flag and verify that the defined fallback remains functional.","test_input":{"flag":"new_feature","initial_state":"enabled","changed_state":"disabled"},"expected_test_result":"The system transitions to the documented fallback without requiring redeployment.","testing_guidance":"Test default state, unavailable flag service, rollout scope, concurrent changes, stale configuration, rollback, and flag removal.","verification_method":"Deployment integration tests and configuration lifecycle review.","verification_guidance":"Verify every production flag has an owner, documented lifecycle, tested fallback, and removal or permanent-governance decision.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROGRESSIVE_DELIVERY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B035","D33-B056","D33-B059"],"depends_on":["D33-B056"],"requires":["D33-B035"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B056"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established feature-flag and progressive-delivery architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B092","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment architecture","concept":"Safe rollout and rollback strategy","knowledge_type":"DEPLOYMENT_STRATEGY_DECISION","question":"How should architecture choose and validate rollout and rollback mechanisms for changes with operational risk?","answer":"Define rollout stages, health criteria, exposure boundaries, rollback or forward-fix conditions, data compatibility constraints, and recovery ownership before deployment. The strategy must account for changes that cannot be safely reversed after persistent state or external side effects have changed.","explanation":"Rollback is not always equivalent to deploying the previous binary. Database migrations, external integrations, queued messages, and irreversible side effects can make software rollback unsafe. Architecture should therefore define reversible and irreversible components separately and establish recovery behavior before rollout.","principle":"A deployment is safely reversible only when all affected state and external effects remain compatible with the rollback strategy.","objective":"Reduce deployment risk by making rollout, rollback, and recovery behavior explicit.","requirements":["Rollout stages are defined.","Health criteria are measurable.","Rollback triggers are explicit.","Persistent-state compatibility is addressed.","Irreversible effects have a recovery strategy."],"constraints":["Some migrations cannot be reversed safely.","Rollback may increase inconsistency if versions are incompatible.","External systems may retain effects after application rollback.","Rollback itself can introduce operational risk."],"preconditions":["Deployment topology is known.","Change impact is assessed.","Persistent and external state changes are identified."],"input":["Release","Current version","Target version","Health signals","State migration","Rollback policy"],"output":"A rollout architecture defining staged exposure, health gates, rollback constraints, and recovery behavior.","expected_behavior":"A release progresses only when defined health conditions are satisfied and stops or recovers when documented failure conditions occur.","incorrect_code":"deploy(new_version)\nif failure:\n    deploy(old_version)\n","corrected_code":"deploy_canary(new_version)\nif health_gate.failed():\n    halt_rollout()\n    execute_documented_recovery()\nelse:\n    expand_rollout()\n","failure_mode":"The previous binary is assumed to be a universally safe rollback target.","expected_error":"Incompatible schema or external state can make rollback fail or corrupt behavior.","cause":"Rollback was modeled only as binary replacement.","root_cause":"Persistent-state and external-effect compatibility were omitted from deployment architecture.","solution":"Define rollout gates, compatibility windows, rollback constraints, and recovery procedures.","edge_case":"A release may partially execute an irreversible external action before failure; the architecture must define compensation or reconciliation rather than assuming rollback erases the effect.","security_implication":"Failed security changes can temporarily expose protected paths; rollout gates should include security-critical checks.","performance_implication":"Staged rollout can temporarily increase infrastructure usage but reduces blast radius during validation.","test_case":"Introduce a controlled canary failure and verify that rollout stops without promoting the affected version further.","test_input":{"rollout_stage":"canary","health_gate":"failed"},"expected_test_result":"Promotion stops and the documented recovery path is invoked.","testing_guidance":"Test canary failure, rollback compatibility, partial migration, queue compatibility, external side effects, and recovery after interrupted deployment.","verification_method":"Deployment rehearsal and release-engineering tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that rollback claims are backed by actual compatibility analysis rather than assuming the previous version is safe.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELEASE_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B026","D33-B056","D33-B065","D33-B091"],"depends_on":["D33-B026","D33-B056"],"requires":["D33-B065"],"extends":["D33-B056"],"refines":["D33-B026"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established deployment, progressive-delivery, and schema-compatibility practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B093","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture evolution","concept":"Legacy migration and strangler architecture","knowledge_type":"MIGRATION_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture support incremental replacement of a legacy system without requiring an immediate full rewrite?","answer":"Define bounded replacement seams, route selected capabilities through new components, preserve explicit ownership of data and behavior, and establish measurable exit criteria for the legacy path. Incremental migration should reduce coupling over time rather than create a permanent parallel architecture with ambiguous authority.","explanation":"Large legacy replacements carry substantial operational and data risk. A staged migration can isolate capabilities and progressively move responsibility, but only if boundaries, ownership, compatibility, and retirement criteria are explicit. The architecture should avoid creating two permanently authoritative systems.","principle":"Incremental replacement requires explicit seams, ownership transfer, compatibility strategy, and retirement criteria.","objective":"Reduce migration risk while progressively moving capabilities from legacy architecture to a target architecture.","requirements":["Migration boundaries are defined.","Authority ownership is explicit.","Routing strategy is controlled.","Data migration is governed.","Legacy retirement criteria exist."],"constraints":["Legacy interfaces may impose constraints.","Two systems may temporarily coexist.","Data synchronization can create consistency complexity.","Migration can last longer than planned."],"preconditions":["Legacy capabilities are inventoried.","Target architecture is defined.","Migration priorities and constraints are known."],"input":["Legacy capability","Target capability","Migration boundary","Routing policy","Data ownership"],"output":"A staged migration architecture with controlled coexistence and explicit retirement conditions.","expected_behavior":"A migrated capability has one clearly defined authority while traffic can transition progressively from legacy to target implementation.","incorrect_code":"def get_customer(id):\n    return legacy.get_customer(id)\n","corrected_code":"def get_customer(id):\n    if migration_router.uses_target("customer", id):\n        return target.get_customer(id)\n    return legacy.get_customer(id)\n","failure_mode":"Legacy and replacement systems both become authoritative for the same business state.","expected_error":"Divergent state and inconsistent behavior can emerge during migration.","cause":"Migration ownership was not defined.","root_cause":"The migration was treated as code replacement rather than an architectural transition.","solution":"Define capability seams, authority transfer, compatibility, reconciliation, and legacy retirement gates.","edge_case":"A migration can be interrupted for months; the architecture must remain safe and operable during an extended transition.","security_implication":"Legacy paths may retain weaker security controls; migration architecture must prevent accidental bypass of target security boundaries.","performance_implication":"Temporary routing and synchronization can increase latency and infrastructure load.","test_case":"Route one bounded capability to the target implementation and verify that authority and fallback behavior remain explicit.","test_input":{"capability":"customer_read","target_enabled":true},"expected_test_result":"The selected capability uses the target path while unaffected capabilities continue using their documented owners.","testing_guidance":"Test dual-run comparisons, routing, authority transfer, rollback, reconciliation, partial migration, and legacy retirement.","verification_method":"Migration rehearsal, data reconciliation, and architecture conformance testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each migration stage has measurable ownership and exit criteria.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LEGACY_MIGRATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B025","D33-B026","D33-B059"],"depends_on":["D33-B009","D33-B026"],"requires":["D33-B025"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B026"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established incremental legacy modernization and strangler-pattern architecture practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B094","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture evolution","concept":"Architecture drift detection and conformance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_CONFORMANCE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture detect when implementation has diverged from approved architectural boundaries?","answer":"Define machine-checkable or reviewable architecture constraints and continuously compare implementation structure, dependencies, deployment topology, and ownership against them. Architecture conformance should identify intentional exceptions separately from accidental drift.","explanation":"Architecture can diverge gradually through dependency shortcuts, misplaced responsibilities, undocumented integrations, or deployment changes. A static architecture document alone cannot detect this drift. Conformance mechanisms translate important architectural rules into observable checks and reviewable evidence.","principle":"Critical architecture constraints should be expressed in a form that can be checked against implementation and deployment reality.","objective":"Detect unintended architecture drift before it becomes difficult or expensive to correct.","requirements":["Architectural rules are explicit.","Conformance evidence is defined.","Exceptions are documented.","Checks run at appropriate lifecycle stages.","Violations have ownership."],"constraints":["Not every architectural property is machine-checkable.","Strict checks can block legitimate changes.","Architecture evolves and rules must be versioned.","Dynamic runtime behavior may require runtime evidence."],"preconditions":["Approved architecture exists.","Critical boundaries are identified.","Implementation structure is accessible to validation tooling."],"input":["Architecture rule","Code dependency graph","Deployment topology","Exception record"],"output":"An architecture-conformance mechanism identifying violations and approved exceptions.","expected_behavior":"Unapproved dependency or boundary violations are detected and surfaced for correction or explicit architectural review.","incorrect_code":"from infrastructure.database import Database\nfrom domain.rules import BusinessRule\n","corrected_code":"from application.services import BusinessService\n\n# Infrastructure access remains behind the approved application boundary.\nservice = BusinessService()\n","failure_mode":"Implementation imports or depends directly on components outside its approved architectural boundary.","expected_error":"Layering and ownership rules become unenforceable and architectural coupling increases.","cause":"Architecture rules existed only as documentation.","root_cause":"No conformance mechanism connected architecture decisions to implementation evidence.","solution":"Define architecture rules and automate appropriate dependency, ownership, deployment, and configuration checks.","edge_case":"An intentional exception may be required; it should be explicitly recorded, scoped, reviewed, and time-bounded where possible.","security_implication":"Architecture drift can create unexpected trust-boundary crossings and privilege exposure.","performance_implication":"Static conformance checks generally add build-time cost rather than production latency.","test_case":"Introduce a prohibited dependency and verify that the conformance check detects it.","test_input":{"dependency":"domain->infrastructure","allowed":false},"expected_test_result":"The architecture check reports a violation unless an approved exception exists.","testing_guidance":"Test forbidden dependencies, allowed dependencies, exception handling, deleted modules, deployment drift, and rule-version changes.","verification_method":"Static architecture tests and deployment conformance checks.","verification_guidance":"Verify that conformance rules represent approved architecture rather than assumptions embedded in tooling.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_CONFORMANCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B030","D33-B060","D33-B058"],"depends_on":["D33-B030"],"requires":["D33-B060"],"extends":["D33-B060"],"refines":["D33-B058"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture conformance and fitness-function practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B095","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture governance","concept":"Architecture fitness functions and continuous architectural verification","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_FITNESS_DECISION","question":"How can important architectural properties remain continuously verifiable after the initial architecture review?","answer":"Translate selected architectural requirements into measurable fitness functions or recurring verification checks. These can address dependency boundaries, latency budgets, availability objectives, security constraints, deployment properties, or data-flow rules. Each check should have an owner, threshold or rule, evidence source, and response to violation.","explanation":"Architecture review is a point-in-time activity, while systems evolve continuously. Fitness functions provide a mechanism for preserving important architectural characteristics as implementation changes. They should target properties that materially matter rather than measuring everything possible.","principle":"Important architectural properties should have repeatable evidence checks when practical.","objective":"Keep architecture aligned with evolving implementation through continuous evidence rather than one-time approval.","requirements":["Important properties are selected.","Measurement or rule is defined.","Threshold or acceptance condition exists.","Evidence source is identified.","Violation response is defined."],"constraints":["Some architectural qualities are difficult to quantify.","Poorly chosen metrics can create gaming or local optimization.","Thresholds can become stale as requirements change.","Continuous checks consume engineering resources."],"preconditions":["Architecture requirements exist.","Measurable architectural properties are identified.","Verification ownership exists."],"input":["Architectural property","Measurement","Threshold","Evidence","Owner"],"output":"A set of continuously evaluated architectural fitness functions linked to requirements.","expected_behavior":"Changes that violate selected architectural properties produce actionable evidence rather than remaining invisible until a major incident or review.","incorrect_code":"def architecture_check():\n    return True\n","corrected_code":"def architecture_check(latency_ms, budget_ms):\n    return latency_ms <= budget_ms\n","failure_mode":"Architecture quality is declared compliant without measuring the property being claimed.","expected_error":"Architecture degradation remains undetected despite nominal review completion.","cause":"Verification was reduced to documentation status.","root_cause":"No repeatable evidence mechanism existed for important architectural properties.","solution":"Define targeted fitness functions with measurable evidence and explicit violation handling.","edge_case":"A threshold can be temporarily exceeded for a documented reason; the exception should preserve visibility rather than disabling the check globally.","security_implication":"Security architecture properties should be continuously checked where feasible, such as prohibited dependency paths or missing control boundaries.","performance_implication":"Fitness functions add validation cost but can prevent expensive architectural regressions.","test_case":"Provide a measured value above the architectural threshold and verify that the fitness function reports non-conformance.","test_input":{"measured_latency_ms":250,"budget_ms":200},"expected_test_result":"The check reports failure because the measured property exceeds the declared requirement.","testing_guidance":"Test threshold boundaries, missing evidence, stale thresholds, exceptions, and false-positive handling.","verification_method":"Automated architecture checks and periodic architecture review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each fitness function traces back to a meaningful requirement or architectural risk.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_FITNESS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B030","D33-B058","D33-B094"],"depends_on":["D33-B030"],"requires":["D33-B058"],"extends":["D33-B094"],"refines":["D33-B060"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture fitness-function and continuous verification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B096","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Operational readiness","concept":"Architecture readiness and operational acceptance criteria","knowledge_type":"OPERATIONAL_READINESS_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should architecture define before a system or major capability is considered operationally ready?","answer":"Define readiness criteria covering observability, failure handling, security controls, backup and recovery, capacity assumptions, ownership, deployment and rollback procedures, supportability, and verification evidence. Operational readiness should be tied to the actual requirements and failure consequences of the capability.","explanation":"A design can be technically implementable while still being operationally incomplete. Architecture must account for how the system will be observed, recovered, secured, changed, and supported. Readiness criteria turn those expectations into explicit acceptance conditions.","principle":"Architecture is operationally complete only when required operation, recovery, security, and verification mechanisms have evidence.","objective":"Prevent deployment of architecturally incomplete capabilities that cannot be safely operated.","requirements":["Operational owner is defined.","Observability exists.","Recovery procedure exists.","Security controls are verified.","Deployment and rollback procedures are defined.","Capacity assumptions are documented."],"constraints":["Readiness criteria differ by criticality.","Some evidence requires production-like environments.","Operational controls can add implementation cost.","Not all risks can be eliminated before deployment."],"preconditions":["System requirements are known.","Failure modes are identified.","Operational ownership exists."],"input":["Capability","Criticality","Operational requirements","Verification evidence"],"output":"An operational-readiness architecture and acceptance checklist linked to system requirements.","expected_behavior":"A capability is not considered ready until required operational evidence satisfies its defined acceptance criteria.","incorrect_code":"def release_ready():\n    return build_passed\n","corrected_code":"def release_ready(evidence):\n    required = {"security", "observability", "recovery", "rollback"}\n    return required.issubset(evidence.passed_controls)\n","failure_mode":"Build success is treated as equivalent to operational readiness.","expected_error":"A deployable artifact may lack recovery, observability, security, or support controls.","cause":"Readiness was defined only at the build or test stage.","root_cause":"Operational requirements were not included in architecture acceptance.","solution":"Define readiness criteria spanning security, observability, recovery, deployment, ownership, and verification.","edge_case":"A low-criticality capability may legitimately have reduced recovery objectives; architecture should scale readiness requirements to impact rather than applying identical controls everywhere.","security_implication":"Security readiness should be verified before exposure to real users or sensitive environments.","performance_implication":"Capacity and performance evidence should reflect expected workload and documented uncertainty rather than fabricated benchmarks.","test_case":"Remove one mandatory readiness evidence item and verify that the capability is rejected as operationally incomplete.","test_input":{"passed_controls":["security","observability","rollback"],"required_control":"recovery"},"expected_test_result":"Readiness fails until the required recovery evidence is supplied.","testing_guidance":"Test missing evidence, expired evidence, changed requirements, emergency exceptions, and post-deployment monitoring.","verification_method":"Operational-readiness review and automated acceptance checks.","verification_guidance":"Trace every readiness gate to a documented requirement, risk, or operational responsibility.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OPERATIONAL_READINESS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B030","D33-B049","D33-B054","D33-B060"],"depends_on":["D33-B060"],"requires":["D33-B054"],"extends":["D33-B060"],"refines":["D33-B058"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established operational readiness and production acceptance practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B097","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture evolution","concept":"Decision reversal criteria and architecture trigger conditions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_EVOLUTION_DECISION","question":"How should architecture define when an existing architectural decision must be reconsidered?","answer":"Record the assumptions, constraints, trigger conditions, and evidence that would invalidate an architectural decision. Examples include workload changes, dependency lifecycle changes, regulatory requirements, reliability failures, cost constraints, or new security requirements. Reconsideration criteria should not automatically imply replacement; they should trigger structured review.","explanation":"Architecture decisions are made under assumptions that can change. Without explicit reversal conditions, teams may continue using an unsuitable design simply because the original decision was documented. Conversely, premature redesign can create unnecessary complexity. Trigger-based review creates a controlled evolution mechanism.","principle":"Architectural decisions should document the conditions under which their assumptions require reassessment.","objective":"Enable evidence-driven architecture evolution without treating every change as an automatic redesign.","requirements":["Decision assumptions are recorded.","Trigger conditions are explicit.","Evidence required for reassessment is defined.","Review ownership is assigned.","Reversal does not occur automatically without evaluation."],"constraints":["Some triggers are difficult to quantify.","Changing architecture can be costly.","Evidence may be ambiguous.","Business requirements can change faster than architecture governance cycles."],"preconditions":["An architecture decision exists.","Its assumptions and trade-offs are documented.","Ownership for review exists."],"input":["Architecture decision","Assumption","Observed evidence","Trigger condition"],"output":"A decision-evolution policy identifying when architectural review must be reopened.","expected_behavior":"Material changes to documented assumptions trigger review while stable conditions do not cause unnecessary redesign.","incorrect_code":"if workload > 1000:\n    replace_architecture()\n","corrected_code":"if decision.trigger_condition_met(observed_context):\n    architecture_review.request(decision.id, evidence=observed_context)\n","failure_mode":"A threshold automatically forces architecture replacement without review.","expected_error":"Architecture can oscillate or incur unnecessary migration risk from simplistic triggers.","cause":"Trigger conditions were confused with replacement decisions.","root_cause":"Architecture evolution lacked an explicit review stage between observation and redesign.","solution":"Define triggers as review conditions and preserve decision evidence, assumptions, and alternatives.","edge_case":"Multiple triggers may occur simultaneously and point in different directions; the architecture review must evaluate the combined context.","security_implication":"Security or compliance changes may require urgent review even when other architectural assumptions remain stable.","performance_implication":"Reassessment can identify when scaling architecture is justified by measured workload rather than premature optimization.","test_case":"Change a documented decision assumption beyond its trigger threshold and verify that review is requested rather than architecture being changed automatically.","test_input":{"assumption":"workload","original_limit":1000,"observed":5000},"expected_test_result":"The decision enters architecture review with the observed evidence attached.","testing_guidance":"Test trigger boundaries, conflicting triggers, missing evidence, temporary excursions, and review completion.","verification_method":"Architecture governance workflow test and decision-record review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each trigger is traceable to an explicit assumption or risk from the original decision.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_EVOLUTION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B040","D33-B058","D33-B060"],"depends_on":["D33-B040"],"requires":["D33-B058"],"extends":["D33-B040"],"refines":["D33-B060"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture decision records and evolution-governance practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B098","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture governance","concept":"Architecture exception management and expiry","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_EXCEPTION_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle intentional deviations from approved architectural rules?","answer":"Model exceptions explicitly with scope, reason, affected components, risk, compensating controls, owner, approval authority, evidence, and expiry or review date. An exception should not silently redefine the architecture; if the deviation becomes permanent, the architecture itself should be reconsidered and updated.","explanation":"Real systems sometimes require deviations from standard architecture because of legacy constraints, emergency conditions, or unusual requirements. Undocumented exceptions become permanent hidden architecture. Explicit exception management preserves visibility and prevents temporary compromises from becoming unmanaged dependencies.","principle":"Architectural exceptions must be explicit, bounded, owned, and periodically reconsidered.","objective":"Permit justified deviations without losing architectural integrity or governance visibility.","requirements":["Exception scope is explicit.","Reason and risk are documented.","Compensating controls are defined.","Owner and approval authority are known.","Review or expiry date exists."],"constraints":["Some exceptions may be required for long periods.","Expiration may be operationally difficult.","Too many exceptions can indicate that the architecture itself is unsuitable.","Emergency exceptions may require accelerated approval."],"preconditions":["Architectural rules are documented.","Exception authority exists.","Affected components can be identified."],"input":["Rule","Exception","Reason","Risk","Compensating control","Expiry"],"output":"A governed architecture-exception record and lifecycle.","expected_behavior":"An approved exception is accepted only within its defined scope and remains visible until resolved, renewed, or incorporated into an updated architecture.","incorrect_code":"# Ignore architecture rule for this service\n","corrected_code":"exception = ArchitectureException(\n    rule="no_direct_db_access",\n    scope="migration-service",\n    reason="legacy_transition",\n    expires_at="2026-12-31"\n)\nexception_registry.require_approval(exception)\n","failure_mode":"Architectural rules are bypassed through undocumented special cases.","expected_error":"Architecture conformance loses meaning and temporary compromises become permanent.","cause":"Exception handling was informal.","root_cause":"Governance did not distinguish approved exceptions from accidental violations.","solution":"Create a bounded exception lifecycle with explicit approval, evidence, compensating controls, and expiry.","edge_case":"An exception may need renewal because migration takes longer than expected; renewal should trigger reassessment rather than silently extending it.","security_implication":"Security architecture exceptions can introduce high-risk trust-boundary violations and require explicit compensating controls.","performance_implication":"Exceptions may permit temporary performance optimizations, but their impact should remain measurable and documented.","test_case":"Create an exception with an expired date and verify that it is reported as requiring review.","test_input":{"exception_status":"expired"},"expected_test_result":"The exception is not silently treated as permanently valid and is surfaced for review.","testing_guidance":"Test creation, approval, expiry, renewal, scope enforcement, revoked exceptions, and permanent-architecture conversion.","verification_method":"Governance workflow and architecture-conformance testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that exceptions are distinguishable from ordinary violations and cannot silently expand in scope.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B094","D33-B095","D33-B097"],"depends_on":["D33-B094"],"requires":["D33-B060"],"extends":["D33-B094"],"refines":["D33-B097"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture exception and governance practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B099","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture traceability","concept":"Requirement-to-architecture-to-verification traceability closure","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture establish end-to-end traceability from requirements through design decisions and verification evidence?","answer":"Create explicit links from each material requirement to architectural mechanisms, implementation boundaries, verification methods, and evidence. Unimplemented requirements, architecture decisions without requirements, and verification evidence without a clear requirement should be detectable as traceability gaps.","explanation":"Architecture assurance is strongest when the reasoning chain remains visible from need to evidence. This extends ordinary requirement traceability by including architecture decisions and verification outcomes. The resulting graph can expose orphaned requirements, unsupported architectural claims, and unverified critical mechanisms.","principle":"Every material architectural requirement should have an explainable path from requirement to mechanism to verification evidence.","objective":"Close the architecture assurance loop and make omissions detectable.","requirements":["Requirements have stable identifiers.","Architecture decisions reference requirements.","Verification references mechanisms or requirements.","Gaps are detectable.","Evidence status is explicit."],"constraints":["Some requirements map to multiple architectural mechanisms.","One mechanism can satisfy multiple requirements.","Evidence may be partial or environment-specific.","Traceability must survive architecture evolution."],"preconditions":["Requirement inventory exists.","Architecture decisions are recorded.","Verification evidence has identifiers."],"input":["Requirement","Architecture decision","Mechanism","Implementation","Verification evidence"],"output":"A traceability graph connecting requirements, architecture mechanisms, implementation, and verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"A material requirement can be traced to the architecture mechanism intended to satisfy it and to evidence showing whether that mechanism has been verified.","incorrect_code":"requirements = load_requirements()\nreturn all(requirements)\n","corrected_code":"for requirement in requirements:\n    mechanisms = architecture.find_mechanisms(requirement.id)\n    evidence = verification.find_evidence(requirement.id, mechanisms)\n    traceability.record(requirement.id, mechanisms, evidence)\n","failure_mode":"Requirement presence is reported without verifying that architecture and evidence exist for it.","expected_error":"Unimplemented or unverified requirements appear complete because inventory existence is mistaken for satisfaction.","cause":"Traceability stopped at requirement registration.","root_cause":"Requirement, architecture, implementation, and verification were modeled as disconnected artifacts.","solution":"Build stable cross-references and explicit satisfaction states across the complete assurance chain.","edge_case":"A requirement may be intentionally deferred or superseded; the traceability graph should record that state rather than deleting the relationship.","security_implication":"Security requirements require evidence tied to the actual enforcement mechanism rather than merely to policy documentation.","performance_implication":"Traceability processing is generally a build or governance concern, but large dependency graphs require efficient indexing.","test_case":"Create a requirement with an architecture decision but no verification evidence and verify that the traceability system reports an incomplete chain.","test_input":{"requirement":"R-001","architecture":"A-001","verification":null},"expected_test_result":"The requirement is reported as architecturally addressed but not verified.","testing_guidance":"Test complete chains, missing architecture, missing implementation, missing evidence, superseded requirements, many-to-many mappings, and version changes.","verification_method":"Traceability graph validation and architecture assurance review.","verification_guidance":"Verify that status reflects evidence state and never upgrades a requirement to satisfied solely because a design artifact exists.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_ASSURANCE_AND_TRACEABILITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B001","D33-B010","D33-B030","D33-B060","D33-B095","D33-B096"],"depends_on":["D33-B030","D33-B060"],"requires":["D33-B001"],"extends":["D33-B030"],"refines":["D33-B060"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established requirements traceability and architecture assurance practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D33-B100","dataset_id":"D33","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture closure","concept":"Architecture baseline, review gate, and controlled evolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_CLOSURE_DECISION","question":"How should a completed architecture be baselined so that implementation can proceed while future changes remain controlled and evidence-driven?","answer":"Establish an architecture baseline containing approved requirements, decisions, constraints, interfaces, deployment boundaries, known risks, rejected alternatives, verification obligations, exceptions, and evolution triggers. Define a review gate that confirms the baseline is sufficiently complete for the intended implementation stage. Subsequent changes should create explicit revisions rather than silently modifying the baseline.","explanation":"Architecture is neither an informal sketch nor an immutable artifact. A useful baseline establishes what has been decided, what remains uncertain, what must be verified, and what conditions would justify reopening decisions. This closes the Requirement → Architecture dataset by connecting selection, assurance, governance, and evolution into one controlled lifecycle.","principle":"Architecture should be baselined as an evidence-linked decision system with explicit verification obligations and controlled evolution.","objective":"Create a stable architectural reference that supports implementation, verification, governance, and future change without losing decision history.","requirements":["Approved requirements are linked.","Architecture decisions and rationale are recorded.","Constraints and assumptions are explicit.","Verification obligations are defined.","Known risks and exceptions are recorded.","Evolution triggers are documented.","Baseline version is immutable or revision-controlled."],"constraints":["Architecture may contain unresolved risks.","Some decisions require later implementation evidence.","Baseline governance must not prevent justified evolution.","The level of detail should match system criticality and complexity."],"preconditions":["Requirement decomposition is complete enough for the implementation stage.","Candidate architectures have been evaluated.","Major trade-offs and rejected alternatives are documented.","Verification strategy exists."],"input":["Requirements","Constraints","Architecture decisions","Trade-offs","Risks","Verification obligations","Exceptions","Evolution triggers"],"output":"A versioned architecture baseline with traceable decisions, verification obligations, known limitations, and controlled revision rules.","expected_behavior":"Implementation references a stable architecture baseline, while any material architectural change produces a new revision with preserved rationale, impact analysis, and verification requirements.","incorrect_code":"architecture = current_design()\nimplementation.use(architecture)\n","corrected_code":"baseline = architecture_registry.approved_baseline(version="1.0")\nimplementation.bind_to(baseline)\nif material_change_detected(change):\n    architecture_registry.open_revision(baseline, change)\n","failure_mode":"Implementation silently diverges from an undocumented or mutable architecture description.","expected_error":"Teams cannot reliably determine which architectural decisions are authoritative or whether implementation satisfies them.","cause":"No controlled architecture baseline or revision mechanism exists.","root_cause":"Architecture was treated as a transient design artifact instead of a governed decision baseline.","solution":"Create a versioned baseline, explicit review gate, traceability graph, verification obligations, exception registry, and controlled revision workflow.","edge_case":"A production emergency may require temporary deviation; the deviation should be recorded as an exception and incorporated into formal architecture review afterward.","security_implication":"Security boundaries and high-impact controls must remain traceable to the approved baseline; unauthorized architectural drift should be detectable.","performance_implication":"Architecture baselining itself has negligible runtime impact but enables explicit capacity and performance obligations to remain attached to the design.","test_case":"Create a material architecture change after baseline approval and verify that implementation cannot silently replace the baseline without creating a new revision and associated verification obligations.","test_input":{"baseline_version":"1.0","material_change":true},"expected_test_result":"A controlled architecture revision is opened with preserved historical baseline, impact analysis, and new verification obligations.","testing_guidance":"Test baseline approval, immutable history, revision creation, exception handling, traceability completeness, verification obligations, and rollback of an architectural revision.","verification_method":"Architecture governance review, traceability validation, conformance checks, and revision workflow testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the final baseline is complete enough for its intended implementation stage and that every material decision has rationale, relationships, evidence requirements, and an identified evolution path.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_AND_ASSURANCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B001","D33-B040","D33-B058","D33-B060","D33-B094","D33-B095","D33-B097","D33-B098","D33-B099"],"depends_on":["D33-B040","D33-B060","D33-B099"],"requires":["D33-B001","D33-B058"],"extends":["D33-B099"],"refines":["D33-B040","D33-B060"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture governance, architecture decision record, traceability, conformance, and continuous assurance practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B001","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology Selection","subtopic":"Technology selection framing","concept":"Requirements should be translated into measurable technology-selection criteria before specific technologies are selected.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should an engineering team select technology from a requirement rather than from familiarity or popularity?","answer":"Convert the requirement into explicit technical constraints and decision criteria, identify viable technology classes and concrete candidates, evaluate them against the criteria, document trade-offs and risks, and preserve credible alternatives. Technology selection should be evidence-driven and requirement-bound rather than preference-driven.","explanation":"A requirement such as low latency, offline operation, transactional integrity, horizontal scale, local deployment, or long-lived workflow execution does not directly identify a technology. The engineering task is to derive the properties the technology must provide, eliminate incompatible candidates, compare remaining candidates against measurable criteria, and record uncertainty. D34 therefore focuses on technology choice while D33 remains responsible for the architectural structure in which the technology is used.","principle":"Select technology because its verified capabilities satisfy the requirements and constraints, not because it is familiar, fashionable, or widely used.","objective":"Create a repeatable and auditable technology-selection process.","requirements":["Explicit functional requirements","Explicit non-functional requirements","Operational constraints","Compatibility requirements","Security requirements","Lifecycle and maintenance requirements"],"constraints":["Avoid technology selection based solely on popularity","Do not assume a technology is suitable because it worked in another system","Do not treat benchmark claims without workload context as proof","Do not hide operational complexity inside the selection decision"],"preconditions":["Requirements have been identified","Relevant architecture boundary is known","Decision criteria can be expressed or evaluated"],"input":{"requirement":"Example: transactional persistence with strong relational querying","constraints":["local development","production deployment","data integrity","long-term maintainability"]},"output":{"selection_artifact":["candidate technologies","evaluation criteria","trade-offs","risks","alternatives","evidence"]},"expected_behavior":"The decision record explains why candidates satisfy or fail explicit requirements and identifies evidence needed for unresolved uncertainty.","incorrect_code":"technology = "postgresql"  # selected without requirements or evaluation","corrected_code":"criteria = {"transactions": True, "relational_queries": True, "operability": True}\ncandidates = ["PostgreSQL", "SQLite"]\n# Evaluate candidates against the actual workload before selection.","failure_mode":"Technology selected before requirements and constraints are translated into decision criteria.","expected_error":"No runtime error is required; the failure is an architecture/engineering decision error.","cause":"Preference or familiarity substituted for explicit evaluation.","root_cause":"Missing requirement-to-capability traceability.","solution":"Create a technology decision record containing requirements, constraints, candidate capabilities, evidence, trade-offs, risks, and alternatives.","edge_case":"Two or more technologies satisfy all mandatory requirements; secondary criteria and operational evidence must then distinguish their applicability without creating an unsupported universal ranking.","security_implication":"Technology selection must account for authentication, authorization, encryption, patching, isolation, auditability, and secure configuration requirements.","performance_implication":"Performance criteria must specify workload, concurrency, data size, latency target, throughput target, and measurement method before performance evidence is considered.","test_case":"Given identical requirements and two viable technologies, verify that the selection record contains explicit criteria and evidence for both candidates.","test_input":{"mandatory_criteria":["transaction_integrity","relational_queries"],"candidate_set":["PostgreSQL","SQLite"]},"expected_test_result":"Every mandatory criterion is mapped to candidate capabilities or identified as requiring further verification.","testing_guidance":"Test the decision process itself by checking for missing criteria, unsupported capability claims, and unexamined alternatives.","verification_method":"Requirement-to-capability traceability review plus primary-source verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify technology capabilities against official documentation for the exact version considered and mark empirical claims as measured, estimated, inferred, or unknown.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Technology versions must be evaluated explicitly; no universal version is implied by this record.","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Established engineering decision practice; capability verification should use primary technology documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B001","D33-B040","D33-B058","D33-B060"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Requirements","Constraints","Evaluation criteria"],"extends":"D33-B040","refines":"D33-B001","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"D33-B099","derived_from":["Requirement-driven technology evaluation practice"],"provenance":["Primary technology documentation should be consulted for each candidate; PostgreSQL official documentation; SQLite official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B002","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Application Runtime Technology","subtopic":"Runtime selection","concept":"Application runtime selection must match workload model, ecosystem requirements, deployment constraints, concurrency model, and operational capabilities.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should an application runtime be selected?","answer":"Evaluate runtime candidates against workload characteristics, concurrency requirements, ecosystem availability, library compatibility, startup and resource constraints, deployment environment, security lifecycle, team capability, and required language semantics.","explanation":"Runtime selection is broader than choosing a programming language. The selected runtime determines execution semantics, concurrency mechanisms, packaging, dependency management, diagnostics, deployment requirements, and available libraries. For I/O-heavy services, an asynchronous runtime may be relevant; CPU-heavy workloads may require process or native execution strategies regardless of language. Python 3.11 provides a mature standard library and documented runtime behavior, but its suitability must still be established against the actual workload.","principle":"Choose the runtime whose execution model and ecosystem satisfy the workload and operational constraints.","objective":"Prevent runtime choice from becoming an unexamined default.","requirements":["Known workload type","Concurrency requirements","Dependency requirements","Deployment target","Runtime lifecycle requirements"],"constraints":["Do not infer performance from language reputation alone","Do not ignore runtime version compatibility","Do not confuse asynchronous I/O with parallel CPU execution","Do not select a runtime without considering production observability and support"],"preconditions":["Workload classification is available","Target deployment environment is known"],"input":{"workload":"I/O-heavy API service","constraints":["Linux deployment","async I/O","mature HTTP ecosystem","Python interoperability"]},"output":{"candidate_runtime":"Python 3.11+","selection_basis":["language ecosystem","asyncio support","deployment compatibility","library availability"]},"expected_behavior":"The runtime decision explicitly links workload properties to runtime capabilities and identifies workload-specific performance verification requirements.","incorrect_code":"# Incorrect decision logic: language popularity treated as the selection criterion\nruntime = "python"","corrected_code":"workload = {"io_bound": True, "async_io": True, "linux": True}\nrequired_capabilities = {"async_runtime": True, "http_ecosystem": True}\n# Candidate runtime must be evaluated against these requirements.","failure_mode":"Runtime chosen without matching execution semantics to workload characteristics.","expected_error":"Potentially unsuitable throughput, latency, resource usage, or concurrency behavior.","cause":"Workload model omitted from technology evaluation.","root_cause":"Technology capability was not traced to operational requirements.","solution":"Define workload characteristics first, evaluate candidate runtimes, then validate critical assumptions using representative tests.","edge_case":"A service can contain both CPU-bound and I/O-bound work; one runtime decision may require worker processes, native extensions, external services, or separate execution units.","security_implication":"Runtime and dependency lifecycle affects exposure to vulnerabilities and secure deployment requirements.","performance_implication":"Claims must be workload-specific and experimentally measured when performance is a selection criterion.","test_case":"Run representative I/O concurrency tests and CPU-bound workload tests using the candidate runtime and record actual measurement methodology.","test_input":{"workload":"1000 concurrent I/O operations","measurement":["latency","throughput","memory","CPU"]},"expected_test_result":"Measured results are captured without assuming a predetermined winner.","testing_guidance":"Use representative production-like workloads and repeat tests across candidate runtime versions where version compatibility matters.","verification_method":"Runtime documentation review plus representative workload testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify supported language/runtime features against the exact version and distinguish documentation-based capability from measured performance.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11.x documentation is an applicable reference; later versions require separate compatibility validation.","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary language/runtime documentation plus workload-specific empirical validation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B039","D33-B056"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Workload characterization"],"extends":"","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"","contrasts_with":"","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Python 3.11 official documentation"],"provenance":["Python 3.11 official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B003","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"API Technology","subtopic":"HTTP API framework selection","concept":"API framework selection depends on protocol requirements, validation needs, concurrency model, ecosystem, operational constraints, and compatibility.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When is an API framework such as FastAPI a candidate for a Python service?","answer":"FastAPI is a candidate when a Python service needs HTTP API development with typed request/response models, automatic API documentation, and an asynchronous-capable application model. Selection still requires evaluation against compatibility, operational, security, testing, and deployment requirements.","explanation":"A framework should be selected for capabilities required by the service rather than merely because it is popular. FastAPI provides documented mechanisms for building APIs and its official tutorial includes tested examples. The technology decision must still establish whether its dependency stack, lifecycle, middleware, validation model, deployment model, and supported Python versions fit the system.","principle":"Select a framework by mapping required API capabilities to documented framework behavior and verifying compatibility with the chosen runtime and deployment environment.","objective":"Make API framework selection reproducible and version-aware.","requirements":["HTTP API support","Request validation","Response serialization","API documentation","Testing support","Deployment compatibility"],"constraints":["Framework version must be pinned or bounded","Runtime/framework compatibility must be verified","Security middleware and authentication must be evaluated separately","Illustrative code must not be treated as execution evidence"],"preconditions":["Python runtime selected or under evaluation","API protocol requirements identified"],"input":{"requirements":["HTTP APIs","typed validation","OpenAPI documentation","async-capable handlers"]},"output":{"candidate":"FastAPI","evaluation_basis":["API feature set","Python compatibility","validation","documentation","testing ecosystem"]},"expected_behavior":"The selected framework satisfies mandatory API capabilities and has an explicit compatibility matrix with the runtime and dependencies.","incorrect_code":"from fastapi import FastAPI\napp = FastAPI()\n# Treating framework availability as proof that it meets production requirements is insufficient.","corrected_code":"from fastapi import FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get("/health")\ndef health() -> dict[str, str]:\n    return {"status": "ok"}\n\n# Production selection still requires version, security, testing,\n# deployment, and operational validation.","failure_mode":"Framework selected from familiarity without evaluating lifecycle, dependency, compatibility, or operational requirements.","expected_error":"Hidden incompatibility or operational failure may appear during integration or deployment.","cause":"Framework capability treated as sufficient evidence of suitability.","root_cause":"Incomplete technology evaluation criteria.","solution":"Evaluate framework capability, exact version compatibility, dependency behavior, testing support, security controls, deployment model, and operational evidence.","edge_case":"An application may need WebSockets, streaming, background jobs, long-lived connections, or unusual protocol behavior that requires separate framework capability verification.","security_implication":"Authentication, authorization, input validation, dependency security, TLS termination, and secure middleware configuration must be explicitly evaluated.","performance_implication":"Framework performance must be measured using representative endpoint behavior and payload sizes rather than generic benchmark numbers.","test_case":"Create representative endpoints and execute API contract, validation, concurrency, authentication, and failure-path tests.","test_input":{"endpoint":"/health","method":"GET","expected_status":200},"expected_test_result":"Endpoint behavior matches the declared API contract and test evidence is recorded separately from framework selection rationale.","testing_guidance":"Use framework-supported testing mechanisms and representative deployment configuration.","verification_method":"Official documentation review, dependency compatibility check, API contract testing, and representative load testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify the exact FastAPI and Python versions used by the implementation; do not infer compatibility from unrelated versions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"FastAPI","framework_version":"Version must be pinned by the implementation; official documentation is versioned.","version_range":"Exact project-supported range must be established before deployment.","dependencies":["FastAPI dependency stack","ASGI server selected by deployment"],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+","FastAPI-compatible environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary FastAPI documentation plus project-specific compatibility and testing evidence.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B031","D33-B035","D33-B060"],"depends_on":["D34-B002"],"requires":["API requirements","Runtime compatibility"],"extends":"","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"HTTP API technology selection","contrasts_with":"","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["FastAPI official documentation"],"provenance":["FastAPI official tutorial and documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B004","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Transactional Database Technology","subtopic":"Client-server relational database selection","concept":"A relational database technology should be evaluated against transaction semantics, relational querying, concurrency, durability, operational scale, extensions, and deployment requirements.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should PostgreSQL be considered as a relational database candidate?","answer":"PostgreSQL is a candidate when the workload requires a mature relational database with transactional semantics, SQL capabilities, concurrency, durability, extensibility, and a client-server operational model. Suitability still depends on workload scale, operational requirements, hosting constraints, and required extensions.","explanation":"The technology decision must distinguish the requirement for relational persistence from the choice of a particular relational engine. PostgreSQL documentation provides versioned manuals and supported-version information. The engineering decision should compare it with alternatives using workload-specific criteria rather than treating PostgreSQL as universally appropriate.","principle":"Select a relational database by matching required transaction, query, concurrency, durability, and operational properties to verified database capabilities.","objective":"Establish evidence-driven relational database selection.","requirements":["Transactional persistence","Relational queries","Durability","Concurrency control","Backup/recovery capability","Operational support"],"constraints":["Client-server operation introduces deployment and administration requirements","Exact supported version must be selected","Schema and extension compatibility must be verified","Performance must be measured against actual workload"],"preconditions":["Data model is sufficiently understood","Consistency and durability requirements are defined"],"input":{"requirements":["ACID-style transactional behavior","complex relational queries","concurrent clients","durable production persistence"]},"output":{"candidate":"PostgreSQL","evaluation_basis":["relational capability","transaction semantics","concurrency","durability","ecosystem"]},"expected_behavior":"The database choice is traceable to explicit data and operational requirements.","incorrect_code":"DB = "postgresql"  # technology selected without evaluating deployment and workload requirements","corrected_code":"database_requirements = {\n    "transactions": True,\n    "relational_queries": True,\n    "concurrency": "defined_by_workload",\n    "durability": True,\n    "backup_restore": True,\n}\n# Compare PostgreSQL with all viable alternatives against these criteria.","failure_mode":"Database selected without determining data access patterns, consistency requirements, or operational constraints.","expected_error":"Architecture may incur unnecessary operational complexity or fail workload requirements.","cause":"Technology name substituted for capability analysis.","root_cause":"Missing data-workload-to-database traceability.","solution":"Document transaction, query, concurrency, durability, recovery, scaling, and operational requirements before selection.","edge_case":"A system may require SQLite locally but PostgreSQL in production; this is a compatibility decision requiring migration/schema/test validation rather than an assumption that the engines are interchangeable.","security_implication":"Database access control, network exposure, credential handling, encryption, patching, auditing, and least privilege must be evaluated.","performance_implication":"Evaluate query plans, connection behavior, transaction contention, indexing, and representative workload measurements.","test_case":"Execute representative transaction and query workloads against the selected database version and validate backup/restore procedures.","test_input":{"operations":["concurrent_reads","concurrent_writes","transaction_rollback","indexed_query"]},"expected_test_result":"Required consistency and durability behavior is demonstrated with recorded evidence.","testing_guidance":"Include failure injection, transaction rollback, concurrency, backup, restore, and schema migration testing.","verification_method":"Official documentation review plus integration, migration, and recovery tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify features against the exact PostgreSQL version used; record unsupported or version-dependent assumptions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"PostgreSQL 18 is current as of the referenced official documentation; project implementations must pin an exact supported version.","dependencies":["PostgreSQL server","Python PostgreSQL driver selected by implementation"],"execution_requirements":["Compatible PostgreSQL instance"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary PostgreSQL documentation plus project-specific workload and recovery testing.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B002","D33-B009","D33-B033","D33-B038"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Data model","Transaction requirements","Recovery requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"Relational database technology","contrasts_with":"D34-B005","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["PostgreSQL official documentation"],"provenance":["PostgreSQL 18 official documentation and supported-version documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B005","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Embedded Database Technology","subtopic":"Local-first relational persistence","concept":"An embedded database is a candidate when local ownership, minimal operational overhead, self-contained deployment, and relational persistence are more important than centralized multi-client database-server capabilities.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When is SQLite an appropriate technology candidate?","answer":"SQLite is a candidate for embedded, local-first, single-process or controlled-concurrency workloads where a self-contained relational database is desirable and a separate database server is unnecessary. Its suitability must be evaluated against concurrency, write throughput, database size, replication, networking, and operational requirements.","explanation":"SQLite is a library-based SQL database engine rather than a client-server database service. Its official documentation explicitly describes appropriate and inappropriate use cases. This makes it particularly relevant to local-first software, embedded applications, desktop tools, mobile software, and development environments, while high-concurrency centralized workloads may require a different technology.","principle":"Choose embedded persistence when the deployment and concurrency model fits an embedded database rather than assuming that every relational workload requires a database server.","objective":"Represent local-first and embedded database technology selection accurately.","requirements":["Relational persistence","Self-contained deployment","Local operation","Low operational overhead"],"constraints":["Concurrency model must be understood","Networked multi-client workloads require separate evaluation","Write contention must be tested","Migration compatibility with any production database must be verified"],"preconditions":["Local ownership requirement exists","Data access pattern is defined"],"input":{"requirements":["local-first operation","SQL queries","minimal infrastructure","embedded deployment"]},"output":{"candidate":"SQLite","evaluation_basis":["embedded architecture","self-contained deployment","SQL support","operational simplicity"]},"expected_behavior":"SQLite is selected only when its concurrency and deployment characteristics match the actual workload.","incorrect_code":"sqlite3.connect("app.db")\n# Assuming this automatically supports arbitrary distributed concurrent workloads is incorrect.","corrected_code":"import sqlite3\n\nconnection = sqlite3.connect("app.db")\nconnection.execute("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON")\n# Validate concurrency, durability, backup, and workload requirements\n# before treating SQLite as the production persistence technology.","failure_mode":"Embedded database chosen for a workload whose concurrency or distribution requirements exceed its intended operating model.","expected_error":"Lock contention, unsuitable write concurrency, operational limitations, or replication requirements may become blocking issues.","cause":"SQLite treated as a universal relational database replacement.","root_cause":"Deployment and concurrency requirements were not evaluated.","solution":"Use SQLite when the workload matches embedded/local-first characteristics and explicitly test concurrency, durability, migration, and backup behavior.","edge_case":"A product can use SQLite locally and a server database centrally, but schema behavior, SQL compatibility, migrations, and synchronization semantics must be explicitly designed and tested.","security_implication":"Local database files require filesystem permissions, encryption strategy where appropriate, secure backups, and protection against unauthorized local access.","performance_implication":"Measure transaction contention and realistic read/write patterns; do not generalize benchmark results across workloads.","test_case":"Run concurrent read/write tests and failure/recovery tests using the expected deployment model.","test_input":{"workload":["local_reads","local_writes","concurrent_reads","transaction_rollback"]},"expected_test_result":"Observed behavior remains within documented application requirements.","testing_guidance":"Include process concurrency, crash recovery, backup, restore, migration, and database-lock behavior tests.","verification_method":"SQLite official appropriate-use documentation plus representative workload testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify assumptions against the exact SQLite version and deployment environment.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"SQLite version is deployment-specific; current official documentation must be consulted for the selected release.","dependencies":["Python sqlite3 standard-library module"],"execution_requirements":["SQLite library compatible with Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary SQLite documentation plus workload-specific validation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B002","D33-B033","D33-B056"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Local deployment requirement","Concurrency characterization"],"extends":"","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"Embedded relational database technology","contrasts_with":"D34-B004","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["SQLite official appropriate-use documentation"],"provenance":["SQLite official documentation and appropriate-use guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B006","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Caching Technology","subtopic":"In-memory cache selection","concept":"Cache technology should be selected only when the workload benefits from derived, reconstructible, low-latency state and the system can define freshness, invalidation, failure, and source-of-truth behavior.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When is Redis a candidate for caching?","answer":"Redis is a candidate when an application requires an external in-memory data store for low-latency access patterns, shared caching, transient state, atomic data structures, or related workloads supported by its documented capabilities. The decision must define source of truth, expiration, invalidation, persistence expectations, and behavior when Redis is unavailable.","explanation":"Redis can serve several roles, including cache, data store, message broker, and streaming engine. D34 must not assume that all of these roles are interchangeable. For a cache decision, the key requirement is that cached data is either reconstructible or that the architecture explicitly accepts Redis as a system-of-record component. Cache technology should therefore be evaluated separately from persistence technology.","principle":"A cache is a performance mechanism with explicit freshness and failure semantics, not an implicit replacement for the source of truth.","objective":"Select cache technology based on cache-specific requirements.","requirements":["Low-latency access","Shared cache where required","Expiration/invalidation strategy","Failure behavior","Capacity constraints"],"constraints":["Do not make cache availability a hidden single point of failure","Do not store authoritative data without explicit architectural justification","Memory capacity and eviction behavior must be evaluated","Cache stampede and stale-data behavior must be designed"],"preconditions":["A measurable caching requirement exists","Source-of-truth technology is identified"],"input":{"requirements":["shared application cache","low-latency reads","TTL support","local development compatibility"]},"output":{"candidate":"Redis","evaluation_basis":["in-memory data structures","expiration","shared deployment","operational ecosystem"]},"expected_behavior":"Redis is introduced only where cache semantics and failure behavior are explicit.","incorrect_code":"cache = redis.Redis()\n# Treating cache.get() as guaranteed authoritative persistence is incorrect.","corrected_code":"def read_value(key, source_of_truth, cache):\n    cached = cache.get(key)\n    if cached is not None:\n        return cached\n    value = source_of_truth.load(key)\n    cache.set(key, value, ex=300)\n    return value","failure_mode":"Cache becomes an undeclared source of truth or causes correctness failures when stale or unavailable.","expected_error":"Stale data, cache misses causing overload, inconsistent reads, or application failure during cache outage.","cause":"Cache semantics were not defined before technology selection.","root_cause":"Failure and freshness requirements omitted from the decision.","solution":"Define source of truth, TTL, invalidation, outage behavior, capacity, and stampede protection before selecting and deploying Redis.","edge_case":"A cache entry may become stale while the underlying transaction is still propagating; consistency requirements determine whether synchronous invalidation, versioning, or short-lived TTL is acceptable.","security_implication":"Cache contents may contain sensitive data and require access controls, network protection, credential management, and appropriate data handling.","performance_implication":"Measure hit rate, latency, memory use, eviction rate, and backend-load reduction rather than assuming caching improves every workload.","test_case":"Disable the cache during representative application operations and verify correctness and controlled degradation.","test_input":{"cache_state":"unavailable","source_of_truth":"available"},"expected_test_result":"Application remains correct according to its declared degradation behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test cache miss, stale value, eviction, outage, restart, stampede, and invalidation scenarios.","verification_method":"Redis official capability documentation plus failure and workload testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify the selected Redis version and deployment mode against required commands, persistence, replication, and availability behavior.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Redis version and deployment mode must be explicitly selected.","dependencies":["Redis server","Redis-compatible Python client"],"execution_requirements":["Redis instance for integration testing"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary Redis documentation plus application-specific cache testing.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B022","D33-B028","D33-B055"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Source-of-truth definition","Cache semantics"],"extends":"D33-B022","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"Cache technology selection","contrasts_with":"","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Redis official documentation"],"provenance":["Redis official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B007","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Messaging Technology","subtopic":"Message broker selection","concept":"Message broker selection depends on delivery semantics, routing, ordering, durability, throughput, consumer model, operational complexity, and failure handling.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should RabbitMQ and Kafka be evaluated as messaging technology candidates?","answer":"Evaluate each against the actual messaging requirement: task distribution and routing may require different capabilities from durable event streaming, partitioned consumption, replay, or high-volume event processing. RabbitMQ and Kafka have different operating models and should not be treated as interchangeable merely because both transport messages.","explanation":"RabbitMQ documentation describes a broker-oriented messaging system with developer and administrator guidance, while Kafka documentation emphasizes topics, partitions, producers, consumers, and stream processing. The technology decision must begin with delivery, ordering, replay, routing, retention, and consumer requirements. No universal ranking exists.","principle":"Select messaging technology according to message semantics and workload rather than using a generic 'message queue' category.","objective":"Prevent inappropriate broker selection caused by treating distinct messaging models as equivalent.","requirements":["Defined delivery semantics","Ordering requirements","Retention/replay requirements","Consumer model","Throughput/latency requirements","Failure and retry behavior"],"constraints":["Do not assume exactly-once business effects from transport semantics alone","Do not select a broker without defining message ownership and acknowledgment behavior","Operational complexity must be included","Performance claims require workload-specific measurement"],"preconditions":["Asynchronous communication requirement exists","Message semantics are defined"],"input":{"requirements":["durable asynchronous processing","multiple consumers","retry handling","possibly ordered processing"]},"output":{"candidates":["RabbitMQ","Apache Kafka"],"evaluation_basis":["routing","delivery","retention","partitioning","consumer model","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"Candidate technologies are compared against message semantics and workload rather than popularity.","incorrect_code":"broker = "kafka"  # selected without defining whether the requirement is queueing or event streaming","corrected_code":"messaging_requirement = {\n    "delivery": "at_least_once",\n    "ordering": "per_entity",\n    "replay": False,\n    "routing": True,\n    "retention": "bounded",\n}\n# Evaluate broker candidates against these explicit semantics.","failure_mode":"Messaging platform selected without defining delivery, retention, ordering, and consumer requirements.","expected_error":"Duplicate processing, lost messages, unexpected ordering, excessive retention cost, or operational mismatch.","cause":"Generic 'message queue' requirement was used instead of concrete semantics.","root_cause":"Messaging behavior was not specified before technology selection.","solution":"Define message lifecycle and semantics first, then compare broker technologies against those requirements.","edge_case":"A system may require both task queues and event streams; separate technology roles may be justified, but duplication should be avoided unless the requirements genuinely differ.","security_implication":"Broker authentication, authorization, encryption, tenant isolation, topic/queue permissions, and secret management must be evaluated.","performance_implication":"Measure end-to-end publish-to-consume latency, throughput, backlog behavior, retry overhead, and recovery behavior under representative load.","test_case":"Publish duplicate, delayed, reordered, and failed messages and verify the declared consumer semantics.","test_input":{"messages":["m1","m2","m1"],"delivery":"at_least_once"},"expected_test_result":"Consumers handle duplicate delivery according to the designed idempotency and acknowledgment model.","testing_guidance":"Test broker restart, consumer failure, retry, dead-letter behavior, backlog recovery, ordering, and duplicate delivery.","verification_method":"Official broker documentation plus integration and failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantics against the exact broker version/configuration; do not infer guarantees from generic product descriptions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"RabbitMQ and Kafka versions must be pinned separately by implementation.","dependencies":["Selected message broker","Compatible client library"],"execution_requirements":["Broker test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary RabbitMQ and Apache Kafka documentation plus workload-specific integration testing.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B024","D33-B032","D33-B034","D33-B057"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Message semantics","Consumer behavior requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B032","specializes":"Messaging technology selection","contrasts_with":"","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["RabbitMQ and Apache Kafka official documentation"],"provenance":["RabbitMQ official documentation","Apache Kafka official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B008","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Search Technology","subtopic":"Full-text and semantic search selection","concept":"Search technology selection depends on corpus size, query semantics, indexing requirements, ranking needs, freshness, filtering, vector retrieval, and operational constraints.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated search engine such as OpenSearch be considered?","answer":"OpenSearch is a candidate when search and analytics requirements exceed what the primary transactional database should handle, especially when distributed full-text search, filtering, analytics, vector search, or specialized retrieval capabilities are required. A dedicated search engine should not be introduced solely because search exists.","explanation":"OpenSearch documentation describes full-text search, analytics, vector search, indexing, REST APIs, and distributed operation. The decision must identify whether those capabilities are genuinely required. For small datasets or simple indexed lookups, an existing database index may satisfy the requirement with less operational complexity.","principle":"Introduce a dedicated search technology only when its search-specific capabilities justify its additional operational and data-synchronization complexity.","objective":"Distinguish simple indexed retrieval from requirements that justify a specialized search engine.","requirements":["Search semantics defined","Corpus characteristics known","Freshness requirements known","Ranking/filtering requirements known","Operational constraints known"],"constraints":["Search index may be derived state","Index synchronization must be designed","Additional infrastructure must be justified","Vector search requires model/data compatibility evaluation"],"preconditions":["Search workload is characterized","Primary source of truth is identified"],"input":{"requirements":["large searchable corpus","full-text queries","faceted filtering","optional vector retrieval"]},"output":{"candidate":"OpenSearch","evaluation_basis":["full-text search","analytics","distributed indexing","vector capabilities","operational cost"]},"expected_behavior":"A dedicated search engine is selected only when search-specific requirements justify it over database-native indexing.","incorrect_code":"search_backend = "opensearch"\n# Adding a distributed search system without a corpus or query requirement is unjustified.","corrected_code":"search_requirements = {\n    "full_text": True,\n    "filters": True,\n    "corpus_size": "measured",\n    "freshness": "defined",\n    "vector_search": False,\n}\n# Compare database-native search with a dedicated engine.","failure_mode":"Specialized search technology introduced without sufficient search complexity or synchronization justification.","expected_error":"Unnecessary infrastructure, stale indexes, synchronization bugs, or increased operational burden.","cause":"Search technology selected before measuring query and corpus requirements.","root_cause":"Search workload was not separated from persistence requirements.","solution":"Characterize search requirements and compare database-native indexes with specialized search candidates.","edge_case":"A system may start with database-native search and later introduce a search engine; migration must define index rebuild, dual-write/reindex strategy, consistency tolerance, and cutover criteria.","security_implication":"Search indexes may duplicate sensitive data and therefore require equivalent access control, retention, encryption, and tenant isolation considerations.","performance_implication":"Measure indexing throughput, query latency, relevance, memory/disk use, refresh latency, and scaling behavior with representative data.","test_case":"Compare representative search queries and indexing workloads against the existing database and candidate search engine.","test_input":{"queries":["exact_term","phrase","filtered_query","semantic_query"],"corpus":"representative"},"expected_test_result":"Measured results and operational trade-offs are documented without presupposing a selected winner.","testing_guidance":"Include index rebuild, partial failure, stale index, ranking relevance, filtering, authorization, and recovery tests.","verification_method":"Official OpenSearch documentation plus representative search relevance and performance tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify features against the exact OpenSearch version and deployed plugins/configuration.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"OpenSearch version must be explicitly selected; official documentation is versioned.","dependencies":["OpenSearch cluster if selected","Compatible client"],"execution_requirements":["Search test corpus","Candidate search deployment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary OpenSearch documentation plus application-specific search testing.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B033","D33-B052"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Search workload characterization"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B033","specializes":"Search technology selection","contrasts_with":"","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["OpenSearch official documentation"],"provenance":["OpenSearch official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B009","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Workflow Technology","subtopic":"Durable workflow execution","concept":"Workflow technology selection depends on workflow duration, crash recovery, retries, external side effects, resumability, scheduling, state durability, and operational requirements.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When is a durable workflow platform such as Temporal a candidate?","answer":"Temporal is a candidate when workflows must survive process, network, or infrastructure failures and resume from durable workflow state, especially for long-running or mission-critical processes. A simpler application-level state machine may be preferable when workflow requirements are short-lived and easily reconstructed.","explanation":"Temporal documentation describes durable workflow execution that can resume after failures and supports long-running application workflows. The technology decision should first establish whether durable workflow semantics are genuinely required. Introducing a workflow platform without a corresponding lifecycle requirement adds operational and programming-model complexity.","principle":"Use durable workflow technology when durable execution semantics are a requirement, not merely because a process contains multiple steps.","objective":"Select workflow technology according to failure recovery and process lifecycle requirements.","requirements":["Workflow durability","Recovery semantics","Retry behavior","External side-effect coordination","Workflow observability","Versioning requirements"],"constraints":["Workflow technology introduces an execution model and operational dependency","External side effects still require idempotency or compensation","Workflow definitions require compatibility/versioning discipline","Not every background job needs a workflow engine"],"preconditions":["Workflow lifecycle is known","Failure and recovery requirements are defined"],"input":{"requirements":["multi-hour workflow","crash recovery","durable progress","external service calls","retries"]},"output":{"candidate":"Temporal","evaluation_basis":["durable execution","recovery","workflow state","long-running processes"]},"expected_behavior":"A durable workflow platform is selected only when its execution guarantees solve a documented requirement.","incorrect_code":"def workflow():\n    step_one()\n    step_two()\n    step_three()\n# Process memory alone does not provide durable workflow state.","corrected_code":"workflow_requirements = {\n    "durable_progress": True,\n    "resume_after_process_crash": True,\n    "external_side_effects": True,\n    "workflow_duration": "hours_or_days",\n}\n# Evaluate a durable workflow platform against these requirements.","failure_mode":"Long-running workflow implemented as ordinary process memory without durable state or recovery semantics.","expected_error":"Progress may be lost after process failure, causing repeated or skipped work.","cause":"Workflow durability requirement was not reflected in technology selection.","root_cause":"Process lifecycle was treated as equivalent to workflow lifecycle.","solution":"Evaluate durable workflow technology when recovery, resumability, and long-running execution are explicit requirements.","edge_case":"A workflow with irreversible external side effects requires idempotency, deduplication, compensation, or explicit human approval regardless of the workflow engine selected.","security_implication":"Workflow state and credentials may contain sensitive business information; access control and secret handling must be evaluated.","performance_implication":"Measure workflow scheduling overhead, task throughput, history/state growth, recovery latency, and worker resource usage.","test_case":"Terminate a workflow worker during execution and verify that the workflow resumes according to its declared recovery semantics.","test_input":{"failure":"worker_process_terminated","workflow_state":"mid_execution"},"expected_test_result":"Recovery behavior matches the selected workflow technology's documented semantics and application requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test worker failure, service failure, retries, duplicate activity execution, workflow version changes, and external side-effect recovery.","verification_method":"Official workflow-platform documentation plus controlled failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact platform version and SDK compatibility; test failure semantics rather than relying solely on documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Temporal Python SDK","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact SDK/platform compatibility must be established for the project.","dependencies":["Temporal service","Temporal Python SDK"],"execution_requirements":["Temporal development/test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary Temporal documentation plus controlled failure testing.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B024","D33-B057"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Workflow durability requirements","Failure semantics"],"extends":"D33-B057","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"Durable workflow technology","contrasts_with":"Simple application state machine","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Temporal official documentation"],"provenance":["Temporal Platform official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B010","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Container Orchestration Technology","subtopic":"Deployment platform selection","concept":"Container orchestration technology should be introduced only when workload count, scheduling, scaling, isolation, availability, and operational requirements justify an orchestration control plane.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should Kubernetes be considered as a deployment technology?","answer":"Kubernetes is a candidate when an organization needs declarative management of containerized workloads, scheduling, service management, scaling, rollout controls, or cluster-level automation that exceeds simpler deployment mechanisms. It should not be selected merely because an application runs in containers.","explanation":"Kubernetes documentation describes it as a platform for managing containerized workloads and services using declarative configuration and automation. Its value depends on operational requirements, organizational expertise, security, resource availability, and the number and complexity of workloads. A single small service may not require a cluster control plane.","principle":"Choose orchestration technology when orchestration capabilities are required strongly enough to justify its operational complexity.","objective":"Prevent unnecessary orchestration while preserving Kubernetes as a valid candidate for genuinely complex deployment requirements.","requirements":["Containerized workloads","Scheduling requirements","Service management","Scaling requirements","Deployment automation","Operational ownership"],"constraints":["Cluster operations add complexity","Security configuration is substantial","Resource requirements must be considered","Team expertise and support model matter","Managed versus self-hosted operation changes the technology decision"],"preconditions":["Deployment workload is characterized","Container requirement is established","Operational ownership is known"],"input":{"requirements":["multiple independently deployed workloads","automated scheduling","declarative deployment","horizontal scaling","service recovery"]},"output":{"candidate":"Kubernetes","evaluation_basis":["orchestration capabilities","declarative management","scaling","deployment automation","operational model"]},"expected_behavior":"Kubernetes is selected only when cluster orchestration requirements are explicit and operational ownership is viable.","incorrect_code":"# Incorrect reasoning:\nuse_kubernetes = True\n# Containerization alone does not establish an orchestration requirement.","corrected_code":"deployment_requirements = {\n    "containers": True,\n    "workload_count": "measured",\n    "autoscaling": "required_or_not",\n    "scheduling": "required_or_not",\n    "deployment_automation": True,\n    "operational_ownership": "defined",\n}\n# Compare Kubernetes with simpler deployment technologies.","failure_mode":"A cluster orchestrator is introduced before operational requirements justify it.","expected_error":"Excess operational complexity, security surface, resource overhead, and deployment burden.","cause":"Containerization was incorrectly treated as proof of orchestration necessity.","root_cause":"Deployment technology selection skipped workload and operations analysis.","solution":"Evaluate workload count, scaling, scheduling, availability, deployment automation, security, resource constraints, and team capability before selecting an orchestrator.","edge_case":"A local development environment may use lightweight Kubernetes or no Kubernetes while production uses Kubernetes; development/production parity and deployment contract must be explicitly managed.","security_implication":"Cluster control-plane security, RBAC, network policies, secrets, admission controls, workload isolation, image provenance, and node security require explicit evaluation.","performance_implication":"Measure scheduling latency, resource overhead, pod startup time, autoscaling response, and workload density using representative infrastructure.","test_case":"Deploy a representative workload and test rollout, rollback, rescheduling, scaling, node failure, and readiness behavior.","test_input":{"events":["deployment","pod_failure","node_failure","scale_out","rollback"]},"expected_test_result":"Required deployment and recovery behavior is achieved within explicitly defined operational targets.","testing_guidance":"Include failure injection, security policy validation, resource-limit tests, upgrade tests, and rollback tests.","verification_method":"Official Kubernetes documentation plus deployment and failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify Kubernetes version support and compatibility for the exact cluster components, APIs, and deployment tooling.","language":"YAML","language_version":"","framework":"Kubernetes","framework_version":"v1.37 documentation current at dataset creation; implementation must pin supported versions","version_range":"Project-supported Kubernetes versions must be explicitly declared.","dependencies":["Container runtime","Kubernetes cluster","kubectl or equivalent management tooling"],"execution_requirements":["Kubernetes test cluster for integration validation"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Primary Kubernetes documentation plus project-specific operational validation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B027","D33-B038","D33-B056","D33-B096"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Deployment requirements","Operational ownership","Container requirement"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B027","specializes":"Container orchestration technology","contrasts_with":"Single-host deployment","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Kubernetes official documentation"],"provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation and supported-version documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B011","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Object Storage Technology","subtopic":"Blob and large-object persistence","concept":"Object storage is a technology candidate when the system needs durable storage for large unstructured objects independently of transactional application records.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should object storage be selected instead of storing large binary objects directly in a relational database?","answer":"Object storage is a candidate when files, media, archives, exports, backups, or other large unstructured objects require durable storage, independent lifecycle management, scalable capacity, and access patterns that do not require transactional row-level database semantics.","explanation":"Large binary objects can create database size, backup, replication, and operational consequences. Object storage separates object bytes from transactional metadata while allowing the application database to retain ownership, authorization, checksums, lifecycle metadata, and object references. The decision must account for consistency, access control, retention, deletion, backup, and availability requirements.","principle":"Store large unstructured objects in a storage technology designed for object lifecycle and capacity when transactional database semantics are not required for the object bytes themselves.","objective":"Select object storage based on object characteristics and lifecycle requirements.","requirements":["Durable object storage","Large-object support","Lifecycle management","Access control","Integrity verification","Defined retention/deletion"],"constraints":["Object metadata and object bytes may have different consistency semantics","Object references must remain valid or explicitly versioned","Unauthorized direct access must be prevented","Backup and disaster recovery scope must include required objects"],"preconditions":["Object size and access patterns are characterized","Transactional metadata requirements are known"],"input":{"objects":["documents","images","exports"],"requirements":["durability","large capacity","lifecycle control"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Object storage","selection_basis":["object lifecycle","capacity","durability","access model","cost model"]},"expected_behavior":"Application stores authoritative business metadata separately while object storage owns the large object payload where appropriate.","incorrect_code":"cursor.execute("INSERT INTO documents(blob) VALUES (?)", (huge_file_bytes,))\n# Treating every large object as ordinary transactional row data may create unsuitable storage behavior.","corrected_code":"document_id = create_document_metadata(name="report.pdf")\nobject_key = f"documents/{document_id}/report.pdf"\nobject_store.put(object_key, file_bytes)\nrecord_object_reference(document_id, object_key)\n# Actual consistency and transactional behavior must be defined by the implementation.","failure_mode":"Large object payloads are coupled unnecessarily to transactional database storage.","expected_error":"Excessive database growth, expensive backups, slower replication, or unsuitable object lifecycle management.","cause":"Storage technology selected without considering object characteristics.","root_cause":"Binary payload and transactional metadata requirements were not separated.","solution":"Evaluate object storage for large unstructured payloads and explicitly design metadata/object consistency and lifecycle semantics.","edge_case":"An application may require atomic business state plus object creation; because object storage and a relational transaction may be separate systems, an explicit state machine, reconciliation process, or transactional outbox pattern may be required.","security_implication":"Object authorization must not rely solely on obscurity of object keys; access policy, signed access mechanisms, encryption, retention, and tenant isolation must be defined.","performance_implication":"Evaluate upload/download throughput, object size distribution, concurrent access, multipart transfer behavior, and metadata lookup latency.","test_case":"Create metadata, upload an object, interrupt the workflow, and verify reconciliation behavior for missing metadata or missing object states.","test_input":{"metadata":"created","object_upload":"interrupted"},"expected_test_result":"The system reaches a defined recoverable state without silently treating an incomplete object as valid.","testing_guidance":"Test upload interruption, duplicate upload, deletion, retention, authorization, checksum mismatch, and recovery scenarios.","verification_method":"Storage-service documentation plus application integration and failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact object-store consistency, lifecycle, access-control, and versioning semantics for the selected provider or implementation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Provider-specific API/version must be pinned by implementation.","dependencies":["Selected object-storage client"],"execution_requirements":["Object-storage test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Established storage architecture practice plus provider-specific primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B052","D33-B096"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Object lifecycle requirements","Data ownership requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"Object storage technology","contrasts_with":"Relational large-object storage","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Object storage architectural practice"],"provenance":["Selected object-storage provider official documentation must be used for concrete implementation claims"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B012","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Secrets Technology","subtopic":"Secret management","concept":"Secrets-management technology should separate credentials and sensitive runtime material from source code, ordinary configuration, and deployment artifacts.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When is a dedicated secrets manager required instead of environment variables or configuration files?","answer":"A dedicated secrets-management system becomes relevant when credentials require centralized access control, rotation, auditing, dynamic retrieval, separation of duties, or protection beyond what static deployment configuration can safely provide.","explanation":"Environment variables and configuration files can be useful for simple deployments, but they do not automatically provide lifecycle management, auditing, rotation, or fine-grained access control. Technologies such as HashiCorp Vault or cloud-native secret managers should therefore be evaluated from actual security and operational requirements rather than treated as mandatory infrastructure.","principle":"Use the least complex secret-management mechanism that satisfies the required confidentiality, lifecycle, access-control, rotation, and audit properties.","objective":"Select secret technology according to threat model and operational requirements.","requirements":["Secret confidentiality","Access control","Rotation requirements","Audit requirements","Deployment integration"],"constraints":["Secrets must not be committed to source control","Secret retrieval must fail safely","Rotation must account for consumers and active sessions","Emergency revocation must be considered"],"preconditions":["Secret classes are identified","Threat model and operational requirements are known"],"input":{"secrets":["database_credentials","API_tokens"],"requirements":["rotation","audit","central_access_control"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Secrets manager","selection_basis":["access policy","rotation","audit","integration","operational complexity"]},"expected_behavior":"Applications retrieve secrets through an explicitly designed mechanism without embedding secret material in source code.","incorrect_code":"DATABASE_PASSWORD = "production-password"\n# Secret material must not be embedded in source code.","corrected_code":"import os\n\nDATABASE_PASSWORD = os.environ["DATABASE_PASSWORD"]\n# Environment variables can be appropriate for some deployment models;\n# stronger requirements may justify a dedicated secrets manager.","failure_mode":"Sensitive credentials are stored in source code, images, logs, or broadly accessible configuration.","expected_error":"Credential disclosure and difficult rotation or revocation.","cause":"Secret-management requirement was treated as ordinary configuration.","root_cause":"Confidentiality and lifecycle requirements were not modeled separately.","solution":"Classify secrets, select an appropriate management mechanism, enforce access control, rotation, audit, and safe failure behavior.","edge_case":"A local-first application may intentionally avoid a central secrets service; local secret storage still requires appropriate filesystem/access protection and must not silently weaken production controls.","security_implication":"Secret exposure can directly enable unauthorized access; secret values should be minimized, protected, rotated, and excluded from logs and source control.","performance_implication":"Secret retrieval latency and availability must be considered, especially during startup and dependency outages; safe caching may be necessary.","test_case":"Scan source/build artifacts for known test secret markers and simulate secret-manager unavailability during application startup.","test_input":{"secret_source":"dedicated_manager","manager_state":"unavailable"},"expected_test_result":"Application follows an explicitly defined fail-safe startup or degraded behavior without exposing or fabricating credentials.","testing_guidance":"Test rotation, revocation, unauthorized retrieval, startup failure, logging, backup, and recovery behavior.","verification_method":"Secret-management product documentation plus security testing and deployment inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact authentication, authorization, rotation, and audit semantics for the selected secrets technology.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Technology-specific version must be pinned by implementation.","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Secret-management mechanism appropriate to deployment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Established secure engineering practice plus official secrets-manager documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B035","D33-B051","D33-B053"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Secret classification","Threat model"],"extends":"D33-B035","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"Secret-management technology","contrasts_with":"Ordinary configuration storage","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Secure configuration and secret-management practice"],"provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance and selected secrets-manager official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B013","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Identity Technology","subtopic":"Authentication provider selection","concept":"Identity technology should be selected according to authentication protocols, federation requirements, lifecycle management, user populations, authorization integration, and operational ownership.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should an identity provider be selected for an enterprise application?","answer":"Evaluate identity-provider candidates against required protocols such as OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect, enterprise federation requirements, user lifecycle, MFA capabilities, tenant isolation, administrative controls, availability, auditability, integration libraries, and deployment constraints.","explanation":"Authentication technology is a security boundary rather than a generic application dependency. The correct technology depends on whether users are internal, external, enterprise-federated, service-to-service, or mixed. The application should avoid implementing cryptographic authentication protocols itself when a mature identity provider can satisfy the requirement, while retaining application-level authorization decisions where appropriate.","principle":"Delegate authentication protocol complexity to a suitable identity system while retaining explicit application authorization and business-policy controls.","objective":"Create evidence-driven identity technology selection.","requirements":["Defined user populations","Authentication protocol requirements","Federation requirements","MFA requirements","Account lifecycle","Audit requirements"],"constraints":["Authentication and authorization are distinct","Provider lock-in must be considered","Token validation and key rotation must be implemented correctly","Availability of the identity dependency affects login and administrative operations"],"preconditions":["Trust model is defined","User and service identities are classified"],"input":{"requirements":["OIDC login","enterprise federation","MFA","central lifecycle management"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Identity provider","selection_basis":["protocol support","federation","MFA","lifecycle","audit","availability"]},"expected_behavior":"The selected identity technology provides required authentication capabilities while application authorization remains explicit.","incorrect_code":"if request.headers.get("X-User"):\n    authorize(request.headers["X-User"])\n# Trusting an unverified client-supplied identity is not authentication.","corrected_code":"# Illustrative only: validate a signed identity token through a trusted\n# OIDC issuer and then apply application authorization rules.\nidentity = validate_oidc_token(token, issuer=TRUSTED_ISSUER)\nif not identity:\n    raise AuthenticationError()\nprincipal = identity.subject","failure_mode":"Application trusts unverified identity claims or implements authentication incorrectly.","expected_error":"Identity spoofing or unauthorized access.","cause":"Identity provider and token-validation requirements were not explicitly designed.","root_cause":"Authentication trust boundary was omitted from technology selection.","solution":"Select a standards-compatible identity technology and explicitly design issuer validation, signature validation, audience/expiry checks, key rotation, and application authorization.","edge_case":"Machine-to-machine clients may require different authentication flows and credential lifecycle from interactive users; both must be modeled separately.","security_implication":"Identity provider compromise or misconfiguration can affect the application's entire authentication boundary; federation trust and administrative access require strong controls.","performance_implication":"Evaluate token validation latency, key retrieval/cache behavior, login throughput, and dependency availability without weakening security checks.","test_case":"Submit expired, malformed, wrong-audience, wrong-issuer, invalid-signature, and valid identity tokens.","test_input":{"tokens":["expired","wrong_issuer","wrong_audience","invalid_signature","valid"]},"expected_test_result":"Only tokens satisfying all required validation rules are accepted.","testing_guidance":"Test authentication flows, token validation, key rotation, logout/session behavior, MFA, federation failure, and provider outage.","verification_method":"Protocol specification review, identity-provider documentation, security testing, and integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact OIDC/OAuth behavior and provider configuration rather than relying on framework defaults.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Provider and library versions must be pinned and security-maintained.","dependencies":["OIDC/OAuth-compatible identity library or provider SDK"],"execution_requirements":["Configured test identity provider"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OAuth/OIDC standards and identity-provider primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B051","D33-B059"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Identity trust model","Authentication requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B051","specializes":"Authentication technology","contrasts_with":"Application-owned password authentication","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect standards"],"provenance":["IETF OAuth specifications","OpenID Connect Core specification"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B014","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Observability Technology","subtopic":"Metrics, logs, and traces","concept":"Observability technology should be selected according to telemetry types, correlation requirements, retention, cardinality, query needs, alerting, deployment constraints, and operational ownership.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should an observability stack be selected?","answer":"First define required telemetry and operational questions, then evaluate compatible technologies for metrics, logs, traces, collection, storage, visualization, and alerting. OpenTelemetry is a candidate for vendor-neutral telemetry instrumentation and collection, while backend selection depends on storage and operational requirements.","explanation":"Observability is not a single product category. Metrics answer aggregate behavior questions, logs provide event/context records, and traces connect distributed operations. A technology selection should therefore distinguish instrumentation from telemetry storage and visualization. OpenTelemetry provides standardized observability instrumentation and collection mechanisms, but it does not by itself define the complete storage or dashboard stack.","principle":"Choose observability technologies from required operational questions and telemetry semantics, separating instrumentation from backend storage and analysis.","objective":"Prevent fragmented or vendor-driven observability architecture.","requirements":["Required telemetry types","Cross-service correlation","Retention requirements","Alerting requirements","Query requirements","Operational ownership"],"constraints":["High-cardinality telemetry can create storage and cost pressure","Sensitive data must not leak into telemetry","Sampling can affect trace completeness","Instrumentation and backend technologies have different lifecycle concerns"],"preconditions":["Operational questions are defined","Service boundaries and failure modes are known"],"input":{"questions":["Which requests fail?","Where does latency occur?","Which business outcomes are affected?"],"telemetry":["metrics","logs","traces"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Observability stack","candidate_instrumentation":"OpenTelemetry","selection_basis":["standardized instrumentation","context propagation","collector ecosystem"]},"expected_behavior":"Telemetry provides enough correlated evidence to investigate system and business behavior without exposing unnecessary sensitive data.","incorrect_code":"logger.info("request", extra={"password": password})\n# Sensitive secrets must never be emitted into logs.","corrected_code":"logger.info("request_completed", extra={"request_id": request_id, "status": status})\n# Telemetry schema should explicitly exclude secrets and unnecessary sensitive data.","failure_mode":"Telemetry is either insufficient for diagnosis or becomes a source of sensitive-data leakage and uncontrolled cardinality.","expected_error":"Poor incident diagnosis, excessive telemetry cost, privacy/security exposure, or unusable observability data.","cause":"Observability technology selected without operational questions and telemetry governance.","root_cause":"Telemetry was treated as generic logging rather than an evidence system.","solution":"Define diagnostic questions, telemetry schema, correlation identifiers, retention, access control, sampling, and backend requirements before selecting technologies.","edge_case":"A low-volume local deployment may only need structured logs and metrics; adding a distributed tracing stack without diagnostic value can increase complexity without improving evidence.","security_implication":"Telemetry often crosses trust boundaries and can contain credentials, personal data, tokens, query parameters, or business-sensitive values; redaction and access control are mandatory design concerns.","performance_implication":"Instrumentation overhead, event volume, sampling, serialization, network export, and backend ingestion must be measured under realistic workloads.","test_case":"Trigger a request spanning multiple components and verify that correlation identifiers permit reconstruction of the operation without exposing secret fields.","test_input":{"operation":"multi_component_request","telemetry":["trace","log","metric"]},"expected_test_result":"The operation can be correlated across telemetry types and sensitive fields are absent or redacted.","testing_guidance":"Test exporter failure, high-cardinality labels, trace sampling, log volume, PII redaction, and clock/timestamp behavior.","verification_method":"OpenTelemetry documentation plus telemetry integration and failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact SDK/exporter versions and semantic conventions used by the implementation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"OpenTelemetry","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact SDK/exporter compatibility must be established.","dependencies":["OpenTelemetry SDK","Selected telemetry backend/exporter"],"execution_requirements":["Telemetry backend for integration testing"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OpenTelemetry primary documentation plus operational observability practice.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B029","D33-B043","D33-B054"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Operational diagnostic requirements"],"extends":"D33-B054","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"Observability technology","contrasts_with":"Application-only logging","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["OpenTelemetry official documentation"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry official documentation and semantic conventions"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B015","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Package Management Technology","subtopic":"Dependency resolution and reproducibility","concept":"Package-management technology must support deterministic dependency specification, compatible resolution, security updates, reproducible environments, and controlled upgrades.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"What should be evaluated when selecting a package manager or dependency-management tool?","answer":"Evaluate dependency resolution correctness, lockfile behavior, reproducibility, workspace support, build isolation, package indexes, security controls, upgrade workflow, language/runtime compatibility, and CI integration.","explanation":"Dependency management is part of the software supply chain. A package manager that resolves dependencies successfully is not sufficient if builds cannot be reproduced, transitive dependencies cannot be audited, or upgrades cannot be controlled. The selected tool should fit the language ecosystem and project structure.","principle":"Dependency technology should make dependency state explicit, reproducible, auditable, and maintainable.","objective":"Reduce dependency drift and supply-chain uncertainty.","requirements":["Dependency specification","Reproducible resolution","Locking where appropriate","Upgrade management","Security visibility","CI compatibility"],"constraints":["Lockfiles do not make malicious or vulnerable packages safe","Transitive dependencies must be included in review","Runtime and package-manager compatibility must be verified","Different environments may require platform-specific resolution"],"preconditions":["Language/runtime selected","Dependency graph requirements known"],"input":{"language":"Python","requirements":["reproducible environments","dependency locking","CI support"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Python package/dependency manager","selection_basis":["resolution","lock behavior","reproducibility","security workflow"]},"expected_behavior":"The dependency graph can be recreated deterministically enough for the project's reproducibility requirements.","incorrect_code":"pip install package\n# Installing an unconstrained dependency in production does not define reproducible dependency state.","corrected_code":"# Illustrative dependency policy rather than an executable lockfile:\nrequirements = [\n    "example-package==1.2.3",\n]\n# Production projects should use their selected package manager's\n# supported lock/resolution mechanism.","failure_mode":"Dependency versions drift between development, CI, and production.","expected_error":"Unexpected API changes, incompatible transitive dependencies, or unreproducible builds.","cause":"Dependency state was not explicitly resolved and controlled.","root_cause":"Package-management requirements were treated as installation commands instead of supply-chain state.","solution":"Adopt a package-management workflow with explicit dependency constraints, reproducible resolution, auditing, and controlled upgrades.","edge_case":"A package may have platform-specific wheels or native dependencies; lock and build strategy must account for target platforms.","security_implication":"Dependency provenance, vulnerability management, package integrity, trusted indexes, and review of transitive dependencies are security requirements.","performance_implication":"Dependency installation time, cache behavior, build isolation, and artifact reuse can affect CI performance but should not compromise reproducibility.","test_case":"Recreate an environment from the project's locked dependency state on a clean machine and compare installed versions.","test_input":{"environment":"clean","dependency_state":"locked"},"expected_test_result":"The resolved dependency graph matches the declared reproducibility requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test clean installation, CI installation, dependency upgrade, downgrade, conflict detection, and artifact verification.","verification_method":"Package-manager documentation plus clean-environment reproducibility testing.","verification_guidance":"Record exact package-manager and runtime versions used to generate and consume lock state.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python package-manager version must be pinned or controlled where lockfile behavior depends on it.","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Clean Python environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Language ecosystem documentation plus reproducibility and supply-chain engineering practice.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B059","D33-B094","D33-B100"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Runtime selection","Dependency policy"],"extends":"","refines":"D34-B001","specializes":"Dependency-management technology","contrasts_with":"Unpinned installation","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Python packaging specifications and packaging-tool documentation"],"provenance":["Python Packaging User Guide and selected package-manager official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B016","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Build Technology","subtopic":"Build and artifact generation","concept":"Build technology should provide deterministic transformation from source and dependencies to deployable artifacts with explicit inputs, outputs, metadata, and verification.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should build technology be selected for a software project?","answer":"Select build technology according to language ecosystem, compilation/transformation requirements, artifact format, reproducibility, caching, incremental builds, cross-platform needs, dependency handling, and CI integration.","explanation":"A build system is part of the engineering control plane. It should make the transformation from source to artifact explicit and repeatable. For compiled languages, compiler orchestration is central; for interpreted languages, packaging and artifact construction may be the dominant concern. The build technology must match the project's actual transformation graph.","principle":"Build technology should make artifact creation deterministic, inspectable, repeatable, and compatible with the deployment contract.","objective":"Connect source code to reproducible deployable artifacts.","requirements":["Explicit build inputs","Artifact definition","Dependency resolution","Reproducibility","CI integration","Failure diagnostics"],"constraints":["Build scripts must not depend on undeclared local state","Toolchain versions must be controlled","Generated artifacts must be traceable to source revisions","Cross-platform differences must be identified"],"preconditions":["Source and deployment targets are known","Artifact contract is defined"],"input":{"project":"Python service","artifact":"deployable application package/container"},"output":{"build_process":["resolve dependencies","package source","generate artifact","attach provenance"]},"expected_behavior":"The same declared source/toolchain/dependency state produces an artifact satisfying the deployment contract.","incorrect_code":"subprocess.run("python setup.py build", shell=True)\n# An ad-hoc build command is not sufficient as a reproducible build specification.","corrected_code":"from pathlib import Path\n\nSOURCE = Path("src")\nARTIFACT = Path("dist")\nassert SOURCE.exists()\nARTIFACT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n# Actual build tooling must be selected and configured for the project.","failure_mode":"Build depends on undocumented local state or unpinned tooling.","expected_error":"Works-on-my-machine failures, non-reproducible artifacts, or CI/production divergence.","cause":"Build process not represented as a controlled dependency graph.","root_cause":"Artifact generation was not treated as a first-class engineering process.","solution":"Select and configure build tooling with explicit inputs, versions, artifact outputs, and verification.","edge_case":"Generated code, native extensions, platform-specific artifacts, and optional dependencies can make builds non-identical across platforms; the supported matrix must be explicit.","security_implication":"Build systems are supply-chain boundaries; untrusted scripts, dependencies, credentials, and generated artifacts require isolation and provenance controls.","performance_implication":"Incremental builds, caching, parallelism, and artifact reuse should be evaluated using actual repository characteristics.","test_case":"Build the same source revision twice in clean environments and compare the declared reproducibility properties.","test_input":{"source_revision":"fixed","environment":"clean_repeated"},"expected_test_result":"Artifacts satisfy the project's reproducibility criterion and differences are explainable and recorded.","testing_guidance":"Test clean builds, incremental builds, cache invalidation, cross-platform builds, dependency changes, and artifact verification.","verification_method":"Build-tool documentation plus clean reproducibility testing.","verification_guidance":"Record exact compiler/interpreter/build-tool versions and configuration used for artifact generation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Build tooling version must be controlled by the project.","dependencies":["Project build backend/tooling"],"execution_requirements":["Clean build environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Established reproducible-build practice plus language/build-tool documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B094","D33-B100","D34-B015"],"depends_on":["D34-B015"],"requires":["Artifact contract","Controlled toolchain"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B094","specializes":"Build technology","contrasts_with":"Ad-hoc build scripts","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Reproducible-build engineering practice"],"provenance":["Python Packaging User Guide; reproducible-build engineering guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B017","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Artifact Repository Technology","subtopic":"Package and container registry","concept":"Artifact repositories provide controlled storage, distribution, retention, provenance, and access management for deployable software artifacts.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When is an artifact repository required?","answer":"An artifact repository is relevant when builds produce versioned packages, containers, binaries, or other deployable artifacts that must be stored, promoted, retrieved, audited, or shared across environments.","explanation":"Source control and artifact storage serve different purposes. Source repositories retain source history, while artifact repositories manage immutable or versioned build outputs and their distribution. A technology decision should consider package formats, access control, retention, replication, immutability, provenance, scanning, and promotion workflow.","principle":"Separate source history from controlled artifact distribution when deployable artifacts need lifecycle management.","objective":"Make artifact promotion and provenance explicit.","requirements":["Artifact versioning","Controlled distribution","Access control","Retention","Provenance","Environment promotion"],"constraints":["Artifact immutability policy must be explicit","Credentials must not be embedded in artifacts","Retention policies must preserve required rollback/recovery artifacts","Artifact integrity must be verifiable"],"preconditions":["Build artifact types are defined","Deployment workflow is known"],"input":{"artifacts":["Python packages","container images"],"requirements":["versioning","promotion","access_control"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Artifact repository/registry","selection_basis":["format support","access control","retention","provenance","promotion"]},"expected_behavior":"Deployment consumes identified artifacts whose origin and version can be traced to a controlled build.","incorrect_code":"deploy("latest")\n# A mutable tag alone does not identify an immutable deployment artifact.","corrected_code":"artifact = {\n    "repository": "approved-registry",\n    "version": "1.4.2",\n    "digest": "sha256:<verified-digest>",\n}\n# Exact artifact identity should be recorded by the deployment system.","failure_mode":"Deployment references mutable or unidentified artifacts.","expected_error":"Unexpected versions, rollback ambiguity, provenance gaps, or supply-chain uncertainty.","cause":"Artifact lifecycle was not separated from source-control lifecycle.","root_cause":"Deployment artifact identity was not made explicit.","solution":"Use a controlled artifact repository or registry when artifact promotion, provenance, retention, or distribution requirements justify it.","edge_case":"A small local application may distribute a signed package directly; introducing a registry is not automatically required if artifact lifecycle is simple and security requirements are satisfied.","security_implication":"Artifact repositories must enforce access control, integrity, provenance, scanning, and protection against unauthorized replacement.","performance_implication":"Artifact transfer speed, caching, geographic distribution, and storage retention affect deployment throughput.","test_case":"Build an artifact, publish it, retrieve it by immutable identity, and verify its digest before deployment.","test_input":{"artifact_identity":"version_plus_digest"},"expected_test_result":"Retrieved artifact matches the recorded identity exactly.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized publication, artifact replacement, retention, promotion, digest verification, and rollback retrieval.","verification_method":"Repository/registry documentation plus artifact-integrity testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify registry support for the exact artifact formats and immutable identity mechanism used.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Repository technology and version must be selected explicitly.","dependencies":["Selected artifact repository/registry"],"execution_requirements":["Artifact repository test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Software supply-chain practice plus artifact-registry documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B094","D33-B100","D34-B016"],"depends_on":["D34-B016"],"requires":["Build artifact definition","Deployment workflow"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B100","specializes":"Artifact repository technology","contrasts_with":"Direct artifact distribution","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Software supply-chain engineering practice"],"provenance":["OCI distribution specification where applicable; selected registry official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B018","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Container Runtime Technology","subtopic":"Container execution","concept":"Container runtime selection concerns execution and isolation of OCI-compatible workloads and must be evaluated independently from orchestration technology.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should a container runtime be distinguished from a container orchestrator?","answer":"A container runtime executes containers, while an orchestrator manages workloads across nodes and handles scheduling, rollout, service discovery, and related cluster concerns. Container runtime selection should therefore focus on local execution, OCI compatibility, isolation, resource controls, security, and host integration rather than cluster orchestration.","explanation":"Conflating runtime and orchestration causes unnecessary technology coupling. A Kubernetes deployment depends on a compatible container runtime, but Kubernetes itself is not the low-level runtime responsible for executing the container process. Runtime choice may matter for local development, production nodes, security posture, and operational compatibility.","principle":"Separate container execution technology from workload orchestration technology and evaluate each against its own requirements.","objective":"Create an accurate container technology decision boundary.","requirements":["Container execution","OCI compatibility where required","Process isolation","Resource controls","Image handling","Host integration"],"constraints":["Runtime behavior depends on host kernel and platform","Security isolation must not be assumed to equal virtual-machine isolation","Runtime and orchestrator compatibility must be verified","Image provenance remains a separate concern"],"preconditions":["Container deployment requirement exists","Target host/platform is known"],"input":{"requirements":["OCI container execution","Linux production hosts","resource limits"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Container runtime","selection_basis":["OCI compatibility","security","resource controls","host integration"]},"expected_behavior":"The runtime executes declared container images with the isolation and resource semantics required by the deployment environment.","incorrect_code":"runtime = "kubernetes"\n# Kubernetes is an orchestrator, not the low-level container runtime.","corrected_code":"container_runtime = "selected OCI-compatible runtime"\norchestrator = "selected separately"\n# The implementation must verify compatibility between the two.","failure_mode":"Container runtime and orchestration responsibilities are conflated.","expected_error":"Incorrect deployment assumptions or incompatible runtime/orchestrator configuration.","cause":"Container technology categories were not separated.","root_cause":"Deployment architecture treated all container tooling as one technology layer.","solution":"Select runtime and orchestration independently, then verify their compatibility and security properties.","edge_case":"Local development may use a desktop container environment while production uses a different runtime; image and behavior compatibility must be tested rather than assumed.","security_implication":"Runtime configuration affects process isolation, privileges, namespaces, capabilities, filesystem access, and host exposure.","performance_implication":"Measure startup time, filesystem behavior, CPU/memory overhead, networking, and image-pull performance where relevant.","test_case":"Run a representative container with restricted privileges and resource limits, then verify filesystem, process, network, and resource isolation.","test_input":{"container":"representative_service","limits":{"cpu":"defined","memory":"defined"},"privileges":"restricted"},"expected_test_result":"The container operates within declared boundaries and cannot access prohibited host resources.","testing_guidance":"Test privilege escalation resistance, filesystem mounts, capabilities, networking, resource limits, and runtime restart behavior.","verification_method":"Container runtime documentation plus security and integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify runtime version, host compatibility, image format support, and security configuration.","language":"YAML","language_version":"","framework":"OCI/container runtime","framework_version":"Implementation-specific","version_range":"Exact runtime and host support matrix must be declared.","dependencies":["Container runtime","Linux or supported host platform"],"execution_requirements":["Container-capable host"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OCI/container-runtime specifications and selected runtime documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B027","D33-B037","D34-B010"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Container requirement","Host platform"],"extends":"D34-B010","refines":"D33-B027","specializes":"Container runtime technology","contrasts_with":"Container orchestration","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["OCI runtime architecture"],"provenance":["Open Container Initiative specifications and selected runtime official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B019","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Scheduling Technology","subtopic":"Job scheduling and periodic execution","concept":"Scheduling technology should be selected according to timing precision, durability, retry semantics, concurrency control, workload duration, dependency handling, and operational requirements.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should a job scheduler be selected for recurring and delayed work?","answer":"Evaluate whether the workload needs simple in-process scheduling, operating-system scheduling, a durable job queue, or a distributed scheduler. The choice depends on whether schedules must survive process restarts, coordinate across instances, enforce retries, preserve job state, or provide operational visibility.","explanation":"A periodic task is not automatically a distributed scheduling problem. A local cron job, application scheduler, durable queue, or workflow platform solves different requirements. The technology should be selected from timing guarantees, failure behavior, ownership, concurrency, and operational visibility rather than from feature volume.","principle":"Match scheduling technology to required durability and coordination semantics; use the simplest mechanism that satisfies those semantics.","objective":"Prevent over-engineering simple schedules while covering durable distributed scheduling needs.","requirements":["Schedule semantics","Durability requirement","Retry behavior","Concurrency policy","Execution ownership","Failure handling"],"constraints":["Wall-clock schedules can be affected by timezone/DST rules","Distributed schedulers require duplicate-execution handling","Missed schedules need explicit semantics","Long-running jobs may require workflow technology instead"],"preconditions":["Job lifecycle is defined","Schedule semantics are known"],"input":{"job":"daily data synchronization","requirements":["survive restart","avoid duplicate concurrent execution","record failure"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Durable scheduler/job system","selection_basis":["durability","coordination","retry","observability"]},"expected_behavior":"Scheduled work executes according to explicitly defined timing, ownership, retry, and duplicate-execution semantics.","incorrect_code":"while True:\n    run_job()\n    time.sleep(86400)\n# This does not provide durable schedule semantics or correct wall-clock scheduling.","corrected_code":"schedule = {\n    "type": "daily",\n    "timezone": "explicit",\n    "misfire_policy": "explicit",\n    "concurrency": "single_instance",\n    "retry_policy": "explicit",\n}\n# Select an actual scheduler based on these requirements.","failure_mode":"Scheduling implemented as an uncoordinated sleep loop or duplicate scheduler instances.","expected_error":"Missed jobs, duplicate jobs, drift, incorrect timezone behavior, or lost schedule state after restart.","cause":"Timing and durability requirements were not specified.","root_cause":"Scheduling treated as a timer rather than a recoverable workload-control problem.","solution":"Define schedule, timezone, misfire, retry, concurrency, ownership, and persistence semantics before selecting scheduler technology.","edge_case":"A daylight-saving transition can create an ambiguous or nonexistent local time; schedules must specify timezone and behavior explicitly.","security_implication":"Scheduled jobs may execute with privileged credentials; scheduler access and job authorization must be controlled.","performance_implication":"Large numbers of scheduled jobs can create burst load; evaluate scheduling overhead, queueing, and load-smoothing behavior.","test_case":"Restart the scheduler around a scheduled execution and verify the declared misfire and duplicate-execution policy.","test_input":{"event":"scheduler_restart","schedule":"daily"},"expected_test_result":"The resulting execution behavior matches the declared schedule recovery policy.","testing_guidance":"Test restart, clock changes, timezone/DST transitions, duplicate workers, retries, long-running jobs, and missed schedules.","verification_method":"Scheduler documentation plus controlled timing/failure tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact timezone, persistence, retry, and concurrency semantics of the selected scheduler.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Scheduler version must be explicitly selected.","dependencies":["Selected scheduler or job system"],"execution_requirements":["Scheduler test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Established scheduling engineering practice plus selected scheduler documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B024","D33-B057"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Job lifecycle","Schedule semantics"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B004","specializes":"Scheduling technology","contrasts_with":"Simple in-process timer","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Scheduling and job-processing engineering practice"],"provenance":["Selected scheduler official documentation; IANA Time Zone Database for timezone semantics"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B020","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Vector Retrieval Technology","subtopic":"Vector database and embedding index selection","concept":"Vector retrieval technology should be selected according to embedding dimensionality, similarity metric, corpus size, update rate, filtering, freshness, persistence, recall requirements, and operational constraints.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a vector database or vector index be introduced?","answer":"A vector index is a candidate when semantic similarity retrieval is a genuine requirement and exact keyword or relational retrieval cannot adequately satisfy the query semantics. The implementation should first define embedding model, similarity metric, corpus characteristics, filtering, update frequency, recall/latency requirements, and source-of-truth behavior.","explanation":"Vector search is an indexing and retrieval technique, not automatically a replacement for relational or lexical search. Depending on corpus size and requirements, vectors may be stored in a database extension, dedicated vector database, search engine, or local index. The technology decision should preserve provenance from retrieved vector records back to authoritative source content.","principle":"Introduce vector retrieval only when semantic similarity is required and select its storage/index technology from measurable retrieval requirements.","objective":"Prevent unnecessary vector infrastructure and preserve retrieval correctness.","requirements":["Embedding model defined","Similarity metric defined","Corpus characteristics known","Filtering requirements","Recall/latency targets","Index update strategy","Source provenance"],"constraints":["Embedding-model changes can invalidate or require re-indexing","Vector similarity is not equivalent to factual relevance","Access-control filters must be enforced during retrieval","Approximate nearest-neighbor indexes may trade recall for latency"],"preconditions":["Semantic retrieval requirement exists","Source corpus is identified"],"input":{"corpus":"engineering knowledge","requirements":["semantic retrieval","metadata filtering","source traceability"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Vector index/database","selection_basis":["retrieval quality","scale","filtering","update model","operational complexity"]},"expected_behavior":"Vector retrieval returns semantically relevant candidates while preserving source identity and enforcing authorization constraints.","incorrect_code":"results = vector_index.search(query_embedding, top_k=10)\n# Returning vector neighbors without authorization filtering or source provenance is insufficient.","corrected_code":"candidates = vector_index.search(query_embedding, top_k=50)\nresults = [\n    item for item in candidates\n    if authorized(item.metadata) and source_exists(item.source_id)\n][:10]\n# Actual relevance and authorization logic must be validated by the implementation.","failure_mode":"Vector retrieval returns semantically similar but unauthorized, stale, or contextually irrelevant content.","expected_error":"Incorrect answers, information disclosure, stale retrieval, or loss of source traceability.","cause":"Vector similarity treated as equivalent to authorization and relevance.","root_cause":"Retrieval semantics were not separated from indexing technology.","solution":"Define embedding/versioning, filtering, authorization, reranking, provenance, evaluation, and reindexing requirements before selecting vector technology.","edge_case":"Changing the embedding model or preprocessing pipeline may require a complete or partial re-index; vector spaces from incompatible models must not be mixed without explicit validation.","security_implication":"Vector indexes can leak sensitive information through retrieval unless tenant/access filters are enforced before results reach downstream reasoning.","performance_implication":"Measure recall, precision-oriented retrieval metrics, query latency, index build time, memory/storage consumption, and update cost on representative data.","test_case":"Evaluate known relevant and irrelevant documents under authorized and unauthorized query contexts.","test_input":{"query":"database transaction isolation","relevant_ids":["known_relevant"],"unauthorized_ids":["restricted"]},"expected_test_result":"Relevant authorized records are retrieved while unauthorized records are excluded.","testing_guidance":"Test retrieval relevance, authorization filters, stale indexes, embedding-version mismatch, deletion propagation, and adversarially similar content.","verification_method":"Vector-index documentation plus retrieval evaluation against a labeled corpus.","verification_guidance":"Record embedding model/version, preprocessing, similarity metric, index configuration, corpus version, and evaluation results.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Vector technology and embedding model versions must be pinned together.","dependencies":["Embedding model","Vector index/database","Embedding-compatible client"],"execution_requirements":["Representative labeled retrieval corpus"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Established information-retrieval practice plus selected vector technology documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B008","D33-B033","D33-B052","D33-B099"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Semantic retrieval requirement","Embedding specification","Authorization model"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B033","specializes":"Vector retrieval technology","contrasts_with":"Lexical/database-native retrieval","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Information retrieval and vector-search engineering practice"],"provenance":["Selected vector database/index official documentation; information-retrieval evaluation literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B031","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Search Technology","subtopic":"Full-text and relevance search","concept":"A dedicated search engine is justified when search requirements include relevance ranking, tokenization, stemming, fuzzy matching, faceting, highlighting, or large-scale text retrieval beyond the capabilities required from the primary transactional store.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated search engine be selected instead of database queries?","answer":"A search engine is a candidate when information retrieval semantics are a primary workload and require capabilities such as relevance scoring, analyzers, fuzzy matching, faceting, or high-volume text indexing. It should not be introduced merely because an application has a search box.","explanation":"Relational databases can provide effective exact and prefix search for many workloads. A dedicated engine such as OpenSearch or Elasticsearch becomes a technology candidate when retrieval semantics, indexing scale, or search-specific features justify a separate derived index. The authoritative source and index synchronization model must remain explicit.","principle":"Choose search technology according to retrieval requirements rather than treating every textual lookup as a search-engine requirement.","objective":"Separate authoritative data storage from specialized information-retrieval technology when the workload requires it.","requirements":["Defined retrieval semantics","Indexing requirements","Relevance requirements","Freshness requirement","Source-of-truth definition"],"constraints":["Search indexes are normally derived state","Index lag and rebuilds must be handled","Search engines add operational complexity","Authorization filtering must remain correct"],"preconditions":["Search workload is characterized","Authoritative data source is identified"],"input":{"queries":["text search","fuzzy matching","faceted filtering"],"dataset":"application records"},"output":{"technology_class":"Search engine","selection_basis":["retrieval quality","indexing","freshness","scale","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"Queries use an index optimized for declared retrieval semantics while authoritative records remain governed by the source-of-truth system.","incorrect_code":"results = sql.execute("SELECT * FROM documents WHERE body LIKE ?", [f"%{query}%"])\n# This may be adequate for small exact/pattern searches but does not provide general relevance search semantics.","corrected_code":"search_results = search_index.query({\n    "query": query,\n    "fields": ["title", "body"],\n    "filters": filters,\n})\n# Production implementation must define authorization and source/index consistency.","failure_mode":"A search engine becomes authoritative or search results expose records the caller cannot access.","expected_error":"Stale results, missing records, poor relevance, authorization leakage, or index inconsistency.","cause":"Derived search state and source-of-truth responsibilities were not separated.","root_cause":"Search was treated as a storage layer rather than a specialized retrieval projection.","solution":"Evaluate dedicated search technology against database-native search and select it only when retrieval requirements justify it.","edge_case":"The primary database may provide sufficient full-text capabilities for small datasets or modest search semantics; avoid premature introduction of a second datastore.","security_implication":"Search indexes can duplicate sensitive fields; document-level authorization and tenant filtering must be enforced consistently.","performance_implication":"Measure indexing throughput, query latency, relevance quality, index size, refresh interval, and rebuild time.","test_case":"Index representative documents and compare retrieval against a defined relevance test set and authorization policy.","test_input":{"query":"distributed transaction","authorized_tenant":"tenant_a"},"expected_test_result":"Returned documents satisfy relevance and authorization requirements under the declared index freshness contract.","testing_guidance":"Test indexing lag, deletes, rebuilds, analyzer behavior, fuzzy queries, authorization filters, high-cardinality facets, and shard failures.","verification_method":"Search-engine documentation plus representative information-retrieval evaluation.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact engine version, analyzer configuration, index mappings, query semantics, and security controls.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Exact search-engine version must be pinned.","dependencies":["Selected search engine","Indexing pipeline"],"execution_requirements":["Representative search corpus","Defined relevance queries"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Established information-retrieval practice plus selected search-engine primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B022","D34-B028"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Search workload characterization","Source-of-truth definition"],"extends":"","refines":"D34-B022","specializes":"Search technology","contrasts_with":"Database-native search","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Information retrieval architecture"],"provenance":["OpenSearch/Elasticsearch official documentation; information-retrieval literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B032","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Object Storage Technology","subtopic":"Blob and immutable artifact storage","concept":"Object storage is appropriate for large unstructured or semi-structured objects whose access pattern differs from transactional row-oriented data, such as documents, media, archives, backups, and build artifacts.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should object storage be used instead of a relational database for files and large artifacts?","answer":"Object storage is a candidate when data consists primarily of independent objects accessed by key or metadata rather than transactional relational joins. It provides a storage abstraction suited to large files and artifacts while metadata and business relationships can remain in a database.","explanation":"Putting large binary objects directly into a transactional database can be valid for some workloads, but object storage often provides a more appropriate technology boundary for large independent artifacts. The architecture must define object identity, metadata ownership, lifecycle, retention, integrity, access control, and consistency behavior.","principle":"Separate large object content from transactional metadata when their access and lifecycle requirements differ materially.","objective":"Choose storage technology according to object size, access pattern, lifecycle, and transactional requirements.","requirements":["Large-object storage","Stable object identity","Metadata linkage","Lifecycle management","Access control","Integrity verification"],"constraints":["Object storage is not a general relational database","Atomic coordination between metadata and object creation requires explicit design","Deletion and retention must be coordinated","Access URLs or tokens must be controlled"],"preconditions":["Object lifecycle is defined","Metadata ownership is defined"],"input":{"objects":["documents","images","archives"],"metadata":["owner","type","checksum","created_at"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Object storage","selection_basis":["object size","lifecycle","durability","access","cost","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"Object content is stored and retrieved independently while authoritative metadata maintains business relationships and lifecycle state.","incorrect_code":"db.execute("INSERT INTO files(content) VALUES (?)", [huge_binary_blob])\n# Storing every large object in transactional rows may create unnecessary database pressure.","corrected_code":"object_key = object_store.put(binary_content)\ndb.insert_file_metadata({\n    "object_key": object_key,\n    "checksum": sha256(binary_content),\n})","failure_mode":"Metadata says an object exists although the object upload failed, or object content remains after metadata deletion.","expected_error":"Dangling metadata, orphaned objects, broken downloads, or uncontrolled storage growth.","cause":"Metadata and object-storage lifecycle were treated as one atomic transaction without a coordination strategy.","root_cause":"Cross-system consistency requirements were not modeled.","solution":"Use explicit upload states, checksums, reconciliation, lifecycle policies, and cleanup workflows.","edge_case":"A client may upload successfully but lose connectivity before receiving the metadata commit response; reconciliation must detect and resolve uncertain state.","security_implication":"Object storage requires strict bucket/container policies, tenant isolation, encryption, controlled access tokens, and protection against unintended public exposure.","performance_implication":"Measure upload/download throughput, multipart behavior, object size distribution, metadata lookup latency, and storage lifecycle operations.","test_case":"Simulate a successful object upload followed by metadata transaction failure and verify reconciliation behavior.","test_input":{"object_upload":"success","metadata_commit":"failure"},"expected_test_result":"The system identifies the inconsistent state and resolves it according to the declared reconciliation policy.","testing_guidance":"Test partial uploads, interrupted downloads, duplicate uploads, deletion races, checksum mismatches, authorization, and retention policies.","verification_method":"Object-storage documentation plus failure/reconciliation tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact consistency, versioning, lifecycle, access-control, and integrity semantics of the selected provider.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Object-storage API/version must be pinned.","dependencies":["Selected object-storage system","Metadata database"],"execution_requirements":["Object-storage test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Object-storage engineering practice plus provider primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B026","D33-B052"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Object lifecycle","Metadata model"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B052","specializes":"Object storage technology","contrasts_with":"Database BLOB storage","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Object-storage architecture"],"provenance":["Selected object-storage provider official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B033","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Message Broker Technology","subtopic":"Durable asynchronous messaging","concept":"A message broker is appropriate when producers and consumers require temporal decoupling, buffering, independent scaling, durable delivery, consumer groups, or explicit retry/dead-letter semantics.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a message broker be introduced instead of direct synchronous calls?","answer":"A message broker is a candidate when asynchronous delivery, producer-consumer decoupling, buffering, independent scaling, durable messages, or consumer-specific delivery semantics are required. It should not be introduced solely because an architecture uses multiple services.","explanation":"Direct calls are often simpler for request-response interactions. A broker becomes valuable when temporal decoupling and durable delivery are material requirements. The selected broker must have documented delivery, ordering, retention, retry, acknowledgment, and failure semantics.","principle":"Use a message broker when asynchronous delivery semantics provide required architectural value that synchronous communication cannot safely provide.","objective":"Match messaging technology to explicit asynchronous delivery requirements.","requirements":["Asynchronous delivery","Durability where required","Retry semantics","Consumer isolation","Ordering requirements","Failure handling"],"constraints":["Messages may be duplicated","Ordering is usually scoped rather than globally guaranteed","Consumers must be idempotent where required","Broker availability becomes part of the system dependency graph"],"preconditions":["Message semantics are defined","Producer and consumer responsibilities are explicit"],"input":{"messages":["OrderCreated","PaymentRequested"],"requirements":["durability","retry","independent_consumers"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Message broker","selection_basis":["delivery","ordering","retention","throughput","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"Messages survive according to the declared durability contract and consumers process them according to acknowledgment/retry semantics.","incorrect_code":"requests.post("http://inventory/internal/reserve", json=event)\n# A synchronous request does not provide durable temporal decoupling.","corrected_code":"broker.publish("inventory.reserve", event)\n# Consumer acknowledgement, retry, idempotency, and dead-letter semantics must be defined.","failure_mode":"Messages are lost, duplicated, processed out of order, or retried indefinitely.","expected_error":"Missing business events, duplicate side effects, processing starvation, or poison-message loops.","cause":"Broker semantics were assumed rather than explicitly designed.","root_cause":"Delivery and failure requirements were not translated into technology requirements.","solution":"Select a broker whose actual delivery, retention, ordering, and retry semantics match the required workload.","edge_case":"A single consumer with low-volume asynchronous work may be adequately served by a database-backed queue rather than a dedicated broker.","security_implication":"Topics/queues require producer and consumer authorization, encryption in transit, tenant isolation, and protection against unauthorized message publication.","performance_implication":"Measure publish latency, consumer throughput, queue depth, retention storage, retry overhead, and backpressure behavior.","test_case":"Publish a message, force consumer failure before acknowledgment, and verify the documented redelivery behavior.","test_input":{"publish":"success","consumer":"fails_before_ack"},"expected_test_result":"The message follows the broker's declared redelivery/retry semantics without violating consumer idempotency requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicates, ordering, consumer restart, broker restart, poison messages, retention expiry, backpressure, and dead-letter handling.","verification_method":"Broker primary documentation plus integration/failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact broker version and client configuration rather than assuming generic message-delivery guarantees.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Exact broker/client versions must be pinned.","dependencies":["Selected message broker","Client library"],"execution_requirements":["Broker test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Messaging architecture practice plus selected broker primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B024","D33-B032","D33-B034","D33-B057"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Delivery semantics","Failure policy"],"extends":"D33-B032","refines":"D33-B034","specializes":"Message broker technology","contrasts_with":"Synchronous HTTP/RPC","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Asynchronous messaging architecture"],"provenance":["Selected message-broker official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B034","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Runtime Technology","subtopic":"Process and concurrency model selection","concept":"Runtime technology must match workload characteristics such as I/O concurrency, CPU-bound computation, isolation requirements, memory behavior, startup characteristics, and deployment constraints.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should runtime technology be selected for an application workload?","answer":"Select runtime technology according to measured or well-characterized workload properties rather than language popularity. I/O-heavy workloads may benefit from asynchronous or event-driven runtimes, CPU-heavy workloads may require multiprocessing or native execution, and strong isolation requirements may favor process or container boundaries.","explanation":"Runtime selection is a technology decision distinct from language selection. Python asyncio, Java virtual threads, Node.js event loops, JVM thread pools, and native processes have different scheduling and resource characteristics. The correct choice depends on the workload and operational constraints.","principle":"Choose runtime and concurrency technology from workload characteristics and correctness requirements.","objective":"Prevent mismatched concurrency models from becoming performance or reliability bottlenecks.","requirements":["Workload classification","Concurrency model","Resource limits","Isolation requirements","Cancellation/recovery semantics"],"constraints":["Concurrency does not automatically increase throughput for CPU-bound work","Blocking operations can stall event-loop systems","Thread/process overhead differs by runtime","Runtime behavior is version-dependent"],"preconditions":["Representative workload is known","Resource constraints are defined"],"input":{"workload":["I/O-bound requests","CPU-bound transformations"],"constraints":["memory","CPU","latency"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Runtime/concurrency model","selection_basis":["workload","latency","throughput","isolation","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"The chosen runtime handles the dominant workload without violating resource or concurrency requirements.","incorrect_code":"async def handler():\n    result = cpu_heavy_function()\n    return result\n# CPU-bound blocking work can monopolize an event loop.","corrected_code":"async def handler(executor, input_data):\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    return await loop.run_in_executor(executor, cpu_heavy_function, input_data)\n# The production design must size and observe the executor explicitly.","failure_mode":"Blocking or CPU-heavy work starves an asynchronous runtime.","expected_error":"High latency, event-loop stalls, request timeouts, or resource exhaustion.","cause":"Workload characteristics were not matched to runtime execution semantics.","root_cause":"Concurrency model selected without workload classification.","solution":"Classify workload and select event-loop, thread, process, native, or hybrid execution according to measured requirements.","edge_case":"A workload may be mixed I/O and CPU; isolate CPU-heavy sections instead of forcing the entire application into one concurrency model.","security_implication":"More execution workers increase resource-consumption surface; concurrency limits and isolation are required to resist resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Measure throughput, tail latency, CPU utilization, context switching, queue depth, memory use, and cancellation behavior.","test_case":"Run representative I/O and CPU workloads while measuring latency and resource saturation under the selected concurrency model.","test_input":{"workload_mix":{"io":0.8,"cpu":0.2},"concurrency":"representative"},"expected_test_result":"Observed resource and latency behavior is compared against explicit requirements without claiming an unmeasured benchmark.","testing_guidance":"Test saturation, cancellation, worker failure, backpressure, blocking-call detection, memory growth, and graceful shutdown.","verification_method":"Runtime documentation plus representative workload testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact runtime version and concurrency implementation semantics.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"asyncio","framework_version":"Python 3.11+ standard library","version_range":"Python 3.11+; exact production version must be pinned.","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":["Representative workload","Controlled CPU/memory limits"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Official Python runtime documentation plus workload-specific measurement.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B003","D33-B023","D33-B055"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Workload characterization"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B003","specializes":"Runtime/concurrency technology","contrasts_with":"Synchronous blocking runtime","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Concurrency and runtime engineering principles"],"provenance":["Python official documentation; corresponding runtime documentation for alternatives"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B035","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Container Technology","subtopic":"Application packaging and isolation","concept":"Containers are useful when reproducible application packaging, dependency isolation, process-level resource controls, or portable deployment units are explicit requirements.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should container technology be introduced?","answer":"Container technology is a candidate when applications require reproducible packaging, dependency isolation, standardized deployment artifacts, resource controls, or orchestration integration. Containers are not automatically required for every application and do not replace application-level security or isolation requirements.","explanation":"Containers provide a packaging and process-isolation mechanism. Docker and OCI-compatible runtimes are common implementations, while orchestration platforms provide additional scheduling and lifecycle capabilities. The technology decision must distinguish packaging needs from orchestration needs.","principle":"Use containers when packaging, isolation, deployment consistency, or orchestration requirements justify the operational layer.","objective":"Determine whether containerization provides material deployment value.","requirements":["Reproducible artifact","Dependency isolation","Resource limits where required","Deployment consistency","Image provenance"],"constraints":["Container isolation is not equivalent to a dedicated virtual machine or hardware isolation","Images must be maintained and scanned","Persistent state requires explicit storage design","Container orchestration adds additional complexity"],"preconditions":["Deployment requirements are defined","Application packaging model is understood"],"input":{"application":"API service","requirements":["reproducible deployment","dependency isolation"]},"output":{"technology_class":"OCI container runtime","selection_basis":["packaging","isolation","portability","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"The application can be packaged into a reproducible artifact with explicitly controlled runtime dependencies.","incorrect_code":"FROM python:latest\nCOPY . /app\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n# Floating base versions reduce reproducibility and make provenance harder to establish.","corrected_code":"FROM python:3.11-slim\nCOPY requirements.txt /app/\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r /app/requirements.txt\nCOPY . /app\n# Production builds should pin/verify dependencies and record image provenance.","failure_mode":"Container images are irreproducible, contain uncontrolled dependencies, or are treated as complete security boundaries.","expected_error":"Unexpected runtime differences, vulnerable dependencies, difficult rollback, or insufficient isolation.","cause":"Container packaging was implemented without reproducibility and threat-model requirements.","root_cause":"Container technology was confused with a complete deployment/security solution.","solution":"Define image reproducibility, dependency provenance, runtime privileges, resource controls, storage, and lifecycle requirements before selecting container tooling.","edge_case":"A local-first or single-host application may not benefit from container orchestration even if containers simplify packaging.","security_implication":"Use minimal images, non-root execution where feasible, controlled capabilities, signed/provenance-aware artifacts, and runtime restrictions appropriate to the threat model.","performance_implication":"Measure startup time, image size, filesystem behavior, resource overhead, and network/storage effects under the actual deployment environment.","test_case":"Build the same source revision twice under controlled inputs and compare artifact provenance/digest behavior.","test_input":{"source_revision":"fixed","dependencies":"locked"},"expected_test_result":"The build process produces a reproducible or explicitly characterized artifact according to the declared reproducibility requirement.","testing_guidance":"Test image startup, health behavior, dependency installation, privilege boundaries, resource limits, network policies, and rollback.","verification_method":"OCI/container runtime documentation plus reproducible-build and security checks.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact base image digest, runtime version, dependency lock state, and build provenance.","language":"Dockerfile","language_version":"","framework":"OCI","framework_version":"Specification/runtime version must be pinned","version_range":"Exact container runtime/base image versions must be declared.","dependencies":["OCI-compatible runtime"],"execution_requirements":["Container build/runtime environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OCI/container engineering practice plus primary runtime documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B027","D33-B056","D33-B094"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Deployment requirements","Artifact provenance requirements"],"extends":"D33-B056","refines":"D33-B094","specializes":"Container technology","contrasts_with":"Direct host deployment","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Container packaging and deployment practice"],"provenance":["OCI specifications; Docker official documentation; reproducible-build guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B036","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Orchestration Technology","subtopic":"Container scheduling and lifecycle","concept":"Container orchestration technology is justified when applications require automated scheduling, replica management, service discovery, health-based replacement, rolling deployment, or multi-node workload coordination.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a container orchestrator such as Kubernetes be introduced?","answer":"An orchestrator is a candidate when workload count, availability requirements, deployment automation, scheduling, service discovery, resource management, or multi-node operations exceed what simpler host or container management can reliably provide. It should not be introduced solely because containers are being used.","explanation":"Containerization and orchestration solve different problems. Kubernetes provides extensive scheduling and lifecycle capabilities but also introduces substantial operational complexity. The decision must compare required capabilities with simpler deployment mechanisms and organizational operating capacity.","principle":"Adopt orchestration when its required lifecycle and scheduling capabilities justify its operational complexity.","objective":"Avoid treating orchestration as a default requirement for every containerized application.","requirements":["Workload scheduling","Replica management","Health-based replacement","Deployment automation","Resource management","Service networking"],"constraints":["Large operational surface","Control-plane dependency","Configuration complexity","Requires operational expertise","Incorrect resource/health configuration can cause instability"],"preconditions":["Multiple workloads or nodes justify scheduling","Container deployment requirements are defined"],"input":{"requirements":["multi-instance deployment","rolling updates","health replacement","resource limits"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Container orchestrator","selection_basis":["scheduling","availability","deployment","operations","complexity"]},"expected_behavior":"Workloads are scheduled and managed according to explicit lifecycle, health, resource, and deployment policies.","incorrect_code":"# Deploying an orchestrator solely because the application is containerized\n# does not establish a requirement for cluster scheduling.","corrected_code":"orchestration_requirements = {\n    "multi_node_scheduling": True,\n    "replica_management": True,\n    "rolling_updates": True,\n    "health_replacement": True,\n}\n# Compare these requirements with simpler deployment options before adoption.","failure_mode":"Operational complexity exceeds the actual deployment requirement.","expected_error":"Configuration drift, difficult incident response, unnecessary infrastructure, or fragile deployments.","cause":"Orchestration capabilities were selected without workload and operational analysis.","root_cause":"Container packaging was incorrectly treated as proof that orchestration was required.","solution":"Evaluate orchestration against workload count, node count, availability, deployment automation, and operational maturity.","edge_case":"A single-host or low-scale service may obtain sufficient reliability from systemd, a managed platform, or another simpler runtime.","security_implication":"The orchestrator control plane and workload identities become high-value security boundaries and require strict RBAC, network controls, secret handling, and upgrade discipline.","performance_implication":"Measure scheduler overhead, startup/replacement latency, resource utilization, network overhead, and control-plane behavior under workload churn.","test_case":"Deploy a representative workload, terminate an instance, and verify health-based replacement and service continuity according to the defined requirement.","test_input":{"failure":"worker_termination","desired_replicas":3},"expected_test_result":"The orchestrator restores the declared desired state within the tested operational contract.","testing_guidance":"Test scheduling failure, node loss, rolling update, rollback, resource pressure, health probes, network failure, and control-plane degradation.","verification_method":"Kubernetes/orchestrator official documentation plus controlled failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact orchestrator version, API versions, health semantics, resource policies, and deployment configuration.","language":"YAML","language_version":"","framework":"Kubernetes","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact Kubernetes/API versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Container runtime","Orchestrator control plane"],"execution_requirements":["Multi-node or equivalent orchestrator test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Kubernetes primary documentation plus orchestration engineering practice.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B027","D33-B038","D33-B056","D34-B035"],"depends_on":["D34-B035"],"requires":["Container deployment requirements","Scheduling requirements"],"extends":"D34-B035","refines":"D33-B027","specializes":"Container orchestration technology","contrasts_with":"Single-host deployment","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Container orchestration architecture"],"provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B037","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Workflow Orchestration Technology","subtopic":"Durable workflow execution","concept":"A durable workflow engine is appropriate when business workflows span long durations, retries, timers, external actions, resumability, and explicit workflow state that should survive process failure.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a durable workflow engine be selected instead of ordinary application code?","answer":"A workflow engine is a candidate when workflows contain long-running waits, retries, timers, human approvals, external side effects, durable state, or recovery requirements that would otherwise require extensive custom orchestration code.","explanation":"Ordinary application functions are often sufficient for short synchronous workflows. Durable workflow technology can persist workflow state and coordinate retries and timers across process restarts. Temporal and similar technologies provide durable workflow execution but introduce a new runtime and operational model.","principle":"Use workflow technology when durable orchestration semantics are themselves a material requirement.","objective":"Avoid building fragile custom workflow persistence and retry machinery when a durable workflow engine is justified.","requirements":["Durable workflow state","Timers","Retries","External activities","Recovery","Workflow versioning"],"constraints":["Workflow engines impose deterministic execution rules in some models","External side effects require idempotency","Workflow definitions require version management","The engine becomes operational infrastructure"],"preconditions":["Workflow lifecycle is characterized","External side effects and recovery requirements are defined"],"input":{"workflow":["approval","external_call","wait","retry","completion"],"duration":"days"},"output":{"technology_class":"Durable workflow engine","selection_basis":["durability","recovery","timers","versioning","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"Workflow progress survives worker restart and resumes according to durable execution semantics.","incorrect_code":"def workflow():\n    call_external_service()\n    time.sleep(86400)\n    call_next_step()\n# Process-local sleeping cannot provide durable long-running workflow execution.","corrected_code":"@workflow_definition\ndef workflow():\n    result = call_activity("external_service")\n    wait_for_duration("24h")\n    return call_activity("next_step", result)\n# Exact API semantics depend on the selected workflow engine.","failure_mode":"Long-running workflow state disappears when a process restarts or deployment changes.","expected_error":"Duplicate external actions, lost approvals, stuck workflows, or inconsistent recovery.","cause":"Workflow state was held only in process memory.","root_cause":"Long-running orchestration was implemented without durable workflow state.","solution":"Evaluate a durable workflow engine when recovery, timers, and long-lived orchestration justify it.","edge_case":"Workflow definitions may change while old executions remain active; compatibility/versioning strategy is mandatory.","security_implication":"Workflow workers may hold authorization to perform consequential external actions; worker identity and activity permissions require least privilege and auditability.","performance_implication":"Measure workflow scheduling latency, history/state growth, worker utilization, activity throughput, and recovery time.","test_case":"Start a workflow, terminate the worker, and restart it before the workflow's next timer or activity.","test_input":{"workflow_state":"waiting","worker":"terminated_then_restarted"},"expected_test_result":"The workflow resumes according to the engine's documented durable execution semantics without losing or duplicating protected side effects.","testing_guidance":"Test worker crashes, activity retries, duplicate delivery, timers, version changes, cancellation, compensation, and external-service failures.","verification_method":"Workflow-engine documentation plus failure/recovery testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact engine version and workflow-versioning semantics; do not infer guarantees from the term durable alone.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Temporal","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact Temporal SDK/server versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Workflow engine","Worker runtime"],"execution_requirements":["Workflow-engine test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Durable workflow engineering practice plus Temporal primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B004","D33-B024","D33-B057","D33-B060"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Long-running workflow requirements"],"extends":"D33-B004","refines":"D33-B057","specializes":"Durable workflow technology","contrasts_with":"Process-local workflow code","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Durable execution architecture"],"provenance":["Temporal official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B038","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Identity Technology","subtopic":"Federated identity and authentication","concept":"An identity provider or federation protocol is appropriate when authentication must be centralized across applications, organizations, or external identity domains.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should federated identity technology be selected?","answer":"Federated identity technology is a candidate when users need centralized authentication across multiple applications or organizations, especially when enterprise identity providers, single sign-on, lifecycle management, and standardized authentication protocols are requirements.","explanation":"Applications should generally avoid implementing password authentication and identity lifecycle logic from scratch when a trusted identity system can provide the required capabilities. OIDC and SAML solve different integration patterns, and selection must match the relying-party and identity-provider requirements.","principle":"Delegate authentication to an appropriate identity system while keeping application authorization under explicit application/domain policy.","objective":"Select identity technology according to authentication and federation requirements without conflating authentication with authorization.","requirements":["Authentication","Identity lifecycle","Single sign-on where required","Federation protocol","Session/token management"],"constraints":["Identity-provider outage affects authentication unless resilience is designed","Token validation and key rotation must be handled","Application authorization remains separate","Protocol and provider compatibility must be verified"],"preconditions":["Identity actors are defined","Authentication requirements are documented"],"input":{"requirements":["enterprise_sso","external_identity_provider","oidc"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Federated identity provider/protocol","selection_basis":["protocol","lifecycle","availability","security","integration"]},"expected_behavior":"Applications authenticate users through the selected identity protocol and validate issuer, audience, signature, expiry, and required claims according to the protocol.","incorrect_code":"user = decode_token_without_verification(token)\n# Decoding token contents is not equivalent to cryptographic validation.","corrected_code":"claims = validate_oidc_token(\n    token,\n    issuer=EXPECTED_ISSUER,\n    audience=EXPECTED_AUDIENCE,\n    jwks=TRUSTED_JWKS,\n)\n# Exact validation requirements depend on the selected protocol/library.","failure_mode":"Application trusts an unverified or incorrectly scoped token.","expected_error":"Authentication bypass, token replay, cross-tenant access, or incorrect identity mapping.","cause":"Token parsing was confused with token validation.","root_cause":"Authentication technology requirements were not translated into explicit trust checks.","solution":"Use a standards-based identity provider/protocol and verify issuer, audience, signature, lifetime, and required claims according to the protocol.","edge_case":"Signing keys rotate; applications must handle key discovery and rotation without accepting untrusted keys.","security_implication":"Identity infrastructure is a critical security boundary; issuer trust, key management, redirect URI controls, token storage, and session handling require rigorous protection.","performance_implication":"Measure authentication latency, token validation overhead, key-cache behavior, and behavior during identity-provider degradation.","test_case":"Present a token with a valid signature but an incorrect audience and verify rejection.","test_input":{"token":{"signature":"valid","audience":"wrong-service"}},"expected_test_result":"The application rejects the token because its audience does not match the declared relying-party requirement.","testing_guidance":"Test issuer mismatch, audience mismatch, expired tokens, key rotation, replay, redirect handling, logout/session expiry, and identity-provider outage.","verification_method":"OIDC/SAML standards and selected identity-provider documentation plus security integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact protocol flow, library version, issuer configuration, JWKS handling, and claim validation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"OpenID Connect","framework_version":"Protocol/library version must be pinned","version_range":"Use the selected OIDC/SAML profile and pinned implementation versions.","dependencies":["Identity provider","OIDC/SAML client"],"execution_requirements":["Identity-provider test tenant"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OIDC/SAML standards plus identity-provider primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B007","D33-B051","D33-B059"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Authentication requirements","Authorization model"],"extends":"D33-B051","refines":"D33-B059","specializes":"Federated identity technology","contrasts_with":"Application-managed passwords","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Federated identity architecture"],"provenance":["OpenID Foundation specifications; OASIS SAML specifications; selected identity-provider documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B039","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Secrets Management Technology","subtopic":"Runtime secret storage","concept":"Dedicated secret-management technology is appropriate when applications require controlled storage, retrieval, rotation, auditing, and scoped access to credentials or cryptographic secrets.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated secrets manager be used instead of environment variables or configuration files?","answer":"A secrets manager is a candidate when secrets require centralized lifecycle management, rotation, auditability, access policies, or secure distribution across multiple workloads. Environment variables or protected deployment configuration can be adequate for simpler systems when their security and lifecycle requirements are satisfied.","explanation":"Secrets differ from ordinary configuration because disclosure can directly compromise protected systems. Dedicated technologies such as HashiCorp Vault, cloud secret managers, or orchestrator-integrated secret facilities provide different operational models. The choice must consider threat model, rotation, availability, access control, and deployment environment.","principle":"Manage secrets according to their sensitivity, lifecycle, and access requirements rather than treating them as ordinary configuration.","objective":"Select appropriate secret-management technology without exposing credentials through source control or uncontrolled configuration.","requirements":["Secret storage","Access control","Rotation","Audit","Secure retrieval","Revocation"],"constraints":["Secrets managers become dependencies","Bootstrap credentials require their own trust model","Secret rotation can break long-lived connections","Application logs must never expose secret values"],"preconditions":["Secret classes are identified","Threat model is defined"],"input":{"secrets":["database_password","api_key","signing_key"],"requirements":["rotation","audit","least_privilege"]},"output":{"technology_class":"Secrets manager","selection_basis":["security","rotation","availability","integration","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"Applications retrieve only authorized secrets through a controlled mechanism and do not persist them in source code or ordinary configuration stores.","incorrect_code":"DB_PASSWORD = "production-password"\n# Hard-coded credentials create unnecessary exposure.","corrected_code":"DB_PASSWORD = secret_manager.get("database/password")\n# Production implementation must define authentication to the secret manager and rotation behavior.","failure_mode":"Credentials leak through source code, images, logs, backups, or broad configuration access.","expected_error":"Credential compromise, unauthorized access, difficult rotation, or inability to audit secret usage.","cause":"Secret lifecycle was treated as ordinary configuration.","root_cause":"Sensitive credential requirements were not separated from general configuration requirements.","solution":"Select a secret-management mechanism appropriate to the deployment threat model and define bootstrap, retrieval, rotation, revocation, and failure semantics.","edge_case":"A local development environment may use a protected developer-specific mechanism rather than the production secret-management system, but production credentials must never be copied into development artifacts.","security_implication":"Secrets management is a high-impact security boundary; access must be least-privileged and secret values must be excluded from logs, telemetry, source control, and error messages.","performance_implication":"Measure retrieval latency, caching behavior, rotation impact, and dependency behavior during secret-manager outages.","test_case":"Rotate a non-production credential and verify that the application obtains the new value without exposing either old or new secret material in logs.","test_input":{"secret":"test_database_password","operation":"rotate"},"expected_test_result":"The application transitions according to the documented rotation contract and no secret value appears in observable logs.","testing_guidance":"Test rotation, revocation, outage, bootstrap, permission denial, audit events, and accidental logging.","verification_method":"Secrets-manager documentation plus security and rotation tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact authentication, storage, access-policy, rotation, and availability semantics of the selected technology.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Secret-management technology/version must be pinned.","dependencies":["Selected secrets manager"],"execution_requirements":["Non-production secret-management environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Secrets-management security practice plus selected technology primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B035","D33-B051","D33-B052"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Threat model","Secret classification"],"extends":"D33-B035","refines":"D33-B051","specializes":"Secrets-management technology","contrasts_with":"Plain configuration files","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Secrets-management architecture and security practice"],"provenance":["HashiCorp Vault official documentation; selected cloud/orchestrator secret-management documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B040","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Observability Technology","subtopic":"Metrics logs and distributed tracing","concept":"Observability technology should be selected according to the signals required to understand system behavior, with metrics, logs, traces, and correlation designed as complementary evidence rather than interchangeable data stores.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should an OpenTelemetry-based observability technology stack be introduced?","answer":"An OpenTelemetry-based stack is a candidate when applications span multiple components or asynchronous boundaries and require standardized collection and propagation of telemetry across services and runtimes. It provides instrumentation and telemetry transport concepts but still requires appropriate storage and visualization backends.","explanation":"Metrics, logs, and traces answer different operational questions. OpenTelemetry can standardize instrumentation and context propagation, while backend technologies store and query telemetry. The technology decision must define signal ownership, sampling, retention, privacy, cardinality, and failure behavior.","principle":"Standardize telemetry collection and correlation around the observability requirements rather than selecting a monitoring product without defining evidence needs.","objective":"Create interoperable observability evidence across application and infrastructure boundaries.","requirements":["Metrics","Logs","Traces","Correlation context","Telemetry transport","Retention policy"],"constraints":["Telemetry can contain sensitive information","High-cardinality attributes can create storage and cost problems","Sampling can remove evidence required for diagnosis","Observability systems must not become critical runtime dependencies unless explicitly designed as such"],"preconditions":["Operational questions are defined","Telemetry data classification exists"],"input":{"signals":["latency","errors","traces","business events"],"requirements":["cross-service correlation","diagnostics"]},"output":{"technology_class":"OpenTelemetry-based observability stack","selection_basis":["instrumentation","interoperability","correlation","backend compatibility","operability"]},"expected_behavior":"Telemetry propagates correlation context across relevant boundaries and reaches configured observability backends according to declared sampling and retention policies.","incorrect_code":"logger.info("request failed")\n# An isolated message without correlation context is insufficient for distributed diagnosis.","corrected_code":"span = tracer.start_span("process_order")\nwith span:\n    logger.info("processing_order", extra={"trace_id": span.trace_id})\n# Exact APIs depend on the selected OpenTelemetry SDK/instrumentation.","failure_mode":"Telemetry exists but cannot connect events across services or is too sparse/noisy to support diagnosis.","expected_error":"Untraceable failures, missing causal context, excessive telemetry volume, or sensitive-data leakage.","cause":"Signal design focused on collection volume instead of operational questions and correlation.","root_cause":"Observability technology was selected without defining evidence requirements.","solution":"Define operational questions, correlation requirements, telemetry schemas, sampling, retention, and privacy rules, then select compatible OpenTelemetry instrumentation and backends.","edge_case":"A low-volume local application may not need distributed tracing infrastructure; structured logs and metrics can satisfy its operational evidence requirements.","security_implication":"Telemetry may contain credentials, identifiers, payload fragments, or sensitive business data; redaction and access controls are mandatory.","performance_implication":"Measure instrumentation overhead, telemetry volume, exporter behavior, cardinality, and backend ingestion under representative workloads.","test_case":"Trace a request crossing synchronous and asynchronous boundaries and verify that correlation identifiers remain connected in telemetry.","test_input":{"flow":["api","worker","database"],"correlation":"trace_context"},"expected_test_result":"The trace and associated telemetry preserve the declared correlation context across the tested boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Test context propagation, sampling, exporter failure, telemetry backpressure, redaction, high-cardinality attributes, and clock/timestamp behavior.","verification_method":"OpenTelemetry specifications/documentation plus integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact SDK versions, instrumentation packages, propagation configuration, exporter behavior, and backend compatibility.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"OpenTelemetry","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact OpenTelemetry SDK/instrumentation versions must be declared.","dependencies":["OpenTelemetry SDK","Telemetry collector/backend"],"execution_requirements":["Multi-component test application","Telemetry backend"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OpenTelemetry primary documentation/specifications plus observability engineering practice.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B029","D33-B043","D33-B054","D33-B060"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Observability requirements","Telemetry data policy"],"extends":"D33-B054","refines":"D33-B029","specializes":"Observability technology","contrasts_with":"Uncorrelated application logging","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Distributed observability architecture"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry official documentation and specifications"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B041","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Database Migration Technology","subtopic":"Schema evolution and controlled migration","concept":"Database migration technology provides repeatable, versioned, auditable changes to database schema and sometimes data while supporting controlled deployment across environments.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated database migration technology be used?","answer":"A migration system is appropriate when database schema changes must be versioned, repeatable, ordered, reviewable, and deployable across multiple environments. Manual production schema editing should not be the normal evolution mechanism.","explanation":"Migration tooling turns database evolution into an explicit artifact associated with application versions. Tools such as Alembic, Flyway, and Liquibase implement different migration models. The technology must support the target database, transaction behavior, rollback limitations, deployment sequencing, and team workflow.","principle":"Treat database schema evolution as versioned software rather than undocumented manual state.","objective":"Make database changes reproducible and traceable across development, testing, staging, and production.","requirements":["Versioned migrations","Ordering","Repeatability","Reviewability","Environment consistency","Deployment integration"],"constraints":["Some DDL operations cannot be safely rolled back","Large data migrations may require separate jobs","Application/schema compatibility must be maintained during rolling deployments","Migration tools do not automatically make unsafe migrations safe"],"preconditions":["Database schema ownership is defined","Deployment strategy is known"],"input":{"migration":"add_customer_status","from_version":"previous_schema","to_version":"new_schema"},"output":{"artifact":"versioned migration","result":"target schema state"},"expected_behavior":"Applying migrations in declared order produces the intended schema state without relying on undocumented manual operations.","incorrect_code":"# Manual production-only change:\nALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN status TEXT;\n# No migration artifact or compatibility strategy exists.","corrected_code":"def upgrade():\n    op.add_column("customers", Column("status", String(), nullable=True))\n\ndef downgrade():\n    op.drop_column("customers", "status")\n# Exact APIs depend on the selected migration tool.","failure_mode":"Application and database versions become incompatible during deployment.","expected_error":"Missing-column errors, incompatible constraints, failed deployments, or irrecoverable migration state.","cause":"Schema changes were deployed without compatibility sequencing.","root_cause":"Database evolution was treated as an ad hoc operational task.","solution":"Use versioned migrations with expand/contract patterns where rolling compatibility is required.","edge_case":"A migration involving billions of rows may need an online or asynchronous data transformation rather than one blocking schema transaction.","security_implication":"Migration credentials should have controlled privileges and should not be embedded in application runtime credentials unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Measure lock duration, migration execution time, table rewrite behavior, index build impact, and production workload interference.","test_case":"Apply migrations to an empty database and an existing representative database, then compare resulting schema state.","test_input":{"database_states":["empty","representative_existing"],"migration_chain":"full"},"expected_test_result":"Both environments converge to the declared target schema without undocumented manual changes.","testing_guidance":"Test fresh installation, upgrade from supported historical versions, partial migration recovery, locking behavior, rollback limitations, and concurrent application traffic.","verification_method":"Migration-tool documentation plus schema-diff and integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact database version, migration-tool version, DDL transaction semantics, and supported rollback behavior.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Alembic","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact Alembic/database versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Target database","Migration tool"],"execution_requirements":["Disposable test database","Representative schema history"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Database migration engineering practice plus selected tool documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B026","D33-B060","D33-B100"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Database schema ownership","Deployment compatibility policy"],"extends":"D33-B026","refines":"D33-B060","specializes":"Database migration technology","contrasts_with":"Manual schema modification","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Database schema evolution practice"],"provenance":["Alembic/Flyway/Liquibase official documentation; database vendor documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B042","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Database Connection Pooling","subtopic":"Connection lifecycle and concurrency","concept":"Connection pooling technology reuses database connections and limits concurrent database sessions, reducing connection-establishment overhead while enforcing an explicit concurrency boundary.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should database connection pooling be used?","answer":"Connection pooling is appropriate for applications making repeated database requests, especially concurrent services where creating a new connection per operation would add latency or exceed database connection capacity.","explanation":"A pool controls how many connections can exist and how callers wait for available connections. Pool size is a resource-management decision, not simply a performance knob. It must account for database capacity, application concurrency, transaction duration, failure recovery, and deployment replica count.","principle":"Treat database connections as a finite shared resource and explicitly control their lifecycle and concurrency.","objective":"Prevent connection storms while maintaining acceptable database access latency.","requirements":["Connection reuse","Maximum pool size","Timeout behavior","Health checking","Connection recycling"],"constraints":["Total connections multiply across application replicas","Long transactions occupy connections","Oversized pools can overload the database","Stale connections require recovery handling"],"preconditions":["Database connection limits are known","Application concurrency is characterized"],"input":{"application_replicas":4,"pool_per_replica":10,"database_connection_budget":50},"output":{"resource_model":"bounded connection pools"},"expected_behavior":"Concurrent requests obtain connections from a bounded pool and fail or wait according to explicit timeout policy when capacity is exhausted.","incorrect_code":"def query(sql):\n    connection = connect_to_database()\n    try:\n        return connection.execute(sql)\n    finally:\n        connection.close()\n# Repeated connection establishment can create unnecessary connection churn.","corrected_code":"pool = create_connection_pool(max_size=10, timeout=5)\n\ndef query(sql):\n    with pool.connection() as connection:\n        return connection.execute(sql)","failure_mode":"Every application replica independently creates excessive connections.","expected_error":"Connection exhaustion, queueing, database overload, or connection timeout errors.","cause":"Pool capacity was selected without accounting for replica count and database limits.","root_cause":"Database connections were not modeled as a finite shared resource.","solution":"Calculate aggregate connection demand and configure bounded pools, acquisition timeouts, recycling, and failure handling.","edge_case":"A serverless or highly elastic deployment can create rapid connection spikes and may require a proxy or database-specific connection-management technology.","security_implication":"Connections must use controlled credentials, TLS where required, and safe secret-management mechanisms.","performance_implication":"Measure pool wait time, active/idle connections, transaction duration, database saturation, and timeout rates.","test_case":"Generate concurrency greater than pool capacity and verify bounded behavior without unbounded connection creation.","test_input":{"concurrency":50,"pool_size":10},"expected_test_result":"At most the configured pool capacity is actively connected per pool instance and excess requests follow the documented wait/timeout behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test connection exhaustion, database restart, stale connections, long transactions, pool saturation, and application shutdown.","verification_method":"Database-driver/pool documentation plus load and failure testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact driver/pool semantics, timeout units, recycling behavior, and database connection limits.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Exact database driver/pool version must be pinned.","dependencies":["Database driver","Target database"],"execution_requirements":["Representative database","Controlled concurrency test"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Database connection-management practice plus driver/database documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B003","D33-B023","D34-B034"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Database capacity model","Concurrency requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B023","specializes":"Connection-pooling technology","contrasts_with":"Per-operation connection creation","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Database resource management"],"provenance":["Selected database-driver and database official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B043","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Data Access Technology","subtopic":"ORM and query abstraction","concept":"ORM or query-builder technology is useful when applications require structured mapping between domain/application objects and relational data, while direct SQL remains appropriate for database-specific or highly optimized operations.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should an ORM or query builder be selected instead of direct SQL?","answer":"An ORM is a candidate when object-relational mapping, unit-of-work behavior, relationship management, and developer productivity are material requirements. A query builder or direct SQL may be preferable when explicit SQL control, complex database-specific operations, or predictable generated queries are more important.","explanation":"ORMs abstract repetitive persistence mechanics but do not eliminate the need to understand relational execution. Poorly controlled ORM usage can create N+1 queries, excessive joins, or inefficient loading. The technology decision should be based on domain complexity, team expertise, query requirements, and database-specific needs.","principle":"Use persistence abstraction where it reduces complexity without hiding behavior that must remain explicitly controlled.","objective":"Select an appropriate data-access abstraction level for the workload and domain.","requirements":["Data mapping","Transaction handling","Query expressiveness","Migration integration","Performance visibility"],"constraints":["Generated SQL must remain inspectable","Complex queries may require lower-level access","Lazy loading can create hidden I/O","ORM behavior varies by version"],"preconditions":["Relational data model exists","Query workload is characterized"],"input":{"domain":"customer_orders","requirements":["mapping","transactions","complex_queries"]},"output":{"technology_class":"ORM/query builder/direct SQL","selection_basis":["abstraction","control","query complexity","team capability"]},"expected_behavior":"Application code uses an explicit data-access boundary while preserving control over important transaction and query behavior.","incorrect_code":"orders = customer.orders\nfor order in orders:\n    print(order.items)\n# Depending on ORM configuration this can cause N+1 queries.","corrected_code":"orders = query_orders_with_items(customer_id)\nfor order in orders:\n    print(order.items)\n# Exact eager-loading/query APIs depend on the selected ORM.","failure_mode":"Abstraction hides repeated database operations or inefficient query generation.","expected_error":"N+1 queries, excessive database round trips, unexpected joins, or transaction-scope errors.","cause":"ORM behavior was assumed rather than inspected and tested.","root_cause":"Persistence abstraction was treated as a substitute for database knowledge.","solution":"Select ORM/query-builder/direct-SQL boundaries explicitly and test generated queries for important workloads.","edge_case":"A hybrid strategy can use an ORM for ordinary CRUD and direct SQL for specialized analytical or performance-sensitive queries.","security_implication":"Parameterized queries and ORM-safe query APIs should be used; dynamic SQL identifiers and raw fragments require explicit validation.","performance_implication":"Measure generated SQL, round trips, execution plans, result sizes, and transaction duration.","test_case":"Execute a representative relationship query and inspect the generated SQL and number of database round trips.","test_input":{"query":"customer_with_orders_and_items"},"expected_test_result":"The query meets the declared correctness and round-trip requirements without hidden N+1 behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test transactions, lazy/eager loading, pagination, concurrency, generated SQL, injection resistance, and migration compatibility.","verification_method":"ORM/query-builder documentation plus SQL inspection and integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact ORM version, generated SQL behavior, transaction semantics, and supported database dialect.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"SQLAlchemy","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact SQLAlchemy/database versions must be declared.","dependencies":["SQLAlchemy","Database driver","Relational database"],"execution_requirements":["Representative relational database"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Relational data-access practice plus selected ORM documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B003","D33-B009","D34-B041","D34-B042"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Relational data model","Query workload characterization"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B009","specializes":"ORM/query-builder technology","contrasts_with":"Direct SQL","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Relational persistence engineering"],"provenance":["SQLAlchemy official documentation; relational database documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B044","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Serialization Technology","subtopic":"Schema-aware data serialization","concept":"Serialization technology should be selected according to interoperability, schema evolution, performance, human readability, size, and compatibility requirements rather than treating all wire formats as interchangeable.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"How should JSON, Protocol Buffers, and other serialization technologies be selected?","answer":"JSON is often useful when human readability and broad interoperability are priorities; schema-aware binary formats such as Protocol Buffers can be appropriate when compactness, typed contracts, and controlled evolution are important. The correct choice depends on protocol, consumers, compatibility, and operational requirements.","explanation":"Serialization is a technology boundary between producers and consumers. Format selection affects payload size, parsing behavior, schema evolution, language interoperability, debugging, and compatibility. A format should not be selected solely because it is popular.","principle":"Choose serialization technology according to contract, compatibility, and transport requirements.","objective":"Ensure serialized data can evolve without silently breaking consumers.","requirements":["Schema definition","Compatibility policy","Encoding/decoding","Language interoperability","Error handling"],"constraints":["Different formats have different evolution rules","Schema defaults and optionality require careful handling","Binary formats can be less human-readable","JSON does not automatically provide schema compatibility"],"preconditions":["Producer/consumer contract is defined","Compatibility direction is known"],"input":{"message":{"id":"123","status":"created"},"compatibility":"backward"},"output":{"serialized_message":"format-specific bytes","schema":"versioned contract"},"expected_behavior":"Consumers decode supported message versions according to explicit compatibility rules.","incorrect_code":"payload = json.dumps(message)\n# No schema/version/compatibility policy is defined.","corrected_code":"message = OrderCreated(order_id="123", status="created")\npayload = protobuf_encode(message)\n# Exact generated APIs depend on the selected schema compiler/runtime.","failure_mode":"A producer changes a field in a way that breaks older consumers.","expected_error":"Decode failures, missing required data, semantic incompatibility, or silent misinterpretation.","cause":"Serialization format was selected without an explicit schema evolution policy.","root_cause":"Wire compatibility was treated as automatic.","solution":"Define schema ownership, compatibility direction, field evolution rules, versioning, and consumer support before selecting the format.","edge_case":"A small internal API may gain little from binary serialization if operational simplicity and human debugging dominate the requirements.","security_implication":"Serialized input must be treated as untrusted; parsers should be kept current and resource limits applied where necessary.","performance_implication":"Measure encoded size, serialization/deserialization latency, allocation behavior, and throughput using representative payloads.","test_case":"Add an optional field in a producer and decode the message using the previous consumer schema.","test_input":{"producer_schema":"new_optional_field","consumer_schema":"previous_version"},"expected_test_result":"The compatibility behavior matches the declared schema-evolution policy.","testing_guidance":"Test unknown fields, removed fields, defaults, enum evolution, malformed payloads, large payloads, and cross-language decoding.","verification_method":"Selected serialization specification/compiler documentation plus compatibility tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify compiler/runtime versions and exact compatibility rules for the chosen format.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Protocol Buffers","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact protobuf compiler/runtime versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Serialization compiler/runtime"],"execution_requirements":["Generated schema artifacts","Cross-version test fixtures"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Serialization standards/specifications plus official implementation documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B025","D33-B032","D34-B033"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Compatibility policy","Consumer inventory"],"extends":"D33-B025","refines":"D33-B032","specializes":"Serialization technology","contrasts_with":"Unversioned ad hoc serialization","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Data interchange and schema evolution practice"],"provenance":["Protocol Buffers official documentation; JSON specification; selected serialization standards"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B045","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Schema Registry Technology","subtopic":"Event and message schema governance","concept":"A schema registry is useful when many producers and consumers exchange schema-governed messages and compatibility must be checked centrally before deployment.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When is a schema registry justified?","answer":"A schema registry is a candidate when an event or messaging ecosystem contains multiple independently deployed producers and consumers whose schema compatibility must be tracked and validated centrally.","explanation":"A schema registry stores and serves schemas and can enforce compatibility policies for supported serialization ecosystems. It does not replace application semantics or consumer testing. The technology becomes useful when schema coordination is otherwise becoming a distributed manual process.","principle":"Centralize machine-checkable schema compatibility when independent message producers and consumers make informal coordination unreliable.","objective":"Prevent incompatible schema evolution across independently deployed message participants.","requirements":["Schema storage","Versioning","Compatibility checks","Producer/consumer discovery","Governance"],"constraints":["Registry availability can affect schema publication or startup depending on integration design","Compatibility rules do not guarantee semantic compatibility","Schemas require ownership and lifecycle management"],"preconditions":["Schema-based messaging exists","Multiple independent consumers/producers exist"],"input":{"subject":"orders.created","schemas":["v1","v2"],"compatibility":"backward"},"output":{"artifact":"registered schema","decision":"compatibility result"},"expected_behavior":"A producer schema change is accepted or rejected according to the configured compatibility policy before incompatible deployment proceeds.","incorrect_code":"producer_schema = new_schema\n# Deploying without compatibility validation can break existing consumers.","corrected_code":"compatibility = registry.check_compatibility(subject, new_schema)\nif not compatibility.allowed:\n    raise ValueError("schema compatibility failure")","failure_mode":"Producer deploys an incompatible schema while older consumers remain active.","expected_error":"Consumer decoding failure or semantic data corruption.","cause":"Schema compatibility was not centrally checked.","root_cause":"Independent message evolution lacked machine-enforced contract governance.","solution":"Introduce schema registry technology when the number and independence of message participants justify centralized schema governance.","edge_case":"A small monolith with a single deployment boundary may not need a registry because schemas can evolve atomically with the application.","security_implication":"Schema registries can expose internal data contracts and require authentication, authorization, and controlled schema publication.","performance_implication":"Measure schema lookup latency, cache behavior, registry availability impact, and publication throughput.","test_case":"Register a schema that removes a field required by the declared compatibility policy and verify rejection.","test_input":{"change":"remove_required_field","compatibility":"backward"},"expected_test_result":"The registry rejects the incompatible schema according to the configured compatibility policy.","testing_guidance":"Test compatible additions, incompatible removals, subject naming, concurrent registration, registry outage, and schema lookup caching.","verification_method":"Registry and serialization documentation plus compatibility integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact registry implementation/version and compatibility-rule semantics.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Confluent Schema Registry","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact registry/client/serialization versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Schema registry","Serialization format"],"execution_requirements":["Registry test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Schema-registry practice plus selected registry primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B025","D34-B033","D34-B044"],"depends_on":["D34-B044"],"requires":["Schema evolution policy","Message ecosystem"],"extends":"D34-B044","refines":"D33-B025","specializes":"Schema registry technology","contrasts_with":"Manual schema coordination","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Schema governance architecture"],"provenance":["Confluent Schema Registry official documentation; serialization specifications"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B046","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Change Data Capture Technology","subtopic":"Database change propagation","concept":"Change data capture technology captures inserts, updates, and deletes from an authoritative data source and propagates them to downstream consumers without requiring application code to publish every change explicitly.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should change data capture be selected for data integration?","answer":"CDC is a candidate when downstream systems need reliable propagation of database changes and modifying every application write path to publish equivalent events is impractical or undesirable.","explanation":"CDC can read database transaction logs or equivalent change streams and produce ordered change records. Debezium and database-native CDC facilities are examples. CDC does not automatically provide business-level event semantics; captured row changes and business events are distinct concepts.","principle":"Use CDC when reliable database-state propagation is required and source-level change capture is more appropriate than application-level event publication.","objective":"Propagate authoritative data changes while preserving explicit source and ordering semantics.","requirements":["Change capture","Ordering semantics","Initial snapshot strategy","Offset management","Schema evolution","Failure recovery"],"constraints":["CDC reflects database changes, not necessarily business intent","Connector offsets require durable management","Schema changes affect downstream consumers","Source database log retention can constrain recovery"],"preconditions":["Database supports suitable change capture mechanism","Downstream synchronization requirement exists"],"input":{"source":"relational_database","changes":["insert","update","delete"],"destination":"downstream_system"},"output":{"stream":"database_change_records"},"expected_behavior":"Committed source changes are emitted according to the selected CDC technology's documented ordering and delivery semantics.","incorrect_code":"def after_write(row):\n    database.write(row)\n    downstream.publish(row)\n# Application-level dual writes can fail between operations.","corrected_code":"database.write(row)\n# CDC captures the committed database change and publishes it downstream.\n# Exact connector configuration is technology-specific.","failure_mode":"Database commit succeeds but downstream publication fails or duplicates occur.","expected_error":"Missing changes, duplicate changes, inconsistent downstream state, or connector lag.","cause":"Change propagation lacked a durable source-derived mechanism.","root_cause":"Dual-write consistency was assumed rather than explicitly managed.","solution":"Evaluate CDC when downstream systems require reliable propagation of authoritative database changes.","edge_case":"A business workflow may require semantic domain events that cannot be reconstructed safely from row-level changes; CDC should not be substituted for those events automatically.","security_implication":"CDC streams can contain complete database records and sensitive fields; access, filtering, encryption, and retention require explicit controls.","performance_implication":"Measure source-log impact, capture lag, throughput, snapshot duration, connector resource use, and downstream processing latency.","test_case":"Commit a transaction containing multiple row changes and verify downstream change ordering according to the selected CDC contract.","test_input":{"transaction":["insert_order","update_customer"],"capture":"enabled"},"expected_test_result":"Downstream receives the captured changes according to the connector's documented transaction and ordering semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test initial snapshots, restarts, offsets, schema changes, deletes, transaction boundaries, connector failure, and source-log truncation scenarios.","verification_method":"Selected CDC technology and database documentation plus controlled integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact connector/database versions and documented delivery/order guarantees.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Debezium","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact Debezium/database versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Source database","CDC connector","Downstream transport"],"execution_requirements":["Representative database and CDC environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CDC engineering practice plus Debezium/database primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B032","D34-B033","D34-B044"],"depends_on":["D34-B033"],"requires":["Database change source","Downstream synchronization requirement"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B009","specializes":"Change data capture technology","contrasts_with":"Application dual-write publication","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Database change propagation architecture"],"provenance":["Debezium official documentation; selected database CDC documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B047","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Data Pipeline Technology","subtopic":"ETL and ELT orchestration","concept":"Data pipeline orchestration technology coordinates extraction, transformation, loading, dependencies, retries, schedules, lineage hooks, and operational state for repeatable data workflows.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated data pipeline orchestrator be introduced?","answer":"A data pipeline orchestrator is a candidate when data workflows contain multiple dependent stages, schedules, retries, backfills, operational state, or cross-system dependencies that are becoming difficult to manage as scripts or cron jobs.","explanation":"Tools such as Apache Airflow and Dagster provide workflow-oriented orchestration for data workloads. The choice should follow data freshness, dependency, backfill, observability, and operational requirements rather than tool popularity.","principle":"Use data orchestration technology when data workflow state and dependency management become first-class operational requirements.","objective":"Make recurring and dependency-rich data processing reproducible and observable.","requirements":["Scheduling","Dependencies","Retries","Backfills","Failure visibility","Run history"],"constraints":["Orchestrators introduce control-plane complexity","Task granularity affects operational overhead","Data correctness remains the responsibility of pipeline logic","Retries can duplicate non-idempotent operations"],"preconditions":["Data workflows are identified","Freshness and recovery requirements are defined"],"input":{"pipeline":["extract","validate","transform","load"],"schedule":"daily"},"output":{"artifact":"orchestrated_data_workflow","state":"observable_run_history"},"expected_behavior":"Pipeline stages execute according to dependency and retry policies with durable run state and visible failures.","incorrect_code":"cron_job = "0 2 * * * python pipeline.py"\n# A single cron command provides little durable workflow state or dependency visibility.","corrected_code":"@dag(schedule="0 2 * * *")\ndef pipeline():\n    extract >> validate >> transform >> load\n# Exact syntax depends on the selected orchestrator/version.","failure_mode":"A failed intermediate stage causes either silent downstream corruption or uncontrolled repeated processing.","expected_error":"Stale datasets, duplicate loads, missed schedules, or unrecoverable backfills.","cause":"Pipeline dependencies and recovery semantics were not explicitly modeled.","root_cause":"Data workflow state was delegated to shell scheduling alone.","solution":"Use an orchestrator when scheduling, dependencies, retries, backfills, and operational state justify the additional technology.","edge_case":"A small single-step local pipeline may be better served by a simple scheduler and versioned script.","security_implication":"Pipeline workers may access databases, object stores, and credentials; task-level permissions and secret handling are required.","performance_implication":"Measure scheduling overhead, task startup time, data throughput, worker utilization, backfill duration, and queue latency.","test_case":"Fail an intermediate pipeline task and verify retry and downstream dependency behavior.","test_input":{"failed_task":"validate","retry_policy":"configured"},"expected_test_result":"Only permitted retries occur and dependent tasks do not execute until their prerequisites satisfy the declared conditions.","testing_guidance":"Test retries, partial completion, backfills, concurrent runs, dependency failures, idempotency, and scheduler recovery.","verification_method":"Selected orchestrator documentation plus controlled pipeline tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact scheduler/task semantics, retry behavior, state persistence, and version compatibility.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Apache Airflow","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact Airflow/provider versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Data sources","Data destinations","Orchestrator"],"execution_requirements":["Test orchestrator environment","Representative pipeline"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Data-engineering practice plus selected orchestrator primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B034","D33-B057","D34-B037"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Pipeline dependency model","Freshness requirements"],"extends":"D33-B034","refines":"D33-B057","specializes":"Data pipeline orchestration technology","contrasts_with":"Cron-only scheduling","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Data workflow orchestration"],"provenance":["Apache Airflow official documentation; selected data-orchestration documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B048","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Data Quality Technology","subtopic":"Automated data validation","concept":"Data-quality technology validates structural, statistical, relational, and business-rule expectations so downstream systems can distinguish valid data from anomalous or incomplete data.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should dedicated data-quality validation technology be introduced?","answer":"Dedicated data-quality technology is useful when pipelines or analytical systems depend on explicit expectations for completeness, uniqueness, validity, freshness, referential integrity, or domain-specific correctness.","explanation":"Validation can be implemented directly in application code or pipeline code. Dedicated frameworks become valuable when checks need reusable definitions, reporting, historical results, failure gates, and centralized visibility. Great Expectations and similar tools are examples.","principle":"Make important data assumptions executable and testable rather than leaving them as undocumented expectations.","objective":"Detect invalid or degraded data before it causes downstream decisions or processing errors.","requirements":["Validation rules","Failure policy","Result storage","Data profiling where required","Integration with pipelines"],"constraints":["Validation cannot prove arbitrary semantic correctness","Checks can become stale as schemas evolve","Large datasets may require sampled or incremental validation","False positives can disrupt pipelines"],"preconditions":["Critical data expectations are defined","Data ownership is established"],"input":{"dataset":"orders","checks":["non_null_order_id","unique_order_id","valid_status"]},"output":{"validation_result":"pass/fail plus evidence"},"expected_behavior":"Declared data-quality expectations execute at appropriate pipeline boundaries and produce auditable validation results.","incorrect_code":"assert orders\n# Truthiness does not establish data quality.","corrected_code":"validate_non_null(orders, "order_id")\nvalidate_unique(orders, "order_id")\nvalidate_allowed_values(orders, "status", ["created", "paid", "cancelled"])","failure_mode":"Invalid data passes silently into downstream systems.","expected_error":"Duplicate entities, missing identifiers, invalid states, stale data, or corrupted analytical outputs.","cause":"Data assumptions were not represented as executable checks.","root_cause":"Data quality was treated as an implicit property rather than a verifiable requirement.","solution":"Define critical expectations and execute them at ingestion, transformation, and output boundaries as appropriate.","edge_case":"A valid dataset may contain legitimate nulls or duplicates; rules must represent domain semantics rather than generic cleanliness assumptions.","security_implication":"Validation systems must avoid exposing sensitive records through error reports and diagnostic samples.","performance_implication":"Measure validation cost, scan volume, incremental-check efficiency, and pipeline impact.","test_case":"Inject duplicate and invalid records into a test dataset and verify that the corresponding expectations fail.","test_input":{"records":[{"order_id":"1","status":"paid"},{"order_id":"1","status":"invalid"}]},"expected_test_result":"Uniqueness and allowed-value checks fail with evidence identifying the affected expectation.","testing_guidance":"Test nulls, duplicates, schema drift, freshness, referential integrity, boundary values, large datasets, and false-positive cases.","verification_method":"Selected data-quality framework documentation plus representative validation tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact expectation semantics and integration behavior for the selected framework/version.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Great Expectations","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact framework version must be declared.","dependencies":["Data source","Validation framework"],"execution_requirements":["Representative test dataset"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Data-quality engineering practice plus selected framework documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B060","D34-B047","D34-B060"],"depends_on":["D34-B047"],"requires":["Data expectations","Data ownership"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B060","specializes":"Data-quality technology","contrasts_with":"Undocumented data assumptions","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Data validation engineering"],"provenance":["Great Expectations official documentation; data-quality engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B049","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Data Catalog and Lineage Technology","subtopic":"Metadata discovery and provenance","concept":"Data catalog and lineage technology records what data assets exist, what they mean, where they originate, how they transform, and which downstream assets depend on them.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When is a data catalog or lineage platform justified?","answer":"A catalog or lineage platform becomes useful when organizations have many datasets, producers, transformations, consumers, or governance requirements and manual knowledge of data ownership and provenance is insufficient.","explanation":"Catalogs improve discovery and ownership metadata, while lineage systems connect upstream sources to downstream datasets and transformations. These technologies are complementary to data storage and orchestration rather than replacements for either.","principle":"Use metadata technology when data discovery, ownership, and transformation provenance become too complex to maintain manually.","objective":"Make data assets and their dependencies discoverable and traceable.","requirements":["Asset inventory","Ownership metadata","Definitions","Lineage","Search","Lifecycle state"],"constraints":["Automatically inferred lineage can be incomplete","Metadata itself requires governance","Lineage may differ between logical and physical transformations","Sensitive metadata requires access controls"],"preconditions":["Data assets and pipelines exist","Ownership model is defined"],"input":{"assets":["orders","customers","revenue_report"],"relations":["derived_from","owned_by"]},"output":{"metadata_graph":"assets + ownership + lineage"},"expected_behavior":"Users can discover governed data assets and trace supported transformations from downstream outputs to declared upstream sources.","incorrect_code":"datasets = ["orders", "customers", "revenue_report"]\n# A list does not capture ownership, definitions, or transformation lineage.","corrected_code":"catalog.register_asset(\n    name="revenue_report",\n    owner="finance-data",\n    source_assets=["orders", "customers"],\n    classification="internal",\n)\n# Exact catalog APIs depend on the selected platform.","failure_mode":"Consumers cannot determine dataset meaning, ownership, freshness, or provenance.","expected_error":"Duplicate pipelines, incorrect source selection, unverifiable reports, or uncontrolled sensitive-data propagation.","cause":"Metadata was distributed across undocumented systems and individual knowledge.","root_cause":"Data discovery and lineage were not treated as explicit metadata requirements.","solution":"Introduce catalog/lineage technology when data estate complexity makes manual metadata management unreliable.","edge_case":"A small application with a few tables may gain little from a dedicated catalog; source-controlled schema documentation can be sufficient.","security_implication":"Catalog metadata can reveal sensitive systems and relationships even when underlying data is inaccessible; catalog access requires authorization.","performance_implication":"Measure metadata ingestion latency, lineage processing time, search latency, and storage growth.","test_case":"Trace a report dataset through its registered transformation chain and verify that each expected upstream dependency is represented.","test_input":{"target":"revenue_report","expected_sources":["orders","customers"]},"expected_test_result":"The catalog returns the declared lineage and ownership metadata without inventing unsupported relationships.","testing_guidance":"Test metadata freshness, missing lineage, renamed assets, deleted datasets, ownership changes, access controls, and automated lineage limitations.","verification_method":"Catalog/lineage documentation plus metadata integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify which lineage relationships are observed, inferred, or manually declared.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"OpenLineage","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact OpenLineage/integration versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Metadata catalog","Pipeline integrations"],"execution_requirements":["Representative data estate or test metadata graph"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Data-governance practice plus OpenLineage/catalog primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B009","D33-B060","D34-B047","D34-B048"],"depends_on":["D34-B047"],"requires":["Data ownership","Metadata model"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B009","specializes":"Data catalog and lineage technology","contrasts_with":"Undocumented data estate","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Data lineage and metadata governance"],"provenance":["OpenLineage official documentation; selected data-catalog documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B050","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Vector Retrieval Technology","subtopic":"Embedding index and semantic retrieval","concept":"Vector indexing technology is appropriate when retrieval depends on semantic similarity between high-dimensional embeddings rather than exact lexical matching, provided embedding quality, metadata filtering, freshness, and evaluation requirements are explicit.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a vector database or vector index be selected for retrieval?","answer":"Vector retrieval technology is a candidate when the application must retrieve semantically similar content from embeddings and lexical or relational search cannot adequately represent the retrieval requirement. It should not be introduced merely because an application uses AI.","explanation":"Vector indexes support approximate or exact nearest-neighbor search over embeddings. They can exist inside a relational database, a dedicated vector database, or a specialized search system. The technology decision must consider embedding model/version, distance metric, filtering, update frequency, index build behavior, and retrieval evaluation.","principle":"Choose vector retrieval technology only when semantic similarity is a demonstrated retrieval requirement.","objective":"Match vector-index technology to semantic retrieval workload and evaluation requirements.","requirements":["Embedding generation","Vector storage","Similarity metric","Metadata filtering","Index updates","Retrieval evaluation"],"constraints":["Embedding quality determines retrieval quality","Changing embedding models may require re-indexing","Approximate indexes trade exactness for efficiency depending on implementation","Vector similarity does not guarantee factual relevance"],"preconditions":["Semantic retrieval requirement exists","Embedding model and dimensionality are defined or explicitly under evaluation"],"input":{"documents":["architecture decision","implementation guide"],"query":"how to recover a failed distributed workflow","embedding_model":"versioned_model"},"output":{"retrieval":"ranked_semantically_similar_documents"},"expected_behavior":"The vector index returns semantically relevant candidates subject to declared metadata filters and retrieval evaluation criteria.","incorrect_code":"results = vector_db.search(query_text)\n# A text query cannot be assumed to be a valid vector query without an embedding step.","corrected_code":"query_vector = embedding_model.embed(query_text)\nresults = vector_index.search(\n    vector=query_vector,\n    top_k=10,\n    filters={"dataset_id": "D34"},\n)","failure_mode":"Retrieval quality degrades after embedding-model changes or metadata filters are ignored.","expected_error":"Irrelevant retrieval, missing relevant records, cross-tenant retrieval, or stale vectors.","cause":"Embedding/index configuration was not treated as a versioned retrieval dependency.","root_cause":"Vector search was adopted without modeling the complete retrieval pipeline.","solution":"Version embeddings, index configuration, metadata filters, evaluation sets, and rebuild procedures; select dedicated vector infrastructure only when required.","edge_case":"For modest datasets, an embedded/local vector index may satisfy requirements without a separate vector database.","security_implication":"Vector indexes can encode sensitive information and must enforce tenant/document authorization at retrieval time; embeddings are not automatically anonymized.","performance_implication":"Measure retrieval latency, index build/update time, memory footprint, recall@k or equivalent retrieval metrics, and filter performance using a fixed evaluation set.","test_case":"Run a fixed semantic-retrieval evaluation set against the selected index and compare retrieved records with human- or rule-defined relevance labels.","test_input":{"queries":"fixed_evaluation_set","top_k":10},"expected_test_result":"Measured retrieval metrics are recorded without claiming an unexecuted benchmark; authorization filters are also verified.","testing_guidance":"Test embedding-version migration, stale records, metadata filtering, duplicate content, empty results, index rebuilds, tenant isolation, and retrieval metrics.","verification_method":"Vector-index documentation plus controlled retrieval evaluation.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact distance metric, index algorithm, dimensionality, filtering semantics, persistence behavior, and embedding-model version.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"FAISS","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact FAISS/vector-index and embedding-model versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Embedding model","Vector index","Metadata store"],"execution_requirements":["Representative corpus","Fixed retrieval evaluation set"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Vector-retrieval engineering practice plus selected vector-index primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B005","D33-B022","D34-B031","D34-B049"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Semantic retrieval requirement","Embedding specification","Evaluation set"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B005","specializes":"Vector retrieval technology","contrasts_with":"Lexical full-text retrieval","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Vector information retrieval"],"provenance":["FAISS official documentation; embedding-model documentation; information-retrieval literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B051","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Authorization Policy Technology","subtopic":"Externalized policy evaluation","concept":"A dedicated policy engine is appropriate when authorization rules become numerous, centrally governed, independently changeable, auditable, or shared across multiple applications.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should a policy engine such as OPA or Cedar be selected instead of embedded authorization conditionals?","answer":"A policy engine is a candidate when authorization logic requires centralized governance, independent policy updates, consistent evaluation across services, or detailed policy testing and audit. Simple application-local authorization can remain embedded when the policy surface is small and tightly coupled to domain behavior.","explanation":"External policy technology separates policy evaluation from application implementation, but it does not automatically understand business semantics. OPA/Rego and Cedar provide different policy models and deployment approaches. The selection must consider policy language, enforcement point, data needed for decisions, latency, availability, and change control.","principle":"Externalize authorization policy when policy complexity and governance requirements justify a separate policy technology.","objective":"Provide consistent, testable, and governed authorization decisions without turning every business rule into external policy.","requirements":["Policy evaluation","Policy versioning","Testing","Auditability","Decision context","Low-latency enforcement"],"constraints":["Policy engines become dependencies if remotely evaluated","Policy/data synchronization can create stale decisions","Business authorization context must be supplied correctly","Policy technology does not replace authentication"],"preconditions":["Authorization model exists","Protected resources and actions are defined"],"input":{"subject":"user","action":"read","resource":"document","context":{"tenant":"tenant_a"}},"output":{"decision":"allow_or_deny","reason":"policy_evaluation_result"},"expected_behavior":"Authorization requests are evaluated against the declared policy and trusted contextual data.","incorrect_code":"if user.role == "admin":\n    return True\n# Scattered role checks become difficult to govern as policy complexity grows.","corrected_code":"decision = policy_engine.evaluate({\n    "subject": user,\n    "action": "read",\n    "resource": document,\n    "context": context,\n})\nreturn decision.allowed","failure_mode":"Different services interpret the same authorization requirement differently.","expected_error":"Authorization inconsistency, privilege escalation, or policy drift.","cause":"Authorization logic was duplicated across application components.","root_cause":"Policy governance requirements were not translated into a shared policy mechanism.","solution":"Evaluate a policy engine when policy complexity, reuse, independent lifecycle, or audit requirements justify externalization.","edge_case":"Highly domain-specific authorization may require application logic to calculate contextual facts before policy evaluation.","security_implication":"Policy engines are security-critical enforcement components; fail-open behavior must not be assumed, policy inputs must be trusted, and policy changes require controlled review.","performance_implication":"Measure policy evaluation latency, cache behavior, context-size overhead, decision throughput, and behavior during policy-service degradation.","test_case":"Evaluate allow and deny cases across tenants and actions using a fixed policy test suite.","test_input":{"subject":"user_a","action":"read","resource":"tenant_b_document"},"expected_test_result":"The decision denies cross-tenant access according to the declared authorization policy.","testing_guidance":"Test deny-by-default, tenant boundaries, role changes, policy versioning, missing context, malformed requests, and policy-engine outage behavior.","verification_method":"Selected policy engine documentation plus authorization test suite.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact policy-language semantics, evaluation API, fail behavior, policy distribution, and versioning model.","language":"Rego","language_version":"","framework":"Open Policy Agent","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact OPA/Cedar version must be declared.","dependencies":["Policy engine","Policy data source"],"execution_requirements":["Policy-engine test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Authorization engineering practice plus OPA/Cedar primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B036","D33-B051","D33-B060"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Authorization model","Policy ownership"],"extends":"D33-B036","refines":"D33-B051","specializes":"Authorization policy technology","contrasts_with":"Scattered embedded authorization checks","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Policy-as-code architecture"],"provenance":["Open Policy Agent official documentation; AWS Cedar documentation; authorization engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B052","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"API Management Technology","subtopic":"API lifecycle and consumer governance","concept":"API management technology is useful when an organization must centrally manage API publication, consumer registration, quotas, lifecycle policies, analytics, documentation, and gateway-level controls across many APIs.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should API management technology be introduced beyond a basic API gateway?","answer":"API management is a candidate when API consumers, products, versions, quotas, subscriptions, developer onboarding, analytics, and lifecycle governance become organizational requirements. A basic gateway can remain sufficient for simpler internal systems.","explanation":"API gateways primarily handle traffic and edge concerns, while API management adds consumer and product lifecycle capabilities. The two can be combined but should not be treated as the same requirement.","principle":"Introduce API management when API consumption itself becomes a governed product lifecycle rather than merely an internal routing concern.","objective":"Select API management technology based on consumer and lifecycle governance requirements.","requirements":["API publication","Consumer management","Quota/rate policies","Version lifecycle","Documentation","Usage analytics"],"constraints":["Management platforms add operational complexity","Gateway authorization does not replace domain authorization","API products may create unnecessary abstraction if the API estate is small"],"preconditions":["External or numerous API consumers exist","API lifecycle requirements are defined"],"input":{"apis":["orders","customers"],"consumers":["internal","partner"],"requirements":["quotas","versioning","analytics"]},"output":{"technology_class":"API management platform","selection_basis":["consumer governance","lifecycle","traffic control","analytics"]},"expected_behavior":"API consumers are managed according to explicit subscription, quota, version, and lifecycle policies.","incorrect_code":"route_request_without_consumer_policy(request)\n# Routing alone does not provide API product governance.","corrected_code":"consumer = api_manager.authenticate_consumer(request)\napi_manager.check_quota(consumer, request.api)\nreturn gateway.forward(request)","failure_mode":"API consumers bypass lifecycle, quota, or compatibility controls.","expected_error":"Uncontrolled consumption, breaking changes, undocumented consumers, or quota violations.","cause":"API traffic management was implemented without consumer lifecycle governance.","root_cause":"Gateway functionality was assumed to cover API product-management requirements.","solution":"Use API management technology only when consumer governance and lifecycle requirements justify it.","edge_case":"A private service-to-service API estate with a small known consumer set may not need a full API management platform.","security_implication":"Consumer credentials, API keys, tokens, quotas, and administrative controls require strict protection and auditing.","performance_implication":"Measure gateway/management overhead, quota-check latency, analytics processing, and throughput impact.","test_case":"Register a consumer with a quota and exceed that quota using controlled requests.","test_input":{"consumer":"partner_a","quota":"100_requests_per_minute","requests":101},"expected_test_result":"The API management layer enforces the declared quota behavior without bypassing application authorization.","testing_guidance":"Test consumer onboarding, quota enforcement, API versioning, credential revocation, documentation synchronization, and gateway failure.","verification_method":"API-management platform documentation plus controlled integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact gateway/API-management version and quota/version-lifecycle semantics.","language":"YAML","language_version":"","framework":"API Management","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact platform/version must be declared.","dependencies":["API gateway","Identity/consumer system"],"execution_requirements":["API-management test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"API-management engineering practice plus selected platform documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B031","D33-B059","D34-B024"],"depends_on":["D34-B024"],"requires":["API consumer model","API lifecycle policy"],"extends":"D34-B024","refines":"D33-B031","specializes":"API management technology","contrasts_with":"Basic gateway routing","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["API product lifecycle architecture"],"provenance":["Selected API-management platform official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B053","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Certificate Management Technology","subtopic":"TLS certificate lifecycle","concept":"Certificate-management technology automates certificate issuance, renewal, distribution, and expiration monitoring when manual certificate lifecycle management is error-prone or operationally distributed.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should automated certificate-management technology be selected?","answer":"Automated certificate management is appropriate when services use TLS certificates at sufficient scale or change frequency that manual issuance and renewal create operational risk. ACME-compatible automation and platform-native certificate systems are common technology options.","explanation":"Certificates have finite lifetimes and require secure private-key handling. Automation can reduce expiration-related outages but must itself be governed and monitored. Certificate automation does not eliminate the need to validate certificate identity, trust chains, key protection, and renewal behavior.","principle":"Automate certificate lifecycle when certificate count, expiration risk, or deployment frequency makes manual management unreliable.","objective":"Maintain valid, correctly scoped certificates throughout their required lifecycle.","requirements":["Certificate issuance","Renewal","Private-key protection","Deployment","Expiration monitoring"],"constraints":["Certificate automation requires a trusted issuance mechanism","Renewal failures must be detected before expiry","Private keys require protected storage","Wildcard and multi-domain certificates have broader exposure implications"],"preconditions":["Certificate authority/trust model is defined","Service identity requirements are known"],"input":{"domain":"api.example.internal","certificate_requirement":"TLS","renewal":"automatic"},"output":{"artifact":"managed_certificate","state":"valid_and_deployed"},"expected_behavior":"Certificates are issued and renewed before expiration and deployed to the intended service endpoints.","incorrect_code":"certificate = manually_copy_certificate_to_server()\n# Manual distribution does not establish renewal or deployment assurance.","corrected_code":"certificate_manager.issue(\n    identifier="api.example.internal",\n    challenge="configured_acme_or_internal_ca",\n)\ncertificate_manager.renew_before_expiry()","failure_mode":"A certificate expires or is deployed to the wrong service.","expected_error":"TLS handshake failures, trust errors, hostname mismatch, or service outage.","cause":"Certificate lifecycle was managed manually or renewal failures were not observed.","root_cause":"Certificate expiration was not treated as an automated lifecycle requirement.","solution":"Select automated certificate-management technology and verify issuance, renewal, deployment, and alerting behavior.","edge_case":"Private internal PKI may use different issuance and trust mechanisms than public ACME certificates.","security_implication":"Private keys must never be exposed through logs, source control, or uncontrolled deployment artifacts; issuance authorization must be tightly scoped.","performance_implication":"Certificate operations generally have low runtime cost, but large-scale renewal events can create control-plane load and deployment churn.","test_case":"Use a non-production certificate with a controlled short lifetime and verify successful renewal before expiry.","test_input":{"certificate":"test","renewal_window":"configured"},"expected_test_result":"Renewal occurs according to the configured policy and the renewed certificate is correctly deployed.","testing_guidance":"Test renewal failure, CA outage, key rotation, hostname mismatch, expired certificates, trust-chain validation, and deployment rollback.","verification_method":"PKI/ACME documentation plus certificate lifecycle tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact CA/client behavior, challenge mechanism, renewal timing, and key-storage semantics.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"ACME","framework_version":"Protocol/client implementation must be pinned","version_range":"Exact ACME client/CA implementation versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Certificate authority","Certificate-management client"],"execution_requirements":["Non-production CA/test certificate environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PKI/ACME standards and certificate-management documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B051","D33-B059","D34-B039"],"depends_on":["D34-B039"],"requires":["Certificate trust model","Service identity requirements"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B051","specializes":"Certificate-management technology","contrasts_with":"Manual certificate renewal","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["PKI lifecycle management"],"provenance":["IETF ACME specifications; selected certificate-authority documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B054","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"CI Technology","subtopic":"Continuous integration execution","concept":"Continuous integration technology automatically builds and validates software changes in controlled environments before they are merged or released.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"What requirements justify dedicated continuous-integration technology?","answer":"CI technology is justified when teams need repeatable automated builds, tests, static checks, dependency validation, artifact generation, and merge/release gates across a shared codebase.","explanation":"CI systems provide controlled execution environments and workflow triggers. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins, and other systems differ in execution model, security, portability, caching, and governance. CI should produce evidence about the commit it tested rather than merely report that a job ran.","principle":"Make required engineering checks reproducible and automatically associated with a specific source revision.","objective":"Detect regressions before integration and create traceable verification evidence.","requirements":["Build","Tests","Static analysis","Artifact generation","Status reporting","Source revision association"],"constraints":["CI environments differ from production","Secrets in CI are high-risk","Untrusted code must not receive privileged credentials","Passing CI does not prove production correctness"],"preconditions":["Repository and build process are defined","Required quality gates are known"],"input":{"commit":"source_revision","checks":["unit_tests","lint","type_check"]},"output":{"result":"pass_or_fail","evidence":["logs","artifacts","commit_identity"]},"expected_behavior":"Each CI run executes the declared checks against a known source revision and records reproducible results.","incorrect_code":"run_tests_locally()\n# Local success is not shared, reproducible CI evidence.","corrected_code":"ci_job:\n  steps:\n    - checkout_fixed_revision\n    - install_locked_dependencies\n    - run_tests\n    - publish_test_results\n# Exact syntax depends on the CI platform.","failure_mode":"CI reports success for a different source state or uses uncontrolled dependencies.","expected_error":"False-positive builds, environment-specific failures, or unreproducible verification.","cause":"CI execution was not tied tightly to source and dependency state.","root_cause":"Verification evidence lacked reproducibility metadata.","solution":"Pin source revision, dependencies, runtime/tool versions, and record artifacts/results for every required check.","edge_case":"External forks or untrusted pull requests may require isolated runners without access to privileged secrets.","security_implication":"CI runners execute code and therefore require isolation, least-privilege credentials, protected branches, and careful handling of untrusted contributions.","performance_implication":"Measure queue time, execution time, cache effectiveness, runner utilization, and test parallelism without compromising determinism.","test_case":"Trigger CI for a fixed commit and verify that build/test results reference exactly that revision.","test_input":{"commit":"fixed_sha","dependencies":"locked"},"expected_test_result":"All reported checks and generated artifacts are associated with the tested revision.","testing_guidance":"Test failed builds, cancellation, retry, dependency installation, secret isolation, artifact integrity, and concurrent jobs.","verification_method":"CI-platform documentation plus repository workflow validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify runner image/version, action/plugin versions, dependency locks, and branch-protection integration.","language":"YAML","language_version":"","framework":"GitHub Actions","framework_version":"Must be pinned where applicable","version_range":"Exact action and runner versions should be declared.","dependencies":["Source repository","CI runner"],"execution_requirements":["CI environment","Test suite"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CI engineering practice plus selected CI-platform documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B060","D33-B095","D33-B100"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Build/test requirements","Source-control workflow"],"extends":"D33-B095","refines":"D33-B060","specializes":"Continuous-integration technology","contrasts_with":"Manual local verification","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Continuous integration practice"],"provenance":["GitHub Actions/GitLab CI/Jenkins official documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B055","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Artifact Registry Technology","subtopic":"Versioned build artifact storage","concept":"Artifact registry technology stores and distributes versioned build outputs such as container images, packages, binaries, and libraries with controlled access and provenance metadata.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should an artifact registry be introduced?","answer":"An artifact registry is appropriate when build outputs must be versioned, promoted, consumed by multiple environments, access-controlled, and traceable to source and build evidence.","explanation":"An artifact registry creates a controlled boundary between building software and deploying software. It should preserve immutable or explicitly versioned artifacts rather than requiring every deployment to rebuild from source.","principle":"Promote tested artifacts rather than rebuilding potentially different artifacts at each deployment stage.","objective":"Provide a stable, traceable distribution mechanism for release artifacts.","requirements":["Versioning","Access control","Immutability policy","Artifact metadata","Promotion workflow"],"constraints":["Registry availability affects artifact retrieval","Mutable tags can weaken traceability","Artifacts require retention and cleanup policy","Artifact integrity must be verifiable"],"preconditions":["Build outputs are defined","Release workflow exists"],"input":{"artifact":"application-image","source_revision":"fixed_sha","build":"ci_run"},"output":{"artifact_reference":"version_or_digest"},"expected_behavior":"Deployment references a specific verified artifact rather than implicitly rebuilding source.","incorrect_code":"deploy(build_from_current_source())\n# Deployment rebuilds can produce different artifacts from the same nominal version.","corrected_code":"artifact = registry.get("application", digest=verified_digest)\ndeploy(artifact)","failure_mode":"Production runs an artifact different from the one tested.","expected_error":"Untraceable releases, environment-specific builds, or rollback ambiguity.","cause":"Build and deployment were not connected through an immutable artifact identity.","root_cause":"Source state was treated as equivalent to built artifact state.","solution":"Store versioned artifacts in a controlled registry and deploy identified artifacts with provenance.","edge_case":"A tiny interpreted local application may not require a dedicated artifact registry if source and deployment are tightly coupled and reproducibility is otherwise assured.","security_implication":"Artifact registries require access controls, integrity verification, malware/dependency scanning where appropriate, and protection against unauthorized replacement.","performance_implication":"Measure upload/download throughput, registry latency, storage growth, caching, and retention cleanup.","test_case":"Build an artifact, record its digest, deploy it to a test environment, and verify that the deployed artifact matches the recorded digest.","test_input":{"artifact":"test_build","expected_digest":"recorded"},"expected_test_result":"Deployment resolves the exact recorded artifact identity.","testing_guidance":"Test artifact immutability, unauthorized replacement, retention, promotion, rollback, digest verification, and registry outage.","verification_method":"Registry documentation plus artifact-provenance testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact registry behavior for immutable digests, tags, permissions, retention, and provenance metadata.","language":"OCI","language_version":"","framework":"Container Registry","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact registry implementation/version must be declared.","dependencies":["Build system","Artifact registry"],"execution_requirements":["Test registry","Build artifact"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLU