{"record_id":"D34-B056","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Dependency Management Technology","subtopic":"Reproducible dependency resolution","concept":"Dependency-management technology records direct and transitive software dependencies, resolves compatible versions, and can produce reproducible installation state.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"Why is dependency-lock technology important for reproducible software builds?","answer":"Dependency-lock technology is important when builds must reproduce a known dependency graph and avoid unexpected upgrades or transitive dependency changes. It should record enough information to recreate the intended environment under the supported package ecosystem.","explanation":"Manifest files describe requested dependencies, while lock files or equivalent resolved metadata capture a specific dependency graph. Exact behavior varies by ecosystem. Dependency management must also address vulnerability updates, compatibility, platform-specific resolution, and supply-chain provenance.","principle":"Separate dependency intent from resolved dependency state and make the resolved state reproducible.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled dependency drift between development, CI, and production.","requirements":["Direct dependencies","Transitive resolution","Locking","Integrity metadata","Update workflow"],"constraints":["Lock files can become stale","Different platforms may resolve different dependency sets","Security updates require controlled lock regeneration","Package registries are external dependencies"],"preconditions":["Package ecosystem is selected","Supported runtime/platform matrix is defined"],"input":{"manifest":["fastapi","pydantic"],"platform":"linux_x86_64"},"output":{"artifact":"resolved_dependency_graph"},"expected_behavior":"Installation from the locked dependency state resolves the intended versions and integrity metadata for the supported environment.","incorrect_code":"pip install fastapi pydantic\n# Unconstrained installation can resolve different versions over time.","corrected_code":"pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.lock\n# Exact locking mechanism depends on the package-management ecosystem.","failure_mode":"Different environments receive different transitive dependencies.","expected_error":"Reproducibility failures, unexpected API changes, incompatible transitive packages, or supply-chain drift.","cause":"Dependency resolution was left unconstrained or lock state was not used.","root_cause":"Dependency intent was confused with dependency resolution state.","solution":"Use the ecosystem's supported lock/resolution mechanism and verify dependency integrity in CI.","edge_case":"Some ecosystems have platform-specific lock information; reproducibility must be defined for each supported target rather than assuming one universal graph.","security_implication":"Dependency sources and integrity metadata must be trusted; lock updates should be reviewed and scanned for known vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Measure dependency-resolution time, cache effectiveness, installation size, and build reproducibility.","test_case":"Install dependencies twice from the same lock state in equivalent environments and compare the resolved graph.","test_input":{"lock_state":"fixed","environment":"equivalent"},"expected_test_result":"The resolved dependency graph matches the declared lock state for the supported platform.","testing_guidance":"Test lock regeneration, platform differences, dependency conflicts, malicious/tampered package scenarios, and controlled security updates.","verification_method":"Package-manager documentation plus dependency graph comparison.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact package-manager version and lock semantics for the selected ecosystem.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"pip","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact Python/package-manager versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Package index","Package manager"],"execution_requirements":["Controlled package environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Software supply-chain practice plus package-manager documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B094","D34-B054","D34-B055"],"depends_on":["D34-B001"],"requires":["Supported runtime matrix","Dependency policy"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B094","specializes":"Dependency-management technology","contrasts_with":"Unconstrained package installation","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Reproducible dependency management"],"provenance":["Python Packaging documentation; pip documentation; ecosystem-specific package-manager documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B057","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Static Analysis Technology","subtopic":"Automated source-code analysis","concept":"Static-analysis technology examines source or intermediate representations without executing the target program to detect classes of defects, unsafe patterns, type inconsistencies, or maintainability problems.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should static-analysis technology be added to the engineering toolchain?","answer":"Static analysis is appropriate when defect classes can be detected mechanically before execution, especially across large codebases where manual review alone cannot reliably cover structural and semantic patterns.","explanation":"Linters, type checkers, AST analyzers, and security analyzers have different scopes. Ruff, mypy, Semgrep, CodeQL, and compiler diagnostics are examples of distinct static-analysis approaches. They should be selected according to detectable defect classes rather than tool popularity.","principle":"Automate detection of mechanically detectable defects before runtime testing.","objective":"Increase early defect detection while keeping findings actionable and tied to defined quality requirements.","requirements":["Rule set","Language support","CI integration","Baseline policy","Suppression governance"],"constraints":["Static analysis produces false positives and false negatives","Rules may be version-dependent","Passing static analysis does not prove runtime correctness","Generated code may require special handling"],"preconditions":["Target languages are supported","Defect classes are identified"],"input":{"source":"repository","checks":["type_errors","unsafe_patterns","style"]},"output":{"findings":["rule","location","severity","message"]},"expected_behavior":"Configured analyzers consistently inspect the intended source revision and report findings according to defined policy.","incorrect_code":"def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a + "x"\n# A type checker can identify the incompatible operation before runtime.","corrected_code":"def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a + b","failure_mode":"Static analysis is configured but findings are ignored or suppressed without evidence.","expected_error":"Known defects remain in the codebase despite nominal analyzer adoption.","cause":"Tool output was not integrated into a defined engineering gate.","root_cause":"Static analysis was treated as a report generator rather than a verification mechanism.","solution":"Define supported analyzers, severity policy, suppression rules, and CI gates aligned with actual defect classes.","edge_case":"Legacy code may require a baseline so new defects are blocked without pretending historical findings were fixed.","security_implication":"Security analyzers can identify dangerous patterns but do not prove absence of vulnerabilities; findings require triage and verification.","performance_implication":"Measure analyzer runtime, incremental analysis behavior, CI cost, and false-positive triage overhead.","test_case":"Introduce a known detectable defect and verify that the configured analyzer identifies it at the expected severity.","test_input":{"defect":"incompatible_type_operation"},"expected_test_result":"The analyzer reports the defect according to the configured rule set.","testing_guidance":"Test rule configuration, suppressions, generated files, incremental changes, false positives, and analyzer version upgrades.","verification_method":"Analyzer documentation plus seeded-defect validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify exact analyzer/rule versions and that CI actually executes the intended configuration.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"mypy","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact analyzer/version must be declared.","dependencies":["Static-analysis tool"],"execution_requirements":["Repository with representative source"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Static-analysis engineering practice plus selected analyzer documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B060","D33-B095","D34-B054"],"depends_on":["D34-B054"],"requires":["Language/toolchain definition","Quality policy"],"extends":"D33-B095","refines":"D33-B060","specializes":"Static-analysis technology","contrasts_with":"Runtime-only defect detection","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Static program analysis"],"provenance":["mypy official documentation; Ruff/Semgrep/CodeQL/compiler documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B058","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Contract Testing Technology","subtopic":"Consumer-provider compatibility verification","concept":"Contract-testing technology verifies that independently developed components continue to satisfy explicitly defined interaction contracts without requiring every integration test to exercise the complete system.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should contract-testing technology be used for APIs or asynchronous messages?","answer":"Contract testing is valuable when independently deployed consumers and providers must preserve interaction compatibility and full end-to-end environments are costly, slow, or insufficient for isolating contract failures.","explanation":"Consumer-driven and provider contracts can verify request/response shapes and selected behavioral expectations. Pact and similar tools implement contract-testing models. Contracts should not attempt to encode every business rule; they should focus on integration boundaries.","principle":"Verify the stability of independently owned interfaces at the contract boundary.","objective":"Detect integration incompatibilities before deployment without relying exclusively on full end-to-end tests.","requirements":["Explicit contract","Consumer/provider ownership","Versioning","Automated verification","Failure reporting"],"constraints":["Contracts can become brittle if they encode implementation details","Passing a contract does not prove complete business correctness","Asynchronous contracts require appropriate message semantics"],"preconditions":["Independent interface ownership exists","Interface behavior can be expressed as a contract"],"input":{"consumer":"order-service","provider":"inventory-service","contract":{"operation":"reserve_stock"}},"output":{"result":"contract_pass_or_fail","evidence":"verified_interaction_expectations"},"expected_behavior":"Provider and consumer remain compatible with the declared contract under the supported versions.","incorrect_code":"# Only provider unit tests; consumer compatibility is assumed.","corrected_code":"contract = define_consumer_expectation(\n    endpoint="/inventory/reserve",\n    request_schema=request_schema,\n    response_schema=response_schema,\n)\nverify_provider_against(contract)","failure_mode":"Provider changes an interface in a way that breaks an independently deployed consumer.","expected_error":"Integration failure after deployment despite isolated component tests passing.","cause":"Interface compatibility was not verified between ownership boundaries.","root_cause":"Component testing was mistaken for contract compatibility evidence.","solution":"Use contract testing when independent deployment creates meaningful compatibility risk.","edge_case":"A tightly coupled monolith deployed atomically may gain little from contract testing because producer and consumer versions change together.","security_implication":"Contract fixtures must not contain production secrets or sensitive customer data.","performance_implication":"Measure contract-suite runtime and environment requirements; contract tests should remain substantially more targeted than broad end-to-end suites.","test_case":"Change a provider response field in a breaking way and verify that the consumer contract fails before deployment.","test_input":{"provider_change":"remove_required_response_field"},"expected_test_result":"The contract test detects incompatibility.","testing_guidance":"Test backward compatibility, version negotiation, optional fields, error responses, asynchronous messages, and contract publication.","verification_method":"Contract-testing framework documentation plus consumer/provider verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that contracts run against the actual provider implementation/version and are not merely schema snapshots.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Pact","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact Pact implementation/version must be declared.","dependencies":["Consumer","Provider","Contract framework"],"execution_requirements":["Consumer/provider test environments"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Contract-testing practice plus selected framework documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B025","D33-B031","D34-B044","D34-B045"],"depends_on":["D34-B044"],"requires":["Interface ownership","Contract definition"],"extends":"D33-B025","refines":"D33-B031","specializes":"Contract-testing technology","contrasts_with":"End-to-end-only integration verification","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Consumer-provider contract testing"],"provenance":["Pact official documentation; contract-testing literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B059","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Property-Based Testing Technology","subtopic":"Generative input exploration","concept":"Property-based testing technology generates many structured inputs to test invariant properties rather than relying only on manually selected examples.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should property-based testing be selected over example-only unit tests?","answer":"Property-based testing is particularly useful when correctness can be expressed as invariants across broad input spaces, such as parsers, serializers, algorithms, state machines, validation logic, and data transformations.","explanation":"Hypothesis and similar frameworks generate test cases and shrink failing examples toward minimal counterexamples. Property-based testing complements rather than replaces example-based tests and requires carefully defined properties.","principle":"Use generated inputs when invariants can expose classes of defects that hand-picked examples may miss.","objective":"Explore a broader input space while producing reproducible counterexamples for invariant violations.","requirements":["Explicit property/invariant","Input strategy","Reproducibility","Failure shrinking","Domain constraints"],"constraints":["Poorly defined properties produce weak tests","Generated inputs may miss important real-world semantics","Some systems require expensive setup per test","Randomized exploration does not replace deterministic verification"],"preconditions":["Correctness properties are expressible","Input domain can be generated safely"],"input":{"function":"normalize_identifier","property":"idempotent"},"output":{"result":"pass_or_counterexample"},"expected_behavior":"Generated valid inputs satisfy the declared invariant, or a minimized counterexample is produced when they do not.","incorrect_code":"def normalize(value):\n    return value.strip().lower()\n# This implementation may violate domain-specific normalization invariants for certain inputs.","corrected_code":"@given(identifier_strategy())\ndef test_normalization_idempotent(value):\n    assert normalize(normalize(value)) == normalize(value)","failure_mode":"A hidden input class violates an invariant that example-based tests did not cover.","expected_error":"Minimal counterexample exposing an invariant violation.","cause":"Test space was too narrow for the algorithm's input domain.","root_cause":"Correctness was evaluated only through manually selected examples.","solution":"Define domain invariants and use property-based generation where the input space is sufficiently structured.","edge_case":"External side effects should not be randomly generated without isolation; pure functions and controlled state machines are better targets.","security_implication":"Generated inputs can exercise parser and validation edge cases defensively; resource limits are required when fuzz-like generation targets expensive operations.","performance_implication":"Measure generation rate, shrink time, test runtime, and coverage of meaningful input classes.","test_case":"Generate many valid identifiers and verify normalization idempotence.","test_input":{"strategy":"generated_identifiers","property":"f(f(x)) == f(x)"},"expected_test_result":"No generated case violates the invariant, or a reproducible minimized counterexample is produced.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic seeds where supported, constrain generators to valid/invalid domains, and preserve failing examples as regression tests.","verification_method":"Property-based testing framework documentation plus invariant-focused test execution.","verification_guidance":"Verify the generated case count, seed/replay behavior, shrinking, and strategy constraints.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Hypothesis","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact Hypothesis/Python versions must be declared.","dependencies":["Hypothesis","Test runner"],"execution_requirements":["Isolated test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Property-based testing practice plus Hypothesis primary documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B060","D34-B054","D34-B057"],"depends_on":["D34-B054"],"requires":["Explicit invariant","Input generator"],"extends":"","refines":"D33-B060","specializes":"Property-based testing technology","contrasts_with":"Example-only testing","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Property-based testing methodology"],"provenance":["Hypothesis official documentation; property-based testing literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B060","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"D36-RICH-JSONL-1.0","topic":"Mutation Testing Technology","subtopic":"Test-suite effectiveness verification","concept":"Mutation-testing technology deliberately introduces small code changes and measures whether the test suite detects them, providing evidence about the fault-detection strength of tests rather than merely their execution coverage.","knowledge_type":"technology_decision","question":"When should mutation testing be introduced to evaluate test-suite strength?","answer":"Mutation testing is useful when teams need evidence that tests detect meaningful implementation faults and ordinary coverage metrics are insufficient. It is especially valuable for critical libraries, business rules, parsers, and verification-heavy components.","explanation":"Mutation testing modifies code using defined mutation operators and expects tests to fail. A surviving mutant indicates that the test suite did not detect that mutation, although not every surviving mutant represents a real defect. Tools such as mutmut and PIT target different languages.","principle":"Evaluate tests by their ability to detect representative faults, not only by the percentage of lines executed.","objective":"Identify weak or redundant tests and improve fault-detection capability.","requirements":["Mutation operators","Test suite","Mutation execution","Survivor analysis","Threshold/governance policy"],"constraints":["Mutation testing can be computationally expensive","Equivalent mutants may survive legitimately","Mutation operators are language/tool dependent","A high mutation score does not prove correctness"],"preconditions":["Stable automated tests exist","Target code has meaningful testable behavior"],"input":{"source":"critical_module","tests":"automated_test_suite"},"output":{"result":"mutant_kill_statistics","evidence":"surviving_mutants"},"expected_behavior":"Representative mutations are executed against the test suite and surviving mutants are classified or investigated.","incorrect_code":"# Treating line coverage as proof of test effectiveness\ncoverage = 95\nassert coverage >= 90","corrected_code":"# Conceptual mutation-testing workflow:\nmutants = generate_mutants(source)\nresults = run_tests_against(mutants)\nanalyze_survivors(results)\n# Exact tooling and mutation operators depend on the language.","failure_mode":"High code coverage masks tests that do not detect meaningful behavioral changes.","expected_error":"Surviving mutants reveal gaps in assertions or missing behavioral scenarios.","cause":"Testing was evaluated primarily through execution coverage.","root_cause":"Coverage was used as a proxy for fault-detection strength.","solution":"Use mutation testing selectively on important components where stronger evidence of test effectiveness is required.","edge_case":"Equivalent mutants can survive because they do not change observable behavior; they require classification rather than blind test additions.","security_implication":"Mutation testing itself is generally defensive, but mutated security-critical code must never be mistaken for production-safe artifacts.","performance_implication":"Mutation testing can be expensive; measure mutant count, execution time, parallelism, and incremental targeting strategy.","test_case":"Introduce a mutation that changes a required comparison and verify that the existing test suite kills it.","test_input":{"mutation":"change_required_comparison","target":"critical_rule"},"expected_test_result":"The relevant tests fail for the mutated implementation.","testing_guidance":"Track killed, survived, timed-out, and equivalent mutants; preserve justified survivors and avoid treating score as correctness proof.","verification_method":"Mutation-testing tool documentation plus seeded mutation verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify mutation operators, test command, timeout behavior, and surviving-mutant classification.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"mutmut","framework_version":"Must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Exact mutation-testing tool/version must be declared.","dependencies":["Test suite","Mutation-testing tool"],"execution_requirements":["Stable automated test suite","Isolated mutation environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"Mutation-testing practice plus selected tool documentation.","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B095","D33-B060","D34-B054","D34-B059"],"depends_on":["D34-B054"],"requires":["Stable test suite","Critical component selection"],"extends":"D33-B095","refines":"D33-B060","specializes":"Mutation-testing technology","contrasts_with":"Line-coverage-only evaluation","contradicts":"","supersedes":"","implements":"","tests":"","verifies":"","derived_from":["Mutation testing methodology"],"provenance":["mutmut official documentation; mutation-testing literature; PIT documentation for JVM ecosystems"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B061","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Search Technology","concept":"Full-text search technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated full-text search technology be selected instead of database-native text search?","answer":"A dedicated search technology is justified when search becomes a first-class workload requiring substantial indexing, relevance tuning, faceting, distributed search, operational isolation, or search-specific scalability. Database-native search is often sufficient when search scope, query complexity, and data volume remain bounded by the primary datastore.","explanation":"The decision should be driven by search requirements rather than by the existence of text fields. Evaluate query semantics, indexing requirements, ranking, filtering, aggregation, freshness, scale, operational complexity, consistency expectations, and synchronization between the source of truth and search index.","principle":"Use the simplest technology that satisfies the required search workload and explicitly model the source-of-truth relationship.","objective":"Select search technology according to measurable search requirements.","requirements":["Text retrieval requirements must be identified.","Relevance and filtering requirements must be defined.","Index freshness requirements must be specified.","Source-of-truth ownership must remain explicit."],"constraints":["A search index may be derived state.","Search indexing introduces synchronization and recovery concerns.","Technology choice must account for operational overhead."],"preconditions":["Search workload characteristics are documented.","Primary data ownership is known."],"input":"Search requirements, corpus characteristics, query patterns, freshness constraints, and operational constraints.","output":"Technology decision with selection criteria, synchronization design, and verification requirements.","expected_behavior":"The selected search technology supports the required retrieval semantics without creating an unnecessary independent source of truth.","incorrect_code":"search_index.write(document); primary_db.write(document)","corrected_code":"primary_db.write(document); publish_index_update(document_id);","failure_mode":"Search index is treated as the authoritative transactional datastore.","expected_error":"No deterministic runtime error is required; the architectural failure appears as stale, missing, or divergent search data.","cause":"Transactional ownership and derived search indexing are not separated.","root_cause":"Search technology was selected without defining source-of-truth semantics.","solution":"Keep authoritative writes in the owning datastore and define reliable index update, replay, rebuild, and freshness mechanisms.","edge_case":"An index becomes unavailable immediately after an authoritative database write.","security_implication":"Search indexes must enforce equivalent authorization boundaries or prevent unauthorized retrieval through the search path.","performance_implication":"Search technology can reduce expensive text-query load on transactional storage, but indexing and synchronization add processing overhead.","test_case":"Write an authoritative record, delay indexing, query search, then process the index update and repeat the query.","test_input":"record_id=R1; index_update_delayed=true","expected_test_result":"The authoritative record remains correct while search availability/freshness follows the explicitly defined indexing behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test indexing failure, replay, deletion, rebuild, stale reads, authorization filtering, and query relevance.","verification_method":"Architecture review plus integration tests for source-of-truth and index synchronization.","verification_guidance":"Verify that authoritative data and derived search data have explicit ownership and recoverable synchronization.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":null,"version_range":"Technology-version specific; pin selected search engine version during implementation.","dependencies":["Primary datastore","Selected search/indexing technology"],"execution_requirements":["Configured datastore and search index"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B022","D34-B033"],"depends_on":["D34-B033"],"requires":["Search workload characterization"],"extends":["D34-B022"],"refines":["D34-B033"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["D34-B022"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Search-system source-of-truth and indexing architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established software architecture practice; technology-specific behavior must be verified against the selected search engine's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B062","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Time-Series Technology","concept":"Time-series database selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is a time-series database appropriate for an engineering workload?","answer":"A time-series database is appropriate when timestamped measurements are a dominant workload and the system requires time-oriented ingestion, retention, windowed queries, aggregation, downsampling, or time-based storage management.","explanation":"Technology selection should consider event timestamp semantics, ingestion rate, retention, aggregation patterns, cardinality, late-arriving data, query windows, compression behavior, and integration with monitoring or analytical systems.","principle":"Choose storage technology according to dominant access patterns and lifecycle requirements rather than data type alone.","objective":"Determine whether time-series-specific storage capabilities justify an additional technology.","requirements":["Timestamp semantics must be defined.","Retention requirements must be explicit.","Expected query and aggregation patterns must be known."],"constraints":["High-cardinality dimensions can affect storage and query behavior.","Late or corrected measurements require explicit handling.","An additional datastore creates operational and synchronization cost."],"preconditions":["The workload is measurably time-oriented.","Retention and query requirements are documented."],"input":"Measurement schema, timestamp behavior, ingestion characteristics, retention policy, and query workload.","output":"Time-series technology decision and storage/query design.","expected_behavior":"The technology supports required temporal ingestion, retention, and query operations while preserving measurement semantics.","incorrect_code":"metrics_table = db.table("metrics"); metrics_table.scan_all_rows()","corrected_code":"metrics_store.write(timestamp, metric_name, value, dimensions); query_window(start_time, end_time)","failure_mode":"A general-purpose transactional table is used for a predominantly temporal workload without evaluating temporal access requirements.","expected_error":"No specific runtime error is implied; degradation may appear as inefficient queries, retention complexity, or excessive storage-management cost.","cause":"Technology selection ignored dominant temporal access patterns.","root_cause":"Storage was selected primarily from familiarity rather than workload characteristics.","solution":"Characterize temporal workload requirements and compare database-native time-series capabilities with dedicated technology before introducing another datastore.","edge_case":"Measurements arrive out of order or are corrected after initial ingestion.","security_implication":"Time-series dimensions must not expose sensitive tenant or operational data across authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Temporal indexing and retention mechanisms can improve time-window workloads, but actual performance depends on workload and configuration.","test_case":"Insert ordered, late, duplicate, and corrected measurements and query multiple time windows.","test_input":"events=[ordered_event,late_event,duplicate_event,corrected_event]","expected_test_result":"Timestamp and correction semantics match the documented storage contract.","testing_guidance":"Test ordering, duplicate handling, late data, retention, aggregation boundaries, and high-cardinality dimensions.","verification_method":"Workload-model review plus representative integration and query tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify temporal semantics and retention behavior against documented requirements and selected technology documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":null,"version_range":"Pin the selected time-series technology and client version.","dependencies":["Time-series datastore or equivalent temporal storage"],"execution_requirements":["Configured time-series storage"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B022","D34-B033"],"depends_on":["D34-B033"],"requires":["Temporal workload characterization"],"extends":[],"refines":["D34-B022"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["General-purpose transactional storage"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Temporal data storage architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established database architecture practice; exact technology capabilities must be verified against official documentation for the selected implementation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B063","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Object Storage","concept":"Object storage technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should object storage be selected for application data?","answer":"Object storage is appropriate for large or immutable/semi-immutable blobs such as documents, media, backups, exports, and build artifacts when access is naturally object-oriented and does not require transactional row-level operations.","explanation":"Object storage separates large binary payloads from transactional metadata. The architecture should define object identity, metadata ownership, lifecycle, integrity, authorization, retention, versioning, and recovery semantics.","principle":"Store large blob content in storage optimized for objects while keeping transactional metadata and ownership in the system of record.","objective":"Select object storage when blob-oriented access and lifecycle management are primary requirements.","requirements":["Object identity must be stable.","Metadata and ownership must be defined.","Lifecycle and retention rules must be explicit.","Integrity and authorization requirements must be documented."],"constraints":["Object storage does not replace transactional relational semantics.","Object references can become stale.","Large-object operations may require multipart or resumable mechanisms."],"preconditions":["The workload contains object-like payloads.","Transactional metadata requirements are known."],"input":"Object size, access pattern, retention, lifecycle, integrity, and authorization requirements.","output":"Object-storage decision and metadata/reference architecture.","expected_behavior":"Large payloads are stored as objects while authoritative business metadata remains governed by the owning transactional system.","incorrect_code":"db.insert_document(binary_blob);","corrected_code":"document_id = db.insert_document_metadata(); object_store.put(document_id, binary_payload);","failure_mode":"Large binary data is embedded indiscriminately inside transactional records.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime error; operational symptoms can include oversized transactions, backup growth, and inefficient retrieval.","cause":"Storage technology was selected without distinguishing metadata from blob content.","root_cause":"Object lifecycle and access pattern were not modeled separately from transactional state.","solution":"Separate object content from transactional metadata where object-oriented storage requirements justify it, with explicit consistency and cleanup handling.","edge_case":"Metadata creation succeeds but object upload fails.","security_implication":"Object access must enforce authorization independently; possession of an object identifier must not automatically grant access.","performance_implication":"Large object transfer can be isolated from transactional database workload, but network and storage transfer costs remain workload-dependent.","test_case":"Create metadata, fail object upload, retry upload, then delete metadata and verify object cleanup policy.","test_input":"metadata_create=true; object_upload=FAIL_THEN_RETRY","expected_test_result":"No orphaned object remains outside the documented cleanup/reconciliation policy.","testing_guidance":"Test partial uploads, retries, deletion, versioning, authorization, integrity checks, and reconciliation.","verification_method":"Integration testing plus reconciliation verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify metadata/object lifecycle invariants and recovery behavior.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":null,"version_range":"Provider-specific; pin SDK and storage API versions.","dependencies":["Object storage service or compatible local implementation","Transactional metadata store"],"execution_requirements":["Configured object-storage endpoint"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B055"],"depends_on":["D34-B055"],"requires":["Object lifecycle definition"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Transactional database blob storage"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Blob/object storage architectural patterns"],"provenance":"Established storage architecture practice; exact API semantics must be verified against the selected provider's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B064","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"Message broker technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When does a message broker become a justified technology dependency?","answer":"A message broker is justified when producers and consumers require decoupled communication, buffering, asynchronous delivery, fan-out, durable message handling, or independent processing rates that cannot be adequately handled by direct synchronous calls.","explanation":"The decision must specify delivery semantics, ordering, durability, retention, acknowledgment, retry behavior, dead-letter handling, consumer independence, and operational ownership. A broker should not be introduced merely because an architecture is described as event-driven.","principle":"Introduce messaging when asynchronous communication semantics provide a concrete architectural capability required by the system.","objective":"Determine whether broker-based communication is required by workload and reliability requirements.","requirements":["Delivery semantics must be specified.","Message ownership and schema must be defined.","Retry and failure behavior must be explicit.","Consumer lifecycle must be understood."],"constraints":["Messaging introduces operational and consistency complexity.","Exactly-once business effects cannot be assumed merely from transport semantics.","Ordering may be scoped rather than global."],"preconditions":["Asynchronous communication requirements exist.","Producer-consumer coupling has been analyzed."],"input":"Communication patterns, throughput characteristics, durability requirements, ordering requirements, and failure model.","output":"Message-broker technology decision and messaging contract.","expected_behavior":"Messages are delivered according to explicitly defined semantics and consumers process them safely under retries and duplication.","incorrect_code":"broker.publish(event); consumer.perform_side_effect(event);","corrected_code":"broker.publish(event_with_id); consumer.deduplicate(event_id); consumer.perform_idempotent_side_effect(event);","failure_mode":"Consumer side effects are executed repeatedly when delivery is retried.","expected_error":"No universal runtime error; duplicate business effects may occur.","cause":"Transport delivery and business-effect idempotency were conflated.","root_cause":"Messaging semantics were not translated into consumer processing requirements.","solution":"Define delivery semantics, message identity, deduplication, idempotency, retry, and dead-letter behavior.","edge_case":"Consumer completes the business effect but crashes before acknowledging the message.","security_implication":"Messages must enforce tenant, identity, authorization, and integrity boundaries appropriate to the data they carry.","performance_implication":"Brokers can absorb bursts and decouple producer/consumer rates, but persistence, serialization, delivery, and operational overhead are workload-dependent.","test_case":"Process a message successfully, force acknowledgement failure, redeliver the same message, and verify business-effect idempotency.","test_input":"message_id=M1; first_ack=FAIL; redelivery=true","expected_test_result":"The business effect occurs according to the idempotency contract without unintended duplication.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate delivery, ordering boundaries, retries, poison messages, consumer restarts, backlog growth, and schema evolution.","verification_method":"Integration and failure-injection tests against documented delivery semantics.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual broker guarantees against official technology documentation rather than assuming stronger semantics.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":null,"version_range":"Pin broker and client versions.","dependencies":["Selected message broker","Consumer/producer client libraries"],"execution_requirements":["Configured broker and test consumers"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B032","D34-B027"],"depends_on":["D33-B032"],"requires":["Explicit messaging semantics"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B032"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Synchronous request-response"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Message-driven architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established distributed-systems architecture practice; selected broker guarantees must be verified against official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B065","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Work Queue","concept":"Work queue technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should a work queue be selected instead of direct request processing?","answer":"A work queue is appropriate when tasks can be processed asynchronously and the system needs buffering, controlled concurrency, retry handling, worker independence, or protection against bursty workloads.","explanation":"Work queues differ from general event streams because the architectural intent is usually task distribution and completion rather than retaining a durable stream of domain events for multiple independent consumers. The decision should define task ownership, visibility timeout or acknowledgment semantics, retry policy, ordering needs, and idempotency.","principle":"Use a queue when work can be safely decoupled from request latency and requires controlled asynchronous execution.","objective":"Select queue technology based on task-processing requirements.","requirements":["Task ownership must be defined.","Retry semantics must be explicit.","Worker concurrency must be controllable.","Task idempotency must be addressed."],"constraints":["Queued work may execute later than request submission.","Retries can produce duplicate execution attempts.","Backlog growth requires monitoring and operational policy."],"preconditions":["The task is safely asynchronous.","Caller behavior can tolerate deferred completion or a job-status model."],"input":"Task characteristics, latency requirements, retry behavior, concurrency requirements, and workload burst profile.","output":"Work-queue technology decision and task-processing contract.","expected_behavior":"Tasks are durably or appropriately buffered and processed according to explicit acknowledgment, retry, and idempotency rules.","incorrect_code":"queue.add(task); worker.execute(task);","corrected_code":"queue.add(task_with_id); worker.claim(task_with_id); worker.execute_idempotently(task_with_id); worker.ack(task_with_id);","failure_mode":"A task is executed multiple times after worker failure or acknowledgement loss.","expected_error":"No universal runtime exception; duplicate side effects may occur.","cause":"Task execution was assumed to be exactly-once.","root_cause":"Queue delivery and business-effect semantics were not separated.","solution":"Design task execution for retry, explicit task identity, idempotency, and recovery.","edge_case":"A worker crashes after completing an external side effect but before acknowledging the task.","security_implication":"Queued tasks must preserve authorization context safely without trusting mutable or user-controlled authorization fields.","performance_implication":"Queues can smooth bursts and control concurrency, but backlog latency and worker overhead depend on workload and configuration.","test_case":"Execute a task, crash before acknowledgment, redeliver it, and verify the resulting business state remains correct.","test_input":"task_id=T1; side_effect=COMPLETED; acknowledgement=FAIL","expected_test_result":"Retry does not produce an unintended duplicate business effect.","testing_guidance":"Test retries, worker crashes, poison tasks, backlog limits, concurrency, cancellation, and task expiration.","verification_method":"Failure-injection and integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify queue semantics and worker behavior against the selected technology documentation and application invariants.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":null,"version_range":"Pin queue service and worker client versions.","dependencies":["Queue technology","Worker runtime"],"execution_requirements":["Configured queue and worker test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B064","D34-B027"],"depends_on":["D34-B064"],"requires":["Asynchronous task requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Synchronous request processing"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Asynchronous work-queue architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established distributed-systems practice; exact queue semantics must be verified against selected technology documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B066","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Scheduling","concept":"Scheduler technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is a dedicated scheduler or job-orchestration technology justified?","answer":"A scheduler is justified when the system requires reliable time-based execution, recurring jobs, delayed work, dependency-aware scheduling, missed-run handling, or operational control beyond simple in-process timers.","explanation":"Technology selection should consider persistence, distributed execution, time zones, misfires, retries, concurrency limits, dependencies, observability, and administrative controls. Simple periodic tasks may not justify a separate scheduling platform.","principle":"Use persistent scheduling technology when scheduling itself is a reliability and operational requirement.","objective":"Select scheduling technology according to temporal execution and recovery requirements.","requirements":["Schedule persistence requirements must be known.","Retry and missed-run behavior must be defined.","Time-zone semantics must be explicit.","Concurrency and overlap rules must be specified."],"constraints":["Distributed schedulers introduce operational dependencies.","Clock and time-zone behavior can produce edge cases.","A scheduler does not automatically make job execution idempotent."],"preconditions":["Recurring or delayed execution is required.","Job ownership and execution semantics are documented."],"input":"Schedule definitions, job characteristics, reliability requirements, and operational controls.","output":"Scheduler technology decision and job execution contract.","expected_behavior":"Scheduled jobs execute according to persistent schedule semantics and documented recovery behavior.","incorrect_code":"while True: time.sleep(3600); run_job()","corrected_code":"scheduler.register(job_id, schedule, idempotency_key); scheduler.dispatch_due_jobs()","failure_mode":"An in-process timer loses scheduled work when the process restarts.","expected_error":"No mandatory exception; missed execution can occur silently.","cause":"Schedule state exists only in volatile process memory.","root_cause":"Scheduling reliability requirements were not modeled.","solution":"Persist schedule state and define misfire, retry, overlap, and recovery semantics when reliable scheduling is required.","edge_case":"A host is unavailable at the scheduled execution time.","security_implication":"Scheduler administration and job execution privileges must be separately authorized.","performance_implication":"Persistent scheduling adds coordination overhead but can prevent duplicated or lost scheduled work when correctly designed.","test_case":"Stop the scheduler around a scheduled execution time and restart it before and after the due time.","test_input":"scheduled_at=T; scheduler_down=true","expected_test_result":"Observed behavior matches the documented missed-run policy.","testing_guidance":"Test restarts, clock changes, time zones, overlapping jobs, retries, misfires, and duplicate execution.","verification_method":"Integration tests with controlled time and process-failure scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify scheduler guarantees against official documentation and application-level idempotency requirements.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":null,"version_range":"Pin scheduler/orchestrator and worker versions.","dependencies":["Scheduler technology","Persistent job store when required"],"execution_requirements":["Controlled scheduler test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B065","D34-B057"],"depends_on":["D34-B057"],"requires":["Persistent scheduling requirements"],"extends":[],"refines":["D34-B057"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["In-process timer"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Reliable scheduling architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established job scheduling practice; exact scheduler semantics must be verified against official technology documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B067","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Container Runtime","concept":"Container runtime technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When does containerization provide a meaningful technology capability rather than packaging convenience?","answer":"Containerization is useful when reproducible application environments, process isolation, immutable deployment artifacts, standardized runtime packaging, or orchestration integration are concrete requirements.","explanation":"Containers package application processes and dependencies into reproducible deployment units. They do not automatically provide application security isolation, fault tolerance, or correct resource management; those properties depend on the runtime, host, orchestration, configuration, and workload.","principle":"Select container technology when standardized runtime packaging and deployment isolation are actual requirements.","objective":"Determine whether containerization satisfies reproducibility and deployment requirements better than alternative packaging approaches.","requirements":["Runtime dependencies must be reproducible.","Image/build provenance must be controlled.","Resource and security boundaries must be defined.","Deployment lifecycle must be documented."],"constraints":["Containers share underlying kernel mechanisms in common implementations.","Image provenance and dependency management remain application responsibilities.","Containerization does not eliminate runtime configuration problems."],"preconditions":["Deployment environment supports the selected runtime.","Application process behavior is compatible with container lifecycle semantics."],"input":"Deployment requirements, runtime dependencies, isolation needs, resource controls, and operational environment.","output":"Container-runtime decision and image/build requirements.","expected_behavior":"The application runs from a reproducible artifact with explicitly defined runtime configuration and resource/security boundaries.","incorrect_code":"FROM python:latest\nCOPY . /app\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt","corrected_code":"FROM python:3.11-slim\nCOPY requirements.txt /app/\nRUN pip install --require-hashes -r /app/requirements.txt\nCOPY . /app","failure_mode":"A mutable floating base image causes deployment environments to change without an explicit application revision.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime error; reproducibility can silently degrade.","cause":"Build inputs were not pinned or provenance-controlled.","root_cause":"Technology selection and build reproducibility requirements were underspecified.","solution":"Pin supported runtime/base-image versions and dependencies, record provenance, and verify image contents.","edge_case":"A dependency becomes unavailable from the configured package source during rebuild.","security_implication":"Images require trusted provenance, vulnerability management, least privilege, and controlled runtime permissions.","performance_implication":"Container startup, image size, filesystem behavior, and resource limits can affect performance; claims require measurement.","test_case":"Build the same source revision twice using controlled inputs and compare declared artifact identity and dependency resolution.","test_input":"source_revision=R1; build_inputs=PINNED","expected_test_result":"Build outputs satisfy the defined reproducibility criteria.","testing_guidance":"Test startup, shutdown, signal handling, resource limits, dependency resolution, image provenance, and runtime permissions.","verification_method":"Reproducible-build checks plus container integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify base image, dependency, and runtime behavior against pinned versions and official runtime documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11","framework":"Container runtime","framework_version":"Pin exact runtime/image tooling versions.","version_range":"Use an explicit supported runtime range.","dependencies":["Container runtime","Pinned base image","Dependency manifest/lock data"],"execution_requirements":["Container build/runtime environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B055","D34-B056"],"depends_on":["D34-B055","D34-B056"],"requires":["Reproducible deployment requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Direct host deployment"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Containerized deployment and reproducibility principles"],"provenance":"Established container engineering practice; exact runtime behavior must be verified against official documentation for the selected runtime and image tooling.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B068","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Orchestration","concept":"Container orchestration technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is container orchestration justified over direct container execution?","answer":"Container orchestration is justified when an application requires coordinated deployment, scheduling across multiple nodes, service discovery, automated replacement, controlled scaling, rollout management, or cluster-level resource scheduling.","explanation":"Orchestration introduces significant operational complexity. A single host or simple deployment platform may be sufficient for bounded workloads. Selection should follow concrete requirements for placement, lifecycle management, availability, networking, scaling, and operations.","principle":"Do not adopt cluster orchestration solely because containers are used; require a workload or operational requirement that the orchestrator addresses.","objective":"Determine whether orchestration capabilities justify their operational complexity.","requirements":["Deployment topology must be understood.","Failure and replacement behavior must be defined.","Resource scheduling requirements must be explicit.","Operational ownership must be available."],"constraints":["Orchestrators add control-plane and operational complexity.","Application correctness remains the application's responsibility.","Cluster-level automation does not remove the need for observability and security."],"preconditions":["Multiple workloads or nodes create a coordination requirement.","The organization can operate or consume the required orchestration platform."],"input":"Workload topology, availability requirements, scaling needs, deployment model, and operational maturity.","output":"Orchestration decision and deployment model.","expected_behavior":"Workloads are scheduled and managed according to explicit placement, lifecycle, resource, and rollout requirements.","incorrect_code":"deploy_to_cluster = True","corrected_code":"deployment_policy = {"replicas":2,"resources":{"cpu":"defined","memory":"defined"},"rollout":"controlled"}","failure_mode":"A complex orchestrator is introduced without requirements that justify its control-plane and operational overhead.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime error; complexity appears through operational burden and configuration failure modes.","cause":"Technology selection was driven by platform fashion rather than workload requirements.","root_cause":"No explicit requirement-to-capability mapping was performed.","solution":"Map each required orchestration capability to a concrete workload requirement and define the operational acceptance criteria.","edge_case":"A cluster is available but the application has only one small, independently deployed workload.","security_implication":"Cluster credentials, workload identity, network policies, secrets, and admission controls require explicit governance.","performance_implication":"Scheduling and network layers add overhead; actual impact must be measured for the target workload.","test_case":"Deploy a workload, terminate an instance, perform a controlled rollout, and verify recovery and rollout invariants.","test_input":"replicas=2; instance_failure=1; rollout=controlled","expected_test_result":"The workload reaches the documented desired state without violating deployment invariants.","testing_guidance":"Test scheduling failures, node loss, rollout interruption, resource exhaustion, networking, and authorization.","verification_method":"Platform integration tests plus deployment conformance checks.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual orchestrator guarantees against its official documentation and configured policies.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Container orchestration platform","framework_version":"Pin selected platform/tooling versions.","version_range":"Version-specific; define supported control-plane and client ranges.","dependencies":["Container runtime","Orchestration control plane"],"execution_requirements":["Configured orchestration environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B027","D34-B067"],"depends_on":["D34-B067"],"requires":["Multi-workload or multi-node coordination requirement"],"extends":["D34-B067"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Direct container execution"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Container orchestration architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established deployment architecture practice; exact orchestration behavior must be verified against the selected platform's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B069","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Serverless Execution","concept":"Serverless compute technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should serverless compute be considered for an application workload?","answer":"Serverless compute can be considered when workloads are naturally event-driven or request-driven, execution can tolerate platform-managed lifecycle constraints, and reducing infrastructure management is a material requirement.","explanation":"The decision must evaluate execution duration, startup behavior, concurrency, state management, networking, observability, portability, deployment packaging, cost model, and platform limits. Serverless is not inherently cheaper, faster, or more scalable for every workload.","principle":"Choose serverless based on workload shape and operational requirements, not generic assumptions about cost or scalability.","objective":"Evaluate serverless execution against explicit workload and lifecycle requirements.","requirements":["Execution duration and concurrency must be characterized.","Persistent state ownership must be externalized where required.","Platform limits must be identified.","Operational and cost requirements must be modeled."],"constraints":["Provider/runtime limits can constrain workloads.","Execution environment may be ephemeral.","Vendor-specific integration can increase coupling."],"preconditions":["Workload is compatible with managed execution constraints.","Required state can be handled through appropriate external systems."],"input":"Invocation model, execution duration, concurrency, state, networking, observability, and portability requirements.","output":"Serverless suitability decision and execution architecture.","expected_behavior":"Stateless or appropriately state-externalized functions execute within documented platform limits and lifecycle semantics.","incorrect_code":"def handler(event):\n    global_state.append(event)\n    return process(global_state)","corrected_code":"def handler(event, state_store):\n    state = state_store.load(event["key"])\n    result = process(state, event)\n    state_store.save(event["key"], result)\n    return result","failure_mode":"Application correctness depends on process-local state surviving across invocations.","expected_error":"State may disappear between invocations; the failure may not present as a deterministic exception.","cause":"Ephemeral execution was treated as persistent process hosting.","root_cause":"Runtime lifecycle constraints were not included in architecture selection.","solution":"Externalize durable state and design explicitly for invocation lifecycle, concurrency, retries, and platform limits.","edge_case":"The platform retries an invocation after a partial external side effect.","security_implication":"Execution identity, secrets, network permissions, and event authorization must be explicitly scoped.","performance_implication":"Startup, invocation, concurrency, and platform scheduling characteristics are workload-specific and require measurement.","test_case":"Invoke the function repeatedly with simulated process replacement and retry after partial side effect.","test_input":"state_persistence=external; invocation_retry=true","expected_test_result":"Application state and business effects remain correct under documented retry and lifecycle behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test retries, timeouts, concurrency, cold/warm lifecycle differences, partial side effects, and external state failures.","verification_method":"Platform integration and failure-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify runtime limits and retry semantics against the selected provider's official documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Serverless runtime","framework_version":"Pin runtime and deployment-tool versions.","version_range":"Provider/runtime-specific.","dependencies":["Serverless execution platform","External state store when required"],"execution_requirements":["Configured serverless runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B064","D34-B067"],"depends_on":["D34-B064"],"requires":["Workload compatibility with managed execution limits"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Long-lived service process"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Managed/serverless execution architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established cloud execution architecture practice; exact runtime limits and retry behavior must be verified against official provider documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B070","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Edge and CDN","concept":"Edge delivery technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should edge delivery or CDN technology be introduced?","answer":"Edge delivery or a content delivery network is appropriate when geographically distributed clients, cacheable content, latency-sensitive delivery, origin-load reduction, or edge-level traffic controls are concrete requirements.","explanation":"The architecture must distinguish static/cacheable content from dynamic personalized responses, define cache keys and invalidation semantics, model consistency requirements, and preserve security and authorization boundaries. A CDN does not automatically make uncached dynamic workloads faster.","principle":"Place delivery capabilities at the edge only when latency, caching, traffic, or edge-control requirements justify the additional caching and invalidation model.","objective":"Determine whether edge delivery technology satisfies measurable distribution and latency requirements.","requirements":["Content cacheability must be defined.","Cache-control and invalidation semantics must be explicit.","Origin protection requirements must be documented.","Authorization behavior for personalized content must be defined."],"constraints":["Cached content can become stale.","Incorrect cache keys can cause data leakage.","Dynamic requests may still depend on origin execution."],"preconditions":["The workload has content or traffic suitable for edge delivery.","Consistency and authorization requirements are known."],"input":"Client geography, content characteristics, latency requirements, cacheability, origin workload, and security constraints.","output":"Edge/CDN technology decision and caching architecture.","expected_behavior":"Cacheable content is served according to explicit freshness and authorization rules while dynamic or sensitive content remains correctly protected.","incorrect_code":"cache.set("/account", account_response, ttl=3600)","corrected_code":"cache.set(public_cache_key, public_response, ttl=300); private_account_response = origin.fetch_with_authentication()","failure_mode":"Personalized content is cached under a shared cache key.","expected_error":"No guaranteed runtime exception; unauthorized data disclosure can occur.","cause":"Cache identity did not include the required authorization/user boundary.","root_cause":"Caching was designed without modeling data sensitivity and cache-key ownership.","solution":"Separate public and private caching, define cache keys explicitly, and prevent sensitive responses from entering shared caches unless authorization-aware caching is proven safe.","edge_case":"A user's authorization changes while a previously cached response remains fresh.","security_implication":"Cache configuration must prevent cross-user or cross-tenant disclosure and account for authorization changes.","performance_implication":"Edge caching can reduce origin traffic and network distance for suitable content, but actual latency and load effects must be measured.","test_case":"Request public and personalized resources from multiple identities and verify cache keys and authorization behavior.","test_input":"users=[U1,U2]; resource_types=[public,private]","expected_test_result":"No private response is served to an unauthorized identity; public content follows documented cache semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test cache poisoning, cache-key separation, invalidation, authorization changes, stale content, and origin failure behavior.","verification_method":"Security-focused integration tests plus cache-policy inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify cache behavior and security controls against the selected CDN/edge platform documentation.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"None","framework_version":null,"version_range":"Pin edge platform configuration/API version and client tooling.","dependencies":["Edge/CDN platform","Origin service"],"execution_requirements":["Configured edge/CDN test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B024","D33-B051"],"depends_on":["D33-B051"],"requires":["Cacheability and authorization analysis"],"extends":["D34-B022"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Origin-only delivery"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["CDN and edge-caching architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established web delivery architecture practice; exact cache and security semantics must be verified against the selected edge/CDN platform's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B071","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Realtime Communication","concept":"WebSocket technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is WebSocket technology appropriate for application communication?","answer":"WebSockets are appropriate when an application requires a long-lived bidirectional connection with low-latency server-to-client and client-to-server messaging, such as interactive collaboration, live operational interfaces, or realtime control surfaces.","explanation":"The decision must consider connection lifetime, reconnection, authentication, message ordering, backpressure, horizontal scaling, intermediary behavior, and fallback requirements. WebSockets should not be selected merely because an application displays frequently changing data.","principle":"Use bidirectional persistent communication when the interaction model genuinely requires it; use ordinary HTTP or other simpler mechanisms when server push is unnecessary.","objective":"Determine whether persistent bidirectional communication is required.","requirements":["Realtime interaction requirements must be explicit.","Connection lifecycle must be defined.","Reconnection and message-loss behavior must be specified.","Authorization must apply throughout the connection lifecycle."],"constraints":["Connections consume server and network resources.","Intermediaries may terminate or limit long-lived connections.","Horizontal scaling requires connection and message-routing design."],"preconditions":["Clients and infrastructure support the selected protocol.","Realtime latency requirements justify persistent connections."],"input":"Interaction model, latency requirement, connection count, message frequency, scaling topology, and security requirements.","output":"Realtime communication technology decision and connection lifecycle contract.","expected_behavior":"Clients maintain authenticated connections and exchange messages according to documented lifecycle, ordering, and failure semantics.","incorrect_code":"socket.onmessage = event => updateUI(event.data);","corrected_code":"socket.onmessage = event => validateMessage(event.data);","failure_mode":"Unvalidated realtime messages are directly trusted by the client or server.","expected_error":"Malformed or unauthorized messages may cause incorrect application state; no universal runtime exception is implied.","cause":"Message validation and authorization were omitted.","root_cause":"Realtime transport was treated as an application trust boundary rather than a communication mechanism.","solution":"Authenticate connections, validate every message against a versioned contract, authorize operations, and define reconnect/replay behavior.","edge_case":"The connection drops after the server processes a message but before the client receives confirmation.","security_implication":"Connection authentication does not replace per-operation authorization and message validation.","performance_implication":"Persistent connections can reduce repeated connection setup but consume connection and memory resources; actual capacity requires measurement.","test_case":"Disconnect a client after server-side processing and reconnect it with the same logical session.","test_input":"message_id=M1; disconnect_after_server_processing=true","expected_test_result":"The application resolves duplicate/replay behavior according to its documented message semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test reconnects, duplicate messages, malformed payloads, authentication expiry, authorization changes, backpressure, and connection exhaustion.","verification_method":"Protocol integration tests and failure-injection tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify connection, close, authentication, and protocol behavior against the selected implementation and official protocol documentation.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"WebSocket API","framework_version":"Browser/runtime dependent","version_range":"Pin server and client WebSocket implementation versions.","dependencies":["WebSocket-capable client","WebSocket-capable server"],"execution_requirements":["Realtime test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B064","D34-B070"],"depends_on":["D34-B064"],"requires":["Bidirectional realtime requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["HTTP request-response","Polling"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["WebSocket and realtime communication architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established realtime communication practice; exact runtime and server behavior must be verified against the selected implementation's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B072","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"RPC Technology","concept":"gRPC technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should gRPC be selected for service-to-service communication?","answer":"gRPC is a candidate when strongly defined service contracts, generated clients, efficient binary serialization, streaming, or internal RPC semantics are important requirements. It should be evaluated against REST/HTTP APIs according to actual interoperability and operational needs.","explanation":"The decision should consider contract ownership, language support, browser compatibility, streaming requirements, observability, gateway integration, debugging, version evolution, and organizational tooling. gRPC is not automatically superior for every service interface.","principle":"Select RPC technology according to communication contract and ecosystem requirements rather than protocol preference.","objective":"Evaluate gRPC against alternative service communication technologies using explicit requirements.","requirements":["Service contract requirements must be defined.","Supported languages and runtimes must be identified.","Compatibility and evolution strategy must be specified.","Operational tooling requirements must be understood."],"constraints":["Direct browser usage may require additional technology depending on the environment.","Generated clients introduce build/toolchain dependencies.","Protocol and schema evolution require discipline."],"preconditions":["Service-to-service or RPC-style communication is required.","The participating platforms support the selected protocol."],"input":"Service interface requirements, language matrix, latency/throughput needs, streaming requirements, and interoperability constraints.","output":"RPC technology decision and contract strategy.","expected_behavior":"Service clients and servers communicate through an explicit versioned contract with compatible serialization and error semantics.","incorrect_code":"client.call("/user", {"id": user_id})","corrected_code":"client.GetUser(GetUserRequest{user_id: user_id})","failure_mode":"Service contracts depend on undocumented internal payload structures.","expected_error":"Compatibility failures may appear as serialization or contract errors after independent deployment.","cause":"Contract evolution was not explicitly governed.","root_cause":"Communication technology was selected without defining schema and compatibility policy.","solution":"Define versioned RPC contracts, generated interfaces where appropriate, compatibility rules, and contract tests.","edge_case":"A client remains on an older contract while the server is upgraded.","security_implication":"Transport authentication and service authorization remain necessary regardless of RPC contract generation.","performance_implication":"Binary serialization and persistent transport can have performance advantages for suitable workloads, but measurements must be workload-specific.","test_case":"Run an older client contract against a newer compatible server implementation.","test_input":"client_contract=v1; server_contract=v2","expected_test_result":"Compatibility behavior matches the declared evolution policy.","testing_guidance":"Test schema evolution, unknown fields, error mapping, streaming, timeouts, retries, and authentication.","verification_method":"Contract tests plus integration tests across supported client/server versions.","verification_guidance":"Verify compatibility rules against the selected RPC implementation and serialization specification.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"gRPC","framework_version":"Pin exact gRPC release","version_range":"Use a documented compatible client/server range.","dependencies":["gRPC runtime","Protocol Buffers compiler/runtime"],"execution_requirements":["Generated service stubs and configured gRPC environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B031","D34-B058"],"depends_on":["D33-B031"],"requires":["Explicit service contract"],"extends":[],"refines":["D34-B058"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["REST/HTTP"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["RPC and contract-first service architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established RPC architecture practice; exact gRPC and Protocol Buffers behavior must be verified against official documentation for selected versions.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B073","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Serialization","concept":"Protocol Buffers technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is Protocol Buffers an appropriate serialization technology?","answer":"Protocol Buffers are appropriate when compact structured serialization, explicit schemas, generated code, and controlled schema evolution are valuable requirements, particularly for service-to-service interfaces and durable structured messages.","explanation":"The primary architectural concern is schema evolution. Field identity, compatibility rules, optionality semantics, unknown-field behavior, and generated-code lifecycle must be defined before adopting the format for durable contracts.","principle":"A serialization format is part of a compatibility contract, not merely an encoding choice.","objective":"Select and govern schema-based binary serialization according to compatibility requirements.","requirements":["Schema ownership must be defined.","Field evolution rules must be documented.","Generated-code versions must be controlled.","Compatibility tests must exist for durable contracts."],"constraints":["Schema changes can break consumers if compatibility rules are violated.","Generated artifacts create toolchain dependencies.","Human readability is lower than text-based formats."],"preconditions":["Structured schema evolution is required.","All relevant consumers can use the selected serialization tooling."],"input":"Message schema, supported languages, compatibility requirements, and deployment lifecycle.","output":"Serialization technology decision and schema evolution policy.","expected_behavior":"Messages remain decodable across all supported compatible versions according to the declared schema-evolution rules.","incorrect_code":"message User { string name = 1; }","corrected_code":"message User { string name = 1; reserved 2; }","failure_mode":"Previously used field numbers are reused for unrelated meanings.","expected_error":"Older consumers may interpret new data incorrectly or incompatible decoding may occur.","cause":"Schema history was not preserved.","root_cause":"Field identity was treated as disposable implementation detail.","solution":"Reserve removed field numbers/names and maintain explicit schema compatibility policy.","edge_case":"A field is removed and later a developer attempts to reuse its numeric identifier.","security_implication":"Serialized messages must not be trusted solely because they conform to schema; authorization and semantic validation remain necessary.","performance_implication":"Binary serialization may reduce payload size and parsing overhead for suitable workloads, but performance must be measured for the actual message shapes.","test_case":"Serialize a message using a newer schema and decode it using all supported older compatible schemas.","test_input":"schema_versions=[v1,v2,v3]","expected_test_result":"All declared compatible schema pairs decode according to the compatibility contract.","testing_guidance":"Test field addition/removal, reserved identifiers, enum evolution, unknown fields, default semantics, and generated-code compatibility.","verification_method":"Schema compatibility testing and generated-code integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify schema rules against the official Protocol Buffers language and runtime documentation.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"Protocol Buffers","framework_version":"Pin compiler and runtime versions","version_range":"Define compiler/runtime compatibility range.","dependencies":["Protocol Buffers compiler","Language runtime"],"execution_requirements":["Schema compiler and generated-code toolchain"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B072","D33-B025"],"depends_on":["D33-B025"],"requires":["Schema ownership and evolution policy"],"extends":["D34-B072"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["JSON text serialization"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Schema-based serialization and compatibility principles"],"provenance":"Protocol Buffers specification and established schema-evolution practice; exact behavior must be verified against official documentation for the selected compiler/runtime version.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B074","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Schema Governance","concept":"Schema registry technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is a schema registry justified for an event or message ecosystem?","answer":"A schema registry is justified when many producers and consumers share durable message schemas and centralized schema discovery, compatibility checking, version governance, or controlled evolution is required.","explanation":"A registry provides governance around schemas; it does not by itself guarantee that producers and consumers behave correctly. The architecture should define ownership, compatibility modes, registration workflow, version lifecycle, and enforcement points.","principle":"Centralize schema governance when independently evolving producers and consumers make compatibility management a system-level requirement.","objective":"Determine whether centralized schema governance is needed.","requirements":["Schema ownership must be explicit.","Compatibility policy must be defined.","Registration and validation workflow must be controlled.","Consumer compatibility must be testable."],"constraints":["Registry availability can become an operational dependency.","Schema compatibility rules cannot guarantee semantic compatibility.","Poor governance can turn the registry into an administrative bottleneck."],"preconditions":["Multiple independently evolving message producers/consumers exist.","Schemas have durable compatibility significance."],"input":"Number of producers/consumers, schema lifecycle, compatibility requirements, and deployment independence.","output":"Schema registry decision and governance model.","expected_behavior":"Message schemas are discoverable, versioned, and validated according to explicit compatibility rules.","incorrect_code":"publish(topic, arbitrary_payload)","corrected_code":"schema = registry.resolve(schema_id); validate(payload, schema); publish(schema_id, payload)","failure_mode":"A producer publishes a payload incompatible with consumers while the schema system is unaware.","expected_error":"Consumer decoding or semantic validation may fail.","cause":"Schema governance was bypassed.","root_cause":"Technology adoption was not connected to enforcement points.","solution":"Integrate schema validation and compatibility checks into producer, CI, or deployment workflows as appropriate.","edge_case":"A schema is technically backward-compatible but changes business meaning.","security_implication":"Schema registries and message metadata may contain sensitive structural information and require access control.","performance_implication":"Schema lookup and validation add processing overhead; caching and deployment-time resolution can reduce runtime dependency where appropriate.","test_case":"Attempt to register an incompatible schema and publish a message using an invalid schema version.","test_input":"new_schema=INCOMPATIBLE; publish=true","expected_test_result":"The incompatible schema or message is rejected according to governance policy.","testing_guidance":"Test compatibility modes, registration authorization, schema rollback, consumer compatibility, and registry unavailability.","verification_method":"Schema compatibility tests and CI/deployment policy verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify registry behavior and compatibility modes against the selected registry's official documentation.","language":"Java","language_version":"21","framework":"Schema registry","framework_version":"Pin selected registry/client versions","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["Schema registry","Message serialization format"],"execution_requirements":["Registry and message-system test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B073","D34-B064"],"depends_on":["D34-B064","D34-B073"],"requires":["Multiple independently evolving message contracts"],"extends":[],"refines":["D33-B025"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Ad hoc schema distribution"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Schema governance architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established event/schema governance practice; exact registry behavior must be verified against the selected product's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B075","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Database Migration","concept":"Database migration technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated database migration technology be used?","answer":"A migration technology is appropriate when database schema changes must be versioned, repeatable, ordered, reviewable, and consistently applied across environments and deployments.","explanation":"Migration tooling converts schema evolution into controlled artifacts tied to application versions. The architecture must distinguish backward-compatible expansion from destructive changes and define rollback or roll-forward behavior.","principle":"Database schema evolution should be treated as versioned deployment state rather than ad hoc manual modification.","objective":"Provide deterministic and auditable database schema evolution.","requirements":["Migration ordering must be deterministic.","Schema ownership must be explicit.","Deployment compatibility windows must be defined.","Destructive operations require controlled handling."],"constraints":["A migration can be irreversible.","Application and schema versions may temporarily coexist.","Large data transformations may require separate operational procedures."],"preconditions":["Persistent schema evolution exists.","Multiple deployment environments require consistent database state."],"input":"Schema changes, deployment versions, compatibility requirements, and migration constraints.","output":"Migration technology decision and migration lifecycle.","expected_behavior":"Each environment reaches the intended schema state through ordered, auditable migration artifacts.","incorrect_code":"db.execute("ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN legacy_field")","corrected_code":"migration.apply("2026_09_expand_users"); deploy_compatible_code(); migration.apply("2026_10_contract_users")","failure_mode":"Application deployment requires a schema change that breaks the currently running application version.","expected_error":"Runtime database errors such as missing columns or incompatible constraints may occur.","cause":"Schema and application changes were deployed as one incompatible step.","root_cause":"No compatibility transition was designed.","solution":"Use expand-and-contract migration patterns where multiple application versions may coexist.","edge_case":"A migration partially applies before deployment failure.","security_implication":"Migration credentials should have narrowly scoped privileges and should not be embedded in application runtime credentials.","performance_implication":"Large migrations can lock tables or consume substantial I/O; operational impact must be measured and planned.","test_case":"Apply migrations to an isolated database, run both old and new compatible application versions, then complete the contract phase.","test_input":"schema_versions=[old,expanded,new]","expected_test_result":"All supported transition states satisfy documented compatibility requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test clean install, upgrade, partial failure, retry, rollback/roll-forward, large-data behavior, and concurrent application versions.","verification_method":"Migration integration tests and schema-state verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify resulting schema against the expected version and test compatibility with supported application versions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Migration tooling","framework_version":"Pin exact migration-tool version","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["Database","Migration tool"],"execution_requirements":["Isolated test database"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B026","D34-B056"],"depends_on":["D33-B026"],"requires":["Versioned database schema"],"extends":["D33-B026"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Manual database changes"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Versioned database migration principles"],"provenance":"Established database deployment practice; exact migration-tool semantics must be verified against the selected tool's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B076","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Connection Management","concept":"Database connection pooling technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is database connection pooling required?","answer":"Connection pooling is appropriate when repeated database access would otherwise create excessive connection setup cost or when a service needs bounded, reusable database connections under concurrent workload.","explanation":"A pool manages a finite set of connections and lets requests reuse them. Pool sizing must account for database capacity, application concurrency, transaction duration, timeouts, and deployment topology. A larger pool is not automatically better.","principle":"Bound and reuse database connections according to database capacity and application concurrency rather than maximizing pool size.","objective":"Select and configure connection pooling according to workload and database limits.","requirements":["Database connection limits must be known.","Application concurrency must be characterized.","Acquire, idle, and lifetime timeouts must be defined.","Transaction boundaries must be explicit."],"constraints":["Oversized pools can overload the database.","Stale connections may fail after network or server events.","Transactions must not leak across pooled connection reuse."],"preconditions":["The application performs repeated database operations.","Database connection lifecycle can be safely pooled."],"input":"Concurrency, database limits, transaction duration, timeout requirements, and deployment topology.","output":"Connection-pooling decision and sizing/configuration policy.","expected_behavior":"Requests acquire bounded connections, complete transactions, and reliably return connections to the pool.","incorrect_code":"connection = db.connect(); query(connection);","corrected_code":"with pool.acquire() as connection:\n    query(connection)","failure_mode":"A connection is not returned to the pool after an exception.","expected_error":"Pool exhaustion may eventually prevent new requests from acquiring connections.","cause":"Connection lifecycle was not scoped to guaranteed cleanup.","root_cause":"Resource ownership was not explicitly modeled.","solution":"Use structured acquisition/release semantics and configure bounded pool limits and timeouts.","edge_case":"The database becomes unavailable while pooled connections remain open.","security_implication":"Pooled connections must preserve tenant and authorization isolation; transaction/session state must not leak between requests.","performance_implication":"Pooling can reduce connection-establishment overhead, but excessive pool size can reduce database stability.","test_case":"Generate concurrent requests exceeding the pool size and force query failures during execution.","test_input":"concurrency=20; pool_size=5; query_failures=true","expected_test_result":"Excess requests wait or fail according to configured timeout policy and connections are eventually reusable.","testing_guidance":"Test exhaustion, timeout, connection invalidation, transaction cleanup, cancellation, and database restart.","verification_method":"Load/concurrency testing plus resource-lifecycle verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify pool behavior and database limits against the selected driver/database documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Database driver/pool","framework_version":"Pin driver and pool versions","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["Database driver","Database server"],"execution_requirements":["Database test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B021","D34-B056"],"depends_on":["D34-B056"],"requires":["Bounded database connection requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Per-request connection creation"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Database resource-pooling principles"],"provenance":"Established database engineering practice; exact pool behavior must be verified against the selected driver and database documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B077","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"ORM and Query Construction","concept":"ORM technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is an ORM appropriate for an application data-access layer?","answer":"An ORM is appropriate when domain models map substantially to relational persistence and the team benefits from managed object mapping, query composition, unit-of-work patterns, and migration integration. It should not be treated as a replacement for understanding relational query behavior.","explanation":"ORM selection should consider query complexity, transaction semantics, generated SQL visibility, performance tuning, schema control, team expertise, and database-specific features. Complex analytical or highly optimized queries may require direct SQL or specialized data-access paths.","principle":"Use an ORM to reduce repetitive mapping and persistence code while retaining explicit control over transactions and important database behavior.","objective":"Evaluate ORM adoption against domain-model and database-access requirements.","requirements":["Transaction boundaries must be explicit.","Generated SQL must be inspectable where performance matters.","N+1 and loading behavior must be understood.","Schema ownership must remain explicit."],"constraints":["Abstraction can hide expensive queries.","ORM behavior varies by framework/version.","Complex database-specific operations may bypass the ORM."],"preconditions":["Relational persistence is required.","Domain-to-relational mapping provides sufficient value."],"input":"Domain model, query complexity, transaction requirements, database features, and team capabilities.","output":"ORM technology decision and data-access policy.","expected_behavior":"ORM operations preserve transaction and data-access semantics while allowing controlled optimization for complex queries.","incorrect_code":"for user in users:\n    print(user.orders)","corrected_code":"users = query_users_with_orders_eagerly(); process(users)","failure_mode":"Lazy loading produces one database query per parent entity.","expected_error":"No mandatory exception; performance degradation may occur through excessive queries.","cause":"Object traversal concealed database access.","root_cause":"ORM abstraction was used without understanding loading semantics.","solution":"Define loading strategy, inspect generated queries, and use explicit query patterns for high-volume operations.","edge_case":"A query that is efficient for one dataset size becomes expensive after growth.","security_implication":"ORM queries must still enforce authorization, tenant boundaries, and safe parameterization.","performance_implication":"ORM convenience can reduce development effort but generated query behavior must be measured for critical paths.","test_case":"Execute a representative parent-child query and count database statements under expected data volume.","test_input":"parents=100; children_per_parent=10","expected_test_result":"Observed query count and latency remain within documented requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test transaction isolation, lazy/eager loading, generated SQL, pagination, bulk operations, and authorization filters.","verification_method":"Integration testing with SQL/query inspection and representative data.","verification_guidance":"Verify ORM behavior against official framework documentation and actual generated queries.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"SQLAlchemy","framework_version":"Pin exact supported release","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["SQLAlchemy","Database driver","Relational database"],"execution_requirements":["Configured relational database"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B076","D34-B022"],"depends_on":["D34-B076"],"requires":["Relational persistence"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Direct SQL data access"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["ORM and relational data-access architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established ORM engineering practice; exact SQLAlchemy behavior must be verified against official documentation for the selected release.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B078","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Embedded Database","concept":"SQLite technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is an embedded relational database such as SQLite appropriate?","answer":"SQLite is appropriate when an application needs a transactional relational database embedded in the application or local device, especially for local-first, single-process, desktop, mobile, testing, or bounded deployments where an independent database server is unnecessary.","explanation":"SQLite provides relational transactions and SQL without requiring a separate database service. Selection must account for concurrency, filesystem durability, deployment topology, write contention, backup requirements, and workload size.","principle":"Use embedded relational storage when local ownership and operational simplicity are requirements and the workload fits the database's concurrency model.","objective":"Determine whether an embedded database satisfies application persistence requirements.","requirements":["Data ownership and filesystem lifecycle must be defined.","Concurrency expectations must be known.","Backup and recovery must be specified.","Durability requirements must be explicit."],"constraints":["Write concurrency differs from client-server database architectures.","Filesystem and process lifecycle affect availability.","A single local database file is not automatically a multi-node database.","Multi-tenant or shared-server requirements may require different topology."],"preconditions":["The workload fits embedded relational storage semantics.","The application can control the database file lifecycle."],"input":"Deployment topology, concurrency, data volume, durability, backup, and local/offline requirements.","output":"Embedded-database technology decision and operational policy.","expected_behavior":"The application uses transactional local relational storage with explicit locking, backup, and recovery behavior.","incorrect_code":"conn.execute("SELECT * FROM records"); conn.execute("UPDATE records SET value='x'")","corrected_code":"with conn:\n    conn.execute("UPDATE records SET value=? WHERE id=?", (value, record_id))","failure_mode":"Multiple statements that must succeed together are executed without an explicit transaction boundary.","expected_error":"Partial state may remain after an interruption or failure.","cause":"Transaction semantics were left implicit.","root_cause":"The database was treated as a file rather than a transactional system.","solution":"Define transaction boundaries explicitly and configure durability/concurrency behavior according to workload requirements.","edge_case":"Two application processes attempt conflicting writes concurrently.","security_implication":"Filesystem permissions and encryption strategy must protect the database file and its backups.","performance_implication":"SQLite can be effective for bounded local workloads; performance and concurrency must be measured for the target device and workload.","test_case":"Run concurrent reads/writes and interrupt a transaction during commit.","test_input":"processes=2; concurrent_writes=true; interruption=true","expected_test_result":"Database integrity remains valid and transaction atomicity follows the configured guarantees.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent access, locking, crash recovery, backup, restore, migrations, and filesystem failure scenarios.","verification_method":"SQLite integration tests and recovery testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify journaling, transaction, locking, and backup behavior against official SQLite documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"sqlite3","framework_version":"Python-version dependent","version_range":"SQLite version depends on linked runtime; record exact runtime SQLite version during implementation.","dependencies":[