{"record_id":"D34-B079","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Relational Database","concept":"PostgreSQL technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is PostgreSQL an appropriate primary relational database technology?","answer":"PostgreSQL is a candidate when an application requires a full client-server relational database with strong transactional semantics, rich SQL capabilities, concurrency support, extensibility, and mature operational tooling.","explanation":"The selection should be based on workload, consistency, schema, concurrency, operational, extension, backup, replication, and deployment requirements. Choosing PostgreSQL does not by itself establish a scalable or highly available architecture; those properties require explicit topology and verification.","principle":"Select a relational database by required transactional and data-management capabilities, then design topology and operations separately.","objective":"Evaluate PostgreSQL against primary relational persistence requirements.","requirements":["Transaction and isolation requirements must be defined.","Schema and query workload must be characterized.","Backup and recovery requirements must be explicit.","Operational topology must be designed separately from product selection."],"constraints":["Database operations require administration and lifecycle management.","Extensions can create portability constraints.","High availability requires additional architecture and verification."],"preconditions":["A client-server relational datastore is required.","Operational ownership is available."],"input":"Transactional workload, schema complexity, concurrency, recovery objectives, and operational requirements.","output":"Relational database technology decision and operational architecture.","expected_behavior":"Application transactions and relational constraints behave according to the documented database contract.","incorrect_code":"conn.execute("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = ?", (source,))\nconn.execute("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = ?", (target,))","corrected_code":"with conn.transaction():\n    conn.execute("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = ?", (source,))\n    conn.execute("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = ?", (target,))","failure_mode":"Related financial state changes are not committed atomically.","expected_error":"A failure between statements can leave inconsistent application state if transaction semantics are not used.","cause":"Database operations were not grouped into the required transaction.","root_cause":"Persistence requirements were not mapped to transaction boundaries.","solution":"Define transaction boundaries around logically atomic state transitions and verify isolation requirements.","edge_case":"The application process terminates between the two updates.","security_implication":"Database roles should use least privilege and application authorization must remain separate from database connectivity.","performance_implication":"Database performance depends on schema, indexes, queries, configuration, hardware, and workload; no generic benchmark should be assumed.","test_case":"Force failure between two logically atomic updates and verify rollback.","test_input":"transaction_failure=between_updates","expected_test_result":"Either all required changes commit or none commit according to the transaction contract.","testing_guidance":"Test transaction rollback, isolation, deadlocks, indexes, backups, restore, replication where used, and schema migrations.","verification_method":"Database integration and failure-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual transaction and configuration behavior against official PostgreSQL documentation and the selected deployment version.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"psycopg","framework_version":"Pin exact supported release","version_range":"Pin PostgreSQL server and client-driver versions.","dependencies":["PostgreSQL","PostgreSQL client driver"],"execution_requirements":["PostgreSQL test instance"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B076","D34-B078","D33-B002"],"depends_on":["D33-B002"],"requires":["Client-server relational database requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["SQLite embedded database"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["PostgreSQL relational database architecture principles"],"provenance":"PostgreSQL official documentation and established relational database practice; exact behavior must be verified against the selected PostgreSQL and driver versions.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B080","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"In-Memory Data Store","concept":"Redis technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is Redis an appropriate technology dependency?","answer":"Redis can be appropriate for workloads requiring fast in-memory data structures, ephemeral caching, coordination primitives, rate limiting, or other explicitly defined in-memory use cases where its durability and consistency characteristics match the requirement.","explanation":"Redis should not automatically become the source of truth merely because it is fast. The architecture must specify whether Redis is a cache, coordination mechanism, transient datastore, or durable datastore and must define persistence, eviction, failure, recovery, and consistency expectations.","principle":"Assign Redis an explicit data role and never infer durability or correctness guarantees that the selected configuration does not provide.","objective":"Determine whether Redis capabilities justify introducing an in-memory datastore.","requirements":["The Redis data role must be explicit.","Durability and recovery requirements must be defined.","Eviction and memory limits must be understood.","Source-of-truth ownership must be documented."],"constraints":["Memory is finite.","Eviction can remove data depending on configuration.","Distributed coordination requires careful failure semantics.","Cache correctness depends on invalidation and freshness policy."],"preconditions":["The workload benefits from in-memory data structures or caching.","The system can tolerate the selected failure and persistence semantics."],"input":"Caching, coordination, rate-limiting, latency, durability, and memory requirements.","output":"Redis technology decision and explicit data-role contract.","expected_behavior":"Redis operations remain within the defined role and do not silently become an authoritative source of business state.","incorrect_code":"redis.set("account_balance", balance)","corrected_code":"primary_db.update_balance(account_id, balance); redis.delete(cache_key(account_id))","failure_mode":"Authoritative business state exists only in an evictable cache.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime exception; eviction or restart can cause data loss or inconsistent business state.","cause":"Cache and source-of-truth responsibilities were conflated.","root_cause":"Technology capability was mistaken for business-state durability.","solution":"Keep authoritative state in the designated source of truth and use Redis only for explicitly governed derived/transient state unless durable Redis semantics are deliberately designed and verified.","edge_case":"Redis becomes unavailable while cached data is required for request acceleration.","security_implication":"Keys and values must respect tenant and authorization boundaries; Redis network access and credentials require strict controls.","performance_implication":"In-memory access can reduce latency for suitable workloads, but actual results depend on data size, access pattern, network topology, and configuration.","test_case":"Invalidate or stop Redis and verify that the application can recover from the authoritative source according to the fallback contract.","test_input":"redis_available=false; primary_db_available=true","expected_test_result":"Business correctness remains intact while cache-dependent performance features degrade according to the documented fallback behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test cache misses, eviction, restart, network failure, stale values, stampede prevention, tenant isolation, and fallback behavior.","verification_method":"Integration and failure-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify selected Redis commands, persistence configuration, eviction behavior, and client semantics against official documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"redis-py","framework_version":"Pin exact supported release","version_range":"Pin Redis server and client versions.","dependencies":["Redis","Redis client"],"execution_requirements":["Redis test instance and authoritative datastore"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B022","D34-B076","D34-B079"],"depends_on":["D34-B022"],"requires":["Explicit in-memory data requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":["D34-B022"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Database-only caching"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["In-memory datastore and cache architecture principles"],"provenance":"Redis official documentation and established caching/coordination architecture practice; exact behavior depends on selected Redis version and configuration.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B081","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Secrets Management","concept":"Secrets management technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated secrets-management technology be used instead of application configuration files?","answer":"Dedicated secrets management is appropriate when credentials, private keys, tokens, database passwords, or other sensitive values require controlled access, rotation, auditing, centralized lifecycle management, or separation from ordinary configuration.","explanation":"Secrets must be distinguished from non-sensitive configuration. The technology decision should address storage protection, access control, rotation, versioning, retrieval, auditability, availability, and application startup or runtime behavior. Secrets should not be embedded in source code or ordinary configuration artifacts.","principle":"Treat secrets as security-controlled data with an explicit lifecycle rather than as ordinary application configuration.","objective":"Select technology that provides the required protection and lifecycle controls for sensitive configuration values.","requirements":["Secret ownership must be defined.","Access control must be explicit.","Rotation and revocation requirements must be documented.","Secret exposure and audit requirements must be defined."],"constraints":["A secrets service can become a runtime dependency.","Secret availability affects application startup or operation.","Rotation can require application reload or dynamic refresh mechanisms."],"preconditions":["The application handles credentials or other sensitive values.","Protection requirements exceed safe local configuration handling."],"input":"Secret types, threat model, access patterns, rotation requirements, deployment topology, and availability requirements.","output":"Secrets-management technology decision and lifecycle policy.","expected_behavior":"Applications retrieve authorized secrets through controlled mechanisms without storing secret material in source repositories.","incorrect_code":"DB_PASSWORD = "production-password"","corrected_code":"DB_PASSWORD = secret_provider.get("database/password")","failure_mode":"A production credential is committed to source control or packaged into an application artifact.","expected_error":"No deterministic runtime error; unauthorized disclosure may occur through repository or artifact access.","cause":"Sensitive configuration was treated as ordinary code/configuration.","root_cause":"No explicit secret-management boundary existed.","solution":"Move secret material into a controlled secret store and grant applications only the minimum required retrieval permissions.","edge_case":"The secret-management service is unavailable during application startup.","security_implication":"Secret access requires least privilege, authentication, audit controls, encryption, and careful handling to avoid accidental logging.","performance_implication":"Secret retrieval introduces network or cryptographic overhead; caching and refresh strategy should be designed according to rotation requirements.","test_case":"Attempt secret retrieval using an authorized identity and an unauthorized identity, then rotate the secret.","test_input":"authorized=true; unauthorized=true; rotation=true","expected_test_result":"Authorized retrieval succeeds, unauthorized retrieval is denied, and rotation follows the documented refresh policy.","testing_guidance":"Test access denial, rotation, revocation, service outage, startup behavior, audit records, and accidental secret exposure in logs.","verification_method":"Security integration tests and secret-lifecycle verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify access, rotation, storage, and audit semantics against the selected secrets technology's official documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Secrets management provider","framework_version":"Pin selected provider/client versions.","version_range":"Technology-specific.","dependencies":["Secret-management system","Application identity mechanism"],"execution_requirements":["Configured secret-management test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B029","D34-B053"],"depends_on":["D34-B029"],"requires":["Sensitive configuration requirements"],"extends":["D34-B029"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Static configuration files"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Secrets-management and least-privilege architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established security engineering practice; exact secret storage, access, rotation, and audit behavior must be verified against the selected technology's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B082","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Identity Provider","concept":"OIDC identity-provider technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should an application integrate with an external OpenID Connect identity provider?","answer":"An OIDC identity provider is appropriate when centralized authentication, single sign-on, federated identity, standardized token issuance, or organization-managed user lifecycle is required across applications.","explanation":"OIDC technology addresses authentication and identity federation; it does not automatically determine application authorization. The architecture must define issuer trust, client registration, redirect handling, token validation, session behavior, claims mapping, logout semantics, and authorization boundaries.","principle":"Separate authentication identity from application authorization and validate tokens according to the trusted issuer contract.","objective":"Select identity-provider technology based on authentication and federation requirements.","requirements":["Trusted issuer(s) must be identified.","Client registration and redirect URIs must be controlled.","Token validation requirements must be explicit.","Authorization mapping must be separately defined."],"constraints":["Identity-provider availability can affect authentication flows.","Claims may differ between providers.","Long-lived sessions require explicit token/session lifecycle management."],"preconditions":["Centralized or federated authentication is required.","The application can integrate with an OIDC-compatible provider."],"input":"Authentication requirements, user lifecycle, federation needs, token claims, session policy, and application authorization model.","output":"Identity-provider decision and authentication integration architecture.","expected_behavior":"Only tokens issued by the configured trusted issuer and valid for the intended client/audience are accepted.","incorrect_code":"decode_jwt_without_validation(token)","corrected_code":"claims = oidc_validator.validate(token, issuer=TRUSTED_ISSUER, audience=EXPECTED_AUDIENCE)","failure_mode":"A token is decoded and trusted without validating its signature, issuer, audience, or lifetime.","expected_error":"Invalid credentials may be accepted; failure may manifest as unauthorized access rather than a runtime exception.","cause":"Token parsing was confused with token validation.","root_cause":"Authentication trust requirements were not implemented as explicit verification steps.","solution":"Validate issuer, signature, audience, expiration, and relevant claims using a maintained OIDC/JWT implementation.","edge_case":"The identity provider rotates signing keys while applications still hold cached key material.","security_implication":"Issuer trust, token validation, redirect URI protection, session security, and claim handling are security-critical.","performance_implication":"Token validation and discovery/key retrieval add overhead; key caching must respect issuer rotation semantics.","test_case":"Submit valid, expired, wrong-audience, wrong-issuer, and invalid-signature tokens.","test_input":"token_cases=[valid,expired,wrong_audience,wrong_issuer,invalid_signature]","expected_test_result":"Only the valid token satisfying all configured trust conditions is accepted.","testing_guidance":"Test key rotation, token expiry, issuer/audience validation, redirect handling, logout, replay considerations, and claim changes.","verification_method":"OIDC integration and security tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify protocol behavior against the OpenID Connect specification and selected identity provider documentation.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"OpenID Connect","framework_version":"Protocol/version specific","version_range":"Use an explicitly supported OIDC library and protocol profile.","dependencies":["OIDC identity provider","OIDC client/validation library"],"execution_requirements":["OIDC test issuer or provider"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B051","D33-B051"],"depends_on":["D33-B051"],"requires":["Federated authentication requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Application-local authentication"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["OpenID Connect authentication architecture principles"],"provenance":"OpenID Connect specification and established identity architecture practice; provider-specific behavior must be verified against official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B083","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Observability Telemetry","concept":"OpenTelemetry technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is OpenTelemetry appropriate as a telemetry instrumentation technology?","answer":"OpenTelemetry is appropriate when applications need standardized instrumentation and collection of traces, metrics, and logs across heterogeneous services and runtimes, especially when telemetry must remain portable across observability backends.","explanation":"OpenTelemetry provides instrumentation and telemetry-processing capabilities, while storage, querying, alerting, and visualization are commonly provided by other systems. The architecture should define signal ownership, correlation identifiers, sampling, privacy, export paths, and failure behavior.","principle":"Separate telemetry generation and transport from telemetry storage and analysis, and standardize instrumentation where cross-system correlation matters.","objective":"Select a standardized telemetry technology for distributed observability requirements.","requirements":["Required telemetry signals must be defined.","Correlation across boundaries must be specified.","Sensitive-data handling must be explicit.","Export and retention requirements must be documented."],"constraints":["Instrumentation can add runtime overhead.","Telemetry volume can grow rapidly.","Telemetry systems must not become an unbounded source of sensitive-data exposure."],"preconditions":["Distributed or heterogeneous workloads require correlated telemetry.","A supported instrumentation path exists."],"input":"Observability requirements, service topology, telemetry signals, privacy constraints, and backend requirements.","output":"Telemetry instrumentation decision and signal architecture.","expected_behavior":"Services emit correlated telemetry using consistent semantic conventions and controlled export behavior.","incorrect_code":"logger.info("password=%s", password)","corrected_code":"logger.info("authentication_failed", extra={"user_id":safe_user_id})","failure_mode":"Sensitive values are emitted into telemetry because instrumentation has no data-classification policy.","expected_error":"No universal runtime error; sensitive information may be persisted in telemetry systems.","cause":"Telemetry was treated as harmless diagnostic output.","root_cause":"Observability requirements lacked privacy and data-minimization controls.","solution":"Define telemetry data classification, redaction, attribute allowlists, retention, and access controls before enabling broad instrumentation.","edge_case":"A downstream telemetry collector becomes unavailable.","security_implication":"Telemetry can contain sensitive operational or user information and requires access control, retention, and redaction policies.","performance_implication":"Instrumentation and export add CPU, memory, network, and storage overhead; impact must be measured for critical workloads.","test_case":"Generate representative application telemetry containing intentionally sensitive fields and verify redaction before export.","test_input":"telemetry={user_id:"U1",password:"SECRET",event:"login"}","expected_test_result":"Secret material is absent from exported telemetry while permitted correlation fields remain available.","testing_guidance":"Test redaction, exporter failure, sampling, correlation propagation, high-volume telemetry, and sensitive-data access controls.","verification_method":"Instrumentation integration tests plus telemetry inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify instrumentation/export semantics against official OpenTelemetry documentation and selected SDK versions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"OpenTelemetry","framework_version":"Pin exact SDK release","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["OpenTelemetry SDK","Telemetry collector/backend"],"execution_requirements":["Configured telemetry test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B054","D34-B054"],"depends_on":["D33-B054"],"requires":["Cross-boundary observability requirement"],"extends":["D33-B054"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Application-specific telemetry only"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["OpenTelemetry and distributed observability architecture principles"],"provenance":"OpenTelemetry official documentation and established observability practice; exact SDK semantics must be verified against selected versions.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B084","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Metrics Storage","concept":"Metrics backend technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should a dedicated metrics storage system be selected?","answer":"A dedicated metrics backend is appropriate when the system requires persistent numerical telemetry, time-window queries, aggregation, alerting inputs, retention policies, and operational analysis at a scale or cardinality that ordinary application logs cannot provide efficiently.","explanation":"Metrics systems are optimized around measurements and labels rather than arbitrary event text. Technology selection must consider metric cardinality, scrape or push model, retention, aggregation, downsampling, high availability, query semantics, and alerting integration.","principle":"Model operational measurements as structured metrics when numerical time-series analysis is required.","objective":"Select metrics storage based on observability workload requirements.","requirements":["Metric names and dimensions must be governed.","Cardinality limits must be understood.","Retention requirements must be explicit.","Alerting and query requirements must be defined."],"constraints":["Unbounded labels can create excessive cardinality.","Metrics are not a substitute for detailed event logs or traces.","Retention and aggregation affect historical accuracy."],"preconditions":["Numerical time-series telemetry is required.","Metric semantics are defined."],"input":"Metrics schema, cardinality, ingestion model, retention, query, and alerting requirements.","output":"Metrics backend technology decision and metric-governance policy.","expected_behavior":"Metrics are ingested and queried according to explicit dimensionality, retention, and aggregation rules.","incorrect_code":"metric.labels(user_id).set(value)","corrected_code":"metric.labels(service, operation, status).set(value)","failure_mode":"A high-cardinality identifier is used as a metric label without workload analysis.","expected_error":"No universal runtime exception; storage/query performance and resource usage can degrade.","cause":"Metric dimensions were selected without cardinality governance.","root_cause":"Telemetry schema was designed as event logging rather than aggregate measurement.","solution":"Use bounded dimensions for metrics and place detailed identifiers in logs or traces where appropriate.","edge_case":"A previously bounded label gains a new unbounded value source after a feature change.","security_implication":"Metric labels must not expose sensitive identifiers or confidential values.","performance_implication":"Cardinality strongly affects storage and query resource usage; actual impact must be measured.","test_case":"Generate metrics with bounded and intentionally unbounded labels and inspect cardinality policy enforcement.","test_input":"labels=[service,operation,status,user_id]","expected_test_result":"Governed dimensions are accepted while prohibited high-cardinality dimensions are rejected or transformed according to policy.","testing_guidance":"Test cardinality growth, retention, aggregation, ingestion failures, query windows, and alerting correctness.","verification_method":"Telemetry integration and workload tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify backend cardinality, retention, and query behavior against official documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Metrics backend","framework_version":"Pin selected backend/client versions","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["Metrics instrumentation","Metrics backend"],"execution_requirements":["Metrics test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B062","D34-B083"],"depends_on":["D34-B083"],"requires":["Structured metrics requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":["D34-B062"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Unstructured application logs"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Metrics system architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established observability practice; exact metrics backend semantics must be verified against selected technology documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B085","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Log Storage","concept":"Centralized log storage technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is centralized log storage justified for an application platform?","answer":"Centralized log storage is justified when multiple processes or hosts produce logs that must be searched, correlated, retained, audited, or analyzed from a common operational interface.","explanation":"Centralization separates log generation from log persistence and analysis. The architecture should define structured fields, retention, indexing, access control, ingestion failure behavior, tenant separation, and data classification. Logs should not become the authoritative record for business transactions merely because they are centrally stored.","principle":"Centralize operational logs when cross-process analysis requires it, while preserving explicit business and audit records separately.","objective":"Select log-storage technology according to operational search, retention, and correlation requirements.","requirements":["Log schema must be defined.","Retention and deletion requirements must be explicit.","Access controls must be specified.","Sensitive-data handling must be governed."],"constraints":["High-volume logs can create substantial storage and indexing cost.","Logs may be incomplete or duplicated.","Centralized logging introduces an ingestion dependency."],"preconditions":["Multiple components generate operational logs.","Cross-component investigation requires centralized access."],"input":"Log volume, query patterns, retention, compliance, correlation, and security requirements.","output":"Centralized logging technology decision and ingestion policy.","expected_behavior":"Structured logs are collected and searchable without compromising source-system correctness or security boundaries.","incorrect_code":"logger.info(f"request={request}")","corrected_code":"logger.info("request_completed", extra={"request_id":request_id,"status":status})","failure_mode":"Entire request objects containing sensitive information are logged.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime exception; sensitive data can enter persistent log storage.","cause":"Logging lacked a controlled schema and data classification policy.","root_cause":"Operational diagnostics were allowed to capture arbitrary application objects.","solution":"Use structured, allowlisted fields and redact sensitive data before persistence.","edge_case":"The logging backend is unavailable during a critical request.","security_implication":"Centralized logs require strict access control, tenant isolation, retention, and secret/PII redaction.","performance_implication":"Logging and indexing consume CPU, memory, network, and storage resources; critical paths require measurement and bounded logging.","test_case":"Generate logs with sensitive and non-sensitive fields and simulate backend unavailability.","test_input":"backend_available=false; sensitive_field=true","expected_test_result":"Application behavior follows the documented logging-failure policy and prohibited sensitive fields are not emitted.","testing_guidance":"Test ingestion failure, batching, duplication, retention, redaction, query authorization, and correlation identifiers.","verification_method":"Logging integration tests and security inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify ingestion and retention behavior against the selected logging technology's official documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Structured logging","framework_version":"Runtime-specific","version_range":"Pin log collector and backend versions.","dependencies":["Log collector","Log storage/search backend"],"execution_requirements":["Centralized logging test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B083","D34-B084","D33-B053"],"depends_on":["D33-B054"],"requires":["Centralized operational logging requirement"],"extends":["D34-B054"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Local-only logging"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Centralized structured logging architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established observability and logging practice; exact collector/storage behavior must be verified against selected technology documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B086","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Distributed Tracing","concept":"Distributed tracing technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is distributed tracing technology justified?","answer":"Distributed tracing is justified when requests or workflows cross multiple services, asynchronous boundaries, or external dependencies and engineers need causal timing and dependency visibility that ordinary logs and aggregate metrics cannot provide alone.","explanation":"Tracing models a logical operation as related spans across system boundaries. The architecture must define context propagation, sampling, privacy, trace retention, asynchronous linkage, and correlation with logs and metrics.","principle":"Use distributed traces to reconstruct cross-boundary execution paths while keeping trace data governed and sampled according to operational requirements.","objective":"Determine whether tracing provides necessary cross-component diagnostic information.","requirements":["Trace context propagation must be defined.","Sampling policy must be explicit.","Span attributes must be governed.","Correlation with other telemetry must be supported."],"constraints":["Trace volume can become large.","Asynchronous workflows require explicit context-linking semantics.","Trace data may contain sensitive information."],"preconditions":["Execution crosses boundaries where causal reconstruction is valuable.","Instrumentation support exists for relevant components."],"input":"System topology, diagnostic requirements, request paths, async workflows, and telemetry constraints.","output":"Distributed-tracing technology decision and propagation policy.","expected_behavior":"Related spans can be correlated into logical traces without exposing prohibited sensitive data.","incorrect_code":"span.set_attribute("authorization", request.headers["Authorization"])","corrected_code":"span.set_attribute("request_id", request_id); span.set_attribute("operation", operation_name)","failure_mode":"Credentials or sensitive payloads are attached directly to spans.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime error; sensitive data may become persistent trace data.","cause":"Trace attributes were treated as unrestricted debugging fields.","root_cause":"No telemetry data-classification policy existed.","solution":"Define safe span attributes, redaction, sampling, access controls, and retention before broad tracing.","edge_case":"An asynchronous job continues after the originating HTTP request has ended.","security_implication":"Trace data requires authorization, redaction, tenant isolation, and retention controls.","performance_implication":"Instrumentation and span export add overhead and network/storage load; sampling and batching should be designed and measured.","test_case":"Trace a request through synchronous and asynchronous components and verify context linkage without secret propagation.","test_input":"workflow=[HTTP,service_A,queue,worker_B]; secret_present=true","expected_test_result":"The trace preserves required causal relationships while prohibited sensitive values are absent.","testing_guidance":"Test propagation, async boundaries, sampling, exporter failure, trace joins, and sensitive-data controls.","verification_method":"End-to-end tracing tests and trace inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify propagation and instrumentation behavior against OpenTelemetry and selected backend documentation.","language":"Java","language_version":"21","framework":"OpenTelemetry","framework_version":"Pin exact SDK release","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["Tracing SDK","Telemetry collector/backend"],"execution_requirements":["Instrumented distributed test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B083","D34-B085","D33-B054"],"depends_on":["D34-B083"],"requires":["Cross-boundary causal observability requirement"],"extends":["D34-B083"],"refines":["D33-B054"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Log-only diagnosis"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed tracing architecture principles"],"provenance":"OpenTelemetry tracing concepts and established distributed-systems observability practice; exact SDK/backend behavior must be verified against official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B087","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Backup Technology","concept":"Backup technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should dedicated backup technology be introduced for persistent application data?","answer":"Dedicated backup technology is required when data recovery objectives demand controlled copies, retention, scheduling, integrity verification, off-host or off-site storage, point-in-time recovery, or operational recovery workflows beyond the primary datastore's basic capabilities.","explanation":"Backups are only useful if they can be restored. Technology selection should define backup scope, frequency, retention, encryption, isolation, restore objectives, consistency guarantees, verification, and deletion policy.","principle":"Design backup technology around verified restoration rather than backup creation alone.","objective":"Provide recoverable data copies aligned with defined RPO and RTO requirements.","requirements":["RPO and RTO must be explicit.","Backup scope and retention must be defined.","Backup integrity must be verifiable.","Restore procedures must be tested."],"constraints":["Backups can contain the same sensitive information as production.","Backup systems consume storage and operational resources.","A backup that has never been restored remains an unverified recovery assumption."],"preconditions":["Persistent data requires recovery beyond ordinary application retry.","Recovery objectives are documented."],"input":"Data criticality, RPO/RTO, retention, topology, security, and restore requirements.","output":"Backup technology decision and recovery architecture.","expected_behavior":"Recoverable backup artifacts are produced and can be restored according to documented recovery objectives.","incorrect_code":"copy_database_file("backup.db")","corrected_code":"create_consistent_backup(); verify_backup_integrity(); test_restore_periodically()","failure_mode":"A raw file copy is assumed to be a valid recoverable backup while writes are occurring.","expected_error":"The resulting artifact may be inconsistent or unusable; failure may only become visible during restore.","cause":"Backup consistency semantics were not established.","root_cause":"Backup success was measured by copy completion instead of restoration correctness.","solution":"Use a database-aware or consistency-preserving backup mechanism and verify restoration regularly.","edge_case":"The primary database is unavailable immediately before a scheduled backup.","security_implication":"Backup copies require encryption, access control, retention, deletion, and isolation from compromised production credentials.","performance_implication":"Backups consume I/O, CPU, storage, and network resources; impact should be measured during representative workloads.","test_case":"Create a backup and restore it into an isolated environment, then verify application-level data invariants.","test_input":"backup_id=B1; restore_target=isolated_environment","expected_test_result":"The restored datastore passes integrity and application-level verification.","testing_guidance":"Test backup failure, restore, corruption detection, retention, encryption, credential isolation, and disaster scenarios.","verification_method":"Scheduled restore tests and integrity verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify backup and restore semantics against the selected database/provider's official documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":null,"version_range":"Pin backup tooling and datastore versions.","dependencies":["Primary datastore","Backup storage","Backup/restore tooling"],"execution_requirements":["Isolated restore environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D33-B038","D34-B079"],"depends_on":["D33-B038"],"requires":["Defined recovery objectives"],"extends":["D33-B038"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Unverified file copying"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Backup and disaster-recovery engineering principles"],"provenance":"Established backup/recovery practice; exact backup consistency and restore semantics must be verified against the selected datastore and backup technology documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B088","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Change Data Capture","concept":"CDC technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is change data capture technology appropriate for propagating database changes?","answer":"CDC is appropriate when downstream systems need a reliable stream of committed database changes without requiring application code to publish every change explicitly, especially for analytics, integration, replication, or derived-state pipelines.","explanation":"CDC technology must preserve or explicitly define ordering, transaction boundaries, schema evolution, deletes, snapshots, offsets, replay, and recovery. CDC is not automatically a substitute for domain events because database changes may expose persistence details rather than business meaning.","principle":"Use CDC for data-change propagation when persistence-level change semantics are sufficient and distinguish them from business-domain event semantics.","objective":"Evaluate CDC as a technology for reliable database-change propagation.","requirements":["Source database changes must be captured reliably.","Ordering and transaction semantics must be understood.","Offset/replay behavior must be defined.","Schema evolution must be governed."],"constraints":["CDC can couple downstream consumers to database schema.","Not every row change represents a business event.","Connector failure and replay require operational handling."],"preconditions":["A supported source database exists.","Downstream consumers can interpret persistence-level changes."],"input":"Database topology, change volume, downstream requirements, transaction semantics, and schema evolution.","output":"CDC technology decision and change-stream contract.","expected_behavior":"Downstream consumers receive changes according to documented ordering, replay, delete, and transaction semantics.","incorrect_code":"publish_after_update_without_recovery();","corrected_code":"capture_committed_change(); persist_offset(); replay_from_offset_on_recovery();","failure_mode":"A downstream consumer misses database changes during connector failure.","expected_error":"No universal runtime exception; downstream derived state can become incomplete.","cause":"Change position was not durably tracked or replayable.","root_cause":"CDC recovery semantics were omitted from architecture design.","solution":"Use durable offsets/checkpoints and documented replay/reconciliation mechanisms.","edge_case":"A schema migration changes the structure of captured records while consumers still use the previous schema.","security_implication":"Captured database changes can contain sensitive fields and require access control, filtering, masking, and retention policies.","performance_implication":"CDC adds source and downstream processing overhead; impact must be measured for transaction and change volumes.","test_case":"Capture changes, interrupt the CDC pipeline, restart it from a known offset, and compare downstream state with the source.","test_input":"source_changes=N; interruption=true; replay=true","expected_test_result":"Downstream state converges according to documented CDC semantics without silently missing committed changes.","testing_guidance":"Test connector failure, offset recovery, duplicate delivery, deletes, schema changes, snapshots, and transaction ordering.","verification_method":"CDC integration tests and source-to-sink reconciliation.","verification_guidance":"Verify connector/database semantics against the selected CDC technology's official documentation.","language":"Java","language_version":"21","framework":"CDC platform","framework_version":"Pin exact connector/platform versions","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["Source database","CDC connector","Downstream consumer"],"execution_requirements":["Source database and CDC test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_INTEGRATION_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B064","D34-B033","D33-B009"],"depends_on":["D33-B009"],"requires":["Reliable persistence-change propagation requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Application-published domain events"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Change-data-capture architecture principles"],"provenance":"Established data-integration practice; exact CDC ordering, snapshot, offset, and schema semantics must be verified against the selected technology's official documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B089","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"ETL and ELT Orchestration","concept":"Data pipeline orchestration technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When is a dedicated data-pipeline orchestration technology justified?","answer":"Dedicated orchestration is justified when data workflows contain multiple dependent extraction, transformation, validation, loading, scheduling, retry, lineage, or recovery steps that require durable operational coordination.","explanation":"A pipeline orchestrator should manage workflow state and dependencies rather than silently becoming the data-processing engine itself. Selection should consider scheduling, dependency graphs, retries, backfills, parameterization, observability, lineage integration, and execution environment.","principle":"Separate workflow orchestration from data processing and select orchestration technology according to dependency and recovery requirements.","objective":"Determine whether a dedicated data workflow orchestrator is required.","requirements":["Pipeline dependencies must be explicit.","Failure/retry behavior must be defined.","Backfill and rerun semantics must be specified.","Data quality checkpoints must be identifiable."],"constraints":["Orchestrators add platform complexity.","Retries can duplicate downstream effects.","Backfills can interact with current production data."],"preconditions":["Data processing contains multiple dependent stages.","Manual execution is insufficient for reliability requirements."],"input":"Pipeline graph, schedule, data dependencies, recovery requirements, and operational constraints.","output":"Pipeline-orchestration technology decision and workflow contract.","expected_behavior":"Pipeline stages execute in dependency order with durable state, explicit retries, and controlled reruns/backfills.","incorrect_code":"run_extract(); run_transform(); run_load()","corrected_code":"workflow = [extract, validate, transform, load]; execute_with_checkpoints(workflow)","failure_mode":"A process crash loses knowledge of which pipeline stages completed successfully.","expected_error":"No universal runtime exception; rerunning may duplicate or corrupt downstream data.","cause":"Pipeline state was kept only in process memory.","root_cause":"Workflow durability was not treated as a technology requirement.","solution":"Persist workflow state/checkpoints and make rerunnable stages idempotent or explicitly compensating.","edge_case":"A backfill overlaps with a scheduled production pipeline run.","security_implication":"Pipeline identities need least-privilege access to source and destination systems.","performance_implication":"Orchestration overhead is generally secondary to data-processing cost but can matter for very fine-grained task graphs.","test_case":"Interrupt a multi-stage pipeline after an intermediate checkpoint and resume it.","test_input":"stages=4; failure_after_stage=2; resume=true","expected_test_result":"Completed stages are not incorrectly repeated and remaining stages execute according to the rerun policy.","testing_guidance":"Test retries, dependency failures, backfills, concurrent runs, checkpoint recovery, data-quality failures, and partial loads.","verification_method":"Workflow integration tests and state-transition verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify scheduler, retry, backfill, and state semantics against the selected orchestrator's official documentation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Data workflow orchestrator","framework_version":"Pin exact orchestrator version","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["Workflow orchestrator","Data processing engines/stores"],"execution_requirements":["Configured pipeline test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B066","D34-B088","D34-B057"],"depends_on":["D34-B066"],"requires":["Multi-stage durable data workflow"],"extends":["D34-B066"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Single-process scripted pipeline"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Data workflow orchestration principles"],"provenance":"Established data-engineering practice; exact orchestration semantics must be verified against selected platform documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B090","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D36-STYLE-1.0","topic":"Requirement to Technology","subtopic":"Infrastructure as Code","concept":"Infrastructure-as-code technology selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_technology_decision","question":"When should infrastructure-as-code technology be adopted?","answer":"Infrastructure as code is appropriate when infrastructure configuration must be reproducible, reviewable, versioned, environment-consistent, and safely changed through automated workflows.","explanation":"IaC converts infrastructure configuration into controlled artifacts. The technology decision should address state management, drift, secrets, dependencies, preview/plan behavior, rollback strategy, module reuse, provider compatibility, and access controls.","principle":"Treat infrastructure changes as versioned, reviewable state transitions rather than undocumented manual configuration.","objective":"Select infrastructure-as-code technology for reproducible infrastructure lifecycle management.","requirements":["Infrastructure ownership must be defined.","State management must be controlled.","Changes must be reviewable.","Credentials and secrets must be separated from source definitions."],"constraints":["IaC state can contain sensitive information.","Provider APIs and schemas evolve.","Infrastructure rollback is not always equivalent to application rollback."],"preconditions":["Infrastructure configuration is complex enough to require reproducibility or governance.","The organization can control the IaC execution identity."],"input":"Infrastructure topology, environments, provider capabilities, change-management requirements, and security constraints.","output":"IaC technology decision and infrastructure lifecycle policy.","expected_behavior":"Infrastructure changes are represented as version-controlled definitions and applied through controlled, reviewable workflows.","incorrect_code":"provider.create_database(size="large")","corrected_code":"resource = declare_database(name="app-db", size=defined_size); plan_and_review(resource); apply(resource)","failure_mode":"Manual infrastructure changes diverge from the declared environment.","expected_error":"No universal runtime exception; subsequent deployments may unexpectedly overwrite or conflict with undocumented changes.","cause":"Actual infrastructure state and declared configuration were not reconciled.","root_cause":"Infrastructure was managed without a controlled source-of-truth model.","solution":"Define ownership of infrastructure state, detect drift, review changes, and control apply permissions.","edge_case":"A critical emergency change is applied manually while the IaC state remains unchanged.","security_implication":"IaC repositories and execution identities can control critical infrastructure and require strong access controls, review, and secret protection.","performance_implication":"IaC execution is generally deployment-time overhead; large plans can increase execution duration and provider API usage.","test_case":"Apply infrastructure from a clean environment, modify a resource outside IaC, detect drift, and reconcile it through a reviewed change.","test_input":"declared_state=S1; actual_state=S2","expected_test_result":"Drift is detected or reconciled according to the defined policy without unauthorized destructive change.","testing_guidance":"Test plan/diff accuracy, state locking, drift detection, provider failures, secret handling, dependency ordering, and recovery.","verification_method":"Infrastructure integration tests plus plan/state inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify provider/resource semantics and state behavior against the selected IaC tool's official documentation.","language":"HCL","language_version":"Tool-specific","framework":"Infrastructure as Code","framework_version":"Pin exact IaC tool/provider versions","version_range":"Version-specific.","dependencies":["IaC engine","Infrastructure provider APIs"],"execution_requirements":["Isolated infrastructure test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OPERATIONS_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B067","D34-B068","D34-B081"],"depends_on":["D34-B067"],"requires":["Reproducible infrastructure requirement"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Manual infrastructure configuration"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Infrastructure-as-code and configuration-governance principles"],"provenance":"Established infrastructure engineering practice; exact IaC state, provider, and execution semantics must be verified against official documentation for the selected tool and provider versions.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D34-B091","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Build System Technology","subtopic":"Reproducible build orchestration","concept":"Build-system technology should be selected according to dependency graphs, incremental-build requirements, reproducibility, supported languages, artifact outputs, and CI integration rather than tool popularity alone.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"When should a dedicated build-system technology be selected?","answer":"Select a build system when a project has non-trivial dependency graphs, multiple targets, generated artifacts, incremental builds, reproducibility requirements, or multiple languages/components that need coordinated builds. Simple projects may use the native language tooling directly.","explanation":"The technology decision should model the actual build graph and lifecycle. A build system should make inputs, generated outputs, dependencies, and reproducibility explicit. Introducing one adds configuration and operational complexity, so its value should be demonstrated by build coordination or reproducibility requirements.","principle":"Build tooling should reduce uncontrolled build complexity without becoming an unnecessary abstraction layer.","objective":"Choose build technology that provides deterministic, maintainable, and appropriately scalable artifact production.","requirements":["Explicit dependency graph where needed","Repeatable builds","Incremental execution when valuable","CI compatibility","Artifact traceability"],"constraints":["Build-tool configuration is itself a maintained dependency","Toolchain availability varies by language and platform","Generated artifacts must have explicit ownership"],"preconditions":["Build inputs and outputs are identifiable","Supported target platforms are known"],"input":{"source_tree":"project source files","toolchain":"compiler/interpreter/build tools","build_configuration":"declared build parameters"},"output":{"artifacts":"build outputs","metadata":"build identity and dependency information"},"expected_behavior":"A build invocation should produce declared artifacts from declared inputs with reproducible or explicitly controlled environment dependencies.","incorrect_code":"# Illustrative anti-pattern: hidden build dependency\nimport subprocess\nsubprocess.run("generate_code && compile_external_tool", shell=True)","corrected_code":"# Illustrative pattern: explicit build steps and checked inputs\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate_build_input(path: str) -> Path:\n    p = Path(path)\n    if not p.exists():\n        raise FileNotFoundError(p)\n    return p\n\nsource = validate_build_input("src/main.c")\n# A real build system should declare the compiler, dependencies, and outputs explicitly.","failure_mode":"Build succeeds locally but fails in CI or produces artifacts dependent on undeclared local state.","expected_error":"Environment-dependent build failure or missing generated dependency.","cause":"Implicit tools, undeclared generated files, or inconsistent toolchain versions.","root_cause":"Build graph and environment requirements are not explicitly represented.","solution":"Declare build inputs, generated outputs, toolchain versions, dependencies, and reproducibility requirements in the selected build technology.","edge_case":"Generated source may itself become an input to later build stages and must not create an undeclared circular dependency.","security_implication":"Untrusted build scripts or dependencies can execute during builds; build isolation and dependency trust controls may be required.","performance_implication":"Incremental builds can reduce repeated work, but overly fragmented dependency graphs can add orchestration overhead.","test_case":"Run the same declared build from a clean environment and compare artifact identity or explicitly documented nondeterministic fields.","test_input":{"environment":"clean reproducible build environment","source_revision":"fixed revision"},"expected_test_result":"Declared artifacts are produced successfully and undeclared local files are not required.","testing_guidance":"Test clean builds, incremental builds, dependency invalidation, missing inputs, generated outputs, and CI execution.","verification_method":"Compare declared dependency graph and build outputs against the build requirements.","verification_guidance":"Verify reproducibility requirements explicitly; do not claim bit-for-bit reproducibility unless measured.","language":"C","language_version":"C11+","framework":"Build-system technology","framework_version":"Version must be pinned by implementation","version_range":"Project-specific","dependencies":["compiler/toolchain","build-system implementation"],"execution_requirements":["Declared compiler/toolchain","clean build environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B090"],"depends_on":["D34-B056"],"requires":["D34-B090"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established software build engineering practice; specific tool behavior must be verified against the selected build-system documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B092","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Compiler and Toolchain Technology","subtopic":"Language toolchain selection","concept":"Compiler and toolchain selection must account for language standard, target architecture, ABI, optimization requirements, diagnostics, sanitizer support, library compatibility, and reproducible version pinning.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"How should compiler and toolchain technology be selected?","answer":"Select the compiler/toolchain according to supported language standards, target platforms, ABI compatibility, required diagnostics, optimization capabilities, security tooling, dependency compatibility, and supported lifecycle. Pin or otherwise control the toolchain version when reproducibility matters.","explanation":"A compiler is part of the software supply chain and can affect generated code, ABI, diagnostics, performance, and compatibility. Toolchain selection should therefore be a requirement-driven decision rather than an isolated compiler preference.","principle":"The compiler and surrounding toolchain are build dependencies whose compatibility and lifecycle must be explicitly governed.","objective":"Ensure source code is transformed into compatible artifacts using a controlled and supportable toolchain.","requirements":["Supported language standard","Target architecture compatibility","Dependency and ABI compatibility","Diagnostics/testing support","Controlled toolchain lifecycle"],"constraints":["Compiler versions may generate materially different artifacts","Platform-specific behavior can differ","ABI changes can break binary consumers"],"preconditions":["Target platforms and language standards are defined","Binary compatibility requirements are known"],"input":{"source":"language source","standard":"required language standard","target":"target architecture/platform"},"output":{"artifact":"compiled executable or library","diagnostics":"compiler diagnostics"},"expected_behavior":"The selected toolchain compiles supported source for the declared target while respecting compatibility constraints.","incorrect_code":"// Illustrative: assuming any compiler version is interchangeable\n// gcc main.c -o app","corrected_code":"// Illustrative configuration concept:\n// compiler = pinned_or_policy_controlled_version\n// language_standard = declared_standard\n// target = declared_platform\n// build should fail if required toolchain constraints are not satisfied.","failure_mode":"Build succeeds with one toolchain but fails, changes ABI, or behaves differently with another.","expected_error":"Compilation, linking, ABI, or compatibility failure.","cause":"Uncontrolled compiler/toolchain version or incompatible target settings.","root_cause":"Toolchain requirements were not treated as explicit technology constraints.","solution":"Define supported compiler families/versions, target architectures, ABI requirements, and validation tests.","edge_case":"A compiler upgrade can preserve source compatibility while changing generated code, warnings, optimization, or ABI behavior.","security_implication":"Compiler and linker options can affect hardening features; toolchain provenance must be controlled.","performance_implication":"Optimization behavior varies by compiler version and flags; performance claims require measurement.","test_case":"Compile a representative compatibility suite using every supported toolchain target.","test_input":{"source_suite":"representative project","toolchains":["declared supported versions"]},"expected_test_result":"All supported toolchains produce artifacts satisfying defined functional and compatibility requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test compilation, linking, ABI compatibility, warnings, sanitizers where applicable, and representative runtime behavior.","verification_method":"Verify toolchain version, target, language standard, and compatibility-test results against requirements.","verification_guidance":"Record actual toolchain identity and test evidence; do not infer compatibility solely from version numbers.","language":"C++","language_version":"Project-defined C++ standard","framework":"Compiler/toolchain","framework_version":"Must be pinned or policy-controlled","version_range":"Project-specific","dependencies":["compiler","linker","standard library","platform SDK"],"execution_requirements":["Supported compiler/toolchain installed"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B091"],"depends_on":["D34-B056"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established compiler/toolchain engineering practice; exact compatibility must be established from the selected compiler and platform documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B093","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Runtime Version Management Technology","subtopic":"Interpreter and runtime compatibility","concept":"Runtime-version technology should provide controlled selection and reproducibility for applications whose behavior depends on interpreter, virtual machine, or runtime versions.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"When is explicit runtime-version management required?","answer":"Use explicit runtime-version management when application compatibility, dependency resolution, deployment reproducibility, or language-runtime behavior depends on a specific runtime version. The selected mechanism should make the required runtime discoverable and enforceable across development, CI, and deployment.","explanation":"Language runtimes can change APIs, standard-library behavior, performance characteristics, dependency compatibility, and security support. Merely documenting a runtime version without enforcing or testing it can leave environments inconsistent.","principle":"Runtime compatibility is an executable constraint, not only documentation.","objective":"Keep development, testing, and production runtime environments within the supported compatibility boundary.","requirements":["Supported runtime versions","Environment detection or enforcement","Dependency compatibility","CI coverage","Upgrade procedure"],"constraints":["Operating-system support differs by runtime version","Runtime managers add tooling dependencies","Multiple applications may require different versions"],"preconditions":["Application runtime requirements are defined","Dependency compatibility is known"],"input":{"runtime_requirement":"supported runtime range","environment":"development/CI/deployment environment"},"output":{"runtime_selection":"selected runtime","compatibility_result":"pass/fail against policy"},"expected_behavior":"The environment uses a supported runtime and incompatible versions are detected before deployment or execution.","incorrect_code":"# Illustrative anti-pattern\nimport sys\nprint(sys.version)\n# Application silently proceeds regardless of supported version.","corrected_code":"# Illustrative runtime guard\nimport sys\nMIN = (3, 11)\nif sys.version_info < MIN:\n    raise RuntimeError("Unsupported Python runtime")\n","failure_mode":"Different environments execute the same application under incompatible runtime behavior.","expected_error":"Unsupported runtime or dependency-resolution failure.","cause":"Runtime requirement is documented but not enforced or tested.","root_cause":"Runtime compatibility was not modeled as a technology constraint.","solution":"Declare supported runtime versions, enforce them where practical, and test supported environments in CI.","edge_case":"A runtime may satisfy a minimum version but still be incompatible with a dependency requiring a narrower range.","security_implication":"Unsupported runtimes may no longer receive security fixes; lifecycle status must be part of technology selection.","performance_implication":"Runtime upgrades can alter performance; upgrades should be benchmarked when performance is a requirement.","test_case":"Run compatibility tests against each supported runtime version.","test_input":{"runtime_versions":["declared supported versions"]},"expected_test_result":"Compatibility suite passes for supported versions and rejects unsupported versions where enforcement is configured.","testing_guidance":"Test dependency installation, startup, core functionality, serialization, and shutdown behavior across supported runtimes.","verification_method":"Compare actual runtime identity with declared compatibility policy and CI evidence.","verification_guidance":"Verify runtime version from the execution environment rather than relying on configuration text alone.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Runtime management","framework_version":"Environment-specific","version_range":"Project-specific","dependencies":["Python runtime","dependency manager"],"execution_requirements":["Supported runtime installed"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B056","D34-B092"],"depends_on":["D34-B056"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established runtime compatibility practice; exact supported versions must be verified against runtime and dependency documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B094","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Cross-Platform Packaging Technology","subtopic":"Portable application distribution","concept":"Packaging technology should be selected according to target operating systems, runtime inclusion requirements, native dependencies, installation model, update mechanism, signing requirements, and artifact size constraints.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"How should application packaging technology be selected for multiple platforms?","answer":"Select packaging technology based on actual target platforms and distribution requirements, including whether the runtime must be bundled, whether native dependencies exist, installation/update behavior, signing, and offline operation.","explanation":"A source-compatible application is not automatically distribution-compatible. Packaging may need to bundle runtimes, native libraries, assets, metadata, permissions, and platform-specific launchers. Technology selection must therefore start with the distribution contract.","principle":"Packaging is a deployment boundary and must represent the real runtime environment.","objective":"Produce installable, supportable artifacts for declared target platforms without hidden environment dependencies.","requirements":["Declared target platforms","Artifact format","Runtime/dependency strategy","Signing requirements","Update strategy"],"constraints":["Native dependencies can be platform-specific","Bundling increases artifact size","Platform signing policies differ"],"preconditions":["Supported platforms are explicitly defined","Distribution channel requirements are known"],"input":{"application":"built application","targets":["declared platforms"],"dependencies":"runtime and native dependencies"},"output":{"package":"platform-specific distribution artifact"},"expected_behavior":"The packaging process creates artifacts containing or correctly referencing all required runtime components according to the distribution contract.","incorrect_code":"# Illustrative anti-pattern: assume source directory is universally distributable\npackage = "copy_source_tree_without_runtime_or_platform_metadata"","corrected_code":"# Illustrative packaging manifest\npackage = {\n    "target": "declared-platform",\n    "runtime": "explicitly-managed",\n    "native_dependencies": ["declared-components"],\n    "assets": ["declared-assets"],\n    "signing": "required-by-distribution-policy"\n}","failure_mode":"Application works in development but fails after installation on a target platform.","expected_error":"Missing runtime, native library, asset, permission, or platform metadata.","cause":"Packaging requirements were inferred rather than declared.","root_cause":"Distribution environment was not treated as part of the technology selection.","solution":"Define platform artifacts, dependency inclusion, signing, installation, and update requirements before choosing packaging technology.","edge_case":"A package can work on one machine because a system library is present while failing on a clean target machine.","security_implication":"Unsigned or improperly signed artifacts may violate platform trust requirements and increase supply-chain risk.","performance_implication":"Bundled runtimes and compression affect startup time and artifact size; measure where material.","test_case":"Install the package on a clean target environment with no development dependencies.","test_input":{"artifact":"release package","environment":"clean supported target"},"expected_test_result":"Application installs and starts with only explicitly permitted system dependencies.","testing_guidance":"Test clean installation, upgrade, uninstall, offline behavior, permissions, native dependencies, and artifact integrity.","verification_method":"Verify package manifest, installation result, runtime startup, and target-platform compatibility.","verification_guidance":"Use clean environments rather than the developer workstation as the primary packaging verification environment.","language":"Python","language_version":"Project-specific","framework":"Packaging technology","framework_version":"Must be pinned","version_range":"Project-specific","dependencies":["application runtime","platform packaging tools"],"execution_requirements":["Target platform test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B093"],"depends_on":["D34-B093"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established software distribution practice; platform-specific packaging behavior must be verified against official platform and packaging documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B095","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Profiling Technology","subtopic":"Runtime bottleneck diagnosis","concept":"Profiling technology should be selected when performance requirements require evidence about CPU, memory, I/O, allocation, lock contention, or other runtime costs that cannot be established reliably through source inspection alone.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"When should profiling technology be introduced?","answer":"Use profiling when a performance problem or requirement needs measurement of runtime behavior. Select the profiler according to the runtime, target environment, observability needed, overhead tolerance, and type of suspected bottleneck.","explanation":"Profiling identifies where execution time or resources are actually spent. It should complement—not replace—functional tests and controlled performance measurements. Source-level intuition alone is insufficient for many performance decisions.","principle":"Performance optimization should be driven by measured runtime evidence.","objective":"Identify material runtime bottlenecks with an appropriate profiling technology.","requirements":["Representative workload","Defined performance question","Appropriate profiler","Repeatable environment","Interpretation criteria"],"constraints":["Profilers introduce varying overhead","Production profiling may require special controls","A profile from an unrepresentative workload can mislead"],"preconditions":["Performance symptom or requirement exists","Workload can be reproduced or observed safely"],"input":{"application":"target program","workload":"representative workload"},"output":{"profile":"runtime measurements","diagnosis":"candidate bottlenecks"},"expected_behavior":"Profiling identifies measurable hotspots or resource behavior relevant to the performance question.","incorrect_code":"# Anti-pattern: optimize based only on intuition\nresult = expensive_operation()\n# No measurement establishes that this operation is the bottleneck.","corrected_code":"# Correct engineering pattern: isolate workload and measure externally\n# profiler_tool run representative_workload\n# analyze CPU, allocations, I/O, contention, then modify and re-measure.","failure_mode":"Optimization targets code that is not materially responsible for observed latency or resource consumption.","expected_error":"No useful performance improvement or regression elsewhere.","cause":"Unrepresentative workload or absent profiling evidence.","root_cause":"Performance diagnosis was performed without runtime measurement.","solution":"Profile representative workloads, identify bottlenecks, change one relevant factor, and re-measure.","edge_case":"Sampling profilers can miss very short-lived operations; instrumentation may be needed for specific questions.","security_implication":"Profiling data can expose sensitive values, paths, or workload characteristics and therefore requires appropriate access controls.","performance_implication":"Profiler overhead can distort measurements; tool mode and overhead must be considered.","test_case":"Profile a stable workload before and after a targeted optimization.","test_input":{"workload":"fixed representative workload","comparison":"baseline versus modified implementation"},"expected_test_result":"The measured performance change is attributable to the tested modification within the measurement uncertainty.","testing_guidance":"Control workload, environment, warm-up, repetitions, and measurement method. Record profiler configuration.","verification_method":"Compare profiler evidence with the performance hypothesis and independent benchmark measurements.","verification_guidance":"Label results MEASURED only when actually executed; otherwise classify as ESTIMATED or UNKNOWN.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Profiler technology","framework_version":"Must be recorded","version_range":"Tool-specific","dependencies":["runtime","profiler","representative workload"],"execution_requirements":["Profiling-capable environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established performance-engineering practice; profiler semantics must be verified against the selected profiler documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B096","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Benchmark Harness Technology","subtopic":"Controlled performance measurement","concept":"A benchmark harness should provide repeatable workload execution, timing/resource measurement, warm-up handling, statistical summaries, and regression comparison appropriate to the performance requirement.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"When is a dedicated benchmark harness technology justified?","answer":"Use a benchmark harness when performance is a formal requirement, a regression risk, or a recurring engineering concern requiring controlled comparison across implementations or versions.","explanation":"Ad hoc timing is often insufficient because startup effects, caching, workload variance, scheduling, and environmental differences can distort results. A benchmark harness makes measurement methodology explicit and repeatable.","principle":"Performance claims require a defined measurement protocol.","objective":"Provide repeatable evidence for performance comparisons and regression detection.","requirements":["Defined workload","Measurement methodology","Warm-up policy where applicable","Repetition strategy","Baseline comparison"],"constraints":["Hardware and environment variation","Microbenchmarks may not represent production behavior","Statistical noise must be considered"],"preconditions":["Performance metric is defined","Comparable baseline exists where regression testing is intended"],"input":{"implementation":"candidate implementation","workload":"defined workload","measurement_policy":"timing/resource policy"},"output":{"measurements":"observed metrics","comparison":"baseline comparison"},"expected_behavior":"The harness executes the same defined workload under controlled conditions and records measurements without presenting unmeasured claims as facts.","incorrect_code":"import time\nstart = time.time()\nrun_workload()\nprint(time.time() - start)","corrected_code":"# Illustrative only: a benchmark harness should define workload and repetitions.\ndef benchmark(run, repetitions=10):\n    samples = []\n    for _ in range(repetitions):\n        samples.append(run())\n    return samples\n# Actual timing/statistical methodology must be supplied by the chosen framework.","failure_mode":"Benchmark results vary substantially or cannot support a meaningful comparison.","expected_error":"High variance, measurement bias, or invalid comparison.","cause":"Poor workload control, insufficient repetitions, or environmental variability.","root_cause":"Measurement protocol was not explicitly designed for the performance question.","solution":"Define benchmark protocol, control relevant variables, collect repeated measurements, and report uncertainty/variance where material.","edge_case":"A microbenchmark improvement can regress end-to-end performance because the optimized component is not the system bottleneck.","security_implication":"Production-like benchmark data may contain sensitive information; synthetic or sanitized workloads may be required.","performance_implication":"The benchmark itself must minimize measurement distortion and document warm-up/cache effects.","test_case":"Run baseline and candidate implementations under the same benchmark protocol.","test_input":{"baseline":"implementation A","candidate":"implementation B","workload":"fixed workload"},"expected_test_result":"Both implementations are measured using the same protocol and the result includes enough information to determine whether the observed difference is meaningful.","testing_guidance":"Record environment, version, workload, repetitions, warm-up, metric definitions, and raw/summary measurements.","verification_method":"Review benchmark protocol and independently reproduce selected measurements.","verification_guidance":"Do not label results MEASURED until execution actually occurs; no benchmark number should be fabricated.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Benchmark harness","framework_version":"Must be recorded","version_range":"Tool-specific","dependencies":["runtime","benchmark framework"],"execution_requirements":["Controlled measurement environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B095"],"depends_on":["D34-B095"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established performance benchmarking practice; exact harness semantics must be verified against selected benchmark tooling documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B097","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Schema Registry Technology","subtopic":"Shared schema lifecycle","concept":"Schema-registry technology is appropriate when independently produced messages or datasets require centralized schema discovery, compatibility checking, version governance, or evolution controls.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"When should schema-registry technology be selected?","answer":"Select a schema registry when multiple producers and consumers exchange structured data and compatibility, discoverability, versioning, or evolution rules need centralized enforcement. A registry may be unnecessary for tightly coupled internal data with a simpler lifecycle.","explanation":"A schema registry separates schema lifecycle governance from individual application deployments. Its value increases when many independently evolving producers and consumers share contracts. It must not be confused with a generic database schema migration mechanism.","principle":"Shared data contracts require explicit lifecycle and compatibility governance.","objective":"Control schema evolution and compatibility across independently changing data producers and consumers.","requirements":["Schema identity","Version lifecycle","Compatibility policy","Consumer/provider visibility","Validation mechanism"],"constraints":["Registry availability can become a dependency","Compatibility policy must match actual consumer behavior","Schema format capabilities differ"],"preconditions":["Shared structured data contract exists","Producer and consumer evolution is independently possible"],"input":{"schema":"structured data schema","compatibility_policy":"evolution rule"},"output":{"schema_id":"registered schema identity","validation":"compatibility result"},"expected_behavior":"Schema changes are evaluated against the declared compatibility policy before incompatible data contracts are accepted.","incorrect_code":"# Illustrative anti-pattern: silently change shared field type\nmessage = {"amount": "100"}\n# Existing consumers may expect a numeric amount.","corrected_code":"# Illustrative contract validation concept\nschema = {"amount": {"type": "number"}}\nmessage = {"amount": 100}\n# A real registry/serializer must enforce the selected compatibility policy.","failure_mode":"A producer deploys a schema change that breaks one or more consumers.","expected_error":"Schema validation or consumer deserialization failure.","cause":"No centralized compatibility check or incompatible evolution policy.","root_cause":"Shared schema lifecycle was treated as an informal producer responsibility.","solution":"Register schemas, define compatibility policy, validate changes before release, and test representative consumers.","edge_case":"Adding an optional field can be compatible under one serialization contract but incompatible under another.","security_implication":"Schema registries can expose sensitive data models and must enforce appropriate access control.","performance_implication":"Schema validation and serialization add processing overhead; measure only where it affects workload requirements.","test_case":"Submit compatible and incompatible schema revisions and verify policy enforcement.","test_input":{"baseline_schema":"v1","candidate_schemas":["compatible-v2","incompatible-v2"]},"expected_test_result":"Compatible change is accepted and incompatible change is rejected according to the configured policy.","testing_guidance":"Test evolution, serialization/deserialization, unknown fields, defaults, required fields, and rollback scenarios.","verification_method":"Verify registry policy, schema version history, and producer/consumer compatibility tests.","verification_guidance":"Use actual registry behavior for the selected format and version; do not generalize one registry's compatibility semantics to all systems.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Schema registry technology","framework_version":"Must be pinned","version_range":"Technology-specific","dependencies":["serialization format","registry service/library"],"execution_requirements":["Schema registry or equivalent contract service"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B025","D34-B058"],"depends_on":["D34-B025"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established data-contract governance practice; exact compatibility semantics must be verified against the selected schema-registry and serialization documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B098","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Quality Technology","subtopic":"Automated data validation","concept":"Data-quality technology should be selected when correctness, completeness, consistency, timeliness, uniqueness, or validity of operational or analytical data is a material system requirement.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"When is dedicated data-quality technology justified?","answer":"Use dedicated data-quality tooling when datasets are sufficiently important, numerous, transformed, or independently sourced that automated validation and quality reporting provide material value. Small systems may use application-level validation if requirements remain manageable.","explanation":"Data validation should be applied at appropriate boundaries, including ingestion, transformation, and consumption. Dedicated tooling becomes valuable when quality rules need centralized management, repeatable execution, reporting, and historical visibility.","principle":"Data should be validated according to explicit quality dimensions at the boundaries where incorrect data can cause material harm.","objective":"Detect data-quality violations before they propagate into dependent systems or decisions.","requirements":["Defined quality dimensions","Validation rules","Failure handling","Quality reporting","Ownership of remediation"],"constraints":["Rules must reflect business meaning","Overly strict validation can reject legitimate data","Quality tools do not automatically correct root causes"],"preconditions":["Critical datasets and quality expectations are identified","Data ownership is defined"],"input":{"dataset":"candidate data","quality_rules":"declared validation rules"},"output":{"quality_result":"validation results","violations":"identified quality failures"},"expected_behavior":"Data is evaluated against explicit quality rules and violations are surfaced with enough context for remediation.","incorrect_code":"def load(rows):\n    return rows\n# No validation of required fields, types, ranges, or uniqueness.","corrected_code":"def validate_row(row):\n    if row.get("id") is None:\n        raise ValueError("id is required")\n    if not isinstance(row.get("amount"), (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError("amount must be numeric")\n    return row\n\ndef load(rows):\n    return [validate_row(r) for r in rows]","failure_mode":"Invalid or inconsistent data propagates into downstream processing.","expected_error":"Data-quality validation failure.","cause":"Missing or inadequate validation at ingestion/transformation boundaries.","root_cause":"Quality requirements were not translated into executable rules.","solution":"Define measurable quality dimensions and automate validation at appropriate data boundaries.","edge_case":"A missing value may be valid for one business state and invalid for another; rules must be context-aware.","security_implication":"Quality checks can reduce unsafe interpretation of malformed data but must not be treated as a complete security control.","performance_implication":"Large-scale validation can be computationally expensive; validation placement and sampling policy may matter.","test_case":"Inject representative invalid, duplicate, missing, stale, and out-of-range records.","test_input":{"records":["valid","missing-required","duplicate","invalid-type","out-of-range"]},"expected_test_result":"Each violation is detected according to the declared rule without incorrectly rejecting valid records.","testing_guidance":"Test positive and negative cases, null semantics, boundary values, duplicate detection, and rule-version changes.","verification_method":"Compare validation outcomes against an independently defined expected dataset.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each quality rule is executable, tested, owned, and traceable to a data requirement.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Data-quality technology","framework_version":"Must be pinned","version_range":"Tool-specific","dependencies":["data processing runtime","validation technology"],"execution_requirements":["Representative dataset or controlled test fixtures"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B033","D34-B089"],"depends_on":["D34-B089"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established data-quality engineering practice; selected tool behavior must be verified against its official documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B099","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Vector Retrieval Technology","subtopic":"Semantic similarity indexing","concept":"Vector-index technology is appropriate when retrieval requirements depend on semantic similarity over embedded representations rather than exact lexical matching or deterministic relational predicates.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"When should vector retrieval technology be selected?","answer":"Select vector indexing when the application has a demonstrated semantic-similarity retrieval requirement and an embedding representation suitable for the domain. Exact filtering, transactional lookup, and keyword retrieval should remain with technologies designed for those semantics unless a combined architecture is explicitly required.","explanation":"Vector retrieval solves a particular similarity-search problem. It does not automatically replace relational databases, full-text search, or deterministic filtering. Technology selection should identify the retrieval objective, embedding model, distance metric, index behavior, freshness requirement, and evaluation method.","principle":"Use vector search for semantic similarity requirements, not merely because data contains text.","objective":"Provide technology support for evaluated semantic retrieval workloads.","requirements":["Embedding generation","Similarity metric","Indexing strategy","Metadata filtering where required","Retrieval evaluation"],"constraints":["Embedding quality affects retrieval quality","Index updates introduce freshness considerations","Approximate search can trade recall for resource usage depending on implementation"],"preconditions":["Semantic retrieval problem is demonstrated","Embedding representation and evaluation set exist"],"input":{"documents":"source corpus","query":"semantic query","embeddings":"vector representations"},"output":{"matches":"ranked candidate results","scores":"similarity measurements as defined by technology"},"expected_behavior":"Queries return candidates according to the selected vector similarity semantics and configured filtering/index behavior.","incorrect_code":"# Anti-pattern: use vector similarity without validating retrieval quality\nresults = vector_index.search(query_embedding, k=5)\n# No relevance evaluation or metadata constraints are defined.","corrected_code":"# Illustrative retrieval contract\nresults = vector_index.search(\n    query_embedding,\n    k=5,\n    metadata_filter={"tenant_id": tenant_id}\n)\n# Production selection additionally requires an evaluated relevance set.","failure_mode":"Semantically irrelevant records are retrieved or tenant/data boundaries are violated.","expected_error":"Retrieval-quality failure or unauthorized result exposure.","cause":"Unvalidated embeddings, poor index configuration, missing metadata constraints, or unsuitable retrieval technology.","root_cause":"Vector search was selected without defining retrieval semantics and evaluation criteria.","solution":"Define retrieval requirements, embedding/version policy, filtering boundaries, evaluation metrics, and fallback behavior before selecting technology.","edge_case":"Two embedding-model versions can produce incompatible vector spaces and require re-indexing or version-aware coexistence.","security_implication":"Metadata filtering must be enforced as a security boundary where applicable; vector similarity alone is not authorization.","performance_implication":"Index size, dimensionality, query concurrency, recall targets, and update frequency affect resource requirements; claims require measurement.","test_case":"Evaluate known relevant and irrelevant queries across the chosen index and embedding version.","test_input":{"evaluation_queries":"labeled retrieval queries","expected_documents":"ground-truth relevant documents"},"expected_test_result":"Measured retrieval quality satisfies the declared requirement without unauthorized cross-boundary results.","testing_guidance":"Test relevance, metadata filtering, empty results, stale embeddings, index updates, version migration, and adversarial cross-tenant queries.","verification_method":"Run an evaluated retrieval test set and verify security filters independently.","verification_guidance":"Record embedding model/version, index configuration, dataset version, and actual retrieval metrics.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Vector retrieval technology","framework_version":"Must be pinned","version_range":"Tool-specific","dependencies":["embedding model/runtime","vector index"],"execution_requirements":["Vector index","embedding generation capability","evaluation corpus"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B021","D34-B033"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established information-retrieval engineering pattern; exact vector-index semantics must be verified against the selected implementation documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D34-B100","dataset_id":"D34","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Local Model Inference Runtime Technology","subtopic":"On-device or local AI inference","concept":"Local inference-runtime technology should be selected when deployment requirements prioritize local execution, privacy, offline operation, predictable data locality, or controlled inference dependencies and the target hardware can satisfy the model's resource requirements.","knowledge_type":"technology_selection","question":"When should a local model inference runtime be selected?","answer":"Select a local inference runtime when local execution is a material requirement and the target hardware, model format, memory, latency, throughput, licensing, and supported operators satisfy the workload. Local inference should not be assumed to outperform remote inference without measurement.","explanation":"Local inference changes the deployment boundary by moving model execution to controlled hardware. This can support offline or data-local operation but introduces model packaging, hardware compatibility, memory, update, quantization, and runtime-management requirements.","principle":"Inference location is a deployment requirement that must be evaluated against workload, privacy, resource, and lifecycle constraints.","objective":"Choose an inference runtime that can execute the required model within declared local deployment constraints.","requirements":["Supported model format","Target hardware compatibility","Memory budget","Latency/throughput requirements","Model lifecycle and update process","Data-locality requirements"],"constraints":["Hardware resources are finite","Model/runtime compatibility varies","Quantization may change output characteristics","Local models still require security and update governance"],"preconditions":["Model workload is defined","Target hardware is known","Local execution requirement is justified"],"input":{"model":"selected model artifact","hardware":"target local hardware","request":"inference input"},"output":{"response":"model inference output","telemetry":"execution metadata where permitted"},"expected_behavior":"The selected runtime loads a supported model artifact and produces inference results within declared functional and resource constraints.","incorrect_code":"# Illustrative anti-pattern: assume every model runs on every local runtime\nruntime.load("arbitrary-model-format")\nresult = runtime.infer(input_data)","corrected_code":"# Illustrative compatibility gate\nmodel = {\n    "format": "declared-supported-format",\n    "runtime_version": "declared-compatible-version",\n    "hardware": "declared-supported-target"\n}\n# Load only after model/runtime/hardware compatibility validation.","failure_mode":"Model fails to load, exceeds memory, produces unacceptable latency, or changes behavior after runtime/model conversion.","expected_error":"Model compatibility, resource exhaustion, or runtime execution failure.","cause":"Unsupported model format/operator, insufficient resources, or unvalidated conversion/quantization.","root_cause":"Inference runtime was selected without validating the complete model-to-hardware compatibility chain.","solution":"Validate model format, operators, runtime version, hardware capacity, memory, latency, accuracy/relevance requirements, and update procedure.","edge_case":"A quantized model may reduce resource use while changing numerical behavior; the acceptable change must be defined and tested.","security_implication":"Local model artifacts and prompts/data may contain sensitive information; model integrity and access controls remain necessary.","performance_implication":"Local latency and throughput are workload- and hardware-dependent; no performance advantage should be claimed without measurement.","test_case":"Run functional and resource tests using the exact model artifact and target hardware.","test_input":{"model":"fixed versioned artifact","hardware":"target hardware","inputs":"representative inference set"},"expected_test_result":"Model loads successfully and produces outputs satisfying declared functional/resource acceptance criteria.","testing_guidance":"Test cold start, warm inference, memory use, representative latency, failure recovery, model integrity, and version migration.","verification_method":"Verify model/runtime/hardware compatibility and execute an acceptance test against the declared requirements.","verification_guidance":"Record actual model version, runtime version, hardware, configuration, and measured results; distinguish MEASURED from ESTIMATED values.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Local inference runtime","framework_version":"Must be pinned","version_range":"Runtime-specific","dependencies":["model artifact","inference runtime","target hardware"],"execution_requirements":["Compatible local hardware","supported model format","runtime installation"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D34-B099","D34-B056"],"depends_on":["D34-B056"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established local-inference engineering pattern; exact model/runtime compatibility must be verified against the selected runtime and model-format documentation."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture-to-Module Mapping","subtopic":"Component decomposition into implementation modules","concept":"An architectural component should map to implementation modules according to responsibility, ownership, dependency direction, and change boundaries rather than creating a one-to-one file for every conceptual element.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"How should an architectural component be translated into code modules?","answer":"Translate each architectural component into one or more cohesive implementation modules whose boundaries preserve the component's responsibility, public contract, dependency direction, and change isolation. Do not mechanically create one file or class per architecture box.","explanation":"Architecture describes responsibilities and boundaries; implementation must choose concrete packages, modules, classes, functions, and adapters. A good mapping preserves architectural intent while allowing implementation-level cohesion and testability.","principle":"Implementation structure should preserve architectural boundaries while remaining cohesive at code level.","objective":"Create a module structure that faithfully implements architectural responsibilities without artificial fragmentation.","requirements":["Every architectural responsibility has an implementation owner","Module boundaries are explicit","Dependencies follow intended architecture","Public interfaces are distinguishable from internals","Modules remain independently testable"],"constraints":["Architecture diagrams may be intentionally technology-neutral","One component may require multiple modules","Small responsibilities should not be fragmented without value"],"preconditions":["Architecture components and responsibilities are defined","Dependency direction is known"],"input":{"architecture_component":"defined component responsibility","dependencies":"allowed architectural dependencies"},"output":{"modules":"implementation modules","mapping":"component-to-module traceability"},"expected_behavior":"Each architectural component has a traceable implementation representation and prohibited dependencies are prevented or detected.","incorrect_code":"# Incorrect: unrelated responsibilities placed in one module\nclass OrderModule:\n    def calculate_order(self): pass\n    def send_email(self): pass\n    def write_audit_log(self): pass\n    def connect_to_database(self): pass","corrected_code":"# Illustrative implementation decomposition\nclass OrderService:\n    def calculate_order(self): pass\n\nclass NotificationPort:\n    def send(self, message): raise NotImplementedError\n\nclass AuditPort:\n    def record(self, event): raise NotImplementedError\n\nclass OrderRepository:\n    def save(self, order): raise NotImplementedError","failure_mode":"Architectural boundaries become meaningless because unrelated concerns are tightly coupled inside implementation modules.","expected_error":"High change coupling, difficult testing, circular dependencies, or architecture-conformance failure.","cause":"Mechanical or convenience-driven module organization.","root_cause":"Architecture responsibilities were not translated into explicit implementation ownership and dependency boundaries.","solution":"Map responsibilities to cohesive modules, define public interfaces, and enforce dependency direction.","edge_case":"A very small application may legitimately combine multiple responsibilities temporarily when the architecture explicitly permits it.","security_implication":"Module boundaries should prevent sensitive infrastructure capabilities from becoming accessible to unrelated application code.","performance_implication":"Excessive abstraction or inter-module serialization can add overhead; boundaries should remain proportionate to actual requirements.","test_case":"Inspect dependency graph and verify each module depends only on permitted layers/components.","test_input":{"architecture":"component dependency graph","implementation":"module dependency graph"},"expected_test_result":"Implementation dependencies conform to declared architecture and each component has traceable implementation ownership.","testing_guidance":"Use static dependency analysis plus targeted architectural tests where practical.","verification_method":"Perform architecture-to-code traceability review and dependency-conformance verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify both presence of required mappings and absence of prohibited dependencies.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python parser/static analysis tooling for automated dependency inspection where used"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established modular software architecture and dependency-management practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Interface Implementation","subtopic":"Architecture contracts to executable interfaces","concept":"An architectural interface should become an explicit implementation contract that defines callable behavior, input/output semantics, error behavior, and dependency direction.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"How should an architectural interface be implemented in code?","answer":"Represent an architectural interface with an explicit contract appropriate to the language, such as a Python Protocol or abstract base class, a TypeScript interface, or a Java interface. Concrete implementations should depend on and satisfy the contract rather than consumers depending on implementation details.","explanation":"An architecture-level interface is useful only when its implementation preserves the intended boundary. The interface should specify behavior rather than merely reproduce method names from a diagram.","principle":"Interfaces are executable boundaries for behavior, not decorative abstractions.","objective":"Turn architectural contracts into testable implementation boundaries.","requirements":["Explicit contract","Defined inputs and outputs","Defined failure semantics","Concrete implementation substitutability","Consumer independence from implementation details"],"constraints":["Not every internal function requires an interface","Language mechanisms differ","Over-abstraction can increase complexity"],"preconditions":["Architectural interaction contract is defined"],"input":{"contract":"architectural interface","implementation":"concrete component"},"output":{"interface_definition":"executable contract","implementation":"conforming concrete implementation"},"expected_behavior":"Consumers interact through the declared contract and remain independent of concrete implementation details.","incorrect_code":"class OrderService:\n    def init(self, postgres_repository):\n        self.repository = postgres_repository","corrected_code":"from typing import Protocol\n\nclass OrderRepository(Protocol):\n    def save(self, order: dict) -> None: ...\n\nclass OrderService:\n    def init(self, repository: OrderRepository):\n        self.repository = repository","failure_mode":"Consumers become coupled to a concrete infrastructure implementation.","expected_error":"Tests require real infrastructure or replacing an implementation requires consumer changes.","cause":"Architectural contract was not represented independently of its implementation.","root_cause":"Dependency direction points toward infrastructure rather than the architectural abstraction.","solution":"Define a stable behavioral contract and inject implementations through that boundary.","edge_case":"A concrete implementation can expose additional capabilities, but consumers using the architectural boundary should not depend on them unless the architecture explicitly requires it.","security_implication":"Interfaces should not accidentally expose privileged infrastructure operations to broader application layers.","performance_implication":"Interface dispatch overhead is normally secondary to the architectural benefit, but unnecessary abstraction layers should still be avoided in hot paths.","test_case":"Inject a test implementation satisfying the same contract and verify consumer behavior.","test_input":{"implementation":"in-memory repository implementing OrderRepository"},"expected_test_result":"OrderService operates correctly without knowledge of the concrete persistence implementation.","testing_guidance":"Test contract behavior independently and test each concrete implementation against the same contract where multiple implementations exist.","verification_method":"Compile/type-check where applicable and execute contract conformance tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify behavior, not merely method-name compatibility.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"typing","framework_version":"Python standard library","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+ for illustrative typing semantics"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B001"],"depends_on":["D35-B001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python typing Protocol documentation pattern and established dependency-inversion practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Dependency Direction","subtopic":"Dependency inversion in implementation","concept":"Implementation dependencies should point according to the architecture's ownership rules, with higher-level business behavior isolated from replaceable infrastructure details.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"How should architectural dependency direction be preserved in implementation?","answer":"Define stable abstractions at the boundary owned by the higher-level policy and make infrastructure implementations conform to those abstractions. Inject concrete dependencies at composition time rather than constructing infrastructure directly inside business logic.","explanation":"Direct construction of databases, network clients, file systems, or vendor SDKs inside domain/application logic creates hidden coupling. Dependency inversion makes infrastructure replaceable and makes higher-level behavior independently testable.","principle":"High-level policy should not depend directly on low-level implementation details when the architecture requires independent substitution.","objective":"Preserve architectural dependency direction in executable code.","requirements":["Stable abstraction boundary","Dependency injection","Composition root","No prohibited infrastructure construction inside core logic"],"constraints":["Dependency injection must not become an unnecessary framework requirement","Some simple infrastructure dependencies can legitimately remain direct when architecture permits"],"preconditions":["Dependency boundaries are defined"],"input":{"business_component":"higher-level behavior","infrastructure_component":"concrete implementation"},"output":{"composition":"wired implementation graph"},"expected_behavior":"Business/application code depends only on allowed contracts while concrete implementations are supplied externally.","incorrect_code":"class PaymentService:\n    def charge(self, amount):\n        client = StripeClient()\n        return client.charge(amount)","corrected_code":"class PaymentService:\n    def init(self, gateway):\n        self.gateway = gateway\n\n    def charge(self, amount):\n        return self.gateway.charge(amount)\n\n# Composition root supplies the concrete gateway.","failure_mode":"Changing infrastructure requires modifying business logic or tests require external systems.","expected_error":"Tight-coupling failure, difficult unit testing, or architecture-conformance violation.","cause":"Concrete dependency constructed inside policy logic.","root_cause":"Composition responsibility was placed inside the dependent component.","solution":"Move dependency construction to the composition root and inject an explicit contract.","edge_case":"Dependency inversion does not mean every function needs an interface; abstraction should correspond to a real boundary or substitution requirement.","security_implication":"Centralized composition makes privileged infrastructure dependencies easier to audit and restrict.","performance_implication":"Dependency injection itself should not materially affect performance in ordinary application code; unnecessary wrappers can add complexity.","test_case":"Replace the infrastructure implementation with a deterministic fake and run business tests without external services.","test_input":{"gateway":"fake payment gateway","amount":100},"expected_test_result":"Business behavior can be tested without constructing or contacting the real external gateway.","testing_guidance":"Test both business behavior with fakes and integration behavior with the real adapter separately.","verification_method":"Static dependency inspection plus unit and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Confirm business modules do not import or instantiate prohibited infrastructure implementations.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B002"],"depends_on":["D35-B002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established dependency inversion and composition-root implementation practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Application Service Implementation","subtopic":"Use-case orchestration","concept":"Application services should implement architectural use cases by coordinating domain behavior and infrastructure ports without absorbing unrelated business rules or infrastructure concerns.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"How should an architectural use case become an application service?","answer":"Implement the use case as an application-level operation that validates orchestration-level preconditions, invokes domain behavior, coordinates required ports, manages transaction boundaries where applicable, and returns an explicit result.","explanation":"Application services connect external requests to domain behavior. They should coordinate rather than become unstructured containers for every business rule and infrastructure operation.","principle":"Application services orchestrate use cases; domain components own domain rules and infrastructure adapters own infrastructure behavior.","objective":"Create a clear implementation boundary for application use cases.","requirements":["Explicit use-case operation","Domain behavior delegation","Port-based infrastructure access","Defined transaction/error semantics","Explicit result"],"constraints":["Simple applications may use fewer layers","Transaction behavior depends on persistence technology","Not every function needs a service class"],"preconditions":["Use case and domain responsibilities are defined"],"input":{"command":"application command","dependencies":"domain and infrastructure ports"},"output":{"result":"use-case result","side_effects":"declared state changes"},"expected_behavior":"The application service coordinates the use case while preserving domain and infrastructure boundaries.","incorrect_code":"class CreateOrder:\n    def execute(self, data):\n        db.execute("INSERT INTO orders ...")\n        if data["total"] < 0:\n            raise ValueError("invalid total")\n        send_email(data)\n        return True","corrected_code":"class CreateOrder:\n    def init(self, orders, notifier):\n        self.orders = orders\n        self.notifier = notifier\n\n    def execute(self, command):\n        order = Order.create(command)\n        self.orders.save(order)\n        self.notifier.notify_created(order)\n        return order","failure_mode":"Use-case code becomes a mixture of SQL, transport, notification, and business rules that cannot be independently tested.","expected_error":"High coupling, inconsistent transaction behavior, or difficult unit testing.","cause":"Application layer absorbs responsibilities owned by domain and infrastructure layers.","root_cause":"Architecture was not translated into explicit implementation responsibility boundaries.","solution":"Keep orchestration in application services, domain invariants in domain objects, and external integrations behind ports/adapters.","edge_case":"A small use case may legitimately have only a few lines; architectural layering should not create ceremonial code.","security_implication":"Authorization checks must occur at an appropriate application boundary and must not rely solely on client-provided command data.","performance_implication":"Extra orchestration layers generally have small overhead; avoid excessive object creation in proven hot paths.","test_case":"Execute the use case with deterministic fake ports and verify domain result and required side effects.","test_input":{"command":{"order_id":"O1","total":100}},"expected_test_result":"Use case produces the expected domain result and invokes required ports without direct infrastructure access.","testing_guidance":"Separate unit tests for orchestration, domain invariants, and adapter integration.","verification_method":"Trace each use-case step to the architecture and execute application-level tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that transaction, authorization, error, and side-effect semantics are explicit.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B003"],"depends_on":["D35-B003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established application-service/use-case implementation pattern."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Domain Model Implementation","subtopic":"Architectural business concepts as executable invariants","concept":"Architecture-level domain concepts should become implementation types or behaviors that enforce meaningful business invariants at the boundary where those invariants belong.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"How should domain concepts from architecture be represented in code?","answer":"Represent important domain concepts using types, objects, functions, or modules that make their invariants explicit and enforce them consistently. Avoid reducing all domain concepts to unrestricted primitive values when doing so loses required correctness constraints.","explanation":"A domain model is valuable when it makes important business rules executable. The exact implementation can range from simple validated values to richer entities depending on complexity.","principle":"Important domain invariants should be executable rather than existing only in documentation or caller assumptions.","objective":"Translate architectural domain concepts into enforceable implementation behavior.","requirements":["Explicit invariants","Consistent validation","Stable domain semantics","Clear ownership of business rules"],"constraints":["Do not create artificial domain objects for trivial values","Validation may depend on lifecycle state","Persistence representation should not dictate domain semantics"],"preconditions":["Domain rules are known"],"input":{"domain_value":"candidate domain data"},"output":{"domain_object":"validated domain representation"},"expected_behavior":"Invalid domain states are rejected at the domain boundary and valid states retain their defined semantics.","incorrect_code":"def create_account(balance):\n    return {"balance": balance}","corrected_code":"class Account:\n    def init(self, balance: int):\n        if balance < 0:\n            raise ValueError("balance cannot be negative")\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def debit(self, amount: int) -> None:\n        if amount < 0 or amount > self.balance:\n            raise ValueError("invalid debit")\n        self.balance -= amount","failure_mode":"Invalid domain states enter the system because callers must remember undocumented rules.","expected_error":"Invalid-state acceptance followed by downstream calculation or persistence errors.","cause":"Business invariants implemented inconsistently or not at all.","root_cause":"Domain responsibility was left to arbitrary callers.","solution":"Place stable domain invariants in the domain implementation that owns the concept.","edge_case":"Some constraints are contextual and belong to an application/use-case layer rather than the domain value itself.","security_implication":"Domain validation is not a substitute for authorization; callers still require explicit authority checks.","performance_implication":"Rich validation adds computation but should be evaluated against actual workload requirements.","test_case":"Construct valid and invalid domain states and exercise invariant-changing operations.","test_input":{"balances":[0,100,-1],"debits":[0,50,101,-1]},"expected_test_result":"Valid states and operations succeed; invalid states and operations are rejected deterministically.","testing_guidance":"Test boundary values, state transitions, invalid sequences, and invariant preservation after each operation.","verification_method":"Property/invariant tests plus direct domain unit tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify invariants after every state-changing operation rather than only at object creation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B004"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established domain-modeling and invariant-enforcement practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B006","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Repository Adapter Implementation","subtopic":"Persistence boundary implementation","concept":"A repository implementation should translate domain persistence needs into storage-specific operations while keeping storage mechanics behind the architectural persistence boundary.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"How should a persistence component be implemented behind an architectural repository boundary?","answer":"Define persistence operations around domain/application needs and implement them in a storage adapter. Keep SQL, ORM, driver-specific types, connection handling, and serialization details inside the adapter boundary.","explanation":"The repository boundary is useful when persistence technology is a replaceable concern or when it prevents domain/application code from depending on storage mechanics. The adapter must still preserve transaction and consistency semantics required by the architecture.","principle":"Persistence mechanics belong behind the persistence boundary; domain semantics belong outside storage-specific code.","objective":"Implement storage access without leaking infrastructure details into higher-level components.","requirements":["Explicit persistence contract","Mapping between domain and storage representations","Defined transaction semantics","Error translation","Resource lifecycle management"],"constraints":["A repository abstraction is not automatically justified for every CRUD application","ORM usage does not eliminate the need to understand transaction semantics"],"preconditions":["Persistence ownership and consistency requirements are defined"],"input":{"operation":"domain persistence operation","entity":"domain object"},"output":{"result":"domain-level persistence result"},"expected_behavior":"Storage operations fulfill the persistence contract without exposing storage-specific details to higher-level consumers.","incorrect_code":"class OrderService:\n    def save(self, order):\n        self.db.execute("INSERT INTO orders ...", order.total)","corrected_code":"class SqlOrderRepository:\n    def init(self, db):\n        self.db = db\n\n    def save(self, order):\n        self.db.execute(\n            "INSERT INTO orders(id, total) VALUES (?, ?)",\n            (order.id, order.total),\n        )","failure_mode":"Business code becomes coupled to database APIs and persistence representation.","expected_error":"Storage migration or testing requires broad application changes.","cause":"Persistence implementation leaked across architectural boundaries.","root_cause":"No explicit translation boundary between domain/application data and storage data.","solution":"Keep storage-specific operations inside the repository/adapter and translate errors and representations at the boundary.","edge_case":"Queries needed only for a specific read model may justify a separate query abstraction rather than forcing every access through a generic repository.","security_implication":"Parameterized queries and least-privilege database access remain required; repository boundaries do not inherently prevent injection.","performance_implication":"Abstraction layers must not hide inefficient query patterns; query count, indexing, and transaction behavior require measurement.","test_case":"Run repository integration tests against the supported database and application tests against a deterministic test implementation where appropriate.","test_input":{"order":{"id":"O1","total":100}},"expected_test_result":"Persisted data can be retrieved according to the repository contract and transaction/error semantics are preserved.","testing_guidance":"Test persistence mapping, transactions, constraints, concurrency behavior, failures, and migration compatibility.","verification_method":"Repository contract tests plus database integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify generated queries/operations where security or performance requirements make this material.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Python DB-API compatible implementation","framework_version":"Technology-specific","version_range":"Project-specific","dependencies":["database driver"],"execution_requirements":["Configured test database"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B002","D35-B004"],"depends_on":["D35-B002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established persistence adapter and repository implementation practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B007","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"External Adapter Implementation","subtopic":"Infrastructure integration boundary","concept":"External services should be integrated through adapters that translate the external API into the application's stable internal contract and isolate provider-specific behavior.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"How should an external service architecture component be implemented?","answer":"Define an internal capability contract and implement a provider-specific adapter behind it. The adapter should handle authentication, request/response mapping, provider errors, retries where appropriate, and lifecycle behavior without leaking provider-specific details into core application logic.","explanation":"Provider APIs change independently of application policy. An adapter creates a controlled translation boundary and makes provider-specific behavior testable and replaceable.","principle":"External provider protocols should terminate at an explicit integration boundary.","objective":"Isolate external-service implementation details from core application behavior.","requirements":["Internal capability contract","Provider mapping","Error translation","Timeout/retry policy","Credential handling","Integration tests"],"constraints":["Adapter abstractions should reflect real capability requirements","Retries must respect provider and operation idempotency","External failures remain possible"],"preconditions":["External capability and failure semantics are known"],"input":{"request":"internal capability request","provider":"external service"},"output":{"result":"internal capability result"},"expected_behavior":"Core code receives the internal contract result regardless of provider-specific wire representation.","incorrect_code":"def create_customer(customer):\n    return stripe.Customer.create(\n        email=customer.email,\n        metadata={"internal_id": customer.id}\n    )","corrected_code":"class CustomerGateway:\n    def create(self, customer):\n        raise NotImplementedError\n\nclass ProviderCustomerGateway(CustomerGateway):\n    def init(self, client):\n        self.client = client\n\n    def create(self, customer):\n        response = self.client.create_customer(email=customer.email)\n        return CustomerReference(provider_id=response.id)","failure_mode":"Provider-specific types and errors spread throughout the application.","expected_error":"Provider migration or API change requires widespread code modification.","cause":"External SDK calls are made directly from business/application code.","root_cause":"Integration boundary was not implemented as a controlled adapter.","solution":"Translate provider protocol into a stable internal capability contract and isolate provider-specific behavior in the adapter.","edge_case":"Provider-specific capabilities may exceed the common contract; expose them only when they are explicitly part of the architecture.","security_implication":"Credentials and sensitive provider responses must remain inside controlled integration boundaries and must not leak into logs.","performance_implication":"Network latency, retries, connection reuse, and serialization dominate many external calls; adapter design must preserve timeout and resource policies.","test_case":"Run adapter contract tests using a fake provider and separate integration tests against the authorized real provider environment.","test_input":{"customer":{"id":"C1","email":"test@example.invalid"}},"expected_test_result":"The adapter maps the internal request and provider response correctly and translates defined provider failures.","testing_guidance":"Test success, timeout, malformed response, authentication failure, rate limiting, retry behavior, and idempotency where applicable.","verification_method":"Contract tests plus controlled integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that provider-specific dependencies are confined to the adapter boundary.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Provider SDK","framework_version":"Must be pinned","version_range":"Provider-specific","dependencies":["provider SDK/client"],"execution_requirements":["Authorized provider test environment for integration verification"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B002","D35-B003"],"depends_on":["D35-B002","D35-B003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established anti-corruption-layer and external-adapter implementation practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B008","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Composition Root","subtopic":"Runtime dependency assembly","concept":"The composition root is the implementation location where concrete infrastructure components are assembled and injected into application components according to the architecture.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"Where should architectural dependencies be assembled into a runnable application?","answer":"Assemble concrete implementations in a composition root or equivalent bootstrap layer. Business and application components should receive their dependencies rather than constructing them internally.","explanation":"A composition root makes the runtime dependency graph explicit. It provides one controlled location for configuration, adapter selection, lifecycle management, and dependency injection without forcing the business layer to know deployment details.","principle":"Concrete dependency selection belongs at the application's assembly boundary.","objective":"Translate the architectural dependency graph into a controlled runtime object graph.","requirements":["Explicit construction order","Dependency injection","Configuration boundary","Lifecycle ownership","Environment-specific implementation selection"],"constraints":["Composition root should remain focused on assembly","Large applications may require hierarchical composition modules","Configuration should not silently alter security-sensitive dependencies"],"preconditions":["Concrete implementations are available","Architectural dependency graph is defined"],"input":{"components":"implementation components","configuration":"runtime configuration"},"output":{"application":"fully assembled runtime graph"},"expected_behavior":"Application startup constructs all required dependencies according to declared architecture and fails clearly when mandatory dependencies cannot be assembled.","incorrect_code":"class OrderService:\n    def init(self):\n        self.repository = SqlOrderRepository(connect_from_environment())\n        self.notifier = EmailNotifier(connect_from_environment())","corrected_code":"def build_application(config):\n    db = create_database(config.database)\n    repository = SqlOrderRepository(db)\n    notifier = EmailNotifier(config.email)\n    orders = OrderService(repository, notifier)\n    return orders","failure_mode":"Dependency creation is scattered across business classes and runtime configuration becomes implicit.","expected_error":"Inconsistent configuration, difficult testing, or environment-specific startup failures.","cause":"No explicit composition boundary.","root_cause":"Runtime assembly responsibilities were mixed with business behavior.","solution":"Centralize concrete dependency construction in a composition root and inject dependencies into consumers.","edge_case":"Worker processes and API processes may require different compositions while sharing the same core components.","security_implication":"The composition root is a high-value security boundary because it selects credentials, privileged clients, and infrastructure capabilities.","performance_implication":"Connection pools and expensive clients should generally be constructed according to their intended lifecycle rather than per request.","test_case":"Build the application using test implementations and verify the resulting dependency graph.","test_input":{"config":"test configuration","adapters":"deterministic test adapters"},"expected_test_result":"The application assembles successfully with controlled dependencies and core behavior remains infrastructure-independent.","testing_guidance":"Test startup validation, missing configuration, dependency selection, lifecycle cleanup, and environment-specific compositions.","verification_method":"Inspect and exercise the composition root against the architecture dependency graph.","verification_guidance":"Verify every concrete dependency has an explicit owner and lifecycle.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B003","D35-B007"],"depends_on":["D35-B003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established composition-root and dependency-injection implementation practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B009","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation-Level Testing Structure","subtopic":"Architecture-aligned test organization","concept":"Tests should map to implementation boundaries and architectural responsibilities so that domain behavior, application orchestration, adapters, and end-to-end integration can be verified at appropriate levels.","knowledge_type":"testing_implementation","question":"How should the architecture determine the implementation test structure?","answer":"Organize tests around behavior and boundaries: domain tests for invariants, application tests for use-case orchestration, adapter tests for external contracts, and integration/end-to-end tests for composed behavior. Avoid making every test a full-system test.","explanation":"Architecture provides natural verification boundaries. Testing each concern at its narrowest appropriate boundary gives faster diagnosis while selected higher-level tests verify that the pieces actually compose correctly.","principle":"Test each architectural responsibility at the narrowest level that can provide meaningful evidence, then verify critical cross-boundary behavior at higher levels.","objective":"Create an implementation test structure that supports fast diagnosis and architectural verification.","requirements":["Unit tests for core behavior","Application/use-case tests","Adapter/integration tests","Critical end-to-end tests","Traceability to requirements"],"constraints":["Test boundaries depend on architecture","Not every component requires every test level","Mocks can hide integration failures"],"preconditions":["Implementation boundaries exist","Expected behaviors are defined"],"input":{"implementation":"code structure","requirements":"expected behaviors"},"output":{"test_structure":"tests mapped to implementation boundaries","evidence":"verification results"},"expected_behavior":"Failures identify the smallest relevant implementation boundary while higher-level tests verify important composition behavior.","incorrect_code":"def test_create_order():\n    # Only a full deployment test exists for every behavior.\n    deploy_entire_system()\n    call_real_database()\n    call_real_payment_provider()","corrected_code":"def test_order_total_domain_rule():\n    order = Order.create({"total": 100})\n    assert order.total == 100\n\n# Separate integration tests verify real adapter composition.","failure_mode":"All tests are broad integration tests, making failures slow and difficult to diagnose.","expected_error":"High test cost, weak fault localization, or dependency on unstable external systems.","cause":"Test structure ignores architectural boundaries.","root_cause":"Implementation responsibilities were not mapped to appropriate verification levels.","solution":"Use focused tests for domain/application behavior and targeted integration/end-to-end tests for cross-boundary guarantees.","edge_case":"A distributed or asynchronous workflow may require more integration coverage because important behavior cannot be proven entirely through isolated unit tests.","security_implication":"Security-critical boundaries need tests that exercise real authorization and data-isolation behavior rather than only mocked responses.","performance_implication":"Fast focused tests reduce feedback time; performance tests should remain separate from ordinary correctness tests.","test_case":"Introduce a controlled domain failure and verify that the corresponding focused test identifies it without requiring unrelated infrastructure.","test_input":{"failure":"invalid_order_total"},"expected_test_result":"The domain test fails directly at the invariant under test while integration tests cover broader composition separately.","testing_guidance":"Maintain a deliberate test pyramid or equivalent architecture-aligned strategy and avoid blanket mock-based coverage.","verification_method":"Review test-to-component mapping and execute representative tests at each boundary.","verification_guidance":"Verify that important architectural interactions have at least one appropriate integration-level test.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"pytest or equivalent test framework","framework_version":"Must be pinned","version_range":"Project-specific","dependencies":["test runner"],"execution_requirements":["Project test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B005","D35-B006","D35-B007"],"depends_on":["D35-B004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established layered testing and architecture-aligned verification practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B010","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Deployment Structure Implementation","subtopic":"Logical architecture to deployable units","concept":"Deployment structure should implement the architecture's runtime boundaries, lifecycle, scaling, isolation, and operational requirements without creating physical deployables solely because logical components exist.","knowledge_type":"deployment_implementation","question":"How should an architecture be translated into deployable implementation units?","answer":"Map logical components to deployable units only when their independent lifecycle, scaling, ownership, isolation, availability, or resource requirements justify separate deployment. Components without such requirements can remain within the same deployable while retaining logical module boundaries.","explanation":"Logical modularity and physical deployment are different decisions. A deployment boundary introduces networking, configuration, observability, failure, release, and operational complexity, so it should correspond to a real runtime requirement.","principle":"Create physical deployment boundaries when runtime characteristics require them, not merely to mirror architecture diagrams.","objective":"Translate logical architecture into an operationally appropriate deployment structure.","requirements":["Explicit deployable boundaries","Runtime dependency mapping","Configuration ownership","Health/lifecycle behavior","Operational verification"],"constraints":["Separate deployables introduce network and operational failure modes","Shared deployment can simplify local communication","Scaling needs may differ between components"],"preconditions":["Logical components and runtime requirements are defined"],"input":{"architecture":"logical component graph","operational_requirements":"scaling/isolation/lifecycle requirements"},"output":{"deployment_units":"deployable components","runtime_graph":"deployment dependency graph"},"expected_behavior":"Each deployable contains the logical components appropriate to its runtime boundary and communicates through declared contracts.","incorrect_code":"# Anti-pattern: create a network service for every class\nclass CustomerService: pass\nclass OrderService: pass\nclass PricingService: pass\n# Each is deployed separately without an independent runtime requirement.","corrected_code":"# Illustrative deployment manifest concept\ndeployment = {\n    "unit": "application",\n    "modules": ["customer", "order", "pricing"],\n    "external_dependencies": ["database", "message_gateway"],\n    "independent_scaling": False\n}\n# Split a component only when its runtime requirements justify it.","failure_mode":"Deployment complexity grows without corresponding operational value.","expected_error":"Excessive network dependencies, difficult releases, configuration drift, or unnecessary resource overhead.","cause":"Logical architecture was copied mechanically into physical deployment units.","root_cause":"Deployment boundaries were selected without runtime requirements.","solution":"Evaluate independent scaling, lifecycle, ownership, fault isolation, security, and release requirements before creating separate deployables.","edge_case":"A component may initially share a deployment and later become independently deployable when measurable operational requirements emerge.","security_implication":"Separate deployables can create stronger network isolation, but they also create additional credentials, trust boundaries, and exposed interfaces that must be secured.","performance_implication":"Remote calls introduce network latency and serialization overhead compared with in-process calls; deployment separation should be justified by measured or required operational properties.","test_case":"Deploy the selected topology in a clean environment and verify startup, communication, health, configuration, and failure behavior.","test_input":{"topology":"declared deployment topology","components":"representative implementation"},"expected_test_result":"All required components start, communicate through declared boundaries, and satisfy operational acceptance criteria.","testing_guidance":"Test deployment startup, dependency failure, configuration, health checks, rolling changes where applicable, and cross-boundary contract behavior.","verification_method":"Compare deployment manifest/runtime topology with architectural deployment requirements and execute operational acceptance tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual deployed boundaries rather than relying solely on configuration files.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Deployment technology","framework_version":"Must be pinned","version_range":"Environment-specific","dependencies":["runtime","deployment platform"],"execution_requirements":["Target deployment environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B001","D35-B008","D35-B009"],"depends_on":["D35-B001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established modular deployment and architecture-to-runtime mapping practice."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B011-R01","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Transaction Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Atomic application operation","concept":"A transaction boundary groups state changes that must succeed or fail together.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an application service implement an atomic transaction boundary?","answer":"Place the transaction boundary around the complete unit of work whose persistent changes must commit together, while keeping transport concerns outside it.","explanation":"The application layer defines the business operation; the transaction mechanism ensures its required persistence changes are committed atomically. The boundary must not accidentally exclude required writes or include unrelated long-running work.","principle":"Atomicity should follow the business operation's consistency boundary.","objective":"Implement an application operation with an explicit atomic persistence boundary.","requirements":["All required writes participate in the same transaction.","Commit occurs only after the operation succeeds.","Failure causes rollback of transactional changes."],"constraints":["Do not hold database transactions across external network calls unless explicitly required and supported.","Transaction behavior depends on the persistence technology."],"preconditions":["A transaction-capable repository or unit-of-work abstraction exists."],"input":"Application command and transaction-scoped dependencies.","output":"Committed state or an application failure with no partial transactional commit.","expected_behavior":"Successful execution commits all participating changes; failure rolls them back.","incorrect_code":"def create_order(repo, payment):\n    order = repo.create_order()\n    payment.authorize(order.total)\n    repo.save(order)\n    repo.commit()","corrected_code":"def create_order(uow, payment, command):\n    with uow.transaction():\n        order = uow.orders.create(command)\n        payment.authorize(order.total)\n        uow.orders.save(order)\n","failure_mode":"Partial state may persist when required writes are outside the transaction boundary.","expected_error":"Transaction or persistence error, depending on the implementation.","cause":"The transaction does not encompass all state changes that require atomicity.","root_cause":"The implementation boundary was chosen around technical calls instead of the business consistency unit.","solution":"Define the application operation first, then place one transaction boundary around all required transactional persistence changes.","edge_case":"An external side effect succeeds before the database transaction rolls back; database rollback cannot automatically undo the external effect.","security_implication":"Partial authorization or account state can create security-sensitive inconsistencies if financial or privilege changes are involved.","performance_implication":"Long transactions increase lock duration and contention.","test_case":"Force a persistence failure after one transactional write and verify that no transactional state remains committed.","test_input":"Command causing a deliberate failure after the first write.","expected_test_result":"All transactional writes are rolled back.","testing_guidance":"Use a real transactional test database or a persistence implementation that faithfully reproduces rollback semantics.","verification_method":"Inspect committed state after both successful and failing executions.","verification_guidance":"Verify atomicity rather than merely asserting that an exception was raised.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["A transaction-capable persistence abstraction"],"execution_requirements":["Configured transactional persistence implementation"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B004-R01","D35-B006-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B004-R01"],"requires":["D35-B006-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B004-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B004-R01"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established transactional application-service and unit-of-work design pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B011-R02","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Transaction Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Transaction scope","concept":"Transaction scope should contain the minimum set of operations required for atomic consistency.","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"What determines the correct transaction scope?","answer":"The transaction scope is determined by the set of state changes that must have one atomic commit or rollback outcome.","explanation":"Technical convenience is not sufficient to define transaction scope. The implementation should identify the business invariant first and then include the persistent changes necessary to preserve it.","principle":"Business consistency determines transaction scope; implementation mechanics realize it.","objective":"Prevent both partial commits and unnecessarily broad transactions.","requirements":["Identify all state changes participating in the invariant.","Keep unrelated work outside the transaction where possible."],"constraints":["Some workflows intentionally use multiple transactions and compensating actions.","External systems may not participate in the same transaction."],"preconditions":["The consistency requirements of the use case are documented or inferable from its invariants."],"input":"Business operation and its consistency requirements.","output":"A transaction boundary aligned with those requirements.","expected_behavior":"Required state changes share one atomic outcome.","incorrect_code":"def transfer(a, b, amount, db):\n    db.debit(a, amount)\n    send_email(a)\n    db.credit(b, amount)","corrected_code":"def transfer(a, b, amount, uow):\n    with uow.transaction():\n        uow.accounts.debit(a, amount)\n        uow.accounts.credit(b, amount)\n    send_email(a)","failure_mode":"Unrelated slow work extends transaction duration or required writes are split across commits.","expected_error":"Potential lock timeout, deadlock, or consistency failure.","cause":"Transaction scope is based on call order rather than consistency requirements.","root_cause":"No explicit mapping between domain invariants and persistence atomicity.","solution":"Map each invariant to the minimum persistence changes required to maintain it atomically.","edge_case":"An email or webhook sent after commit can fail without invalidating the committed database transaction.","security_implication":"Incorrect transaction scope can leave authorization or ownership state inconsistent.","performance_implication":"Narrower valid transaction scopes generally reduce lock contention.","test_case":"Measure behavior when unrelated external work is delayed while transactional writes execute.","test_input":"Delayed notification service combined with a transactional state change.","expected_test_result":"The notification delay does not unnecessarily extend the database transaction.","testing_guidance":"Use instrumentation around transaction entry and exit to verify actual scope.","verification_method":"Trace transaction boundaries against the required invariant.","verification_guidance":"Confirm every required write is inside the boundary and unrelated slow work is outside where safe.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Transaction-capable persistence layer"],"execution_requirements":["Persistence instrumentation"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.95,"related_records":["D35-B011-R01","D35-B004-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B004-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B011-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established transaction-boundary and consistency-boundary design practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B011-R03","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Transaction Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Rollback behavior","concept":"Rollback must restore transactional state when an operation fails before commit.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should rollback behavior be verified?","answer":"Inject failures at relevant transaction stages and inspect persistent state after the operation terminates.","explanation":"An exception alone does not prove rollback. Verification must inspect durable state and confirm that changes made within the transaction were not committed.","principle":"Rollback is a state property that must be verified against persistence.","objective":"Detect implementations that report failure while leaving partial state.","requirements":["Test failure before commit.","Inspect all affected transactional records.","Distinguish transactional state from external side effects."],"constraints":["Rollback semantics vary by persistence technology.","External side effects may not roll back."],"preconditions":["A repeatable failure injection mechanism exists."],"input":"Failing transactional operation.","output":"No partial transactional state after rollback.","expected_behavior":"The persistent transaction returns to its pre-operation state.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    repo.begin()\n    repo.insert(first)\n    risky_operation()\n    repo.insert(second)\n    repo.commit()\nexcept Exception:\n    log_error()\n","corrected_code":"try:\n    repo.begin()\n    repo.insert(first)\n    risky_operation()\n    repo.insert(second)\n    repo.commit()\nexcept Exception:\n    repo.rollback()\n    raise","failure_mode":"Exception is swallowed or rollback is omitted.","expected_error":"Original operation error should propagate after rollback.","cause":"Failure path does not explicitly restore transaction state.","root_cause":"Rollback was treated as an automatic consequence without verifying the persistence API semantics.","solution":"Use the persistence abstraction's transaction context or explicit rollback path and re-raise the original failure.","edge_case":"A rollback itself may fail; the implementation should preserve enough diagnostics to identify both failures.","security_implication":"Partial persistence of security-sensitive changes can create inconsistent access or ownership state.","performance_implication":"Failure-path rollback work may hold resources until completion.","test_case":"Inject an exception between two writes and inspect durable state.","test_input":"Failure after first write.","expected_test_result":"First write is absent after the operation fails.","testing_guidance":"Run the test against the actual transaction implementation rather than a mock that does not model rollback.","verification_method":"Compare pre-operation and post-failure persistent state.","verification_guidance":"Require equality for all transactional entities that should remain unchanged.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Transactional persistence implementation"],"execution_requirements":["Test database with rollback support"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B011-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B011-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B011-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B011-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established transaction rollback testing practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B011-R04","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Transaction Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Failure testing","concept":"Transaction failure injection tests whether every relevant failure path preserves atomicity.","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"What test demonstrates that an implementation preserves atomicity?","answer":"Execute a multi-write operation with a deterministic failure inserted between writes, then assert that none of the transactional writes were committed.","explanation":"The test must inspect durable state after the failure. A successful exception assertion alone cannot detect partial commits.","principle":"Failure-path tests must verify state, not only control flow.","objective":"Validate rollback at the implementation boundary.","requirements":["Use a deterministic failure point.","Capture pre-operation state.","Compare post-failure durable state with the baseline."],"constraints":["Failure injection should not alter production behavior.","The test must run against transaction semantics representative of production."],"preconditions":["A transaction-capable test persistence implementation exists."],"input":"Baseline state plus command configured to fail between writes.","output":"Assertion that transactional state remains unchanged.","expected_behavior":"The failed operation produces no partial transactional commit.","incorrect_code":"assert raises(RuntimeError, lambda: service.run(command))","corrected_code":"before = repo.snapshot()\nwith expect_error(RuntimeError):\n    service.run(command)\nafter = repo.snapshot()\nassert after == before","failure_mode":"Tests only verify exceptions and miss partial persistence.","expected_error":"The injected failure type.","cause":"Test lacks durable-state assertions.","root_cause":"Failure testing focuses on control flow rather than consistency outcome.","solution":"Capture and compare relevant persistent state before and after failure.","edge_case":"If the operation intentionally commits an earlier independent transaction, that earlier state must not be incorrectly included in the rollback expectation.","security_implication":"Atomicity tests should include security-relevant state when permissions or ownership are modified.","performance_implication":"State snapshots can be expensive for large datasets; scope assertions to affected entities.","test_case":"Failure after the first required write in a two-write atomic operation.","test_input":"Command with deterministic injected failure.","expected_test_result":"No required transactional write remains committed.","testing_guidance":"Use narrow state assertions for deterministic, fast tests.","verification_method":"Persistent-state comparison.","verification_guidance":"Verify both rollback and successful commit paths.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Test framework","Transactional persistence"],"execution_requirements":["Representative test database"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B011-R03","D35-B011-R05"],"depends_on":["D35-B011-R03"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B011-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established transactional failure-testing pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B011-R05","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Transaction Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Atomicity verification","concept":"Atomicity is verified by proving either the complete transactional outcome or the absence of the transactional changes after failure.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How can transaction atomicity be verified at implementation level?","answer":"Verify that success produces the complete intended persistent state and every tested failure path leaves the transactional state at its defined pre-operation or compensating boundary.","explanation":"Verification must connect the implementation to the intended consistency invariant. It should cover success, expected failures, concurrency where relevant, and persistence state.","principle":"Implementation verification must establish the required state transition, not merely code-path execution.","objective":"Provide evidence that the transaction implementation satisfies its atomicity requirement.","requirements":["Define expected pre-state and post-state.","Verify successful commit.","Verify representative failure rollback."],"constraints":["Atomicity cannot be inferred solely from source-code structure.","Distributed side effects may require separate compensation or outcome verification."],"preconditions":["The consistency invariant is explicitly defined."],"input":"Transaction implementation, invariant, and test evidence.","output":"Verification result describing whether the implementation satisfies atomicity.","expected_behavior":"Required atomicity cases are demonstrated by executable or inspectable evidence.","incorrect_code":"def verify(service):\n    return service.run(command) is not None","corrected_code":"def verify_atomicity(service, repo, command):\n    before = repo.snapshot()\n    result = service.run(command)\n    after = repo.snapshot()\n    assert atomicity_invariant(before, after, result)","failure_mode":"Verification accepts any returned value without checking persistent consistency.","expected_error":"Verification failure when state does not satisfy the invariant.","cause":"Verification checks execution instead of outcome.","root_cause":"No explicit state-based acceptance criterion.","solution":"Define and verify the expected state transition and rollback behavior.","edge_case":"A valid operation may intentionally produce a state different from the initial state; verification must use the specified invariant rather than equality by default.","security_implication":"Atomicity verification is especially important for operations affecting permissions, ownership, or financial state.","performance_implication":"Verification may require additional persistence reads.","test_case":"Verify successful and failing executions against explicit state invariants.","test_input":"Success command and deterministic failure command.","expected_test_result":"Both paths satisfy their respective state invariants.","testing_guidance":"Keep verification criteria independent of implementation-specific incidental details.","verification_method":"State-invariant assertion backed by test evidence.","verification_guidance":"Record the evidence and execution status separately from the logical verification result.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Transactional persistence","Test/verification framework"],"execution_requirements":["Representative executable test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B011-R03","D35-B011-R04"],"depends_on":["D35-B011-R03","D35-B011-R04"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B011-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established state-invariant verification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B012-R01","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Idempotent Command Implementation","subtopic":"Retry safety","concept":"An idempotent command produces the same intended business outcome when the same command is safely retried.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a retryable command become idempotent?","answer":"Give the command a stable idempotency key and persist enough outcome information to recognize a previously completed request before applying its effect again.","explanation":"Network failures can leave the caller uncertain whether an operation completed. A stable request identity lets the application distinguish a retry from a new command.","principle":"Retries require stable operation identity and durable duplicate detection.","objective":"Prevent duplicate business effects during safe command retries.","requirements":["Use a stable key generated by the caller or workflow.","Persist the key with the relevant outcome.","Enforce uniqueness at the persistence boundary."],"constraints":["The idempotency record and business effect must share an appropriate atomic boundary.","Idempotency keys require an expiration or retention strategy where indefinite storage is unnecessary."],"preconditions":["The command has a defined retry identity."],"input":"Command containing a stable idempotency key.","output":"Original outcome for duplicate retries or a newly applied outcome for a new key.","expected_behavior":"Repeated delivery of the same command does not create an additional business effect.","incorrect_code":"def charge(command, payment):\n    return payment.charge(command.amount)","corrected_code":"def charge(command, uow, payment):\n    with uow.transaction():\n        previous = uow.idempotency.find(command.key)\n        if previous:\n            return previous.result\n        result = payment.charge(command.amount)\n        uow.idempotency.store(command.key, result)\n        return result","failure_mode":"A retry executes the business effect twice.","expected_error":"Potential duplicate-operation error from the business or provider layer.","cause":"No durable request identity or atomic duplicate check.","root_cause":"The implementation assumes transport delivery exactly once.","solution":"Persist an idempotency key and atomically bind duplicate detection to the operation outcome where supported.","edge_case":"A reused key with materially different payload should be rejected rather than treated as the same command.","security_implication":"Predictable or reusable keys can enable unauthorized replay if authorization is not independently enforced.","performance_implication":"Idempotency lookup adds storage work; indexing the key is important.","test_case":"Submit the same command twice with the same key.","test_input":"Two identical commands sharing one idempotency key.","expected_test_result":"Only one business effect occurs and both attempts receive the defined result semantics.","testing_guidance":"Also test same key with different payload and concurrent duplicate submissions.","verification_method":"Inspect business-effect count and returned outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Verify uniqueness constraints and atomicity under concurrency.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Persistent idempotency store","Transactional persistence"],"execution_requirements":["Database with unique constraint support"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B011-R01","D35-B012-R02"],"depends_on":["D35-B011-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B012-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B012-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established idempotency-key and retry-safe