{"record_id":"D35-B012-R05","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Idempotent Command Implementation","subtopic":"Idempotency verification","concept":"Idempotency is verified by measuring business-effect cardinality and command outcome consistency across retries.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"What evidence verifies that a command is idempotent?","answer":"Evidence should show that repeated equivalent requests produce the defined same logical outcome without creating additional business effects.","explanation":"Returning the same response is insufficient if the underlying operation happened twice. Verification must inspect the actual effect and persistence guarantees.","principle":"Verify logical effect cardinality, not merely response similarity.","objective":"Establish retry safety with implementation evidence.","requirements":["Observe business-effect count.","Verify duplicate detection persistence.","Test concurrent duplicate delivery."],"constraints":["Some operations intentionally produce new effects on every invocation and should not be labeled idempotent.","External provider semantics may limit end-to-end guarantees."],"preconditions":["The intended idempotency scope is documented."],"input":"Implementation and retry test evidence.","output":"Verification result for idempotency.","expected_behavior":"At most one intended business effect occurs per logical command identity within the defined scope.","incorrect_code":"def verify(result1, result2):\n    return result1 == result2","corrected_code":"def verify(effect_count, result1, result2):\n    return effect_count == 1 and result1 == result2","failure_mode":"Verification accepts duplicate side effects when responses match.","expected_error":"Verification failure.","cause":"No effect-cardinality criterion.","root_cause":"Idempotency was reduced to response equality.","solution":"Verify persistent effects and retry outcomes together.","edge_case":"A command may return different representations of the same stored outcome; verification should compare semantic outcome rather than serialized identity where appropriate.","security_implication":"Replay protection for sensitive commands must be verified independently from authentication.","performance_implication":"Effect inspection may require additional queries or audit records.","test_case":"Verify sequential and concurrent retries.","test_input":"Repeated same-key commands.","expected_test_result":"One logical effect and consistent defined outcome.","testing_guidance":"Keep verification evidence tied to the command's documented idempotency scope.","verification_method":"Effect-count and persistence-state verification.","verification_guidance":"Record test execution evidence separately from the assertion logic.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Idempotency persistence","Test framework"],"execution_requirements":["Representative persistence and concurrency environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B012-R01","D35-B012-R04"],"depends_on":["D35-B012-R04"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B012-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B012-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established idempotency verification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B013-R01","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Control Implementation","subtopic":"Optimistic concurrency","concept":"Optimistic concurrency control rejects stale updates by requiring the expected version to match the current persisted version.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should optimistic concurrency be implemented for an update?","answer":"Read the entity version, include that expected version in the update predicate, increment the version on success, and treat zero affected rows as a concurrency conflict.","explanation":"The conditional update makes the version check and state change one persistence operation. A stale writer therefore cannot silently overwrite a newer state.","principle":"Validate the expected version at the same atomic persistence boundary as the write.","objective":"Prevent lost updates without holding long-lived locks.","requirements":["Persist a monotonic version or equivalent concurrency token.","Require expected version from the caller or loaded entity.","Atomically compare and update the version."],"constraints":["Version values must be managed consistently.","The technique does not automatically resolve semantic conflicts."],"preconditions":["Entities have a concurrency token."],"input":"Entity identifier, expected version, and proposed changes.","output":"Successful update with incremented version or concurrency conflict.","expected_behavior":"Only a writer holding the current expected version can perform the conditional update.","incorrect_code":"entity = repo.get(entity_id)\nentity.name = new_name\nrepo.save(entity)","corrected_code":"updated = repo.update_if_version(entity_id, expected_version, {"name": new_name})\nif not updated:\n    raise ConcurrencyConflict(entity_id, expected_version)","failure_mode":"A stale writer overwrites a newer update.","expected_error":"ConcurrencyConflict or equivalent application error.","cause":"Write does not condition on the previously observed version.","root_cause":"Read-modify-write sequence lacks an atomic concurrency predicate.","solution":"Use an atomic compare-and-update operation.","edge_case":"Two updates to independent fields may still conflict even when their semantic changes could have been merged.","security_implication":"Stale authorization or ownership updates must not silently overwrite newer security state.","performance_implication":"Optimistic control avoids many locks but may increase retries under contention.","test_case":"Load the same entity twice and attempt both updates with the same version.","test_input":"Two writers using one expected version.","expected_test_result":"One update succeeds and the other receives a concurrency conflict.","testing_guidance":"Use actual concurrent transactions or a deterministic equivalent.","verification_method":"Inspect version and final entity state.","verification_guidance":"Verify exactly one conditional update succeeds for a shared version.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Persistence supporting conditional update"],"execution_requirements":["Database or store with atomic conditional writes"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B013-R02","D35-B013-R04"],"depends_on":["D35-B006-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B006-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B013-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B013-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established optimistic concurrency control pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B013-R02","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Control Implementation","subtopic":"Concurrency token","concept":"A concurrency token represents the persisted revision that a writer observed before attempting an update.","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"What role does a concurrency token play in architecture-to-implementation mapping?","answer":"It carries the entity revision from the read boundary into the write boundary so the persistence layer can detect stale updates.","explanation":"The token creates an explicit contract between the loaded representation and the conditional update. It should be treated as part of concurrency semantics rather than incidental metadata.","principle":"A write must identify the revision on which its proposed change is based.","objective":"Make stale-write detection explicit and testable.","requirements":["Token is persisted.","Read results expose the token where updates are allowed.","Writes require the expected token."],"constraints":["Token semantics vary by storage technology.","A timestamp should not be assumed safe as a concurrency token without appropriate guarantees."],"preconditions":["The entity supports revision tracking."],"input":"Persisted entity revision.","output":"Concurrency-aware entity representation.","expected_behavior":"A stale token causes the update to be rejected rather than silently applied.","incorrect_code":"entity.updated_at = now()\nrepo.save(entity)","corrected_code":"entity.version = loaded_version\nrepo.update_if_version(entity.id, entity.version, changes)","failure_mode":"Timestamp or mutable metadata is used without an atomic comparison.","expected_error":"Concurrency conflict may be missed.","cause":"No explicit compare-and-update token.","root_cause":"Revision information is not part of the write contract.","solution":"Expose a dedicated version/concurrency token and enforce it during persistence.","edge_case":"Clock precision and synchronization make timestamps unsuitable for some concurrency requirements.","security_implication":"Security policy changes can be lost if stale writes are accepted.","performance_implication":"Small numeric versions are cheap to index and compare.","test_case":"Attempt an update with an intentionally stale token.","test_input":"Expected version lower than current persisted version.","expected_test_result":"Update is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Test token behavior independently from business field validation.","verification_method":"Conditional-write outcome inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify token increments exactly once on successful updates.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Revision-aware persistence"],"execution_requirements":["Persistence supporting version comparison"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B013-R01","D35-B013-R03"],"depends_on":["D35-B006-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B013-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established concurrency-token design practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B013-R03","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Control Implementation","subtopic":"Conflict handling","concept":"A concurrency conflict is an expected application outcome that should be translated into a deterministic retry or user-resolution path.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a stale update be handled after the persistence layer detects a conflict?","answer":"Translate the persistence conflict into a typed application conflict and require an explicit retry, refresh, or merge strategy rather than silently overwriting the newer state.","explanation":"Concurrency control detects the conflict; application policy determines how it is resolved. Detection and resolution should remain separate responsibilities.","principle":"Detect stale state at persistence and resolve it according to application semantics.","objective":"Prevent hidden lost updates while keeping conflict handling explicit.","requirements":["Persistence reports conflict deterministically.","Application layer exposes a typed conflict.","Caller has a defined resolution path."],"constraints":["Automatic retry is safe only when the operation semantics permit it.","Merge strategies must preserve domain invariants."],"preconditions":["Conditional update is implemented."],"input":"Concurrency conflict from persistence.","output":"Typed application-level conflict and resolution metadata.","expected_behavior":"The stale operation does not overwrite newer state.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    repo.update_if_version(...)\nexcept Exception:\n    return repo.force_update(...)","corrected_code":"updated = repo.update_if_version(...)\nif not updated:\n    raise ConcurrencyConflict(entity_id)","failure_mode":"Conflict is hidden by a forced overwrite.","expected_error":"ConcurrencyConflict.","cause":"Application treats conflict as an infrastructure nuisance instead of a consistency signal.","root_cause":"No explicit conflict policy.","solution":"Expose the conflict and apply a documented retry, refresh, or merge policy.","edge_case":"Automatic retries can repeatedly fail under high contention.","security_implication":"Forced overwrites can revert security controls or ownership decisions.","performance_implication":"Repeated retries under contention can amplify load.","test_case":"Cause a stale update and verify that no force-update fallback occurs.","test_input":"Stale version update.","expected_test_result":"Typed conflict is returned and current state remains intact.","testing_guidance":"Assert both exception type and final persisted state.","verification_method":"Conflict-path state inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify resolution policy separately from conflict detection.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Application error taxonomy","Conditional persistence"],"execution_requirements":["Representative persistence implementation"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B013-R01","D35-B019-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B013-R01"],"requires":["D35-B019-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B013-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B013-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B013-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established optimistic-concurrency conflict handling practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B013-R04","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Control Implementation","subtopic":"Race testing","concept":"A deterministic concurrent-update test should prove that stale writers cannot both commit the same revision.","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"How should optimistic concurrency be tested?","answer":"Arrange two writers to read the same revision, then attempt conditional updates and assert that exactly one succeeds.","explanation":"The key behavior is the atomic compare-and-update. A sequential test cannot establish that the implementation handles competing writers.","principle":"Concurrency correctness requires a test that exercises competing revisions.","objective":"Detect lost-update vulnerabilities.","requirements":["Two writers observe one revision.","Both attempt writes.","Persistence outcome is inspected."],"constraints":["Thread scheduling alone is not deterministic enough for reliable race tests.","The persistence system must provide atomic conditional writes."],"preconditions":["Concurrency token support exists."],"input":"Two updates against one initial revision.","output":"One success and one conflict.","expected_behavior":"The current revision advances once for the successful write.","incorrect_code":"first = repo.get(id)\nsecond = repo.get(id)\nrepo.save(first)\nrepo.save(second)","corrected_code":"first = repo.get(id)\nsecond = repo.get(id)\nassert repo.update_if_version(id, first.version, first_changes)\nassert not repo.update_if_version(id, second.version, second_changes)","failure_mode":"Both writers commit despite sharing the same original revision.","expected_error":"ConcurrencyConflict for the losing writer.","cause":"Conditional comparison is not atomic.","root_cause":"The implementation performs separate read and write checks.","solution":"Use one atomic conditional write operation and test it under competing writers.","edge_case":"A storage engine may serialize operations and mask concurrency bugs in a test environment that differs from production.","security_implication":"Race tests should include concurrent changes to security-relevant attributes where applicable.","performance_implication":"High-contention conflict rates should be measured separately from correctness tests.","test_case":"Two writers update one entity from the same version.","test_input":"Shared version with distinct changes.","expected_test_result":"Exactly one conditional update succeeds.","testing_guidance":"Prefer database-supported atomic conditional updates and controlled barriers.","verification_method":"Count successful updates and inspect final version.","verification_guidance":"Verify final state corresponds to one successful writer only.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Transactional persistence","Concurrency test harness"],"execution_requirements":["Representative concurrent storage"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B013-R01","D35-B013-R03","D35-B013-R05"],"depends_on":["D35-B013-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B013-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established optimistic-concurrency race-testing practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
