{"record_id":"D35-B015-R03","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Domain Event Implementation","subtopic":"Dispatch timing","concept":"Domain-event dispatch timing must match whether the event represents an attempted action, a committed state change, or another explicitly defined occurrence.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should event dispatch timing be selected?","answer":"Define whether the event means an in-memory domain occurrence or a committed durable fact, then dispatch at the corresponding lifecycle point.","explanation":"Publishing an event before persistence commit can expose a state that later rolls back. Publishing after commit changes reliability and retry considerations.","principle":"Event semantics determine dispatch timing.","objective":"Prevent consumers from observing events whose intended state change did not become durable.","requirements":["Define event semantic point.","Align dispatch with transaction outcome.","Use reliable delivery where events cross persistence boundaries."],"constraints":["Exactly-once external delivery is generally not guaranteed by ordinary application code.","Some internal domain events intentionally remain in-process."],"preconditions":["Event semantics are explicitly documented."],"input":"Domain operation and transaction lifecycle.","output":"Event dispatched at the defined semantic boundary.","expected_behavior":"Consumers observe events consistent with the event's declared meaning.","incorrect_code":"event_bus.publish(OrderApproved(...))\nrepo.commit()","corrected_code":"with uow.transaction():\n    order.approve()\n    uow.save(order)\n    uow.record_domain_event(OrderApproved(order.id))\n# durable dispatch strategy occurs according to the event boundary","failure_mode":"Consumer observes an approval event although persistence later rolls back.","expected_error":"Downstream inconsistency rather than necessarily a runtime exception.","cause":"Event publicatio