{"record_id":"D35-B016-R01","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Integration Event Publishing","subtopic":"External event boundary","concept":"Integration-event publishing translates internal domain occurrences into externally consumable contracts at a defined integration boundary.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an integration event be implemented from an internal domain event?","answer":"Map the internal event to a stable external contract and publish it through an explicit integration boundary without exposing internal domain objects.","explanation":"Internal domain models and external consumers evolve at different rates. An integration event should therefore be a deliberate contract rather than a direct serialization of internal objects.","principle":"External event contracts are integration boundaries, not domain-object dumps.","objective":"Publish stable events to external consumers while preserving internal model independence.","requirements":["Define external event schema.","Map internal facts to external fields.","Define version and compatibility policy.","Provide delivery semantics."],"constraints":["External consumers may evolve independently.","Delivery may be at-least-once or another explicitly defined semantic rather than exactly-once."],"preconditions":["A domain occurrence exists and an external integration is required."],"input":"Internal domain event.","output":"External integration-event message.","expected_behavior":"External consumers receive a stable contract representing the relevant domain occurrence.","incorrect_code":"broker.publish(json.dumps(domain_event.dict))","corrected_code":"message = OrderApprovedV1(order_id=event.order_id, total=event.total)\nbroker.publish(message.serialize())","failure_mode":"Internal implementation changes break external consumers.","expected_error":"Schema compatibility or consumer deserialization error.","cause":"Internal event structure is exposed directly.","root_cause":"No explicit external contract boundary.","solution":"Introduce a versioned integration-event mapper and contract.","edge_case":"Consumers may receive duplicate events and must have defined deduplication semantics where delivery is at-least-once.","security_implication":"External event payloads require data minimization, authentication/integrity controls, and authorization at the integration boundary.","performance_implication":"Serialization and network delivery add latency and throughput constraints.","test_case":"Publish an internal event and validate the resulting external schema.","test_input":"OrderApproved internal event.","expected_test_result":"External message conforms to the declared integration contract.","testing_guidance":"Use consumer-contract or schema tests for externally visible event formats.","verification_method":"External schema validation and mapping inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify internal implementation details are absent unless explicitly contracted.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Event mapper","Message transport"],"execution_requirements":["Configured integration transport for execution tests"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B015-R01","D35-B016-R02","D35-B017-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B015-R01"],"requires":["D35-B017-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B015-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B016-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B016-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established integration-event contract and anti-corruption boundary pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B016-R02","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Integration Event Publishing","subtopic":"Contract versioning","concept":"Externally published event schemas require explicit compatibility and versioning rules.","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"Why should integration events use explicit contract versions?","answer":"Independent consumers cannot safely depend on undocumented internal structure, so external event contracts need an explicit compatibility policy and version semantics.","explanation":"A producer may evolve its internal model without intending to break consumers. Versioned external contracts make compatibility decisions explicit.","principle":"External contracts evolve through governed compatibility rules.","objective":"Prevent accidental integration breakage during producer evolution.","requirements":["Define schema version semantics.","Document compatible and incompatible changes.","Test supported consumer versions."],"constraints":["Versioning strategy must match the transport and consumer ecosystem.","Not every field change requires a new major contract version if compatibility rules permit it."],"preconditions":["External consumers exist or are expected."],"input":"Current and proposed external event schemas.","output":"Versioned compatibility contract.","expected_behavior":"Producer changes are evaluated against explicit compatibility rules.","incorrect_code":"event = {"order": internal_order.dict}","corrected_code":"event = {"type": "OrderApproved", "version": 1, "order_id": order.id}","failure_mode":"Consumers break after internal model changes.","expected_error":"Consumer schema/contract error.","cause":"No stable external schema.","root_cause":"Internal and external contracts were treated as identical.","solution":"Define and version the external event contract separately.","edge_case":"A producer may need dual publishing during migration between contract versions.","security_implication":"Versioned contracts make security-sensitive fields and compatibility changes reviewable.","performance_implication":"Supporting multiple versions can increase serialization and testing overhead.","test_case":"Change an internal field and confirm external contract remains unchanged when the field is not contracted.","test_input":"Internal model extension.","expected_test_result":"Existing external contract remains valid.","testing_guidance":"Maintain schema compatibility tests across supported versions.","verification_method":"Schema diff and consumer compatibility testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify each supported version independently.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["External schema definition"],"execution_requirements":["Schema validation tooling"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B016-R01","D35-B016-R03"],"depends_on":["D35-B016-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B016-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B016-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established API and event contract-versioning practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B016-R03","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Integration Event Publishing","subtopic":"Delivery semantics","concept":"Integration-event implementations must explicitly define delivery semantics and consumer expectations.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should delivery semantics be represented in an integration-event implementation?","answer":"Document and implement the actual delivery model, such as at-least-once delivery, and require consumers to handle duplicates when that model permits them.","explanation":"A producer should not claim exactly-once delivery merely because it publishes one message per local event. Crashes, retries, and broker semantics can produce duplicates.","principle":"Advertise only the delivery guarantee the implementation can substantiate.","objective":"Align integration behavior with truthful delivery semantics.","requirements":["Define delivery guarantee.","Implement retry/recovery behavior.","Document duplicate handling requirements."],"constraints":["End-to-end delivery semantics depend on producer, transport, and consumer behavior.","Exactly-once effects require stronger end-to-end conditions than simple message publication."],"preconditions":["Transport behavior is understood."],"input":"Durable integration event and transport policy.","output":"Published event with defined delivery semantics.","expected_behavior":"Delivery behavior matches the documented guarantee.","incorrect_code":"broker.publish(message)\n# guaranteed exactly once","corrected_code":"broker.publish(message)\n# delivery guarantee: transport/application contract must be explicitly defined","failure_mode":"System documentation promises stronger delivery than implementation provides.","expected_error":"Potential duplicate or missing event during failure scenarios.","cause":"Delivery semantics were assumed rather than established.","root_cause":"No end-to-end reliability contract.","solution":"Define actual transport and recovery semantics and test them under failure.","edge_case":"Producer crash after broker acceptance but before local acknowledgment can cause duplicate publication on retry.","security_implication":"Duplicate security-sensitive events can trigger repeated downstream actions unless consumers are idempotent.","performance_implication":"Retries and durable delivery increase storage and network load.","test_case":"Simulate publication acknowledgment uncertainty and retry.","test_input":"Message with uncertain broker acknowledgment.","expected_test_result":"Observed behavior matches documented duplicate/retry semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test failure between local state commit and message acknowledgment.","verification_method":"Failure-injection delivery test.","verification_guidance":"Do not label exactly-once unless end-to-end evidence supports the claim.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Message transport","Durable event record"],"execution_requirements":["Representative message transport"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B016-R01","D35-B017-R01","D35-B018-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B017-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B016-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B016-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B016-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed-delivery reliability pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B016-R04","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Integration Event Publishing","subtopic":"Contract testing","concept":"Integration-event tests should validate external schema compatibility and defined delivery behavior.","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"What should integration-event publishing tests cover?","answer":"Tests should validate event schema, mapping from internal events, supported versions, and representative delivery failure/retry behavior.","explanation":"An integration event is both a data contract and a delivery behavior. Testing only serialization leaves reliability and compatibility defects undetected.","principle":"Test the contract and its delivery semantics.","objective":"Detect integration boundary failures before external consumers encounter them.","requirements":["Schema validation.","Mapping validation.","Version compatibility tests.","Failure/retry tests where applicable."],"constraints":["End-to-end transport tests may be slower than contract tests.","Consumer-specific behavior belongs in consumer-contract or integration tests."],"preconditions":["External event contract is defined."],"input":"Internal event and integration transport configuration.","output":"Contract and delivery test evidence.","expected_behavior":"Published messages conform to supported contracts and defined delivery behavior.","incorrect_code":"assert json.dumps(event)","corrected_code":"message = map_event(event)\nassert validate_schema(message, version=1)\nassert message["type"] == "OrderApproved"","failure_mode":"Serialization succeeds while external contract is wrong.","expected_error":"Schema validation or integration test failure.","cause":"Tests do not validate semantic contract.","root_cause":"Serialization was treated as equivalent to compatibility.","solution":"Validate schema, version, mapping, and representative delivery behavior.","edge_case":"Backward-compatible schema additions can still break consumers with strict unknown-field handling.","security_implication":"Tests should ensure sensitive fields are not emitted unintentionally.","performance_implication":"Contract tests are cheap; transport failure tests may be more expensive.","test_case":"Publish each supported event version against representative consumer schemas.","test_input":"Versioned OrderApproved messages.","expected_test_result":"All supported contracts validate and incompatible versions fail predictably.","testing_guidance":"Separate schema-contract tests from live broker integration tests.","verification_method":"Schema and delivery-behavior assertions.","verification_guidance":"Record transport test execution separately from static schema validation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Schema validator","Message transport test harness"],"execution_requirements":["Contract-test and representative transport environments"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B016-R01","D35-B016-R02","D35-B016-R03","D35-B016-R05"],"depends_on":["D35-B016-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B016-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established integration-contract testing practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B016-R05","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Integration Event Publishing","subtopic":"Boundary verification","concept":"Integration-event verification requires evidence that the external message matches the declared contract and delivery guarantee.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should an integration-event publisher be verified?","answer":"Verify the mapped message against the applicable schema version and verify failure/retry behavior against the documented delivery semantics.","explanation":"A correct local event does not prove a correct external integration. Verification must cross the integration boundary.","principle":"Verify what external consumers actually receive and what delivery behavior they can rely upon.","objective":"Establish truthful integration-contract conformance.","requirements":["Schema conformance evidence.","Version compatibility evidence.","Delivery-semantics evidence where claimed."],"constraints":["Unexecuted transport tests cannot be presented as delivery verification.","Third-party provider behavior requires provider-specific evidence."],"preconditions":["External contract and delivery policy are defined."],"input":"Publisher implementation, schema, and test evidence.","output":"Integration conformance result.","expected_behavior":"External output conforms to the applicable contract and documented guarantee.","incorrect_code":"return publisher.publish(message) is not None","corrected_code":"return schema_valid and version_supported and delivery_behavior_verified","failure_mode":"Verification treats a successful publish call as proof of end-to-end delivery.","expected_error":"Verification failure.","cause":"Verification boundary ends at the local API call.","root_cause":"External integration semantics were not included in acceptance criteria.","solution":"Verify schema and, where claimed, delivery behavior across the relevant boundary.","edge_case":"A broker acknowledgment may prove acceptance by the broker but not downstream consumer processing.","security_implication":"Boundary verification should include message integrity and sensitive-data controls where applicable.","performance_implication":"End-to-end verification can require transport and consumer infrastructure.","test_case":"Verify supported event version and representative delivery failure path.","test_input":"Published integration event.","expected_test_result":"Contract passes and observed delivery behavior matches the documented guarantee.","testing_guidance":"Keep broker acceptance, consumer processing, and business-effect verification as distinct evidence levels.","verification_method":"Schema validation plus integration failure/recovery evidence.","verification_guidance":"Never collapse transport acceptance into business outcome verification.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Integration event publisher","Schema validation","Message transport"],"execution_requirements":["Representative integration environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B016-R01","D35-B016-R03","D35-B016-R04"],"depends_on":["D35-B016-R04"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B016-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B016-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established integration-boundary verification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B017-R01","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Outbox Implementation","subtopic":"Transactional event persistence","concept":"The outbox pattern persists an event in the same transaction as the state change so a later dispatcher can publish it reliably.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an outbox be implemented for reliable integration-event publication?","answer":"Persist the business state change and an outbox message in the same transaction, then publish pending outbox records asynchronously and mark them according to the actual delivery lifecycle.","explanation":"The shared transaction closes the gap between committed business state and durable knowledge that an event must be published.","principle":"Persist state and required publication intent atomically before asynchronous delivery.","objective":"Prevent loss of required integration events after successful state changes.","requirements":["Outbox record participates in the same transaction as the business change.","Outbox has stable message identity.","Dispatcher tracks publication state."],"constraints":["Outbox does not by itself guarantee exactly-once consumer effects.","Dispatcher must handle crashes and retries."],"preconditions":["Transactional persistence exists.","An integration event is required."],"input":"Business state change and integration-event payload.","output":"Committed business state plus pending outbox record.","expected_behavior":"If business state commits, the corresponding outbox intent also commits.","incorrect_code":"repo.save(order)\nbroker.publish(event)\nrepo.commit()","corrected_code":"with uow.transaction():\n    uow.orders.save(order)\n    uow.outbox.add(message_id, event_payload)","failure_mode":"Business state commits but event publication intent is lost after a process failure.","expected_error":"Potential missing integration event.","cause":"Broker publication is not atomically coupled with the local state change.","root_cause":"Distributed side effect was placed outside the local transaction without a durable handoff.","solution":"Write an outbox record in the same transaction and dispatch it separately.","edge_case":"Dispatcher may publish the same outbox record more than once; consumers need appropriate deduplication when delivery is at-least-once.","security_implication":"Outbox data must be protected because it may contain externally transmitted business information.","performance_implication":"Outbox writes and polling add storage and dispatch overhead.","test_case":"Commit business state, terminate the dispatcher before publication, then restart it.","test_input":"Committed state with pending outbox message.","expected_test_result":"Pending message remains discoverable and can be retried.","testing_guidance":"Test crash points around transaction commit and dispatch acknowledgment.","verification_method":"Inspect transaction state and outbox durability.","verification_guidance":"Verify committed business state always has the required publication intent.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Transactional database","Outbox dispatcher"],"execution_requirements":["Persistent database and dispatcher process"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B016-R01","D35-B016-R03","D35-B018-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B011-R01","D35-B016-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B016-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B016-R01"],"tests":["D35-B017-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B017-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established transactional outbox pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B017-R02","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Outbox Implementation","subtopic":"Outbox record contract","concept":"An outbox record needs enough metadata to identify, order, retry, and audit publication according to the system's requirements.","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"What information should an outbox record contain?","answer":"Typical fields include stable message identity, event type/version, payload or payload reference, creation time, publication state, attempt metadata, and relevant correlation information.","explanation":"The exact schema depends on delivery requirements, but the dispatcher needs durable information to recover pending messages and distinguish retries.","principle":"Durable publication intent requires durable operational metadata.","objective":"Design an outbox record that supports reliable dispatch and diagnosis.","requirements":["Stable message identity.","Event type/version.","Durable pending state.","Retry/attempt information where needed."],"constraints":["Payload retention and sensitive-data handling must be deliberate.","Ordering should only be promised if the implementation preserves it."],"preconditions":["Integration-event requirements are defined."],"input":"Event contract and delivery requirements.","output":"Outbox schema.","expected_behavior":"Dispatcher can identify and process pending messages without reconstructing lost intent.","incorrect_code":"outbox.append(event_payload)","corrected_code":"outbox.add(id=message_id, event_type=event_type, version=1, payload=payload, status="PENDING")","failure_mode":"Dispatcher cannot safely distinguish messages or recover failed attempts.","expected_error":"Duplicate, stuck, or untraceable publication behavior.","cause":"Outbox stores payload without operational identity/state.","root_cause":"Outbox was treated as a simple list rather than a durable delivery queue.","solution":"Model message identity and lifecycle explicitly.","edge_case":"Large payloads may require storing a durable reference rather than duplicating data.","security_implication":"Outbox records require access controls and encryption appropriate to their payload sensitivity.","performance_implication":"Indexes should support pending-message selection and retry scheduling.","test_case":"Create, dispatch, fail, retry, and complete an outbox record.","test_input":"One pending event with simulated publication failures.","expected_test_result":"Record lifecycle remains internally consistent and recoverable.","testing_guidance":"Test state transitions and concurrent dispatcher claims.","verification_method":"Outbox state-machine and persistence inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify no record becomes permanently invisible without an explicit terminal state.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Persistent outbox store"],"execution_requirements":["Transactional database"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B017-R01","D35-B017-R03"],"depends_on":["D35-B017-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B017-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B017-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established outbox schema and dispatcher-state design practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B017-R03","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Outbox Implementation","subtopic":"Dispatcher recovery","concept":"An outbox dispatcher must recover pending or uncertain messages after process failure without losing publication intent.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an outbox dispatcher handle a crash during publication?","answer":"Use durable message state and a retry/lease mechanism so an interrupted dispatch becomes eligible for later processing according to the defined delivery semantics.","explanation":"A dispatcher can crash after sending a message but before marking it complete. The safe default is to allow replay and require downstream idempotency where duplicate delivery is possible.","principle":"Recovery should favor durable intent and truthful at-least-once semantics over unsupported exactly-once claims.","objective":"Prevent permanently lost outbox messages after dispatcher failure.","requirements":["Durable pending state.","Recoverable claim/lease or equivalent mechanism.","Retry policy.","Stable message identity."],"constraints":["Lease duration must account for processing time.","Duplicate publication remains possible around acknowledgment boundaries."],"preconditions":["Outbox records and message identities exist."],"input":"Pending or in-flight outbox record.","output":"Recovered publication attempt or terminal failure state.","expected_behavior":"Interrupted work becomes recoverable without deleting the message prematurely.","incorrect_code":"message = outbox.next()\nbroker.publish(message)\noutbox.delete(message.id)","corrected_code":"message = outbox.claim_pending()\ntry:\n    broker.publish(message.payload)\n    outbox.mark_published(message.id)\nexcept Exception:\n    outbox.release_for_retry(message.id)","failure_mode":"Crash or uncertain acknowledgment causes message loss or unbounded duplicate processing.","expected_error":"Transport error or dispatcher failure.","cause":"Message is deleted or permanently marked before publication outcome is durable.","root_cause":"No explicit dispatcher lifecycle or recovery semantics.","solution":"Use durable states and retryable claims, accepting duplicates where the delivery model permits them.","edge_case":"Crash after broker acceptance but before local completion can cause duplicate publication after recovery.","security_implication":"Repeated sensitive events may cause repeated downstream actions; consumers should enforce appropriate idempotency.","performance_implication":"Polling, leasing, and retries consume database and transport resources.","test_case":"Terminate dispatcher after publication but before completion marking.","test_input":"In-flight outbox record.","expected_test_result":"Record remains recoverable and duplicate behavior matches documented semantics.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at every dispatcher state transition.","verification_method":"Recovery-state inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify no pending record becomes silently lost.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Outbox store","Message broker/transport"],"execution_requirements":["Persistent outbox and representative transport"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B017-R01","D35-B017-R02","D35-B016-R03"],"depends_on":["D35-B017-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B017-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B017-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B017-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established outbox dispatcher recovery pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B017-R04","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Outbox Implementation","subtopic":"Recovery testing","concept":"Outbox recovery tests must exercise failures before publication, after publication, and around completion marking.","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"What failure points should an outbox implementation test?","answer":"At minimum test transaction rollback, dispatcher interruption before publication, transport failure, and interruption after publication but before durable completion marking.","explanation":"Each failure point exposes a different reliability risk. In particular, the post-publication pre-acknowledgment window is where duplicate publication can arise.","principle":"Failure injection should cover every durable lifecycle transition.","objective":"Validate that the outbox cannot silently lose required publication intent.","requirements":["Test transactional creation.","Test dispatch failure.","Test dispatcher crash/restart.","Test uncertain acknowledgment."],"constraints":["Some crash scenarios require process-level integration tests.","Exactly-once claims require end-to-end evidence beyond the outbox itself."],"preconditions":["Outbox lifecycle states are defined."],"input":"Outbox record and controlled failure scenarios.","output":"Recovery and delivery evidence.","expected_behavior":"Pending messages remain recoverable and duplicate behavior is understood.","incorrect_code":"assert dispatcher.process(message)","corrected_code":"assert outbox.status(message.id) == "PENDING"\nrestart_dispatcher()\nassert outbox.eventually_recoverable(message.id)","failure_mode":"Tests cover only successful publication.","expected_error":"Assertion or recovery failure.","cause":"Failure windows are not exercised.","root_cause":"Outbox was tested as a normal queue rather than a crash-recovery mechanism.","solution":"Inject failures at each lifecycle boundary.","edge_case":"Transport may accept a message even when the client observes a timeout.","security_implication":"Recovery tests should include events whose repeated processing has security impact.","performance_implication":"Crash-recovery integration tests are more expensive than unit tests and should complement them.","test_case":"Simulate timeout after transport acceptance.","test_input":"Message with uncertain publication acknowledgment.","expected_test_result":"Recovery behavior permits duplicate publication only according to the declared delivery model.","testing_guidance":"Record exact failure injection point and observed transport behavior.","verification_method":"Lifecycle-state and transport-observation assertions.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer broker acceptance or consumer processing from local dispatcher state alone.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Outbox implementation","Transport test harness"],"execution_requirements":["Process-control and representative transport environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B017-R01","D35-B017-R03","D35-B017-R05"],"depends_on":["D35-B017-R03"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B017-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established outbox crash-recovery testing practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B017-R05","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Outbox Implementation","subtopic":"Reliability verification","concept":"Outbox verification proves atomic publication intent and recoverability, not automatically end-to-end exactly-once business effects.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"What can an outbox implementation legitimately be verified to guarantee?","answer":"It can be verified to durably couple local state change with publication intent and to recover pending messages according to its dispatcher semantics; end-to-end delivery and effect guarantees require additional evidence.","explanation":"The outbox closes a local transaction-to-message-intent gap. It does not by itself establish broker delivery, consumer processing, or exactly-once business effects.","principle":"Verification claims must match the boundary actually covered by evidence.","objective":"Prevent overclaiming outbox reliability.","requirements":["Verify shared transaction.","Verify pending-message recovery.","Verify documented dispatcher behavior."],"constraints":["External transport and consumer behavior are outside the local outbox boundary.","Crash tests may be environment-specific."],"preconditions":["Outbox and dispatcher implementation exist."],"input":"Implementation, transaction evidence, and recovery tests.","output":"Scoped reliability verification result.","expected_behavior":"Claims are limited to demonstrated guarantees.","incorrect_code":"return outbox_enabled == True","corrected_code":"return transaction_atomicity_verified and recovery_verified and delivery_scope_documented","failure_mode":"Outbox presence is treated as proof of exactly-once delivery.","expected_error":"Verification failure.","cause":"Verification boundary exceeds implementation evidence.","root_cause":"Local durability was confused with end-to-end delivery.","solution":"State and verify each guarantee at its actual system boundary.","edge_case":"A tightly integrated transport may provide stronger semantics, but those require separate evidence.","security_implication":"Sensitive downstream actions require consumer-side idempotency and authorization verification.","performance_implication":"Recovery guarantees may require durable indexes, leases, and retry processing.","test_case":"Verify commit/recovery behavior without claiming consumer processing.","test_input":"Outbox transaction and dispatcher recovery tests.","expected_test_result":"Local publication intent is verified; untested downstream effects remain unverified.","testing_guidance":"Maintain separate verification records for producer, transport, consumer, and business effect.","verification_method":"Boundary-specific reliability evidence.","verification_guidance":"Mark unsupported guarantees as UNVERIFIED rather than inferring them.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Outbox implementation","Transactional persistence","Dispatcher"],"execution_requirements":["Representative persistence and dispatcher environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B017-R01","D35-B017-R03","D35-B017-R04"],"depends_on":["D35-B017-R04"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B017-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B017-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established transactional-outbox verification boundary"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B018-R01","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Inbox Deduplication Implementation","subtopic":"Idempotent message consumption","concept":"An inbox records consumed message identities so redelivered messages do not repeat non-idempotent consumer effects.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should inbox deduplication be implemented for an at-least-once event consumer?","answer":"Persist a stable message identifier in an inbox table within the same appropriate transaction as the consumer's business effect, rejecting or short-circuiting duplicate message identities.","explanation":"At-least-once delivery can produce duplicates. A durable inbox makes duplicate detection persistent across process restarts.","principle":"Consumer deduplication must be durable and coupled to the effect it protects.","objective":"Prevent repeated business effects from duplicate messages.","requirements":["Stable message identity.","Unique inbox constraint.","Atomic relation between deduplication and protected business effect where required."],"constraints":["Inbox retention must cover the duplicate-delivery window.","Some consumers can be naturally idempotent and may not require an inbox."],"preconditions":["Incoming messages have stable identifiers."],"input":"Message and consumer context.","output":"Applied business effect or duplicate no-op.","expected_behavior":"A duplicate message does not repeat the protected effect.","incorrect_code":"def handle(message):\n    apply_effect(message)\n    return ack(message)","corrected_code":"with uow.transaction():\n    if uow.inbox.exists(message.id):\n        return "DUPLICATE"\n    uow.inbox.insert(message.id)\n    apply_effect(message)\n    return "APPLIED"","failure_mode":"Consumer applies the same business effect twice after message redelivery.","expected_error":"Duplicate-effect or unique-constraint handling outcome.","cause":"No durable message identity tracking.","root_cause":"Consumer assumes delivery exactly once.","solution":"Persist consumed message identity atomically with the protected effect.","edge_case":"A crash after effect commit but before acknowledgment causes redelivery; inbox state must already prevent repetition.","security_implication":"Duplicate security-sensitive events can trigger repeated actions if deduplication is absent.","performance_implication":"Inbox lookup and insert add persistence overhead and require appropriate indexing.","test_case":"Deliver the same message twice.","test_input":"Two messages with identical stable IDs.","expected_test_result":"Business effect occurs once.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate delivery after process restart and under concurrent handling.","verification_method":"Inspect inbox and business-effect state.","verification_guidance":"Verify uniqueness is enforced at persistence level.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Persistent inbox store","Transactional persistence"],"execution_requirements":["Representative consumer database"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B017-R01","D35-B018-R02","D35-B018-R04"],"depends_on":["D35-B016-R03"],"requires":["D35-B011-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B016-R03"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B018-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B018-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established transactional inbox/idempotent-consumer pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B018-R02","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Inbox Deduplication Implementation","subtopic":"Message identity","concept":"Inbox deduplication depends on a stable message identity whose scope matches the duplicate-delivery semantics.","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"What makes a message identifier suitable for inbox deduplication?","answer":"It must uniquely identify the logical published message within the deduplication scope and remain stable across redelivery attempts.","explanation":"Transport delivery attempts are not necessarily distinct logical messages. The consumer needs an identifier that persists across retries and redelivery.","principle":"Deduplication identity must survive transport retries and uniquely identify the protected message.","objective":"Prevent false duplicates and missed duplicates.","requirements":["Stable across redelivery.","Unique within the defined consumer scope.","Documented retention scope."],"constraints":["Identifiers from different producers may collide if scope is too broad.","Message identity does not replace payload validation or authorization."],"preconditions":["Publisher supplies a stable message identity."],"input":"Incoming message identity and scope.","output":"Deduplication key.","expected_behavior":"All redeliveries of one logical message map to one inbox identity.","incorrect_code":"dedupe_key = uuid4()","corrected_code":"dedupe_key = message.message_id","failure_mode":"Every redelivery receives a new deduplication key.","expected_error":"Duplicate effect may occur.","cause":"Consumer generates a new identity per delivery attempt.","root_cause":"Transport attempt identity was confused with message identity.","solution":"Use the publisher's stable message identifier or a contractually defined equivalent.","edge_case":"A message may legitimately be republished as a new logical message with a new identity even when its payload is identical.","security_implication":"Message IDs should not be treated as bearer credentials.","performance_implication":"Compact indexed identifiers make inbox lookup efficient.","test_case":"Deliver one message through multiple redelivery attempts.","test_input":"One stable message ID.","expected_test_result":"All attempts map to one inbox record.","testing_guidance":"Test IDs across serialization/deserialization and transport retry boundaries.","verification_method":"Persisted identity comparison.","verification_guidance":"Verify scope and uniqueness assumptions explicitly.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Message contract"],"execution_requirements":["Message transport or test fixtures"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B018-R01","D35-B016-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B016-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B018-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B018-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established message-identity and inbox-deduplication practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B018-R03","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Inbox Deduplication Implementation","subtopic":"Concurrent duplicate handling","concept":"Concurrent duplicate deliveries require an atomic uniqueness constraint because separate existence checks can race.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must inbox uniqueness be enforced by the persistence layer?","answer":"Two concurrent consumers can both observe that a message is absent before either inserts it, so application-level existence checks alone cannot reliably prevent duplicate effects.","explanation":"The uniqueness constraint makes duplicate detection atomic at the persistence boundary. One consumer wins the insertion and the other must handle the uniqueness conflict without repeating the effect.","principle":"Concurrency-sensitive deduplication requires an atomic uniqueness guarantee.","objective":"Prevent duplicate effects under concurrent delivery.","requirements":["Unique constraint on message identity.","Atomic inbox insertion.","Defined duplicate-conflict handling."],"constraints":["Database conflict behavior differs by technology.","Business effect must share an appropriate transaction with inbox insertion."],"preconditions":["Persistent inbox table exists."],"input":"Concurrent deliveries of one message.","output":"One applied effect and one duplicate outcome.","expected_behavior":"Only one consumer enters the protected effect path.","incorrect_code":"if not inbox.exists(message.id):\n    apply_effect(message)\n    inbox.insert(message.id)","corrected_code":"try:\n    with uow.transaction():\n        inbox.insert_unique(message.id)\n        apply_effect(message)\nexcept DuplicateMessage:\n    return "DUPLICATE"","failure_mode":"Both consumers pass the existence check and apply the effect.","expected_error":"DuplicateMessage or persistence uniqueness conflict.","cause":"Check and insert are separate operations.","root_cause":"Concurrency control was omitted from deduplication.","solution":"Use an atomic unique insertion and transaction boundary.","edge_case":"If the effect occurs before the inbox insert, a failure can leave an applied effect without a deduplication record.","security_implication":"Concurrent duplicate security events require strict atomicity to prevent repeated actions.","performance_implication":"Unique indexes improve lookup but add write contention under hot keys.","test_case":"Deliver the same message concurrently to two consumers.","test_input":"Two workers, one message ID.","expected_test_result":"One worker applies the effect and the other resolves as duplicate.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled concurrent execution against the real persistence mechanism.","verification_method":"Effect count plus inbox uniqueness inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify the losing worker cannot execute the protected effect.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Transactional persistence","Unique inbox constraint"],"execution_requirements":["Concurrent database environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B018-R01","D35-B018-R02","D35-B018-R04"],"depends_on":["D35-B018-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B018-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B018-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B018-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established concurrency-safe inbox deduplication pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
