{"record_id":"D35-B018-R05","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Inbox Deduplication Implementation","subtopic":"Consumer verification","concept":"Inbox verification establishes that duplicate delivery cannot repeat the protected business effect within the defined deduplication scope.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"What evidence verifies an inbox deduplication implementation?","answer":"Verify that stable message identity is persisted, uniqueness is enforced, duplicate deliveries produce no additional protected effect, and crash recovery preserves deduplication state.","explanation":"Verification must cover the actual business-effect boundary. Merely seeing an inbox record is insufficient if the effect can occur before or outside its transaction.","principle":"Verify deduplication at the protected effect boundary.","objective":"Establish truthful duplicate-suppression guarantees.","requirements":["Identity persistence evidence.","Unique constraint evidence.","Effect-cardinality evidence.","Recovery evidence."],"constraints":["Guarantee scope ends at the defined consumer effect boundary.","External downstream effects require separate idempotency verification."],"preconditions":["Inbox tests exist."],"input":"Inbox implementation and test evidence.","output":"Deduplication verification result.","expected_behavior":"One logical message produces no more than the defined number of protected effects.","incorrect_code":"return inbox_record_exists","corrected_code":"return unique_identity_verified and effect_count == 1 and recovery_preserved","failure_mode":"Verification checks storage but not the protected effect.","expected_error":"Verification failure.","cause":"Acceptance criterion is disconnected from business outcome.","root_cause":"Inbox was verified as a table rather than a consistency mechanism.","solution":"Verify identity, uniqueness, transaction coupling, and effect cardinality.","edge_case":"Consumers with intentionally idempotent effects may not need an inbox; verification should reflect the actual architecture.","security_implication":"Sensitive downstream actions need separate authorization and effect verification.","performance_implication":"Verification may require additional queries and controlled concurrency.","test_case":"Verify sequential, concurrent, and recovered duplicate deliveries.","test_input":"Repeated stable message identity.","expected_test_result":"Protected effect occurs once within the defined scope.","testing_guidance":"Keep transport delivery verification separate from consumer business-effect verification.","verification_method":"Persistence, concurrency, and effect-state evidence.","verification_guidance":"Mark untested recovery windows as unverified.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Inbox implementation","Transactional persistence","Test harness"],"execution_requirements":["Representative consumer environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B018-R01","D35-B018-R04"],"depends_on":["D35-B018-R04"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B018-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B018-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established idempotent-consumer verification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B019-R01","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Error Taxonomy Implementation","subtopic":"Typed application failures","concept":"An application error taxonomy translates operational failures into stable semantic categories that callers can handle without depending on infrastructure exceptions.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a typed application error taxonomy be implemented?","answer":"Define stable application-level error types for meaningful failure categories and map lower-level failures into those types at the appropriate boundary.","explanation":"Infrastructure libraries expose technology-specific exceptions. Application services need stable semantic failures such as validation, conflict, not-found, authorization, or dependency-unavailable conditions.","principle":"Application error semantics should remain stable across infrastructure implementations.","objective":"Create a predictable failure contract between implementation layers.","requirements":["Define semantic error categories.","Preserve useful diagnostic context.","Avoid leaking infrastructure-specific types across application boundaries."],"constraints":["Do not collapse materially different failures into one generic error.","Sensitive diagnostics should not be exposed to untrusted callers."],"preconditions":["Application failure semantics are defined."],"input":"Infrastructure or domain failure.","output":"Typed application-level failure.","expected_behavior":"Callers can handle meaningful failure categories independently of infrastructure technology.","incorrect_code":"except Exception:\n    raise RuntimeError("operation failed")","corrected_code":"except UniqueConstraintError as exc:\n    raise ConflictError("resource already exists") from exc","failure_mode":"All failures become indistinguishable or infrastructure-specific.","expected_error":"Typed application error.","cause":"No explicit error taxonomy or translation boundary.","root_cause":"Failure semantics were allowed to leak from implementation details.","solution":"Define typed application errors and translate infrastructure failures at the boundary.","edge_case":"Unexpected exceptions should not be falsely classified as known business errors.","security_implication":"Error responses must avoid leaking stack traces, credentials, queries, or sensitive infrastructure details.","performance_implication":"Typed exceptions add negligible overhead relative to typical I/O failure handling.","test_case":"Trigger representative infrastructure failures and verify semantic application errors.","test_input":"Unique constraint violation and missing record.","expected_test_result":"Each maps to its documented application category.","testing_guidance":"Test both known mappings and unknown-exception behavior.","verification_method":"Exception-type and error-contract assertions.","verification_guidance":"Verify causal chaining is preserved internally where useful without exposing internals externally.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Application error definitions","Infrastructure adapters"],"execution_requirements":["Representative infrastructure implementation"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B007-R01","D35-B020-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B007-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B019-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B019-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established application-error taxonomy and exception-translation pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B019-R02","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Error Taxonomy Implementation","subtopic":"Error category design","concept":"Error categories should distinguish failures that require materially different handling.","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"How should application error categories be separated?","answer":"Separate categories when callers need different recovery, user messaging, authorization handling, retry behavior, or operational response.","explanation":"A useful taxonomy reflects handling semantics rather than merely mirroring every underlying exception class.","principle":"Categorize failures by required response semantics.","objective":"Avoid both over-specific infrastructure leakage and over-generic application errors.","requirements":["Categories have clear handling semantics.","Categories remain stable across infrastructure changes.","Unexpected failures remain distinguishable."],"constraints":["Too many categories increase complexity.","Too few categories force unsafe or incorrect handling."],"preconditions":["Failure-handling requirements are known."],"input":"Known failure scenarios.","output":"Application error taxonomy.","expected_behavior":"Each material failure scenario maps to an appropriate category.","incorrect_code":"class AppError(Exception): pass","corrected_code":"class AppError(Exception): pass\nclass ValidationError(AppError): pass\nclass ConflictError(AppError): pass\nclass NotFoundError(AppError): pass\nclass AuthorizationError(AppError): pass","failure_mode":"Caller cannot determine whether to retry, correct input, or deny access.","expected_error":"Generic application failure.","cause":"Taxonomy contains insufficient semantic distinctions.","root_cause":"Error categories were designed around implementation convenience rather than caller needs.","solution":"Define categories from observable handling requirements.","edge_case":"A provider timeout may be retryable in one operation but non-retryable in another; taxonomy and operation policy should remain separable.","security_implication":"Authorization and authentication failures require distinct security handling from ordinary validation failures.","performance_implication":"Taxonomy size has little direct runtime impact; excessive translation logic can increase maintenance cost.","test_case":"Map a representative failure matrix to application categories.","test_input":"Validation, conflict, missing resource, authorization, dependency timeout.","expected_test_result":"Each category maps to documented handling semantics.","testing_guidance":"Review taxonomy against actual caller branches to detect unused or ambiguous categories.","verification_method":"Failure-matrix coverage analysis.","verification_guidance":"Verify every externally meaningful failure has one documented semantic category.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Application error model"],"execution_requirements":["Application layer"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B019-R01","D35-B019-R03"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B019-R04"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established semantic error-taxonomy design practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B019-R03","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Error Taxonomy Implementation","subtopic":"Unknown failure handling","concept":"Unknown failures should remain distinguishable from known semantic errors so unexpected defects are not misclassified as recoverable conditions.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should unexpected exceptions be handled in a typed application error taxonomy?","answer":"Translate only known failures with high confidence; preserve unexpected failures as unexpected internal errors while logging controlled diagnostic context.","explanation":"A broad catch-all translation can cause callers to retry, ignore, or expose an error incorrectly. Unknown failures require separate operational handling.","principle":"Do not manufacture semantic certainty for unknown failures.","objective":"Prevent incorrect recovery behavior caused by overbroad exception translation.","requirements":["Known mappings are explicit.","Unknown failures remain identifiable.","External responses hide sensitive diagnostics."],"constraints":["Logging must respect data sensitivity.","Operational alerting should distinguish unexpected failures."],"preconditions":["Known infrastructure-to-application mappings are defined."],"input":"Unknown runtime exception.","output":"Internal-error classification and controlled external response.","expected_behavior":"Unknown failure is not misclassified as validation, conflict, or authorization.","incorrect_code":"except Exception:\n    raise ValidationError("invalid request")","corrected_code":"except KnownPersistenceError as exc:\n    raise ConflictError() from exc\nexcept Exception:\n    raise InternalApplicationError()","failure_mode":"Unexpected defects are reported as client-correctable errors.","expected_error":"InternalApplicationError or equivalent internal classification.","cause":"Catch-all handler maps every exception to one semantic type.","root_cause":"Error translation lacks a confidence boundary.","solution":"Translate only recognized failures and preserve unknown-failure classification.","edge_case":"A new infrastructure exception may initially fall into the unknown category until an evidence-backed mapping is added.","security_implication":"Unknown errors must not expose implementation details to untrusted callers.","performance_implication":"Centralized exception translation has low overhead but can affect observability volume.","test_case":"Raise an unexpected exception and inspect internal and external error behavior.","test_input":"Synthetic unexpected runtime error.","expected_test_result":"External response is controlled and internal classification remains unexpected.","testing_guidance":"Assert no sensitive exception details cross the external boundary.","verification_method":"Error-class and response-contract inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify unknown exceptions are observable for diagnosis.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Application error taxonomy","Error boundary"],"execution_requirements":["Application runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B019-R01","D35-B020-R01"],"depends_on":["D35-B019-R01"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B019-R01"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B019-R04"],"verifies":["D35-B019-R05"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established exception-boundary and defensive error-handling practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
