{"record_id":"D35-B024-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Controller/Handler Implementation","subtopic":"Transport-to-use-case delegation","concept":"A controller or handler should translate transport concerns and delegate business execution to an application operation.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should an API handler implement?","answer":"The handler should parse and validate transport input, establish request context, invoke the application operation, and map its result to the transport response.","explanation":"Keeping business orchestration outside the handler prevents transport code from becoming an application service.","principle":"Controllers coordinate boundaries; application services coordinate use cases.","objective":"Maintain a thin transport layer.","requirements":["Request parsing","Input validation","Application invocation","Response mapping"],"constraints":["No domain workflow orchestration in the controller"],"preconditions":["Application use case exists"],"input":"HTTP request","output":"HTTP response","expected_behavior":"Handler delegates business execution and maps the result.","incorrect_code":"@app.post("/orders")\ndef create_order(request):\n    validate(request)\n    customer = db.get(request.customer_id)\n    total = sum(x.price for x in request.items)\n    db.insert_order(customer, total)\n    return {"ok": True}","corrected_code":"@app.post("/orders")\ndef create_order(request):\n    command = map_request(request)\n    result = create_order_use_case.execute(command)\n    return map_response(result)","failure_mode":"Transport handler becomes business-service implementation.","expected_error":"ArchitectureViolation","cause":"Use-case logic implemented in controller.","root_cause":"Boundary responsibilities are not separated.","solution":"Delegate to application service/use case.","edge_case":"Handler-specific protocol behavior still belongs in the transport layer.","