{"record_id":"D35-B027-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Authorization Enforcement Implementation","subtopic":"Application boundary authorization","concept":"Authorization should be enforced at an application boundary close to the protected operation, not only at the UI.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY","question":"Where should authorization be enforced?","answer":"At the boundary that owns the protected operation, with transport-level checks as additional defense rather than the sole control.","explanation":"Non-UI callers can bypass presentation-layer restrictions.","principle":"Authorization protects operations, not merely interfaces.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized execution regardless of caller.","requirements":["Explicit policy","Trusted principal","Protected operation boundary"],"constraints":["UI visibility is not an authorization mechanism"],"preconditions":["Authenticated principal exists or operation supports anonymous policy"],"input":"Principal and command","output":"Authorized operation or denial","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized principals cannot execute protected operation.","incorrect_code":"if user_can_see_button:\n    service.execute(command)","corrected_code":"decision = policy.authorize(principal, command)\nif not decision.allowed:\n    raise AuthorizationDenied()\nservice.execute(command, principal)","failure_mode":"Hidden UI is mistaken for access control.","expected_error":"AuthorizationDenied","cause":"Authorization enforced only in presentation layer.","root_cause":"Operation boundary lacks policy enforcement.","solution":"Enforce policy before protected application execution.","edge_case":"Same use case exposed through API, CLI, and background worker.","security_implication":"Central operation authorization reduces alternate-path bypass risk.","performance_implication":"Policy evaluation adds bounded per-operation work.","test_case":"Invoke protected use case directly with unauthorized princip