{"record_id":"D35-B031-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Boundary Enforcement","concept":"Translate architectural module boundaries into enforceable implementation boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an architectural module boundary be implemented so that dependencies do not bypass the intended design?","answer":"Represent each architectural boundary as an explicit module or package interface, expose only stable public contracts, keep implementation details private, and enforce dependency direction through project structure, visibility rules, and automated architecture checks.","explanation":"A diagram alone does not enforce architecture. The implementation must make allowed dependencies easy and forbidden dependencies difficult. Public interfaces should represent architectural contracts while internal types remain behind the boundary.","principle":"Architectural boundaries should be represented by enforceable dependency boundaries rather than documentation alone.","objective":"Prevent implementation dependencies from violating the intended architecture.","requirements":["Define module ownership","Define public interfaces","Define permitted dependency direction","Keep internal implementation details private","Add automated dependency validation where practical"],"constraints":["Existing code may contain legacy cross-module dependencies","Some infrastructure dependencies may legitimately be shared"],"preconditions":["Module responsibilities and dependency direction are defined"],"input":"A module architecture containing Domain, Application, Infrastructure, and Interface layers.","output":"A project structure with explicit interfaces and validated dependency direction.","expected_behavior":"Application code depends on domain abstractions and permitted infrastructure interfaces without importing unrelated interface-layer implementation details.","incorrect_code":"from ui.database_adapter import DatabaseAdapter\n\ndef create_order(service):\n    return service.create_order(DatabaseAdapter())","corrected_code":"from application.ports import OrderRepository\n\ndef create_order(service, repository: OrderRepository):\n    return service.create_order(repository)","failure_mode":"Dependency inversion is bypassed by importing a concrete implementation from another architectural layer.","expected_error":"Architecture validation should report a forbidden dependency; runtime behavior may otherwise appear correct.","cause":"The implementation follows physical convenience rather than the architectural dependency contract.","root_cause":"No enforceable boundary exists between architectural layers.","solution":"Expose an abstraction at the owning boundary, inject the implementation from an allowed composition layer, and validate dependency direction.","edge_case":"A shared utility module may be legitimately imported by multiple layers but must not become an unrestricted service-locator dependency.","security_implication":"Boundary violations can expose privileged infrastructure capabilities to components that should not access them.","performance_implication":"Additional abstraction boundaries usually have small direct overhead, but excessive indirection can complicate hot paths.","test_case":"Attempt to import a concrete infrastructure component from an interface-layer module.","test_input":"Dependency graph containing an Interface -> Infrastructure edge when only Infrastructure -> Application is allowed.","expected_test_result":"Architecture validation rejects the forbidden edge.","testing_guidance":"Test both allowed and forbidden dependency relationships; do not test only successful runtime behavior.","verification_method":"Static dependency analysis plus module-level tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the dependency graph matches the architecture specification and that public contracts remain stable.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python project with importable modules","Architecture validation tooling or custom dependency checker"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B032-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35 architectural boundary"],"tests":["D35-B031-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["C4-style component boundary principles","Dependency inversion principle"],"provenance":["C4 Model architecture guidance","Clean Architecture dependency rule","Python module system documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B031-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Interface Contracts","concept":"Implement architectural interfaces as explicit contracts","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an architecture-level interface become an implementation-level contract?","answer":"Define the interface's responsibilities, inputs, outputs, error semantics, lifecycle, and invariants explicitly, then implement against that contract rather than exposing arbitrary concrete behavior.","explanation":"An architecture component usually represents a responsibility rather than a class. The implementation should turn that responsibility into a stable contract that can be independently tested and replaced.","principle":"Interfaces should express architectural responsibility and behavioral contracts, not merely method names.","objective":"Make architectural components replaceable and independently verifiable.","requirements":["Define inputs","Define outputs","Define error behavior","Define invariants","Define lifecycle expectations"],"constraints":["The interface should not expose implementation-specific details unnecessarily"],"preconditions":["The component responsibility is clearly defined"],"input":"Architecture component named PaymentAuthorizer.","output":"An explicit implementation contract for payment authorization.","expected_behavior":"Different implementations can satisfy the same authorization contract without changing callers.","incorrect_code":"class PaymentAuthorizer:\n    def authorize(self, payload):\n        return self.gateway.internal_call(payload)","corrected_code":"from typing import Protocol\n\nclass PaymentAuthorizer(Protocol):\n    def authorize(self, payment_id: str, amount: int) -> bool: ...\n\nclass GatewayPaymentAuthorizer:\n    def authorize(self, payment_id: str, amount: int) -> bool:\n        return self._gateway.authorize(payment_id, amount)","failure_mode":"The architectural component is represented only by a concrete implementation and callers become coupled to its internals.","expected_error":"Replacement implementation requires changes across unrelated callers.","cause":"No stable behavioral contract was defined.","root_cause":"Architecture responsibility was not translated into an explicit interface.","solution":"Define a narrow interface containing required behavior and inject concrete implementations behind it.","edge_case":"A protocol should not expose internal gateway response formats unless those formats are part of the architectural contract.","security_implication":"A narrow contract can reduce accidental exposure of privileged operations.","performance_implication":"Interface dispatch can add minor overhead depending on language and implementation, but the architectural benefit is generally independent of that micro-cost.","test_case":"Replace the gateway implementation with a fake implementation satisfying the same protocol.","test_input":"Fake authorize implementation returning deterministic results.","expected_test_result":"Caller behavior remains unchanged because it depends on the contract rather than gateway internals.","testing_guidance":"Contract-test every production implementation against the same interface-level behavioral requirements.","verification_method":"Interface contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify inputs, outputs, error behavior, and invariants independently from implementation internals.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+","typing.Protocol"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B031-R001","D35-B031-R003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35 interface contract"],"tests":["D35-B031-R005"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Dependency inversion principle","Design by contract concepts"],"provenance":["Python typing.Protocol documentation","Clean Architecture interface boundary principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B031-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Dependency Injection","concept":"Implement dependency injection at the composition boundary","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Where should concrete dependencies be selected when architecture requires dependency inversion?","answer":"Concrete dependencies should normally be selected in a composition root or equivalent wiring layer, while business components receive abstractions through constructor or explicit dependency injection.","explanation":"Selecting dependencies inside business logic creates hidden coupling and makes testing and replacement harder. A composition boundary centralizes implementation choices.","principle":"Constructors consume dependencies; composition roots decide which concrete implementations are supplied.","objective":"Separate dependency selection from business behavior.","requirements":["Define dependency interfaces","Inject dependencies explicitly","Centralize concrete dependency construction"],"constraints":["Legacy systems may require gradual migration from internally constructed dependencies"],"preconditions":["A component has one or more external dependencies"],"input":"OrderService requiring an OrderRepository.","output":"An OrderService constructed with an injected repository.","expected_behavior":"OrderService does not instantiate or discover the repository implementation itself.","incorrect_code":"class OrderService:\n    def init(self):\n        self.repo = SqlOrderRepository()","corrected_code":"class OrderService:\n    def init(self, repo):\n        self.repo = repo\n\nservice = OrderService(SqlOrderRepository())","failure_mode":"Business component directly constructs infrastructure dependency.","expected_error":"Tests and alternative deployments require changes inside business code.","cause":"Composition responsibility is embedded in the component.","root_cause":"Dependency construction is not separated from business behavior.","solution":"Inject the dependency and construct it at the composition root.","edge_case":"A tiny immutable value object may be directly constructed without violating architectural dependency direction if it has no external infrastructure concern.","security_implication":"Centralized composition makes security-sensitive implementations and configuration easier to audit.","performance_implication":"Dependency injection itself should not be treated as a performance optimization; measure any dispatch or allocation overhead if it affects a critical path.","test_case":"Instantiate OrderService with an in-memory repository.","test_input":"FakeRepository implementing required repository operations.","expected_test_result":"OrderService operates without importing or constructing SQL infrastructure.","testing_guidance":"Use dependency substitution to test business behavior independently of external infrastructure.","verification_method":"Static import inspection plus dependency substitution tests.","verification_guidance":"Confirm no prohibited infrastructure construction occurs inside the business component.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B031-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B031-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35 dependency injection boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Dependency inversion principle","Composition root pattern"],"provenance":["Dependency Injection design guidance","Clean Architecture dependency rule"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B031-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Architecture Testing","concept":"Convert dependency rules into executable architecture tests","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"How can an architectural dependency rule be tested automatically?","answer":"Represent allowed dependency relationships as machine-readable rules and inspect the project's import or dependency graph during CI or validation.","explanation":"Architecture tests catch structural regressions that ordinary unit tests may miss. They should validate boundaries independently of whether the affected runtime path is currently exercised.","principle":"Architectural constraints should be executable whenever practical.","objective":"Detect architectural drift before deployment.","requirements":["Define allowed dependency rules","Build or inspect dependency graph","Fail validation on prohibited relationships"],"constraints":["Static analysis may not capture dynamic imports or runtime dependency discovery"],"preconditions":["Architecture dependency rules are explicit"],"input":"Dependency graph and allowed-edge specification.","output":"Pass/fail architecture validation result.","expected_behavior":"A prohibited dependency causes validation failure.","incorrect_code":"allowed = {"domain": {"application", "shared"}}\n# Dependency graph is never checked","corrected_code":"allowed = {"domain": {"shared"}, "application": {"domain", "shared"}}\n\ndef is_allowed(source, target):\n    return target in allowed.get(source, set())","failure_mode":"Architecture rules exist only in documentation and are not checked.","expected_error":"Architectural drift reaches later testing or production unnoticed.","cause":"No automated structural validation exists.","root_cause":"Architecture has not been converted into executable constraints.","solution":"Implement deterministic dependency checks and run them in CI.","edge_case":"Generated code may need an explicit exemption mechanism rather than unrestricted exclusion.","security_implication":"Dependency checks can prevent security-sensitive layers from being imported by untrusted or presentation components.","performance_implication":"Static dependency analysis is normally validation-time overhead rather than runtime overhead.","test_case":"Inject a forbidden domain-to-infrastructure dependency into the graph.","test_input":"source=domain,target=infrastructure.","expected_test_result":"Architecture check returns failure.","testing_guidance":"Test permitted edges, forbidden edges, cycles, and explicitly documented exceptions.","verification_method":"Automated architecture test in CI.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the test itself runs against the real project dependency graph rather than a hand-written mock graph only.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Project source tree","Dependency graph extraction mechanism"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B031-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B031-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B031-R001"],"derived_from":["Architecture fitness function concepts","Dependency graph validation"],"provenance":["Martin Fowler architecture fitness function guidance","Static dependency analysis practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B031-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Contract Verification","concept":"Verify implementation against architectural invariants","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should an implementation be verified against architectural requirements?","answer":"Define observable invariants for each architectural boundary and verify them through structural, contract, integration, and runtime tests as appropriate.","explanation":"Architecture requirements often concern properties such as isolation, dependency direction, failure containment, and consistency. These cannot be verified solely by checking whether individual functions return expected values.","principle":"Verify architectural properties at the level where the property exists.","objective":"Establish evidence that implementation preserves architectural intent.","requirements":["Identify architectural invariants","Map each invariant to a verification method","Record verification evidence"],"constraints":["Some architectural properties are difficult to prove completely with dynamic tests"],"preconditions":["Architectural invariants are explicitly defined"],"input":"Requirement that an authentication component must not persist credentials.","output":"Verification plan covering code structure, data flow, and runtime behavior.","expected_behavior":"No prohibited credential persistence path is present and runtime tests demonstrate compliant behavior.","incorrect_code":"def authenticate(password, db):\n    db.save("password", password)\n    return verify(password)","corrected_code":"def authenticate(password, verifier):\n    return verifier.verify(password)","failure_mode":"Implementation satisfies functional authentication behavior but violates an architectural security invariant.","expected_error":"Security architecture verification should fail.","cause":"Architectural invariant was not included in implementation verification.","root_cause":"Verification focused only on functional output.","solution":"Add invariant-specific structural and behavioral checks.","edge_case":"Temporary in-memory handling may still require lifecycle and logging checks even when persistent storage is prohibited.","security_implication":"Failure to verify architectural security boundaries can create systemic vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Additional verification may increase build time but reduces the risk of expensive architectural regressions.","test_case":"Scan authentication implementation and execute tests checking for persistence operations.","test_input":"Authentication implementation containing a database write.","expected_test_result":"Verification fails because prohibited persistence is detected.","testing_guidance":"Map every security-critical architectural invariant to at least one explicit verification mechanism.","verification_method":"Static analysis plus targeted integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Record which invariant was tested, the evidence produced, and whether verification is complete or partial.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Source-analysis capability","Test environment for authentication component"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B031-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B031-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B031-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B031-R002"],"derived_from":["Security architecture verification principles"],"provenance":["OWASP secure design guidance","Architecture verification practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B032-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Layered Architecture","concept":"Implement a layered architecture without collapsing responsibilities","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a layered architecture be mapped to source code?","answer":"Create modules corresponding to architectural responsibilities and ensure each layer exposes only the contracts required by adjacent or explicitly permitted layers.","explanation":"Layered architecture becomes ineffective when every layer can call every other layer. Implementation should preserve responsibility boundaries and make data movement explicit.","principle":"One-way dependency direction should reflect the architectural layer direction.","objective":"Preserve separation of concerns in executable code.","requirements":["Define layer responsibilities","Define allowed dependency direction","Keep cross-layer translation explicit"],"constraints":["Legacy systems may require adapters during migration"],"preconditions":["Layer responsibilities are documented"],"input":"Presentation, application, domain, and infrastructure layers.","output":"Source structure preserving layer direction.","expected_behavior":"Presentation invokes application services; application coordinates domain behavior; infrastructure implements required external adapters.","incorrect_code":"class UI:\n    def save(self, data):\n        return Domain.save_to_sql(data)","corrected_code":"class UI:\n    def save(self, data, application_service):\n        return application_service.save(data)","failure_mode":"Presentation layer directly accesses persistence implementation.","expected_error":"Architecture validation identifies an illegal dependency.","cause":"Layer responsibilities were not preserved during implementation.","root_cause":"The implementation maps user-facing behavior directly to infrastructure instead of traversing the application boundary.","solution":"Route requests through the application contract and isolate persistence behind an adapter.","edge_case":"Read-only query paths may legitimately use a specialized query layer if the architecture explicitly permits it.","security_implication":"Layer isolation can reduce direct exposure of privileged persistence capabilities.","performance_implication":"Additional layers can add call and translation overhead; optimize only after measurement.","test_case":"Attempt to import persistence adapter directly from presentation module.","test_input":"Presentation -> Infrastructure dependency.","expected_test_result":"Structural test rejects the dependency.","testing_guidance":"Test both dependency direction and responsibility placement.","verification_method":"Architecture test plus integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the actual source dependency graph matches the documented layer graph.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python project","Architecture validation mechanism"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B032-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B031-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Layered architecture"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Layered architecture principles"],"provenance":["Layered architecture literature","Clean Architecture dependency principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B032-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Adapters","concept":"Translate external representations at architectural boundaries","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should external API or database representations be translated at an architectural boundary?","answer":"External representations should be converted into internal domain or application representations at the boundary so external schema changes do not propagate through core business logic.","explanation":"Adapters isolate vendor-specific fields, transport protocols, serialization formats, and persistence schemas from the core model.","principle":"Translate external data once at the boundary instead of leaking external representation throughout the system.","objective":"Reduce coupling between architecture and external technologies.","requirements":["Define internal representation","Implement boundary mapper","Handle incompatible or missing external fields explicitly"],"constraints":["Mapping adds transformation logic and must preserve required semantics"],"preconditions":["External and internal representations differ or may evolve independently"],"input":"External JSON user record.","output":"Internal User entity.","expected_behavior":"Core logic operates on the internal entity without depending on JSON field names.","incorrect_code":"def authorize(user_json):\n    if user_json["external_status"] == "active":\n        return True","corrected_code":"def to_user(payload):\n    return User(status=payload["external_status"])\n\ndef authorize(user):\n    return user.status == "active"","failure_mode":"External schema becomes part of core business logic.","expected_error":"External field rename requires changes across unrelated domain logic.","cause":"No translation boundary exists.","root_cause":"Transport representation leaked into internal architecture.","solution":"Introduce an adapter or mapper at the boundary.","edge_case":"Lossless mapping may be required when downstream components need fields not currently modeled; the contract should make that need explicit.","security_implication":"Boundary validation prevents malformed or unexpected external data from entering trusted internal representations.","performance_implication":"Mapping creates allocation and CPU overhead; measure if processing high-volume data.","test_case":"Change external field naming while preserving internal semantic meaning.","test_input":"External payload using a renamed status field mapped by adapter.","expected_test_result":"Only adapter mapping changes; internal authorization logic remains unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Use adapter contract tests and malformed-input tests.","verification_method":"Boundary contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no core module imports or interprets the external transport schema directly.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B032-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B031-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Adapter boundary"],"tests":["D35-B032-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Hexagonal architecture","Anti-corruption layer concepts"],"provenance":["Ports and Adapters architecture","Domain-driven design anti-corruption layer guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B032-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Data Ownership","concept":"Assign explicit ownership of mutable data","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should architectural data ownership be implemented?","answer":"Assign each mutable aggregate or state store a clear owner responsible for invariants and mutation, and expose controlled operations rather than unrestricted shared mutation.","explanation":"Unclear data ownership causes hidden coupling and makes invariants difficult to enforce. The implementation should make the owner of state and mutation explicit.","principle":"The component that owns an invariant should control mutation of the state required to preserve it.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled shared state from undermining architectural boundaries.","requirements":["Identify state owner","Define mutation API","Protect invariants","Avoid unrestricted shared mutable references"],"constraints":["Some high-throughput systems intentionally use shared memory but require stronger synchronization and ownership protocols"],"preconditions":["Architecture identifies mutable state"],"input":"Order state shared by multiple services.","output":"Order aggregate owning its state transitions.","expected_behavior":"Callers request valid transitions rather than mutating internal fields directly.","incorrect_code":"order.status = "paid"\norder.balance = 0","corrected_code":"order.mark_paid()\nassert order.balance == 0","failure_mode":"Callers mutate state without enforcing domain invariants.","expected_error":"Invalid state transitions can be accepted without detection.","cause":"Mutable representation is exposed directly.","root_cause":"State ownership is not enforced in implementation.","solution":"Encapsulate mutation behind invariant-preserving operations.","edge_case":"Serialization/deserialization may require controlled reconstruction rather than normal mutation methods.","security_implication":"Explicit ownership reduces unauthorized or accidental state modification paths.","performance_implication":"Encapsulation may introduce method calls but can enable localized optimization and invariant checking.","test_case":"Attempt an invalid state transition through the public API.","test_input":"Order already cancelled, followed by mark_paid().","expected_test_result":"Operation is rejected according to the aggregate contract.","testing_guidance":"Test legal transitions, illegal transitions, and concurrent mutation where applicable.","verification_method":"State-machine tests and code inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that public mutation paths preserve declared invariants.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"C++17+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["C++ compiler supporting selected standard"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B032-R004"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Explicit state ownership"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Encapsulation principles","Domain-driven aggregate concepts"],"provenance":["C++ Core Guidelines","Domain-driven design aggregate guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B032-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Boundary Testing","concept":"Test architectural adapters independently from core logic","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"What should be tested at an architecture adapter boundary?","answer":"Adapter tests should verify mapping, validation, error translation, required fields, boundary conditions, and preservation of internal semantic contracts.","explanation":"An adapter is a translation boundary. Testing only the downstream service can miss incorrect mapping or unsafe handling of external data.","principle":"Test both sides of a translation boundary and verify semantic preservation.","objective":"Detect integration and translation errors before they reach core logic.","requirements":["Test valid mappings","Test malformed input","Test missing fields","Test external errors","Test semantic preservation"],"constraints":["External systems may be unavailable during local testing"],"preconditions":["Adapter contract is defined"],"input":"Representative external payloads and error responses.","output":"Adapter test suite with deterministic assertions.","expected_behavior":"Valid inputs map correctly and invalid inputs fail predictably.","incorrect_code":"def to_user(payload):\n    return User(payload["name"])\n# No validation of missing or malformed fields","corrected_code":"def to_user(payload):\n    name = payload.get("name")\n    if not isinstance(name, str) or not name:\n        raise ValueError("invalid user name")\n    return User(name=name)","failure_mode":"Malformed external data reaches internal model.","expected_error":"Unexpected KeyError, type error, or invalid domain state.","cause":"Adapter assumes external data is valid.","root_cause":"Boundary validation is absent.","solution":"Validate and normalize external data before constructing trusted internal objects.","edge_case":"Optional external fields should have explicit default semantics rather than implicit falsy conversions.","security_implication":"Boundary validation reduces malformed-input and injection risk.","performance_implication":"Validation adds processing cost proportional to payload size.","test_case":"Pass missing, wrong-type, empty, and valid name values.","test_input":"{}, {"name":123}, {"name":""}, {"name":"Ada"}.","expected_test_result":"Only the valid representation is accepted.","testing_guidance":"Use table-driven tests for boundary cases and external error mappings.","verification_method":"Automated adapter unit and contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Ensure tests cover both normal and adversarial boundary inputs.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python test runner"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B032-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B032-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B032-R002"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Boundary validation principles"],"provenance":["OWASP input validation guidance","Contract testing practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B032-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Migration Boundaries","concept":"Use anti-corruption adapters during architectural migration","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How can a legacy component be integrated without allowing its model to contaminate a new architecture?","answer":"Place an explicit translation adapter around the legacy component and convert legacy concepts into the new architecture's internal model at the boundary.","explanation":"Migration rarely allows immediate replacement of every legacy dependency. A boundary adapter limits the blast radius while allowing incremental replacement.","principle":"Contain legacy models at explicit migration boundaries.","objective":"Support incremental architectural modernization without spreading legacy coupling.","requirements":["Define legacy boundary","Define translation mapping","Keep new core independent of legacy representation","Test both directions where required"],"constraints":["Some semantic differences cannot be mapped losslessly"],"preconditions":["Legacy component must remain temporarily available"],"input":"Legacy customer representation and new customer domain model.","output":"Adapter translating between models.","expected_behavior":"New components depend on the new model while only the adapter understands legacy details.","incorrect_code":"def process(customer):\n    if customer.legacy_type == 7:\n        return legacy_customer_code(customer)","corrected_code":"def from_legacy(value):\n    return Customer(kind=map_legacy_kind(value.legacy_type))\n\ndef process(customer: Customer):\n    return customer.kind","failure_mode":"Legacy representation leaks into new domain logic.","expected_error":"Removal of the legacy component requires changes across supposedly modern modules.","cause":"No anti-corruption boundary exists.","root_cause":"Migration integration was treated as direct reuse rather than translation.","solution":"Contain legacy semantics inside a dedicated adapter.","edge_case":"If the legacy model contains semantics absent from the new model, the adapter must record loss or reject unsupported cases rather than silently discarding meaning.","security_implication":"Legacy authentication or authorization semantics should not be silently trusted without explicit mapping and verification.","performance_implication":"Translation introduces overhead but can prevent broader coupling that would make future changes more expensive.","test_case":"Replace legacy representation with a semantically equivalent version requiring different field names.","test_input":"Two legacy payloads mapping to the same new Customer value.","expected_test_result":"New-domain behavior remains identical.","testing_guidance":"Test mapping equivalence and unsupported legacy cases.","verification_method":"Adapter contract tests plus dependency inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that new-domain modules do not import legacy types.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B032-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B032-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Migration boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Anti-corruption layer concept","Strangler migration pattern"],"provenance":["Domain-driven design anti-corruption layer guidance","Incremental modernization practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B033-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Event-Driven Boundaries","concept":"Implement architecture events as explicit immutable facts","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an architectural event be represented in implementation?","answer":"Represent an event as an immutable fact containing the event type, relevant identifiers, timestamp or logical ordering information, and sufficient payload to communicate the documented contract.","explanation":"Events communicate that something happened. Treating them as mutable commands or arbitrary dictionaries weakens their semantics and makes consumers harder to reason about.","principle":"An event describes a completed fact and should not be silently mutated after publication.","objective":"Create reliable asynchronous boundaries between architectural components.","requirements":["Define event schema","Define producer ownership","Define consumer expectations","Define ordering and delivery assumptions"],"constraints":["Exactly-once processing cannot be assumed merely because an event abstraction exists"],"preconditions":["Architecture intentionally uses event-driven communication"],"input":"OrderPlaced architectural event.","output":"Versioned immutable event representation.","expected_behavior":"Consumers can process the event according to a stable schema and tolerate documented delivery semantics.","incorrect_code":"event = {"type":"order_placed","order": order}\nevent["type"] = "order_cancelled"","corrected_code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass OrderPlaced:\n    order_id: str\n    occurred_at: str","failure_mode":"Published event is mutable or semantically ambiguous.","expected_error":"Consumers can observe inconsistent event meaning.","cause":"Event representation does not enforce immutability or contract semantics.","root_cause":"Architectural event concept was reduced to an unconstrained mutable structure.","solution":"Define explicit immutable event types and schema versioning.","edge_case":"Large payloads may be better represented by identifiers plus retrievable state, provided consistency semantics are explicit.","security_implication":"Events may propagate sensitive data to multiple consumers; payload minimization and access controls are required.","performance_implication":"Serialization, transport, and fan-out costs should be measured for high-volume event streams.","test_case":"Attempt to mutate an event after creation.","test_input":"OrderPlaced instance followed by assignment to order_id.","expected_test_result":"Mutation is rejected by the immutable representation.","testing_guidance":"Test schema validation, serialization, version compatibility, and duplicate delivery handling.","verification_method":"Contract and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that implementation matches documented event semantics and delivery guarantees.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B033-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Event-driven architecture boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Event-driven architecture","Event sourcing terminology"],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns","Python dataclasses documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B033-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Event Consumers","concept":"Make event consumers idempotent when delivery can repeat","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an event consumer be implemented when duplicate delivery is possible?","answer":"Use a stable event identifier or domain idempotency key and persist or otherwise reliably track successful processing so repeated delivery does not repeat non-idempotent side effects.","explanation":"At-least-once delivery can cause duplicates. Idempotency belongs at the consumer boundary whenever repeating the side effect would be incorrect.","principle":"Design side-effecting consumers for the actual delivery guarantee rather than an assumed exactly-once model.","objective":"Prevent duplicate event processing from producing duplicate side effects.","requirements":["Stable event identifier","Idempotency record or equivalent mechanism","Atomicity between processing and side-effect state where required"],"constraints":["Distributed systems may require transactional or durable coordination"],"preconditions":["Event delivery may repeat"],"input":"Repeated OrderPlaced event with the same event_id.","output":"One effective business side effect.","expected_behavior":"The first delivery performs the operation; subsequent duplicate deliveries produce no additional side effect.","incorrect_code":"def handle(event):\n    charge_customer(event.order_id)\n    send_receipt(event.order_id)","corrected_code":"def handle(event, store):\n    if store.was_processed(event.event_id):\n        return\n    charge_customer(event.order_id)\n    send_receipt(event.order_id)\n    store.mark_processed(event.event_id)","failure_mode":"Duplicate event causes duplicate side effects.","expected_error":"Customer may be charged or notified more than once.","cause":"Consumer assumes unique delivery.","root_cause":"Idempotency requirement was not translated into implementation.","solution":"Use a durable idempotency mechanism appropriate to the side-effect transaction boundary.","edge_case":"Marking an event processed before the side effect can suppress a retry after a failed side effect; ordering and transaction semantics must therefore be designed together.","security_implication":"Duplicate authorization or financial actions can become security or integrity incidents.","performance_implication":"Idempotency checks add storage or lookup overhead.","test_case":"Deliver the same event twice.","test_input":"Two identical event_id values.","expected_test_result":"Business side effect occurs once.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate, concurrent duplicate, failed-first-attempt, and replay scenarios.","verification_method":"Integration test using deterministic duplicate delivery.","verification_guidance":"Verify durable behavior across process restart if the architecture requires durability.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Durable event-processing store","Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B033-R001","D35-B033-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Idempotent event consumer"],"tests":["D35-B033-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Enterprise Integration Patterns idempotent consumer","At-least-once delivery semantics"],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns","Distributed systems messaging guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B033-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Command/Event Separation","concept":"Keep commands distinct from events","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What implementation distinction should exist between commands and events?","answer":"A command requests an action and may be rejected; an event records that something happened. They should have distinct types, naming, ownership, and processing semantics.","explanation":"Confusing commands and events leads to ambiguous ownership and retry behavior. Commands are directed requests; events are facts that may have multiple consumers.","principle":"Request intent and recorded fact are different architectural concepts and should remain distinguishable in code.","objective":"Preserve clear semantics at message boundaries.","requirements":["Separate command and event types","Define producer and consumer semantics","Define failure behavior"],"constraints":["Some systems use shared transport infrastructure but should still preserve message semantics"],"preconditions":["Architecture uses asynchronous or message-based communication"],"input":"Request to place an order and notification that an order was placed.","output":"PlaceOrder command and OrderPlaced event.","expected_behavior":"Command handling may reject the request; event consumers react to the resulting fact.","incorrect_code":"class OrderPlaced:\n    pass\n# Consumer interprets OrderPlaced as a request to place the order","corrected_code":"class PlaceOrder:\n    order_id: str\n\nclass OrderPlaced:\n    order_id: str","failure_mode":"A fact is incorrectly treated as an instruction.","expected_error":"Replay or multiple consumers may trigger unintended repeated commands.","cause":"Command and event semantics are conflated.","root_cause":"Architecture message types were not translated into distinct implementation contracts.","solution":"Create separate message types and document their direction and side-effect semantics.","edge_case":"An event consumer may issue a command as a consequence, but the two messages remain separate contracts.","security_implication":"Commands may require authorization that passive event observation does not; conflating them can bypass controls.","performance_implication":"Separate types add negligible direct cost compared with transport and processing.","test_case":"Replay an event and verify that it does not itself authorize a new command.","test_input":"Previously emitted OrderPlaced event.","expected_test_result":"Consumer performs only the event-defined reaction.","testing_guidance":"Test authorization, replay, and duplicate semantics separately for commands and events.","verification_method":"Message contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify names, ownership, authorization, and side-effect semantics.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"TypeScript 5.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B033-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Command/event boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Enterprise Integration Patterns","CQRS terminology"],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns","CQRS architectural guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B033-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Event Replay","concept":"Verify replay safety for event-driven implementations","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"How should replay behavior be tested in an event-driven architecture?","answer":"Replay a representative sequence from a known state and verify that consumers produce deterministic or explicitly documented outcomes without violating idempotency and ordering guarantees.","explanation":"Replay is useful for recovery, rebuilding projections, and diagnosis, but only if the architecture defines what replay means for each consumer.","principle":"Replay behavior must be an explicit contract, not an accidental property.","objective":"Verify that event processing remains safe under recovery and historical replay.","requirements":["Define replay semantics","Define idempotency behavior","Define ordering requirements","Define side-effect suppression or compensation"],"constraints":["External side effects may not be safely replayable"],"preconditions":["Event history or representative fixtures exist"],"input":"Ordered sequence of historical events.","output":"Replay verification result.","expected_behavior":"Internal projections rebuild correctly and external non-replayable side effects are not duplicated.","incorrect_code":"for event in history:\n    send_external_email(event)\n    rebuild_projection(event)","corrected_code":"for event in history:\n    rebuild_projection(event)\n# External side effects are disabled or separately controlled during replay","failure_mode":"Replay repeats irreversible external effects.","expected_error":"Duplicate external actions occur during recovery.","cause":"Replay path does not distinguish reconstruction from live side effects.","root_cause":"Replay semantics were not represented in implementation architecture.","solution":"Separate replay-safe projection logic from live side-effect processing.","edge_case":"Some compensating actions may intentionally be generated during replay, but they require explicit authorization and idempotency controls.","security_implication":"Replay can expose or reprocess sensitive data and must obey access controls.","performance_implication":"Large event histories may require batching, checkpointing, or parallel projection where ordering permits.","test_case":"Replay a known event sequence twice.","test_input":"Same event sequence from a clean projection state.","expected_test_result":"Projection state converges to the documented expected state without duplicate external side effects.","testing_guidance":"Test clean replay, partial replay, duplicate events, out-of-order events where supported, and restart recovery.","verification_method":"Deterministic replay test.","verification_guidance":"Compare resulting state and side-effect records against expected fixtures.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Event fixtures","Replay-capable consumer"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B033-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B033-R002"],"derived_from":["Event replay architecture","Event sourcing principles"],"provenance":["Event sourcing literature","Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B033-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Failure Isolation","concept":"Implement event consumer failure isolation","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an event consumer failure be prevented from destabilizing unrelated consumers?","answer":"Isolate consumer processing, define retry and dead-letter behavior where applicable, preserve event observability, and prevent one failing consumer from blocking unrelated processing paths unless the architecture explicitly requires coupling.","explanation":"Event-driven architecture reduces coupling only when failure handling is also decoupled. A shared processing loop without isolation can recreate synchronous failure propagation.","principle":"Failure boundaries should align with communication boundaries unless stronger coupling is explicitly required.","objective":"Contain consumer failures and preserve system availability.","requirements":["Independent consumer failure handling","Retry policy","Dead-letter or quarantine strategy where applicable","Observability"],"constraints":["Retry without idempotency can amplify side effects"],"preconditions":["Multiple consumers process a shared event stream"],"input":"Event that causes one consumer to fail repeatedly.","output":"Failed consumer is isolated while other eligible consumers continue.","expected_behavior":"Failure is recorded and retried or quarantined according to policy without silently dropping the event.","incorrect_code":"for consumer in consumers:\n    consumer.handle(event)","corrected_code":"for consumer in consumers:\n    try:\n        consumer.handle(event)\n    except Exception as exc:\n        record_failure(consumer.name, event.id, exc)","failure_mode":"One consumer exception aborts the entire dispatch cycle.","expected_error":"Unrelated consumers fail to receive the event.","cause":"No consumer-level failure boundary exists.","root_cause":"Implementation treated multiple architectural consumers as one failure domain.","solution":"Isolate consumer execution and define explicit retry/quarantine semantics.","edge_case":"Some event contracts require all consumers to succeed before the event is considered complete; that coupling must be explicit.","security_implication":"Failure records must not leak sensitive event payloads into unrestricted logs.","performance_implication":"Isolation and retries can increase resource consumption; bounded concurrency and retry budgets are required.","test_case":"Force one consumer to fail while another succeeds.","test_input":"Event with ConsumerA raising an exception and ConsumerB succeeding.","expected_test_result":"ConsumerA failure is recorded; ConsumerB completes according to the event contract.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated failure, retry exhaustion, quarantine, and successful independent consumers.","verification_method":"Integration failure-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both containment and durable observability of the failure.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Event dispatcher","Test consumers"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B033-R002","D35-B033-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Consumer failure boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Bulkhead and failure-isolation patterns","Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"provenance":["Release It! failure containment concepts","Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B034-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"State Machines","concept":"Translate explicit state-transition architecture into a state machine","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an architecture-defined lifecycle be implemented when only specific state transitions are allowed?","answer":"Represent states explicitly, define permitted transitions, reject undefined transitions, and centralize transition rules so lifecycle invariants are not duplicated across callers.","explanation":"A lifecycle diagram is most useful when translated into an executable state machine. Directly assigning state fields bypasses transition constraints.","principle":"Represent allowed transitions explicitly and reject transitions not defined by the architecture.","objective":"Preserve lifecycle invariants in implementation.","requirements":["Enumerate states","Enumerate allowed transitions","Define transition guards","Define invalid-transition behavior"],"constraints":["Real systems may need persisted transition history and concurrency control"],"preconditions":["Architecture defines a finite or otherwise explicit lifecycle"],"input":"Order states: CREATED, PAID, SHIPPED, CANCELLED.","output":"State machine enforcing valid transitions.","expected_behavior":"CREATED can become PAID or CANCELLED; PAID can become SHIPPED; invalid transitions are rejected.","incorrect_code":"order.status = "SHIPPED"","corrected_code":"ALLOWED = {\n    "CREATED": {"PAID", "CANCELLED"},\n    "PAID": {"SHIPPED"},\n    "SHIPPED": set(),\n    "CANCELLED": set(),\n}\n\ndef transition(order, target):\n    if target not in ALLOWED[order.status]:\n        raise ValueError("invalid transition")\n    order.status = target","failure_mode":"Caller directly changes lifecycle state.","expected_error":"Impossible or unsafe states can be persisted.","cause":"State representation is mutable without transition enforcement.","root_cause":"Architecture lifecycle was not implemented as an explicit state machine.","solution":"Centralize transition validation and state mutation.","edge_case":"A recovery workflow may require a special transition; it should be modeled explicitly rather than bypassing validation.","security_implication":"Unauthorized lifecycle transitions can bypass approval or security controls.","performance_implication":"Transition validation is normally inexpensive; persisted state-machine histories can add storage overhead.","test_case":"Attempt SHIPPED -> PAID.","test_input":"Current state SHIPPED, target PAID.","expected_test_result":"Transition is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Test every allowed transition and representative invalid transitions.","verification_method":"State-machine transition tests.","verification_guidance":"Compare implemented transition graph with architectural lifecycle specification.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B034-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Lifecycle state machine"],"tests":["D35-B034-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Finite state machine principles"],"provenance":["Finite state machine engineering principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B034-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Transition Guards","concept":"Implement architectural transition guards as explicit predicates","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should state-transition preconditions be represented in code?","answer":"Represent each transition's preconditions as explicit guard logic and evaluate them before mutating state.","explanation":"Architectural workflows often specify conditions such as authorization, payment confirmation, resource availability, or approval. Hidden guards scattered across callers are difficult to verify.","principle":"Transition eligibility should be evaluated at the transition boundary.","objective":"Prevent invalid or unauthorized state transitions.","requirements":["Define guard conditions","Evaluate guards before mutation","Return explicit failure semantics"],"constraints":["Some guards depend on external state and require consistency considerations"],"preconditions":["A transition has documented prerequisites"],"input":"Request to ship an order requiring payment confirmation.","output":"Successful transition only when guard passes.","expected_behavior":"Unpaid order cannot transition to SHIPPED.","incorrect_code":"def ship(order):\n    order.status = "SHIPPED"","corrected_code":"def ship(order):\n    if not order.payment_confirmed:\n        raise ValueError("payment required")\n    order.status = "SHIPPED"","failure_mode":"Transition occurs without required precondition.","expected_error":"Business invariant is violated.","cause":"Guard is omitted from transition implementation.","root_cause":"Architectural prerequisite was treated as caller responsibility.","solution":"Enforce the prerequisite at the transition boundary.","edge_case":"Guard state may become stale between validation and mutation; transactional or optimistic-concurrency mechanisms may be required.","security_implication":"Authorization guards must be enforced server-side and cannot rely solely on caller assertions.","performance_implication":"External guard checks can add latency and should be minimized or cached only when semantics permit.","test_case":"Ship unpaid and paid orders.","test_input":"payment_confirmed=false and true.","expected_test_result":"Only the paid order transitions.","testing_guidance":"Test each guard independently and combinations where guards interact.","verification_method":"Guard unit tests plus state transition integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that guards cannot be bypassed through alternate mutation paths.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 17+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Java 17+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Java compiler/runtime supporting selected language version"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B034-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B034-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Transition guard"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["State machine guard principles"],"provenance":["Finite state machine design principles","Secure server-side authorization practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B034-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"State Persistence","concept":"Persist state and transition history consistently","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What must be considered when persisting an architectural state transition?","answer":"The implementation should atomically preserve the new state and any required transition record so observers cannot see a state that has no corresponding history or history that was never applied.","explanation":"If state and history are written independently, failures can create contradictory representations. Transactional boundaries should match the consistency requirement.","principle":"Persist state and its required audit/history evidence within the same consistency boundary when atomicity is required.","objective":"Maintain consistency between current state and transition history.","requirements":["Define persistence transaction boundary","Persist state","Persist required transition record","Handle retry/concurrency"],"constraints":["Distributed stores may not provide a single transaction across all resources"],"preconditions":["Architecture requires durable transition history"],"input":"Order transition CREATED -> PAID.","output":"Current state PAID plus corresponding transition record.","expected_behavior":"Either both required records are committed or neither is visible as committed.","incorrect_code":"db.save_state(order)\nlog_transition(order)\n# Failure between operations creates inconsistent state/history","corrected_code":"with db.transaction():\n    db.save_state(order)\n    db.log_transition(order)","failure_mode":"State and history diverge after partial failure.","expected_error":"Audit trail does not represent actual current state.","cause":"Writes use separate failure boundaries.","root_cause":"Persistence transaction scope does not match architectural consistency requirement.","solution":"Use an appropriate atomic transaction or explicit distributed consistency protocol.","edge_case":"External event publication may require an outbox or equivalent pattern instead of a cross-system transaction.","security_implication":"Inconsistent audit records can undermine incident investigation and authorization evidence.","performance_implication":"Transactions may increase contention; scope should be kept appropriately narrow.","test_case":"Inject failure between state and history writes.","test_input":"Persistence operation failing after state write.","expected_test_result":"No invalid partial committed representation remains under the selected transaction semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test rollback, crash recovery, retries, and concurrent transitions.","verification_method":"Transactional integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Inspect persisted state and history after injected failures.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B034-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B034-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["State persistence boundary"],"tests":["D35-B034-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Transactional consistency principles","Transactional outbox pattern"],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns","Database transaction principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B034-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"State-Machine Testing","concept":"Test state-machine completeness and invalid transitions","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"How should an architectural state machine be tested for completeness?","answer":"Generate or enumerate all defined states and transitions, test every permitted transition, and test representative invalid transitions from each terminal or constrained state.","explanation":"Testing only the happy path leaves gaps in lifecycle behavior. A state transition matrix provides a direct mapping from architecture to executable tests.","principle":"A transition specification should have corresponding positive and negative test evidence.","objective":"Detect missing, extra, or incorrectly guarded lifecycle transitions.","requirements":["Transition matrix","Positive tests","Negative tests","Terminal-state tests"],"constraints":["Large state machines may require generated or property-based testing"],"preconditions":["Transition graph is defined"],"input":"State transition graph.","output":"Coverage report over states and transitions.","expected_behavior":"All required transitions have tests and prohibited transitions are rejected.","incorrect_code":"def test_order():\n    order = Order("CREATED")\n    order.pay()\n    order.ship()\n# Only one happy path","corrected_code":"def test_invalid_terminal_transition():\n    order = Order("CANCELLED")\n    with raises(ValueError):\n        order.ship()","failure_mode":"Test suite validates only successful paths.","expected_error":"Illegal transitions can survive unnoticed.","cause":"Architecture transition graph was not mapped to test cases.","root_cause":"Lifecycle testing lacks systematic transition coverage.","solution":"Build a transition matrix and test positive and negative edges.","edge_case":"Guards may make the same transition valid or invalid depending on context; test both conditions.","security_implication":"Authorization-sensitive transitions require explicit negative tests for unauthorized callers.","performance_implication":"Exhaustive transition testing can grow with state count; generated tests can reduce manual duplication.","test_case":"Enumerate each state and each candidate transition.","test_input":"Transition matrix including terminal states.","expected_test_result":"Coverage identifies missing tests and implementation mismatches.","testing_guidance":"Track transition coverage separately from line coverage.","verification_method":"State-machine test coverage analysis.","verification_guidance":"Compare implementation graph, specification graph, and test graph for consistency.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B034-R001","D35-B034-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B034-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B034-R001"],"verifies":["D35-B034-R001"],"derived_from":["State-machine testing","Model-based testing concepts"],"provenance":["Model-based testing principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B034-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Concurrency and State","concept":"Prevent lost updates when architectural state is concurrently modified","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_PERFORMANCE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a shared state transition be implemented when multiple actors can update the same entity concurrently?","answer":"Use an explicit concurrency-control strategy such as optimistic version checks, locking, or another mechanism appropriate to the consistency requirements, and reject stale transitions.","explanation":"A valid transition can still be incorrect if another actor changes the state between read and write. Architecture must therefore specify concurrency semantics where shared mutable state exists.","principle":"State transition correctness includes concurrency correctness.","objective":"Prevent lost updates and invalid concurrent transitions.","requirements":["Define concurrency policy","Detect stale state","Serialize or reject conflicting updates"],"constraints":["Locking and versioning have different scalability and failure characteristics"],"preconditions":["Multiple writers can update the same state"],"input":"Two actors reading version 4 and attempting different transitions.","output":"One valid transition and one detected conflict.","expected_behavior":"The second stale writer cannot silently overwrite the first update.","incorrect_code":"order = db.load(order_id)\norder.status = "SHIPPED"\ndb.save(order)","corrected_code":"order = db.load(order_id)\nexpected_version = order.version\norder.status = "SHIPPED"\ndb.save_if_version(order, expected_version)","failure_mode":"Last writer silently overwrites another state transition.","expected_error":"Lost update or invalid lifecycle state.","cause":"No concurrency control exists between read and write.","root_cause":"Architecture omitted implementation of shared-state consistency semantics.","solution":"Use version checks, locks, or an equivalent concurrency protocol.","edge_case":"A retry after conflict must re-read state and re-evaluate guards rather than replaying the stale mutation blindly.","security_implication":"Race conditions can bypass authorization or approval sequencing.","performance_implication":"Lock contention or conflict retries affect throughput and latency and should be measured.","test_case":"Run two concurrent updates against the same version.","test_input":"Both writers start with version 4.","expected_test_result":"At most one update commits for version 4; the other receives a conflict.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic concurrency tests where possible and supplement with stress testing for high-risk paths.","verification_method":"Concurrent integration tests plus persistence-level verification.","verification_guidance":"Inspect committed versions and transition history for lost updates.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B034-R001","D35-B034-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B034-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Concurrent state transition control"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Optimistic concurrency control","Concurrent state-machine design"],"provenance":["Database concurrency-control principles","Distributed systems consistency guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B035-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Error Boundaries","concept":"Map architectural failure domains to explicit exception boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should architecture-defined failure domains be represented in implementation?","answer":"Place explicit error boundaries around components that have different recovery, retry, or reporting semantics and translate low-level failures into stable boundary-level errors.","explanation":"Letting infrastructure exceptions propagate arbitrarily couples upper layers to implementation details and makes failure handling inconsistent.","principle":"Failure handling should occur at the boundary where the system understands the appropriate recovery semantics.","objective":"Prevent low-level failures from leaking through architectural contracts.","requirements":["Define failure categories","Define boundary error contract","Define retry/recovery semantics","Preserve diagnostic evidence"],"constraints":["Some errors must retain structured causes for observability and incident diagnosis"],"preconditions":["Architecture identifies component failure domains"],"input":"Database timeout occurring inside an application service.","output":"Stable application-level transient failure with preserved diagnostic context.","expected_behavior":"Caller receives a documented error category rather than database-specific implementation details.","incorrect_code":"def create_order(data):\n    return postgres.insert(data)","corrected_code":"def create_order(data):\n    try:\n        return postgres.insert(data)\n    except TimeoutError as exc:\n        raise TemporaryDependencyFailure("order store unavailable") from exc","failure_mode":"Infrastructure exception leaks into business/API contract.","expected_error":"Callers become dependent on database-specific exceptions.","cause":"No error translation boundary exists.","root_cause":"Failure architecture was not translated into implementation.","solution":"Catch and translate errors at the appropriate boundary while retaining causal context.","edge_case":"Do not collapse all errors into one generic exception when callers need different recovery behavior.","security_implication":"Internal connection details should not be exposed through external error responses.","performance_implication":"Exception handling on failure paths generally matters less than normal-path performance, but high-frequency expected exceptions should be redesigned.","test_case":"Inject a database timeout.","test_input":"Persistence adapter raises TimeoutError.","expected_test_result":"Application service raises documented TemporaryDependencyFailure with original cause retained internally.","testing_guidance":"Test transient, permanent, validation, authorization, and unknown failures separately.","verification_method":"Exception contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify both external error semantics and internal causal observability.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B035-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B031-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Failure-domain boundary"],"tests":["D35-B035-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Error boundary and exception translation principles"],"provenance":["Enterprise error-handling practices","Python exception chaining documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B035-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Retry Semantics","concept":"Implement retries only for failures classified as retryable","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should architectural retry policy be implemented without amplifying failures?","answer":"Classify failures explicitly, retry only operations whose semantics permit retry, apply bounded attempts and backoff, and ensure side effects are idempotent or protected by an idempotency mechanism.","explanation":"Blind retries can turn a transient outage into a larger incident and can duplicate non-idempotent actions.","principle":"Retry is a semantic decision, not a generic exception-handling rule.","objective":"Translate architectural resilience requirements into safe retry behavior.","requirements":["Failure classification","Bounded retry count","Backoff policy","Idempotency or transaction safety","Observability"],"constraints":["Retries consume capacity and can increase latency"],"preconditions":["Architecture specifies retryable dependency failures"],"input":"Transient network timeout during an idempotent read.","output":"Bounded retry sequence followed by success or final failure.","expected_behavior":"Transient read is retried according to policy; validation or authorization errors are not blindly retried.","incorrect_code":"for _ in range(10):\n    try:\n        return operation()\n    except Exception:\n        pass","corrected_code":"for attempt in range(3):\n    try:\n        return operation()\n    except TemporaryDependencyFailure:\n        if attempt == 2:\n            raise\n        sleep(backoff(attempt))","failure_mode":"All failures are retried indiscriminately.","expected_error":"Retry storms, duplicated side effects, or excessive latency.","cause":"Failure classification and retry limits are absent.","root_cause":"Resilience architecture was reduced to generic exception retry.","solution":"Define retryable categories and bounded backoff at the appropriate boundary.","edge_case":"A timeout does not always prove that an operation was not applied; writes require idempotency or reconciliation semantics.","security_implication":"Repeated authentication or authorization requests can create abuse opportunities if rate limits are absent.","performance_implication":"Backoff increases latency intentionally; retry amplification can consume substantial capacity.","test_case":"Simulate transient timeout followed by success and permanent validation failure.","test_input":"Failure sequence [timeout, timeout, success] and [validation_error].","expected_test_result":"First sequence succeeds within retry budget; second is not retried as transient.","testing_guidance":"Test retry exhaustion, jitter if used, cancellation, and idempotency.","verification_method":"Failure-injection tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual retry count, delay policy, and final error semantics.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.20+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B035-R001","D35-B035-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B035-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Retry policy"],"tests":["D35-B035-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Retry pattern","Release It! stability principles"],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns","Go error handling and context guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B035-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Translate dependency timeout requirements into explicit deadlines","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should architecture-level timeout requirements be implemented as explicit deadlines?","answer":"Every bounded external operation should receive a timeout or deadline appropriate to the end-to-end latency budget so a failed dependency cannot consume an unbounded portion of system resources.","explanation":"Without deadlines, a single blocked dependency can retain threads, tasks, connections, or memory and cause cascading resource exhaustion.","principle":"A component should not wait longer than the architecture's failure budget permits.","objective":"Contain latency and resource consumption caused by unavailable dependencies.","requirements":["Define timeout budget","Propagate deadline where supported","Release resources after timeout","Distinguish timeout from successful completion"],"constraints":["Too-short timeouts can create unnecessary failures; values must reflect measured system requirements"],"preconditions":["External or potentially blocking operation exists"],"input":"HTTP request with a 2-second service-level deadline.","output":"Bounded operation that terminates when the deadline expires.","expected_behavior":"Operation cannot block indefinitely.","incorrect_code":"response = client.get(url)","corrected_code":"response = client.get(url, timeout=2.0)","failure_mode":"External call blocks without bounded completion.","expected_error":"Thread, task, or connection remains occupied beyond the architectural latency budget.","cause":"Timeout requirement was not implemented.","root_cause":"Availability architecture omitted operational deadline semantics.","solution":"Apply explicit timeout/deadline controls and propagate them through dependent calls where possible.","edge_case":"A nested dependency should not receive a full parent deadline if the parent also needs time for response processing.","security_implication":"Unbounded waits can be exploited for resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Timeouts improve failure containment but may increase observed error rates if budgets are configured incorrectly.","test_case":"Use a dependency that deliberately exceeds the configured timeout.","test_input":"Operation duration greater than deadline.","expected_test_result":"Caller receives a timeout failure within the bounded policy.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, cancellation, retry interaction, and cleanup.","verification_method":"Latency-bound integration test.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual elapsed time and confirm resource cleanup.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B035-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B035-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Timeout boundary"],"tests":["D35-B035-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Timeout and deadline propagation principles","Distributed systems failure containment"],"provenance":["Google SRE reliability practices","Distributed systems timeout guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B035-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Failure Injection","concept":"Use controlled failure injection to verify architectural resilience","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"How can an architecture's failure-handling design be verified rather than assumed?","answer":"Inject controlled dependency failures such as timeouts, connection errors, malformed responses, and partial failures, then verify the documented recovery and containment behavior.","explanation":"Happy-path integration tests cannot establish resilience. Controlled failure injection tests the actual boundaries where architecture claims failure isolation or recovery.","principle":"Resilience properties require failure-path evidence.","objective":"Verify that implementation behaves according to failure-domain architecture.","requirements":["Inject representative failures","Observe recovery behavior","Verify containment","Verify evidence and diagnostics"],"constraints":["Failure injection must be controlled and must not target unauthorized production systems"],"preconditions":["Failure behavior is documented"],"input":"Dependency simulator producing timeout and malformed-response failures.","output":"Observed resilience test results.","expected_behavior":"System applies configured failure handling without corrupting state or leaking sensitive details.","incorrect_code":"def test_service():\n    assert service.get_data() == expected\n# Only success path","corrected_code":"def test_timeout_is_contained():\n    dependency.fail_with_timeout()\n    assert service.get_data() == documented_fallback","failure_mode":"Resilience assumptions remain untested.","expected_error":"Production-only failure modes expose missing recovery logic.","cause":"Tests exercise only successful dependency behavior.","root_cause":"Failure scenarios were not mapped from architecture into tests.","solution":"Build deterministic failure injection fixtures and verify each documented failure mode.","edge_case":"Injected failures must preserve realistic timing and error semantics to avoid false confidence.","security_implication":"Failure tests should verify that internal dependency details are not exposed to untrusted callers.","performance_implication":"Failure-injection suites add test time but can be isolated from normal performance benchmarks.","test_case":"Inject timeout, connection reset, invalid response, and repeated failure.","test_input":"Four controlled failure scenarios.","expected_test_result":"Each produces the documented failure behavior and containment result.","testing_guidance":"Record which failure mode was injected and which architectural requirement it verifies.","verification_method":"Failure-injection integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit evidence for each resilience requirement rather than a generic pass.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B035-R001","D35-B035-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B035-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B035-R001","D35-B035-R003"],"derived_from":["Chaos engineering principles","Failure injection testing"],"provenance":["Principles of Chaos Engineering","Google SRE testing practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B035-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Error Observability","concept":"Preserve causal error information across architectural boundaries","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should implementation preserve useful diagnostic information when translating errors?","answer":"Expose stable error categories to callers while retaining structured internal cause, context, correlation identifiers, and relevant operation metadata for authorized diagnostics.","explanation":"Replacing a low-level exception with a generic message can protect abstraction boundaries but may destroy evidence needed for diagnosis. The implementation should separate external semantics from internal diagnostic context.","principle":"Abstract the error contract without discarding the causal evidence required for verification and diagnosis.","objective":"Maintain debuggability while preserving architectural encapsulation.","requirements":["Stable external error category","Causal chain","Correlation identifier","Structured diagnostic context","Sensitive-data filtering"],"constraints":["Diagnostic context must respect privacy and security requirements"],"preconditions":["Errors cross an architectural boundary"],"input":"Database timeout with internal connection metadata.","output":"External dependency-unavailable error plus restricted internal diagnostic record.","expected_behavior":"Caller receives safe error semantics while authorized diagnostics retain cause information.","incorrect_code":"except TimeoutError:\n    raise RuntimeError("failed")","corrected_code":"except TimeoutError as exc:\n    record_error(correlation_id, type(exc).name)\n    raise TemporaryDependencyFailure("dependency unavailable") from exc","failure_mode":"Error abstraction removes all diagnostic evidence or exposes sensitive infrastructure details.","expected_error":"Either diagnosis becomes difficult or external responses leak internals.","cause":"External error contract and internal diagnostic evidence are treated as the same representation.","root_cause":"Architecture lacks a dual-channel error handling design.","solution":"Separate safe caller-facing errors from structured internal diagnostics.","edge_case":"Stack traces may contain secrets through exception arguments; sanitize before external logging or telemetry.","security_implication":"Error logs can become sensitive-data stores and require access control and retention policies.","performance_implication":"Structured error telemetry adds small overhead, particularly under high failure rates.","test_case":"Trigger dependency failure and inspect external response and internal diagnostic record.","test_input":"Timeout containing simulated internal endpoint metadata.","expected_test_result":"External response omits sensitive internals while authorized diagnostic record preserves the causal category.","testing_guidance":"Test error sanitization, correlation, cause preservation, and log volume under repeated failures.","verification_method":"Integration test plus log/telemetry inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the diagnostic record is sufficient for root-cause analysis without violating data-handling requirements.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B035-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B035-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Causal error observability"],"tests":["D35-B035-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Structured logging","Secure error handling"],"provenance":["OWASP error handling guidance","OpenTelemetry observability concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B036-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Separate configuration from implementation behavior","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should architecture-defined configuration be implemented without embedding environment-specific values in business logic?","answer":"Represent configuration as validated data supplied at the composition boundary and inject it into components that require it.","explanation":"Hard-coded configuration couples implementation to one deployment context and makes validation, testing, and controlled rollout harder.","principle":"Deployment configuration is data supplied to implementation, not hidden behavior embedded in business code.","objective":"Make implementations environment-independent and explicitly configurable.","requirements":["Define configuration schema","Validate configuration","Inject configuration","Separate secrets from ordinary configuration"],"constraints":["Some compile-time configuration may be required for low-level systems"],"preconditions":["Architecture identifies configurable behavior"],"input":"Service timeout and feature configuration.","output":"Validated configuration object consumed by the service.","expected_behavior":"Business logic uses supplied configuration and does not read arbitrary environment variables throughout execution.","incorrect_code":"def request():\n    timeout = float(os.environ.get("TIMEOUT", "30"))\n    return client.get(timeout=timeout)","corrected_code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass ServiceConfig:\n    timeout_seconds: float\n\nclass Service:\n    def init(self, config):\n        self.config = config","failure_mode":"Configuration retrieval is scattered throughout implementation.","expected_error":"Tests and deployments behave differently because hidden configuration dependencies exist.","cause":"Configuration boundary is absent.","root_cause":"Architecture requirement for environment separation was not implemented.","solution":"Load and validate configuration once at composition time and inject it.","edge_case":"Dynamic configuration reload requires explicit consistency and lifecycle semantics.","security_implication":"Secrets must not be mixed into ordinary configuration logs or error messages.","performance_implication":"Centralized configuration avoids repeated environment parsing or remote configuration lookups.","test_case":"Instantiate service with different configuration objects.","test_input":"Timeout values 1.0 and 30.0.","expected_test_result":"Service behavior reflects injected configuration without reading process-global state.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, invalid, boundary, and secret-related configuration cases.","verification_method":"Configuration schema tests and dependency inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that components receive configuration through declared interfaces.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B036-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B031-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Configuration boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Twelve-Factor configuration principles","Dependency injection"],"provenance":["The Twelve-Factor App configuration principle","Configuration management practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B036-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Configuration Validation","concept":"Validate configuration before starting dependent components","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"When should configuration errors be detected?","answer":"Validate required configuration at startup or at the earliest safe boundary before components that depend on it begin normal operation.","explanation":"Failing early prevents partially initialized systems from operating with invalid assumptions.","principle":"Invalid configuration should fail at the boundary before it can produce ambiguous runtime behavior.","objective":"Prevent deployment-time configuration defects from becoming runtime data or security failures.","requirements":["Schema validation","Required-field checks","Range checks","Cross-field consistency checks"],"constraints":["Some values are intentionally dynamic and require runtime validation"],"preconditions":["Configuration schema is defined"],"input":"Configuration with timeout=-1.","output":"Startup validation failure.","expected_behavior":"Application does not enter normal operation with invalid configuration.","incorrect_code":"config = load_config()\nstart_server(config)","corrected_code":"config = validate_config(load_config())\nstart_server(config)","failure_mode":"Invalid configuration reaches running components.","expected_error":"Runtime failures or unsafe behavior occur later and with less diagnostic clarity.","cause":"Configuration validation is deferred.","root_cause":"Architecture startup boundary does not enforce configuration invariants.","solution":"Validate before dependent component initialization.","edge_case":"A dynamic setting may need runtime validation on update in addition to startup validation.","security_implication":"Invalid security configuration can weaken authentication, authorization, or transport controls.","performance_implication":"Startup validation adds negligible runtime overhead after initialization.","test_case":"Provide missing and out-of-range configuration values.","test_input":"Missing timeout and timeout=-1.","expected_test_result":"Startup validation rejects both configurations.","testing_guidance":"Test defaults, required values, invalid ranges, conflicting options, and secret references.","verification_method":"Configuration validation tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no dependent component starts before validation succeeds.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B036-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Configuration validation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Fail-fast configuration validation"],"provenance":["Configuration management practices","Application startup validation principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B036-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Feature Flags","concept":"Implement feature flags as controlled architectural decision points","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should feature flags be implemented so they do not become uncontrolled architectural branches?","answer":"Centralize flag evaluation, define ownership and lifecycle, validate allowed values, and remove obsolete flags rather than allowing indefinite branching throughout the codebase.","explanation":"Feature flags can support incremental rollout but create architectural complexity if scattered across business logic without ownership or retirement criteria.","principle":"A feature flag is temporary or governed configuration, not a substitute for architecture.","objective":"Control rollout while limiting branching complexity.","requirements":["Central flag definition","Explicit ownership","Default behavior","Rollout semantics","Retirement process"],"constraints":["Flags may need dynamic evaluation and consistent rollout semantics"],"preconditions":["Architecture identifies behavior requiring controlled rollout"],"input":"Feature flag enable_new_parser.","output":"Deterministic behavior selected according to policy.","expected_behavior":"Flag evaluation is explicit and observable.","incorrect_code":"if os.getenv("NEW") == "1":\n    path_a()\nelse:\n    path_b()","corrected_code":"if feature_flags.new_parser:\n    path_a()\nelse:\n    path_b()","failure_mode":"Uncontrolled environment-based branching spreads across implementation.","expected_error":"Different components interpret the same flag inconsistently.","cause":"Flag semantics are not centralized.","root_cause":"Feature rollout requirement was implemented as arbitrary configuration checks.","solution":"Define a governed feature-flag abstraction and lifecycle.","edge_case":"A request-scoped flag must not accidentally become process-global when architecture requires per-user or per-tenant behavior.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive flags require strict authorization and auditability.","performance_implication":"Flag evaluation should be cheap; remote evaluation on hot paths may add latency.","test_case":"Evaluate enabled, disabled, missing, and invalid flag states.","test_input":"true, false, missing, malformed.","expected_test_result":"Behavior follows documented defaults and invalid values are handled deterministically.","testing_guidance":"Test both branches and flag lifecycle removal.","verification_method":"Configuration and branch integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify all flag references use the governed mechanism and obsolete flags are removed.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"TypeScript 5.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B036-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Feature flag boundary"],"tests":["D35-B036-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Feature toggle patterns"],"provenance":["Martin Fowler feature toggles guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B036-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Configuration Testing","concept":"Test configuration equivalence across deployment environments","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"How can configuration-related architecture defects be detected before deployment?","answer":"Validate representative environment configurations against the same schema and verify that required invariants hold across supported deployment profiles.","explanation":"A configuration schema may be valid syntactically while still violating cross-field or environment-specific architectural requirements.","principle":"Deployment profiles should be tested as data against the same configuration contract.","objective":"Detect environment-specific configuration drift before runtime.","requirements":["Representative profiles","Schema validation","Cross-field checks","Secret-reference validation"],"constraints":["Production secrets should not be copied into test fixtures"],"preconditions":["Supported deployment profiles are defined"],"input":"Development, staging, and production-like configuration fixtures without real secrets.","output":"Configuration validation matrix.","expected_behavior":"All supported profiles satisfy required invariants or produce explicit documented exceptions.","incorrect_code":"assert load_config("dev")\nassert load_config("prod")","corrected_code":"for profile in profiles:\n    config = load_profile(profile)\n    validate_config(config)\n    validate_cross_field_invariants(config)","failure_mode":"Configuration is tested only for existence rather than semantic correctness.","expected_error":"Deployment fails or behaves incorrectly despite passing basic configuration checks.","cause":"Configuration test coverage is shallow.","root_cause":"Architecture configuration contract is not represented as testable invariants.","solution":"Build profile-based validation tests.","edge_case":"Environment-specific infrastructure may make some checks unavailable locally; mark them as partial verification rather than claiming complete validation.","security_implication":"Test fixtures must not contain production secrets.","performance_implication":"Configuration validation has low execution cost and can run on every build.","test_case":"Validate every supported deployment profile.","test_input":"Sanitized profile fixtures.","expected_test_result":"Invalid profiles fail with precise diagnostics.","testing_guidance":"Keep profiles version-controlled and sanitized.","verification_method":"Automated configuration validation.","verification_guidance":"Record profile, schema version, validation result, and exceptions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B036-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B036-R002"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Configuration validation practices"],"provenance":["Twelve-Factor configuration principles","Configuration testing practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B036-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Secret Boundaries","concept":"Keep secret acquisition outside ordinary business components","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an architecture's secret-management boundary be translated into implementation?","answer":"Business components should receive narrowly scoped secret-dependent capabilities or resolved configuration through a controlled composition boundary rather than reading secret stores directly throughout the application.","explanation":"Direct secret-store access from many components spreads privileged capabilities and makes auditing, testing, and rotation harder.","principle":"Minimize the number of components that can retrieve or handle secrets directly.","objective":"Reduce secret exposure and preserve architectural security boundaries.","requirements":["Centralized secret acquisition","Least-privilege access","No secret logging","Rotation semantics"],"constraints":["Some components legitimately require direct cryptographic key access and need specialized controls"],"preconditions":["Architecture defines a secret-management service or boundary"],"input":"Database credential required by a repository adapter.","output":"Repository configured with controlled credential access.","expected_behavior":"Domain and application layers never retrieve the credential directly.","incorrect_code":"class OrderService:\n    def save(self, order):\n        password = secret_store.get("db_password")\n        db.connect(password)","corrected_code":"class OrderRepository:\n    def init(self, db_connection):\n        self.db = db_connection","failure_mode":"Business logic gains direct access to secret storage.","expected_error":"Secret exposure and privileged dependency spread across the architecture.","cause":"Secret retrieval is embedded in business behavior.","root_cause":"Security boundary was not represented in dependency structure.","solution":"Resolve or inject the narrowly scoped capability at composition time.","edge_case":"Short-lived credential rotation may require a controlled credential provider abstraction rather than a static injected value.","security_implication":"Centralized secret access supports least privilege, auditing, and reduced accidental exposure.","performance_implication":"Credential retrieval should not occur on every business operation unless required by rotation semantics.","test_case":"Inspect dependency graph and test business service with a fake repository.","test_input":"OrderService without secret-store capability.","expected_test_result":"Business service can operate without access to secret storage.","testing_guidance":"Test secret absence, rotation, authorization, and logging behavior.","verification_method":"Static dependency analysis plus security tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify secret-store imports/capabilities are limited to authorized infrastructure boundaries.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B036-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Secret-management boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Least privilege","Secret management architecture"],"provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST access-control principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B037-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"API Boundaries","concept":"Translate architectural API contracts into versioned implementation interfaces","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an architectural API boundary be implemented so clients are not coupled to internal models?","answer":"Define explicit request and response schemas at the API boundary, map them to internal application models, and establish compatibility and versioning rules independently from internal implementation types.","explanation":"Using domain objects directly as API payloads leaks internal representation and makes API compatibility harder to control.","principle":"External API contracts are public boundaries and should be independently versioned from internal models.","objective":"Preserve client compatibility while allowing internal evolution.","requirements":["Explicit API schema","Input validation","Output mapping","Versioning policy","Compatibility rules"],"constraints":["Some internal and external models may intentionally align but should not be coupled accidentally"],"preconditions":["An external client consumes the architecture boundary"],"input":"CreateUser API request.","output":"Validated request mapped to internal CreateUser command.","expected_behavior":"Internal model changes do not automatically change the public API contract.","incorrect_code":"def create_user(domain_user):\n    return save(domain_user)","corrected_code":"def create_user(request):\n    command = CreateUser(name=request.name, email=request.email)\n    return application.create_user(command)","failure_mode":"API schema is identical to internal domain representation by accident.","expected_error":"Internal refactoring breaks external clients.","cause":"Boundary contract is not explicit.","root_cause":"API architecture was implemented as direct domain exposure.","solution":"Create explicit transport schemas and mapping.","edge_case":"Backward-compatible aliases can support migration but should have documented deprecation timelines.","security_implication":"Explicit schemas reduce accidental exposure of internal fields.","performance_implication":"Serialization and mapping add overhead; optimize only after measuring actual API bottlenecks.","test_case":"Add an internal field and verify API response remains unchanged unless explicitly contracted.","test_input":"Domain user with internal audit field.","expected_test_result":"Audit field is not exposed automatically.","testing_guidance":"Use API contract tests and backward-compatibility tests.","verification_method":"Schema contract and integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify public schema independently from internal model definitions.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"TypeScript 5.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B037-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B032-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["API boundary"],"tests":["D35-B037-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["API contract design","Hexagonal architecture"],"provenance":["OpenAPI specification concepts","API design best practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B037-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"API Compatibility","concept":"Implement backward-compatible API evolution deliberately","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What implementation changes are generally safer for a versioned API contract?","answer":"Prefer additive changes that preserve existing required fields and semantics; treat removal, incompatible type changes, and changed meaning as breaking changes requiring an explicit compatibility strategy.","explanation":"Compatibility is behavioral, not only syntactic. A field can retain its name while changing meaning and still break consumers.","principle":"Preserve established client-observable semantics unless a versioned breaking change is intentional.","objective":"Prevent accidental API contract breakage.","requirements":["Compatibility policy","Schema diff checks","Deprecation process","Consumer communication"],"constraints":["Legacy consumers may depend on undocumented behavior; contract tests help expose such dependencies"],"preconditions":["Existing clients consume the API"],"input":"Existing response plus new optional field.","output":"Backward-compatible response schema.","expected_behavior":"Existing clients continue to parse and interpret required fields correctly.","incorrect_code":"return {"user_id": str(user.id)}\n# Later changes user_id from string to integer without versioning","corrected_code":"return {"user_id": str(user.id), "display_name": user.display_name}","failure_mode":"A seemingly small change alters a public field's type or semantics.","expected_error":"Existing clients fail parsing or make incorrect assumptions.","cause":"Compatibility analysis is absent.","root_cause":"Public API evolution is treated as internal refactoring.","solution":"Use schema compatibility checks and explicit versioning for breaking changes.","edge_case":"Changing an optional field from absent to null can still break strict clients.","security_implication":"Compatibility changes to authorization or identity fields require especially strict review.","performance_implication":"Compatibility layers can temporarily add transformation overhead.","test_case":"Run old-client contract tests against the new server response.","test_input":"Response containing additive optional field.","expected_test_result":"Old client contract remains valid.","testing_guidance":"Test old and new client contracts where supported.","verification_method":"Consumer-driven contract testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify both structural and semantic compatibility.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 17+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B037-R001","D35-B037-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B037-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Backward-compatible API evolution"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Semantic versioning principles","API compatibility practices"],"provenance":["Semantic Versioning specification","OpenAPI compatibility practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B037-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"API Validation","concept":"Validate untrusted API input at the architectural boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Where should validation of untrusted API input occur?","answer":"Validate syntax, type, size, required fields, allowed values, and relevant semantic constraints at the external boundary before constructing trusted internal commands or entities.","explanation":"Validation at the boundary reduces the number of components that need to defend against malformed external representations.","principle":"Untrusted input becomes trusted only after explicit validation and normalization.","objective":"Prevent malformed external data from violating internal assumptions.","requirements":["Schema validation","Size limits","Semantic validation","Safe normalization"],"constraints":["Business rules may require deeper validation inside domain logic as well"],"preconditions":["API receives untrusted external input"],"input":"User registration request.","output":"Validated internal command.","expected_behavior":"Malformed or disallowed input is rejected before reaching privileged operations.","incorrect_code":"def register(request):\n    user = User(request["email"], request["role"])\n    return save(user)","corrected_code":"def register(request):\n    validate_request(request)\n    command = CreateUser(email=normalize_email(request.email), role=request.role)\n    return application.register(command)","failure_mode":"Unvalidated input enters trusted business or persistence code.","expected_error":"Malformed data, injection, or authorization bypass can occur depending on downstream assumptions.","cause":"Boundary validation is missing.","root_cause":"Architecture trust transition is not explicit in implementation.","solution":"Validate and normalize before crossing into trusted internal components.","edge_case":"Normalization rules must not silently change security-sensitive identifiers.","security_implication":"Input validation is a defense layer but must not replace authorization or output encoding.","performance_implication":"Validation consumes CPU proportional to input size; enforce size limits before expensive processing.","test_case":"Submit malformed role, oversized email, missing field, and valid request.","test_input":"Representative valid and invalid API requests.","expected_test_result":"Invalid requests are rejected deterministically.","testing_guidance":"Include boundary values and adversarial but non-destructive test fixtures.","verification_method":"API validation and security tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify validation occurs before privileged operations.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B037-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B037-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["API trust boundary"],"tests":["D35-B037-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Input validation principles"],"provenance":["OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet","NIST secure software development guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B037-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Contract Testing","concept":"Verify API implementation against its declared contract","knowledge_type":"TEST_VERIFICATION","question":"How should an API implementation be verified against its architectural contract?","answer":"Validate the implementation's request and response schemas, status/error semantics, required headers or metadata, and compatibility expectations against the declared API contract.","explanation":"A server can return plausible responses while violating required schema or error semantics. Contract tests detect these mismatches.","principle":"The declared API contract should be executable as a verification target.","objective":"Detect divergence between API architecture and implementation.","requirements":["Schema validation","Response validation","Error contract validation","Compatibility checks"],"constraints":["Contract tests do not prove all business behavior"],"preconditions":["Machine-readable or otherwise explicit API contract exists"],"input":"API implementation and contract definition.","output":"Contract verification result.","expected_behavior":"Implementation conforms to documented request, response, and error semantics.","incorrect_code":"return {"id": user.id, "name": user.name}\n# Contract requires id as string","corrected_code":"return {"id": str(user.id), "name": user.name}","failure_mode":"Implementation violates declared schema type.","expected_error":"Contract validator rejects response.","cause":"Implementation was not verified against the external contract.","root_cause":"Architecture contract and implementation tests are disconnected.","solution":"Run automated contract validation in CI and integration environments.","edge_case":"Contract versions must be selected explicitly when multiple API versions are supported.","security_implication":"Contract verification can detect accidental exposure of fields but does not replace security testing.","performance_implication":"Contract serialization and validation add test-time overhead rather than necessarily runtime overhead.","test_case":"Return a response with an incorrect field type.","test_input":"id as integer where contract requires string.","expected_test_result":"Contract test fails.","testing_guidance":"Validate success and error responses across representative endpoints.","verification_method":"Automated contract test.","verification_guidance":"Record contract version and implementation version used during verification.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"TypeScript 5.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B037-R001","D35-B037-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B037-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B037-R001"],"verifies":["D35-B037-R001"],"derived_from":["Contract testing principles"],"provenance":["OpenAPI specification concepts","Consumer-driven contract testing practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B037-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"API Security Boundary","concept":"Separate authentication, authorization, and business validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should API security responsibilities be separated in implementation?","answer":"Authenticate the caller, authorize access to the requested resource or operation, then apply business validation; do not treat successful authentication as authorization.","explanation":"These are different decisions. Combining them in one loosely defined check creates ambiguous security boundaries and makes testing difficult.","principle":"Identity verification, permission evaluation, and business validity are separate architectural concerns.","objective":"Preserve clear security responsibility boundaries.","requirements":["Authentication mechanism","Authorization policy","Business validation","Audit evidence"],"constraints":["Some systems combine middleware and service checks but the semantic responsibilities must remain distinct"],"preconditions":["API operation requires controlled access"],"input":"Authenticated caller requesting modification of a resource.","output":"Authorized and business-valid operation or explicit rejection.","expected_behavior":"Authenticated users without required permission are rejected before mutation.","incorrect_code":"if user.is_authenticated:\n    update_resource(resource)","corrected_code":"if not user.is_authenticated:\n    raise Unauthorized()\nif not policy.can_update(user, resource):\n    raise Forbidden()\nvalidate_business_rules(resource)\nupdate_resource(resource)","failure_mode":"Authentication is treated as sufficient authorization.","expected_error":"Users with valid identities may perform unauthorized actions.","cause":"Security responsibilities are conflated.","root_cause":"Architecture did not separate identity, permission, and business-rule boundaries.","solution":"Implement distinct checks with explicit ordering and evidence.","edge_case":"Object-level authorization must be evaluated against the actual resource rather than only a broad role.","security_implication":"This boundary is security-critical; every privileged operation should have an explicit authorization path.","performance_implication":"Authorization checks may require additional lookups; cache only when consistency and revocation semantics permit.","test_case":"Authenticated caller lacking resource permission attempts modification.","test_input":"valid identity, unauthorized resource.","expected_test_result":"Request is rejected without mutation.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthenticated, authenticated-but-unauthorized, authorized, and invalid-business-state cases.","verification_method":"Security integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that authorization occurs before side effects and that audit evidence identifies the decision.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 17+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B037-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B037-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["API security boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Authentication and authorization separation","Object-level authorization principles"],"provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","NIST access-control principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B038-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Persistence Mapping","concept":"Translate persistence architecture into repository implementations","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a persistence component be implemented when architecture isolates storage from business logic?","answer":"Define a repository or persistence port in the application/domain boundary and implement database-specific behavior in an infrastructure adapter.","explanation":"This prevents SQL, ORM, driver, and schema details from becoming dependencies of business logic.","principle":"Persistence technology should implement an architectural port rather than define the business model.","objective":"Keep business logic independent of storage technology.","requirements":["Repository contract","Persistence adapter","Mapping between storage and domain","Transaction semantics"],"constraints":["Complex queries may require specialized query ports rather than forcing everything through generic repositories"],"preconditions":["Architecture separates domain/application from infrastructure"],"input":"Order retrieval requirement.","output":"OrderRepository interface and database adapter.","expected_behavior":"Application code requests orders through a stable contract.","incorrect_code":"def get_order(order_id):\n    return db.execute("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id=?", [order_id])","corrected_code":"class OrderRepository:\n    def get(self, order_id):\n        raise NotImplementedError\n\nclass SqlOrderRepository(OrderRepository):\n    def get(self, order_id):\n        return map_row(self.db.query(order_id))","failure_mode":"Business layer becomes coupled to SQL representation.","expected_error":"Storage migration requires changes throughout business code.","cause":"Persistence adapter is absent.","root_cause":"Architecture boundary was implemented as direct database access.","solution":"Define storage port and map persistence representation at the infrastructure boundary.","edge_case":"Reporting/read models may intentionally use denormalized projections and should have explicit query contracts.","security_implication":"Centralized persistence adapters make query authorization and parameterization easier to audit.","performance_implication":"Mapping and abstraction can add overhead; specialized queries may be necessary for performance-critical paths.","test_case":"Replace SQL repository with in-memory repository.","test_input":"Equivalent repository contract implementation.","expected_test_result":"Application behavior remains unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Contract-test repository implementations and separately test SQL-specific behavior.","verification_method":"Repository contract tests plus integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify business modules do not import database driver APIs.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B038-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B031-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Persistence port"],"tests":["D35-B038-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Repository pattern","Ports and Adapters architecture"],"provenance":["Domain-driven design repository guidance","Hexagonal architecture"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B038-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Transaction Boundaries","concept":"Align persistence transaction boundaries with application consistency requirements","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an application transaction boundary be selected from architecture?","answer":"Place transaction scope around the set of state changes that must succeed or fail together according to the application's consistency invariant.","explanation":"A transaction that is too small can leave partial state; one that is too broad can create contention and coupling.","principle":"Transaction boundaries should follow business consistency boundaries rather than arbitrary method boundaries.","objective":"Preserve atomicity for required state changes.","requirements":["Identify atomic state changes","Define transaction scope","Define rollback semantics","Handle external side effects separately"],"constraints":["Distributed side effects may require an outbox or saga rather than a single database transaction"],"preconditions":["Multiple persistence changes participate in one invariant"],"input":"Order creation requiring order row and inventory reservation.","output":"Transactionally consistent local state or explicit distributed workflow.","expected_behavior":"Required local changes do not commit partially.","incorrect_code":"save_order(order)\nreserve_inventory(order)\n# Separate transactions may commit only one operation","corrected_code":"with db.transaction():\n    save_order(order)\n    reserve_inventory(order)","failure_mode":"Partial local state violates consistency invariant.","expected_error":"Order exists without required inventory reservation or vice versa.","cause":"Transaction boundary does not match business invariant.","root_cause":"Architecture consistency requirement was not translated into persistence scope.","solution":"Align local transaction scope with atomic invariant; use distributed coordination patterns when necessary.","edge_case":"External payment capture should not be assumed to participate in the same local transaction.","security_implication":"Partial authorization or entitlement state can create access inconsistencies.","performance_implication":"Long transactions increase lock duration and contention.","test_case":"Fail inventory reservation after order persistence begins.","test_input":"Injected reservation failure.","expected_test_result":"Local atomic changes roll back according to transaction semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test rollback, deadlocks, retries, and external side-effect boundaries.","verification_method":"Transactional integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect final database state after injected failures.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B038-R001","D35-B038-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B038-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Transaction boundary"],"tests":["D35-B038-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["ACID transaction principles","Transactional consistency design"],"provenance":["Database transaction principles","Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B038-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Persistence Security","concept":"Implement parameterized persistence operations at the infrastructure boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should architecture enforce safe database query construction?","answer":"Keep database access inside authorized persistence components and use parameterized query APIs rather than concatenating untrusted input into query text.","explanation":"Architectural isolation alone does not prevent injection if the persistence implementation constructs unsafe queries.","principle":"Security invariants must be enforced by the concrete adapter as well as the architecture.","objective":"Prevent injection and preserve persistence boundary security.","requirements":["Parameterized queries","Input validation where appropriate","Least-privilege database credentials","Auditability"],"constraints":["Validation does not replace parameterization"],"preconditions":["Application persists or queries user-influenced data"],"input":"User-supplied order identifier.","output":"Safe database query operation.","expected_behavior":"Identifier is treated as data, not executable query syntax.","incorrect_code":"query = "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id='" + order_id + "'"\ndb.execute(query)","corrected_code":"db.execute("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id=?", [order_id])","failure_mode":"Untrusted input changes database query semantics.","expected_error":"Potential injection or unintended data access.","cause":"Query is constructed through string concatenation.","root_cause":"Security requirement was not implemented in the persistence adapter.","solution":"Use parameterized queries or a safe query API.","edge_case":"Dynamic identifiers such as column names require allowlisted mapping rather than ordinary parameterization.","security_implication":"Unsafe query construction can expose or modify unauthorized data.","performance_implication":"Prepared or parameterized queries may improve plan reuse in some database systems, but performance must be measured for the actual driver/database.","test_case":"Supply a value containing query metacharacters.","test_input":"Non-destructive injection test fixture in an isolated test database.","expected_test_result":"Value is treated as data and no unintended query structure is produced.","testing_guidance":"Use authorized isolated test environments and static analysis for unsafe query construction.","verification_method":"Static security analysis plus integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual database API invocation is parameterized.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B038-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B038-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Secure persistence adapter"],"tests":["D35-B038-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Parameterized query security principle"],"provenance":["OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-89"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B038-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Persistence Verification","concept":"Verify persistence adapters against behavioral contracts","knowledge_type":"TEST_VERIFICATION","question":"How should multiple persistence implementations be verified against one architecture contract?","answer":"Define repository behavior tests that can run against each implementation, then add implementation-specific tests for database semantics, transactions, indexes, and failure behavior.","explanation":"A shared contract verifies architectural behavior while implementation-specific tests verify technology-dependent details.","principle":"Separate portable behavioral verification from storage-specific verification.","objective":"Prevent replacement implementations from silently violating application semantics.","requirements":["Shared repository contract","Implementation-specific tests","Failure-path coverage","Transaction behavior"],"constraints":["Database-specific semantics may not be reproducible in an in-memory fake"],"preconditions":["At least one repository contract exists"],"input":"In-memory and SQL repository implementations.","output":"Contract test results plus implementation-specific evidence.","expected_behavior":"Both implementations satisfy required contract semantics.","incorrect_code":"def test_repository(repo):\n    assert repo.get("x")\n# No checks for missing records, updates, or transaction behavior","corrected_code":"def test_missing_record(repo):\n    assert repo.get("missing") is None\n\ndef test_round_trip(repo):\n    repo.save(Order("x"))\n    assert repo.get("x").id == "x"","failure_mode":"Repository tests cover only one trivial success case.","expected_error":"Implementations can differ in important edge semantics without detection.","cause":"Contract is under-specified and under-tested.","root_cause":"Architecture contract was not translated into comprehensive behavioral tests.","solution":"Create a reusable repository contract suite and supplement it with adapter-specific tests.","edge_case":"Concurrency and transaction isolation may require tests that only a real database can provide.","security_implication":"Authorization and tenant-isolation semantics must be verified at persistence boundaries where applicable.","performance_implication":"Database integration tests are slower than unit tests; maintain a focused suite and separate expensive tests where appropriate.","test_case":"Run contract suite against each repository implementation.","test_input":"Shared repository test fixtures.","expected_test_result":"All required behavioral tests pass for supported implementations.","testing_guidance":"Include missing data, duplicate data, transaction failure, concurrency, and authorization scenarios.","verification_method":"Contract test suite plus integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Record which implementation, database version, and configuration were tested.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B038-R001","D35-B038-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B038-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B038-R001"],"verifies":["D35-B038-R001","D35-B038-R002"],"derived_from":["Contract testing","Repository pattern"],"provenance":["Contract testing practices","Database integration testing principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B038-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Data Migration","concept":"Implement versioned data migration paths","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an architecture change requiring persistent schema evolution be implemented safely?","answer":"Create explicit versioned migrations, define forward compatibility or rollout sequencing, validate transformed data, and provide a controlled rollback or recovery strategy where rollback is technically possible.","explanation":"Changing code and persistent schema simultaneously can create incompatible intermediate states during deployment. Migration architecture must account for rollout order and existing data.","principle":"Persistent schema evolution is a compatibility problem, not merely a code-change problem.","objective":"Evolve stored data without corrupting existing deployments or clients.","requirements":["Migration versioning","Data transformation rules","Compatibility window","Validation","Recovery strategy"],"constraints":["Some destructive schema changes cannot be safely rolled back without backup or forward migration"],"preconditions":["Persistent schema must change"],"input":"Old column status_code requiring migration to status.","output":"Migrated schema and data with documented compatibility behavior.","expected_behavior":"Existing records are transformed correctly and application versions can coexist according to rollout policy.","incorrect_code":"ALTER TABLE orders DROP COLUMN status_code;\n# Application still deployed with code expecting status_code","corrected_code":"-- Phase 1: add new representation\n-- Phase 2: backfill and validate\n-- Phase 3: deploy code using new representation\n-- Phase 4: remove old representation after compatibility window","failure_mode":"Schema is changed before all consumers support the new representation.","expected_error":"Older application instances fail or data becomes inaccessible.","cause":"Migration sequencing ignores deployment compatibility.","root_cause":"Architecture schema evolution was implemented as an immediate destructive change.","solution":"Use staged, versioned migration and compatibility sequencing.","edge_case":"Large tables may require online or batched migration techniques to avoid unacceptable locking or downtime.","security_implication":"Migration scripts must use least privilege and protect sensitive transformed data.","performance_implication":"Backfills can consume substantial I/O and CPU; measure and schedule them according to system capacity.","test_case":"Run migration against representative old data and validate all transformed records.","test_input":"Fixture containing normal, null, legacy, and boundary values.","expected_test_result":"Data satisfies new invariants and migration validation reports no unexplained loss.","testing_guidance":"Test migration on realistic snapshots and rehearse recovery before production use.","verification_method":"Migration integration test and data reconciliation.","verification_guidance":"Compare pre-migration and post-migration invariants and record migration version.","language":"SQL","language_version":"SQL standard/dialect-specific","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B038-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B038-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Versioned data migration"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Expand-and-contract migration pattern","Database migration practices"],"provenance":["Martin Fowler evolutionary database design guidance","Database migration engineering practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B039-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Implement observability as a cross-cutting architectural capability","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should observability requirements be represented in implementation?","answer":"Instrument important architectural boundaries with structured logs, metrics, traces or equivalent evidence, while keeping telemetry semantics consistent across components.","explanation":"Observability should expose system behavior without forcing business components to know infrastructure-specific telemetry details.","principle":"Observability should cross architectural boundaries through stable instrumentation contracts.","objective":"Make implementation behavior diagnosable and measurable.","requirements":["Structured events","Correlation identifiers","Relevant metrics","Trace context where applicable","Sensitive-data controls"],"constraints":["Instrumentation must avoid excessive overhead and telemetry volume"],"preconditions":["Architecture defines operational observability requirements"],"input":"Request crossing API, application, and persistence boundaries.","output":"Correlated telemetry describing the request lifecycle.","expected_behavior":"Operators can identify where latency or failure occurred without inspecting arbitrary application output.","incorrect_code":"print("database failed")","corrected_code":"logger.error("database_operation_failed", extra={"request_id": request_id, "operation": "save_order"})","failure_mode":"Unstructured or absent telemetry prevents reliable diagnosis.","expected_error":"Failures cannot be correlated across architectural components.","cause":"Observability requirement is not implemented as a boundary capability.","root_cause":"Operational architecture was treated as documentation rather than executable instrumentation.","solution":"Define telemetry contracts and instrument important boundaries consistently.","edge_case":"High-volume events may require sampling or aggregation while preserving required evidence.","security_implication":"Telemetry can contain sensitive data and must be minimized, protected, and access-controlled.","performance_implication":"Instrumentation adds CPU, memory, and I/O overhead; measure production-relevant impact.","test_case":"Trigger a failure across multiple components and correlate telemetry.","test_input":"Request with stable correlation identifier.","expected_test_result":"Failure evidence can be associated across component boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Test correlation, missing context, high-cardinality fields, and sensitive-data filtering.","verification_method":"Integration observability test.","verification_guidance":"Verify telemetry fields against the observability contract and inspect for secret leakage.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B039-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Observability boundary"],"tests":["D35-B039-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Observability engineering","Three pillars terminology"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry documentation","Google SRE observability practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B039-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Health Checks","concept":"Implement health checks according to dependency semantics","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should architectural health checks distinguish process liveness from dependency readiness?","answer":"Separate liveness checks, which determine whether the process should be restarted, from readiness checks, which determine whether the component can safely receive traffic or perform its role.","explanation":"A failed external dependency does not necessarily mean a process is dead. Conflating the checks can cause restart loops or route traffic to an unready component.","principle":"Health signals must correspond to the operational decision they control.","objective":"Prevent incorrect automated recovery decisions.","requirements":["Define liveness semantics","Define readiness semantics","Avoid expensive checks in liveness","Document dependency requirements"],"constraints":["Some services intentionally degrade without becoming unready"],"preconditions":["Deployment or orchestrator uses health signals"],"input":"Service with database dependency temporarily unavailable.","output":"Liveness remains healthy while readiness reflects inability to serve database-dependent operations, according to architecture.","expected_behavior":"Automated recovery does not restart a healthy process solely because an external dependency is unavailable unless architecture explicitly requires it.","incorrect_code":"def health():\n    db.query("SELECT 1")\n    return {"healthy": True}","corrected_code":"def liveness():\n    return {"healthy": True}\n\ndef readiness():\n    return {"ready": db_available()}","failure_mode":"One health endpoint controls both restart and traffic decisions.","expected_error":"Dependency outage can trigger unnecessary process restarts or readiness ambiguity.","cause":"Operational semantics are conflated.","root_cause":"Health architecture was not decomposed into explicit decisions.","solution":"Define separate liveness and readiness contracts.","edge_case":"A critical local invariant failure may legitimately make liveness fail even when external dependencies are healthy.","security_implication":"Health endpoints should expose minimal operational information to untrusted clients.","performance_implication":"Health probes must be cheap enough not to create load during outages.","test_case":"Simulate database outage while process remains healthy.","test_input":"db_available=false.","expected_test_result":"Readiness fails according to contract while liveness remains successful.","testing_guidance":"Test dependency outage, startup, shutdown, and degraded modes.","verification_method":"Deployment-level health probe tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify probe behavior matches the orchestration policy.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.20+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B039-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B035-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Operational health boundary"],"tests":["D35-B039-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Kubernetes health probe concepts","Site reliability engineering practices"],"provenance":["Kubernetes documentation","Google SRE practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B039-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Translate degraded-mode architecture into explicit fallback behavior","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a component implement a documented degraded mode?","answer":"Define which capabilities remain available, which are disabled, what fallback data or behavior is acceptable, and how the system signals degraded operation.","explanation":"A fallback is safe only when its semantics are explicit. Returning stale or incomplete data without indication can be worse than failing clearly.","principle":"Degraded behavior must be a documented contract with bounded semantic loss.","objective":"Preserve useful service behavior without silently violating correctness requirements.","requirements":["Define degraded capabilities","Define fallback semantics","Signal degraded state","Define recovery path"],"constraints":["Not all operations have safe fallbacks"],"preconditions":["Architecture defines an acceptable degraded mode"],"input":"Recommendation service losing a non-critical ranking dependency.","output":"Basic recommendation result using approved fallback behavior.","expected_behavior":"Critical functions continue while degraded behavior is observable and bounded.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    return ranking_service.rank(items)\nexcept Exception:\n    return items","corrected_code":"try:\n    return ranking_service.rank(items)\nexcept RankingDependencyUnavailable:\n    record_degraded_mode()\n    return approved_fallback(items)","failure_mode":"Generic exception produces arbitrary fallback behavior.","expected_error":"System returns semantically unsafe or misleading results.","cause":"Fallback behavior is not explicitly designed.","root_cause":"Degraded-mode architecture is absent from implementation contract.","solution":"Define bounded fallback behavior and failure signaling.","edge_case":"Fallback must not be used for security or financial decisions unless explicitly approved by architecture.","security_implication":"Fail-open fallback behavior can bypass security controls if not carefully constrained.","performance_implication":"Fallback may reduce dependency load but can increase local computation.","test_case":"Disable non-critical dependency and invoke service.","test_input":"Dependency unavailable.","expected_test_result":"Approved fallback is returned and degraded state is observable.","testing_guidance":"Test fallback correctness, stale data limits, recovery, and security-sensitive operations.","verification_method":"Failure-mode integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify fallback behavior against the architecture's documented degraded-mode contract.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B039-R002","D35-B039-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B035-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Graceful degradation boundary"],"tests":["D35-B039-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Graceful degradation patterns","Reliability engineering"],"provenance":["Google SRE practices","Release It! stability patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B039-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Operational Verification","concept":"Verify operational architecture through failure and recovery scenarios","knowledge_type":"TEST_VERIFICATION","question":"What evidence is needed to verify that operational architecture is actually implemented?","answer":"Execute controlled startup, dependency failure, recovery, shutdown, overload, and degraded-mode scenarios and compare observed behavior with documented operational invariants.","explanation":"Operational architecture cannot be verified solely by reading source code. Runtime evidence is required for timing, cleanup, recovery, and integration properties.","principle":"Operational claims require operational evidence.","objective":"Distinguish documented operational design from behavior demonstrated by execution.","requirements":["Scenario definitions","Controlled test environment","Expected outcomes","Evidence capture"],"constraints":["Production-scale behavior may require separate staging or controlled production validation"],"preconditions":["Operational requirements and test scenarios exist"],"input":"Service operational scenario matrix.","output":"Evidence-backed verification results.","expected_behavior":"Observed behavior matches documented operational requirements or discrepancies are recorded.","incorrect_code":"assert service.health() == True\n# No failure or recovery scenario","corrected_code":"dependency.fail()\nassert service.readiness() == False\ndependency.recover()\nassert wait_until_ready(service)","failure_mode":"Only static or happy-path checks support operational claims.","expected_error":"Recovery defects remain undiscovered.","cause":"Operational scenarios are not executed.","root_cause":"Architecture verification stops at code inspection.","solution":"Execute controlled operational scenarios and record evidence.","edge_case":"If a scenario cannot be executed locally, mark it unverified instead of treating design intent as execution evidence.","security_implication":"Operational tests must avoid exposing production secrets or affecting unauthorized systems.","performance_implication":"Overload and recovery tests require realistic resource limits to produce useful evidence.","test_case":"Dependency failure followed by recovery.","test_input":"Controlled dependency simulator.","expected_test_result":"Service enters documented degraded state and recovers within the defined operational condition.","testing_guidance":"Capture timestamps, logs, state transitions, and recovery evidence.","verification_method":"Scenario-based integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Mark each operational requirement EXECUTED/PASSED, EXECUTED_FAILED, or UNVERIFIED based on actual evidence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B039-R002","D35-B039-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B039-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B039-R002","D35-B039-R003"],"verifies":["D35-B039-R002","D35-B039-R003"],"derived_from":["Scenario-based verification","SRE operational readiness practices"],"provenance":["Google SRE practices","Software verification principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B039-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Auditability","concept":"Implement architectural decisions with traceable evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should implementation preserve traceability from an architectural decision to executable evidence?","answer":"Associate important architectural requirements with implementation components, tests, configuration, and verification records so reviewers can trace requirement to implementation and evidence.","explanation":"Without traceability, architecture documents can diverge from code and test suites can omit important properties. Traceability makes gaps visible.","principle":"Important architecture requirements should have identifiable implementation and verification evidence.","objective":"Enable systematic architecture compliance review.","requirements":["Stable requirement identifiers","Implementation references","Test references","Verification status"],"constraints":["Not every low-level design choice needs formal traceability"],"preconditions":["Architecture contains requirements that matter to correctness, security, or operations"],"input":"Requirement AR-SEC-01: unauthorized users cannot modify orders.","output":"Traceability links to authorization implementation and negative tests.","expected_behavior":"Reviewer can follow the requirement to code and test evidence.","incorrect_code":"# Requirement exists only in architecture document\n# No implementation/test reference","corrected_code":"REQUIREMENT = "AR-SEC-01"\n# Implementation and tests reference the same stable identifier","failure_mode":"Architecture requirement has no identifiable evidence path.","expected_error":"Compliance review cannot determine whether the requirement is implemented or verified.","cause":"Traceability is omitted.","root_cause":"Architecture artifacts and engineering artifacts are disconnected.","solution":"Use stable requirement IDs and maintain implementation/test/verification relationships.","edge_case":"Generated code may require source-template traceability rather than line-level mapping.","security_implication":"Security requirements benefit strongly from explicit traceability and negative test evidence.","performance_implication":"Traceability metadata has negligible runtime impact but can increase engineering maintenance effort.","test_case":"Select a security requirement and trace it to implementation and tests.","test_input":"Stable requirement ID.","expected_test_result":"All required traceability links exist or missing evidence is explicitly reported.","testing_guidance":"Automate traceability completeness checks where practical.","verification_method":"Traceability audit.","verification_guidance":"Verify links refer to real records or source locations and do not claim nonexistent execution evidence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B039-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B039-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Architecture traceability"],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B039-R004"],"derived_from":["Requirements traceability","Architecture compliance practices"],"provenance":["Systems engineering traceability principles","Software verification and validation practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B040-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Cross-Cutting Security","concept":"Implement cross-cutting security requirements through centralized and local controls","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an architecture's cross-cutting security requirements be translated into implementation?","answer":"Use centralized controls for shared policy enforcement where appropriate and local component checks for resource-specific invariants, ensuring no critical security property depends on a single bypassable layer.","explanation":"Cross-cutting concerns such as authentication, authorization, input validation, secrets, and auditability often span multiple components. A single generic middleware layer cannot enforce every resource-specific rule.","principle":"Security architecture should use defense in depth while keeping each control's responsibility explicit.","objective":"Translate security architecture into enforceable implementation controls.","requirements":["Central authentication","Explicit authorization","Boundary validation","Secure defaults","Audit evidence"],"constraints":["Duplicate security checks should not contradict each other"],"preconditions":["Security requirements are documented"],"input":"Protected order-modification operation.","output":"Layered security implementation.","expected_behavior":"Request identity, permission, input validity, and operation-specific invariants are independently enforced.","incorrect_code":"def update_order(request):\n    authenticate(request)\n    return db.update(request.data)","corrected_code":"def update_order(request):\n    principal = authenticate(request)\n    authorize(principal, request.order_id, "update")\n    command = validate_update(request.data)\n    return application.update_order(principal, command)","failure_mode":"Security architecture is represented by one broad check without operation-specific enforcement.","expected_error":"A caller may pass generic authentication while violating resource-level authorization or input invariants.","cause":"Security responsibilities are collapsed.","root_cause":"Cross-cutting architecture was implemented as a single control rather than coordinated controls.","solution":"Separate authentication, authorization, validation, and business invariants while defining their ordering.","edge_case":"Internal service-to-service calls still require explicit trust and authorization assumptions; network location alone should not be treated as proof of legitimacy.","security_implication":"Defense-in-depth reduces reliance on one control but each control must remain correctly scoped.","performance_implication":"Multiple checks add overhead; measure high-frequency paths and avoid redundant expensive operations.","test_case":"Authenticated but unauthorized caller submits malformed modification.","test_input":"Valid identity, denied resource, malformed payload.","expected_test_result":"Request is rejected before mutation and produces appropriate audit evidence.","testing_guidance":"Test control ordering and bypass paths, not just individual checks.","verification_method":"Security integration and negative testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify every critical security requirement has an enforcement point and corresponding test evidence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B037-R005","D35-B040-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B037-R005"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Cross-cutting security architecture"],"tests":["D35-B040-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Defense-in-depth","Secure-by-design principles"],"provenance":["OWASP ASVS","NIST Secure Software Development Framework"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B040-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Least Privilege","concept":"Translate architectural least-privilege requirements into narrowly scoped capabilities","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should least privilege be represented in application architecture and implementation?","answer":"Give components only the permissions and capabilities required for their documented responsibilities, preferably through narrowly scoped interfaces rather than broad administrative clients.","explanation":"A component can violate least privilege even when its code performs only legitimate actions if its injected dependency has unnecessary privileges.","principle":"Capability scope should match responsibility scope.","objective":"Limit the impact of compromised or defective components.","requirements":["Define capability scope","Use separate credentials or roles where practical","Restrict operations","Audit privileged actions"],"constraints":["Operational simplicity can conflict with fine-grained credentials; the tradeoff should be explicit"],"preconditions":["Components access privileged resources"],"input":"Reporting component requiring read-only order data.","output":"Read-only repository capability.","expected_behavior":"Reporting component cannot perform order mutations through its injected dependency.","incorrect_code":"reporting = ReportingService(AdminDatabaseClient())","corrected_code":"reporting = ReportingService(ReadOnlyOrderRepository())","failure_mode":"Read-only component receives administrative capability.","expected_error":"A compromised reporting path could perform unauthorized writes.","cause":"Capability is broader than responsibility.","root_cause":"Least-privilege architecture was not translated into dependency selection.","solution":"Create narrowly scoped interfaces and credentials for each responsibility.","edge_case":"Administrative maintenance jobs may legitimately need broader capabilities but should be isolated and explicitly authorized.","security_implication":"Least privilege reduces blast radius from defects and compromise.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained capability layers may require additional connections or credential management; measure operational overhead.","test_case":"Attempt a write through the reporting component.","test_input":"ReportingService with ReadOnlyOrderRepository.","expected_test_result":"Write operation is unavailable or explicitly denied.","testing_guidance":"Test capability absence as well as denied execution.","verification_method":"Authorization and dependency-boundary testing.","verification_guidance":"Inspect injected dependencies and effective permissions.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.20+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B040-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B038-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Least-privilege capability"],"tests":["D35-B040-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Principle of least privilege","Capability-based security concepts"],"provenance":["NIST access-control principles","OWASP least privilege guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B040-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Secure Defaults","concept":"Implement secure architectural defaults explicitly","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should secure defaults be represented in implementation?","answer":"Initialize security-sensitive configuration to the safest reasonable behavior, require explicit opt-in for weaker behavior, and validate incompatible combinations before operation.","explanation":"Security often fails when an omitted configuration value silently enables a permissive mode.","principle":"Absence of explicit permission should not silently create privileged or unsafe behavior.","objective":"Reduce insecure deployment risk caused by missing configuration.","requirements":["Safe defaults","Explicit opt-in for risky modes","Configuration validation","Auditability"],"constraints":["Some development environments may need relaxed behavior but should require explicit configuration"],"preconditions":["Component has security-sensitive configurable behavior"],"input":"Feature controlling anonymous access.","output":"Configuration defaulting to anonymous access disabled.","expected_behavior":"Omitted configuration does not enable anonymous access.","incorrect_code":"allow_anonymous = config.get("ALLOW_ANONYMOUS", True)","corrected_code":"allow_anonymous = config.get("ALLOW_ANONYMOUS", False)","failure_mode":"Missing configuration activates permissive behavior.","expected_error":"Deployment can unintentionally expose protected functionality.","cause":"Unsafe default was chosen.","root_cause":"Secure-default requirement was not translated into configuration initialization.","solution":"Choose restrictive defaults and require explicit enablement for exceptional behavior.","edge_case":"A secure default must remain compatible with required functionality; otherwise operators may disable the control to restore service.","security_implication":"Secure defaults reduce accidental exposure but do not replace authorization and monitoring.","performance_implication":"Restrictive defaults may reduce performance in some modes; any exception should be explicit and measured.","test_case":"Start with security configuration omitted.","test_input":"Empty configuration.","expected_test_result":"Protected behavior remains enabled.","testing_guidance":"Test omitted, malformed, explicit secure, and explicit exceptional configurations.","verification_method":"Configuration security tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect defaults and verify generated deployment configuration does not override them unexpectedly.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B036-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Secure configuration defaults"],"tests":["D35-B040-R004"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Secure-by-default principle"],"provenance":["OWASP Secure Coding Practices","NIST secure configuration guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B040-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Security Architecture Verification","concept":"Verify security architecture through negative-path testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_VERIFICATION","question":"Why should security architecture verification emphasize denied and malformed paths?","answer":"Security failures often occur when controls are bypassed, omitted, incorrectly ordered, or inconsistently applied, so verification must explicitly exercise unauthorized, malformed, stale, and boundary-condition requests.","explanation":"A successful authorized request proves functionality but does not establish that unauthorized paths are rejected.","principle":"Security verification must demonstrate both allowed behavior and enforced denial conditions.","objective":"Provide evidence that security architecture is actually enforced.","requirements":["Unauthorized cases","Malformed input cases","Privilege-boundary cases","Bypass-path cases","Audit verification"],"constraints":["Tests must remain within authorized isolated environments"],"preconditions":["Security requirements and enforcement points are defined"],"input":"Security test matrix for protected operation.","output":"Evidence showing allowed and denied behavior.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized operations are rejected before protected side effects occur.","incorrect_code":"def test_update():\n    assert update_as_admin()","corrected_code":"def test_unauthorized_update():\n    assert update_as_user_without_permission() == FORBIDDEN","failure_mode":"Test suite proves only positive authorization.","expected_error":"Authorization bypass remains undetected.","cause":"Negative security cases are absent.","root_cause":"Security architecture was mapped only to happy-path functionality.","solution":"Build a denial-oriented security test matrix and verify side effects remain absent.","edge_case":"A denied request may still produce safe audit evidence; tests should verify both denial and audit semantics.","security_implication":"Negative testing is essential evidence for access-control enforcement.","performance_implication":"Security test suites add validation cost but should be optimized for deterministic coverage rather than omitted.","test_case":"Run authenticated-but-unauthorized update and malformed authorized request.","test_input":"Two negative cases.","expected_test_result":"Both are rejected and no protected state mutation occurs.","testing_guidance":"Test every critical permission boundary and alternate route to the same protected operation.","verification_method":"Security integration tests and audit inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify denial result, absence of unauthorized side effect, and appropriate audit evidence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B040-R002","D35-B040-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B040-R002","D35-B040-R003"],"verifies":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B040-R002","D35-B040-R003"],"derived_from":["Negative security testing","OWASP verification practices"],"provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","NIST security testing guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}
{"record_id":"D35-B040-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D35-SCHEMA-1.0","topic":"Architecture to Implementation","subtopic":"Implementation Completeness","concept":"Determine whether architecture-to-implementation coverage is complete","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should an architecture implementation be assessed for completeness before claiming the architecture is implemented?","answer":"Map each architectural component, boundary, invariant, dependency rule, security property, operational requirement, and failure behavior to implementation evidence and verification evidence; mark missing or unverified items explicitly.","explanation":"A system can contain all named components while still missing critical architectural behavior. Completeness requires coverage of properties, not just component names.","principle":"Architecture implementation completeness is evidence coverage, not source-code volume or component count.","objective":"Prevent false claims that an architecture has been fully implemented.","requirements":["Architecture inventory","Implementation mapping","Test mapping","Verification status","Known limitations"],"constraints":["Some properties require runtime or production-scale evidence that may not be available locally"],"preconditions":["Architecture specification and implementation inventory exist"],"input":"Architecture requirement matrix and source/test inventory.","output":"Implementation coverage assessment.","expected_behavior":"Each requirement is marked COVERED, PARTIALLY_COVERED, MISSING, NOT_APPLICABLE, or UNKNOWN with supporting evidence.","incorrect_code":"implementation_status = "complete"","corrected_code":"coverage = {\n    "AR-001": "COVERED",\n    "AR-002": "PARTIALLY_COVERED",\n    "AR-003": "MISSING"\n}\nstatus = "NOT_LOCKABLE" if any(v in {"MISSING", "UNKNOWN"} for v in coverage.values()) else "REVIEW_REQUIRED"","failure_mode":"Implementation completeness is declared from code presence without evidence mapping.","expected_error":"Missing architectural behaviors remain hidden behind a false completion claim.","cause":"No requirement-to-implementation traceability exists.","root_cause":"Completeness is measured by implementation volume rather than evidence coverage.","solution":"Perform systematic architecture-to-implementation coverage and verification audit.","edge_case":"A requirement may be intentionally not applicable to a deployment profile, but that decision must be documented and evidenced.","security_implication":"Unverified security requirements must not be treated as implemented security controls.","performance_implication":"Coverage auditing has no meaningful runtime impact but requires engineering review effort.","test_case":"Provide a matrix with one missing and one partially verified requirement.","test_input":"AR-001=COVERED, AR-002=PARTIALLY_COVERED, AR-003=MISSING.","expected_test_result":"Overall status is not fully verified and missing evidence is surfaced.","testing_guidance":"Audit concepts, implementation, invalid implementation, edge cases, tests, security, performance, versions, and language-specific behavior.","verification_method":"Architecture implementation coverage audit.","verification_guidance":"Do not convert NOT_EXECUTED or ILLUSTRATIVE evidence into VERIFIED status without actual execution or authoritative evidence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"none","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_GUIDANCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D35-B039-R005","D35-B040-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B039-R005"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Architecture implementation audit"],"tests":[],"verifies":["D35-B039-R005","D35-B040-R004"],"derived_from":["Requirements traceability","Verification and validation principles"],"provenance":["Systems engineering verification principles","Software architecture compliance practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","updated_at":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z"}

{"record_id":"D35-B041-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation Boundary Design","subtopic":"Application Boundary","concept":"Explicit application boundary implementation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should an application boundary be implemented so that external callers cannot bypass application-level orchestration?","answer":"Expose application use cases through explicit entry points and keep domain and infrastructure details behind those boundaries. External adapters should depend on application contracts rather than invoking repositories, domain services, or infrastructure directly.","explanation":"Architecture becomes enforceable when the application boundary has a concrete implementation surface. Controllers, workers, CLI handlers, and scheduled jobs should enter through application services or command handlers instead of reproducing orchestration independently.","principle":"One architectural use case should have one authoritative orchestration boundary.","objective":"Prevent duplicated orchestration and architectural bypasses.","requirements":["Define explicit application entry points","Keep infrastructure behind interfaces","Route external adapters through application services"],"constraints":["The boundary must remain independently testable","Adapters must not own business orchestration"],"preconditions":["Application use-case contracts exist"],"input":"A request from an HTTP handler, worker, CLI, or scheduler.","output":"Application-level command/result processing.","expected_behavior":"All supported entry points invoke the intended application boundary without directly coupling to infrastructure.","incorrect_code":"def handle_http(request, repository):\n    entity = repository.load(request.id)\n    entity.apply_business_rule()\n    repository.save(entity)","corrected_code":"class UpdateEntity:\n    def init(self, repository):\n        self.repository = repository\n\n    def execute(self, entity_id):\n        entity = self.repository.load(entity_id)\n        entity.apply_business_rule()\n        self.repository.save(entity)\n\n\ndef handle_http(request, use_case):\n    return use_case.execute(request.id)","failure_mode":"Adapter bypasses the application boundary.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime error; architectural coupling is the failure.","cause":"Business orchestration is implemented inside an external adapter.","root_cause":"Architecture has no enforced application entry point.","solution":"Move orchestration into an application use case and make adapters delegate to it.","edge_case":"A background worker and HTTP adapter must execute the same use case without duplicating logic.","security_implication":"Centralized authorization and validation can be bypassed if adapters implement independent workflows.","performance_implication":"Centralization may add a small call layer but reduces duplicated processing and inconsistent optimizations.","test_case":"Invoke the use case through two different adapters and verify equivalent application behavior.","test_input":"Same logical command delivered by HTTP and worker adapters.","expected_test_result":"Both adapters delegate to the same application orchestration path.","testing_guidance":"Unit-test the application boundary independently and integration-test each adapter's delegation.","verification_method":"Static dependency inspection plus adapter integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify adapters reference application contracts rather than infrastructure implementations.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B001-R001","D35-B004-R001","D35-B024-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B004-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":["D35-B001-R001"],"refines":["D35-B004-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["application_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Application Service pattern","Clean Architecture dependency-boundary principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B041-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation Boundary Design","subtopic":"Command Contract","concept":"Explicit command object implementation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why implement application inputs as explicit command objects?","answer":"Command objects make use-case inputs explicit, transport-independent, and structurally testable.","explanation":"A command object separates application semantics from HTTP requests, queue messages, or CLI argument structures. This makes the use case reusable across entry points.","principle":"Application contracts should represent business intent rather than transport-specific structures.","objective":"Create stable application input contracts.","requirements":["Explicit input fields","Validation at the appropriate boundary","No transport-specific dependency"],"constraints":["Avoid embedding HTTP or messaging types in the command"],"preconditions":["A defined application use case exists"],"input":"Transport-independent command data.","output":"Typed application command.","expected_behavior":"The use case receives a stable representation of intent.","incorrect_code":"def create_user(request):\n    return service.create(request.headers, request.json)","corrected_code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass CreateUser:\n    email: str\n    display_name: str\n\n\ndef execute(command: CreateUser):\n    return user_service.create(command.email, command.display_name)","failure_mode":"Application logic depends directly on transport objects.","expected_error":"Coupling and difficult unit testing rather than a mandatory runtime exception.","cause":"Transport structure is used as application contract.","root_cause":"No explicit command boundary exists.","solution":"Introduce immutable command data structures.","edge_case":"Different transports provide equivalent intent with different field names.","security_implication":"Explicit contracts make validation and authorization inputs easier to audit.","performance_implication":"Small command allocation overhead is generally predictable; measure if commands are created at extreme volume.","test_case":"Construct a command without an HTTP request and execute the use case.","test_input":"CreateUser(email='a@example.com', display_name='A').","expected_test_result":"Use case executes independently of transport infrastructure.","testing_guidance":"Test command validation and use-case behavior separately.","verification_method":"Unit test construction and application execution without adapter dependencies.","verification_guidance":"Inspect imports to ensure command objects do not depend on transport frameworks.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B004-R002","D35-B023-R001","D35-B024-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B004-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B004-R002"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["application_command_contract"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Domain-driven design application command patterns","Clean Architecture boundary principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B041-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation Boundary Design","subtopic":"Result Contract","concept":"Explicit application result implementation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should application results be represented without leaking infrastructure details?","answer":"Return explicit application result objects containing business-relevant outcome data rather than database rows, ORM objects, or transport responses.","explanation":"The application layer should communicate outcome semantics. Transport adapters can translate those results into HTTP, messaging, or CLI representations.","principle":"Application output contracts should be independent of persistence and transport representations.","objective":"Prevent infrastructure representation leakage.","requirements":["Stable result structure","Business-relevant fields","No ORM or transport dependency"],"constraints":["Do not expose internal persistence state unnecessarily"],"preconditions":["A use-case boundary exists"],"input":"Application execution outcome.","output":"Transport-independent result object.","expected_behavior":"Adapters can map the result into their required external representation.","incorrect_code":"def execute(command):\n    return database.query(UserTable).first()","corrected_code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass UserResult:\n    user_id: str\n    display_name: str\n\n\ndef execute(command):\n    user = repository.find(command.user_id)\n    return UserResult(user_id=user.id, display_name=user.name)","failure_mode":"Persistence representation leaks through application boundary.","expected_error":"Architecture violation; exact runtime error depends on the persistence layer.","cause":"Use case returns infrastructure object directly.","root_cause":"No explicit result contract.","solution":"Map persistence/domain data into an application result.","edge_case":"A result requires optional fields that are unavailable for certain implementations.","security_implication":"Explicit mapping reduces accidental exposure of sensitive persistence fields.","performance_implication":"Mapping introduces object creation and should be measured if result volume is extremely high.","test_case":"Replace the repository with a fake and verify the use case returns the defined result type.","test_input":"Fake user record with internal persistence metadata.","expected_test_result":"Internal persistence metadata is absent from the application result.","testing_guidance":"Assert result structure and absence of infrastructure-only attributes.","verification_method":"Unit test plus static dependency inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify result module imports no persistence framework.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B004-R003","D35-B006-R001","D35-B023-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B004-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B023-R002"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["application_result_contract"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Clean Architecture boundary principles","Repository pattern implementation guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B041-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation Boundary Design","subtopic":"Boundary Isolation","concept":"Transport-independent orchestration","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"How can the same application workflow be reused by HTTP, messaging, and scheduled execution?","answer":"Implement the workflow once behind an application service and create thin adapters for each transport.","explanation":"Multiple delivery mechanisms should translate external inputs into application commands and translate application results back into transport-specific outputs.","principle":"Delivery mechanisms are replaceable adapters, not business workflow owners.","objective":"Avoid workflow duplication across transports.","requirements":["Shared application service","Thin adapters","Transport-specific translation"],"constraints":["Adapters must not introduce conflicting business rules"],"preconditions":["Application use case is transport-independent"],"input":"HTTP request, message, or scheduled trigger.","output":"Common application outcome.","expected_behavior":"Different adapters converge on the same application use case.","incorrect_code":"def http_handler(req):\n    perform_workflow(req.data)\n\ndef worker(message):\n    perform_workflow_with_different_rules(message.data)","corrected_code":"def http_handler(req, use_case):\n    return use_case.execute(to_command(req))\n\ndef worker(message, use_case):\n    return use_case.execute(to_command(message))","failure_mode":"Equivalent workflows diverge across adapters.","expected_error":"No required runtime error; inconsistent business behavior is the failure.","cause":"Workflow implemented separately for each transport.","root_cause":"Application orchestration is not centralized.","solution":"Delegate all entry points to the same use-case contract.","edge_case":"A transport has extra metadata that is not part of business intent.","security_implication":"Centralized workflow makes common authorization and audit rules easier to enforce.","performance_implication":"Adapter translation should remain lightweight; transport-specific batching may still be handled outside the core workflow when architecturally appropriate.","test_case":"Feed equivalent commands through HTTP and worker adapters.","test_input":"Equivalent logical request represented in two transports.","expected_test_result":"The same application use case is invoked with equivalent command data.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests for each adapter and shared use-case tests.","verification_method":"Integration tests plus dependency graph inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify both adapters depend on the application contract.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B001-R002","D35-B004-R001","D35-B024-R004","D35-B036-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B004-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["transport_independent_application"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Hexagonal architecture","Ports and adapters architecture"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B041-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation Boundary Design","subtopic":"Boundary Enforcement","concept":"Preventing direct infrastructure access","knowledge_type":"architecture_enforcement","question":"How should an architecture prevent external adapters from directly accessing infrastructure?","answer":"Combine package/module dependency rules with code review and architecture tests that reject forbidden imports or dependency directions.","explanation":"Documentation alone does not guarantee architectural boundaries. Automated dependency checks can make important architecture rules executable.","principle":"Important architecture constraints should be mechanically testable.","objective":"Detect architecture bypasses before deployment.","requirements":["Defined dependency rules","Automated architecture tests","Clear violation reporting"],"constraints":["Rules must match the intended architecture","Generated or test-only modules may require explicit exclusions"],"preconditions":["Module boundaries are defined"],"input":"Source tree and architecture dependency rules.","output":"Pass/fail architecture validation.","expected_behavior":"Forbidden dependencies are detected deterministically.","incorrect_code":"# application/service.py\nfrom infrastructure.database import Database","corrected_code":"# application/service.py\nfrom application.ports import UserRepository","failure_mode":"Inner layer directly imports outer infrastructure.","expected_error":"Architecture test failure.","cause":"Dependency points inward-to-outward instead of through a port.","root_cause":"Boundary is documented but not mechanically enforced.","solution":"Depend on an abstraction and inject the infrastructure implementation at composition time.","edge_case":"A module legitimately requires infrastructure for a migration or bootstrap path.","security_implication":"Boundary enforcement reduces accidental bypass of centralized security controls.","performance_implication":"Static architecture tests add build-time cost rather than request-path latency.","test_case":"Introduce a forbidden import and run architecture validation.","test_input":"Application module importing infrastructure module.","expected_test_result":"Architecture validation fails and identifies the dependency violation.","testing_guidance":"Keep architecture rules in automated CI tests.","verification_method":"Static dependency analysis.","verification_guidance":"Require a deterministic failing test for each prohibited dependency category.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x plus an architecture-analysis implementation if used","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B003-R001","D35-B040-R001","D35-B040-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B003-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B040-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["dependency_boundary_enforcement"],"tests":["D35-B040-R003"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Clean Architecture dependency rule","ArchUnit-style architecture testing concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B042-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Pipeline Composition","concept":"Explicit application pipeline","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should multiple application steps be composed without creating hidden control flow?","answer":"Represent the workflow as explicit sequential stages or commands with clearly defined inputs, outputs, and failure behavior.","explanation":"Explicit pipelines make execution order visible and allow individual stages to be tested, replaced, or instrumented without hiding control flow in callbacks or global state.","principle":"Workflow sequencing should be explicit and observable.","objective":"Make orchestration deterministic and testable.","requirements":["Explicit stage ordering","Defined stage contracts","Defined failure semantics"],"constraints":["Avoid unnecessary abstraction layers","Do not hide side effects inside unrelated stages"],"preconditions":["Stages have compatible contracts"],"input":"Initial application state or command.","output":"Final application result or controlled failure.","expected_behavior":"Stages execute in declared order and stop or compensate according to defined policy.","incorrect_code":"def execute(data):\n    return step3(step2(step1(data)))","corrected_code":"def execute(data, steps):\n    value = data\n    for step in steps:\n        value = step(value)\n    return value","failure_mode":"Workflow order or stage behavior becomes difficult to inspect.","expected_error":"Potential incorrect sequencing or uncontrolled exceptions.","cause":"Implicit composition hides stage boundaries.","root_cause":"Pipeline contract is not represented explicitly.","solution":"Use explicit stage collection and defined orchestration policy.","edge_case":"A stage is optional based on a feature capability.","security_implication":"Explicit stages make security-sensitive processing points auditable.","performance_implication":"Iterative stage dispatch introduces small overhead; avoid dynamic pipelines where static composition is sufficient and performance is critical.","test_case":"Provide three deterministic stages and verify execution order.","test_input":"Stages append their identifiers to an execution trace.","expected_test_result":"Trace matches declared stage order.","testing_guidance":"Test ordering, failure propagation, and optional-stage behavior independently.","verification_method":"Unit test execution trace and failure scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Assert both output and stage ordering.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B004-R004","D35-B038-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B004-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["explicit_application_pipeline"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Pipeline architecture pattern","Application orchestration principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B042-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Conditional Flow","concept":"Explicit conditional workflow branches","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should architecture-level conditional workflows be represented in implementation?","answer":"Represent significant branches as named policy decisions or explicit strategy objects rather than deeply nested conditionals spread across adapters.","explanation":"Named decision points preserve architecture intent and make alternative paths independently testable.","principle":"Architecturally significant alternatives should have explicit decision boundaries.","objective":"Prevent conditional business behavior from becoming scattered implementation logic.","requirements":["Named decision boundary","Defined alternatives","Test coverage for each supported branch"],"constraints":["Do not introduce strategy abstractions for trivial conditions"],"preconditions":["The workflow contains meaningful alternative paths"],"input":"Command plus decision context.","output":"Selected execution path.","expected_behavior":"Exactly the applicable branch is selected according to documented rules.","incorrect_code":"if user.admin:\n    if account.active:\n        if region == 'x':\n            do_a()\n        else:\n            do_b()","corrected_code":"class AccountPolicy:\n    def choose(self, user, account, region):\n        if not user.admin:\n            return 'DENY'\n        if not account.active:\n            return 'DENY'\n        return 'A' if region == 'x' else 'B'","failure_mode":"Business branch logic becomes duplicated or inconsistent.","expected_error":"Incorrect branch selection rather than a required exception.","cause":"Decision rules are embedded in unrelated orchestration code.","root_cause":"No explicit policy boundary.","solution":"Extract meaningful decision logic into a named policy.","edge_case":"Multiple policies apply and their precedence must be explicitly defined.","security_implication":"Authorization-related branches must fail closed and should not rely on ambiguous defaults.","performance_implication":"Policy dispatch is normally inexpensive; complex policies should be measured rather than assumed.","test_case":"Test authorized, inactive, unsupported-region, and supported-region cases.","test_input":"Multiple combinations of user, account, and region.","expected_test_result":"Each combination maps to the documented branch.","testing_guidance":"Use table-driven tests for decision matrices.","verification_method":"Decision-table tests and code review.","verification_guidance":"Verify every supported branch has explicit tests.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B027-R001","D35-B032-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B027-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["policy_boundary"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["conditional_workflow"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Policy object pattern","Application service design principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B042-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Failure Routing","concept":"Controlled orchestration failure paths","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should an application workflow handle a failure in one stage?","answer":"Define failure policy at the orchestration boundary: fail fast, retry through a bounded mechanism, compensate, or continue only when the stage is explicitly optional.","explanation":"Unspecified failure propagation creates inconsistent partial behavior. The architecture should determine which failures stop the workflow and which are recoverable.","principle":"Failure behavior is part of the workflow contract.","objective":"Prevent accidental continuation after critical failures.","requirements":["Failure classification","Defined propagation policy","Explicit optional-stage semantics"],"constraints":["Do not silently swallow failures","Retries must be bounded and appropriate"],"preconditions":["Stages expose predictable failure categories"],"input":"Workflow stage execution.","output":"Controlled success, retry, compensation, or failure.","expected_behavior":"Failure follows the declared policy.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    critical_step()\nexcept Exception:\n    pass\nnext_step()","corrected_code":"try:\n    critical_step()\nexcept RecoverableError:\n    raise\nexcept CriticalError:\n    raise","failure_mode":"Critical failure is swallowed and workflow continues.","expected_error":"Application-specific failure should propagate to the owning boundary.","cause":"Broad exception suppression.","root_cause":"Failure policy is absent or hidden.","solution":"Classify failures and explicitly route them according to workflow policy.","edge_case":"A telemetry failure should not necessarily fail the primary business operation.","security_implication":"Suppressing authorization or integrity failures can produce unsafe behavior.","performance_implication":"Explicit recovery may increase latency when retries are used; bounded retry policy is required.","test_case":"Inject recoverable and critical failures into individual stages.","test_input":"Stage raises RecoverableError and CriticalError.","expected_test_result":"Each failure follows its configured workflow policy.","testing_guidance":"Test failure classes independently and test maximum retry limits where retries exist.","verification_method":"Failure-path unit and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Confirm no broad exception handler silently discards critical errors.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B020-R001","D35-B036-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B019-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B019-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["workflow_failure_policy"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Resilient distributed system failure-handling principles","Structured exception handling principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B042-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Compensation","concept":"Compensating workflow implementation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"When should a workflow implement compensation rather than rollback?","answer":"Compensation is appropriate when completed side effects cannot participate in one atomic transaction and a later failure requires an explicit corrective action.","explanation":"A database rollback cannot undo an external email, payment request, or remote API mutation. Such workflows require explicit compensation or reconciliation semantics.","principle":"Non-transactional side effects require explicit recovery semantics.","objective":"Prevent partial external state from being mistaken for atomic success.","requirements":["Identify irreversible or external side effects","Define compensation where feasible","Record incomplete states"],"constraints":["Compensation may itself fail","Compensation is not equivalent to rollback"],"preconditions":["Workflow contains non-atomic side effects"],"input":"Workflow with completed external side effect and later failure.","output":"Compensated, reconciled, or explicitly incomplete state.","expected_behavior":"System records and manages partial completion according to defined recovery policy.","incorrect_code":"database.commit()\npayment_provider.charge()\n# assumes database rollback can undo charge","corrected_code":"payment_id = payment_provider.charge()\ntry:\n    database.commit()\nexcept Exception:\n    recovery.enqueue_compensation(payment_id)\n    raise","failure_mode":"External side effect remains after local transaction failure.","expected_error":"Recovery work must be recorded; exact provider error varies.","cause":"Atomic transaction boundary incorrectly assumed to include external system.","root_cause":"Architecture confuses local transaction atomicity with distributed atomicity.","solution":"Use compensation, durable recovery state, or reconciliation.","edge_case":"Compensation is impossible or semantically unsafe; explicit manual or automated reconciliation is required.","security_implication":"Recovery records may contain sensitive transaction identifiers and require access controls.","performance_implication":"Compensation introduces additional work and possible queue latency.","test_case":"Force local commit failure after external operation succeeds.","test_input":"Successful external operation followed by database failure.","expected_test_result":"Recovery action is durably recorded or an explicit incomplete state is produced.","testing_guidance":"Test partial-success scenarios and compensation failure separately.","verification_method":"Failure-injection integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify durable recovery state exists before reporting unresolved completion.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x plus test doubles for external provider and persistence","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B011-R001","D35-B016-R001","D35-B017-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B016-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["compensating_transaction"],"contrasts_with":["local_atomic_rollback"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["workflow_compensation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns, compensating transaction concept","Distributed transaction design principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B042-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Workflow State","concept":"Persisted workflow state","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should long-running application workflows preserve state between execution steps?","answer":"Persist explicit workflow state containing the workflow identity, current stage, relevant data, and recovery metadata rather than relying on process memory.","explanation":"Long-running or restartable workflows cannot safely depend on an in-memory call stack. Explicit state allows recovery after process termination.","principle":"Restartable workflows require durable state.","objective":"Enable recovery and continuation across process restarts.","requirements":["Stable workflow identifier","Current stage","Durable state","Recovery metadata"],"constraints":["Persist only necessary state","Sensitive state requires protection"],"preconditions":["Workflow may outlive one process execution"],"input":"Workflow command and durable state.","output":"Updated durable workflow state.","expected_behavior":"A restarted worker can determine the next valid workflow action from persisted state.","incorrect_code":"workflow_state = {}\nworkflow_state[workflow_id] = {'step': 3}","corrected_code":"class WorkflowState:\n    def init(self, workflow_id, step):\n        self.workflow_id = workflow_id\n        self.step = step\n\nrepository.save(WorkflowState(workflow_id, 3))","failure_mode":"Workflow progress disappears on process restart.","expected_error":"Missing-state or duplicate-processing behavior.","cause":"Progress stored only in memory.","root_cause":"Long-running workflow treated as a single process-local transaction.","solution":"Persist explicit workflow state and define transition rules.","edge_case":"State exists but the recorded step has already been externally completed.","security_implication":"Persisted workflow data must follow least-privilege and data-retention requirements.","performance_implication":"Persistence adds I/O per transition; batching and checkpoint frequency should be selected based on recovery requirements.","test_case":"Persist state, terminate the worker, and resume from the stored state using a test repository.","test_input":"Workflow state at stage 3.","expected_test_result":"Restarted execution loads stage 3 and continues according to transition rules.","testing_guidance":"Test restart, duplicate resume, corrupted state, and invalid transition scenarios.","verification_method":"State-machine integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify persisted state and transition validation independently.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x plus a persistence implementation for real execution","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B014-R001","D35-B017-R001","D35-B036-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B014-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":["D35-B014-R001"],"refines":[],"specializes":["long_running_workflow"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["durable_workflow_state"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Workflow state-machine design principles","Durable execution architecture concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B043-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Observability Implementation","subtopic":"Correlation","concept":"Correlation identifier propagation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should correlation identifiers be propagated through application boundaries?","answer":"Create or accept a correlation identifier at the boundary and explicitly propagate it through logs, application context, asynchronous task envelopes, and downstream calls where appropriate.","explanation":"A correlation identifier allows related operations to be investigated across components without coupling business objects to logging infrastructure.","principle":"Observability context should follow execution flow without becoming business state.","objective":"Enable end-to-end operational traceability.","requirements":["Stable identifier format","Boundary initialization","Propagation mechanism","Structured logging integration"],"constraints":["Do not expose sensitive data as identifiers","Avoid global mutable context"],"preconditions":["Application has an execution context concept"],"input":"Incoming request or internally generated operation.","output":"Execution context containing correlation identifier.","expected_behavior":"All related log records include the same correlation identifier.","incorrect_code":"CORRELATION_ID = request.headers.get('X-Correlation-ID')","corrected_code":"from contextvars import ContextVar\n\ncorrelation_id = ContextVar('correlation_id', default=None)\n\ndef set_correlation(value):\n    return correlation_id.set(value)","failure_mode":"Concurrent operations share or lose correlation state.","expected_error":"Misattributed logs rather than necessarily a runtime exception.","cause":"Process-global mutable identifier.","root_cause":"Execution context is not isolated per request/task.","solution":"Use task-local context propagation and explicit propagation across asynchronous boundaries.","edge_case":