{"record_id":"D35-B043-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Observability Implementation","subtopic":"Structured Event Logging","concept":"Structured application event logging","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should application events be logged so that they remain machine-queryable?","answer":"Emit structured records with stable event names, timestamps, correlation identifiers, severity, and bounded contextual fields instead of assembling free-form strings as the primary representation.","explanation":"Structured events make logs usable by automated diagnostics, search, alerting, and analytics while preserving an application-level observability contract.","principle":"Operational events should be represented as structured data.","objective":"Make runtime evidence machine-readable.","requirements":["Stable event name","Timestamp","Severity","Correlation context","Structured fields"],"constraints":["Avoid secrets and unnecessary personal data","Field names should remain stable"],"preconditions":["Logging infrastructure accepts structured records"],"input":"Application event.","output":"Structured log record.","expected_behavior":"Consumers can filter and correlate events without parsing message text.","incorrect_code":"logger.info(f'payment failed for {user_email}: {error}')","corrected_code":"logger.info('payment_failed', extra={'payment_id': payment_id, 'error_type': type(error).name})","failure_mode":"Important operational fields are embedded only in prose.","expected_error":"No required runtime error; downstream parsing becomes fragile.","cause":"Free-form logging used as data transport.","root_cause":"No structured event contract.","solution":"Define stable event schemas and serialize fields separately.","edge_case":"Error details may contain untrusted or sensitive content.","security_implication":"Structured logging still requires redaction and access controls.","performance_implication":"Serialization adds overhead; field volume should be bounded.","test_case":"Emit an event and capture the resulting record.","test_input":"payment_failed event with payment identifier.","expected_test_result":"Stable event name and required structured fields are present.","testing_guidance":"Contract-test required fields and sensitive-data redaction.","verification_method":"Structured log capture and schema assertions.","verification_guidance":"Verify required fields without depending on exact human-readable message wording.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B030-R001","D35-B043-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B030-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["free_form_logging"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["structured_logging"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry logging concepts","Structured logging engineering practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B043-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Observability Implementation","subtopic":"Metrics","concept":"Application metric boundary","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Where should application metrics be emitted in an architecture?","answer":"Emit metrics at stable architectural boundaries such as use-case execution, queue processing, dependency calls, and important state transitions.","explanation":"Metrics tied to meaningful boundaries reveal system behavior without requiring every internal function to produce telemetry.","principle":"Instrumentation should follow operationally meaningful boundaries.","objective":"Measure application behavior at useful abstraction levels.","requirements":["Stable metric names","Explicit units","Bounded labels","Defined aggregation semantics"],"constraints":["Avoid high-cardinality labels","Do not measure every function by default"],"preconditions":["Metrics backend or test collector exists"],"input":"Application operation.","output":"Metric observation.","expected_behavior":"Metrics identify operation volume, latency, and relevant failures without unbounded cardinality.","incorrect_code":"metrics.increment('request', labels={'user_id': user_id})","corrected_code":"metrics.increment('request', labels={'operation': 'create_user'})","failure_mode":"Metric cardinality grows with unbounded identifiers.","expected_error":"Backend-dependent resource or storage pressure.","cause":"User-specific identifiers used as metric labels.","root_cause":"No metric cardinality boundary.","solution":"Use bounded categorical dimensions and keep identifiers in logs/traces where appropriate.","edge_case":"A bounded tenant dimension may still become large at scale and requires an explicit cardinality budget.","security_implication":"Metrics should not expose sensitive identifiers.","performance_implication":"High-cardinality metrics can impose substantial storage and processing overhead.","test_case":"Record metrics for many distinct users and inspect label cardinality.","test_input":"1000 unique user identifiers.","expected_test_result":"Metric labels remain bounded when the corrected implementation is used.","testing_guidance":"Add cardinality checks to instrumentation tests where practical.","verification_method":"Metric schema and cardinality test.","verification_guidance":"Verify identifiers are not emitted as labels unless explicitly bounded.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Metrics collector implementation required for runtime execution","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_STANDARD","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B030-R001","D35-B043-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B030-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["metric_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry metrics specification concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B043-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Observability Implementation","subtopic":"Timing","concept":"Boundary latency measurement","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should latency be measured around an application operation?","answer":"Measure elapsed time with a monotonic clock around the operation boundary and record the result using a defined unit.","explanation":"Wall-clock time can change because of clock synchronization or administrative adjustments. Elapsed-duration measurement should use a monotonic time source.","principle":"Duration measurement requires a monotonic clock.","objective":"Produce reliable elapsed-time measurements.","requirements":["Monotonic clock","Start/end boundary","Explicit time unit"],"constraints":["Do not compare monotonic timestamps with wall-clock timestamps","Measurement overhead should be understood"],"preconditions":["Operation has a clear start and completion boundary"],"input":"Application operation.","output":"Elapsed duration observation.","expected_behavior":"Measured duration remains meaningful across wall-clock adjustments.","incorrect_code":"start = datetime.now()\nrun_operation()\nduration = datetime.now() - start","corrected_code":"from time import monotonic\n\nstart = monotonic()\nrun_operation()\nduration = monotonic() - start","failure_mode":"Duration can be distorted by wall-clock adjustments.","expected_error":"Incorrect latency measurement rather than necessarily a runtime error.","cause":"Wall-clock timestamps used for elapsed duration.","root_cause":"Clock semantics are not distinguished.","solution":"Use monotonic time for durations and UTC-aware wall time for timestamps.","edge_case":"Operation crosses system suspend/resume boundaries; clock semantics should be understood for the target platform.","security_implication":"Timing data can sometimes reveal sensitive behavior and should be access-controlled where necessary.","performance_implication":"Timing calls have overhead but are generally small; exact cost is platform-dependent.","test_case":"Mock the clock and verify elapsed duration calculation.","test_input":"Monotonic values 10.0 and 10.25.","expected_test_result":"Duration equals 0.25 seconds.","testing_guidance":"Inject the clock dependency when deterministic unit testing is required.","verification_method":"Unit test with controlled clock.","verification_guidance":"Verify the implementation uses a monotonic source for durations.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_STANDARD","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B033-R001","D35-B033-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B033-R004"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["wall_clock_duration"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["latency_measurement"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python time module documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B043-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Observability Implementation","subtopic":"Error Evidence","concept":"Exception evidence preservation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should application errors preserve enough evidence for diagnosis without exposing sensitive information?","answer":"Capture typed error identity, operation context, correlation identifiers, and safe structured metadata while preserving the original exception chain.","explanation":"Replacing exceptions with generic messages destroys diagnostic evidence. Conversely, logging raw exception data can expose secrets. The implementation must preserve cause while controlling emitted fields.","principle":"Diagnostic evidence should be preserved with deliberate information boundaries.","objective":"Support root-cause analysis without uncontrolled sensitive-data disclosure.","requirements":["Typed error identity","Exception chaining","Correlation context","Safe structured metadata"],"constraints":["Do not log secrets or raw sensitive payloads"],"preconditions":["Application has an error taxonomy"],"input":"Caught application or infrastructure exception.","output":"Preserved exception chain and safe operational evidence.","expected_behavior":"The owning boundary can diagnose the failure without receiving sensitive payload dumps.","incorrect_code":"except Exception as exc:\n    raise ApplicationError(str(exc))","corrected_code":"except InfrastructureError as exc:\n    raise ApplicationError('dependency_failure') from exc","failure_mode":"Original failure context is discarded or sensitive text is propagated blindly.","expected_error":"ApplicationError with preserved cause in the corrected implementation.","cause":"Exception conversion ignores cause and information boundaries.","root_cause":"Error translation is treated as string replacement.","solution":"Translate by semantic category and preserve causal chaining.","edge_case":"The original exception contains credentials or request payloads.","security_implication":"Raw exception serialization may disclose secrets or personal data.","performance_implication":"Exception chaining has negligible normal-path impact but failure logging volume can be significant.","test_case":"Raise an infrastructure error containing sensitive text and inspect translated output.","test_input":"InfrastructureError containing a simulated secret.","expected_test_result":"Application error category is safe while the internal cause remains available to controlled diagnostics.","testing_guidance":"Test both exception identity and redaction behavior.","verification_method":"Failure-path unit test and log inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify external error response excludes internal exception details.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B020-R001","D35-B025-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B020-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B020-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["error_evidence_preservation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python exception chaining documentation","OWASP logging and error-handling guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B044-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resilience Implementation","subtopic":"Timeout Boundary","concept":"Dependency timeout implementation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should an application enforce timeouts on external dependencies?","answer":"Pass an explicit bounded timeout or deadline into dependency operations and ensure the lower-level implementation honors it.","explanation":"Without timeouts, a stalled dependency can consume workers and propagate resource exhaustion through the system.","principle":"Every potentially blocking external dependency requires an explicit time bound appropriate to its operation.","objective":"Contain dependency stalls and prevent unbounded resource occupation.","requirements":["Explicit timeout","Timeout propagation","Defined timeout failure type"],"constraints":["Timeout must fit the operation's legitimate completion window","Do not treat timeout as proof that the remote operation did not execute"],"preconditions":["Dependency supports timeout or cancellation semantics"],"input":"Dependency operation plus timeout/deadline.","output":"Dependency result or controlled timeout failure.","expected_behavior":"The caller stops waiting after the configured bound.","incorrect_code":"response = client.get(url)","corrected_code":"response = client.get(url, timeout=5.0)","failure_mode":"Worker remains blocked indefinitely.","expected_error":"Timeout-related exception from the dependency client.","cause":"No explicit timeout.","root_cause":"Timeout policy omitted from dependency contract.","solution":"Define and propagate bounded timeout/deadline semantics.","edge_case":"The remote operation may continue after the client times out.","security_implication":"Unbounded waits can become a denial-of-service amplification vector.","performance_implication":"Timeout values affect concurrency, tail latency, and resource retention; they must be measured and tuned rather than assumed optimal.","test_case":"Use a dependency stub that blocks longer than the configured timeout.","test_input":"Dependency delay greater than timeout.","expected_test_result":"Caller receives a controlled timeout failure within the configured bound, subject to scheduling overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, cancellation, retry interaction, and resource cleanup.","verification_method":"Integration test with deterministic delayed dependency.","verification_guidance":"Verify timeout is actually passed to the lower-level client.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Dependency client with timeout support for real execution","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B033-R004","D35-B036-R002","D35-B039-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["dependency_timeout"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE Book, handling overload and latency concepts","Python client timeout patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B044-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resilience Implementation","subtopic":"Circuit Breaking","concept":"Circuit breaker boundary","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should repeated dependency failures be isolated from the rest of an application?","answer":"Use an explicit circuit state machine that limits calls to a failing dependency and transitions between closed, open, and half-open states according to measured failure and recovery conditions.","explanation":"Circuit breaking is a resilience mechanism that prevents repeated calls from consuming resources when a dependency is known to be failing.","principle":"Repeated dependency failure should be bounded by an explicit stateful policy.","objective":"Limit cascading resource consumption during dependency failure.","requirements":["Failure threshold policy","Open-state behavior","Recovery probe policy","State transition rules"],"constraints":["Circuit state must be scoped correctly","Do not infer dependency health from one arbitrary failure"],"preconditions":["Dependency failure classification exists"],"input":"Dependency call outcomes.","output":"Allow, reject, or probe decision.","expected_behavior":"Calls are restricted when the circuit is open and recovery is tested according to policy.","incorrect_code":"for _ in range(1000):\n    dependency.call()","corrected_code":"if breaker.allow_call():\n    try:\n        result = dependency.call()\n        breaker.record_success()\n    except Exception:\n        breaker.record_failure()\n        raise\nelse:\n    raise DependencyUnavailable()","failure_mode":"Application repeatedly calls an unavailable dependency.","expected_error":"Controlled DependencyUnavailable when the circuit is open.","cause":"No failure isolation mechanism.","root_cause":"Dependency availability is assumed rather than represented as runtime state.","solution":"Implement a bounded circuit-breaker policy.","edge_case":"A dependency failure is operation-specific and should not open a global circuit for unrelated operations.","security_implication":"Failing open may expose protected operations; default behavior must match the security boundary.","performance_implication":"Circuit checks add small local overhead while potentially reducing expensive failed dependency calls.","test_case":"Drive the breaker through closed, open, and recovery states using deterministic outcomes.","test_input":"Configured failure threshold followed by successful probe.","expected_test_result":"State transitions follow the documented policy.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent transitions and clock-dependent recovery logic.","verification_method":"State-machine unit tests plus dependency integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify state transitions are deterministic and race-safe.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B033-R004","D35-B044-R001","D35-B039-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B044-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["circuit_breaker"],"contrasts_with":["unbounded_retry"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["dependency_failure_isolation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Release It! stability pattern","Microsoft Azure Architecture Center circuit breaker pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B044-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resilience Implementation","subtopic":"Bulkheading","concept":"Resource pool isolation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How can one dependency prevent resource exhaustion across unrelated operations?","answer":"Isolate dependency work using bounded concurrency, dedicated worker pools, or resource limits so one dependency cannot consume all shared execution capacity.","explanation":"Bulkheading converts an architectural isolation requirement into explicit resource allocation.","principle":"Shared capacity should not allow one failure domain to consume all resources.","objective":"Contain resource exhaustion and cascading failure.","requirements":["Bounded concurrency","Separate or reserved capacity","Defined rejection behavior"],"constraints":["Capacity limits require workload-aware tuning","Do not assume one pool size fits all workloads"],"preconditions":["Operations can be classified by dependency or workload"],"input":"Work item requiring dependency capacity.","output":"Accepted execution or controlled rejection.","expected_behavior":"One workload cannot exceed its allocated concurrency/resource boundary.","incorrect_code":"executor.submit(call_slow_dependency)\nexecutor.submit(call_slow_dependency)","corrected_code":"slow_dependency_pool = ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4)\nslow_dependency_pool.submit(call_slow_dependency)","failure_mode":"Slow dependency consumes all worker capacity.","expected_error":"Controlled queueing or rejection rather than global worker exhaustion.","cause":"All dependency operations share one unconstrained pool.","root_cause":"No resource isolation boundary.","solution":"Use bounded resource pools and explicit overload behavior.","edge_case":"A pool is bounded but its queue is unbounded and still consumes memory.","security_implication":"Resource exhaustion can affect availability of security-critical functions.","performance_implication":"Lower concurrency may reduce throughput; capacity must be validated through measurement under representative load.","test_case":"Submit more slow dependency operations than the configured capacity.","test_input":"10 tasks with a four-worker isolated pool.","expected_test_result":"At most the configured concurrent capacity executes simultaneously.","testing_guidance":"Test saturation, queue behavior, cancellation, and shutdown.","verification_method":"Concurrency integration test.","verification_guidance":"Measure active concurrency rather than assuming pool configuration is enforced.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B036-R001","D35-B039-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["bulkhead"],"contrasts_with":["shared_unbounded_pool"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["resource_isolation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Release It! stability pattern","Google SRE resource isolation concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B044-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resilience Implementation","subtopic":"Retry Coordination","concept":"Bounded retry implementation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should retries be implemented without creating retry storms?","answer":"Retry only failures classified as transient, apply bounded attempts and backoff, and coordinate retry behavior with timeout and idempotency policies.","explanation":"Retries can improve transient-failure recovery but can amplify load when many clients retry simultaneously or when the operation is not safe to repeat.","principle":"Retries are a controlled recovery policy, not a generic exception handler.","objective":"Recover from appropriate transient failures while limiting amplification.","requirements":["Transient-failure classification","Maximum attempts","Backoff","Idempotency policy","Timeout budget"],"constraints":["Do not retry permanent failures","Do not exceed overall operation deadline"],"preconditions":["Operation has defined retry semantics"],"input":"Dependency operation and retry policy.","output":"Result or terminal failure.","expected_behavior":"Only retryable failures are retried within bounded limits.","incorrect_code":"while True:\n    try:\n        return dependency.call()\n    except Exception:\n        continue","corrected_code":"for attempt in range(3):\n    try:\n        return dependency.call()\n    except TransientError:\n        if attempt == 2:\n            raise\n        sleep(backoff(attempt))","failure_mode":"Unbounded retries amplify dependency failure.","expected_error":"Terminal TransientError after the configured attempt budget.","cause":"Infinite retry loop and broad exception handling.","root_cause":"Retry policy lacks bounded semantics.","solution":"Classify failures and enforce attempt, time, and idempotency limits.","edge_case":"A request times out after the remote side completed the operation.","security_implication":"Repeated authorization-sensitive operations can have unintended effects if not idempotent.","performance_implication":"Retry increases latency and load; no universal optimal retry count exists without workload and dependency measurements.","test_case":"Inject three transient failures and verify the fourth attempt is not made when maximum attempts are three.","test_input":"Three sequential TransientError outcomes.","expected_test_result":"Exactly three attempts occur and terminal failure is returned.","testing_guidance":"Test transient, permanent, timeout, cancellation, and idempotency cases.","verification_method":"Deterministic retry-policy unit test.","verification_guidance":"Assert attempt count, delay policy, and terminal behavior.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x plus injectable sleep/clock for deterministic testing","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B012-R001","D35-B036-R002","D35-B044-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B012-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["unbounded_retry"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["bounded_retry"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE Book, handling overload and retries","AWS Builders Library, timeouts and retries"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B044-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resilience Implementation","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Explicit degraded-mode implementation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should an application implement degraded behavior when a non-critical dependency is unavailable?","answer":"Define an explicit degraded capability state and return a documented fallback or partial result only for operations where degraded behavior is semantically safe.","explanation":"Silent fallback can corrupt business semantics. Degraded behavior must be explicitly designed at the architecture boundary.","principle":"Fallback is a business and correctness decision, not merely an availability mechanism.","objective":"Preserve safe core behavior during non-critical dependency failure.","requirements":["Dependency criticality classification","Defined fallback semantics","Observable degraded state"],"constraints":["Never substitute stale or incomplete data without explicit semantics","Critical dependencies must fail closed when required"],"preconditions":["The dependency is classified as non-critical for the specific operation"],"input":"Primary operation with unavailable optional dependency.","output":"Primary result plus explicit degraded state or documented fallback.","expected_behavior":"Safe core operation continues only when architecture permits it.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    recommendation = recommender.get()\nexcept Exception:\n    recommendation = []\nreturn recommendation","corrected_code":"if recommender.available():\n    recommendation = recommender.get()\n    degraded = False\nelse:\n    recommendation = None\n    degraded = True\nreturn Result(recommendation=recommendation, degraded=degraded)","failure_mode":"Missing dependency data is silently represented as valid empty data.","expected_error":"Potential semantic corruption rather than a mandatory exception.","cause":"Fallback value has same representation as legitimate empty result.","root_cause":"Degraded state is not explicit.","solution":"Represent degraded behavior explicitly in the result contract.","edge_case":"Consumers incorrectly ignore the degraded marker.","security_implication":"Security-critical dependency failures should not be converted into permissive fallback behavior.","performance_implication":"Fallback can reduce dependency calls but may increase downstream reconciliation or user-visible latency.","test_case":"Disable the optional dependency and inspect the result.","test_input":"Dependency unavailable.","expected_test_result":"Result explicitly indicates degraded mode and does not misrepresent missing data as normal empty data.","testing_guidance":"Test both primary and degraded semantics.","verification_method":"Contract test for result state.","verification_guidance":"Verify downstream consumers handle degraded status intentionally.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B039-R003","D35-B025-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B039-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["degraded_mode"],"contrasts_with":["silent_fallback"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["graceful_degradation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE availability and graceful degradation principles","Resilient application architecture patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B045-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Implementation","subtopic":"Versioned Contract","concept":"API version boundary","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a versioned API boundary be implemented without duplicating the entire application?","answer":"Keep version-specific transport contracts and mapping at the API boundary while delegating shared business behavior to stable application contracts.","explanation":"API evolution often changes field names, representations, or semantics without requiring independent business implementations for every version.","principle":"Version transport contracts at the edge while preserving stable internal application semantics where compatible.","objective":"Support controlled API evolution.","requirements":["Explicit version contract","Version-specific mapping","Compatibility policy"],"constraints":["Do not hide semantic breaking changes behind superficial version labels","Old versions require an explicit support lifecycle"],"preconditions":["An API compatibility strategy exists"],"input":"Versioned external request.","output":"Version-specific response mapped from application result.","expected_behavior":"Each supported API version honors its documented contract.","incorrect_code":"def v1_handler(req):\n    return application.execute(req)\n\ndef v2_handler(req):\n    return application.execute(req)","corrected_code":"def v1_handler(req):\n    command = map_v1_request(req)\n    return map_v1_response(application.execute(command))\n\ndef v2_handler(req):\n    command = map_v2_request(req)\n    return map_v2_response(application.execute(command))","failure_mode":"Transport versions leak directly into application internals or silently diverge.","expected_error":"Contract incompatibility detected by API tests.","cause":"No explicit version mapping boundary.","root_cause":"API representation is treated as application contract.","solution":"Version