{"record_id":"D35-B045-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Implementation","subtopic":"Compatibility","concept":"Backward-compatible response evolution","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should an API response evolve while preserving existing clients?","answer":"Prefer additive compatible changes such as optional fields when the API contract permits them, and avoid changing the meaning, type, or requiredness of existing fields without an explicit compatibility strategy.","explanation":"Clients may deserialize and depend on existing fields. Compatibility must consider actual client behavior rather than only server-side code.","principle":"Existing contract semantics should remain stable unless a versioned change is intentional.","objective":"Reduce accidental client breakage.","requirements":["Compatibility definition","Schema contract tests","Explicit breaking-change policy"],"constraints":["Not every additive field is safe for every client ecosystem","Semantic changes can be breaking even when syntax is unchanged"],"preconditions":["Existing API clients consume the response"],"input":"Existing response contract and proposed change.","output":"Compatible or explicitly versioned contract.","expected_behavior":"Existing clients continue to interpret existing fields correctly.","incorrect_code":"return {'status': 1}  # previously documented as string 'active'","corrected_code":"return {'status': 'active', 'status_code': 1}","failure_mode":"Existing field meaning or type changes unexpectedly.","expected_error":"Client parsing or semantic failure.","cause":"Existing field changed instead of adding a compatible representation.","root_cause":"Compatibility treated as a syntax-only concern.","solution":"Preserve existing field semantics and introduce explicit new fields or versions.","edge_case":"Clients reject unknown fields despite a specification permitting them.","security_implication":"Compatibility changes must not weaken validation or authorization assumptions.","performance_implication":"Additional response fields increase payload size; measure impact for high-volume APIs.","test_case":"Run old response contract tests against the new response.","test_input":"Response containing original fields plus additive field.","expected_test_result":"Existing contract assertions continue to pass.","testing_guidance":"Maintain representative consumer contract tests.","verification_method":"Consumer-driven contract testing or schema compatibility testing.","verification_guidance":"Check type, requiredness, semantic meaning, and serialization behavior.","language":"JSON","language_version":"RFC-aligned JSON semantics","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"API contract dependent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Contract test tooling if automated validation is used","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_STANDARD","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B025-R001","D35-B045-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B025-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["breaking_response_change"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["backward_compatible_response"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["RFC 9110 HTTP semantics","API schema compatibility practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B045-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Implementation","subtopic":"Request Evolution","concept":"Backward-compatible request evolution","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should optional request capabilities be added without breaking existing clients?","answer":"Introduce optional fields with explicit defaults and validate combinations at the application boundary, while preserving the behavior of requests that omit the new capability.","explanation":"Adding a request field is only compatible when omission retains a defined previous behavior and validation does not accidentally reinterpret existing inputs.","principle":"Compatibility requires preserving the semantics of existing requests.","objective":"Add capabilities without accidental behavior changes.","requirements":["Explicit default","Validation rules","Existing-request regression tests"],"constraints":["Default behavior must be documented","A default cannot safely mask an ambiguous business meaning"],"preconditions":["Existing request contract has defined behavior"],"input":"Old or new request representation.","output":"Normalized application command.","expected_behavior":"Old requests map to the previous behavior and new requests activate only the intended capability.","incorrect_code":"enabled = request.get('new_mode', True)","corrected_code":"enabled = request.get('new_mode', False)","failure_mode":"Existing clients unexpectedly activate a new capability.","expected_error":"Behavioral regression rather than necessarily a runtime exception.","cause":"New option defaults to a changed behavior.","root_cause":"Compatibility default was not derived from existing semantics.","solution":"Choose a default preserving previous behavior and test omission explicitly.","edge_case":"The old behavior itself is unsafe or deprecated and must be replaced through a deliberate version change.","security_implication":"New security-sensitive options should default to the safer established behavior unless contract semantics require otherwise.","performance_implication":"New optional processing should not run for legacy requests unless explicitly enabled.","test_case":"Submit requests with and without the new field.","test_input":"Legacy request and request with new_mode=true.","expected_test_result":"Legacy request preserves prior behavior; explicit option activates new behavior.","testing_guidance":"Include regression tests for omitted fields.","verification_method":"Contract and regression tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify default mapping against the previous API contract.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B021-R001","D35-B045-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B021-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["unsafe_default_activation"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["compatible_request_evolution"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["API backward-compatibility design principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B045-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Implementation","subtopic":"Content Negotiation","concept":"Representation negotiation boundary","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Where should content representation negotiation be implemented?","answer":"Perform representation negotiation at the transport boundary and map the selected representation to the same application-level semantics.","explanation":"JSON, XML, or other representations are transport concerns. Application use cases should not need to know how their results are serialized for a particular client.","principle":"Representation format belongs to the delivery boundary.","objective":"Prevent serialization concerns from contaminating application logic.","requirements":["Supported media-type policy","Explicit serializer mapping","Consistent application result"],"constraints":["Unsupported representations must be rejected clearly","Security-sensitive representations require independent validation"],"preconditions":["The API supports more than one representation or explicit content negotiation"],"input":"Request headers and application result.","output":"Negotiated serialized representation.","expected_behavior":"Supported representation is selected according to the API contract.","incorrect_code":"def business_logic(accept_header):\n    if 'xml' in accept_header:\n        return xml_response()\n    return json_response()","corrected_code":"def handler(request, use_case):\n    result = use_case.execute(to_command(request))\n    serializer = choose_serializer(request.accept)\n    return serializer.serialize(result)","failure_mode":"Business logic depends on transport representation.","expected_error":"Unsupported representation should result in a boundary-level protocol error.","cause":"Serialization decision made inside application logic.","root_cause":"Transport boundary is not isolated.","solution":"Negotiate and serialize at the adapter boundary.","edge_case":"A representation changes available fields and therefore requires a distinct application query contract.","security_implication":"Content-type confusion and unsafe deserialization must be prevented at the boundary.","performance_implication":"Serialization costs vary by representation; measure payload size and encoding cost for actual workloads.","test_case":"Request supported and unsupported representations.","test_input":"Accept headers for supported and unsupported media types.","expected_test_result":"Supported type is selected; unsupported type receives controlled rejection.","testing_guidance":"Test negotiation independently from application behavior.","verification_method":"Adapter contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify application code does not branch on transport representation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"HTTP framework required for real endpoint execution","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_STANDARD","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B023-R002","D35-B024-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B024-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["transport_aware_business_logic"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["representation_negotiation_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["RFC 9110 content negotiation semantics"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B045-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Implementation","subtopic":"Deprecation","concept":"API deprecation boundary","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should API deprecation be represented in implementation?","answer":"Expose an explicit deprecation lifecycle through documentation, response metadata where applicable, monitoring, and a defined removal policy rather than silently removing behavior.","explanation":"Deprecation is an operational lifecycle requiring consumers to have a path to migration.","principle":"Removal should be preceded by observable and documented deprecation.","objective":"Make API lifecycle changes predictable for consumers.","requirements":["Deprecation policy","Migration guidance","Usage monitoring","Removal criteria"],"constraints":["Actual consumer population may be unknown","A deprecation period should match contractual and operational requirements"],"preconditions":["An API version or field is scheduled for retirement"],"input":"Deprecated API request.","output":"Valid response plus deprecation signal where defined by policy.","expected_behavior":"Consumers can identify deprecated behavior and migrate before removal.","incorrect_code":"def old_endpoint(request):\n    raise NotImplementedError","corrected_code":"def old_endpoint(request):\n    result = use_case.execute(to_command(request))\n    response = serialize(result)\n    response.headers['Deprecation'] = 'true'\n    return response","failure_mode":"Previously supported API disappears without migration path.","expected_error":"Client-facing failure after abrupt removal.","cause":"Deprecation and removal treated as one event.","root_cause":"No lifecycle boundary.","solution":"Implement an explicit deprecation stage and measure remaining usage before removal.","edge_case":"A security-critical vulnerability may require accelerated removal despite normal deprecation timelines.","security_implication":"S