{"record_id":"D35-B046-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Implementation","subtopic":"Async Coordination","concept":"Async task ownership","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should asynchronous tasks be coordinated so their lifecycle is not lost?","answer":"Create tasks within an explicit owner scope, retain handles when results or cancellation matter, and ensure shutdown awaits or cancels owned tasks according to policy.","explanation":"Fire-and-forget tasks can outlive request scopes, hide failures, and prevent deterministic shutdown.","principle":"Every asynchronous task requires an explicit lifecycle owner.","objective":"Prevent orphaned asynchronous work and hidden failures.","requirements":["Task ownership","Cancellation policy","Completion handling","Shutdown integration"],"constraints":["Do not retain completed task handles indefinitely","Cancellation must be propagated intentionally"],"preconditions":["Application uses asynchronous task execution"],"input":"Async operation request.","output":"Owned task and controlled lifecycle result.","expected_behavior":"Task completion or failure is observable by its owner.","incorrect_code":"asyncio.create_task(do_work())","corrected_code":"task = asyncio.create_task(do_work())\ntry:\n    await task\nfinally:\n    if not task.done():\n        task.cancel()","failure_mode":"Task failure or cancellation is unobserved.","expected_error":"Task exception may be reported separately or become effectively lost depending on runtime handling.","cause":"Task created without lifecycle owner.","root_cause":"Asynchronous execution detached from ownership.","solution":"Create tasks inside explicit lifecycle scopes or use a managed worker boundary.","edge_case":"Long-running background tasks intentionally outlive the request but must be owned by an application worker subsystem.","security_implication":"Orphaned tasks may continue privileged operations after their initiating context is gone.","performance_implication":"Awaiting or tracking tasks consumes bookkeeping resources but improves bounded lifecycle behavior.","test_case":"Create a task that fails and verify the owner observes the exception.","test_input":"Async task raising a controlled exception.","expected_test_result":"Owner receives or records the failure.","testing_guidance":"Test cancellation, timeout, owner shutdown, and task failure.","verification_method":"Async unit/integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify every created task has a documented owner.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"asyncio","framework_version":"Python standard library","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_STANDARD","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B036-R001","D35-B039-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["unowned_fire_and_forget"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["async_task_lifecycle"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python asyncio task documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B046-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Implementation","subtopic":"Cancellation","concept":"Cancellation-aware operation","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should long-running application operations respond to cancellation?","answer":"Propagate cancellation through awaited boundaries, release owned resources in finally blocks, and avoid converting cancellation into an ordinary success or generic failure.","explanation":"Cancellation is a control-flow signal used for shutdown, deadlines, and caller intent. Swallowing it can keep resources and work alive unexpectedly.","principle":"Cancellation must remain observable and resource-safe.","objective":"Ensure bounded lifecycle termination.","requirements":["Cancellation propagation","Resource cleanup","Explicit cancellation semantics"],"constraints":["Do not suppress cancellation accidentally","External operations may have limited cancellation support"],"preconditions":["Operation can be cancelled by its runtime or owner"],"input":"Running asynchronous operation plus cancellation signal.","output":"Cancelled operation with cleaned-up resources.","expected_behavior":"Operation terminates according to cancellation policy and releases owned resources.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    await long_operation()\nexcept Exception:\n    return None","corrected_code":"try:\n    await long_operation()\nfinally:\n    await release_resources()","failure_mode":"Cancellation is swallowed by broad exception handling.","expected_error":"Cancellation-specific control flow may be lost.","cause":"Generic exception handling around cancellable operation.","root_cause":"Cancellation is not modeled as distinct lifecycle behavior.","solution":"Propagate cancellation and perform cleanup in finally.","edge_case":"A cleanup operation itself can be cancelled and may require a bounded shielded cleanup policy.","security_implication":"Cancelled privileged operations must not leave partial authorization-sensitive resources active.","performance_implication":"Prompt cancellation releases resources sooner and can improve capacity under load.","test_case":"Cancel an operation while it is awaiting a controllable delay.","test_input":"Cancellation during await.","expected_test_result":"Cancellation propagates and cleanup executes.","testing_guidance":"Test cancellation at multiple operation stages.","verification_method":"Deterministic async cancellation test.","verification_guidance":"Verify cleanup executes and cancellation is not converted into successful output.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"asyncio","framework_version":"Python standard library","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_STANDARD","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B033-R004","D35-B046-R003","D35-B039-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B046-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["cancellation_aware_operation"],"contrasts_with":["swallowed_cancellation"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["cancellation_propagation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python asyncio cancellation documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B046-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Implementation","subtopic":"Lock Ordering","concept":"Deadlock-resistant lock ordering","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How can multiple locks be acquired without introducing circular wait?","answer":"Define a global lock acquisition order and require all code paths to acquire multiple locks according to that order, or redesign to reduce lock composition.","explanation":"Circular wait is a classic deadlock condition. Architecture can prevent it by establishing deterministic lock ordering.","principle":"Multiple synchronization resources require a consistent acquisition order.","objective":"Reduce deadlock risk in multi-lock operations.","requirements":["Defined ordering","Consistent enforcement","Lock scope documentation"],"constraints":["Ordering must remain stable as the system evolves","A single global order can be difficult in highly dynamic resource graphs"],"preconditions":["Operations require multiple locks"],"input":"Two or more synchronization resources.","output":"Consistent lock acquisition behavior.","expected_behavior":"All multi-lock paths follow the same ordering policy.","incorrect_code":"with lock_b:\n    with lock_a:\n        update()","corrected_code":"with lock_a:\n    with lock_b:\n        update()","failure_mode":"Two threads acquire locks in opposite orders.","expected_error":"Potential deadlock or timeout.","cause":"Inconsistent lock ordering.","root_cause":"No synchronization ordering rule.","solution":"Define and enforce a deterministic order.","edge_case":"Dynamic lock sets require sorting by a stable unique resource key.","security_implication":"Deadlocks can become availability vulnerabilities when attackers can trigger conflicting operations.","performance_implication":"Lock contention may dominate execution; ordering itself does not guarantee low contention.","test_case":"Create two concurrent operations tha