{"record_id":"D35-B047-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Serialization Boundary","subtopic":"Null Semantics","concept":"Explicit null and missing-field semantics","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why must serialization distinguish a missing field from an explicit null value when the domain requires the distinction?","answer":"Because missing can mean unchanged, unknown, or not supplied, while null can mean explicitly cleared or known empty. The serializer and application mapper must preserve the intended distinction.","explanation":"Collapsing missing and null values can change update semantics and produce unintended data changes.","principle":"Serialization must preserve semantically significant distinctions.","objective":"Prevent incorrect updates caused by lossy mapping.","requirements":["Defined missing/null semantics","Mapper preserving those semantics","Tests for both cases"],"constraints":["Only preserve the distinction when the domain contract requires it"],"preconditions":["Update or partial representation distinguishes omission from explicit clearing"],"input":"Serialized object with missing or null field.","output":"Application command preserving field state.","expected_behavior":"Missing and null produce distinct application semantics when required.","incorrect_code":"name = payload.get('name')","corrected_code":"name_present = 'name' in payload\nname = payload.get('name') if name_present else UNSET","failure_mode":"Omitted field is interpreted as explicit null.","expected_error":"Incorrect update behavior.","cause":"Dictionary lookup collapses absence and null.","root_cause":"Serialization semantics not modeled in application contract.","solution":"Use an explicit sentinel or equivalent optional-state representation.","edge_case":"Nested objects have independent missing/null semantics.","security_implication":"Incorrect field interpretation can accidentally clear security-sensitive configuratio