{"record_id":"D35-B047-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Serialization Boundary","subtopic":"Deserialization Safety","concept":"Safe deserialization boundary","knowledge_type":"security_implementation","question":"How should untrusted serialized data be deserialized?","answer":"Use data-only formats and constrained parsers for untrusted input, validate the resulting structure, and avoid deserializers capable of executing arbitrary code.","explanation":"Some serialization mechanisms can reconstruct executable objects or invoke code during deserialization. External input must therefore be treated as untrusted data.","principle":"Deserialization must not grant execution authority to untrusted input.","objective":"Prevent code execution and unsafe object construction through serialized data.","requirements":["Data-only parser","Schema validation","Input size limits","Explicit allowed types"],"constraints":["Do not deserialize arbitrary object graphs from untrusted sources","Legacy formats may require migration or isolated handling"],"preconditions":["Input originates outside the trusted execution boundary"],"input":"Untrusted serialized payload.","output":"Validated plain data structure or controlled rejection.","expected_behavior":"Only permitted data types and structures are accepted.","incorrect_code":"import pickle\nobj = pickle.loads(untrusted_bytes)","corrected_code":"import json\nobj = json.loads(untrusted_text)\nvalidate_schema(obj)","failure_mode":"Untrusted input can trigger unsafe object reconstruction.","expected_error":"Security compromise or controlled validation error depending on implementation.","cause":"Executable-capable deserialization used on untrusted data.","root_cause":"Serialization format treated as inert data despite executable semantics.","solution":"Use constrained data-only serialization and validation.","edge_case":"Even JSON can carry maliciously large or deeply nested structures and requires