{"record_id":"D35-B048-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Lifecycle Implementation","subtopic":"Context Management","concept":"Deterministic resource cleanup","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should resources such as files, locks, and connections be released deterministically?","answer":"Use explicit lifecycle ownership and context-management constructs that guarantee cleanup when control leaves the managed scope.","explanation":"Garbage collection is not a substitute for deterministic release of scarce or externally visible resources.","principle":"Resource lifetime should be explicit and bounded by ownership scope.","objective":"Prevent resource leaks.","requirements":["Resource owner","Acquire/release contract","Exception-safe cleanup"],"constraints":["Cleanup must execute on normal and exceptional paths","Nested ownership must be explicit"],"preconditions":["Resource requires explicit release"],"input":"Resource acquisition operation.","output":"Managed resource lifecycle.","expected_behavior":"Resource is released after the owning scope exits.","incorrect_code":"file = open(path)\ndata = file.read()\nreturn data","corrected_code":"with open(path, 'rb') as file:\n    data = file.read()\nreturn data","failure_mode":"Resource remains open after operation.","expected_error":"Resource exhaustion or platform-specific file-lock behavior.","cause":"Manual cleanup omitted on some paths.","root_cause":"Resource lifetime not represented structurally.","solution":"Use context management or equivalent deterministic lifecycle mechanism.","edge_case":"A resource must intentionally outlive the current scope and therefore needs a higher-level owner.","security_implication":"Unreleased handles can retain access to protected resources longer than intended.","performance_implication":"Deterministic cleanup releases scarce resources earlier and can improve capacity.","test_case":"Raise an exception inside the managed scope and verify cleanup occurs.","test_input":"Resource operation followed by deliberate exception.","expected_test_result":"Resource release executes despite the exception.","testing_guidance":"Test normal, exceptional, and cancellation paths.","verification_method":"Lifecycle unit test and resource-state inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify cleanup is structurally guaranteed.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"contextlib","framework_version":"Python standard library","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_STANDARD","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B034-R004","D35-B046-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B034-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["manual_cleanup"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["deterministic_resource_lifecycle"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python context manager documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B048-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Lifecycle Implementation","subtopic":"Connection Pooling","concept":"Connection ownership and pool lifecycle","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should pooled connections be acquired and returned safely?","answer":"Treat the pool as an owned infrastructure resource, acquire connections within a bounded scope, and guarantee release back to the pool in success and failure paths.","explanation":"Connection pools improve reuse but create a finite shared resource. Leaking a pooled connection eventually exhausts the pool.","principle":"Pooled resources must have deterministic checkout and return semantics.","objective":"Prevent connection-pool exhaustion.","requirements":["Pool ownership","Bounded checkout scope","Guaranteed return","Shutdown lifecycle"],"constraints":["Pool size must be validated for workload","A returned connection must not be used afterward"],"preconditions":["A connection pool is used"],"input":"Database or service operation.","output":"Operation result with connection returned to pool.","expected_behavior":"Every successful or failed checkout returns the resource unless the pool explicitly discards it.","incorrect_code":"conn = pool.acquire()\nreturn conn.execute(query)","corrected_code":"conn = pool.acquire()\ntry:\n    return conn.execute(query)\nfinally:\n    pool.release(conn)","failure_mode":"Connection remains checked out.","expected_error":"Pool exhaustion or timeout under sustained load.","cause":"Missing release on return path.","root_cause":"Pool resource lifecycle is implicit.","solution":"Wrap checkout in deterministic release semantics.","edge_case":"Connection is broken and must be invalidated rather than returned to the healthy pool.","security_implication":"Leaked sessions may retain credentials or transaction context longer than intended.","performance_implication":"Pool size and wait behavior directly affect throughput and latency; tune from measurements.","test_case":"Force an operation exception and verify connection release.","test_input":"Query raising an exception.","expected_test_result":"Connection is released or invalidated according to pool policy.","testing_guidance":"Test success, failure, cancellation, and pool exhaustion.","verification_method":"Integration test with instrumented pool.","verification_guidance":"Track checkout count against release count.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Pool implementation dependent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Connection-pool implementation required","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B006-R001","D35-B037-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B037-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["pooled_resource_lifecycle"],"contrasts_with":["leaked_pool_connection"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["connection_pool_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Database connection pooling engineering practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B048-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Lifecycle Implementation","subtopic":"Shutdown","concept":"Graceful resource shutdown","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should application-owned resources be closed during shutdown?","answer":"Register a shutdown lifecycle that stops accepting new work, drains or cancels owned work according to policy, and closes resources in dependency order.","explanation":"Closing resources in arbitrary order can create new failures during shutdown. Lifecycle dependencies should be explicit.","principle":"Shutdown is an architectural lifecycle phase with ordering requirements.","objective":"Prevent resource corruption and incomplete shutdown.","requirements":["Stop-new-work phase","Drain/cancel policy","Resource close ordering","Shutdown timeout"],"constraints":["Shutdown cannot wait indefinitely","Critical cleanup may require escalation after a bounded deadline"],"preconditions":["Application owns long-lived resources"],"input":"Shutdown signal.","output":"Closed resources and terminated workers.","expected_behavior":"New work is rejected or stopped, owned work follows policy, and resources close in correct order.","incorrect_code":"database.close()\nworker.start()","corrected_code":"stop_accepting_work()\nawait worker.stop()\nawait database.close()","failure_mode":"Dependencies close before workers finish using them.","expected_error":"Shutdown-time operation failures.","cause":"No lifecycle dependency ordering.","root_cause":"Shutdown treated as process termination rather than an explicit phase.","solution":"Implement ordered graceful shutdown.","edge_case":"Worker fails to stop before deadline and requires cancellation or forced termination.","security_implication":"Shutdown should revoke or close privileged resources before process exit where required.","performance_implication":"Graceful drain increases shutdown duration but reduces partial work; timeout policy must reflect operational requirements.","test_case":"Trigger shutdown while work is active.","test_input":"Active worker and open database resource.","expected_test_result":"New work stops, active work follows policy, then database closes.","testing_guidance":"Test normal shutdown, timeout, worker failure, and repeated shutdown calls.","verification_method":"Lifecycle integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify close ordering and bounded shutdown duration.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"asyncio","framework_version":"Python standard library","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B036-R005","D35-B039-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B039-R005"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["graceful_shutdown"],"contrasts_with":["abrupt_shutdown"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["resource_shutdown_lifecycle"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python asyncio shutdown concepts","Kubernetes application termination lifecycle concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B048-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Lifecycle Implementation","subtopic":"Temporary Resources","concept":"Temporary resource cleanup","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should temporary files and directories be managed?","answer":"Create temporary resources through dedicated temporary-resource APIs and ensure cleanup occurs through a deterministic owner scope.","explanation":"Temporary resources can accumulate after crashes or exceptional paths if lifecycle ownership is unclear.","principle":"Temporary resources require explicit creation and cleanup semantics.","objective":"Prevent persistent temporary-resource leakage.","requirements":["Safe temporary creation","Unique resource naming","Cleanup policy"],"constraints":["Do not construct predictable temporary filenames for security-sensitive data","Crash cleanup may require separate operational cleanup mechanisms"],"preconditions":["Operation requires temporary filesystem state"],"input":"Temporary resource request.","output":"Owned temporary path/resource.","expected_behavior":"Resource is isolated during use and removed when ownership ends.","incorrect_code":"path = '/tmp/app-work'\nopen(path, 'w').write(data)","corrected_code":"from tempfile import NamedTemporaryFile\n\nwith NamedTemporaryFile(mode='w+', delete=True) as file:\n    file.write(data)\n    file.flush()","failure_mode":"Temporary data collides, leaks, or is exposed to another process.","expected_error":"File collision, permission failure, or residual sensitive data.","cause":"Predictable path and unmanaged lifecycle.","root_cause":"Temporary resource boundary not implemented using a lifecycle-safe abstraction.","solution":"Use secure temporary-resource APIs and deterministic cleanup.","edge_case":"A subprocess requires the temporary path after the creating scope exits.","security_implication":"Predictable temporary paths can enable symlink or race attacks; sensitive temporary data also requires appropriate permissions.","performance_implication":"Temporary storage performance varies by filesystem and should be measured for large data flows.","test_case":"Create a temporary resource, write data, exit scope, and verify cleanup.","test_input":"Temporary text payload.","expected_test_result":"Resource is removed according to the API's documented cleanup semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test cleanup after success and exception.","verification_method":"Filesystem integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify permissions and path isolation in the target environment.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"tempfile","framework_version":"Python standard library","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_STANDARD","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B034-R001","D35-B034-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B034-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":["temporary_resource_boundary"],"contrasts_with":["predictable_temp_path"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["temporary_resource_management"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python tempfile documentation","OWASP secure temporary file handling principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B048-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Lifecycle Implementation","subtopic":"Resource Ownership","concept":"Single-owner resource model","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"Why should every long-lived resource have a clearly defined owner?","answer":"A single architectural owner should control initialization, health, usage policy, and shutdown of each long-lived resource unless shared ownership is explicitly designed.","explanation":"Unclear ownership leads to duplicate initialization, conflicting shutdown behavior, and hidden dependencies.","principle":"Lifecycle ownership should be singular and explicit.","objective":"Make resource lifecycle deterministic.","requirements":["Named owner","Initialization policy","Usage contract","Shutdown policy"],"constraints":["Shared resources still need one lifecycle owner","Ownership does not necessarily mean exclusive access"],"preconditions":["The application contains a long-lived resource"],"input":"Resource definition and component graph.","output":"Explicit lifecycle ownership mapping.","expected_behavior":"Only the designated owner starts and closes the resource.","incorrect_code":"service_a = Database()\nservice_b = Database()","corrected_code":"database = Database()\nservice_a = Service(database)\nservice_b = Service(database)\n","failure_mode":"Multiple components independently create and close the same logical resource.","expected_error":"Connection duplication, conflicting lifecycle, or resource exhaustion.","cause":"Lifecycle responsibility distributed implicitly.","root_cause":"No composition-root ownership model.","solution":"Create the resource once and inject it into consumers.","edge_case":"Tests require isolated resources and therefore intentionally create independent instances.","security_implication":"Duplicate resources can bypass centralized credential or policy configuration.","performance_implication":"Shared