{"record_id":"D35-B049-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture-to-Implementation Testing","subtopic":"Contract Testing","concept":"Executable interface contract","knowledge_type":"testing_implementation","question":"How can an architectural interface contract be made executable?","answer":"Define contract tests against the interface behavior and execute them against each implementation, including fakes or adapters where appropriate.","explanation":"An interface is stronger when its behavioral expectations are executable rather than only documented.","principle":"Important interface contracts should have reusable behavioral tests.","objective":"Prevent implementations from silently diverging from architecture contracts.","requirements":["Explicit interface behavior","Reusable contract test suite","Implementation fixtures"],"constraints":["Contract tests should assert behavior rather than implementation details","Not every internal helper requires a contract test"],"preconditions":["An interface or port has multiple implementations or significant behavioral requirements"],"input":"Implementation of an architectural contract.","output":"Pass/fail contract test result.","expected_behavior":"Every implementation satisfies required contract semantics.","incorrect_code":"class Repo:\n    pass","corrected_code":"class UserRepository:\n    def get(self, user_id):\n        raise NotImplementedError\n\n\ndef assert_repository_contract(repo):\n    assert repo.get('missing') is None","failure_mode":"Implementation claims to satisfy interface but violates expected behavior.","expected_error":"AssertionError or contract-specific test failure.","cause":"Interface checks structure but not behavior.","root_cause":"Behavioral contract is undocumented or untested.","solution":"Create reusable contract tests.","edge_case":"Implementations intentionally differ in optional capabilities; separate core contract from capability-specific c