{"record_id":"D35-B049-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture-to-Implementation Testing","subtopic":"Invariant Testing","concept":"Executable domain invariant","knowledge_type":"testing_implementation","question":"How should important domain invariants be enforced at runtime and in tests?","answer":"Encode the invariant in the domain model where appropriate and create tests that attempt both valid and invalid transitions.","explanation":"A domain invariant should not depend solely on caller discipline. The implementation should reject states that violate the domain contract.","principle":"Critical invariants belong as close as practical to the state they protect.","objective":"Prevent invalid domain states.","requirements":["Explicit invariant","Enforcement point","Positive and negative tests"],"constraints":["Not every business rule is a domain invariant","Cross-aggregate invariants may require application-level coordination"],"preconditions":["The invariant can be expressed at the chosen boundary"],"input":"Domain operation and current state.","output":"Valid transition or controlled rejection.","expected_behavior":"Invalid state transitions are rejected consistently.","incorrect_code":"class Account:\n    balance = 0\n\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        self.balance -= amount","corrected_code":"class Account:\n    def init(self, balance=0):\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        if amount < 0 or amount > self.balance:\n            raise ValueError('invalid withdrawal')\n        self.balance -= amount","failure_mode":"Domain state becomes invalid.","expected_error":"ValueError for invalid withdrawal in the illustrative implementation.","cause":"State mutation lacks invariant enforcement.","root_cause":"Invariant exists only as an implicit assumption.","solution":"Enforce the invariant at the domain operation boundary.","edge_case":"Currency precision and concurrent updates require additional design beyond this simple example.","security_implication":"Financial or authorization invariants must be enforced server-side and not trusted to callers.","performance_implication":"Invariant checks are usually small but may become expensive when they require external queries; those should be modeled separately.","test_case":"Attempt withdrawal greater than balance.","test_input":"balance=100, amount=101.","expected_test_result":"Operation is rejected and balance remains unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Test boundary values, invalid values, and state preservation after rejection.","verification_method":"Domain unit test.","verification_guidance":"Verify failed operations do not partially mutate state.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"none","framework_version":"none","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B005-R001","D35-B014-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B005-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B005-R001"],"specializes":["domain_invariant"],"contrasts_with":["caller_only_validation"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["runtime_domain_invariant"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Domain-driven design invariant principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B049-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture-to-Implementation Testing","subtopic":"Property Testing","concept":"Property-based implementation validation","knowledge_type":"testing_implementation","question":"When is property-based testing useful for architecture-level implementation behavior?","answer":"Use property-based testing when behavior can be expressed as general invariants over many generated inputs rather than a small fixed set of examples.","explanation":"Properties can reveal edge cases that example-based tests omit, particularly for parsers, serializers, state transitions, normalization, and collection algorithms.","principle":"Test general invariants where the input space is broader than practical hand-written examples.","objective":"Discover implementation failures across diverse valid and invalid inputs.","requirements":["Explicit property","Input generation strategy","Failure shrinking or reproducible cases where tooling supports it"],"constraints":["Properties must be semantically correct","Generated inputs require resource limits"],"preconditions":["Behavior has meaningful general invariants"],"input":"Generated input satisfying defined assumptions.","output":"Property pass/fail result.","expected_behavior":"All generated inputs satisfy the declared invariant within configured test bounds.","incorrect_code":"def normalize(values):\n    return values.sort()","corrected_code":"def normalize(values):\n    return sorted(values)","failure_mode":"Function returns None because in-place sort returns no list.","expected_error":"Assertion or downstream type failure.","cause":"Incorrect assumption about list.sort return semantics.","root_cause":"Implementation contract was not tested against a general property.","solution":"Return a new sorted sequence and test properties such as ordering and element preservation.","edge_case":"Inputs containing incomparable elements require an explicit