{"record_id":"D35-B049-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture-to-Implementation Testing","subtopic":"Failure Injection","concept":"Controlled dependency failure injection","knowledge_type":"testing_implementation","question":"How can resilience behavior be verified without waiting for real outages?","answer":"Inject deterministic dependency failures through test doubles, fault-injection hooks, or controlled test environments and verify the architecture's defined recovery behavior.","explanation":"Resilience paths are often difficult to validate through normal success-path testing. Controlled failure injection makes them executable and repeatable.","principle":"Important failure paths require deterministic tests.","objective":"Verify timeout, retry, fallback, compensation, and circuit-breaker behavior.","requirements":["Injectable dependency","Deterministic failure mode","Expected recovery assertion"],"constraints":["Test injection mechanisms must not be enabled accidentally in production","Failure models should reflect plausible dependency failures"],"preconditions":["Dependency is represented by an interface or controllable boundary"],"input":"Configured simulated failure.","output":"Observed resilience behavior.","expected_behavior":"Application follows the documented failure policy.","incorrect_code":"def process(client):\n    return client.call()\n# No way to inject a failure deterministically","corrected_code":"def process(client):\n    return client.call()\n\nclass FailingClient:\n    def call(self):\n        raise TimeoutError()","failure_mode":"Resilience behavior cannot be tested deterministically.","expected_error":"Test cannot reliably exercise the intended failure path.","cause":"Concrete dependency is hard-coded.","root_cause":"Dependency boundary is not injectable.","solution":"Depend on an interface and provide controlled failing implementations in tests.","edge_case":"Injected failure occurs afte