{"record_id":"D35-B049-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture-to-Implementation Testing","subtopic":"Architecture Regression","concept":"Architecture regression test","knowledge_type":"architecture_enforcement","question":"How should architecture regressions be detected after implementation changes?","answer":"Encode critical architecture rules as automated tests and run them as part of the normal validation pipeline.","explanation":"Architectural drift is cumulative. A regression test converts a previously identified architectural requirement into a repeatable guard.","principle":"Architecture rules should become executable regression constraints.","objective":"Prevent previously fixed architecture violations from returning.","requirements":["Stable rule definition","Automated test","CI integration","Actionable failure message"],"constraints":["Rules should target meaningful architectural boundaries","Avoid brittle tests tied to incidental file names"],"preconditions":["Architecture requirements have been identified"],"input":"Current source dependency graph.","output":"Architecture compliance result.","expected_behavior":"A newly introduced prohibited dependency causes validation failure.","incorrect_code":"# module_a.py\nfrom forbidden.infrastructure import InternalClient","corrected_code":"# module_a.py\nfrom application.ports import ClientPort","failure_mode":"Previously prohibited dependency reappears unnoticed.","expected_error":"Architecture regression test failure.","cause":"Architecture rule exists only in documentation or code review memory.","root_cause":"No automated regression guard.","solution":"Add the dependency rule to the architecture test suite.","edge_case":"Intentional architectural changes require updating the rule and its evidence together.","security_implication":"Regression tests can protect security boundaries such as domain-to-infrastructure isolation.","performance_implication":"Stat