{"record_id":"D35-B051-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Command Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Handler exception boundary","concept":"A command bus should preserve domain/application failures while translating unexpected infrastructure failures at an explicit boundary.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Should a command bus catch every exception?","answer":"No. Catch only exceptions that the boundary can classify, translate, audit, or otherwise handle correctly. Unexpected exceptions should remain visible to the owning error boundary.","explanation":"Catching every exception inside dispatch can hide programming defects and destroy diagnostic information.","principle":"Error handling should occur at the boundary that understands the error semantics.","objective":"Prevent command infrastructure from masking application defects.","requirements":["Typed expected failures","Explicit translation boundary","Preservation of diagnostic context"],"constraints":["No blanket exception suppression","No conversion of every failure into success"],"preconditions":["Application error taxonomy exists"],"input":"Handler execution and possible exception.","output":"Expected domain/application failure or propagated unexpected failure.","expected_behavior":"Known failures retain their semantics while unexpected failures remain observable.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    return handler.handle(command)\nexcept Exception:\n    return None","corrected_code":"try:\n    return handler.handle(command)\nexcept ExpectedApplicationError:\n    raise\nexcept Exception:\n    raise","failure_mode":"Unexpected defects are silently converted into empty results.","expected_error":"Unexpected exception remains propagated to the outer error boundary.","cause":"Broad exception catch with no classification.","root_cause":"Infrastructure boundary assumes responsibility for errors it cannot semantically interpret.","solution":"Use narrow exception handling and delegate unknown failures upward.","edge_case":"Cleanup may still be required even when an exception propagates; use finally for resource cleanup without suppressing the error.","security_implication":"Do not leak internal exception details through external response boundaries.","performance_implication":"Exception paths should remain exceptional; normal control flow should not depend on exceptions.","test_case":"Make a handler raise a known application error and an unexpected programming error.","test_input":"Two handler implementations producing different exception classes.","expected_test_result":"Known error follows defined application semantics; unexpected error propagates.","testing_guidance":"Test both classification branches and verify stack/context preservation.","verification_method":"Unit tests and error-boundary integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify no unexpected exception is swallowed.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B020-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B019-R001"],"requires":["D35-B020-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B019-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Command error boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Exception boundary and error taxonomy patterns"],"provenance":["Established error-handling pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"INVALID IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B051-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Command Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Dispatch observability","concept":"Command dispatch should expose enough structured metadata to diagnose routing and execution without recording sensitive payloads indiscriminately.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should command dispatch record for observability?","answer":"Record command type, outcome classification, duration, correlation identifier, and safe execution metadata while applying the system's telemetry redaction policy.","explanation":"The dispatch layer is a useful diagnostic boundary, but logging complete command payloads can create unnecessary security and privacy exposure.","principle":"Observability should expose execution behavior without duplicating sensitive business data.","objective":"Make command routing diagnosable while minimizing telemetry risk.","requirements":["Structured event fields","Duration measurement","Outcome classification","Redaction policy"],"constraints":["No unrestricted payload logging","No secret or credential capture"],"preconditions":["Structured logging/telemetry boundary exists"],"input":"Command execution metadata.","output":"Safe structured dispatch telemetry.","expected_behavior":"Successful and failed dispatches produce diagnosable but appropriately redacted telemetry.","incorrect_code":"logger.info("command=%r", command)","corrected_code":"logger.info("command_dispatch", extra={"command_type": type(command).name, "outcome": outcome, "correlation_id": context.correlation_id})","failure_mode":"Sensitive command contents are copied into logs.","expected_error":"Telemetry policy violation detected by review or automated redaction tests.","cause":"Whole-object logging.","root_cause":"Observability implementation does not separate operational metadata from business payload.","solution":"Emit allow-listed metadata and test redaction.","edge_case":"A command may contain identifiers that are individually harmless but sensitive when combined.","security_implication":"Telemetry becomes a secondary data store and must follow data-minimization and access-control requirements.","performance_implication":"Structured metadata collection should avoid expensive serialization of complete command objects.","test_case":"Dispatch a command containing a secret-like field and inspect emitted telemetry.","test_input":"Command with token/password-like values.","expected_test_result":"Sensitive values are absent or appropriately redacted.","testing_guidance":"Use automated telemetry assertions for secret, credential, and high-sensitivity field leakage.","verification_method":"Instrumentation tests and static review.","verification_guidance":"Verify allow-listed fields rather than relying only on blacklist redaction.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library logging"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B030-R001","D35-B041-R005","D35-B043-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B030-R001"],"requires":["D35-B041-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Safe command telemetry"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Structured logging and telemetry data-minimization practices"],"provenance":["OWASP Logging guidance; established observability patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B052-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Query dispatch","concept":"A query bus can provide a read-side dispatch boundary while preserving the distinction between queries and state-changing commands.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a query bus differ from a command bus?","answer":"A query bus dispatches read-oriented requests to query handlers and should make the read-only intent explicit; it should not silently perform state changes.","explanation":"Separating query dispatch makes read dependencies easier to reason about and enables different optimization and authorization policies without mixing command semantics.","principle":"Read intent should remain explicit at the application boundary.","objective":"Implement predictable query dispatch without conflating reads and writes.","requirements":["Query contract","Deterministic handler mapping","Explicit result contract"],"constraints":["No hidden writes","No implicit command execution"],"preconditions":["Query handlers are registered"],"input":"Query object and context.","output":"Query result.","expected_behavior":"A query invokes only its designated read handler and returns its defined result.","incorrect_code":"def get_customer(query):\n    customer = repository.get(query.id)\n    customer.last_accessed = now()\n    repository.save(customer)\n    return customer","corrected_code":"def get_customer(query, repository):\n    return repository.get(query.id)","failure_mode":"Read path unexpectedly mutates state.","expected_error":"Architecture or test invariant failure for query-side mutation.","cause":"Query handler performs write operations.","root_cause":"Read/write responsibility is not enforced at the application boundary.","solution":"Keep query handlers read-oriented and place required state changes in explicit commands.","edge_case":"Auditing access can itself be a write; model it as a separate explicit concern rather than hiding it inside query semantics.","security_implication":"Read authorization must be evaluated independently of command authorization.","performance_implication":"Read handlers can often use optimized projections without changing domain write models.","test_case":"Execute a query against a repository spy and assert no write operation occurs.","test_input":"Query requesting one entity.","expected_test_result":"Only read operations are invoked.","testing_guidance":"Use mocks/spies to detect unexpected persistence writes.","verification_method":"Unit tests plus architectural review.","verification_guidance":"Verify query handlers cannot silently mutate persistent state.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.95,"related_records":["D35-B004-R001","D35-B006-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B051-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["Command dispatch semantics"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Query dispatch boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["CQRS and application-boundary patterns"],"provenance":["Established CQRS/application architecture pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B052-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Query result contracts","concept":"Query handlers should return explicit result types rather than loosely structured dictionaries whose semantics are implicit.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should query results have explicit contracts?","answer":"Explicit result contracts make field meaning, optionality, versioning, and test expectations machine-checkable.","explanation":"Loose dictionaries can accumulate undocumented fields and inconsistent null semantics across handlers.","principle":"Application boundaries should communicate stable data contracts.","objective":"Make query output semantics explicit and verifiable.","requirements":["Typed result contract","Defined optionality","Stable field semantics"],"constraints":["No undocumented output fields","No ambiguous null/error conventions"],"preconditions":["Consumer requirements are defined"],"input":"Query handler data.","output":"Typed query result.","expected_behavior":"Consumers receive a documented and structurally validated result.","incorrect_code":"return {"customer": customer, "data": None}","corrected_code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass CustomerView:\n    customer_id: str\n    display_name: str\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass CustomerQueryResult:\n    customer: CustomerView | None","failure_mode":"Consumers interpret loosely structured output differently.","expected_error":"Schema or type validation failure.","cause":"Implicit result shape.","root_cause":"The query boundary lacks a formal output contract.","solution":"Define typed result objects and validate external serialization separately.","edge_case":"An empty result must be distinguishable from an execution failure.","security_implication":"Result contracts should expose only fields authorized for the requesting context.","performance_implication":"Projection-specific result types can reduce unnecessary data retrieval and serialization.","test_case":"Validate a populated and empty query result.","test_input":"Existing and nonexistent customer identifiers.","expected_test_result":"Both results conform to the same contract and preserve explicit empty semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test required fields, optional fields, null semantics, and unauthorized field exclusion.","verification_method":"Schema/type tests and integration serialization tests.","verification_guidance":"Compare actual serialized output against the declared contract.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library dataclasses"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B023-R001","D35-B025-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B023-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B023-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Query result contract"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Typed boundary and DTO patterns"],"provenance":["Established typed application-contract pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B052-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Query handler composition","concept":"Query handlers should receive dependencies through explicit composition rather than creating repositories or services internally.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should dependencies enter query handlers?","answer":"Inject query dependencies through constructors or explicit handler parameters and assemble them in the composition root.","explanation":"Internal dependency creation prevents substitution in tests and couples the application layer to concrete infrastructure.","principle":"Dependency direction should remain visible in executable code.","objective":"Preserve testability and architectural dependency direction in query handlers.","requirements":["Dependency injection","Composition-root assembly","Stable handler interface"],"constraints":["No hidden infrastructure construction in handlers"],"preconditions":["Dependencies have interfaces or stable contracts"],"input":"Configured handler dependencies.","output":"Composable query handler.","expected_behavior":"Handler execution uses only injected dependencies.","incorrect_code":"class UserQueryHandler:\n    def handle(self, query):\n        repo = SqlUserRepository("db.sqlite")\n        return repo.find(query.user_id)","corrected_code":"class UserQueryHandler:\n    def init(self, repository):\n        self.repository = repository\n\n    def handle(self, query):\n        return self.repository.find(query.user_id)","failure_mode":"Query handler cannot be isolated from infrastructure in tests.","expected_error":"Test requires real infrastructure unexpectedly.","cause":"Concrete repository construction inside handler.","root_cause":"Composition responsibility leaked into application logic.","solution":"Inject repository abstraction and construct concrete adapter at the composition root.","edge_case":"Very small pure dependencies may be passed as values rather than interfaces; the architectural concern is hidden construction, not abstraction for its own sake.","security_implication":"Centralized composition makes security-sensitive adapters easier to audit and replace.","performance_implication":"Dependency injection avoids repeated resource construction during requests.","test_case":"Inject an in-memory fake repository and execute the handler.","test_input":"Fake repository containing a known entity.","expected_test_result":"Handler returns expected result without database access.","testing_guidance":"Test with fake, failing, and latency-injecting dependencies.","verification_method":"Unit tests and dependency graph inspection.","verification_guidance":"Confirm infrastructure modules are not instantiated from query-handler code.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B003-R001","D35-B008-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B008-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Dependency injection in query handlers"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Dependency inversion and composition-root patterns"],"provenance":["Established dependency-injection architecture pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B052-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Query authorization","concept":"A query boundary must enforce authorization before returning protected data and must not assume that successful lookup implies permission.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Where should authorization be enforced for query handlers?","answer":"Authorization should be evaluated at an explicit application boundary using authoritative actor, tenant, resource, and action context before protected data is returned.","explanation":"A repository lookup only establishes data existence or retrieval; it does not establish that the caller may access the data.","principle":"Data retrieval and authorization are separate concerns.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized data disclosure through read paths.","requirements":["Authoritative identity context","Resource authorization decision","Tenant isolation where applicable"],"constraints":["Never trust client-provided authorization claims without validation","Do not use object existence as authorization"],"preconditions":["Authentication context is established"],"input":"Query plus trusted execution context.","output":"Authorized result or authorization failure.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized requests receive no protected result.","incorrect_code":"def handle(query):\n    return repository.get(query.user_id)","corrected_code":"def handle(query, context, authorizer):\n    resource = repository.get(query.user_id)\n    authorizer.require(context, "user.read", resource)\n    return resource","failure_mode":"An authenticated but unauthorized actor receives protected data.","expected_error":"AuthorizationError.","cause":"Read handler assumes authentication or ownership without checking authorization.","root_cause":"Authorization was omitted from the query application boundary.","solution":"Require an explicit authorization decision before result exposure.","edge_case":"Resource may not exist; systems must define whether nonexistent and unauthorized cases are intentionally indistinguishable.","security_implication":"This boundary directly protects confidentiality and tenant isolation.","performance_implication":"Authorization checks should avoid unnecessary duplicate data retrieval while retaining correct policy evaluation.","test_case":"Query a resource as an authorized and unauthorized actor.","test_input":"Same resource, two different execution contexts.","expected_test_result":"Authorized actor receives permitted result; unauthorized actor receives authorization failure or defined indistinguishable response.","testing_guidance":"Test cross-tenant access and privilege-boundary cases.","verification_method":"Security unit tests and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify protected data is absent from response, logs, and exceptions on denial.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B026-R001","D35-B027-R001","D35-B028-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B027-R001"],"requires":["D35-B026-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Query authorization boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["OWASP authorization guidance; access-control architecture patterns"],"provenance":["OWASP authorization guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B052-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Query caching separation","concept":"Query caching should remain outside query business semantics so cached and uncached execution produce equivalent authorized results.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should caching interact with a query handler?","answer":"Place caching in an explicit decorator or infrastructure boundary with keys that include all authorization-relevant context and invalidation semantics appropriate to the data.","explanation":"Embedding cache state inside business query logic makes correctness and invalidation difficult to reason about.","principle":"Cross-cutting performance behavior should not silently redefine application semantics.","objective":"Add query caching without changing authorization or result correctness.","requirements":["Explicit cache boundary","Correct cache key","Defined TTL/invalidation","Authorization-aware scope"],"constraints":["Do not cache across incompatible tenants or authorization contexts","Do not treat cache as authoritative source unless explicitly designed"],"preconditions":["Cache consistency requirements are defined"],"input":"Query and authorized execution context.","output":"Equivalent query result from cache or source.","expected_behavior":"Cache hits return only data valid for the requesting context and freshness contract.","incorrect_code":"cache[query.id] = repository.get(query.id)","corrected_code":"key = (context.tenant_id, context.permission_scope, query.id)\nif key in cache:\n    return cache[key]\nresult = repository.get(query.id)\ncache[key] = result\nreturn result","failure_mode":"A cached result crosses tenant or authorization boundaries.","expected_error":"Security test failure or authorization violation.","cause":"Cache key excludes security scope.","root_cause":"Cache is treated as a generic optimization rather than a data-access boundary.","solution":"Define cache identity from all relevant scope and freshness dimensions.","edge_case":"Permission changes can invalidate previously cached results even when underlying data is unchanged.","security_implication":"Incorrect cache scoping can cause cross-tenant or cross-user disclosure.","performance_implication":"Caching can reduce source load but introduces memory use, invalidation cost, and stale-data risk.","test_case":"Populate cache under one tenant and query the same resource under another tenant.","test_input":"Identical resource identifier with different tenant contexts.","expected_test_result":"Second tenant cannot receive the first tenant's cached data.","testing_guidance":"Test key isolation, expiration, invalidation, cache misses, and source failures.","verification_method":"Security and consistency integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect cache keys and prove unauthorized cached data cannot be returned.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B028-R001","D35-B035-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B035-R001"],"requires":["D35-B028-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Authorized query caching boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Caching and authorization boundary patterns"],"provenance":["Established caching architecture pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B053-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Factory Implementation","subtopic":"Creation boundary","concept":"Factories centralize construction when object creation requires policy, validation, dependency selection, or multiple coordinated steps.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"When should an architecture use a factory implementation?","answer":"Use a factory when construction has meaningful policy or coordination that would otherwise be duplicated across callers; do not introduce a factory for trivial direct constructors.","explanation":"A factory is an architectural boundary when creation itself contains rules. It should not become a generic dumping ground for unrelated object behavior.","principle":"Centralize nontrivial creation policy without hiding domain behavior.","objective":"Make complex object construction explicit and consistent.","requirements":["Defined creation contract","Creation invariants","Explicit dependency inputs"],"constraints":["No unrelated business operations","No unnecessary abstraction for trivial construction"],"preconditions":["Construction has nontrivial policy or coordination"],"input":"Validated creation parameters and dependencies.","output":"Valid object instance.","expected_behavior":"All created objects satisfy required construction invariants.","incorrect_code":"def create_order(data):\n    order = Order()\n    order.status = "ready"\n    return order","corrected_code":"class OrderFactory:\n    def create(self, order_id, customer_id):\n        if not order_id or not customer_id:\n            raise ValueError("order_id and customer_id are required")\n        return Order.new(order_id=order_id, customer_id=customer_id)","failure_mode":"Callers create partially initialized objects.","expected_error":"ValueError for invalid creation input.","cause":"Construction rules are duplicated or absent.","root_cause":"Object lifecycle invariant is not centralized.","solution":"Define one creation boundary for nontrivial construction.","edge_case":"Factories should not bypass domain constructors merely to simplify serialization or persistence.","security_implication":"Creation authorization remains separate from construction validity.","performance_implication":"Factories should not perform unnecessary network or database calls during ordinary construction.","test_case":"Create valid and invalid objects through the factory.","test_input":"Complete and incomplete creation parameters.","expected_test_result":"Valid object satisfies invariants; invalid input is rejected deterministically.","testing_guidance":"Test all required construction invariants and dependency-selection branches.","verification_method":"Unit tests and invariant inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify every externally reachable construction path uses an approved invariant-preserving mechanism.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B005-R001","D35-B008-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B005-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Factory creation boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Factory and domain-construction patterns"],"provenance":["Established object-creation architecture pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B053-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Factory Implementation","subtopic":"Factory selection","concept":"A factory can select an implementation from a finite supported set using explicit policy rather than scattering conditional construction across callers.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a factory select between implementations?","answer":"Define an explicit selection policy, validate supported options, and map each option to a known implementation.","explanation":"Centralized selection prevents callers from duplicating provider or strategy selection logic.","principle":"Implementation selection should be deterministic and auditable.","objective":"Centralize supported implementation selection.","requirements":["Supported-option definition","Deterministic mapping","Invalid-option handling"],"constraints":["No arbitrary dynamic class loading from untrusted input","No silent fallback to an incompatible implementation"],"preconditions":["Selection options are defined"],"input":"Validated implementation selector.","output":"Configured implementation.","expected_behavior":"Each supported selector maps to exactly one intended implementation.","incorrect_code":"return globals()"user_supplied_class"","corrected_code":"IMPLEMENTATIONS = {\n    "local": LocalProcessor,\n    "remote": RemoteProcessor,\n}\n\ndef create_processor(mode):\n    try:\n        cls = IMPLEMENTATIONS[mode]\n    except KeyError:\n        raise ValueError(f"Unsupported mode: {mode}")\n    return cls()","failure_mode":"Untrusted input controls arbitrary class construction.","expected_error":"ValueError for unsupported mode.","cause":"Dynamic lookup uses caller-controlled class names.","root_cause":"Selection policy is not constrained to an allow-listed set.","solution":"Use explicit selector-to-implementation mapping.","edge_case":"A valid selector may require different dependencies; selection and dependency composition must remain compatible.","security_implication":"Never allow untrusted values to resolve arbitrary classes, modules, or executable code.","performance_implication":"Static mapping provides predictable low-cost selection.","test_case":"Select every supported implementation and an unsupported value.","test_input":"Known selectors plus malicious class-like string.","expected_test_result":"Only supported implementations can be constructed.","testing_guidance":"Test allow-list completeness and malicious selector inputs.","verification_method":"Security-focused unit tests and static inspection.","verification_guidance":"Confirm selection cannot escape the declared implementation map.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B007-R001","D35-B029-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B008-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":["D35-B053-R001"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Safe implementation selection"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Allow-list and factory-selection patterns"],"provenance":["Established secure dynamic-selection pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B053-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Factory Implementation","subtopic":"Factory lifecycle","concept":"Factories should define whether produced objects are transient, scoped, cached, or shared instead of leaving lifecycle semantics implicit.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must factory lifecycle semantics be explicit?","answer":"Different lifecycle choices affect state isolation, concurrency, resource usage, and correctness; therefore the factory contract should state whether instances are new, scoped, or shared.","explanation":"A shared mutable instance can create cross-request state leakage while repeatedly creating expensive resources can exhaust dependencies.","principle":"Object lifetime is an architectural property, not an incidental implementation detail.","objective":"Prevent lifecycle-related correctness and resource defects.","requirements":["Defined lifetime policy","Thread/concurrency semantics","Resource ownership rules"],"constraints":["No accidental global mutable instances","No undocumented shared state"],"preconditions":["Lifecycle requirements are understood"],"input":"Factory request and scope.","output":"Object with documented lifecycle.","expected_behavior":"Object lifetime matches the declared scope.","incorrect_code":"class Factory:\n    instance = ExpensiveMutableService()\n\n    def create(self):\n        return self.instance","corrected_code":"class Factory:\n    def create(self):\n        return ExpensiveMutableService()","failure_mode":"Mutable shared state leaks between independent operations.","expected_error":"State-isolation test failure.","cause":"Factory accidentally returns one process-wide mutable instance.","root_cause":"Lifecycle semantics were not specified.","solution":"Declare and enforce object lifetime explicitly.","edge_case":"Some clients intentionally require shared stateless resources; sharing should be deliberate and concurrency-safe.","security_implication":"Shared objects must not retain tenant or actor-specific state across requests.","performance_implication":"Long-lived resources may reduce construction cost but require bounded lifecycle and cleanup.","test_case":"Create two instances and verify state mutation in one does not affect the other when transient scope is required.","test_input":"Two factory calls followed by isolated state mutation.","expected_test_result":"Instances have independent state.","testing_guidance":"Test lifecycle identity, state isolation, concurrency, and cleanup.","verification_method":"Lifecycle unit tests and concurrency tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify declared lifetime against composition-root configuration.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B008-R001","D35-B048-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B008-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":["D35-B053-R001"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Factory lifecycle policy"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Dependency-lifetime and resource-scope patterns"],"provenance":["Established dependency lifecycle pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"EDGE-CASE"}

{"record_id":"D35-B053-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Factory Implementation","subtopic":"Factory failure classification","concept":"Creation failures should distinguish invalid input, unsupported configuration, unavailable dependency, and internal programming errors.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a factory classify creation failures?","answer":"Use distinct failure categories for caller-invalid input, unsupported selection, unavailable dependency, and unexpected defects so outer boundaries can respond appropriately.","explanation":"A single generic creation exception prevents consumers from deciding whether to correct input, retry, reconfigure, or investigate a defect.","principle":"Failure semantics should preserve actionable distinctions.","objective":"Make factory failures diagnosable and correctly actionable.","requirements":["Typed failure taxonomy","Cause preservation","Boundary-specific translation"],"constraints":["No blanket exception conversion","No loss of root cause"],"preconditions":["Error taxonomy is defined"],"input":"Creation request and dependency state.","output":"Object or classified creation failure.","expected_behavior":"Failure category accurately represents the reason creation could not complete.","incorrect_code":"except Exception:\n    raise FactoryError("creation failed")","corrected_code":"except UnsupportedModeError as exc:\n    raise FactoryConfigurationError(str(exc)) from exc\nexcept DependencyUnavailableError as exc:\n    raise FactoryDependencyError(str(exc)) from exc","failure_mode":"All creation failures become indistinguishable.","expected_error":"Specific factory failure classes.","cause":"Overly broad error translation.","root_cause":"Factory boundary does not preserve actionable failure semantics.","solution":"Classify expected failures and preserve original causes.","edge_case":"A dependency may fail with a transient condition that should not be mislabeled as invalid configuration.","security_implication":"External error responses should not reveal internal dependency details.","performance_implication":"Failure classification itself should remain lightweight.","test_case":"Trigger invalid input, unsupported mode, dependency failure, and unexpected defect.","test_input":"Four controlled failure scenarios.","expected_test_result":"Each scenario maps to the correct failure class or propagates an unexpected defect.","testing_guidance":"Assert exception type and causal chain.","verification_method":"Unit and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect exception chains and ensure no unexpected failure is suppressed.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B020-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B019-R001"],"requires":["D35-B020-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Factory failure taxonomy"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Typed exception and error-translation patterns"],"provenance":["Established error taxonomy pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"INVALID IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B053-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Factory Implementation","subtopic":"Factory testing","concept":"Factories should be tested at both policy level and integration level so selection and constructed dependency graphs are verified.","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"What should factory tests verify?","answer":"Tests should verify valid selections, invalid selections, construction invariants, dependency wiring, lifecycle semantics, and failure classification.","explanation":"A factory can be locally correct while still assembling incompatible implementations, so policy and composition behavior both require verification.","principle":"Creation policy and constructed behavior must be tested separately and together.","objective":"Detect construction and composition defects before deployment.","requirements":["Unit tests","Composition tests","Failure tests"],"constraints":["Do not claim runtime verification without execution","Do not test only the happy path"],"preconditions":["Factory contract is defined"],"input":"Factory test scenarios.","output":"Test evidence about construction behavior.","expected_behavior":"All supported creation paths satisfy their contracts.","incorrect_code":"def test_factory():\n    assert factory.create("default") is not None","corrected_code":"def test_factory_rejects_unknown_mode(factory):\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n        factory.create("unsupported")\n\ndef test_factory_selects_expected_type(factory):\n    assert isinstance(factory.create("local"), LocalProcessor)","failure_mode":"Tests verify only non-null output.","expected_error":"Missing assertions allow wrong implementation selection to pass.","cause":"Weak factory test oracle.","root_cause":"Test suite does not encode factory policy.","solution":"Assert exact selection, invariants, invalid inputs, and dependency compatibility.","edge_case":"If implementations are intentionally interchangeable, assert the shared contract rather than concrete type where appropriate.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive implementation selection should have negative tests for unsupported or untrusted selectors.","performance_implication":"Factory tests should detect accidental expensive construction or resource leaks where relevant.","test_case":"Exercise every supported selector and invalid selector.","test_input":"Complete factory selection matrix.","expected_test_result":"Every supported selector creates a contract-compatible implementation; invalid selectors fail safely.","testing_guidance":"Maintain a selection matrix and add regression cases for newly supported implementations.","verification_method":"Automated unit and composition tests.","verification_guidance":"Record executed test results separately from source-level validation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"pytest","framework_version":"Version unspecified","version_range":"Compatible pytest release","dependencies":["pytest"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment with pytest installed","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B040-R005","D35-B053-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B053-R001","D35-B053-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B053-R001","D35-B053-R002"],"verifies":["Factory selection contract"],"derived_from":["Factory testing patterns"],"provenance":["Established software testing practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","implementation_coverage":"TEST"}

{"record_id":"D35-B054-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Strategy Implementation","subtopic":"Strategy contract","concept":"A strategy boundary encapsulates interchangeable algorithms behind a stable contract while keeping selection outside the strategy implementations.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should the Strategy pattern be implemented?","answer":"Define a stable strategy interface, implement each algorithm independently, and inject the selected strategy into the consuming component.","explanation":"The consumer should depend on behavior rather than conditional branches for every algorithm variation.","principle":"Vary algorithms behind a stable behavioral contract.","objective":"Implement interchangeable behavior without duplicating orchestration logic.","requirements":["Stable strategy contract","Independent implementations","Explicit selection"],"constraints":["Strategies must honor common semantics","No strategy-specific behavior hidden in the consumer"],"preconditions":["Algorithms share a meaningful contract"],"input":"Strategy input.","output":"Contract-defined strategy result.","expected_behavior":"The consumer can substitute compatible strategies without changing its orchestration logic.","incorrect_code":"if mode == "a":\n    result = algorithm_a(data)\nelif mode == "b":\n    result = algorithm_b(data)","corrected_code":"from typing import Protocol\n\nclass Strategy(Protocol):\n    def execute(self, data): ...\n\nclass Context:\n    def init(self, strategy: Strategy):\n        self.strategy = strategy\n\n    def run(self, data):\n        return self.strategy.execute(data)","failure_mode":"Consumer accumulates algorithm-specific branches.","expected_error":"Contract violation when strategies return incompatible results.","cause":"Variation is implemented in orchestration code.","root_cause":"No explicit behavioral abstraction exists.","solution":"Define and inject a common strategy contract.","edge_case":"If algorithms differ materially in semantics, forcing them behind one interface may hide important differences.","security_implication":"Strategy selection must not permit unauthorized algorithms or unsafe execution paths.","performance_implication":"Indirection adds small dispatch overhead but can enable algorithm-specific performance optimization.","test_case":"Run the same context with two contract-compatible strategies.","test_input":"Shared input processed by two implementations.","expected_test_result":"Both implementations satisfy the same result contract.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests so every strategy is checked against the same behavioral requirements.","verification_method":"Interface/contract tests and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify substitutability rather than merely method existence.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library typing"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B053-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Strategy behavioral boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Strategy pattern and dependency inversion patterns"],"provenance":["Established object-oriented design pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B054-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Strategy Implementation","subtopic":"Strategy substitutability","concept":"Interchangeable strategies must satisfy the same externally observable contract even when their internal algorithms differ.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How can strategy substitutability be verified?","answer":"Define contract tests that execute every strategy against shared inputs, edge cases, and failure conditions and assert the common behavioral contract.","explanation":"A matching method signature is insufficient if strategies disagree on null handling, units, error semantics, or output invariants.","principle":"Behavioral compatibility is stronger than interface compatibility.","objective":"Prevent strategy substitutions from causing semantic regressions.","requirements":["Shared contract tests","Common edge-case expectations","Failure semantics"],"constraints":["Do not assume mathematical equivalence where algorithms intentionally differ","Define permitted result variation explicitly"],"preconditions":["Strategy contract defines observable behavior"],"input":"Strategy implementation set and contract test suite.","output":"Compatibility evidence.","expected_behavior":"Every strategy passes mandatory shared invariants.","incorrect_code":"assert hasattr(strategy, "execute")","corrected_code":"def assert_strategy_contract(strategy):\n    result = strategy.execute(10)\n    assert result is not None\n    assert isinstance(result, int)\n    assert result >= 0","failure_mode":"A strategy passes structural checks while violating semantic expectations.","expected_error":"Contract assertion failure.","cause":"Verification checks interface shape only.","root_cause":"Behavioral contract is underspecified or untested.","solution":"Create reusable contract tests for all strategies.","edge_case":"Floating-point algorithms may require tolerance-based assertions instead of exact equality.","security_implication":"Contract tests should include malicious or boundary inputs where strategies process untrusted data.","performance_implication":"Contract suites can include resource or latency thresholds only when measured and environment-qualified.","test_case":"Run common contract tests against all registered strategies.","test_input":"Normal, boundary, invalid, and adversarial inputs.","expected_test_result":"All required behavioral invariants pass.","testing_guidance":"Separate universal invariants from algorithm-specific expected differences.","verification_method":"Automated contract testing.","verification_guidance":"Require every new strategy to pass the shared contract suite before registration.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"pytest","framework_version":"Version unspecified","version_range":"Compatible pytest release","dependencies":["pytest"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment with pytest installed","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B054-R001","D35-B040-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B054-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B054-R001"],"verifies":["Strategy substitutability"],"derived_from":["Contract testing and substitutability principles"],"provenance":["Established contract-testing pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","implementation_coverage":"VERIFICATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B054-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Strategy Implementation","subtopic":"Strategy state isolation","concept":"A strategy implementation should avoid retaining request-specific mutable state unless its lifecycle explicitly guarantees safe isolation.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should strategies usually avoid mutable request state?","answer":"Stateless strategies are easier to share safely, test, and execute concurrently; if state is required, its ownership and lifecycle must be explicit.","explanation":"A shared strategy instance containing request-specific state can produce race conditions or cross-request contamination.","principle":"Shared behavioral components should not accidentally retain request state.","objective":"Prevent concurrency and lifecycle defects in strategy implementations.","requirements":["Explicit state policy","Safe concurrency behavior","Defined lifecycle"],"constraints":["No hidden request-specific fields in shared instances"],"preconditions":["Strategy lifecycle is defined"],"input":"Concurrent strategy calls.","output":"Independent results.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent calls do not influence each other's request-specific behavior.","incorrect_code":"class Strategy:\n    self.current_user = None\n\n    def execute(self, user):\n        self.current_user = user\n        return self.process()","corrected_code":"class Strategy:\n    def execute(self, user):\n        return self.process(user)","failure_mode":"One execution overwrites state used by another execution.","expected_error":"Concurrency test detects inconsistent result or context.","cause":"Mutable request state stored on shared object.","root_cause":"Lifecycle semantics are incompatible with retained mutable state.","solution":"Pass request state as method data or isolate instances per scope.","edge_case":"Algorithm caches may be safe if immutable or properly synchronized and independent of security context.","security_implication":"Shared request state can cause user or tenant data leakage.","performance_implication":"Stateless strategies are generally easier to share without synchronization overhead.","test_case":"Execute strategy concurrently with distinct user inputs.","test_input":"Interleaved concurrent calls.","expected_test_result":"Each call produces the result associated only with its own input.","testing_guidance":"Use repeated concurrency tests to expose race-dependent defects.","verification_method":"Concurrency tests and lifecycle review.","verification_guidance":"Inspect mutable fields and prove their ownership scope.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B053-R003","D35-B051-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B053-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":["D35-B054-R001"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Strategy state-isolation policy"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Concurrency and object-lifecycle patterns"],"provenance":["Established concurrent-state design practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"EDGE-CASE"}

{"record_id":"D35-B054-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Strategy Implementation","subtopic":"Strategy error semantics","concept":"Strategies sharing a contract should expose compatible failure semantics even when internal algorithms differ.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should errors be standardized across interchangeable strategies?","answer":"Define contract-level failure categories and translate implementation-specific exceptions at the strategy boundary.","explanation":"Consumers should not need to know whether an algorithm uses a database, library, or remote service merely to interpret ordinary failure categories.","principle":"Implementation-specific failures should be normalized at the appropriate abstraction boundary.","objective":"Preserve strategy interchangeability during failure conditions.","requirements":["Common failure contract","Implementation-specific translation","Cause preservation"],"constraints":["Do not hide unexpected programming errors","Do not collapse materially different failure semantics"],"preconditions":["Failure categories are defined"],"input":"Strategy operation and underlying failures.","output":"Contract-compatible result or failure.","expected_behavior":"Expected failures are represented consistently across strategies.","incorrect_code":"except Exception:\n    raise StrategyError("failed")","corrected_code":"except ProviderTimeout as exc:\n    raise StrategyUnavailable("provider timeout") from exc\nexcept Exception:\n    raise","failure_mode":"All failures become indistinguishable or unexpected defects are hidden.","expected_error":"StrategyUnavailable for known provider timeout; unexpected errors propagate.","cause":"Overbroad exception translation.","root_cause":"Boundary does not distinguish expected implementation failures from defects.","solution":"Translate only known implementation-specific exceptions.","edge_case":"One strategy may have a failure mode that another cannot produ