{"record_id":"D35-B054-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Strategy Implementation","subtopic":"Strategy registration","concept":"Strategy registration should be explicit and validated so unsupported or incompatible implementations cannot enter the runtime strategy set.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should strategies be registered safely?","answer":"Register strategies through an explicit allow-list, validate that each implementation satisfies the required contract, and reject duplicate or unsupported identifiers.","explanation":"Registration is part of the executable architecture and should be subject to the same invariant enforcement as other dependency composition.","principle":"Extensibility must remain constrained by explicit architectural contracts.","objective":"Make strategy extension safe and deterministic.","requirements":["Allow-listed identifiers","Contract validation","Duplicate detection"],"constraints":["No arbitrary plugin loading from untrusted input","No silent replacement"],"preconditions":["Strategy contract exists"],"input":"Strategy registration definitions.","output":"Validated strategy registry.","expected_behavior":"Only compatible registered strategies become available for selection.","incorrect_code":"strategies[name] = importlib.import_module(module).Strategy()","corrected_code":"SUPPORTED = {"fast": FastStrategy, "safe": SafeStrategy}\n\ndef register(name):\n    if name not in SUPPORTED:\n        raise ValueError("unsupported strategy")\n    return SUPPORTED"name"","failure_mode":"Untrusted input loads arbitrary executable modules.","expected_error":"ValueError for unsupported identifier.","cause":"Dynamic import based on caller-controlled module path.","root_cause":"Extensibility mechanism lacks a trust boundary.","solution":"Use explicit registration or a separately secured plugin mechanism.","edge_case":"Trusted plugin ecosystems may legitimately use dynamic discovery but require signed/trusted packages and isolated loading policies.","security_implication":"Dynamic executable loading is a high-risk trust boundary.","performance_implication":"Explicit registry lookup is predictable and inexpensive.","test_case":"Attempt registration with a supported identifier and arbitrary module-like identifier.","test_input":"Known and untrusted registration names.","expected_test_result":"Only known strategies can be selected.","testing_guidance":"Include security regression tests for arbitrary import/path execution.","verification_method":"Static security review and automated negative tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify registry contents and loading path are not caller-controlled.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B053-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":["D35-B054-R001"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Safe strategy registry"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Allow-list security and plugin architecture patterns"],"provenance":["Established secure extensibility pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B055-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Specification Pattern Implementation","subtopic":"Composable criteria","concept":"A specification object represents a reusable business or query predicate that can be composed without embedding conditional logic throughout consumers.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What does a specification implementation provide?","answer":"It encapsulates a criterion as a composable object or function, allowing AND, OR, and NOT combinations while preserving explicit semantics.","explanation":"Specifications can make complex selection rules reusable and testable, especially when criteria are applied in multiple application paths.","principle":"Reusable decision criteria should have explicit composition semantics.","objective":"Represent complex predicates without duplicating conditional logic.","requirements":["Predicate contract","Composition operators","Deterministic evaluation"],"constraints":["Do not use specifications to hide unrelated side effects","Define null and missing-value semantics"],"preconditions":["A reusable criterion exists"],"input":"Candidate object and specification.","output":"Boolean satisfaction result.","expected_behavior":"Specification evaluation returns a deterministic result based only on defined inputs.","incorrect_code":"if user.active and (user.role == "admin" or user.score > 80):\n    ...","corrected_code":"class Specification:\n    def init(self, predicate):\n        self.predicate = predicate\n\n    def is_satisfied_by(self, value):\n        return self.predicate(value)\n\n    def and(self, other):\n        return Specification(lambda v: self.is_satisfied_by(v) and other.is_satisfied_by(v))","failure_mode":"Criteria are duplicated and drift across consumers.","expected_error":"Specification consistency test failure.","cause":"Rules are encoded independently in multiple call sites.","root_cause":"No reusable predicate boundary exists.","solution":"Extrac