{"record_id":"D35-B055-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Specification Pattern Implementation","subtopic":"Specification purity","concept":"A specification used as a predicate should not silently perform side effects such as persistence, mutation, or external calls.","knowledge_type":"INVALID IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why are side effects inside specifications problematic?","answer":"Side effects make repeated evaluation unpredictable, complicate composition, and can cause hidden writes or external calls during operations expected to be pure filtering.","explanation":"A caller should be able to evaluate a predicate without unexpectedly changing system state.","principle":"Predicate semantics should not imply hidden mutation.","objective":"Prevent side-effect-driven correctness defects.","requirements":["Explicit evaluation semantics","No hidden writes for pure specifications"],"constraints":["External calls require a separate explicit policy boundary"],"preconditions":["Specification is declared pure"],"input":"Candidate object.","output":"Boolean result without state mutation.","expected_behavior":"Repeated evaluation of unchanged input produces the same result absent explicitly declared external state.","incorrect_code":"class Specification:\n    def is_satisfied_by(self, user):\n        self.audit_repository.save({"user": user.id})\n        return user.active","corrected_code":"class ActiveUserSpecification:\n    def is_satisfied_by(self, user):\n        return user.active","failure_mode":"Filtering triggers unexpected persistence.","expected_error":"Side-effect detection test failure.","cause":"Audit write embedded in predicate evaluation.","root_cause":"Cross-cutting behavior was hidden inside a pure-looking abstraction.","solution":"Separate audit/event behavior from predicate evaluation.","edge_case":"Time-dependent or externally sourced specifications are not pure and should explicitly declare those semantics.","security_implication":"Hidden external calls can bypass expected authorization and auditing boundaries.","performance_implication":"Side effects inside predicates can multiply I/O by the number of evaluated objects.","test_case":"Evaluate the specification twice and assert no repository mutation occurred.","test_input":"One candidate object and empty audit repository.","expected_test_result":"Both evaluations return the same result and repository remains unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Use mocks to assert no external calls or writes occur for pure specifications.","verification_method":"Unit tests and static review.","verification_guidance":"Verify the specification's declared purity matches implementation behavior.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B055-R001","D35-B043-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B055-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B055-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Specification purity"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Pure-function and predicate design principles"],"provenance":["Established functional and domain-modeling pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"INVALID IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B055-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Specification Pattern Implementation","subtopic":"Specification-to-query translation","concept":"When specifications are used against persistent data, translation into a database query must preserve semantic equivalence and parameter safety.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What is the risk when translating specifications into database queries?","answer":"The translated query can accidentally change predicate semantics, null handling, collation, or security scope, so translation requires explicit tests and parameterized query construction.","explanation":"An in-memory predicate and database expression can produce different results for nulls, strings, dates, numeric precision, or relationships.","principle":"Query translation is a semantic transformation that requires verification.","objective":"Preserve specification meaning when executing criteria in persistence infrastructure.","requirements":["Defined translation rules","Parameterized values","Parity tests"],"constraints":["No string concatenation of untrusted values","No unverified semantic assumptions"],"preconditions":["Specification has a persistence representation"],"input":"Specification plus query builder.","output":"Parameterized database query.","expected_behavior":"Database results match the specification contract for supported values.","incorrect_code":"sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + spec.name + "'"","corrected_code":"sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?"\nparams = (spec.name,)","failure_mode":"Query behavior differs from specification or permits injection.","expected_error":"Query correctness or security test failure.","cause":"Unsafe string construction or incorrect semantic translation.","root_cause":"Specification translation is treated as string generation instead of a verified transformation.","solution":"Use parameterized queries and translation tests for edge semantics.","edge_case":"Database null semantics often differ from language Boolean semantics and require explicit handling.","security_implication":"Parameterized queries are required for untrusted values; never embed raw input in SQL text.","performance_implication":"Server-side filtering can reduce transferred data, but query plans should be evaluated using actual measurements rather than assumed performance.","test_case":"Translate specifications containing normal, null-like, and special-character values.","test_input":"Criteria with apostrophes, nulls, and boundary strings.","expected_test_result":"Correct results are returned without query syntax alteration or injection.","testing_guidance":"Combine semantic parity tests with SQL-injection regression tests.","verification_method":"Database integration tests and query inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify parameters remain separate from SQL syntax.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Database driver with parameterized query support"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment plus test database","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B044-R002","D35-B055-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B044-R002"],"requires":["D35-B055-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Specification query translation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["OWASP SQL Injection Prevention guidance; specification translation patterns"],"provenance":["OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet; established query-builder practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B055-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Specification Pattern Implementation","subtopic":"Composite specification complexity","concept":"Deeply nested specifications can become difficult to understand and expensive to evaluate, so composition should remain explicit and bounded.","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What happens when specification composition becomes excessively complex?","answer":"The resulting predicate may become difficult to verify, optimize, or translate. Complex policies should be decomposed into named specifications or moved to a dedicated policy abstraction.","explanation":"Composability is useful until the composition graph obscures semantics or produces excessive evaluation cost.","principle":"Abstraction should improve reasoning rather than merely hide complexity.","objective":"Keep specification composition understandable and operationally bounded.","requirements":["Named complex criteria","Defined evaluation order where relevant","Complexity review"],"constraints":["Avoid unbounded recursive composition","Do not hide expensive I/O in predicate evaluation"],"preconditions":["Composite specifications are supported"],"input":"Multiple specification components.","output":"Bounded composite specification.","expected_behavior":"Composition remains understandable and its evaluation characteristics are known.","incorrect_code":"spec = a & b & c & d & e & f & g & h & i & j","corrected_code":"eligibility = BasicEligibility()\nrisk_eligible = eligibility & RiskEligibility()\nfinal_policy = ApprovedCustomerPolicy(risk_eligible)","failure_mode":"A large anonymous predicate becomes unreviewable.","expected_error":"Architecture review failure or excessive evaluation cost.","cause":"Composition is performed without semantic naming or complexity limits.","root_cause":"Abstraction boundaries are missing at meaningful policy concepts.","solution":"Introduce named specifications or a policy object at domain-relevant boundaries.","edge_case":"A mathematically large predicate can still be valid if generated and formally tested; complexity alone does not prove incorrectness.","security_implication":"Complex authorization predicates increase the risk of missed deny conditions.","performance_implication":"Repeated expensive predicates can create significant evaluation cost; measure rather than assume.","test_case":"Evaluate a complex policy against a full truth-table subset and representative boundary cases.","test_input":"Combinations of criteria and adversarial values.","expected_test_result":"Policy matches documented semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use decision tables for security-sensitive composite policies.","verification_method":"Decision-table testing and code review.","verification_guidance":"Verify each named policy component independently before verifying composition.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.94,"related_records":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B055-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B055-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Complex policy composition"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Policy decomposition and specification patterns"],"provenance":["Established policy-design practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"EDGE-CASE"}

{"record_id":"D35-B055-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Specification Pattern Implementation","subtopic":"Specification verification","concept":"Security-sensitive specifications require explicit negative and boundary tests rather than only positive examples.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should a security-sensitive specification be verified?","answer":"Test permitted, denied, boundary, missing-context, and cross-scope cases using a decision table derived from the documented policy.","explanation":"Positive-only tests can prove that valid access works while failing to detect unintended authorization expansion.","principle":"Security policy verification must test denial boundaries as first-class behavior.","objective":"Detect authorization and policy-composition defects.","requirements":["Decision table","Positive tests","Negative tests","Boundary tests"],"constraints":["Do not infer authorization from absence of an exception alone","Do not rely on code coverage as proof of policy correctness"],"preconditions":["Policy semantics are documented"],"input":"Policy decision matrix.","output":"Verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Observed decisions match the documented policy matrix.","incorrect_code":"assert policy.is_satisfied_by(valid_user)","corrected_code":"assert policy.is_satisfied_by(valid_user)\nassert not policy.is_satisfied_by(unauthorized_user)\nassert not policy.is_satisfied_by(cross_tenant_user)","failure_mode":"Unauthorized cases are never tested.","expected_error":"Missing negative verification evidence.","cause":"Test suite covers only successful cases.","root_cause":"Policy verification is treated as ordinary functional testing.","solution":"Create explicit denial and boundary cases from the policy model.","edge_case":"Default-deny behavior should be tested when required context is missing or malformed.","security_implication":"Incorrect specification verification can directly permit unauthorized actions or disclosure.","performance_implication":"Decision-table tests should remain deterministic and independent of external service availability where possible.","test_case":"Execute every mandatory policy row against representative identities and resources.","test_input":"Authorized, unauthorized, cross-tenant, missing-context, and boundary cases.","expected_test_result":"Each observed result matches the policy decision table.","testing_guidance":"Keep policy test vectors versioned with the policy definition.","verification_method":"Automated decision-table testing.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence for both allow and deny behavior before treating the policy as verified.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"pytest","framework_version":"Version unspecified","version_range":"Compatible pytest release","dependencies":["pytest"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment with pytest installed","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B027-R001","D35-B055-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B027-R001","D35-B055-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B055-R001","D35-B055-R004"],"verifies":["Security-sensitive specification behavior"],"derived_from":["OWASP authorization testing guidance; decision-table testing"],"provenance":["OWASP access-control guidance; established policy-testing practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","implementation_coverage":"VERIFICATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B056-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Plugin Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Explicit extension contract","concept":"A plugin boundary should define a narrow stable contract for externally implemented extensions.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should an architecture define before implementing plugins?","answer":"Define the plugin lifecycle, input/output contract, capabilities, trust level, error semantics, version compatibility, and resource limits before implementing loading logic.","explanation":"Without an explicit contract, plugins become uncontrolled extensions that can bypass architectural and security assumptions.","principle":"Extensibility begins with a constrained contract, not dynamic loading.","objective":"Create a verifiable extension boundary.","requirements":["Plugin interface","Lifecycle definition","Capability model","Compatibility policy"],"constraints":["No unrestricted host access","No undocumented lifecycle behavior"],"preconditions":["Extension requirements are stable enough to specify"],"input":"Plugin implementation.","output":"Contract-compliant extension behavior.","expected_behavior":"Plugin interacts with the host only through declared capabilities and lifecycle methods.","incorrect_code":"plugin.run(host_application.dict)","corrected_code":"class PluginContext:\n    def init(self, logger, config):\n        self.logger = logger\n        self.config = config\n\nclass Plugin:\n    def initialize(self, context): ...\n    def execute(self, request): ...\n    def shutdown(self): ...","failure_mode":"Plugin receives unrestricted host internals.","expected_error":"Architecture/security boundary violation.","cause":"Host object is passed directly to extension.","root_cause":"Plugin contract does not constrain capabilities.","solution":"Expose a narrow capability-oriented context.","edge_case":"A plugin requiring a new capability should trigger an explicit contract/version change rather than silently accessing host internals.","security_implication":"Plugin code is part of the trusted-computing boundary unless isolated; capabilities must be deliberately granted.","performance_implication":"A narrow contract can reduce unnecessary data transfer and extension overhead.","test_case":"Load a plugin against the declared context and attempt access outside exposed capabilities.","test_input":"Compliant plugin and unauthorized host-access attempt.","expected_test_result":"Compliant operations work; undeclared capabilities are unavailable.","testing_guidance":"Test lifecycle ordering, capability exposure, compatibility, and failure handling.","verification_method":"Contract tests and security review.","verification_guidance":"Inspect the plugin context for unintended host references.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B054-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Plugin contract boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Capability-oriented extension architecture"],"provenance":["Established plugin and capability-boundary patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B056-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Plugin Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Plugin lifecycle","concept":"Plugin lifecycle states should be explicit so initialization, execution, failure, and shutdown are not ambiguous.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should plugin lifecycle states be explicit?","answer":"Explicit lifecycle states prevent execution before initialization, repeated initialization, unsafe shutdown, and ambiguous recovery after plugin failure.","explanation":"Lifecycle state is part of the extension contract and should be represented as a controlled state machine when the plugin has meaningful phases.","principle":"Extension lifecycle transitions should be explicit and verifiable.","objective":"Prevent invalid plugin lifecycle operations.","requirements":["Lifecycle states","Allowed transitions","Failure state"],"constraints":["No execution before successful initialization","Shutdown must be safe and bounded"],"preconditions":["Plugin lifecycle contract exists"],"input":"Lifecycle operation.","output":"Updated plugin lifecycle state.","expected_behavior":"Only allowed lifecycle transitions succeed.","incorrect_code":"plugin.execute(request)","corrected_code":"if plugin.state != PluginState.READY:\n    raise RuntimeError("plugin is not ready")\nplugin.execute(request)","failure_mode":"Execution occurs before initialization.","expected_error":"RuntimeError for invalid lifecycle state.","cause":"Lifecycle state is not enforced.","root_cause":"Plugin execution boundary lacks state invariants.","solution":"Represent and validate lifecycle state transitions.","edge_case":"A failed shutdown should not necessarily restore READY; define a terminal or FAILED state explicitly.","security_implication":"Uninitialized security-sensitive plugins must not receive production data.","performance_implication":"Lifecycle checks are inexpensive compared with plugin execution.","test_case":"Attempt execute before initialize and after shutdown.","test_input":"Plugin in CREATED and STOPPED states.","expected_test_result":"Execution is rejected outside READY state.","testing_guidance":"Test every state transition and invalid transition.","verification_method":"State-machine tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify transition graph against the architectural lifecycle specification.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B014-R001","D35-B056-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B014-R001"],"requires":["D35-B056-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Plugin lifecycle state machine"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Explicit state-machine implementation patterns"],"provenance":["Established lifecycle-state design pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B056-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Plugin Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Plugin compatibility","concept":"Plugin compatibility should be checked against explicit host contract and capability versions before activation.","knowledge_type":"VERSION","question":"How should plugin compatibility be implemented?","answer":"Publish a host/plugin contract version and capability requirements, then reject or isolate plugins whose declared requirements are incompatible.","explanation":"Importing a plugin successfully does not establish semantic compatibility with the host runtime.","principle":"Loadability is not compatibility.","objective":"Prevent incompatible extensions from entering active execution.","requirements":["Contract version","Capability requirements","Compatibility policy"],"constraints":["No silent semantic downgrade","No activation of unsupported contract versions"],"preconditions":["Compatibility rules are defined"],"input":"Plugin metadata and host capability set.","output":"Compatibility decision.","expected_behavior":"Only compatible plugins become READY.","incorrect_code":"if plugin_module.importable:\n    plugin = plugin_module.Plugin()","corrected_code":"if not host_contract.supports(plugin.required_contract):\n    raise CompatibilityError("unsupported plugin contract")\nplugin = plugin_module.Plugin()","failure_mode":"Plugin loads but fails later because required capabilities are absent.","expected_error":"CompatibilityError before activation.","cause":"Compatibility is inferred from import success.","root_cause":"Contract compatibility was not modeled explicitly.","solution":"Validate declared compatibility before lifecycle activation.","edge_case":"A plugin may support multiple host versions; represent supported ranges explicitly.","security_implication":"Compatibility metadata must not be trusted as authorization to access capabilities.","performance_implication":"Compatibility checks should occur once at activation rather than repeatedly during execution.","test_case":"Activate plugins requiring supported and unsupported contracts.","test_input":"Two plugin metadata definitions.","expected_test_result":"Supported plugin activates; incompatible plugin is rejected before execution.","testing_guidance":"Maintain compatibility matrix tests.","verification_method":"Activation tests and metadata validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify no incompatible plugin reaches READY state.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERSIONING_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B045-R001","D35-B046-R001","D35-B056-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B056-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Plugin compatibility gate"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Explicit contract-version compatibility patterns"],"provenance":["Established API/plugin compatibility pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"VERSION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B056-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Plugin Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Plugin resource limits","concept":"An extension boundary should constrain execution time, memory/resource use, concurrency, and external capabilities where the threat model requires it.","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should plugin execution have resource limits?","answer":"Unbounded extensions can exhaust shared resources or prevent the host from making progress, so the architecture should define appropriate resource controls and failure isolation.","explanation":"Resource limits depend on the execution model. In-process Python code cannot be made fully safe merely by setting a timer; stronger isolation may require separate processes or sandboxing.","principle":"Resource ownership and isolation must match the trust model.","objective":"Contain extension-induced resource failures.","requirements":["Defined resource budget","Cancellation or isolation mechanism","Failure containment"],"constraints":["Do not claim process isolation from application-level timeouts","Do not assume memory limits exist without enforcement"],"preconditions":["Threat and resource model defined"],"input":"Plugin execution request and resource policy.","output":"Completed result or bounded failure.","expected_behavior":"Plugin cannot consume shared resources indefinitely under the supported isolation model.","incorrect_code":"signal.alarm(5)\nplugin.execute(request)","corrected_code":"# Illustrative boundary: actual enforcement requires an execution model\n# with a controllable worker/process and explicit cancellation policy.\nresult = worker_pool.execute(plugin, request, timeout=5)","failure_mode":"Application-level timeout gives the appearance of isolation without actually stopping unsafe work.","expected_error":"Timeout or worker termination according to the real execution mechanism.","cause":"Timeout is represented but not enforced against the executing workload.","root_cause":"Resource isolation semantics are not matched to runtime architecture.","solution":"Use enforceable worker/process/container isolation when hard limits are required.","edge_case":"Cooperative cancellation may not stop CPU-bound or native-extension work.","security_implication":"Untrusted plugins should not receive unrestricted in-process privileges merely because a timeout exists.","performance_implication":"Isolation adds process or IPC overhead that should be measured in the target environment.","test_case":"Run a deliberately long-running plugin under the supported isolation mechanism.","test_input":"Plugin exceeding configured execution budget.","expected_test_result":"Execution is bounded according to the actual enforcement mechanism.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, cancellation, crash, memory pressure, and recovery separately.","verification_method":"Integration and fault-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify observed termination and resource release rather than trusting configuration values.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Execution-isolation mechanism appropriate to deployment"],"execution_requirements":"Runtime with enforceable worker/process isolation when hard limits are required","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B056-R001","D35-B056-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B056-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Plugin resource containment"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Process isolation and resource-governance patterns"],"provenance":["Established systems isolation practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B056-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Plugin Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Plugin failure isolation","concept":"Plugin failures should be contained so an extension failure cannot silently corrupt host state or prevent unrelated capabilities from operating.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should plugin failures be isolated?","answer":"Define failure states, transactional boundaries, cleanup behavior, and host-level containment appropriate to whether the plugin executes in-process or in an isolated worker.","explanation":"Exception catching alone does not provide state isolation. Shared-memory mutations may already have occurred before an exception is raised.","principle":"Failure containment requires state and resource boundaries, not merely exception handling.","objective":"Prevent extension failures from becoming host-wide corruption or outages.","requirements":["Failure classification","State boundary","Cleanup policy","Recovery policy"],"constraints":["Do not claim rollback for nontransactional side effects","Do not suppress plugin failure silently"],"preconditions":["Plugin lifecycle and trust model exist"],"input":"Plugin failure event.","output":"Contained failure state and diagnostic evidence.","expected_behavior":"Host records the failure, cleans owned resources, and prevents unsafe continued execution.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    plugin.execute(request)\nexcept Exception:\n    pass","corrected_code":"try:\n    plugin.execute(request)\nexcept KnownPluginError as exc:\n    plugin.mark_failed(exc)\n    logger.error("plugin_failed", extra={"plugin": plugin.name})\n    raise","failure_mode":"Plugin failure is silently ignored after partial side effects.","expected_error":"KnownPluginError or host-level classified plugin failure.","cause":"Blanket exception suppression.","root_cause":"Failure containment was confused with exception suppression.","solution":"Classify, record, clean up, and transition plugin state explicitly.","edge_case":"If the plugin has performed irreversible external actions, host rollback may be impossible; verification must account for this.","security_implication":"A failed plugin may remain compromised or partially active and should not automatically regain sensitive capabilities.","performance_implication":"Isolation and cleanup mechanisms consume resources and should have bounded policies.","test_case":"Force a plugin to fail after allocating resources and before returning a result.","test_input":"Injected plugin failure.","expected_test_result":"Failure is recorded, resources are released, and plugin does not remain falsely READY.","testing_guidance":"Use fault injection at multiple lifecycle points.","verification_method":"Fault-injection integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify state, resource, telemetry, and recovery invariants after failure.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESILIENCE_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B056-R002","D35-B056-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B056-R002"],"requires":["D35-B056-R004"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Plugin failure containment"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Fault containment and resilience patterns"],"provenance":["Established fault-containment architecture pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B057-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Ownership Implementation","subtopic":"Ownership contract","concept":"Every acquired resource should have an explicit owner responsible for acquisition, use, release, and failure cleanup.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What is a resource ownership contract?","answer":"It identifies who or what component acquires a resource, how long it owns it, who may use it, and how release occurs on success and failure.","explanation":"Ambiguous ownership causes leaks, double release, premature release, and shutdown defects.","principle":"Resource lifetime should have one authoritative owner.","objective":"Make resource lifecycle deterministic.","requirements":["Owner definition","Lifetime definition","Release responsibility"],"constraints":["No double ownership without explicit transfer semantics","No resource escape beyond declared lifetime"],"preconditions":["Resource acquisition exists"],"input":"Resource acquisition operation.","output":"Owned resource with lifecycle contract.","expected_behavior":"Exactly one component is responsible for final release under normal lifecycle conditions.","incorrect_code":"resource = acquire()\nreturn resource","corrected_code":"from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef owned_resource(factory):\n    resource = factory()\n    try:\n        yield resource\n    finally:\n        resource.close()","failure_mode":"Caller receives a resource without clear release responsibility.","expected_error":"Resource leak or premature close.","cause":"Ownership boundary is implicit.","root_cause":"Lifecycle responsibility is not encoded in architecture.","solution":"Define explicit ownership and use context-managed lifetime where appropriate.","edge_case":"Ownership transfer can be valid but must be explicit and documented.","security_implication":"Unreleased sensitive resources can retain credentials, file descriptors, or data longer than intended.","performance_implication":"Correct release prevents resource exhaustion and connection/file-descriptor growth.","test_case":"Acquire a resource and force an exception during use.","test_input":"Resource with observable close operation.","expected_test_result":"Resource is closed even when use fails.","testing_guidance":"Test success, failure, cancellation, and nested ownership.","verification_method":"Lifecycle tests and resource-leak instrumentation.","verification_guidance":"Verify every acquisition path has a release path.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library contextlib"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B034-R001","D35-B048-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B034-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Resource ownership boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Resource ownership and RAII/context-management patterns"],"provenance":["Python context management documentation; established resource-lifecycle practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B057-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Ownership Implementation","subtopic":"Ownership transfer","concept":"Resource ownership transfer must explicitly define when the previous owner stops releasing the resource and the new owner assumes responsibility.","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why is resource ownership transfer dangerous when implicit?","answer":"Implicit transfer can cause double close, use-after-close, or leaks because multiple components may believe they own the same resource or neither assumes responsibility.","explanation":"Ownership is a lifecycle contract, so transfer must be represented as a clear state transition.","principle":"Ownership transfer is a first-class lifecycle event.","objective":"Prevent ownership ambiguity.","requirements":["Transfer operation","Ownership state","Transfer invariants"],"constraints":["No use after transfer unless contract permits it","No release by former owner after successful transfer"],"preconditions":["Resource supports transfer semantics"],"input":"Owned resource and new owner.","output":"Resource associated with new owner.","expected_behavior":"Exactly one active owner remains after transfer.","incorrect_code":"def give_to(other):\n    other.resource = self.resource","corrected_code":"def transfer(self, other):\n    if self.resource is None:\n        raise RuntimeError("resource already transferred")\n    resource = self.resource\n    self.resource = None\n    other.take_ownership(resource)","failure_mode":"Both owners attempt cleanup.","expected_error":"Double-release or ownership-state error.","cause":"Reference sharing without ownership transfer semantics.","root_cause":"Resource identity and ownership are conflated.","solution":"Clear ownership before assigning the resource to the new owner.","edge_case":"If transfer fails midway, define whether ownership remains with the source or moves atomically.","security_implication":"Transferred resources may contain credentials or sensitive data; ownership changes must preserve access controls.","performance_implication":"Transfer should not unnecessarily duplicate expensive resources.","test_case":"Transfer a resource and invoke cleanup from both old and new owners.","test_input":"One resource with observable release count.","expected_test_result":"Exactly one valid release occurs.","testing_guidance":"Test successful transfer, failed transfer, repeated transfer, and post-transfer use.","verification_method":"Lifecycle unit tests.","verification_guidance":"Assert ownership state before and after every transfer.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B057-R001","D35-B048-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B057-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Explicit ownership transfer"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Ownership-transfer and resource-lifecycle patterns"],"provenance":["Established resource ownership pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"EDGE-CASE"}

{"record_id":"D35-B057-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Ownership Implementation","subtopic":"Cleanup ordering","concept":"Resources with dependencies should be released in an order that respects dependency relationships.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why does cleanup ordering matter?","answer":"A dependent resource may require its parent resource to remain available during its own cleanup, so teardown order should generally reverse acquisition order when dependencies are nested.","explanation":"Closing a shared transport before its consumers can cause cleanup failures or incomplete flushing.","principle":"Teardown must respect resource dependency direction.","objective":"Implement deterministic and safe resource shutdown.","requirements":["Acquisition graph","Teardown order","Failure-tolerant cleanup"],"constraints":["Do not release prerequisites before dependents unless explicitly supported"],"preconditions":["Resource dependencies are known"],"input":"Resource dependency graph.","output":"Ordered cleanup sequence.","expected_behavior":"Dependent resources finish cleanup before their required parent resources are released.","incorrect_code":"database.close()\ntransaction.close()","corrected_code":"transaction.close()\ndatabase.close()","failure_mode":"Dependent resource cleanup runs after its prerequisite is unavailable.","expected_error":"Cleanup exception or incomplete flush.","cause":"Teardown order violates dependency direction.","root_cause":"Resource dependency graph was not reflected in shutdown logic.","solution":"Release resources in reverse dependency order.","edge_case":"Independent resources can be cleaned in parallel if their ownership and thread-safety semantics permit it.","security_implication":"Cleanup failures can leave sensitive resources open longer than intended.","performance_implication":"Parallel cleanup can shorten shutdown but introduces coordination complexity.","test_case":"Create a parent resource and dependent child and record close order.","test_input":"Nested resource lifecycle.","expected_test_result":"Child closes before parent.","testing_guidance":"Test cleanup after success, failure, and partial initialization.","verification_method":"Lifecycle integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Assert observed cleanup ordering rather than only checking final state.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B008-R001","D35-B057-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B057-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Dependency-aware resource teardown"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Resource dependency and reverse-teardown patterns"],"provenance":["Established resource lifecycle practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B057-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Resource Ownership Implementation","subtopic":"Partial initialization cleanup","concept":"A component that acquires multiple resources must clean up resources already acquired when a later acquisition fails.","knowledge_type":"INVALID IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What is partial initialization cleanup?","answer":"If initialization acquires resources sequentially and a later acquisition fails, previously acquired resources must be released before the initialization failure escapes.","explanation":"Without rollback of already-acquired resources, startup failures can leak descriptors, sockets, locks, or temporary files.","principle":"Failed initialization must release resources acquired before the failure point.","objective":"Prevent resource leaks during partial construction.","requirements":["Acquisition tracking","Failure cleanup","Idempotent cleanup where appropriate"],"constraints":["Do not attempt to close resources that were never acquired","Do not mask the original initialization failure unnecessarily"],"preconditions":["Initialization acquires multiple resources"],"input":"Sequential resource acquisition.","output":"Fully initialized object or cleaned failure.","expected_behavior":"Any initialization failure leaves acquired resources released according to their ownership contracts.","incorrect_code":"a = acquire_a()\nb = acquire_b()\nc = acquire_c()","corrected_code":"a = b = c = None\ntry:\n    a = acquire_a()\n    b = acquire_b()\n    c = acquire_c()\nexcept Exception:\n    for resource in (c, b, a):\n        if resource is not None:\n            resource.close()\n    raise","failure_mode":"Earlier resources remain allocated after later acquisition fails.","expected_error":"Original acquisition exception with no leaked resources.","cause":"No partial cleanup path.","root_cause":"Initialization assumes all acquisitions succeed.","solution":"Track ownership and clean up in reverse order on failure.","edge_case":"Cleanup itself can fail; define whether cleanup errors are logged, aggregated, or replace the original error.","security_implication":"Partial initialization can leave sensitive handles or temporary data accessible after startup failure.","performance_implication":"Leak prevention protects long-running process stability and startup retries.","test_case":"Force the third acquisition to fail.","test_input":"Successful first two acquisitions followed by injected third failure.","expected_test_result":"First two resources are released and the original failure remains observable.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at every acquisition position.","verification_method":"Fault-injection lifecycle tests.","verification_guidance":"Measure release counts and preserve the original failure identity where required.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B008-R001","D35-B057-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B057-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Partial initialization cleanup"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Exception-safe resource acquisition patterns"],"provenance":["Established resource-lifecycle practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","implementation_coverage":"INVALID IMPLEMENTATION"}

