{"record_id":"D35-B058-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Boundary Contract Implementation","subtopic":"Contract enforcement","concept":"A boundary contract should be enforced at the point where data enters or leaves an architectural component.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Where should architectural boundary contracts be enforced?","answer":"Enforce the contract at the boundary closest to the external or inter-module transition so invalid data cannot silently propagate inward.","explanation":"Validating only deep inside business logic allows invalid states to cross multiple layers and makes failures harder to localize.","principle":"Reject invalid boundary states as close as practical to their source.","objective":"Prevent contract violations from propagating through the architecture.","requirements":["Explicit contract","Boundary validator","Clear failure semantics"],"constraints":["Do not duplicate identical validation unnecessarily across every layer","Do not trust already-invalid external data"],"preconditions":["Boundary contract is defined"],"input":"Boundary payload.","output":"Validated internal representation or boundary failure.","expected_behavior":"Only contract-compliant values enter the internal boundary.","incorrect_code":"def process(payload):\n    # assumes caller already validated\n    return domain.process(payload)","corrected_code":"def process(payload):\n    validated = Contract.parse(payload)\n    return domain.process(validated)","failure_mode":"Invalid external data reaches internal logic.","expected_error":"ContractValidationError.","cause":"Boundary assumes upstream validation.","root_cause":"Contract enforcement responsibility is ambiguous.","solution":"Make the boundary responsible for validating its own incoming contract.","edge_case":"Internal trusted boundaries may use stronger type guarantees, but trust assumptions must be explicit.","security_implication":"Boundary validation is necessary for 