{"record_id":"D35-B058-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Boundary Contract Implementation","subtopic":"Contract ownership","concept":"Every boundary contract should have an explicit owner responsible for defining semantics and approving breaking changes.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why does a boundary contract need an owner?","answer":"Without ownership, incompatible changes can be introduced without understanding consumer impact or migration requirements.","explanation":"The technical contract is also a coordination mechanism between components and versions.","principle":"Stable contracts require explicit governance ownership.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled contract drift.","requirements":["Contract owner","Consumer identification","Change policy"],"constraints":["No breaking change without compatibility analysis","No undocumented contract mutation"],"preconditions":["Multiple components depend on boundary"],"input":"Contract change proposal.","output":"Approved compatible contract change or migration plan.","expected_behavior":"Changes identify affected consumers and compatibility impact before release.","incorrect_code":"# silently add incompatible field semantics\npayload["status"] = 7","corrected_code":"# define and version the contract before changing semantics\nSTATUS_V2 = {"pending": 0, "ready": 1, "failed": 2}","failure_mode":"Consumers interpret the same field differently after deployment.","expected_error":"Contract compatibility test failure.","cause":"Semantic contract changed without versioning or migration.","root_cause":"Contract ownership and change governance were absent.","solution":"Assign ownership and require compatibility analysis for changes.","edge_case":"Additive optional fields may be compatible under one serialization policy but incompatible under strict consumers.","security_implication":"Contract changes can accidentally expose new sensitive fields.","performance_implication":"Additional fields can increase serialization and transport costs; measure where relevant.","test_case":"Change a field's semantics and run consumer compatibility tests.","test_input":"Old consumer with new provider output.","expected_test_result":"The change is detected as incompatible unless a documented compatibility mechanism exists.","testing_guidance":"Maintain producer-consumer compatibility matrices.","verification_method":"Contract tests and change review.","verification_guidance":"Verify semantic as well as structural compatibility.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python environment if code contract tests are used","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_API_GOVERNANCE_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B045-R001","D35-B046-R001","D35-B058-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B045-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Contract ownership"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["API contract governance and compatibility practices"],"provenance":["Established API governance pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","implementation_coverage":"VERSION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B058-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Boundary Contract Implementation","subtopic":"Consumer-driven compatibility","concept":"Consumer expectations should be represented as executable contract tests where practical so provider changes can be verified against actual usage requirements.","knowledge_type":"TEST","question":"How can consumer expectations become executable?","answer":"Encode agreed provider behavior in contract tests that run against the provider implementation or a compatible test deployment.","explanation":"Documentation alone can drift from actual consumer requirements; executable contracts provide regression evidence.","principle":"Important integration assumptions should be executable when practical.","objective":"Detect provider changes that violate consumer-visible behavior.","requirements":["Consumer contract","Provider test target","Automated execution"],"constraints":["Tests should represent supported contract, not undocumented accidental behavior","Do not claim tests passed unless executed"],"preconditions":["Provider-consumer contract is defined"],"input":"Contract test suite and provider implementation.","output":"Compatibility test result.","expected_behavior":"Provider satisfies all mandatory consumer contract assertions.","incorrect_code":"def test_provider():\n    assert provider is not None","corrected_code":"def test_provider_contract(provider):\n    response = provider.get_customer("known-id")\n    assert response.status == "active"\n    assert response.customer_id == "known-id"","failure_mode":"Provider tests prove availability but not contract behavior.","expected_error":"Contract assertion failure when provider semantics drift.","cause":"Weak test oracle.","root_cause":"Consumer-visible requirements were not encoded.","solution":"Write assertions around stable externally observable behavior.","edge_case":"Consumer-specific assumptions may conflict; maintain one authoritative contract and explicitly document optional behaviors.","security_implication":"Contracts should test that unauthorized fields or actions remain unavailable.","performance_implication":"Contract tests can become expensive if they require full deployments; use layered contract suites.","test_case":"Run contract suite against current provider and an intentionally incompatible provider fixture.","test_input":"Compatible and incompatible implementations.","expected_test_result":"Current implementation passes required contract; incompatible implementation fails.","testing_guidance":"Keep contract tests deterministic and independent from incidental implementation details.","verification_method":"Automated contract testing.","verification_guidance":"Record actual execution results and environment metadata.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"pytest","framework_version":"Version unspecified","version_range":"Compatible pytest release","dependencies":["pytest"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment and provider test implementation","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B040-R005","D35-B058-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B058-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B058-R001"],"verifies":["Provider-consumer contract"],"derived_from":["Contract testing practice"],"provenance":["Established consumer/provider contract-testing pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","implementation_coverage":"TEST"}

{"record_id":"D35-B058-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Boundary Contract Implementation","subtopic":"Contract mismatch diagnostics","concept":"Boundary failures should expose enough structured information to identify contract mismatch without leaking sensitive payloads.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should a contract mismatch error contain?","answer":"It should identify the contract/version, failing field or rule where safe, and a correlation reference, while excluding sensitive payload content.","explanation":"Actionable diagnostics reduce debugging time without turning validation errors into a data-exfiltration channel.","principle":"Diagnostic detail should be useful, bounded, and safe.","objective":"Make contract failures diagnosable without exposing sensitive input.","requirements":["Error code/type","Contract identifier","Safe field information","Correlation reference"],"constraints":["No raw secret/password/token values","No unrestricted payload echo"],"preconditions":["Structured error taxonomy exists"],"input":"Contract validation failure.","output":"Safe structured failure.","expected_behavior":"Operators can identify the violated contract and locate the request without receiving sensitive payload contents.","incorrect_code":"raise ValueError(f"Invalid payload: {payload}")","corrected_code":"raise ContractValidationError(code="INVALID_CUSTOMER", contract="customer.v2", correlation_id=correlation_id)","failure_mode":"Validation failure logs or returns complete sensitive payload.","expected_error":"Structured ContractValidationError.","cause":"Exception message interpolates entire payload.","root_cause":"Diagnostics are not separated from data content.","solution":"Use structured allow-listed diagnostic fields.","edge_case":"Field names themselves can be sensitive in unusual schemas and may require redaction.","security_implication":"Error responses are attacker-visible in many systems and should not expose internal or sensitive information.","performance_implication":"Structured error construction should avoid serializing large invalid payloads.","test_case":"Trigger validation with secret-bearing payload fields.","test_input":"Invalid payload containing password-like values.","expected_test_result":"Error contains contract metadata but not secret values.","testing_guidance":"Automate checks for sensitive-value leakage in error bodies and logs.","verification_method":"Security regression tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect both external response and internal telemetry paths.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B025-R001","D35-B030-R001","D35-B041-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B025-R001"],"requires":["D35-B030-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Safe contract mismatch diagnostics"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Secure error handling and logging practices"],"provenance":["OWASP secure error-handling/logging guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

{"record_id":"D35-B058-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Boundary Contract Implementation","subtopic":"Contract regression verification","concept":"Contract verification should be repeated when schema, implementation, dependencies, or version boundaries change.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"When should boundary contract tests run?","answer":"They should run as part of relevant CI/regression workflows whenever contract definitions, serializers, adapters, providers, or compatibility-sensitive dependencies change.","explanation":"A previously verified contract can become invalid after an unrelated-looking implementation or dependency change.","principle":"Verification evidence is version-specific and must be refreshed after relevant change.","objective":"Prevent stale compatibility evidence.","requirements":["Change detection","Contract test suite","Versioned evidence"],"constraints":["Do not reuse old test results as proof for changed code without revalidation"],"preconditions":["Contract tests exist"],"input":"Changed component and contract suite.","output":"New verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Relevant contract tests execute against the changed implementation.","incorrect_code":"# CI skips contract tests after serializer change\nrun_unit_tests_only()","corrected_code":"run_unit_tests()\nrun_contract_tests()\nrun_integration_tests_for_changed_boundary()","failure_mode":"Contract-breaking change reaches release despite unrelated unit tests passing.","expected_error":"CI verification gap.","cause":"Regression suite does not include affected contract tests.","root_cause":"Change-to-verification dependency is not modeled.","solution":"Associate boundary changes with mandatory contract verification.","edge_case":"A dependency update may alter behavior without source changes in the boundary module.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive contract changes require regression tests for both allowed and denied behavior.","