{"record_id":"D35-B059-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"State Persistence Implementation","subtopic":"State snapshot boundary","concept":"Persistent state should be represented through an explicit serialization and restoration boundary rather than directly persisting arbitrary in-memory objects.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should application state be persisted safely?","answer":"Define a versioned state representation, serialize only supported fields, validate restored data, and make restoration failures explicit.","explanation":"Arbitrary object serialization couples persistence to runtime implementation details and can create compatibility and security problems.","principle":"Persisted state is a versioned data contract, not an object-memory dump.","objective":"Make state persistence durable, portable, and verifiable.","requirements":["Explicit state schema","Version identifier","Validation on restore","Migration policy"],"constraints":["No arbitrary executable object deserialization","No persistence of transient runtime state unless intended"],"preconditions":["State model is defined"],"input":"Application state representation.","output":"Validated persisted snapshot.","expected_behavior":"Persisted state can be restored only when it satisfies the supported schema/version contract.","incorrect_code":"import pickle\nblob = pickle.dumps(runtime_object)","corrected_code":"import json\nsnapshot = {\n    "schema_version": 1,\n    "state": {"status": runtime_object.status}\n}\nblob = json.dumps(snapshot).encode("utf-8")","failure_mode":"Runtime object representation becomes an implicit persistence contract.","expected_error":"SchemaValidationError or migration error.","cause":"Arbitrary object serialization.","root_cause":"Persistence boundary does not define a stable data representation.","solution":"Use explicit versioned data structures and safe serialization.","edge_case":"Some trusted internal persistence systems may