{"record_id":"D35-B059-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"State Persistence Implementation","subtopic":"State restoration validation","concept":"Restored state must be validated against current domain and architectural invariants before becoming active runtime state.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"Why must persisted state be validated during restoration?","answer":"Persisted data can be old, corrupted, incomplete, or incompatible; validation prevents invalid state from entering the live system.","explanation":"Successful parsing only proves that the serialization format was readable, not that the resulting state is semantically valid.","principle":"Deserialization and semantic validation are separate verification stages.","objective":"Prevent invalid persisted state from contaminating runtime behavior.","requirements":["Schema validation","Domain invariant validation","Activation gate"],"constraints":["Do not activate partially validated state","Do not equate parse success with correctness"],"preconditions":["Current state invariants are defined"],"input":"Decoded persisted state.","output":"Validated active state or restoration failure.","expected_behavior":"Only state satisfying all required invariants becomes active.","incorrect_code":"state = json.loads(blob)\napplication.state = state","corrected_code":"state = json.loads(blob)\nvalidated = StateSchema.validate(state)\ndomain.validate_invariants(validated)\napplication.state = validated","failure_mode":"Malformed or semantically invalid state becomes active.","expected_error":"ValidationError or domain invariant error.","cause":"Restoration stops after decoding.","root_cause":"Semantic verification is missing from persistence boundary.","solution":"Validate structure and domain invariants before activation.","edge_case":"Some state fields may be safely reconstructed rather than persisted; define canonical reconstruction rules.","security_implication":"Tampered state can alter authorization or operational behavior if integrity and validation are absent.","performance_implication":"Validation adds restoration cost but is normally preferable to runtime corruption.","test_case":"Restore state violating a required invariant.","test_input":"Structurally valid but semantically invalid snapshot.","expected_test_result":"Activation is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Test structural corruption and semantic invalidity separately.","verification_method":"Restoration integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Assert active state remains unchanged when restoration fails.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library or chosen validation library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B005-R001","D35-B059-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B005-R001","D35-B059-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B059-R001"],"verifies":["Persisted-state invariants"],"derived_from":["State validation and invariant enforcement patterns"],"provenance":["Established persistence verification practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"VERIFICATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B059-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"State Persistence Implementation","subtopic":"Atomic snapshot write","concept":"A persisted state snapshot should be written atomically when partial files could leave the application with an invalid or ambiguous state.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a local state snapshot be written atomically?","answer":"Write the complete snapshot to a temporary file, flush according to durability requirements, and atomically replace the target file.","explanation":"Directly overwriting the active file can leave truncated data after process or system failure.","principle":"Persistent state transitions should avoid exposing partial representations.","objective":"Prevent corrupted active snapshots during writes.","requirements":["Temporary file","Complete write","Atomic replacement","Defined durability policy"],"constraints":["Atomic rename semantics depend on filesystem/platform","Durability requires explicit fsync policy where necessary"],"preconditions":["Filesystem supports required atomic replacement semantics"],"input":"Complete serialized snapshot.","output":"Either previous valid snapshot or complete new snapshot.","expected_behavior":"Readers never observe an intentionally partial target file under the supported filesystem semantics.","incorrect_code":"with open(path, "w") as f:\n    f.write(data)","corrected_code":"from pathlib import Path\n\np = Path(path)\ntmp = p.with_suffix(p.suffix + ".tmp")\ntmp.write_text(data, encoding="utf-8")\ntmp.replace(p)","failure_mode":"Process interruption leaves a truncated active snapshot.","expected_error":"Restoration validation failure or missing state.","cause":"Target file is overwritten directly.","root_cause":"Persistence update is not atomic at the file boundary.","solution":"Use temporary write plus atomic replacement and define durability requirements.","edge_case":"A crash during temporary-file write or rename still requires startup recovery policy.","security_implication":"Atomic replacement should preserve appropriate file permissions and avoid exposing temporary sensitive files.","performance_implication":"Temporary writes add filesystem operations but can materially improve recovery correctness.","test_case":"Inject failure before replacement and inspect active snapshot.","test_input":"Write operation interrupted after temporary file creation.","expected_test_result":"Previous active snapshot remains intact and temporary artifact follows cleanup/recovery policy.","testing_guidance":"Test interrupted write, concurrent readers, permission failure, and disk-full scenarios.","verification_method":"Filesystem integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual platform semantics in the deployment environment.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library pathlib"],"execution_requirements":"Filesystem supporting atomic replacement semantics","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_FILE_SYSTEM_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B034-R001","D35-B059-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B034-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Atomic state snapshot persistence"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Atomic file replacement pattern"],"provenance":["Established filesystem atomic-replacement practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"IMPLEMENTATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B059-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"State Persistence Implementation","subtopic":"State corruption recovery","concept":"State persistence architecture should define what happens when the current snapshot is unreadable, invalid, or integrity-inconsistent.","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should an application do when persisted state is corrupted?","answer":"Detect corruption, preserve diagnostic evidence, avoid activating invalid state, and follow an explicit recovery policy such as a known-good snapshot, reconstruction, or controlled startup failure.","explanation":"Silently substituting defaults can destroy state semantics and hide serious integrity failures.","principle":"Corruption should produce an explicit recovery decision, not an accidental reset.","objective":"Recover safely from invalid persisted state.","requirements":["Corruption detection","Recovery source","Integrity policy","Operator-visible status"],"constraints":["No silent data loss","No activation of unverified fallback state"],"preconditions":["Recovery options are defined"],"input":"Invalid or unreadable snapshot.","output":"Recovered verified state or controlled failure.","expected_behavior":"System follows documented recovery policy and records the outcome.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    state = load(path)\nexcept Exception:\n    state = default_state()","corrected_code":"try:\n    state = load_and_validate(path)\nexcept StateCorruptionError as exc:\n    audit_recovery(exc)\n    state = load_known_good_snapshot_and_validate()","failure_mode":"Corruption silently resets application state.","expected_error":"StateCorruptionError or controlled recovery failure.","cause":"Broad exception fallback to defaults.","root_cause":"Recovery semantics were not distinguished from ordinary startup defaults.","solution":"Classify corruption and use explicit recovery sources.","edge_case":"No known-good snapshot may exist; controlled start