{"record_id":"D35-B059-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"State Persistence Implementation","subtopic":"Persistence verification","concept":"Persistence correctness requires round-trip, corruption, compatibility, and recovery verification rather than merely successful serialization.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"What should state persistence verification cover?","answer":"Verify round-trip equivalence, schema compatibility, invariant restoration, interrupted writes, corruption detection, and recovery behavior.","explanation":"A serializer can successfully write and read data while still losing fields, changing semantics, or accepting unsafe state.","principle":"Persistence verification must cover the full lifecycle from write through recovery.","objective":"Establish evidence for durable-state correctness.","requirements":["Round-trip tests","Migration tests","Corruption tests","Recovery tests"],"constraints":["Do not claim persistence durability from unit serialization tests alone","Environment-specific filesystem behavior must be tested where relevant"],"preconditions":["Persistence contract is defined"],"input":"Persistence implementation and scenario matrix.","output":"Versioned verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"All mandatory persistence invariants hold across supported lifecycle scenarios.","incorrect_code":"def test_save_load():\n    save(state)\n    assert load() is not None","corrected_code":"def test_round_trip_preserves_contract():\n    save(state)\n    restored = load_and_validate()\n    assert restored == expected_state\n\ndef test_corrupt_snapshot_is_rejected():\n    corrupt_storage()\n    with pytest.raises(StateCorruptionError):\n        load_and_validate()","failure_mode":"Tests prove only that something can be loaded.","expected_error":"Assertion or validation failure for lost/invalid state.","cause":"Weak verification oracle.","root_cause":"Persistence semantics are not encoded in tests.","solution":