{"record_id":"D35-B060-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation Boundary Governance","subtopic":"Capability exposure","concept":"An implementation boundary should expose only the capabilities required by its consumer rather than passing broad infrastructure objects through the architecture.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should implementation boundaries expose narrow capabilities?","answer":"Narrow capability interfaces reduce coupling, simplify testing, limit accidental authority, and make architectural dependencies explicit.","explanation":"Passing broad objects such as database connections, application containers, or service locators allows downstream code to bypass intended boundaries.","principle":"Least capability is both an architectural and security property.","objective":"Reduce coupling and prevent boundary bypass.","requirements":["Narrow interface","Explicit capability list","Consumer-specific contract"],"constraints":["Do not expose generic service locators as universal access","Do not use interface narrowing to hide required semantics"],"preconditions":["Consumer requirements are known"],"input":"Consumer dependency requirements.","output":"Minimal capability interface.","expected_behavior":"Consumer can perform required work without access to unrelated infrastructure.","incorrect_code":"class Service:\n    def init(self, application_container):\n        self.container = application_container","corrected_code":"class Service:\n    def init(self, customer_repository, authorizer):\n        self.customer_repository = customer_repository\n        self.authorizer = authorizer","failure_mode":"Service gains unrestricted access to infrastructure.","expected_error":"Architecture fitness violation.","cause":"Broad container dependency.","root_cause":"Capability requirements were not modeled explicitly.","solution":"Inject only required capabilities.","edge_case":"A shared capability may expose multiple operations; keep the interface cohesive rather than artificially splitting every method.","security_implication":"Narrow interfaces reduce the authority available to compromised or buggy components.","performance_implication":"Smaller interfaces can reduce accidental expensive operations but do not inherently guarantee performance gains.","test_case":"Inspect service dependencies and attempt access to unrelated infrastructure.","test_input":"Service with narrow injected interfaces.","expected_test_result":"Required operations are available; unrelated infrastructure is inaccessible.","testing_guidance":"Use architecture tests to reject forbidden broad dependencies.","verification_method":"Static architecture checks plus unit tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify dependency graphs against declared capability boundaries.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B003-R001","D35-B040-R001","D35-B056-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B003-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Least-capability implementation boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Dependency inversion and least-privilege principles"],"provenance":["Established dependency architecture and least-privilege security principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":"SECURITY"}

