{"record_id":"D35-B060-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation Boundary Governance","subtopic":"Boundary change impact","concept":"Changes to an implementation boundary should identify affected callers, adapters, tests, contracts, and operational dependencies before modification is merged.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should architecture-to-implementation boundary changes be assessed?","answer":"Trace the boundary's incoming and outgoing dependencies, update affected contracts and adapters, and run the verification suites associated with the dependency graph.","explanation":"Changing a central interface can affect many components even when the source edit is small.","principle":"Change impact follows dependency relationships rather than line count.","objective":"Prevent incomplete boundary migrations.","requirements":["Dependency graph","Impact analysis","Targeted regression tests"],"constraints":["Do not assume small diffs have small architectural impact","Do not remove compatibility adapters without consumer evidence"],"preconditions":["Boundary dependencies are discoverable"],"input":"Boundary change proposal.","output":"Impact analysis and verification plan.","expected_behavior":"All materially affected components are identified and verified.","incorrect_code":"rename_interface()\nrun_unit_tests_for_changed_module_only()","corrected_code":"affected = dependency_graph.reverse_dependencies("CustomerRepository")\nrun_contract_tests(affected)\nrun_integration_tests(affected)","failure_mode":"Consumers break because only the changed module was tested.","expected_error":"Downstream contract or integration test failure.","cause":"Impact analysis ignored reverse dependencies.","root_cause":"Verification was scoped by file instead of architecture.","solution":"Use dependency-aware impact analysis.","edge_case":"Dynamic runtime dependencies may not be visible to static analysis and require integration coverage.","securi