{"record_id":"D35-B060-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation Boundary Governance","subtopic":"Architecture implementation closure","concept":"An architecture-to-implementation boundary is complete only when its contract, implementation, failure behavior, security properties, tests, and verification evidence are aligned.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"When can an architecture implementation boundary be considered complete?","answer":"Completion requires evidence that the documented contract is implemented, invalid paths are handled, relevant security constraints hold, tests cover required behavior, and verification status is truthful.","explanation":"Source code presence alone does not establish that an architectural boundary works correctly or safely.","principle":"Implementation completeness is evidence-based, not file-based.","objective":"Define a rigorous closure criterion for architecture-to-implementation work.","requirements":["Contract coverage","Implementation coverage","Invalid-path coverage","Security review","Testing evidence","Verification evidence"],"constraints":["No claim of verified behavior without execution or appropriate evidence","Known limitations must remain recorded"],"preconditions":["Architecture contract and implementation scope are defined"],"input":"Architecture specification plus implementation and evidence set.","output":"Complete, partial, or unverified implementation status.","expected_behavior":"Boundary is marked complete only when mandatory coverage gates are satisfied.","incorrect_code":"status = "complete" if implementation_file.exists() else "missing"","corrected_code":"status = evaluate_gates(\n    contract_covered=True,\n    implementation_covered=True,\n    invalid_paths_tested=True,\n    security_reviewed=True,\n    verification_evidence_present=True,\n)","failure_mode":"Implementation is marked complete because source files exist.","expected_error":"Closure-gate failure.","cause":"Completion criterion is based on artifact presence rather than evidence.","root_cause":"Architecture implementation lacks explicit quality gates.","solution":"Use structured implementation, testing, security, and verification gates.","edge_case":"Some boundaries may be intentionally unverified because execution dependencies are unavailable; mark them UNVERIFIED rather than complete.","security_implication":"Security-critical boundaries require explicit security evidence before being considered complete.","performance_implication":"Performance verification is required when the architecture specifies measurable performance constraints; otherwise it should remain not applicable rather than fabricated.","test_case":"Evaluate one fully verified boundary and one implementation-only boundary.","test_input":"Two evidence sets with different coverage.","expected_test_result":"Only the fully evidenced boundary reaches COMPLETE; the other remains PARTIAL or UNVERIFIED.","testing_guidance":"Use a machine-readable closure checklist and prevent manual status inflation.","verification_method":"Automated completeness gate plus human review for unresolved architectural questions.","verification_guidance":"Every mandatory gate must have linked evidence or an explicit NOT_APPLICABLE/UNVERIFIED status.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Dataset/code analysis environment","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_GOVERNANCE","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B040-R005","D35-B058-R005","D35-B060-R002","D35-B060-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R005"],"requires":["D35-B060-R002"],"extends":["D35-B040-R005"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Architecture implementation closure gate"],"tests":[],"verifies":["Architecture implementation completeness"],"derived_from":["Evidence-based software verification and architecture fitness practices"],"provenance":["Established verification-gate pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","implementation_coverage":"VERIFICATION"}

{"record_id":"D35-B061-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture Implementation","subtopic":"Event Dispatcher","concept":"An event dispatcher routes an already-created event to the handlers registered for its event type while keeping dispatch policy explicit and testable.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an event dispatcher be implemented at an architectural boundary?","answer":"Define a typed event contract, maintain explicit event-type-to-handler registration, resolve handlers deterministically, invoke them according to documented delivery semantics, and isolate dispatch infrastructure from domain event definitions.","explanation":"The dispatcher is an infrastructure/application boundary rather than the business rule itself. It should not silently invent ordering, retries, persistence, or delivery guarantees. Those guarantees must be represented explicitly by the surrounding architecture.","principle":"Make event routing explicit, deterministic, and independent of business logic.","objective":"Translate an event-driven architecture into a maintainable executable dispatch boundary.","requirements":["Typed event contract","Explicit handler registration","Deterministic resolution","Defined failure semantics","Testable dispatch lifecycle"],"constraints":["Do not assume durable delivery unless persistence exists","Do not silently swallow handler failures","Do not introduce global mutable handler state"],"preconditions":["An event type is defined","At least one compatible handler can be registered"],"input":"Domain or application event instance.","output":"Dispatch result or explicitly classified dispatch failure.","expected_behavior":"The dispatcher resolves the correct handlers and invokes them according to the documented dispatch contract.","incorrect_code":"handlers"event.class" (event)","corrected_code":"class EventDispatcher:\n    def init(self):\n        self._handlers = {}\n\n    def register(self, event_type, handler):\n        self._handlers.setdefault(event_type, []).append(handler)\n\n    def dispatch(self, event):\n        for handler in tuple(self._handlers.get(type(event), ())):\n            handler(event)","failure_mode":"Missing handler, incorrect type registration, handler exception, or accidental mutation during dispatch.","expected_error":"A defined dispatch or handler error should be propagated or classified according to the event delivery contract.","cause":"Routing and failure semantics are implicit or handler registration is incorrect.","root_cause":"The architecture does not distinguish event definition from dispatch infrastructure.","solution":"Introduce an explicit dispatcher with typed registration and documented failure behavior.","edge_case":"A handler registers another handler while an event is being dispatched; dispatch should use a defined snapshot or mutation policy.","security_implication":"Handlers must not receive events outside their authorized tenant or security context.","performance_implication":"Handler lookup should be bounded and registration should avoid repeated dynamic discovery during every dispatch.","test_case":"Register two handlers for one event and verify both receive the same event instance in documented order.","test_input":"Event E with handlers H1 and H2.","expected_test_result":"H1 and H2 are invoked according to the configured dispatch policy.","testing_guidance":"Test registration, missing handlers, multiple handlers, handler exceptions, ordering policy, and mutation during dispatch.","verification_method":"Static inspection plus unit tests for routing and failure behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no handler routing occurs through undocumented global state and that delivery guarantees match the surrounding architecture.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime for execution testing"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B015-R001","D35-B016-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B002-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":"D35-B015-R001","refines":"D35-B015-R001","specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Event dispatch boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Established event-driven architecture patterns"],"provenance":"Established event-driven architecture and messaging patterns; implementation example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B061-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture Implementation","subtopic":"Handler Registration","concept":"Handler registration is the explicit architectural mapping between an event type and one or more processing components.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should event handlers be registered explicitly rather than discovered implicitly?","answer":"Explicit registration makes dependencies, supported event types, startup validation, ordering policy, and testing observable.","explanation":"Implicit discovery can make architecture difficult to inspect and can create runtime failures when modules are omitted or incompatible handlers are loaded.","principle":"Architectural dependencies should be visible and verifiable.","objective":"Implement predictable handler registration.","requirements":["Explicit registration mechanism","Duplicate-registration policy","Type compatibility","Startup validation where appropriate"],"constraints":["Avoid hidden module scanning when deterministic composition is required","Do not permit incompatible handlers"],"preconditions":["Event and handler contracts exist"],"input":"Event type and compatible handler.","output":"Registered handler mapping.","expected_behavior":"Only compatible handlers become routable for the declared event type.","incorrect_code":"registry[event_name] = handler","corrected_code":"class HandlerRegistry:\n    def init(self):\n        self._items = {}\n\n    def register(self, event_type, handler):\n        if not callable(handler):\n            raise TypeError("handler must be callable")\n        self._items.setdefault(event_type, []).append(handler)","failure_mode":"Incorrect registration, accidental overwrite, incompatible handler, or duplicate registration.","expected_error":"TypeError for invalid handler or an explicitly defined registration error for prohibited duplicates.","cause":"Registration lacks contract validation.","root_cause":"The registry is treated as a generic dictionary instead of an architectural boundary.","solution":"Wrap registration in a typed registry with explicit duplicate and compatibility policies.","edge_case":"The same handler is registered twice; behavior must be explicitly defined rather than accidental.","security_implication":"Registration should not allow untrusted code to introduce handlers into a privileged execution path.","performance_implication":"Registration occurs infrequently; optimize for deterministic validation rather than micro-optimizing lookup.","test_case":"Register a valid handler and an invalid non-callable value.","test_input":"Valid callable and integer 42.","expected_test_result":"Valid registration succeeds and invalid registration raises the defined error.","testing_guidance":"Test valid registration, duplicates, incompatible handlers, empty registry, and startup validation.","verification_method":"Unit testing plus composition-root inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify every production event handler has an intentional registration path.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.95,"related_records":["D35-B008-R001","D35-B061-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B061-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":null,"refines":"D35-B061-R001","specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Event handler registration boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Dependency composition and event-driven architecture patterns"],"provenance":"Established architecture pattern; code is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B061-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture Implementation","subtopic":"Event Ordering","concept":"Event ordering must be an explicit delivery property rather than an accidental consequence of collection iteration or thread scheduling.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should event ordering be represented in implementation?","answer":"Define the required ordering scope, preserve sequence metadata where required, and implement ordering only within the boundary that actually guarantees it.","explanation":"Global ordering is expensive and often unnecessary. Many architectures require ordering only per aggregate, key, partition, stream, or workflow. The implementation should encode that scope rather than claiming universal ordering.","principle":"Guarantees must be no stronger than the mechanism that enforces them.","objective":"Prevent accidental or overstated event-ordering guarantees.","requirements":["Ordering scope","Sequence or ordering key where required","Defined behavior for missing or duplicate sequence values"],"constraints":["Do not claim global ordering from an unordered collection","Do not reorder events without a documented policy"],"preconditions":["Events have a defined relationship requiring ordering"],"input":"Events with ordering metadata.","output":"Ordered processing sequence or explicit ordering failure.","expected_behavior":"Events are processed according to the documented ordering scope.","incorrect_code":"for event in set(events):\n    dispatcher.dispatch(event)","corrected_code":"events = sorted(events, key=lambda event: event.sequence)\nfor event in events:\n    dispatcher.dispatch(event)","failure_mode":"Out-of-order processing, duplicate sequence values, missing sequence values, or false global-order assumptions.","expected_error":"Explicit ordering validation error when required ordering metadata is missing or invalid.","cause":"Ordering is inferred from an unsuitable data structure or runtime scheduling behavior.","root_cause":"The architecture failed to specify ordering guarantees and scope.","solution":"Specify ordering semantics and enforce only the required scope.","edge_case":"Two events have the same sequence number; the system needs a deterministic tie policy or must reject the ambiguity.","security_implication":"Ordering must not permit stale authorization events to overwrite newer security state.","performance_implication":"Sorting large streams increases computational cost; partitioned ordering can reduce the scope.","test_case":"Provide events with sequence values 2, 1, and 3 and verify processing order.","test_input":"[E2,E1,E3].","expected_test_result":"Processing occurs as E1, E2, E3 when sequence ordering is required.","testing_guidance":"Test out-of-order input, duplicate sequences, missing sequence metadata, and concurrent delivery assumptions.","verification_method":"Deterministic unit tests and architecture-contract review.","verification_guidance":"Verify the implementation does not infer stronger ordering than the transport or persistence layer guarantees.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B014-R001","D35-B016-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Defined ordering contract"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Event ordering policy","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Ordered event-stream processing patterns"],"provenance":"Established distributed and event-processing architecture principles; example not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","PERFORMANCE","VERIFICATION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B061-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture Implementation","subtopic":"Handler Failure Isolation","concept":"Independent event handlers should have explicitly defined failure isolation so one handler cannot unintentionally corrupt unrelated processing.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should handler failures be isolated?","answer":"Define whether handlers execute sequentially or independently, classify failures, and ensure transaction and retry boundaries prevent one failure from producing undefined side effects in unrelated handlers.","explanation":"Failure isolation depends on delivery semantics. Sequential handlers can provide deterministic ordering but couple failures. Independent execution can improve isolation but requires explicit concurrency, retry, and observability policies.","principle":"Failure boundaries must align with transaction and delivery boundaries.","objective":"Make handler failure behavior deterministic and recoverable.","requirements":["Failure classification","Defined transaction scope","Retry policy where applicable","Failure observability"],"constraints":["Do not silently continue after a critical invariant failure","Do not retry non-idempotent handlers without a safety mechanism"],"preconditions":["Handlers and event delivery semantics are defined"],"input":"Event and handler execution result.","output":"Successful processing or classified handler failure.","expected_behavior":"A handler failure follows the documented isolation and recovery policy.","incorrect_code":"for handler in handlers:\n    try:\n        handler(event)\n    except Exception:\n        pass","corrected_code":"for handler in handlers:\n    try:\n        handler(event)\n    except Exception as exc:\n        failures.append((handler, exc))\nif failures:\n    raise EventHandlingError(failures)","failure_mode":"Exception swallowing, partial processing, uncontrolled retry, or inconsistent state.","expected_error":"A classified EventHandlingError containing handler failures.","cause":"Failure policy is absent or exceptions are discarded.","root_cause":"Delivery semantics and handler transaction boundaries are unspecified.","solution":"Classify handler failures and explicitly define whether processing stops, continues, retries, or compensates.","edge_case":"One of five handlers fails after two previous handlers have committed side effects.","security_implication":"A security-critical handler failure must not be hidden by successful non-critical handlers.","performance_implication":"Independent handler execution may improve throughput but introduces coordination and consistency costs.","test_case":"Cause one handler to raise and verify the configured policy is applied.","test_input":"Three handlers where H2 raises RuntimeError.","expected_test_result":"The dispatcher returns or raises the documented aggregate failure without silently losing H2's failure.","testing_guidance":"Test single failure, multiple failures, retryable/non-retryable classification, and partial side effects.","verification_method":"Failure-injection tests and transaction-boundary inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that failure behavior is explicit and observable.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B047-R001","D35-B050-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B061-R001"],"requires":["Failure policy"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Handler failure isolation","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Fault-isolation and event-processing patterns"],"provenance":"Established reliability architecture pattern; code is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","INVALID IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B061-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture Implementation","subtopic":"Event Processing Verification","concept":"Event processing must be verified against routing, ordering, failure, duplication, and side-effect expectations rather than merely checking that a dispatcher returned.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"What should be verified in an event-driven implementation?","answer":"Verify event-to-handler routing, ordering guarantees, failure behavior, duplicate handling, side effects, authorization context, and observability according to the stated delivery contract.","explanation":"A dispatcher returning without error does not prove that event processing satisfied architectural requirements. Verification must test externally meaningful behavior and failure paths.","principle":"Verify delivery semantics and outcomes, not just invocation.","objective":"Establish evidence that event implementation satisfies its architecture contract.","requirements":["Behavioral tests","Failure-path tests","Duplicate-event tests where applicable","Security-context tests"],"constraints":["Do not equate successful invocation with successful business outcome","Do not claim exactly-once semantics without evidence"],"preconditions":["Delivery contract is specified"],"input":"Controlled event-processing scenarios.","output":"Verification results mapped to requirements.","expected_behavior":"Each stated delivery guarantee has a corresponding test or verification method.","incorrect_code":"assert dispatcher.dispatch(event) is None","corrected_code":"dispatcher.dispatch(event)\nassert handler.received == [event]\nassert state_after == expected_state","failure_mode":"False-positive tests that validate only invocation.","expected_error":"Assertion failure when routing or outcome does not satisfy the contract.","cause":"Tests observe internal calls rather than architectural outcomes.","root_cause":"Verification criteria were not derived from delivery requirements.","solution":"Map every delivery guarantee to observable tests and evidence.","edge_case":"The handler executes successfully but its transaction rolls back afterward.","security_implication":"Verification must confirm tenant and authorization context are preserved during event processing.","performance_implication":"High-volume event tests should validate resource behavior without being mistaken for production benchmarks.","test_case":"Process a valid event, duplicate it, inject a handler failure, and inspect final state.","test_input":"Valid event plus duplicate and injected failure scenarios.","expected_test_result":"The system behaves according to its documented delivery and idempotency semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic fixtures and failure injection; record execution evidence separately from illustrative examples.","verification_method":"Requirement-to-test traceability plus executed integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Mark unexecuted examples as illustrative and only mark requirements verified when corresponding evidence exists.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B018-R001","D35-B061-R001","D35-B061-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B061-R001"],"requires":["Defined delivery contract"],"extends":null,"refines":"D35-B061-R004","specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":null,"tests":"Event delivery implementation","verifies":"Event delivery requirements","derived_from":["Software verification and event-driven architecture practices"],"provenance":"Verification methodology is established practice; no execution was performed for this record.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","EDGE-CASE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B062-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Command Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Command Envelope","concept":"A command envelope carries the command payload together with execution metadata required by the application boundary.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What belongs in a command envelope?","answer":"A command envelope should contain a typed command payload and only the metadata required for routing, correlation, authorization context, idempotency, or tracing.","explanation":"The envelope separates transport and execution metadata from domain command data. It should remain stable and explicit rather than becoming an unrestricted metadata bag.","principle":"Keep application command data and transport metadata explicitly separated.","objective":"Implement a stable command dispatch boundary.","requirements":["Typed command payload","Command identifier","Correlation context where required","Explicit metadata policy"],"constraints":["Do not place arbitrary sensitive data into metadata","Do not duplicate authoritative business state in routing metadata"],"preconditions":["A command contract exists"],"input":"Command payload plus execution metadata.","output":"Immutable or effectively immutable command envelope.","expected_behavior":"The command reaches its handler with sufficient context and without hidden mutable dependencies.","incorrect_code":"command.meta = request.dict","corrected_code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass CommandEnvelope:\n    command: object\n    command_id: str\n    correlation_id: str | None = None","failure_mode":"Metadata leakage, mutable command state, missing identity, or hidden transport coupling.","expected_error":"Validation error for missing required envelope fields.","cause":"Transport request objects are passed directly into application dispatch.","root_cause":"No explicit command boundary exists.","solution":"Introduce a narrow command envelope and map external requests into it.","edge_case":"A command is retried with the same command_id but a different payload.","security_implication":"Authorization context must be validated and must not be trusted solely because it appears in client-supplied metadata.","performance_implication":"Envelope creation should be lightweight and should not perform external I/O.","test_case":"Create an envelope and attempt to mutate its command_id.","test_input":"CommandEnvelope(command=CreateUser(), command_id='c1').","expected_test_result":"Mutation is rejected because the envelope is frozen.","testing_guidance":"Test required metadata, immutability, duplicate IDs, correlation propagation, and sensitive metadata exclusion.","verification_method":"Unit tests and boundary inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify external transport data is mapped rather than directly reused as the command envelope.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B021-R001","D35-B023-R001","D35-B026-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Command contract"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Command envelope boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Command Bus and application-service patterns"],"provenance":"Established application architecture pattern; illustrative Python example not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","SECURITY","TEST","VERIFICATION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B062-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Command Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Handler Resolution","concept":"A command bus resolves one appropriate handler for a command type using an explicit registration policy.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should command handlers be resolved?","answer":"Use an explicit command-type-to-handler registry and reject missing or ambiguous mappings rather than selecting a handler through undocumented conventions.","explanation":"Commands normally represent directed requests and therefore should not accidentally fan out to multiple handlers. Explicit resolution catches architectural errors early.","principle":"A command should have an intentional execution target.","objective":"Implement deterministic command-to-handler resolution.","requirements":["Typed registry","Exactly-one-handler policy unless explicitly designed otherwise","Startup or dispatch validation"],"constraints":["No ambiguous fallback handler","No implicit name-based routing without a documented contract"],"preconditions":["Command handler implementations exist"],"input":"Command instance.","output":"Exactly one resolved handler.","expected_behavior":"The bus resolves the registered handler for the exact command type.","incorrect_code":"handler = handlers.get(command.class.name, default_handler)","corrected_code":"handler = handlers.get(type(command))\nif handler is None:\n    raise LookupError(f"No handler for {type(command).name}")","failure_mode":"Wrong handler, silent fallback, missing handler, or ambiguous registration.","expected_error":"LookupError for missing handler; explicit registration error for ambiguity.","cause":"Routing relies on names or generic fallback behavior.","root_cause":"Command dispatch is not modeled as a strict architectural boundary.","solution":"Use exact type-based registration with explicit resolution errors.","edge_case":"A subclass of a command exists; decide whether exact matching or polymorphic matching is supported.","security_implication":"A privileged command must never fall through to an unintended generic handler.","performance_implication":"Dictionary lookup by exact type is generally inexpensive; registration validation is preferable to dynamic discovery on every dispatch.","test_case":"Register handler H for command C and dispatch C.","test_input":"C()","expected_test_result":"H is selected exactly once.","testing_guidance":"Test exact match, missing handler, duplicate registration, subclass behavior, and unauthorized command paths.","verification_method":"Unit tests plus registry validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every command exposed by the application has exactly the intended handler mapping.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript runtime/toolchain for execution testing"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.95,"related_records":["D35-B062-R001","D35-B063-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Command registry"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Command handler resolution","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Command Bus architecture patterns"],"provenance":"Established application-dispatch pattern; TypeScript example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","SECURITY"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B062-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Command Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Dispatch Lifecycle","concept":"Command dispatch should expose a defined lifecycle from validation through handler invocation and result/error propagation.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What lifecycle should a command bus implement?","answer":"A typical lifecycle is validate envelope, resolve handler, establish execution context, invoke handler, classify result or failure, and return a defined outcome.","explanation":"The bus coordinates execution concerns without embedding the command's business rules. Each stage should be observable and independently testable.","principle":"Orchestration belongs in the dispatch boundary; business decisions belong in handlers/domain services.","objective":"Implement a predictable command execution lifecycle.","requirements":["Validation","Resolution","Context propagation","Invocation","Result/error classification"],"constraints":["No business-rule duplication in the bus","No hidden external I/O during handler resolution"],"preconditions":["Valid command contract and handler registry"],"input":"Command envelope.","output":"Handler result or classified command failure.","expected_behavior":"Each lifecycle stage executes once according to the command contract.","incorrect_code":"return handlers"type(command)" (command)","corrected_code":"def dispatch(envelope, registry):\n    validate(envelope)\n    handler = registry.resolve(type(envelope.command))\n    return handler.handle(envelope)","failure_mode":"Skipped validation, incorrect context, double dispatch, or swallowed handler errors.","expected_error":"Defined validation, resolution, or handler error.","cause":"Dispatch is implemented as a single opaque call.","root_cause":"Lifecycle responsibilities are not separated.","solution":"Model dispatch stages explicitly and test each boundary.","edge_case":"Handler returns a value that violates the declared command result contract.","security_implication":"Authorization context must be established before privileged handler execution.","performance_implication":"Avoid repeated validation or dependency resolution when safe and deterministic.","test_case":"Inject failures into validation, resolution, and handler invocation separately.","test_input":"Three dispatch scenarios with one failure at each stage.","expected_test_result":"Each failure is classified at the correct lifecycle boundary.","testing_guidance":"Use mocks/fakes only for boundary isolation; also include integration tests for actual composition.","verification_method":"Lifecycle-focused unit tests and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Trace command_id across each stage and confirm no stage is silently skipped.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Java 17+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JDK 17+ for execution testing"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B004-R001","D35-B062-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B062-R001","D35-B062-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Command dispatch lifecycle","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Application service and command bus patterns"],"provenance":"Established application architecture pattern; Java example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B062-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Command Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Command Middleware","concept":"Command middleware wraps dispatch with cross-cutting concerns such as authorization, tracing, validation, and transaction coordination.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How can command middleware be implemented without polluting command handlers?","answer":"Represent middleware as composable wrappers around a terminal handler invocation and define ordering explicitly.","explanation":"Cross-cutting concerns belong around the application dispatch boundary when they apply consistently across commands. Ordering is important because authentication, authorization, transaction, and observability may have dependencies.","principle":"Cross-cutting policies should be composable and ordered explicitly.","objective":"Implement command middleware as an architectural boundary.","requirements":["Composable middleware contract","Explicit ordering","Short-circuit capability","Exception propagation policy"],"constraints":["Middleware must not duplicate domain logic","Ordering must be documented"],"preconditions":["Command dispatch contract exists"],"input":"Command and terminal handler.","output":"Command result or middleware/handler failure.","expected_behavior":"Middleware executes in defined order and can short-circuit when policy requires.","incorrect_code":"def dispatch(command):\n    auth(command)\n    log(command)\n    return handler(command)","corrected_code":"def with_auth(next_handler):\n    def wrapped(command):\n        authorize(command)\n        return next_handler(command)\n    return wrapped\n\npipeline = with_auth(handler)","failure_mode":"Incorrect ordering, bypassed middleware, recursion errors, or swallowed exceptions.","expected_error":"Policy-specific authorization or validation error, or propagated handler failure.","cause":"Cross-cutting behavior is hard-coded into individual handlers.","root_cause":"No composable dispatch pipeline exists.","solution":"Implement a defined middleware/decorator pipeline.","edge_case":"Two middleware components both attempt to translate the same exception.","security_implication":"Authorization middleware must execute before privileged side effects.","performance_implication":"Middleware adds call overhead; keep wrappers bounded and avoid unnecessary allocations.","test_case":"Compose logging and authorization middleware and verify authorization executes before handler invocation.","test_input":"Authorized and unauthorized commands.","expected_test_result":"Authorized command reaches handler; unauthorized command is short-circuited.","testing_guidance":"Test ordering, short-circuiting, exception propagation, duplicate middleware, and bypass attempts.","verification_method":"Pipeline unit tests and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify all command entry points use the intended pipeline.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B038-R001","D35-B027-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B062-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":"D35-B038-R001","refines":null,"specializes":"Command dispatch middleware","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Command middleware pipeline","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Middleware and decorator architecture patterns"],"provenance":"Established middleware pattern; illustrative code not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE","VERIFICATION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B062-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Command Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Dispatch Verification","concept":"A command bus is verified by proving correct handler selection, context propagation, lifecycle behavior, and failure semantics.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should a command bus be verified?","answer":"Use tests that map command contracts to handler resolution, authorization, middleware ordering, result propagation, duplicate handling, and failure classification.","explanation":"A command bus can appear operational while routing commands incorrectly. Verification must cover both successful and rejected dispatches.","principle":"Command dispatch correctness is behavioral, not merely structural.","objective":"Verify command routing and execution guarantees.","requirements":["Handler-resolution tests","Middleware tests","Failure tests","Context-propagation tests"],"constraints":["Do not treat mock invocation alone as proof of end-to-end behavior","Do not claim exactly-once command execution without evidence"],"preconditions":["Command contract and expected lifecycle are documented"],"input":"Command dispatch scenarios.","output":"Requirement-level verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Each dispatch requirement has observable evidence.","incorrect_code":"assert bus.dispatch(command) is not None","corrected_code":"result = bus.dispatch(envelope)\nassert result.status == "SUCCESS"\nassert audit.command_id == envelope.command_id","failure_mode":"Tests pass while command context, authorization, or side effects are wrong.","expected_error":"Assertion or domain-specific verification failure.","cause":"Verification focuses only on returned values.","root_cause":"Command requirements are not traced to observable outcomes.","solution":"Build requirement-to-test mappings for command dispatch.","edge_case":"Handler succeeds but transaction rollback causes the intended side effect