{"record_id":"D35-B063-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Query Envelope","concept":"A query envelope represents a read request and its routing/context metadata without permitting command-like side effects.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a query envelope differ from a command envelope?","answer":"A query envelope should identify a read operation and carry only the metadata required for routing and read authorization; the architecture should explicitly prohibit unintended state mutation.","explanation":"Separating query and command contracts makes read/write intent visible and allows different scaling, consistency, caching, and authorization policies.","principle":"Read intent should be explicit at the application boundary.","objective":"Implement a clear query dispatch contract.","requirements":["Typed query","Query identifier where needed","Read authorization context","Defined result contract"],"constraints":["No hidden write side effects","Do not assume all queries are strongly consistent"],"preconditions":["Query contract exists"],"input":"Typed query request.","output":"Typed query result.","expected_behavior":"The query reaches a read handler without implicit command execution.","incorrect_code":"def query(request):\n    return service.handle(request)","corrected_code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass UserById:\n    user_id: str\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass QueryEnvelope:\n    query: object\n    query_id: str","failure_mode":"Query invokes mutation, result shape is ambiguous, or transport concerns leak into read handlers.","expected_error":"Defined query validation or authorization error.","cause":"Queries and commands share an undifferentiated application interface.","root_cause":"Read/write intent is not represented architecturally.","solution":"Define typed query envelopes and read-only handler contracts.","edge_case":"A read operation triggers lazy persistence or cache population with externally visible side effects.","security_implication":"Read authorization must apply before accessing protected data.","performance_implication":"Queries can use read-specific caching and projections without changing command semantics.","test_case":"Dispatch a query and verify no write repository method is invoked.","test_input":"UserById query.","expected_test_result":"Only read operations execute.","testing_guidance":"Use spies/fakes for repository boundaries and integration tests for actual data access.","verification_method":"Read-only behavior tests and architecture inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify query handlers cannot accidentally invoke mutation paths.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B062-R001","D35-B064-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Query contract"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":"Command Bus","contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Query envelope boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["CQRS and application query patterns"],"provenance":"Established read/write separation pattern; code illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE","TEST","VERIFICATION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B063-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Handler Resolution","concept":"A query bus resolves the handler responsible for a query type through an explicit mapping.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should query handlers use explicit type-based resolution?","answer":"Explicit resolution prevents accidental fallback, makes read dependencies inspectable, and enables deterministic testing.","explanation":"A query may have multiple projections or implementations, but the selected policy should be explicit rather than hidden behind naming conventions.","principle":"Read routing should be deterministic and inspectable.","objective":"Implement safe query handler resolution.","requirements":["Exact or explicitly polymorphic matching","Missing-handler detection","Duplicate-handler policy"],"constraints":["No silent fallback to arbitrary query handlers"],"preconditions":["Query handlers are registered"],"input":"Query instance.","output":"Resolved read handler.","expected_behavior":"The intended handler is selected or an explicit resolution error occurs.","incorrect_code":"handler = handlers.get(type(query), default_query)","corrected_code":"handler = handlers.get(type(query))\nif handler is None:\n    raise LookupError(f"No query handler for {type(query).name}")","failure_mode":"Wrong projection, fallback query, or missing handler hidden by default behavior.","expected_error":"LookupError when no handler is registered.","cause":"Generic fallback routing.","root_cause":"Query resolution lacks an explicit contract.","solution":"Use exact registration and fail clearly when resolution is impossible.","edge_case":"A query can legally target different projections based on explicit capability metadata.","security_implication":"Projection selection must not bypass field-level or tenant authorization.","performance_implication":"Resolution should avoid runtime reflection scans where a direct registry is sufficient.","test_case":"Dispatch a registered query and an unregistered query.","test_input":"KnownQuery and UnknownQuery.","expected_test_result":"KnownQuery resolves correctly; UnknownQuery fails explicitly.","testing_guidance":"Test exact mappings, duplicates, polymorphism policy, and authorization-aware projections.","verification_method":"Unit and integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect registry composition and confirm every public query has an intentional handler.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.75+","framework":null,"framework_version":"None","version_range":"Rust 1.75+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Rust toolchain for execution testing"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.95,"related_records":["D35-B062-R002","D35-B063-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Query registry"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Query handler resolution","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Application dispatch architecture patterns"],"provenance":"Established architecture pattern; Rust representation is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","SECURITY"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B063-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Read-Only Contract","concept":"A query handler contract should make read-only intent explicit and prevent application code from silently performing unrelated mutations.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How can query handlers enforce read-only behavior?","answer":"Use separate read interfaces or repositories, prohibit mutation dependencies by architecture rules, and verify that query paths do not invoke write operations.","explanation":"Language-level immutability alone cannot guarantee absence of side effects. Architectural dependency restrictions and tests provide stronger evidence.","principle":"Read-only behavior is a dependency and verification property, not merely a naming convention.","objective":"Implement and verify a read-only query boundary.","requirements":["Separate read dependency","No write repository dependency","Architecture regression tests"],"constraints":["Some infrastructure operations such as cache writes may exist but must be explicitly classified","Do not claim mathematical purity from interface separation alone"],"preconditions":["Read and write boundaries are distinguishable"],"input":"Query handler implementation.","output":"Read result without business-state mutation.","expected_behavior":"The handler uses only approved read dependencies.","incorrect_code":"class UserQuery:\n    def handle(self, query):\n        user = self.repository.get(query.user_id)\n        user.last_seen = now()\n        self.repository.save(user)\n        return user","corrected_code":"class UserQuery:\n    def handle(self, query):\n        return self.read_repository.get(query.user_id)","failure_mode":"Query mutates domain state or performs hidden write operations.","expected_error":"Architecture/test failure when write dependency is introduced.","cause":"Query and command responsibilities are mixed.","root_cause":"No enforceable read/write dependency boundary.","solution":"Separate interfaces and add architecture-level dependency checks.","edge_case":"Audit logging or cache updates occur during reads; classify these separately from business-state mutation.","security_implication":"Read-only design must still enforce authorization before data retrieval.","performance_implication":"Separate read models may improve query performance but add synchronization complexity.","test_case":"Scan/query dependency graph and run the query with a write repository that raises if called.","test_input":"User query with write repository trap.","expected_test_result":"Query completes without invoking the write repository.","testing_guidance":"Combine static dependency checks with runtime spies.","verification_method":"Architecture test plus integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify both source dependencies and runtime behavior.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime and architecture-test tooling"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B063-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B063-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Read-only query boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["CQRS and architecture dependency principles"],"provenance":"Established architectural separation pattern; code is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","INVALID IMPLEMENTATION","TEST","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE","VERIFICATION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B063-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Query Bus Implementation","subtopic":"Result Contract","concept":"Query results should use explicit result contracts so callers do not depend on undocumented persistence models.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should query handlers return explicit result types?","answer":"Explicit result types isolate the application boundary from persistence implementation details and make compatibility and testing clearer.","explanation":"Returning ORM or database objects directly couples the application and transport layers to infrastructure representation.","principle":"Boundary contracts should expose intent, not infrastructure representation.","objective":"Implement stable query result boundaries.","requirements":["Defined result shape","Mapping boundary","Null/not-found semantics","Version policy"],"constraints":["Do not expose mutable persistence entities directly unless intentionally designed","Do not hide partial-data semantics"],"preconditions":["Query result requirements are defined"],"input":"Query result from data access layer.","output":"Application-level result object.","expected_behavior":"Consumers receive a stable contract independent of storage implementation.","incorrect_code":"return database_session.query(User).get(user_id)","corrected_code":"row = repository.get(user_id)\nif row is None:\n    return None\nreturn UserView(id=row.id, name=row.name)","failure_mode":"Persistence model leakage, accidental mutation, or unstable result schema.","expected_error":"Defined not-found or mapping error.","cause":"Repository objects are returned directly.","root_cause":"No DTO/view-model boundary exists.","solution":"Map persistence results into application query results.","edge_case":"A database row contains fields unavailable to the requesting tenant or role.","security_implication":"Result mapping must enforce data minimization and authorization.","