{"record_id":"D35-B064-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Domain Policy / Rule Engine Boundary","subtopic":"Policy Interface","concept":"A policy boundary encapsulates a business decision rule behind an explicit interface so callers do not depend on rule implementation details.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should a domain policy be represented in code?","answer":"Represent a policy with explicit inputs, decision output, and documented invariants; keep infrastructure concerns outside the policy.","explanation":"Policies often evolve independently of orchestration. A narrow interface makes them testable and allows multiple implementations when the architecture requires them.","principle":"Business decisions should be explicit, deterministic where possible, and independently testable.","objective":"Translate domain policy concepts into executable boundaries.","requirements":["Explicit input contract","Decision output","Deterministic behavior where applicable","No infrastructure dependency"],"constraints":["Do not hide I/O inside deterministic policy evaluation","Do not mix authorization infrastructure with business policy unless explicitly required"],"preconditions":["Policy semantics are defined"],"input":"Domain facts/context.","output":"Policy decision.","expected_behavior":"The policy evaluates only declared inputs and produces the documented decision.","incorrect_code":"def discount(order):\n    db = connect()\n    return db.query(...)","corrected_code":"class DiscountPolicy:\n    def evaluate(self, order_total, customer_tier):\n        if customer_tier == "premium" and order_total >= 100:\n            return 0.10\n        return 0.0","failure_mode":"Hidden I/O, undocumented rule precedence, or policy output ambiguity.","expected_error":"Validation error for missing required policy inputs.","cause":"Policy is implemented as an opaque service with unrelated infrastructure responsibilities.","root_cause":"Decision logic is not separated from orchestration.","solution":"Define a narrow policy interface with explicit inputs and output.","edge_case":"Two policy conditions overlap; precedence must be explicit.","security_implication":"Do not treat a business policy decision as equivalent to authorization unless the architecture explicitly defines that relationship.","performance_implication":"Pure policies are generally inexpensive, but complex rule sets should be measured rather than assumed fast.","test_case":"Evaluate boundary values for order_total and customer_tier.","test_input":"99, 100, and 101 with premium tier.","expected_test_result":"Only values meeting the declared rule receive the premium discount.","testing_guidance":"Use decision tables and boundary-value tests.","verification_method":"Unit tests against explicit policy truth tables.","verification_guidance":"Verify every documented rule and conflict case has corresponding tests.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B005-R001","D35-B027-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B005-R001"],"requires":["Defined policy semantics"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Domain policy boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Domain-driven design and rule-engine patterns"],"provenance":"Established domain policy pattern; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B064-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Domain Policy / Rule Engine Boundary","subtopic":"Rule Composition","concept":"Rule composition combines independently defined predicates or decisions into a larger policy without obscuring their semantics.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How can multiple domain rules be composed safely?","answer":"Represent rules as explicit components and compose them using documented logical or decision semantics rather than embedding a large conditional tree.","explanation":"Composition makes individual rules testable and makes changes easier to reason about. The composition operator itself becomes part of the policy contract.","principle":"Make rule composition semantics explicit.","objective":"Implement composable domain policies.","requirements":["Independent rule contracts","Composition operator","Conflict semantics","Deterministic evaluation policy"],"constraints":["Do not use short-circuiting when all rules must be evaluated for auditability","Do not hide rule precedence"],"preconditions":["Individual rules are defined"],"input":"Domain context.","output":"Composite policy decision.","expected_behavior":"Rules are evaluated according to the declared composition semantics.","incorrect_code":"if a and b or c:\n    return True","corrected_code":"def eligible(ctx):\n    rule_a = rule_a_check(ctx)\n    rule_b = rule_b_check(ctx)\n    rule_c = rule_c_check(ctx)\n    return (rule_a and rule_b) or rule_c","failure_mode":"Operator-precedence ambiguity, hidden short-circuiting, or inconsistent rule evaluation.","expected_error":"Explicit policy conflict or evaluation error when required inputs are unavailable.","cause":"Rules are embedded in opaque boolean expressions.","root_cause":"No explicit composition model.","solution":"Name individual rules and compose them using documented semantics.","edge_case":"A rule has side effects; such rules should not be treated as pure predicates without explicit architecture support.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive rules require explicit deny/allow precedence and must not rely on accidental boolean precedence.","performance_implication":"Evaluating all rules may cost more than short-circuit evaluation; choose based on semantic requirements and measure when performance matters.","test_case":"Test all combinations of three boolean rules.","test_input":"Eight truth-table combinations.","expected_test_result":"Composite output matches the documented Boolean policy.","testing_guidance":"Generate complete decision-table coverage for small rule sets.","verification_method":"Truth-table tests and policy review.","verification_guidance":"Verify composition operators are unambiguous and documented.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript toolchain"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B064-R001","D35-B064-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B064-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Composable domain rules","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Rule composition","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Rule-engine and decision-table patterns"],"provenance":"Established rule composition pattern; code is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE","VERIFICATION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B064-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Domain Policy / Rule Engine Boundary","subtopic":"Deterministic Evaluation","concept":"A deterministic policy returns the same decision for the same declared inputs and policy version when external state is excluded.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should deterministic policy evaluation be implemented?","answer":"Pass all decision inputs explicitly, avoid hidden clocks/randomness/I/O, and identify the policy version when decisions must be reproducible.","explanation":"Determinism enables replay, testing, debugging, and audit. If external state is necessary, it should be captured as an explicit input snapshot.","principle":"Reproducibility requires complete input visibility.","objective":"Implement replayable domain decisions.","requirements":["Explicit inputs","No hidden nondeterminism","Policy version identification","Stable output semantics"],"constraints":["Do not read current time implicitly","Do not access mutable global state"],"preconditions":["Decision inputs can be represented explicitly"],"input":"Policy input snapshot.","output":"Deterministic decision.","expected_behavior":"Repeated evaluation with identical inputs and policy version yields identical output.","incorrect_code":"def risk_level(amount):\n    return "high" if amount > random_threshold() else "low"","corrected_code":"def risk_level(amount, threshold):\n    return "high" if amount > threshold else "low"","failure_mode":"Replay produces different results.","expected_error":"No runtime error is required; nondeterminism is the failure condition.","cause":"Hidden random, time, environment, or global state.","root_cause":"Incomplete policy input model.","solution":"Capture every decision input explicitly and version policy logic where reproducibility matters.","edge_case":"A policy depends on external data that changes between evaluations; capture a versioned snapshot for replay.","security_implication":"Reproducibility must not expose confidential policy inputs or protected data beyond authorized scope.","performance_implication":"Snapshotting external inputs adds storage/serialization overhead; measure it when required.","test_case":"Evaluate the same policy input one hundred times and compare outputs.","test_input":"Fixed policy input and version.","expected_test_result":"All outputs are identical.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated execution, process restart, timezone changes, environment changes, and policy-version differences.","verification_method":"Deterministic property testing and replay comparison.","verification_guidance":"Verify all non-deterministic dependencies are either removed or explicitly captured.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.75+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Rust 1.75+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Rust toolchain"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B033-R001","D35-B064-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R001"],"requires":["Explicit policy input model"],"extends":null,"refines":"D35-B064-R001","specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Deterministic policy evaluation","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Deterministic computation and replayability principles"],"provenance":"Established deterministic-system pattern; Rust example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","TEST","VERIFICATION","PERFORMANCE","VERSION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B064-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Domain Policy / Rule Engine Boundary","subtopic":"Conflict Resolution","concept":"When multiple rules produce conflicting decisions, the conflict-resolution policy must be explicit and independently testable.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should conflicting domain rules be resolved?","answer":"Define precedence, aggregation, deny-overrides, priority, or explicit conflict failure according to the business contract; never rely on collection order accidentally.","explanation":"Conflicts are common as rule sets evolve. An implicit last-write-wins behavior can change merely because registration order changes.","principle":"Conflict resolution is part of policy semantics.","objective":"Make rule conflicts deterministic and auditable.","requirements":["Conflict representation","Precedence policy","Tie behavior","Conflict tests"],"constraints":["Do not infer priority from registration order unless that is the documented policy"],"preconditions":["Multiple rules can apply simultaneously"],"input":"Set of applicable rule results.","output":"Resolved decision or explicit conflict.","expected_behavior":"Identical rule inputs produce the same resolution under the declared precedence policy.","incorrect_code":"decision = None\nfor rule in rules:\n    if rule.matches(ctx):\n        decision = rule.decision","corrected_code":"matches = [r.decision for r in rules if r.matches(ctx)]\nreturn resolve_conflict(matches)","failure_mode":"Decision changes because rule registration order changes.","expected_error":"PolicyConflict when no valid resolution exists.","cause":"Implicit overwrite semantics.","root_cause":"No explicit conflict model.","solution":"Introduce a dedicated conflict-resolution function or policy.","edge_case":"Two rules have equal priority but contradictory decisions.","security_implication":"For security-sensitive decisions, precedence must be explicit and fail-safe behavior must be documented.","performance_implication":"Conflict evaluation can require examining all applicable rules; optimize only after measuring.","test_case":"Apply two equal-priority contradictory rules.","test_input":"ALLOW and DENY at equal priority.","expected_test_result":"The documented tie policy is applied, such as explicit conflict failure.","testing_guidance":"Test precedence, ties, contradictory rules, disabled rules, and rule-version combinations.","verification_method":"Decision-table and property-based testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify registration order does not alter outcomes unless explicitly specified.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B064-R002","D35-B027-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B064-R002"],"requires":["Conflict policy"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Rule conflict resolution","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Decision-table and policy-engine patterns"],"provenance":"Established rule conflict pattern; example not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B064-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Domain Policy / Rule Engine Boundary","subtopic":"Policy Testing and Versioning","concept":"Domain policies require version-aware tests so changes in business rules are distinguishable from regressions.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should domain policy changes be tested and versioned?","answer":"Maintain explicit policy versions where reproducibility matters, preserve decision tables for critical rules, and run regression tests against known boundary and conflict cases.","explanation":"A policy change may be intentional rather than a defect. Version-aware testing preserves the ability to explain why a decision changed.","principle":"Policy evolution must remain traceable.","objective":"Verify policy correctness across revisions.","requirements":["Policy identity/version","Regression suite","Boundary cases","Conflict cases"],"constraints":["Do not overwrite historical expected behavior without recording the intentional policy change"],"preconditions":["Policy semantics and versioning strategy are defined"],"input":"Policy version and test corpus.","output":"Pass/fail evidence mapped to policy version.","expected_behavior":"Tests demonstrate whether each policy revision satisfies its intended decision contract.","incorrect_code":"expected = current_policy.evaluate(case)\nassert actual == expected","corrected_code":"expected = fixtures[policy_version][case.id]\nactual = policy.evaluate(case, version=policy_version)\nassert actual == expected","failure_mode":"Tests automatically accept changed behavior and therefore cannot detect regressions.","expected_error":"Assertion failure for unexpected decision changes.","cause":"Expected outputs are generated from the implementation under test.","root_cause":"No independent policy oracle or versioned expected results.","solution":"Maintain independent expected decisions and explicit policy versions.","edge_case":"A legitimate policy change invalidates some historical expectations; update them only with documented change evidence.","security_implication":"Policy changes affecting authorization or security controls require stronger review and regression coverage.","performance_implication":"Large decision corpora can be executed incrementally but critical cases must remain mandatory.","test_case":"Run historical policy cases against a new policy version.","test_input":"Versioned decision corpus.","expected_test_result":"Intentional changes are explicitly identified; unrelated cases remain stable.","testing_guidance":"Separate regression failures from approved behavior changes.","verification_method":"Versioned golden tests and decision-table review.","verification_guidance":"Verify expected outputs are independent from the implementation being tested.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go toolchain"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B064-R003","D35-B040-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B064-R003"],"requires":["Versioned policy corpus"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":null,"tests":"Domain policy implementation","verifies":"Policy version behavior","derived_from":["Regression testing and policy versioning practices"],"provenance":"Established verification practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["TEST","VERIFICATION","VERSION","SECURITY","EDGE-CASE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B065-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Workflow / Process Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Workflow State","concept":"A workflow implementation represents progress through explicit states and transitions rather than inferring progress from incidental side effects.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should workflow state be represented?","answer":"Use an explicit workflow state model with valid transitions, persisted progress when workflows must survive process failure, and clear terminal states.","explanation":"Explicit state makes resumability, auditing, validation, and failure recovery possible. Implicit state hidden in database rows or flags is harder to reason about.","principle":"Workflow progress should be explicit and recoverable.","objective":"Implement durable and inspectable workflow state.","requirements":["State model","Transition rules","Terminal states","Persistence strategy where required"],"constraints":["Do not permit arbitrary state mutation","Do not treat a side effect as proof that a workflow transition completed"],"preconditions":["Workflow states and transitions are defined"],"input":"Current state and requested transition.","output":"New workflow state or transition failure.","expected_behavior":"Only declared transitions are accepted.","incorrect_code":"workflow.status = request.status","corrected_code":"ALLOWED = {"NEW": {"RUNNING"}, "RUNNING": {"DONE", "FAILED"}}\nif target not in ALLOWED[current]:\n    raise ValueError("invalid transition")\nworkflow.status = target","failure_mode":"Illegal transitions, lost progress, or state/side-effect divergence.","expected_error":"ValueError or domain-specific transition error.","cause":"State is directly assigned without transition validation.","root_cause":"Workflow is modeled as mutable data rather than a state machine.","solution":"Define explicit states and transition functions.","edge_case":"A process crashes after performing a side effect but before persisting the transition.","security_implication":"Privileged workflow states must not be directly client-controlled.","performance_implication":"Persisting every transition adds I/O but may be required for recovery and auditability.","test_case":"Attempt every defined and undefined transition.","test_input":"Transition matrix.","expected_test_result":"Valid transitions succeed and invalid transitions are rejected.","testing_guidance":"Use complete transition-matrix coverage for finite workflows.","verification_method":"State-machine tests and persistence integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify persisted state and actual side effects cannot silently diverge without detection.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B014-R001","D35-B037-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B014-R001"],"requires":["Workflow transition model"],"extends":"D35-B014-R001","refines":null,"specializes":"Workflow state","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Workflow state boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["State machine and workflow orchestration patterns"],"provenance":"Established workflow architecture pattern; code illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B065-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Workflow / Process Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Step Execution","concept":"A workflow step should have an explicit contract for inputs, outputs, completion, failure, and retry behavior.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should a workflow step contract contain?","answer":"Define step identity, input requirements, output/result semantics, success criteria, failure classification, timeout/retry policy, and whether the step is idempotent.","explanation":"Without explicit step contracts, workflow orchestration becomes a collection of opaque calls with undefined recovery behavior.","principle":"Orchestration requires explicit step semantics.","objective":"Implement independently verifiable workflow steps.","requirements":["Step identity","Input/output contract","Success criterion","Failure classification","Retry/idempotency policy"],"constraints":["Do not infer success solely from lack of exception","Do not retry unsafe steps automatically"],"preconditions":["Workflow definition exists"],"input":"Workflow context and step input.","output":"Step result and updated workflow context.","expected_behavior":"The step reports an explicit outcome that the orchestrator can reason about.","incorrect_code":"step(context)\ncontext["step_done"] = True","corrected_code":"result = step.execute(context)\nif not result.success:\n    raise StepFailed(result.error)\ncontext = context.with_result(step.id, result)","failure_mode":"False completion, missing output, or unsafe retry.","expected_error":"StepFailed or typed step-specific failure.","cause":"Completion is represented by an unrelated mutable flag.","root_cause":"Step outcome contract is undefined.","solution":"Return structured step results and define success criteria.","edge_case":"Step produces an external side effect but response is lost; orchestrator must not blindly repeat it.","security_implication":"Step authorization should be evaluated for the current workflow context, not inherited indefinitely.","performance_implication":"Persisting every step result improves recovery but increases storage and I/O.","test_case":"Return success, business rejection, timeout, and unknown outcome from a step.","test_input":"Four step outcomes.","expected_test_result":"Orchestrator handles each according to its defined policy.","testing_guidance":"Test outcome classification and retry safety independently.","verification_method":"Step contract tests plus workflow integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify success criteria represent actual required outcomes rather than invocation completion.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Java 17+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JDK 17+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B065-R001","D35-B047-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B065-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Workflow step contract","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Workflow and reliable execution patterns"],"provenance":"Established workflow implementation pattern; Java example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","INVALID IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B065-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Workflow / Process Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Compensation","concept":"Compensation reverses or offsets completed effects when an atomic transaction cannot span all workflow steps.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should compensation be represented in a workflow?","answer":"Associate explicitly defined compensation behavior with steps whose side effects can be meaningfully reversed or offset, and distinguish compensation from guaranteed rollback.","explanation":"Compensation is not equivalent to database rollback. Some external effects cannot be reversed exactly, so the workflow must model residual risk and failure outcomes.","principle":"Distributed compensation is an explicit business recovery action, not magical rollback.","objective":"Implement safe workflow compensation.","requirements":["Compensation contract","Eligibility criteria","Compensation ordering","Failure handling"],"constraints":["Do not claim exact rollback of irreversible external effects","Do not compensate actions that are not safely reversible without explicit policy"],"preconditions":["Workflow contains compensatable effects"],"input":"Completed workflow steps and compensation policy.","output":"Compensation actions/results.","expected_behavior":"Compensation is attempted only for eligible completed effects and its outcome is recorded.","incorrect_code":"except Exception:\n    undo_everything()","corrected_code":"completed = [s for s in steps if s.completed]\nfor step in reversed(completed):\n    if step.compensation is not None:\n        step.compensation.execute()","failure_mode":"Incorrect compensation order, double compensation, or compensation of incomplete steps.","expected_error":"CompensationFailure when a required compensating action cannot complete.","cause":"Compensation is treated as a generic undo operation.","root_cause":"Irreversible distributed side effects are not modeled explicitly.","solution":"Define compensation per step and persist compensation state.","edge_case":"Compensation itself fails after partially reversing an earlier effect.","security_implication":"Compensation actions may have high impact and require the same authorization controls as forward actions.","performance_implication":"Compensation increases recovery latency and external calls; no performance guarantee should be assumed.","test_case":"Complete three steps, fail the fourth, and verify eligible compensations execute in the declared order.","test_input":"Workflow with three completed compensatable steps.","expected_test_result":"Compensation runs in reverse dependency order and records each result.","testing_guidance":"Test partial completion, compensation failure, repeated recovery, and irreversible actions.","verification_method":"Failure-injection integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify compensation is not described as atomic rollback and that residual states are observable.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime and test doubles for external systems"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B065-R002","D35-B066-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B065-R002"],"requires":["Compensation policy"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Workflow compensation","contrasts_with":"Atomic transaction rollback","contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Compensation boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Saga and compensating-transaction patterns"],"provenance":"Established distributed transaction pattern; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B065-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Workflow / Process Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Resume and Recovery","concept":"Recoverable workflows persist enough state to identify completed, failed, pending, and compensating steps after process interruption.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What information is required to resume a workflow safely?","answer":"Persist workflow identity/version, current state, completed step outcomes, pending step information, attempt metadata, and recovery-relevant context according to data-minimization rules.","explanation":"A restartable process cannot safely reconstruct progress from in-memory state alone. Recovery must distinguish completed effects from merely attempted calls.","principle":"Resume from durable evidence, not assumptions.","objective":"Implement crash-safe workflow recovery.","requirements":["Durable workflow state","Step status","Attempt identity","Workflow version","Recovery policy"],"constraints":["Do not infer completion from process restart","Do not persist secrets unnecessarily"],"preconditions":["Workflow requires recovery across process boundaries"],"input":"Persisted workflow snapshot.","output":"Resumed workflow execution state.","expected_behavior":"The orchestrator resumes only from recorded evidence and defined recovery rules.","incorrect_code":"if not process_alive:\n    run_all_steps_again()","corrected_code":"state = store.load(workflow_id)\nfor step in state.pending_steps:\n    orchestrator.resume(step, state)","failure_mode":"Duplicate external effects or skipped required steps after restart.","expected_error":"RecoveryError when durable state is missing or inconsistent.","cause":"Progress exists only in memory or is inferred from elapsed time.","root_cause":"No durable workflow state model.","solution":"Persist workflow progress and define recovery transitions.","edge_case":"The durable state says a step is pending while the external system actually completed it.","security_implication":"Recovery must revalidate authorization and tenant scope before resuming privileged work.","performance_implication":"Frequent persistence adds I/O; checkpoint frequency should follow recovery requirements.","test_case":"Terminate orchestration after a step call and restart from persisted state.","test_input":"Workflow with interrupted step.","expected_test_result":"Recovery follows the documented uncertain-outcome policy rather than blindly duplicating effects.","testing_guidance":"Inject crashes at each step boundary.","verification_method":"Crash-recovery integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify recovery behavior for completed, pending, failed, and uncertain steps.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go toolchain and durable test store"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B065-R001","D35-B017-R001","D35-B066-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B065-R001"],"requires":["Durable workflow state"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Workflow recovery","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Workflow recovery boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Durable workflow and distributed recovery patterns"],"provenance":"Established reliability pattern; Go example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B065-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Workflow / Process Orchestration Implementation","subtopic":"Workflow Verification","concept":"Workflow verification must establish that state transitions, step outcomes, recovery, and compensation satisfy the declared process contract.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should workflow orchestration be verified?","answer":"Test valid and invalid transitions, successful completion, each failure boundary, restart recovery, compensation, cancellation, and uncertain external outcomes.","explanation":"Workflow correctness is primarily a state-and-outcome property. Happy-path testing alone cannot establish recovery safety.","principle":"Verify the workflow state machine and side-effect semantics together.","objective":"Provide evidence for workflow correctness under normal and failure conditions.","requirements":["Transition coverage","Failure injection","Recovery tests","Compensation tests","Outcome verification"],"constraints":["Do not mark a workflow verified from unit tests of individual steps alone"],"preconditions":["Workflow contract and recovery policy exist"],"input":"Workflow test scenarios.","output":"State transition and outcome evidence.","expected_behavior":"Observed states and effects match the declared workflow contract.","incorrect_code":"assert workflow.run() == "done"","corrected_code":"result = workflow.run()\nassert result.state == "DONE"\nassert result.completed_steps == expected_steps\nassert result.uncompensated_effects == []","failure_mode":"Workflow reaches nominal DONE state while a required effect failed or remained uncertain.","expected_error":"Verification assertion failure or explicit workflow integrity error.","cause":"Verification checks state label without checking side effects.","root_cause":"Workflow state and outcome evidence are not linked.","solution":"Verify state, step outcomes, and required effects together.","edg