{"record_id":"D35-B066-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Saga / Distributed Transaction Coordination","subtopic":"Saga Coordinator","concept":"A saga coordinator tracks a sequence of distributed actions and determines forward, compensation, and recovery behavior without pretending the distributed process is atomic.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What is the core responsibility of a saga coordinator?","answer":"The coordinator tracks saga progress, invokes the next permitted action, records outcomes, and initiates compensation or recovery when required.","explanation":"A saga coordinates multiple local transactions across boundaries. It cannot provide a single atomic commit across independent systems unless an actual distributed transaction mechanism exists.","principle":"Coordinate distributed effects explicitly; do not simulate atomicity with control flow.","objective":"Implement a transparent saga coordination boundary.","requirements":["Saga identity","Step state","Action contracts","Compensation policy","Recovery policy"],"constraints":["No false atomicity claim","No uncontrolled parallelism when ordering is required"],"preconditions":["Saga steps and compensation semantics are defined"],"input":"Saga state and current step.","output":"Updated saga state and action decision.","expected_behavior":"The coordinator advances only after the current step outcome satisfies its contract.","incorrect_code":"for action in actions:\n    action()\ncommit_everything()","corrected_code":"for step in saga.pending_steps():\n    result = step.execute()\n    saga.record(step.id, result)\n    if not result.success:\n        saga.start_compensation()\n        break","failure_mode":"State loss, skipped compensation, or false atomicity assumptions.","expected_error":"SagaExecutionError or CompensationRequired according to contract.","cause":"Distributed actions are treated like one local transaction.","root_cause":"No explicit saga state and outcome model.","solution":"Persist saga progress and define forward/compensation transitions.","edge_case":"A remote action times out after possibly succeeding.","security_implication":"Each saga action must enforce current authorization and scope.","performance_implication":"Cross-system coordination is latency-bound by participating systems; no universal throughput claim should be made.","test_case":"Complete first action, fail second, and verify compensation begins.","test_input":"Three-step saga with failure at step two.","expected_test_result":"Step one is recorded as completed and compensation policy is initiated.","testing_guidance":"Test every step failure and uncertain remote response.","verification_method":"Integration tests with deterministic remote-system fakes.","verification_guidance":"Verify saga state persists independently of process memory.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime and external-system test doubles"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B065-R003","D35-B065-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B065-R003"],"requires":["Saga definition"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Saga coordinator","contrasts_with":"Local atomic transaction","contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Saga coordination","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Saga and distributed transaction patterns"],"provenance":"Established distributed transaction pattern; code illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B066-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Saga / Distributed Transaction Coordination","subtopic":"Forward Action Contract","concept":"Each saga forward action requires explicit success, failure, timeout, idempotency, and compensation semantics.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why does every saga action need an explicit contract?","answer":"Distributed coordination cannot safely infer whether an action can be retried, compensated, or considered complete. Those semantics must be declared by the action boundary.","explanation":"A network timeout can represent either failure or unknown completion. The coordinator needs action-specific semantics to decide the next safe step.","principle":"Uncertainty must be modeled, not collapsed into failure or success.","objective":"Make saga actions safe to coordinate.","requirements":["Outcome states","Idempotency policy","Timeout semantics","Compensation definition","Verification capability"],"constraints":["Do not retry unsafe actions automatically","Do not classify unknown remote completion as definite failure"],"preconditions":["Remote action contract is documented"],"input":"Action request.","output":"Typed action outcome.","expected_behavior":"The coordinator can distinguish success, failure, and uncertain completion.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    remote.call()\nexcept TimeoutError:\n    return "FAILED"","corrected_code":"try:\n    remote.call()\n    return ActionOutcome.SUCCESS\nexcept TimeoutError:\n    return ActionOutcome.UNKNOWN","failure_mode":"Duplicate side effects caused by treating timeout as definite failure.","expected_error":"UNKNOWN outcome requiring recovery/verification.","cause":"Network uncertainty is collapsed into a binary result.","root_cause":"Remote action outcome model is insufficient.","solution":"Represent unknown outcomes explicitly and provide verification or reconciliation paths.","edge_case":"Remote system completes the action after the coordinator has initiated compensation.","security_implication":"Compensation must not unintentionally revoke or alter resources belonging to another authorization scope.","performance_implication":"Outcome verification introduces additional calls and latency.","test_case":"Simulate a timeout followed by a remote query showing the action completed.","test_input":"Timeout then successful remote verification.","expected_test_result":"Coordinator reconciles to completed state without duplicating the action.","testing_guidance":"Test success, failure, timeout, duplicate response, and delayed completion.","verification_method":"Failure-injection integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify unknown outcomes have explicit recovery paths.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go toolchain"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B047-R001","D35-B066-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B066-R001"],"requires":["Remote action outcome contract"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Saga action contract","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed systems failure semantics"],"provenance":"Established distributed failure-handling principle; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B066-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Saga / Distributed Transaction Coordination","subtopic":"Compensation Ordering","concept":"Saga compensation should reverse dependencies where possible, but compensation order must be derived from actual effect dependencies rather than blindly assuming reverse list order.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should compensation order be determined?","answer":"Use explicit dependency relationships between effects and compensate in an order that safely removes or offsets dependent effects before their prerequisites when required.","explanation":"Reverse execution order is common but is not universally correct. Parallel or dependent effects may require a graph-based compensation plan.","principle":"Compensation order follows effect dependencies.","objective":"Prevent unsafe compensation sequencing.","requirements":["Effect dependency model","Compensation ordering policy","Partial-compensation handling"],"constraints":["Do not assume reverse order is always safe","Do not compensate a prerequisite while dependent effects remain active"],"preconditions":["Saga effects and dependencies are known"],"input":"Completed effects and dependency graph.","output":"Safe compensation sequence.","expected_behavior":"Compensation respects declared dependencies.","incorrect_code":"for step in reversed(completed_steps):\n    step.undo()","corrected_code":"for step in compensation_plan(completed_steps, dependencies):\n    step.compensate()","failure_mode":"Compensating prerequisite before dependent effect.","expected_error":"CompensationPlanningError when dependencies cannot produce a safe plan.","cause":"Compensation sequence is derived only from execution order.","root_cause":"Effect dependency relationships are not modeled.","solution":"Build explicit compensation dependencies.","edge_case":"Two effects are independent and can be compensated concurrently, but one compensation fails.","security_implication":"Compensation dependencies may cross authorization boundaries and must retain scope validation.","performance_implication":"Dependency planning adds graph-processing cost; parallel compensation can reduce elapsed time only when semantics allow it.","test_case":"Create effects A and B where B depends on A and verify B compensates before A.","test_input":"Dependency graph B -> A.","expected_test_result":"B compensation occurs before A.","testing_guidance":"Test linear, branching, independent, and cyclic dependency graphs.","verification_method":"Graph validation plus integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Reject cyclic or ambiguous compensation plans when they cannot be safely executed.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.75+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Rust 1.75+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Rust toolchain"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B066-R001","D35-B066-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B066-R001"],"requires":["Effect dependency graph"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Saga compensation planning","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Compensation dependency ordering","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed dependency and compensation patterns"],"provenance":"Established dependency-based recovery concept; Rust example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B066-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Saga / Distributed Transaction Coordination","subtopic":"Persistent Progress","concept":"A saga that must survive process failure needs durable progress and outcome records independent of coordinator memory.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must saga progress be persisted?","answer":"Persistent progress allows recovery to distinguish completed, pending, failed, compensated, and uncertain actions after coordinator failure.","explanation":"Without durable progress, restart can cause duplicate effects or skipped compensation. The persistence boundary is part of saga correctness.","principle":"Distributed recovery requires durable evidence of progress.","objective":"Implement restartable saga coordination.","requirements":["Durable saga identity","Step outcome persistence","Versioned saga definition","Recovery state"],"constraints":["Do not persist unnecessary secrets","Do not overwrite historical outcomes without preserving auditability"],"preconditions":["Saga spans process or system boundaries"],"input":"Saga execution state.","output":"Durable saga record.","expected_behavior":"A new coordinator instance can reconstruct the safe next action from durable state.","incorrect_code":"self.current_step += 1","corrected_code":"store.record_step_outcome(saga_id, step_id, outcome)\nstate = store.load(saga_id)\nnext_step = planner.next(state)","failure_mode":"Restart causes duplicate or skipped actions.","expected_error":"SagaRecoveryError for missing or inconsistent state.","cause":"Progress stored only in process memory.","root_cause":"Saga durability was omitted from the architecture.","solution":"Persist state and define recovery transitions.","edge_case":"Two coordinator instances recover the same saga concurrently.","security_implication":"Recovery records require tenant isolation and authorization controls.","performance_implication":"Durability adds storage operations; batching is safe only when it preserves required recovery semantics.","test_case":"Kill coordinator after persisting one step and resume from another instance.","test_input":"Persisted saga with one completed step.","expected_test_result":"Recovery resumes from the correct state without repeating the completed step unless contract explicitly requires reconciliation.","testing_guidance":"Test process termination at every persistence boundary.","verification_method":"Crash-recovery integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify durable state is sufficient to reconstruct recovery decisions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime and durable storage test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B017-R001","D35-B065-R004","D35-B066-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B017-R001"],"requires":["Durable saga store"],"extends":null,"refines":"D35-B066-R001","specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Saga persistence boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Durable workflow and saga patterns"],"provenance":"Established distributed recovery pattern; code illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B066-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Saga / Distributed Transaction Coordination","subtopic":"Saga Verification","concept":"Saga verification must test distributed failure boundaries, uncertain outcomes, compensation, and convergence toward an accepted final state.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should saga implementations be verified?","answer":"Execute controlled scenarios covering each action failure, timeout, duplicate delivery, coordinator restart, compensation failure, and final-state reconciliation.","explanation":"Saga correctness is about recovery under partial failure. Happy-path completion is insufficient evidence.","principle":"Verify distributed failure behavior, not only successful orchestration.","objective":"Establish evidence for saga safety and recoverability.","requirements":["Failure matrix","Crash-recovery tests","Compensation tests","Uncertain-outcome tests","Final-state verification"],"constraints":["Do not claim atomicity","Do not equate saga completion with remote-system correctness without outcome verification"],"preconditions":["Saga contract and expected convergence state are defined"],"input":"Controlled distributed failure scenarios.","output":"Evidence of recovery and final state.","expected_behavior":"Each failure scenario reaches the documented recovery or explicit unresolved state.","incorrect_code":"assert saga.run() == "COMPLETED"","corrected_code":"result = saga.run()\nassert result.recovery_state in {"COMPLETED", "REQUIRES_RECONCILIATION"}\nassert verify_external_state(result)","failure_mode":"Coordinator reports success while remote state remains inconsistent.","expected_error":"Verification failure or reconciliation-required state.","cause":"Verification stops at coordinator state.","root_cause":"No external outcome verification contract.","solution":"Verify required remote effects independently.","edge_case":"A remote system becomes unavailable during final verification.","security_implication":"Reconciliation must not bypass authorization because the saga is already active.","performance_implication":"Verification adds remote calls and should be scoped to critical effects.","test_case":"Complete a saga with a timeout on an external action, then verify remote state.","test_input":"Timeout plus later remote availability.","expected_test_result":"Saga reaches completion only after required external state is verified or is explicitly marked unresolved.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic fault injection and record actual execution evidence.","verification_method":"Integration and recovery testing.","verification_guidance":"Trace each action outcome to final-state evidence.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go toolchain and controllable external-system doubles"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B066-R002","D35-B066-R004","D35-B050-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B066-R004"],"requires":["External outcome verification"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":null,"tests":"Saga coordinator","verifies":"Saga recovery and convergence","derived_from":["Distributed systems verification practices"],"provenance":"Established distributed verification methodology; Go example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["TEST","VERIFICATION","EDGE-CASE","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B067-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Lock / Coordination Boundary","subtopic":"Lock Abstraction","concept":"A distributed lock abstraction coordinates exclusive ownership across processes only when the underlying mechanism provides the required ownership and lease guarantees.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should a distributed lock abstraction expose?","answer":"It should expose acquisition, ownership identity, lease/expiry semantics where applicable, release, and failure behavior without pretending local mutex semantics are distributed guarantees.","explanation":"Distributed locks depend on failure detection, clocks, network behavior, and the storage/coordination system. The abstraction must preserve those limitations.","principle":"Distributed coordination guarantees must match the underlying mechanism.","objective":"Implement a truthful distributed lock boundary.","requirements":["Ownership identity","Acquire result","Lease semantics where supported","Safe release","Failure classification"],"constraints":["Do not use an ordinary process mutex for distributed coordination","Do not claim mutual exclusion beyond documented mechanism guarantees"],"preconditions":["A coordination mechanism with appropriate semantics exists"],"input":"Lock key and owner identity.","output":"Acquired or not-acquired result.","expected_behavior":"Only the mechanism's documented ownership semantics are relied upon.","incorrect_code":"global_lock = threading.Lock()","corrected_code":"class DistributedLock:\n    def acquire(self, key, owner_id, lease_seconds):\n        raise NotImplementedError\n\n    def release(self, key, owner_id):\n        raise NotImplementedError","failure_mode":"Multiple processes believe they own the same resource.","expected_error":"LockUnavailable or OwnershipError.","cause":"Local synchronization is used across process boundaries.","root_cause":"Distributed coordination requirement was implemented with a local primitive.","solution":"Use a coordination mechanism whose documented semantics match the requirement.","edge_case":"The owner loses connectivity while its lease remains uncertain.","security_implication":"Lock ownership must not be treated as authorization; authorization remains independently required.","performance_implication":"Distributed lock acquisition incurs network/storage latency.","test_case":"Run two independent processes against the same lock key using the actual coordination implementation.","test_input":"Same key, different owner IDs.","expected_test_result":"Observed behavior matches the coordination system's documented ownership guarantee.","testing_guidance":"Test contention, expiry, owner mismatch, process crash, and network interruption.","verification_method":"Integration tests against the real coordination mechanism or a semantics-faithful test environment.","verification_guidance":"Do not mark distributed guarantees verified using only an in-process fake.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Actual distributed coordination backend for meaningful execution testing"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B067-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Distributed coordination backend"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":"Local mutex","contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Distributed lock abstraction","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed coordination patterns"],"provenance":"Established distributed systems principle; no backend execution performed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE","VERSION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B067-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Lock / Coordination Boundary","subtopic":"Lease Semantics","concept":"A lease-based lock grants ownership for a bounded period and requires explicit handling of expiry and renewal uncertainty.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should lease expiry be modeled explicitly?","answer":"Because a process can continue operating after its lease expires or become uncertain about whether renewal succeeded, ownership must be validated before protected operations when required.","explanation":"Lease duration alone does not guarantee that a process stops executing when ownership expires. Fencing or ownership tokens may be required by the protected resource.","principle":"Lease ownership is time-bounded and must not be treated as permanent authority.","objective":"Implement safe lease-based coordination.","requirements":["Lease identity","Expiry semantics","Renewal policy","Ownership validation","Optional fencing token"],"constraints":["Do not assume local clocks are sufficient for distributed ownership decisions","Do not continue privileged writes after ownership is uncertain"],"preconditions":["The coordination mechanism supports lease semantics"],"input":"Lease request and owner identity.","output":"Lease token and expiry information.","expected_behavior":"Protected work respects the documented lease and ownership semantics.","incorrect_code":"lock.acquire()\ndo_sensitive_work()\nlock.release()","corrected_code":"lease = lock.acquire(key, owner)\nif not lease.acquired:\n    raise LockUnavailable()\nif not resource.accepts(lease.fencing_token):\n    raise OwnershipLost()\nresource.write(..., fencing_token=lease.fencing_token)","failure_mode":"Expired owner performs writes after another owner acquires the resource.","expected_error":"OwnershipLost or resource-level fencing rejection.","cause":"Lock state and resource authority are not linked.","root_cause":"Lease expiry is treated as sufficient protection without resource-level ownership validation.","solution":"Use fencing/ownership validation where the protected resource requires it.","edge_case":"Lease renewal succeeds locally but its response is delayed or lost.","security_implication":"Ownership tokens are not a replacement for authorization and must not grant broader privileges.","performance_implication":"Renewal traffic and fencing validation add overhead.","test_case":"Expire owner A's lease, acquire by B, then attempt protected write by A.","test_input":"Two owners and one resource.","expected_test_result":"A's stale write is rejected when fencing is required.","testing_guidance":"Test expiry, renewal uncertainty, clock variation, process pause, and stale owners.","verification_method":"Distributed integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify stale-owner protection at the actual protected resource.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Java 17+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JDK and real coordination/resource implementation for meaningful testing"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B067-R001","D35-B067-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B067-R001"],"requires":["Lease-capable coordination mechanism"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Lease-based distributed lock","contrasts_with":"Unbounded local lock","contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Lease semantics","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed lease and fencing patterns"],"provenance":"Established distributed coordination principle; Java example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B067-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Lock / Coordination Boundary","subtopic":"Contention Handling","concept":"Lock contention requires bounded waiting, explicit acquisition failure, and application-specific backpressure rather than unbounded blocking.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should lock contention be handled?","answer":"Use a defined acquisition timeout or non-blocking attempt, classify contention separately from backend failure, and choose a retry/backoff policy appropriate to the workload.","explanation":"Unbounded lock waiting can exhaust worker capacity and amplify failures. Contention is a normal coordination condition and should be observable.","principle":"Bound coordination waiting and make contention explicit.","objective":"Prevent lock contention from cascading into resource exhaustion.","requirements":["Bounded acquisition","Contention classification","Backoff policy","Observability"],"constraints":["Do not spin aggressively","Do not treat contention as an infrastructure outage without evidence"],"preconditions":["Lock acquisition semantics are defined"],"input":"Lock key and wait budget.","output":"Acquired or bounded failure result.","expected_behavior":"The caller either acquires the lock within the budget or receives an explicit contention outcome.","incorrect_code":"while not lock.acquire():\n    pass","corrected_code":"deadline = monotonic() + wait_seconds\nwhile monotonic() < deadline:\n    if lock.try_acquire():\n        break\n    sleep(backoff())\nelse:\n    raise LockContended()","failure_mode":"Busy waiting, thread starvation, or cascading latency.","expected_error":"LockContended after the configured wait budget.","cause":"No bounded acquisition policy.","root_cause":"Contention is treated as an exceptional case rather than an expected coordination state.","solution":"Bound waiting and apply controlled backoff.","edge_case":"Many workers retry simultaneously after a shared lock becomes available.","security_implication":"A lock should not become an authorization bypass; access remains policy-controlled.","performance_implication":"Backoff reduces contention but increases individual wait time; tune using measurements rather than assumptions.","test_case":"Start many workers competing for one lock.","test_input":"N workers and one lock key.","expected_test_result":"Waiters either acquire within policy or fail boundedly without busy-spin behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test contention, timeout, cancellation, and retry storms.","verification_method":"Concurrency tests and resource-usage observation.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual wait and CPU behavior if performance claims are required.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go toolchain and distributed lock implementation"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B047-R002","D35-B067-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B067-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Lock contention handling","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Concurrency and backoff patterns"],"provenance":"Established concurrency principle; Go example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B067-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Lock / Coordination Boundary","subtopic":"Ownership Validation","concept":"Release operations must validate ownership so one process cannot release another process's lock.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must distributed lock release include ownership validation?","answer":"A lock key alone is insufficient because ownership can change between acquisition and release. Release should prove that the caller owns the current lease/token.","explanation":"Without ownership validation, a delayed or restarted process can release a lock currently owned by another process.","principle":"Mutation of coordination state requires ownership proof.","objective":"Prevent stale owners from releasing active locks.","requirements":["Owner identity/token","Atomic ownership validation","Safe release semantics"],"constraints":["Do not use unconditional delete by key","Do not rely only on local memory for ownership"],"preconditions":["Lock backend supports ownership-aware release"],"input":"Lock key and owner token.","output":"Release success or ownership failure.","expected_behavior":"Only the current owner can release its lock.","incorrect_code":"backend.delete(lock_key)","corrected_code":"backend.release_if_owner(lock_key, owner_token)","failure_mode":"Stale process deletes another owner's lock.","expected_error":"OwnershipError when token does not match.","cause":"Release operation does not compare ownership.","root_cause":"Lock release is treated as a simple key deletion.","solution":"Use atomic compare-and-release semantics supported by the coordination backend.","edge_case":"The original owner token is reused after restart; tokens must have sufficient uniqueness.","security_implication":"Owner tokens should not expose privileged credentials or become reusable authorization artifacts.","performance_implication":"Atomic compare-and-release is typically preferable to a separate read followed by delete because it avoids a race.","test_case":"Acquire with owner A, acquire with B after expiry, then attempt release with A.","test_input":"Owner A stale token and owner B current token.","expected_test_result":"A's release fails and B's lock remains active.","testing_guidance":"Test stale owners, expiry, duplicate releases, and concurrent release attempts.","verification_method":"Integration concurrency tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify ownership validation occurs atomically where required.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Actual coordination backend for distributed verification"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B067-R001","D35-B067-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B067-R001"],"requires":["Ownership-aware release primitive"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Lock release ownership","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Ownership validation","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed locking correctness patterns"],"provenance":"Established distributed locking principle; illustrative code not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","SECURITY","VERIFICATION","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B067-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Lock / Coordination Boundary","subtopic":"Lock Verification","concept":"Distributed lock verification requires multi-process failure and contention tests against the actual coordination semantics.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should distributed locking be verified?","answer":"Test competing owners, expiry, stale-owner behavior, process failure, renewal uncertainty, release ownership, and protected-resource effects using a real coordination mechanism when distributed guarantees matter.","explanation":"An in-memory fake can verify application flow but cannot establish distributed mutual exclusion or lease behavior.","principle":"Distributed guarantees require distributed evidence.","objective":"Establish truthful evidence for lock correctness.","requirements":["Multi-process tests","Failure injection","Ownership tests","Expiry tests","Protected-resource tests"],"constraints":["Do not claim distributed correctness from unit tests alone"],"preconditions":["Actual coordination implementation is available for integration testing"],"input":"Distributed lock scenarios.","output":"Observed coordination behavior and evidence.","expected_behavior":"Observed behavior matches documented coordination guarantees.","incorrect_code":"assert lock.acquire()","corrected_code":"owner_a = process_a.acquire(key)\nowner_b = process_b.try_acquire(key)\nassert not owner_b.acquired\nprocess_a.release(owner_a)","failure_mode":"Verification tests only one process and therefore miss distributed races.","expected_error":"Test assertion failure when concurrent ownership occurs.","cause":"Insufficient test topology.","root_cause":"Distributed property is being tested as a local property.","solution":"Use multi-process or distributed integration tests.","edge_case":"Process pause exceeds lease duration while it still attempts protected work.","security_implication":"Tests must include stale-owner attempts against protected resources.","performance_implication":"Distributed tests are slower and environment-sensitive; keep them separate from fast unit tests.","test_case":"Run two independent processes with the same key and inject owner pause.","test_input":"Contending processes and lease expiration.","expected_test_result":"Only permitted ownership behavior occurs and stale work is rejected where fencing is required.","testing_guidance":"Record environment, backend version, timing assumptions, and actual execution results.","verification_method":"Distributed integration and fault-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Never mark lease/fencing semantics verified solely from mocks.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Multi-process test environment and actual coordination backend"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B067-R002","D35-B067-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B067-R001"],"requires":["Distributed test environment"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":null,"tests":"Distributed lock implementation","verifies":"Distributed lock guarantees","derived_from":["Distributed systems verification practices"],"provenance":"Verification methodology is established; execution not performed for this dataset record.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE","EDGE-CASE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B068-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Consistency Model","concept":"The consistency boundary defines what freshness and ordering guarantees callers may rely upon when reading or updating distributed state.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must consistency guarantees be explicit?","answer":"Different storage and replication paths provide different visibility guarantees; callers need a documented contract instead of assuming every read observes the latest write.","explanation":"Strong, eventual, session, causal, and other consistency models have different application implications. The implementation should expose only the guarantees actually supported.","principle":"Consistency is a contract, not an assumption.","objective":"Translate consistency requirements into explicit implementation boundaries.","requirements":["Consistency model","Read/write path definition","Staleness expectations","Reconciliation policy"],"constraints":["Do not claim strong consistency from eventual storage","Do not hide stale-read behavior"],"preconditions":["Data flow and storage semantics are known"],"input":"Read/write request and consistency requirement.","output":"Data result with applicable consistency semantics.","expected_behavior":"Caller receives data within the documented consistency guarantee.","incorrect_code":"write_primary(value)\nreturn read_replica()","corrected_code":"write_primary(value)\nreturn read_primary_until_visible(value.key)","failure_mode":"Read-after-write violation or stale data presented as current.","expected_error":"ConsistencyUnavailable or stale-result indicator when the required guarantee cannot be met.","cause":"Read path does not match requested consistency.","root_cause":"Consistency semantics are undocumented.","solution":"Define and enforce the required consistency level per operation.","edge_case":"A user writes data and immediately reads through a replica.","security_implication":"Authorization decisions must not rely on stale state when stale permissions could grant unintended access.","performance_implication":"Stronger consistency can increase latency or reduce availability depending on architecture; measure actual behavior.","test_case":"Write a value and immediately perform reads through each configured read path.","test_input":"Known write followed by immediate reads.","expected_test_result":"Observed behavior matches the documented consistency model.","testing_guidance":"Test read-after-write, concurrent updates, replication delay, and reconciliation.","verification_method":"Integration tests against actual storage/replication topology.","verification_guidance":"Record measured behavior rather than assuming backend documentation alone proves application-level consistency.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Actual storage topology for meaningful consistency tests"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B068-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Documented consistency requirement"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Consistency boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed consistency principles"],"provenance":"Established distributed-systems concept; code illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["CONCEPT","IMPLEMENTATION","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B068-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Read-After-Write","concept":"Read-after-write behavior requires a defined mechanism when a caller expects its own recent write to be visible immediately.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How can read-after-write consistency be implemented?","answer":"Use a read path with the required visibility guarantee, session/version tokens, primary reads, or another documented mechanism appropriate to the storage system.","explanation":"Simply performing a write and then reading a replica does not guarantee visibility. The implementation must connect the read to the write's consistency context.","principle":"Visibility guarantees require an explicit mechanism.","objective":"Implement reliable read-after-write behavior.","requirements":["Write acknowledgment semantics","Visibility mechanism","Timeout/failure policy"],"constraints":["Do not busy-wait indefinitely for replication","Do not claim visibility merely because a write returned successfully"],"preconditions":["Storage exposes a suitable consistency mechanism"],"input":"Write result and subsequent read request.","output":"Read result satisfying the requested visibility guarantee or explicit failure.","expected_behavior":"The read either observes the required write or returns a documented consistency failure.","incorrect_code":"write(value)\nreturn replica.read(key)","corrected_code":"receipt = write(value)\nreturn read(key, consistency_token=receipt.visibility_token)","failure_mode":"Caller receives stale data after a successful write.","expected_error":"ConsistencyTimeout if required visibility is not reached within the budget.","cause":"Read does not carry the write's visibility context.","root_cause":"Read-after-write requirement is not represented in the API.","solution":"Propagate an explicit consistency token or select an appropriate read path.","edge_case":"The visibility token expires before the read occurs.","security_implication":"Consistency tokens must not expose internal topology or authorization secrets.","performance_implication":"Waiting for visibility can add latency; use bounded deadlines.","test_case":"Introduce controlled replication delay and issue a read with a visibility requirement.","test_input":"Write receipt plus delayed replica.","expected_test_result":"Read waits or uses an appropriate path according to the configured policy and never falsely reports stale data as fresh.","testing_guidance":"Test immediate reads, delayed reads, token expiry, and timeout.","verification_method":"Storage integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual visibility delay when a performance or freshness claim is required.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go toolchain and consistency-capable storage backend"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B068-R001","D35-B033-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B068-R001"],"requires":["Storage visibility mechanism"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Read-after-write consistency","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Read-after-write boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed consistency patterns"],"provenance":"Established consistency pattern; Go example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B068-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Stale-Read Handling","concept":"Applications that permit eventual consistency should represent acceptable staleness and define behavior when data is older than the contract allows.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should stale reads be handled?","answer":"Track or infer freshness metadata where supported, compare it with the operation's freshness requirement, and either return the data with an explicit stale state or use a stronger read path.","explanation":"Silently returning stale data can violate application semantics. The boundary should make freshness visible when it matters.","principle":"Allowed staleness must be explicit.","objective":"Implement safe handling of eventually consistent data.","requirements":["Freshness requirement","Staleness detection","Fallback or rejection policy"],"constraints":["Do not fabricate freshness timestamps","Do not assume replication delay is constant"],"preconditions":["Data source provides sufficient freshness information"],"input":"Read result and freshness metadata.","output":"Fresh, stale, or unavailable result.","expected_behavior":"Results outside the freshness contract are handled according to policy.","incorrect_code":"return replica.get(key)","corrected_code":"result = replica.get(key)\nif result.age > max_age:\n    return StaleResult(result)\nreturn FreshResult(result)","failure_mode":"Old data is returned as if current.","expected_error":"StaleData or explicit stale-result state.","cause":"No freshness contract.","root_cause":"Eventual consistency is treated as transparent infrastructure.","solution":"Define and enforce freshness requirements at the boundary.","edge_case":"Clock differences make age calculation unreliable; use storage-provided version or logical freshness metadata where possible.","security_implication":"Stale authorization or entitlement data can produce incorrect access decisions.","performance_implication":"Freshness checks add little local work but stronger fallback reads can increase latency.","test_case":"Inject a result older than the configured maximum age.","test_input":"Result age greater than max_age.","expected_test_result":"The result is classified stale and fallback/rejection policy executes.","testing_guidance":"Test freshness boundaries and unavailable metadata.","verification_method":"Integration and boundary-condition tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify stale behavior against documented application requirements.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime and representative storage behavior"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B068-R001","D35-B068-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B068-R001"],"requires":["Freshness contract"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Stale-read boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Eventual consistency and cache freshness patterns"],"provenance":"Established consistency pattern; code illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE","VERIFICATION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B068-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Reconciliation","concept":"Reconciliation compares distributed representations and applies a defined conflict-resolution policy to converge state where possible.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should consistency reconciliation be implemented?","answer":"Identify comparable versions, detect divergence, classify conflicts, apply an explicit resolution policy, and record unresolved cases rather than silently choosing a value.","explanation":"Reconciliation is necessary when independent systems can temporarily or permanently diverge. Silent last-write-wins behavior can destroy information and hide operational defects.","principle":"Divergence should be detected and resolved explicitly.","objective":"Implement a trustworthy consistency-reconciliation boundary.","requirements":["Version/identity metadata","Difference detection","Conflict policy","Resolution evidence","Unresolved-state handling"],"constraints":["Do not silently overwrite conflicting state","Do not assume timestamps alone establish causality"],"preconditions":["Comparable state representations exist"],"input":"Two or more state representations.","output":"Reconciled state or explicit unresolved conflict.","expected_behavior":"Differences are classified and resolved according to documented rules.","incorrect_code":"target.update(source)","corrected_code":"diff = compare(target, source)\nif diff.conflicts:\n    return UnresolvedConflict(diff)\nreturn apply_non_conflicting_changes(target, diff)","failure_mode":"Silent data loss or oscillating reconciliation.","expected_error":"ReconciliationConflict for unresolved divergence.","cause":"Reconciliation is implemented as unconditional overwrite.","root_cause":"No conflict model or convergence policy.","solution":"Compare versions and require explicit conflict resolution.","edge_case":"Two systems independently modify the same field.","security_implication":"Reconciliation must preserve tenant and authorization boundaries and must not merge data across scopes.","performance_implication":"Large reconciliation scans can be expensive; incremental change tracking may reduce work if supported.","test_case":"Create independent conflicting updates and verify they become an explicit conflict.","test_input":"Same entity, divergent field values.","expected_test_result":"Conflict is detected rather than silently overwritten.","testing_guidance":"Test identical state, additive changes, conflicting changes, deletion/update conflicts, and repeated reconciliation.","verification_method":"Deterministic reconciliation tests plus integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify repeated reconciliation converges or remains explicitly unresolved without oscillation.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript runtime/toolchain"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B068-R001","D35-B040-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B068-R001"],"requires":["Conflict-resolution policy"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Consistency reconciliation","contrasts_with":"Blind overwrite","contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Reconciliation boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Distributed reconciliation and conflict-resolution patterns"],"provenance":"Established distributed data pattern; TypeScript example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B068-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Consistency Verification","concept":"Consistency verification compares actual storage behavior against explicitly stated freshness, ordering, and convergence guarantees.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should consistency guarantees be verified?","answer":"Use controlled multi-client and multi-replica tests to measure read-after-write behavior, stale-read boundaries, concurrent updates, and reconciliation outcomes.","explanation":"Documentation can establish the intended backend semantics, but application composition can weaken or change those guarantees. Verification should cover the actual architecture.","principle":"Verify consistency at the application boundary, not only at isolated storage components.","objective":"Establish evidence for application-level consistency behavior.","requirements":["Topology-aware tests","Concurrency tests","Freshness tests","Reconciliation tests"],"constraints":["Do not infer strong guarantees from a single-node test","Do not convert measured behavior into an unsupported universal guarantee"],"preconditions":["Actual storage architecture is available for integration testing"],"input":"Consistency test scenarios.","output":"Observed consistency evidence.","expected_behavior":"Observed behavior falls within the declared contract.","incorrect_code":"assert write(value)\nassert read(key) == value","corrected_code":"receipt = write(value)\nresult = read_with_contract(key, receipt.visibility_token)\nassert result.satisfies_consistency_requirement","failure_mode":"Tests pass locally but fail under replication or concurrency.","expected_error":"Consistency assertion failure.","cause":"Test environment lacks distributed behavior.","root_cause":"Consistency verification is performed at the wrong architectural layer.","solution":"Run topology-aware integration tests.","edge_case":"Replication delay exceeds normal test assumptions.","security_implication":"Consistency tests should include security-sensitive state where stale data could affect authorization.","performance_implication":"Latency measurements must be labeled measured and tied to a specific environment/configuration.","test_case":"Run concurrent writes and reads against the actual topology.","test_input":"Controlled concurrent workload.","expected_test_result":"Observed behavior matches documented consistency requirements.","testing_guidance":"Capture topology, versions, configuration, workload, and measured results.","verification_method":"Executed integration and concurrency testing.","verification_guidance":"Do not claim guarantees beyond the tested and documented scope.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Java 17+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JDK and actual distributed storage topology"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B068-R001","D35-B068-R002","D35-B068-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B068-R001"],"requires":["Distributed integration environment"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":null,"contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":null,"tests":"Consistency boundary","verifies":"Consistency contract","derived_from":["Distributed systems verification practices"],"provenance":"Verification approach is established; no execution performed for this record.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE","EDGE-CASE","VERSION"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B069-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Batch Processing Boundary","subtopic":"Batch Contract","concept":"A batch-processing boundary defines item selection, chunking, success/failure semantics, ordering requirements, and completion criteria.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should a batch-processing contract define?","answer":"Define the input population, batch size semantics, item outcome model, retry behavior, partial failure policy, checkpoint policy, and final completion condition.","explanation":"Without these definitions, a batch job can report success despite skipped or failed items.","principle":"Batch completion must be derived from item-level evidence.","objective":"Implement a truthful batch-processing boundary.","requirements":["Input scope","Item identity","Batch/chunk semantics","Outcome model","Completion criteria"],"constraints":["Do not define success as process exit alone","Do not silently drop malformed items"],"preconditions":["Input population and processing rules are defined"],"input":"Batch item collection or iterator.","output":"Batch processing report with item-level outcomes.","expected_behavior":"Every selected item reaches a defined outcome or explicit unresolved state.","incorrect_code":"for item in items:\n    process(item)\nreturn "SUCCESS"","corrected_code":"results = []\nfor item in items:\n    try:\n        process(item)\n        results.append((item.id, "SUCCESS"))\n    except Exception as exc:\n        results.append((item.id, "FAILED", str(exc)))\nreturn BatchResult(results)","failure_mode":"Silent item loss, partial failure hidden by overall success, or unbounded memory use.","expected_error":"BatchFailed or partial-result status according to contract.","cause":"No item-level outcome model.","root_cause":"Batch processing treated as one opaque operation.","solution":"Represent item outcomes and completion criteria explicitly.","edge_case":"Input 