{"record_id":"D35-B070-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Pipeline / Processing Boundary","subtopic":"Stage Composition","concept":"Pipeline composition connects stage outputs to compatible downstream inputs while preserving ordering, failure, and resource semantics.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should pipeline stages be composed?","answer":"Validate compatibility at composition time where possible, define execution order explicitly, and keep stage dependencies visible in the composition root.","explanation":"Implicit stage discovery can create incorrect pipelines. Explicit composition makes architecture and dependency direction inspectable.","principle":"Pipeline topology should be explicit and validated.","objective":"Implement deterministic pipeline composition.","requirements":["Explicit stage ordering","Contract compatibility","Dependency visibility","Composition validation"],"constraints":["Do not connect incompatible schemas implicitly","Do not hide critical stages behind dynamic discovery"],"preconditions":["Individual stage contracts exist"],"input":"Ordered stage definitions.","output":"Executable pipeline.","expected_behavior":"Only compatible stages are composed in the declared order.","incorrect_code":"pipeline = auto_discover_all_processors()","corrected_code":"pipeline = Pipeline([\n    ValidateInput(),\n    Normalize(),\n    Enrich(),\n    Persist()\n])\npipeline.validate_contracts()","failure_mode":"Wrong stage order or incompatible data contract.","expected_error":"PipelineCompositionError.","cause":"Composition is dynamic and unvalidated.","root_cause":"Pipeline topology is not represented explicitly.","solution":"Declare stage order and validate contracts during composition.","edge_case":"A stage has optional outputs consumed by different downstream branches.","security_implication":"Composition validation must prevent unauthorized data sources from entering sensitive pipelines.","performance_implication":"Static composition validation adds negligible runtime overhead compared with data processing and can prevent expensive failures.","test_case":"Compose compatible stages and then replace one stage with an incompatible output schema.","test_input":"Valid and invalid pipeline definitions.","expected_test_result":"Valid composition succeeds; incompatible composition fails before processing.","testing_guidance":"Test order, compatibility, missing stage, optional branch, and duplicate stage cases.","verification_method":"Composition tests and architecture validation.","verification_guidance":"Verify the production composition root matches the documented pipeline graph.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript toolchain"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B008-R001","D35-B070-R001","D35-B040-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B070-R001"],"requires":["Pipeline stage contracts"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Pipeline composition","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Pipeline composition boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Composition-root and data-pipeline architecture patterns"],"provenance":"Established architecture pattern; TypeScript example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B070-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Pipeline / Processing Boundary","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Backpressure prevents an upstream producer from overwhelming a slower downstream processing stage.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should backpressure be represented in a pipeline?","answer":"Bound intermediate buffers or queues and define producer behavior when capacity is exhausted, such as blocking, bounded rejection, dropping under explicit policy, or rate adaptation.","explanation":"Unbounded buffering converts a throughput mismatch into memory growth. Backpressure makes resource limits part of the pipeline contract.","principle":"Resource capacity must constrain data flow.","objective":"Implement bounded pipeline flow.","requirements":["Bounded buffer","Capacity policy","Producer behavior","Consumer recovery"],"constraints":["Do not silently drop data unless explicitly permitted","Do not use unbounded queues for untrusted or uncontrolled producers"],"preconditions":["Pipeline has producer and consumer rate mismatch potential"],"input":"Produced records.","output":"Bounded flow into downstream stage.","expected_behavior":"Producer behavior remains within declared resource and data-loss semantics when downstream processing slows.","incorrect_code":"queue = []\nwhile source:\n    queue.append(source.read())","corrected_code":"queue = BoundedQueue(max_items=1000)\nqueue.put(record, timeout=deadline)","failure_mode":"Memory exhaustion or uncontrolled producer latency.","expected_error":"BackpressureTimeout or bounded-capacity error.","cause":"Intermediate buffering has no capacity limit.","root_cause":"Pipeline resource constraints are not modeled.","solution":"Use bounded queues and explicit saturation policy.","edge_case":"Downstream consumer stops permanently; producer must eventually observe bounded failure or cancellation.","security_implication":"Attackers can exploit unbounded buffering to exhaust resources.","performance_implication":"Backpressure can reduce throughput under saturation but protects system stability; actual behavior must be measured.","test_case":"Slow the consumer below producer rate until the queue reaches capacity.","test_input":"Producer rate greater than consumer rate.","expected_test_result":"Queue remains bounded and producer follows the saturation policy.","testing_guidance":"Test sustained overload, recovery, cancellation, and data-loss policy.","verification_method":"Load/fault tests with bounded resource observation.","verification_guidance":"Record measured queue depth and resource behavior rather than asserting unsupported performance limits.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go toolchain and pipeline runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B036-R001","D35-B070-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B070-R002"],"requires":["Bounded buffering mechanism"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Pipeline backpressure","contrasts_with":"Unbounded buffering","contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Backpressure boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Streaming systems and bounded-resource patterns"],"provenance":"Established backpressure principle; Go example illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B070-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Pipeline / Processing Boundary","subtopic":"Failure Isolation","concept":"Pipeline failure isolation determines whether one bad record, failed stage, or unavailable dependency stops the entire processing graph.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should pipeline failures be isolated?","answer":"Classify failures at record, batch, stage, and dependency levels and define which failures can be isolated without violating data correctness.","explanation":"Some malformed records can be rejected independently, while schema corruption or a critical dependency failure may require stopping the pipeline.","principle":"Failure scope should match the integrity boundary.","objective":"Prevent localized failures from becoming uncontrolled pipeline corruption while preserving correctness.","requirements":["Failure classification","Isolation scope","Dead-letter/rejection policy where applicable","Critical-stage policy"],"constraints":["Do not isolate failures that invalidate downstream correctness","Do not silently discard records"],"preconditions":["Pipeline stages define failure semantics"],"input":"Processing failure.","output":"Rejected record, failed stage, paused pipeline, or recovery action.","expected_behavior":"Failure is handled at the smallest safe scope.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    process_all(data)\nexcept Exception:\n    continue","corrected_code":"for record in data:\n    try:\n        process(record)\n    except RecordValidationError as exc:\n        rejected.append((record.id, str(exc)))\n    except CriticalDependencyError:\n        raise","failure_mode":"Critical failures are swallowed or isolated when they should stop processing.","expected_error":"CriticalDependencyError or typed record rejection.","cause":"All exceptions receive identical treatment.","root_cause":"Failure taxonomy does not map to pipeline boundaries.","solution":"Define failure categories and safe isolation scopes.","edge_case":"A malformed record indicates a systemic schema-version mismatch affecting every record.","security_implication":"Security-policy violations should not be downgraded to ordinary malformed-data errors.","performance_implication":"Per-record exception handling can have overhead; use normal control flow for expected validation failures when appropriate.","test_case":"Inject one record validation failure and one dependency outage.","test_input":"Mixed failure scenarios.","expected_test_result":"Record failure is isolated; dependency outage follows critical-stage policy.","testing_guidance":"Test each failure class and escalation path.","verification_method":"Fault-injection integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify isolation does not create silent data loss or downstream corruption.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":null,"framework_version":null,"version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime and controllable pipeline dependencies"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B039-R001","D35-B070-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B070-R001"],"requires":["Failure taxonomy"],"extends":null,"refines":null,"specializes":"Pipeline failure isolation","contrasts_with":null,"contradicts":null,"supersedes":null,"implements":"Pipeline failure boundary","tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["Fault-isolation and data-processing reliability patterns"],"provenance":"Established failure-isolation principle; code illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","implementation_coverage":["IMPLEMENTATION","INVALID IMPLEMENTATION","EDGE-CASE","TEST","VERIFICATION","SECURITY","PERFORMANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D35-B070-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Pipeline / Processing Boundary","subtopic":"End-to-End Pipeline Verification","concept":"End-to-end pipeline verification proves that data maintains its intended identity, schema, semantics, security scope, and completeness across all stages.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION","question":"How should an end-to-end data pipeline be verified?","answer":"Use representative fixtures and failure scenarios to verify stage contracts, data lineage, completeness, backpressure behavior, failure isolation, security boundaries, and final outputs.","explanation":"Individual stage tests cannot prove that composed transformations preserve meaning. End-to-end verification must follow data through the complete architecture.","principle":"Pipeline correctness is a property of composition, not merely individual stages.","objective":"Establish evidence for end-to-end pipeline correctness.","requirements":["Representative data fixtures","Stage contract checks","Lineage checks","Completeness checks","Failure tests","Security tests"],"constraints":["Do not rely only on synthetic happy-path data","Do not claim production-scale performance from functional tests"],"preconditions":["Pipeline topology and contracts are documented"],"input":"Representative end-to-end dataset and controlled failures.","output":"Verification evidence and final output comparison.","expected_behavior":"Final output satisfies semantic, completeness, security, and transformation requirements.","incorrect_code":"assert pipeline.run(data)","corrected_code":"result = pipeline.run(data)\nassert result.schema == expected_schema\nassert result.ids == expected_ids\nassert result.security_scope == expected_scope\nassert verify_invariants(result)","failure_mode":"Pipeline returns syntactically valid data with missing, duplicated, transformed, or unauthorized records.","expected_error":"PipelineVerificationError.","cause":