{"record_id":"D35-B071-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Gateway / Edge Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Request routing","concept":"Implement an explicit edge boundary that validates and routes external requests without embedding domain behavior.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should an API gateway or edge boundary be implemented so that routing remains separate from business logic?","answer":"Implement the edge component as a transport-facing boundary responsible for request normalization, authentication-context extraction, routing, rate-policy integration, and response translation. Delegate business behavior to application services rather than implementing domain decisions in the gateway.","explanation":"An edge boundary should isolate external protocol concerns from application and domain concerns. This makes internal use cases testable without the gateway and prevents transport-specific behavior from becoming accidental business policy.","principle":"External protocol concerns belong at the edge; business decisions belong behind application boundaries.","objective":"Translate and route external requests while preserving architectural dependency direction.","requirements":["Route requests to the correct application endpoint.","Normalize transport-level input.","Propagate validated request context.","Translate application outcomes into transport responses."],"constraints":["Do not place domain rules in the gateway.","Do not trust client-supplied authorization claims without validation.","Do not expose internal exceptions directly."],"preconditions":["A routing configuration exists.","Application handlers expose stable contracts."],"input":"HTTP request with method, path, headers, and body.","output":"HTTP response or delegated application result.","expected_behavior":"Valid requests are routed to the intended application boundary and transport concerns remain isolated.","incorrect_code":"def gateway(request):\n    if request.path == '/orders':\n        if request.body['total'] > 10000:\n            return 'VIP approval'\n        return order_service(request.body)","corrected_code":"def gateway(request, routes):\n    route = routes.resolve(request.method, request.path)\n    context = route.context_factory(request)\n    result = route.handler.handle(request.body, context)\n    return route.response_mapper.map(result)","failure_mode":"Gateway contains business decisions.","expected_error":"No universal runtime error; architectural violation may remain hidden until business behavior diverges.","cause":"Transport and business concerns are implemented in the same component.","root_cause":"Missing architectural boundary between edge handling and application services.","solution":"Keep gateway responsibilities limited to edge concerns and delegate use cases.","edge_case":"Unknown route or malformed request must terminate at the edge without invoking application logic.","security_implication":"Authentication and authorization context must be validated before trusted identity or tenant information reaches application code.","performance_implication":"Routing should be lightweight; expensive business computation should not occur at the edge.","test_case":"Send a valid request, an unknown route, and malformed input and verify only valid requests reach the application handler.","test_input":"POST /orders with a valid request body.","expected_test_result":"The configured order handler receives the normalized request context.","testing_guidance":"Test routing independently from application behavior and include unknown-route, malformed-input, and context-propagation cases.","verification_method":"Static architectural inspection plus automated boundary tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify gateway dependencies point inward toward application interfaces and that domain modules are not imported by transport routing code.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B024-R001","D35-B025-R001","D35-B027-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B002-R001"],"requires":["D35-B024-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B071"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture","Microsoft Architecture Center, gateway and edge integration patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B071-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Gateway / Edge Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Request normalization","concept":"Normalize external representations before application processing.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why should request normalization occur at the edge boundary?","answer":"Normalization should convert protocol-specific representations into a stable internal request contract before application logic executes.","explanation":"External clients may differ in casing, serialization, headers, optional fields, or transport conventions. Centralizing normalization prevents these differences from leaking through the application.","principle":"Normalize once at the boundary and keep internal contracts stable.","objective":"Prevent transport-specific representation differences from contaminating application logic.","requirements":["Define accepted external representations.","Validate required fields.","Convert them into an internal contract."],"constraints":["Do not silently change semantically meaningful values.","Reject ambiguous representations when normalization cannot be deterministic."],"preconditions":["An internal application request contract exists."],"input":"External request representation.","output":"Validated normalized application input.","expected_behavior":"Equivalent valid external representations produce equivalent internal inputs.","incorrect_code":"def handle(body):\n    return service.create(body)","corrected_code":"def handle(body):\n    normalized = normalize_order_request(body)\n    validate_order_request(normalized)\n    return service.create(normalized)","failure_mode":"Raw transport payload leaks into domain/application code.","expected_error":"Potential validation or type errors at arbitrary downstream locations.","cause":"No normalization boundary.","root_cause":"Application service contract is coupled to external representation.","solution":"Introduce explicit normalization and validation at the edge.","edge_case":"Two external representations that appear similar but have different semantics must not be merged silently.","security_implication":"Normalization must not bypass validation or create alternate parsing paths that evade security controls.","performance_implication":"Normalization should be deterministic and bounded in cost.","test_case":"Provide equivalent payload representations and verify they produce the same normalized application request.","test_input":"Two syntactically different but semantically equivalent JSON payloads.","expected_test_result":"Both produce the same normalized internal representation.","testing_guidance":"Include missing fields, unexpected fields, type mismatches, null values, and equivalent representations.","verification_method":"Contract tests between edge and application layers.","verification_guidance":"Verify application services receive only the defined internal request contract.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B021-R001","D35-B022-R001","D35-B023-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B021-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B071"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B071-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Gateway / Edge Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Response translation","concept":"Translate internal outcomes into stable external response contracts.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should an edge boundary translate application outcomes into API responses?","answer":"Use an explicit response mapper that converts known application outcomes into protocol-level status codes and representations, while preventing internal implementation details from escaping.","explanation":"Application results and exceptions should not be coupled directly to HTTP or another external protocol. Explicit translation preserves stable external contracts while allowing internal implementations to evolve.","principle":"Protocol response semantics are an edge concern.","objective":"Maintain a stable external API contract independent of internal application implementation.","requirements":["Define response mappings.","Define known error mappings.","Prevent accidental internal exception exposure."],"constraints":["Do not map every exception to success.","Do not expose stack traces or internal identifiers.","Unknown failures should map to a safe generic response."],"preconditions":["Application outcomes are classified.","External response contract is defined."],"input":"Application result or classified application error.","output":"Protocol response.","expected_behavior":"Known outcomes map deterministically to documented responses.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    result = service.execute(command)\n    return result\nexcept Exception as exc:\n    return {'error': str(exc)}","corrected_code":"try:\n    result = service.execute(command)\n    return response_mapper.success(result)\nexcept ApplicationError as exc:\n    return response_mapper.application_error(exc)\nexcept Exception:\n    return response_mapper.internal_error()","failure_mode":"Internal errors leak through the API.","expected_error":"Potential information disclosure or unstable response contract.","cause":"Generic exception string is returned directly.","root_cause":"Missing response translation boundary.","solution":"Classify errors and map only approved categories to external responses.","edge_case":"An unexpected exception must not reveal implementation details.","security_implication":"Error responses must avoid secrets, stack traces, SQL details, filesystem paths, and internal topology.","performance_implication":"Response mapping should add negligible processing overhead.","test_case":"Trigger a known application error and an unexpected exception and verify different safe response mappings.","test_input":"Known validation failure followed by simulated unexpected exception.","expected_test_result":"Known failure maps to documented client-facing error; unexpected failure maps to generic internal-error response.","testing_guidance":"Test every documented response mapping and verify sensitive exception information is absent.","verification_method":"API contract tests and negative security tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect serialized responses for prohibited internal details.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B020-R001","D35-B025-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B020-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B071"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OWASP, Error Handling and Information Leakage guidance","Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B071-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Gateway / Edge Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Route isolation","concept":"Keep route configuration separate from handler implementation.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How can route definitions remain independently testable?","answer":"Represent route metadata as data or small configuration objects containing method, path, context construction, handler, and response mapping, then test resolution separately from handler execution.","explanation":"Separating route resolution from handler logic reduces coupling and permits exhaustive routing tests without invoking business behavior.","principle":"Configuration selects behavior; application components implement behavior.","objective":"Make route resolution deterministic and independently testable.","requirements":["Explicit route representation.","Deterministic route matching.","Explicit handler reference."],"constraints":["Ambiguous routes must be rejected.","Dynamic path matching must not shadow more specific routes unexpectedly."],"preconditions":["Routing strategy is defined."],"input":"HTTP method and path.","output":"Resolved route or no-match result.","expected_behavior":"A request resolves to exactly one intended route or is rejected as unmatched/ambiguous.","incorrect_code":"if path.startswith('/users'):\n    return users_handler\nif path.startswith('/users/admin'):\n    return admin_handler","corrected_code":"routes = [\n    Route('GET', '/users/admin', admin_handler),\n    Route('GET', '/users', users_handler),\n]\nroute = router.resolve('GET', path)","failure_mode":"Generic route shadows a specific route.","expected_error":"Request reaches the wrong handler.","cause":"Order-dependent prefix matching.","root_cause":"Ambiguous or underspecified route-resolution semantics.","solution":"Define deterministic matching precedence or reject ambiguous route definitions.","edge_case":"Overlapping static and dynamic routes.","security_implication":"Incorrect route resolution can bypass intended authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Route lookup should avoid unbounded linear scans where route count is large.","test_case":"Register overlapping routes and verify the intended precedence or explicit ambiguity rejection.","test_input":"GET /users/admin.","expected_test_result":"The administrative route resolves deterministically.","testing_guidance":"Test exact, prefix, dynamic, unknown, and overlapping routes.","verification_method":"Route-resolution unit and architecture tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify every externally reachable route maps to an approved handler.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_REASONING","confidence":0.95,"related_records":["D35-B024-R001","D35-B027-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B071"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Microsoft Architecture Center, API gateway and routing patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B071-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"API Gateway / Edge Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Boundary verification","concept":"Verify that the edge boundary enforces architectural responsibilities rather than merely route correctness.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should an API edge boundary be verified architecturally?","answer":"Verify routing, normalization, context propagation, response translation, dependency direction, security enforcement, and absence of domain logic in the edge component.","explanation":"Successful endpoint tests alone cannot prove architectural correctness. Boundary tests and static dependency checks are needed to verify separation of responsibilities.","principle":"Behavioral correctness and architectural correctness are separate verification concerns.","objective":"Detect architectural regression in the external boundary.","requirements":["Boundary contract tests.","Dependency-direction checks.","Security and error-handling tests."],"constraints":["Do not treat passing happy-path endpoint tests as proof of architectural compliance."],"preconditions":["Architecture rules are defined.","Edge component has identifiable dependencies."],"input":"Route definitions, source dependencies, and boundary test cases.","output":"Verification result.","expected_behavior":"The edge satisfies its contract and does not depend directly on domain internals.","incorrect_code":"def verify_gateway():\n    return gateway('/orders') == 200","corrected_code":"def verify_gateway_architecture():\n    assert route_contracts_are_valid()\n    assert edge_dependencies_point_inward()\n    assert internal_errors_are_translated()\n    assert authorization_context_is_validated()","failure_mode":"Verification checks only endpoint availability.","expected_error":"Architectural violations remain undetected.","cause":"Verification scope is too narrow.","root_cause":"No explicit architecture-level acceptance criteria.","solution":"Add structural and boundary-level verification.","edge_case":"A route can return the expected response while importing a prohibited domain implementation directly.","security_implication":"Architectural coupling can bypass centralized security boundaries.","performance_implication":"Static checks should run cheaply in CI; integration tests can cover runtime behavior.","test_case":"Introduce a prohibited edge-to-domain dependency and verify architecture validation fails.","test_input":"Gateway module importing a domain implementation directly.","expected_test_result":"Architecture test reports a dependency-direction violation.","testing_guidance":"Combine static dependency tests with API contract and security tests.","verification_method":"Architecture fitness tests plus boundary integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Require all relevant checks to pass before considering the boundary implementation verified.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_REASONING","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B040-R001","D35-B027-R001","D35-B025-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B071"],"tests":["D35-B071-R001"],"verifies":["D35-B071-R001","D35-B071-R002","D35-B071-R003","D35-B071-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Microsoft Architecture Center, API gateway patterns","Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B072-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service-to-Service Communication Implementation","subtopic":"Communication contract","concept":"Implement service communication through explicit contracts rather than shared internal implementation details.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should service-to-service communication be implemented across architectural boundaries?","answer":"Define a stable communication contract and adapter at each service boundary. The caller depends on the contract, while transport details remain inside the adapter.","explanation":"Explicit contracts prevent service internals from becoming distributed coupling points and make transport changes localized.","principle":"Services communicate through contracts, not implementation internals.","objective":"Create independently evolvable service boundaries.","requirements":["Explicit request and response contracts.","Transport adapter.","Timeout and failure semantics."],"constraints":["Do not share mutable internal domain objects across services.","Do not assume remote calls are equivalent to local calls."],"preconditions":["A service ownership boundary exists.","Contract semantics are documented."],"input":"Service request contract.","output":"Service response or classified communication failure.","expected_behavior":"Remote communication uses the defined contract and handles remote failure explicitly.","incorrect_code":"from other_service.internal_models import Customer\n\ndef call(customer: Customer):\n    return remote.invoke(customer)","corrected_code":"class CustomerClient:\n    def get_customer(self, customer_id: str):\n        request = GetCustomerRequest(customer_id=customer_id)\n        return self.transport.send(request)","failure_mode":"Shared internal model creates cross-service coupling.","expected_error":"Contract breakage when provider internals change.","cause":"Caller imports provider implementation types.","root_cause":"Missing service contract boundary.","solution":"Introduce an explicit external contract and client adapter.","edge_case":"Provider adds fields or changes internal representation without changing the public contract.","security_implication":"Service authentication, authorization, and trust boundaries must remain explicit.","performance_implication":"Remote latency and serialization costs must be treated as part of the contract.","test_case":"Replace provider internals without changing the contract and verify the client remains compatible.","test_input":"Stable GetCustomer request against changed provider internals.","expected_test_result":"Client behavior remains unchanged when the external contract remains compatible.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests and failure-injection tests for timeout, unavailable provider, malformed response, and authentication failure.","verification_method":"Consumer/provider contract verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify only contract-approved fields and semantics cross the service boundary.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B007-R001","D35-B045-R001","D35-B047-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B007-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B072"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Integration patterns and service boundary principles","Microsoft Architecture Center, service-to-service communication guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B072-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service-to-Service Communication Implementation","subtopic":"Timeout propagation","concept":"Propagate bounded deadlines across service calls.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why should service-to-service calls propagate a deadline rather than wait indefinitely?","answer":"A caller should provide a bounded deadline or timeout budget that downstream calls respect, preventing an unavailable dependency from consuming unbounded resources.","explanation":"Without bounded time, waiting requests can accumulate and cause resource exhaustion or cascading failure.","principle":"Remote calls must have explicit bounded waiting semantics.","objective":"Contain latency and prevent indefinite resource occupation.","requirements":["Caller-defined timeout or deadline.","Downstream enforcement.","Clear timeout error classification."],"constraints":["Timeout values must reflect the operation's actual latency budget.","Retries must not exceed the overall deadline."],"preconditions":["The communication layer supports timeout/deadline propagation."],"input":"Request plus remaining deadline.","output":"Response or timeout failure.","expected_behavior":"The remote operation terminates or is abandoned when the deadline is exceeded.","incorrect_code":"response = client.get(url)","corrected_code":"deadline = clock.monotonic() + 2.0\nresponse = client.get(url, timeout=max(0, deadline - clock.monotonic()))","failure_mode":"Remote call waits indefinitely.","expected_error":"Timeout or resource exhaustion may occur only much later at unrelated layers.","cause":"No explicit timeout.","root_cause":"Remote operation treated as a local synchronous operation.","solution":"Propagate and enforce a bounded deadline.","edge_case":"The remaining deadline becomes zero between calculation and invocation.","security_implication":"Unbounded calls can become a denial-of-service amplification vector.","performance_implication":"Bounded calls improve resource utilization and reduce tail-latency amplification.","test_case":"Simulate a provider that never responds and verify the caller terminates within its deadline.","test_input":"Provider response delay greater than caller deadline.","expected_test_result":"Client returns a classified timeout rather than waiting indefinitely.","testing_guidance":"Test nested calls to ensure each downstream timeout fits inside the remaining budget.","verification_method":"Timeout integration tests with controlled time.","verification_guidance":"Verify no communication path lacks an explicit bounded timeout policy.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B033-R001","D35-B047-R001","D35-B050-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R001"],"requires":["D35-B047-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B072"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Builders Library, Timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter","Google SRE, handling overload and latency"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B072-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service-to-Service Communication Implementation","subtopic":"Failure classification","concept":"Classify remote failures before deciding whether a request is retryable.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should service communication failures be classified?","answer":"Translate transport and provider failures into explicit categories such as timeout, unavailable, rejected, invalid response, and non-retryable business failure before applying retry policy.","explanation":"Retry behavior depends on failure semantics. Treating every exception as retryable can amplify load and duplicate non-idempotent operations.","principle":"Failure semantics must precede retry decisions.","objective":"Prevent unsafe or wasteful retries.","requirements":["Typed communication failures.","Retryability metadata or policy.","Provider response classification."],"constraints":["Do not retry authentication, validation, or known permanent failures by default.","Do not classify ambiguous outcomes as safely retryable without evidence."],"preconditions":["Communication adapter owns transport error translation."],"input":"Transport/provider failure.","output":"Classified communication error.","expected_behavior":"Each failure is mapped to an explicit semantic category.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    return client.send(request)\nexcept Exception:\n    return retry(request)","corrected_code":"try:\n    return client.send(request)\nexcept TimeoutError as exc:\n    raise RetryableTimeout(str(exc))\nexcept AuthenticationError as exc:\n    raise NonRetryableFailure(str(exc))","failure_mode":"All failures trigger retry.","expected_error":"Repeated permanent failures or duplicate operations.","cause":"Exception taxonomy is absent.","root_cause":"Retry policy is coupled directly to low-level exceptions.","solution":"Introduce a communication failure taxonomy and explicit retryability semantics.","edge_case":"A timeout occurs after the provider accepted the request but before the response reached the caller.","security_implication":"Repeated authentication failures can create unnecessary load or obscure security events.","performance_implication":"Correct classification reduces pointless retries and retry storms.","test_case":"Inject timeout, authentication failure, malformed response, and explicit transient-unavailable responses.","test_input":"Four distinct simulated provider failures.","expected_test_result":"Each failure receives the intended retry classification.","testing_guidance":"Test ambiguous outcomes separately because timeout does not prove the remote operation was not accepted.","verification_method":"Failure-injection tests and policy inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify retry policy consumes semantic classifications rather than raw exception types alone.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B019-R001","D35-B047-R001","D35-B012-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B019-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B072"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Builders Library, Timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter","Google SRE reliability principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B072-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service-to-Service Communication Implementation","subtopic":"Compatibility testing","concept":"Verify service communication contracts independently of provider implementation.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should compatibility between communicating services be verified?","answer":"Use explicit contract tests that validate required request, response, error, and compatibility semantics without requiring callers to know provider internals.","explanation":"Unit tests on each service can pass while their integration contract is broken. Contract tests target the boundary directly.","principle":"Verify the interface between independently owned components.","objective":"Detect service contract drift before deployment.","requirements":["Versioned contract definition.","Consumer expectations.","Provider contract verification."],"constraints":["Contract tests should not encode provider internals.","Tests must distinguish compatible extensions from breaking changes."],"preconditions":["Service contract is defined."],"input":"Contract plus representative requests/responses.","output":"Compatibility verification result.","expected_behavior":"Compatible changes pass while breaking contract changes fail.","incorrect_code":"assert provider_customer.dict == consumer_customer.dict","corrected_code":"assert contract.validate_response(provider_response)\nassert consumer_expectations.validate(provider_response)","failure_mode":"Tests couple directly to provider internals.","expected_error":"False failures or missed breaking changes.","cause":"Implementation structure used as the contract.","root_cause":"No explicit boundary contract.","solution":"Validate documented contract semantics instead of internal object structure.","edge_case":"Provider adds an optional response field.","security_implication":"Contract tests should include authorization and data-exposure constraints where relevant.","performance_implication":"Contract suites should remain deterministic and bounded enough for CI.","test_case":"Add an optional response field and remove a required field in separate test versions.","test_input":"Compatible extension and breaking removal.","expected_test_result":"Optional extension remains compatible; required-field removal fails verification.","testing_guidance":"Cover schema, semantics, errors, authentication expectations, and version compatibility.","verification_method":"Consumer-driven or provider contract testing.","verification_guidance":"Require contract verification before releasing incompatible service versions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B045-R001","D35-B046-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B045-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B072"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Integration Contract Testing","Microsoft Architecture Center, API evolution guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B072-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service-to-Service Communication Implementation","subtopic":"Remote failure containment","concept":"Prevent one service failure from automatically exhausting caller resources.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How can service-to-service communication contain downstream failure?","answer":"Combine bounded timeouts, controlled retries, circuit-breaking or equivalent admission control, concurrency limits, and explicit fallback/degraded semantics where appropriate.","explanation":"A dependency failure can otherwise propagate through a call graph and consume threads, connections, queues, and CPU across multiple services.","principle":"Contain dependency failure at the boundary.","objective":"Prevent cascading resource exhaustion.","requirements":["Timeouts.","Bounded retry policy.","Concurrency or admission control.","Explicit degraded behavior where supported."],"constraints":["Fallback must not violate business correctness or security.","Retries must be bounded by the original deadline."],"preconditions":["Failure policy is defined for the dependency."],"input":"Remote operation request.","output":"Successful result or contained failure/degraded result.","expected_behavior":"Dependency failure remains bounded to the intended caller scope.","incorrect_code":"for _ in range(100):\n    try:\n        return remote.call()\n    except Exception:\n        pass\nraise RuntimeError('failed')","corrected_code":"for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n    try:\n        return remote.call(timeout=remaining_deadline())\n    except RetryableFailure:\n        if not retry_budget_allows(attempt):\n            break\nreturn fallback_or_fail()","failure_mode":"Unbounded retry amplifies dependency failure.","expected_error":"Resource exhaustion, retry storm, or cascading latency.","cause":"No bounded retry/admission policy.","root_cause":"Remote failure treated as an isolated exception instead of a systemic resource problem.","solution":"Apply bounded failure containment at the communication boundary.","edge_case":"Dependency partially recovers while callers still have exhausted retry budgets.","security_implication":"Fallback must not bypass authorization or expose stale sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Failure containment should reduce amplification during dependency outages.","test_case":"Simulate sustained provider failure under concurrent caller load.","test_input":"Dependency unavailable for longer than retry budget.","expected_test_result":"Callers fail or degrade within bounded resource and time limits.","testing_guidance":"Measure bounded attempts, timeout budgets, concurrent in-flight requests, and recovery behavior.","verification_method":"Failure-injection and load tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify the containment mechanism itself does not become an unbounded queue or retry source.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B047-R001","D35-B048-R001","D35-B050-R001","D35-B077-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B047-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B072"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Builders Library, Timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter","Release It!, stability patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B073-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Message Routing / Topic Partitioning Implementation","subtopic":"Routing contract","concept":"Implement message routing from explicit message type and routing metadata.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should messages be routed to consumers?","answer":"Define routing keys or message types as explicit contract data and resolve them through a deterministic routing table.","explanation":"Implicit routing based on payload inspection or consumer assumptions creates hidden coupling and ambiguous delivery behavior.","principle":"Routing decisions should be explicit, deterministic, and observable.","objective":"Deliver each message to the intended processing path.","requirements":["Stable routing identity.","Deterministic resolver.","Explicit unknown-route behavior."],"constraints":["Unknown message types must not silently execute arbitrary handlers.","Routing metadata must be validated."],"preconditions":["Message envelope defines routing metadata."],"input":"Message envelope.","output":"Selected handler/topic/partition or rejected message.","expected_behavior":"The same routing metadata consistently resolves to the same intended destination.","incorrect_code":"def route(message):\n    if 'email' in str(message.payload):\n        return email_handler","corrected_code":"def route(message, routes):\n    route = routes.get(message.message_type)\n    if route is None:\n        raise UnknownMessageType(message.message_type)\n    return route","failure_mode":"Payload content accidentally determines routing.","expected_error":"Wrong handler or silent message loss.","cause":"Routing metadata is implicit.","root_cause":"No explicit routing contract.","solution":"Route using validated message type or routing key.","edge_case":"Unknown or deprecated message type.","security_implication":"Routing metadata must not allow arbitrary handler selection or code execution.","performance_implication":"Indexed routing tables provide predictable lookup cost.","test_case":"Resolve known, unknown, and deprecated message types.","test_input":"Three message envelopes with different message types.","expected_test_result":"Known type resolves; unknown/deprecated types follow explicit policy.","testing_guidance":"Test every routing key and failure path.","verification_method":"Routing unit tests and contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify routing is deterministic and independent of payload content unless payload-based routing is explicitly part of the contract.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_EVENT_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B061-R002","D35-B073-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B061-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B073"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns, Message Router"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B073-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Message Routing / Topic Partitioning Implementation","subtopic":"Partition-key semantics","concept":"Use stable partition keys when per-entity ordering or affinity is required.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"When should a message stream use a stable partition key?","answer":"Use a stable key derived from the entity or ordering domain when messages for that domain must be processed with consistent partition affinity.","explanation":"Partitioning can provide localized ordering and parallelism, but only within the guarantees of the underlying messaging system. A key should represent the actual ordering domain.","principle":"Partition by the unit whose relative ordering matters.","objective":"Balance parallel processing with required ordering semantics.","requirements":["Defined ordering domain.","Stable key extraction.","Known partitioning semantics."],"constraints":["A partition key does not guarantee global ordering.","Hot keys can create load imbalance."],"preconditions":["Messaging infrastructure supports partitioning."],"input":"Message with ordering-domain identifier.","output":"Partition assignment.","expected_behavior":"Messages sharing the same ordering key are routed according to the messaging system's documented partition semantics.","incorrect_code":"partition = hash(random_uuid()) % partition_count","corrected_code":"partition = stable_hash(message.ordering_key) % partition_count","failure_mode":"Messages from one ordering domain are randomly distributed.","expected_error":"Required per-key ordering may be violated.","cause":"Unstable partition key.","root_cause":"Partitioning does not reflect business ordering domain.","solution":"Use a stable ordering-domain key.","edge_case":"One key receives disproportionate traffic.","security_implication":"Sensitive identifiers should not be exposed in routing metadata unless appropriate; use derived opaque keys when needed.","performance_implication":"Partitioning increases parallelism but hot keys can become bottlenecks.","test_case":"Send multiple messages for the same key and verify partition affinity.","test_input":"Messages with ordering keys A, A, B.","expected_test_result":"A messages use the same partition according to the system's partitioning contract; B may use another partition.","testing_guidance":"Test hot keys, null keys, key changes, and partition-count changes.","verification_method":"Messaging-system integration tests plus deterministic key tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify assumptions against the actual broker's documented partition semantics; do not infer stronger ordering guarantees.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MESSAGING_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B061-R003","D35-B068-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B061-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B073"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns, Message Channel and Message Dispatcher concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B073-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Message Routing / Topic Partitioning Implementation","subtopic":"Consumer selection","concept":"Separate routing from consumer execution.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why should message routing be separated from consumer execution?","answer":"A router should select the destination while the consumer owns processing behavior, allowing routing policies to change without duplicating processing logic.","explanation":"Combining routing and processing makes routing rules difficult to test and increases the chance that one consumer accidentally performs another consumer's responsibilities.","principle":"Selection and execution are separate responsibilities.","objective":"Keep message delivery topology independently testable.","requirements":["Routing resolver.","Consumer contract.","Explicit dispatch step."],"constraints":["Router should not contain business processing.","Consumer failures must not corrupt routing state."],"preconditions":["Consumers expose stable handlers."],"input":"Resolved route and message.","output":"Consumer processing result.","expected_behavior":"Router selects a consumer and delegates processing without changing message semantics.","incorrect_code":"def route_and_process(message):\n    if message.type == 'invoice':\n        save_invoice_and_send_email(message)\n    elif message.type == 'shipment':\n        update_inventory(message)","corrected_code":"def dispatch(message, router):\n    consumer = router.resolve(message.type)\n    return consumer.handle(message)","failure_mode":"Router becomes a business-processing component.","expected_error":"Routing changes require modifying business logic.","cause":"Selection and processing are coupled.","root_cause":"No separation between routing and consumption.","solution":"Introduce a dispatch boundary.","edge_case":"Multiple consumers intentionally subscribe to one event; fan-out semantics must be explicit.","security_implication":"Consumer authorization should not be inferred solely from routing selection.","performance_implication":"Dispatch should avoid unnecessary serialization/deserialization between local routing stages.","test_case":"Change routing for one message type without modifying the consumer and verify processing remains unchanged.","test_input":"Route type invoice from consumer A to consumer B.","expected_test_result":"New consumer receives the message according to the updated routing policy.","testing_guidance":"Test single-target, fan-out, unknown-target, and consumer-failure scenarios.","verification_method":"Routing and dispatch unit tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify router contains no domain-processing operations.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_REASONING","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B061-R001","D35-B061-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B061-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B073"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns, Message Router and Message Dispatcher"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B073-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Message Routing / Topic Partitioning Implementation","subtopic":"Unknown-message handling","concept":"Make unknown message handling explicit and auditable.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What should happen when a consumer receives an unknown message type?","answer":"Reject, quarantine, or route the message to an explicitly defined compatibility or dead-letter path rather than silently discarding it or invoking a default handler.","explanation":"Silent fallback can turn schema or deployment errors into data loss. Explicit handling preserves diagnosability and supports controlled compatibility.","principle":"Unknown inputs require explicit failure semantics.","objective":"Prevent silent message loss or unsafe default processing.","requirements":["Unknown-type policy.","Observable rejection/quarantine path.","Preservation of diagnostic metadata."],"constraints":["Do not invoke arbitrary default handlers.","Do not discard unknown messages silently."],"preconditions":["Message type can be inspected before processing."],"input":"Unknown message.","output":"Rejected, quarantined, or explicitly compatibility-routed message.","expected_behavior":"Unknown messages follow documented policy and remain observable.","incorrect_code":"handler = routes.get(message.type, default_handler)\nhandler(message)","corrected_code":"handler = routes.get(message.type)\nif handler is None:\n    quarantine(message, reason='unknown_message_type')\n    return\nhandler(message)","failure_mode":"Unknown messages invoke a generic handler.","expected_error":"Potential semantic corruption or silent data loss.","cause":"Unsafe default routing.","root_cause":"Unknown-message policy not modeled explicitly.","solution":"Quarantine or reject unknown messages with diagnostic context.","edge_case":"A new producer deploys before all consumers are upgraded.","security_implication":"Unknown content must not reach privileged processing paths by default.","performance_implication":"Quarantine storage must be bounded and monitored.","test_case":"Send a message with an unsupported type and verify quarantine behavior.","test_input":"message_type='future.event.v3'.","expected_test_result":"Message is quarantined or rejected according to policy; no business handler executes.","testing_guidance":"Test unknown, deprecated, malformed, and partially compatible messages.","verification_method":"Negative integration testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify the message remains recoverable for diagnosis where policy requires it.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B045-R001","D35-B061-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B045-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B073"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns, Invalid Message Channel","Martin Fowler, evolutionary architecture principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B073-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Message Routing / Topic Partitioning Implementation","subtopic":"Routing verification","concept":"Verify routing correctness independently from message processing correctness.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should message routing be verified?","answer":"Construct routing matrices covering message type, version, tenant or context where relevant, destination, partition key, unknown types, and failure paths; then test the resolver independently from consumers.","explanation":"Consumer tests cannot prove that the right consumer receives a message. Routing requires its own verification surface.","principle":"Delivery correctness is distinct from processing correctness.","objective":"Detect routing errors before they become processing or data-integrity failures.","requirements":["Routing matrix.","Deterministic resolver tests.","Negative routing tests."],"constraints":["Do not use successful consumer execution as evidence that routing was correct."],"preconditions":["Routing contract exists."],"input":"Routing rules and representative message envelopes.","output":"Routing verification report.","expected_behavior":"Every supported routing case maps to its intended destination and unsupported cases follow explicit policy.","incorrect_code":"def test_routing():\n    assert any_consumer_processed(message)","corrected_code":"def test_routing():\n    assert router.resolve(message_type='invoice') is invoice_consumer\n    assert router.resolve(message_type='unknown') is None","failure_mode":"Tests validate only downstream processing.","expected_error":"Wrong destination may remain undetected.","cause":"Routing and processing tests are conflated.","root_cause":"Missing routing-specific acceptance criteria.","solution":"Test route resolution directly.","edge_case":"Route precedence changes after a new route is added.","security_implication":"Routing tests should cover security-sensitive destinations.","performance_implication":"Routing tests should detect pathological route lookup complexity where relevant.","test_case":"Add an overlapping route and verify the routing test detects ambiguity or correct precedence.","test_input":"Two overlapping routing definitions.","expected_test_result":"Test either resolves according to documented precedence or rejects the ambiguous configuration.","testing_guidance":"Maintain a routing matrix as part of the architecture contract.","verification_method":"Unit, contract, and configuration validation tests.","verification_guidance":"Require zero unexplained routing cases before release.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_REASONING","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B073-R001","D35-B073-R004","D35-B040-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B073"],"tests":["D35-B073-R001","D35-B073-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B073-R001","D35-B073-R002","D35-B073-R003","D35-B073-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns","Architecture fitness function principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B074-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event Replay / Reprocessing Implementation","subtopic":"Replay contract","concept":"Implement replay as a controlled reprocessing operation over retained event data.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What should an event replay boundary define?","answer":"A replay boundary should define source range or selection, destination processing mode, ordering assumptions, isolation from live traffic where required, idempotency behavior, authorization, and observability.","explanation":"Replay is not simply reading old events again. Reprocessing can alter current state, trigger side effects, or duplicate external actions.","principle":"Historical data replay is an operationally controlled processing mode.","objective":"Reprocess historical events safely and deterministically within documented limits.","requirements":["Explicit replay scope.","Authorization.","Idempotent or side-effect-aware handlers.","Auditability."],"constraints":["Replay must not silently trigger unsafe external side effects.","Historical event semantics must be compatible with the current consumer."],"preconditions":["Events are retained and addressable.","Replay policy exists."],"input":"Replay request specifying event selection and processing mode.","output":"Replay job/result and processing records.","expected_behavior":"Only authorized events are reprocessed according to the selected replay policy.","incorrect_code":"for event in all_events:\n    current_handler(event)","corrected_code":"def replay(selection, handler, policy):\n    authorize_replay(policy)\n    for event in selection:\n        handler.process(event, mode='REPLAY', policy=policy)","failure_mode":"Replay processes unrestricted historical data.","expected_error":"Unexpected state changes or duplicate side effects.","cause":"No explicit replay scope or mode.","root_cause":"Replay treated as ordinary consumption.","solution":"Create a dedicated replay contract and execution mode.","edge_case":"Historical event schema is no longer compatible with the current handler.","security_implication":"Replay can expose or mutate sensitive historical data; authorization and audit are mandatory.","performance_implication":"Large replays require bounded concurrency, checkpointing, and resource controls.","test_case":"Replay a small known event range with side effects disabled and verify only selected events are processed.","test_input":"Replay selection containing event IDs E1-E3.","expected_test_result":"Only E1-E3 enter replay mode and replay metadata is recorded.","testing_guidance":"Test selection boundaries, authorization, schema compatibility, duplicate processing, cancellation, and restart.","verification_method":"Controlled replay integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify replay scope, mode, authorization, and resulting state before allowing production use.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_EVENT_PROCESSING_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B061-R001","D35-B075-R001","D35-B069-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B061-R001"],"requires":["D35-B018-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B074"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Event Sourcing and event replay concepts","Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B074-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event Replay / Reprocessing Implementation","subtopic":"Replay isolation","concept":"Separate replay processing from live processing when simultaneous execution could interfere.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why may replay need isolation from live event processing?","answer":"Replay should use an isolated processing context or controlled scheduling when historical processing could compete with or alter live state, consume excessive capacity, or produce duplicate side effects.","explanation":"Replay can be computationally expensive and semantically different from live processing. Isolation prevents historical work from destabilizing current operations.","principle":"Historical processing must not implicitly compete with or override live processing semantics.","objective":"Contain replay resource and state impact.","requirements":["Replay execution context.","Resource limits.","Explicit interaction policy with live processing."],"constraints":["Isolation does not automatically guarantee semantic independence.","State writes require explicit policy."],"preconditions":["Replay workload can be separated or throttled."],"input":"Replay workload.","output":"Isolated replay processing result.","expected_behavior":"Replay stays within its resource and semantic boundary.","incorrect_code":"def replay(events):\n    for event in events:\n        live_consumer(event)","corrected_code":"def replay(events, replay_consumer, limiter):\n    for event in events:\n        limiter.acquire()\n        replay_consumer.process(event, mode='REPLAY')","failure_mode":"Replay competes directly with live processing.","expected_error":"Latency degradation, resource starvation, or unintended live-state mutation.","cause":"No replay-specific execution policy.","root_cause":"Replay considered identical to live consumption.","solution":"Introduce isolated or explicitly throttled replay processing.","edge_case":"Replay catches up to events currently being processed live.","security_implication":"Replay credentials should be scoped to the minimum required historical data and actions.","performance_implication":"Resource quotas and concurrency limits prevent replay from consuming all processing capacity.","test_case":"Run replay while simulated live traffic continues and verify live processing remains within its defined resource budget.","test_input":"Historical replay workload plus concurrent live workload.","expected_test_result":"Replay is bounded and live processing remains isolated according to policy.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrency, throttling, cancellation, and resource exhaustion.","verification_method":"Controlled load and isolation tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify replay cannot bypass live authorization or resource policies.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RELIABILITY_REASONING","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B050-R001","D35-B077-R001","D35-B069-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B050-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B074"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE reliability principles","Martin Fowler, event replay concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B074-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event Replay / Reprocessing Implementation","subtopic":"Side-effect control","concept":"Separate state reconstruction from external side effects during replay.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should replay prevent unintended external side effects?","answer":"Use an explicit replay mode that either suppresses external effects, routes them to a safe sink, or requires a separately authorized effect policy.","explanation":"Historical reprocessing may invoke email, payments, notifications, provisioning, or other irreversible operations. Re-executing those effects can create real-world duplicates.","principle":"Replay semantics must explicitly define external-effect behavior.","objective":"Prevent accidental duplicate or unauthorized side effects.","requirements":["Replay mode.","Effect policy.","Audit record for suppressed or redirected effects."],"constraints":["Suppression must not be mistaken for successful external delivery.","Safe sinks must remain clearly distinct from production destinations."],"preconditions":["Application can distinguish state processing from external effects."],"input":"Historical event and replay policy.","output":"Reconstructed state and effect decision.","expected_behavior":"External effects follow the replay policy rather than default live behavior.","incorrect_code":"def process(event):\n    update_state(event)\n    send_email(event.customer)\n\nfor event in history:\n    process(event)","corrected_code":"def process(event, mode):\n    update_state(event)\n    if mode == 'LIVE':\n        send_email(event.customer)\n    elif mode == 'REPLAY':\n        record_suppressed_effect(event, 'send_email')","failure_mode":"Replay sends production side effects.","expected_error":"Duplicate notifications or irreversible external actions.","cause":"No distinction between live and replay execution.","root_cause":"Side effects are inseparable from replay policy.","solution":"Introduce explicit execution mode and effect handling.","edge_case":"A side effect is required to reconstruct a state transition; the policy must distinguish necessary state writes from external effects.","security_implication":"Replay must not gain broader privileges than live processing.","performance_implication":"Recording suppressed effects can increase storage volume and must be bounded.","test_case":"Replay an event that normally sends a notification and verify the production notification path is not invoked.","test_input":"Historical notification event in REPLAY mode.","expected_test_result":"State processing occurs according to policy; production notification is suppressed or safely redirected.","testing_guidance":"Test every side-effect category individually.","verification_method":"Mocked side-effect boundary plus integration verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that replay mode is propagated through all side-effect-capable components.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"SAFETY_CRITICAL_ARCHITECTURAL_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B007-R001","D35-B074-R001","D35-B043-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B007-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B074"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Event Sourcing and replay concepts","Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B074-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event Replay / Reprocessing Implementation","subtopic":"Checkpointed replay","concept":"Checkpoint replay progress so interrupted reprocessing can resume without restarting the entire selection.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should replay progress be checkpointed?","answer":"Persist a replay job identity, selection, processing cursor or equivalent durable position, and outcome metadata so the job can resume according to defined ordering and idempotency semantics.","explanation":"Long replays can be interrupted by process failure, deployment, or operator cancellation. Durable progress reduces repeated work but does not by itself guarantee exactly-once effects.","principle":"Durable progress and idempotent processing are complementary safeguards.","objective":"Enable recoverable replay without falsely claiming exactly-once execution.","requirements":["Durable job state.","Checkpoint position.","Idempotent or duplicate-safe processing."],"constraints":["Checkpoint advancement must correspond to the documented processing acknowledgment semantics.","A checkpoint must not imply downstream side effects succeeded unless verified."],"preconditions":["Replay source provides a stable ordering or cursor."],"input":"Replay job and current cursor.","output":"Updated checkpoint and processing outcome.","expected_behavior":"An interrupted replay can resume from a safe checkpoint according to documented semantics.","incorrect_code":"for event in events:\n    process(event)\n# no durable progress","corrected_code":"for event in events:\n    process(event)\n    checkpoint_store.commit(job_id, event.sequence)","failure_mode":"Replay restarts from the beginning after interruption.","expected_error":"Excessive repeated work and potential duplicate effects.","cause":"Progress exists only in memory.","root_cause":"No durable replay state.","solution":"Persist replay checkpoints and combine them with idempotent handling.","edge_case":"Process fails after the event is processed but before checkpoint persistence.","security_implication":"Checkpoint records can contain sensitive event identifiers and require appropriate access controls.","performance_implication":"Checkpoint frequency trades recovery granularity against persistence overhead.","test_case":"Interrupt replay after processing a checkpoint boundary and resume the job.","test_input":"Replay of ordered events 1-100 with checkpoint every 10 events.","expected_test_result":"Resume follows the documented checkpoint/idempotency semantics without losing events.","testing_guidance":"Test crash before processing, after processing, before checkpoint, and after checkpoint.","verification_method":"Failure-injection recovery test.","verification_guidance":"Do not label the replay exactly-once unless the complete processing system actually establishes that guarantee.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_PROCESSING_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B069-R004","D35-B018-R001","D35-B017-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B018-R001"],"requires":["D35-B069-R004"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B074"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Enterprise Integration Patterns, Idempotent Receiver","Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B074-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Event Replay / Reprocessing Implementation","subtopic":"Replay verification","concept":"Verify replay outcome against an explicit expected-state or invariant model.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should a replay operation be verified?","answer":"Verify selection, processing counts, errors, state invariants, suppressed effects, and resulting state against a defined expected outcome. Do not equate successful iteration with successful reconstruction.","explanation":"Replay can complete technically while producing incorrect state or silently skipping incompatible events.","principle":"Processing completion is not equivalent to semantic correctness.","objective":"Establish evidence that replay produced the intended state and side-effect behavior.","requirements":["Replay audit metadata.","Expected invariants or comparison state.","Error and skipped-event accounting."],"constraints":["Historical-to-current semantic changes may require explicit migration logic.","Exact state equality may not be possible when nondeterminism exists."],"preconditions":["Expected invariants are defined."],"input":"Replay result and verification criteria.","output":"Verification result.","expected_behavior":"Replay is accepted only when required invariants and processing evidence are satisfied.","incorrect_code":"assert replay_job.status == 'completed'","corrected_code":"assert replay_job.status == 'completed'\nassert replay_job.failed_events == 0\nassert verify_state_invariants()\nassert verify_effect_policy()","failure_mode":"Job completion is treated as proof of correctness.","expected_error":"Semantic corruption remains undetected.","cause":"Verification checks execution status only.","root_cause":"No semantic replay acceptance criteria.","solution":"Verify state, events, failures, and effect policy separately.","edge_case":"Replay intentionally skips unsupported historical events; verification must compare skips against approved policy.","security_implication":"Verification records should not expose sensitive event payloads unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Full state comparison may be expensive; define bounded invariants and sampling only when justified.","test_case":"Inject one incompatible historical event and verify replay is not accepted as fully successful.","test_input":"Replay containing one unsupported event.","expected_test_result":"Verification reports the incomplete result according to policy.","testing_guidance":"Cover complete, partial, failed, and intentionally skipped replay outcomes.","verification_method":"Post-replay invariant and audit verification.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence for every non-success outcome.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B074-R001","D35-B069-R005","D35-B040-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B074"],"tests":["D35-B074-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B074-R001","D35-B074-R002","D35-B074-R003","D35-B074-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE verification and correctness principles","Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B075-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Correlation / Context Propagation Implementation","subtopic":"Correlation context","concept":"Propagate a stable correlation identifier across related operations.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should distributed operations preserve correlation context?","answer":"Create or accept a validated correlation identifier at the boundary and propagate it through application calls, asynchronous messages, logs, and tracing contexts according to the system contract.","explanation":"A distributed operation can cross processes and asynchronous boundaries. Correlation metadata allows related records to be reconstructed without coupling business logic to transport details.","principle":"Operational context should travel with the operation while remaining separate from business state.","objective":"Make distributed execution reconstructable for debugging and verification.","requirements":["Correlation identifier.","Propagation mechanism.","Validation and bounded format."],"constraints":["Correlation IDs are not authentication credentials.","Do not trust arbitrary client values as security identity."],"preconditions":["The system has a context propagation contract."],"input":"Incoming request or message context.","output":"Validated propagated context.","expected_behavior":"Related downstream operations carry the same correlation context unless a new independent operation is intentionally created.","incorrect_code":"current_request_id = global_request_id","corrected_code":"def with_context(request, context):\n    correlation_id = context.correlation_id or new_correlation_id()\n    return request.with_context(correlation_id=correlation_id)","failure_mode":"Correlation state stored in unsafe global mutable state.","expected_error":"Concurrent operations may receive incorrect identifiers.","cause":"Context lifetime is not tied to operation lifetime.","root_cause":"Global state used for request-specific context.","solution":"Use explicit scoped context propagation.","edge_case":"Background task continues after the originating request ends.","security_implication":"Correlation metadata must not be treated as proof of identity or authorization.","performance_implication":"Context propagation should be lightweight and bounded.","test_case":"Run concurrent operations with distinct correlation IDs and verify no cross-contamination.","test_input":"Two concurrent operations with IDs A and B.","expected_test_result":"Every downstream record for operation A carries A and operation B carries B.","testing_guidance":"Test synchronous, asynchronous, queued, and retry paths.","verification_method":"Context propagation tests and log/tracing correlation checks.","verification_guidance":"Verify correlation identity survives every intended architectural boundary.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B030-R001","D35-B041-R001","D35-B075-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B030-R001"],"requires":["D35-B041-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B075"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["W3C Trace Context","OpenTelemetry context propagation documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B075-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Correlation / Context Propagation Implementation","subtopic":"Trace context","concept":"Separate trace context from business correlation while allowing them to be linked.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Should trace context and business correlation identifiers be treated as the same value?","answer":"They may be linked but should remain conceptually distinct. Trace context represents distributed tracing state, while a business correlation identifier represents an operational or business-level relationship.","explanation":"Tracing systems may create and rotate trace/span identifiers according to tracing semantics, while business workflows may need a stable identifier across longer or asynchronous lifetimes.","principle":"Operational identifiers should match their intended lifetime and semantics.","objective":"Avoid using one identifier for incompatible responsibilities.","requirements":["Explicit trace context handling.","Optional business correlation identifier.","Defined relationship between them."],"constraints":["Do not expose tracing identifiers as authentication tokens.","Do not assume one trace remains valid for an entire business workflow."],"preconditions":["Tracing and business correlation needs are identified."],"input":"Trace context and optional business correlation identifier.","output":"Propagated operational context.","expected_behavior":"Tracing and business correlation remain semantically correct while being linkable.","incorrect_code":"business_order_id = trace_id","corrected_code":"context = {\n    'trace_id': trace_context.trace_id,\n    'correlation_id': business_correlation_id,\n}","failure_mode":"Trace identity is used as business identity.","expected_error":"Correlation breaks when trace boundaries change.","cause":"Different identifier lifetimes are conflated.","root_cause":"No distinction between observability and business context.","solution":"Store separate identifiers and link them explicitly.","edge_case":"An asynchronous workflow creates multiple traces.","security_implication":"Neither tracing nor correlation identifiers should confer authorization.","performance_implication":"Additional context fields have small but nonzero propagation and storage cost.","test_case":"Split one business workflow across two traces and verify the business correlation remains stable.","test_input":"Workflow with trace A followed by trace B.","expected_test_result":"Both traces can be linked to the same business correlation identifier.","testing_guidance":"Test synchronous, asynchronous, retry, and long-running workflow boundaries.","verification_method":"Trace/log correlation verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify identifiers are not accidentally used as access-control decisions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B041-R001","D35-B043-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B041-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B075"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["W3C Trace Context","OpenTelemetry specification"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B075-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Correlation / Context Propagation Implementation","subtopic":"Async propagation","concept":"Explicitly propagate context across asynchronous execution boundaries.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why can asynchronous execution lose correlation context?","answer":"Asynchronous tasks can outlive or detach from the initiating execution context, so context propagation must be defined by the task submission and worker boundaries rather than assumed implicitly.","explanation":"Thread-local or request-local context may not automatically survive queues, worker processes, serialization, or delayed execution.","principle":"Every asynchronous boundary is a context boundary unless propagation is explicitly guaranteed.","objective":"Preserve operational context across queued and background work.","requirements":["Serializable context representation.","Task-envelope propagation.","Worker restoration."],"constraints":["Do not serialize sensitive credentials into task context.","Context must remain bounded in size."],"preconditions":["Worker architecture supports task envelopes."],"input":"Task plus originating context.","output":"Worker execution with restored context.","expected_behavior":"Worker logs and traces can associate the task with its originating operation.","incorrect_code":"queue.put(task)\n# worker assumes request-local context still exists","corrected_code":"queue.put(TaskEnvelope(task=task, correlation_id=context.correlation_id))","failure_mode":"Worker loses correlation metadata.","expected_error":"Disconnected logs and incomplete traces.","cause":"Context was not included in task boundary.","root_cause":"Asynchronous boundary treated as transparent.","solution":"Serialize only required operational context into the task envelope.","edge_case":"Task is retried by another worker; correlation must remain stable while attempt identity may change.","security_implication":"Task context must exclude secrets and untrusted identity claims unless explicitly protected and validated.","performance_implication":"Small context envelopes reduce queue and serialization overhead.","test_case":"Submit a task, process it in a separate worker context, and verify correlation metadata is restored.","test_input":"Task with correlation ID C123.","expected_test_result":"Worker execution records contain C123.","testing_guidance":"Test retries, dead-lettering, worker restarts, and cross-process execution.","verification_method":"Queue integration tests and log correlation assertions.","verification_guidance":"Verify only approved context fields cross the asynchronous boundary.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ASYNC_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B036-R001","D35-B075-R001","D35-B018-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B075"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry context propagation documentation","W3C Trace Context"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B075-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Correlation / Context Propagation Implementation","subtopic":"Context validation","concept":"Validate propagated context before accepting it into trusted operational processing.","knowledge_type":"security","question":"Why should propagated correlation and tracing metadata be validated?","answer":"External or cross-service context can be malformed, oversized, or semantically invalid. Validation prevents context from becoming an unbounded or unsafe input channel.","explanation":"Observability metadata is still input. It should be constrained even though it is not normally business data.","principle":"Operational metadata must satisfy the same basic input-boundary discipline as other external data.","objective":"Keep context propagation safe and bounded.","requirements":["Format validation.","Length limits.","Character/encoding constraints where applicable."],"constraints":["Validation must preserve protocol semantics.","Do not use context validation as a substitute for authentication."],"preconditions":["Context schema is defined."],"input":"Incoming context headers or message metadata.","output":"Accepted normalized context or rejection.","expected_behavior":"Malformed or oversized context is rejected or safely normalized.","incorrect_code":"correlation_id = request.headers.get('X-Correlation-ID')","corrected_code":"raw = request.headers.get('X-Correlation-ID')\ncorrelation_id = validate_correlation_id(raw)","failure_mode":"Unbounded user-controlled context is accepted.","expected_error":"Log injection, excessive memory/storage use, or malformed propagation.","cause":"No validation or length restriction.","root_cause":"Operational metadata treated as inherently trusted.","solution":"Validate and bound context at the edge.","edge_case":"Missing correlation ID should result in controlled generation where policy permits.","security_implication":"Prevent header/log injection and avoid treating correlation data as trusted identity.","performance_implication":"Validation should be constant or bounded relative to context size.","test_case":"Submit oversized and malformed correlation metadata.","test_input":"Correlation value exceeding configured maximum length.","expected_test_result":"Input is rejected or replaced according to explicit policy.","testing_guidance":"Test missing, malformed, oversized, encoded, and duplicate context values.","verification_method":"Boundary security tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify context cannot alter authorization decisions.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DEFENSIVE_SECURITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B021-R001","D35-B026-R001","D35-B030-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B021-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B075"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OWASP, input validation and logging guidance","W3C Trace Context"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B075-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Correlation / Context Propagation Implementation","subtopic":"Propagation verification","concept":"Verify context propagation across every architectural boundary.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should distributed context propagation be verified?","answer":"Trace a representative operation across synchronous calls, asynchronous tasks, retries, and error paths and assert that required context fields remain consistent and correctly scoped.","explanation":"A context implementation may work in synchronous tests but fail at queues, retries, worker boundaries, or exception paths.","principle":"Verify context continuity at boundaries, not only inside one process.","objective":"Detect lost, contaminated, or incorrectly trusted operational context.","requirements":["Cross-boundary test scenario.","Context assertions.","Negative contamination test."],"constraints":["Tests must distinguish correlation continuity from authentication continuity."],"preconditions":["Propagation contract is documented."],"input":"End-to-end operation with multiple boundaries.","output":"Propagation verification result.","expected_behavior":"Required context survives intended boundaries without cross-request contamination.","incorrect_code":"assert request_context.correlation_id == worker_context.correlation_id","corrected_code":"assert all(record.correlation_id == expected_id for record in operation_records)\nassert no_record_from_other_operation_is_correlated(expected_id)","failure_mode":"Test checks one hop only.","expected_error":"Cross-boundary propagation failures remain undetected.","cause":"Insufficient test topology.","root_cause":"Propagation considered a local implementation detail.","solution":"Test the full boundary chain.","edge_case":"Retry attempt changes span identity but preserves business correlation.","security_implication":"Verification must ensure context does not become an authorization channel.","performance_implication":"End-to-end context tests should remain small and deterministic.","test_case":"Execute an operation through API, application, queue, worker, retry, and error path.","test_input":"Correlation C123 across multiple boundaries.","expected_test_result":"Required context remains associated with C123; unrelated operation context never appears.","testing_guidance":"Include concurrent operations to expose context leakage.","verification_method":"End-to-end propagation verification.","verification_guidance":"Record exactly which propagation guarantees were verified; do not claim guarantees beyond tested boundaries.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B075-R001","D35-B075-R003","D35-B040-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B075"],"tests":["D35-B075-R003","D35-B075-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B075-R001","D35-B075-R002","D35-B075-R003","D35-B075-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry context propagation documentation","W3C Trace Context"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B076-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Rate Limiting / Quota Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Rate-limit contract","concept":"Implement explicit rate and quota policies at the boundary where resource consumption can be controlled.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What should an implementation-level rate-limit boundary define?","answer":"Define the limiting identity, resource, window or algorithm, allowed rate, burst semantics, rejection behavior, and state-storage consistency requirements.","explanation":"A rate limiter is meaningful only when the unit being limited and the resource being protected are explicit.","principle":"A quota is a contract over a defined resource and identity scope.","objective":"Bound resource consumption without accidentally limiting unrelated operations.","requirements":["Limit key.","Capacity/rate.","Window or token semantics.","Rejection behavior."],"constraints":["Do not rely on client-controlled identity for security-sensitive quotas.","Distributed limiters require explicit consistency assumptions."],"preconditions":["Protected resource and policy are identified."],"input":"Request plus limit context.","output":"Allow or reject decision.","expected_behavior":"Requests are admitted only within the configured policy.","incorrect_code":"if requests_this_second < 100:\n    return allow()\nrequests_this_second += 1","corrected_code":"key = rate_limit_key(context)\nif limiter.allow(key, cost=1):\n    return allow()\nreturn reject_with_rate_limit()","failure_mode":"Counter is global and unsafely mutable.","expected_error":"Incorrect limits under concurrency or across tenants.","cause":"No explicit key or atomic state.","root_cause":"Rate policy lacks a defined scope and concurrency model.","solution":"Implement a scoped limiter with atomic state semantics appropriate to deployment topology.","edge_case":"Burst traffic exactly at the policy boundary.","security_implication":"Limit keys must prevent attackers from trivially rotating identities to bypass security-sensitive quotas.","performance_implication":"Limiter state lookup must be bounded and efficient; distributed coordination can add latency.","test_case":"Send requests below, at, and above the configured limit for one key.","test_input":"Rate policy of 10 requests per defined window.","expected_test_result":"Requests are admitted/rejected according to documented policy.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrency, boundary conditions, key isolation, reset behavior, and distributed consistency assumptions.","verification_method":"Deterministic limiter unit tests plus concurrency tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual implementation matches its documented rate algorithm rather than assuming generic rate-limit behavior.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B050-R001","D35-B028-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B028-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B076"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["IETF RFC 6585, HTTP 429 Too Many Requests","OWASP API Security guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B076-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Rate Limiting / Quota Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Token bucket semantics","concept":"Represent burst capacity and sustained rate explicitly when using token-bucket-style limiting.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What must a token-bucket implementation represent?","answer":"It must represent current token availability, refill rate, capacity, elapsed time used for refill, and the cost of the requested operation.","explanation":"The token bucket permits bounded bursts while maintaining a defined long-term refill rate. Incorrect time or capacity calculations can invalidate the policy.","principle":"Rate-limiter state must model both instantaneous capacity and replenishment.","objective":"Implement deterministic bounded admission under a token-bucket policy.","requirements":["Capacity.","Refill rate.","Token count.","Time source."],"constraints":["Use a monotonic time source for elapsed-duration calculations.","Concurrent updates must be atomic or serialized."],"preconditions":["Rate algorithm is selected."],"input":"Request cost and current time.","output":"Allow/reject decision and updated bucket state.","expected_behavior":"Tokens refill according to elapsed time up to capacity and are consumed by admitted requests.","incorrect_code":"elapsed = time.time() - last\n# wall-clock changes can make elapsed negative or unexpectedly large","corrected_code":"elapsed = monotonic_now() - last\navailable = min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * refill_rate)","failure_mode":"Wall-clock changes corrupt refill calculations.","expected_error":"Incorrect admission decisions.","cause":"Wall-clock time used for duration measurement.","root_cause":"Time semantics not matched to rate-limiter calculation.","solution":"Use monotonic elapsed time and atomic state updates.","edge_case":"Clock adjustment or process suspension.","security_implication":"Incorrect limits can weaken abuse protection.","performance_implication":"Per-request state updates should avoid expensive coordination when a local limiter is sufficient.","test_case":"Advance a controlled monotonic clock and verify refill and capacity behavior.","test_input":"Empty bucket followed by controlled elapsed intervals.","expected_test_result":"Token count increases according to rate but never exceeds capacity.","testing_guidance":"Use injected time rather than real sleeping for deterministic tests.","verification_method":"Deterministic algorithm tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify concurrency semantics separately from arithmetic correctness.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ALGORITHM_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B033-R001","D35-B076-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B033-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B076"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["IETF rate-control algorithm literature and established token bucket semantics"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B076-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Rate Limiting / Quota Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Tenant quota isolation","concept":"Partition quota state by an explicit tenant or principal scope when policy requires isolation.","knowledge_type":"security","question":"How should tenant quotas avoid cross-tenant interference?","answer":"Construct quota keys from validated tenant/principal context and keep quota state logically isolated so one tenant's consumption does not consume another tenant's allowance.","explanation":"A global quota can unintentionally let one tenant exhaust resources allocated to another or make usage accounting meaningless.","principle":"Resource policy must follow the intended ownership boundary.","objective":"Enforce independent resource budgets.","requirements":["Trusted tenant context.","Tenant-scoped limit key.","Isolation tests."],"constraints":["Tenant identity must come from validated context, not arbitrary request body data.","Shared global infrastructure may still require aggregate limits."],"preconditions":["Tenant context has been authenticated and authorized."],"input":"Validated tenant context and request cost.","output":"Tenant-scoped admission decision.","expected_behavior":"Consumption by tenant A does not consume tenant B's independent quota.","incorrect_code":"key = 'api_requests'","corrected_code":"key = ('api_requests', validated_tenant_id)\nreturn limiter.allow(key)","failure_mode":"All tenants share one quota unintentionally.","expected_error":"Cross-tenant resource interference.","cause":"Limit key lacks tenant dimension.","root_cause":"Quota scope does not match tenancy model.","solution":"Include validated tenant identity in quota key and define aggregate limits separately.","edge_case":"A request legitimately consumes both tenant and global resources.","security_implication":"Incorrect tenant key derivation can become a cross-tenant isolation vulnerability.","performance_implication":"Higher key cardinality increases state storage requirements.","test_case":"Exhaust tenant A quota and send a request under tenant B.","test_input":"Tenant A over quota; tenant B within quota.","expected_test_result":"A is rejected according to policy; B remains independently evaluated.","testing_guidance":"Test tenant spoofing, missing tenant context, aggregate quotas, and key lifecycle.","verification_method":"Multi-tenant security and quota isolation tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify quota identity comes from trusted authorization context.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DEFENSIVE_MULTI_TENANT_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B028-R001","D35-B027-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B028-R001","D35-B027-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B076"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","Multi-tenant architecture isolation principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B076-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Rate Limiting / Quota Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Failure policy","concept":"Define fail-open or fail-closed behavior when the limiter's state dependency is unavailable.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What should happen if a distributed rate limiter cannot evaluate a request?","answer":"The system must explicitly define whether the protected operation fails closed, uses a bounded local fallback, or follows another controlled degradation policy based on the resource's risk.","explanation":"An unavailable limiter is not equivalent to an unlimited resource. The correct behavior depends on the consequence of admitting requests without enforcement.","principle":"Control failure behavior is part of the security and reliability contract.","objective":"Avoid accidental policy bypass during limiter failure.","requirements":["Dependency failure policy.","Bounded fallback if used.","Observability of degraded enforcement."],"constraints":["Fail-open can weaken security controls.","Fail-closed can reduce availability.","Fallback must not silently become permanent."],"preconditions":["Risk classification of the protected operation exists."],"input":"Request plus limiter availability state.","output":"Admission or controlled rejection.","expected_behavior":"Limiter failure follows the documented policy and exposes degraded state.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    return distributed_limiter.allow(key)\nexcept Exception:\n    return True","corrected_code":"try:\n    return distributed_limiter.allow(key)\nexcept LimiterUnavailable:\n    return bounded_fallback_or_reject(policy, key)","failure_mode":"Any limiter failure becomes unlimited access.","expected_error":"Quota/security policy bypass.","cause":"Unconditional fail-open fallback.","root_cause":"No explicit failure semantics.","solution":"Define risk-based failure policy and bounded fallback.","edge_case":"Limiter dependency is partially available and returns stale state.","security_implication":"High-risk operations may require fail-closed behavior.","performance_implication":"Fallback should avoid creating a local bottleneck or memory leak.","test_case":"Make the limiter unavailable and verify the configured failure policy.","test_input":"Limiter outage during protected request.","expected_test_result":"Request follows documented degraded enforcement behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test complete outage, stale response, timeout, and recovery.","verification_method":"Failure-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Record whether the policy was enforced, degraded, or unavailable during the test.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"SECURITY_AND_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B039-R001","D35-B050-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B039-R001"],"requires":["D35-B050-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B076"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","Google SRE reliability principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B076-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Rate Limiting / Quota Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Quota verification","concept":"Verify both admission behavior and isolation properties of quota enforcement.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should a rate-limit implementation be verified?","answer":"Verify algorithmic boundaries, concurrent behavior, identity isolation, failure policy, reset/refill semantics, and observability of rejected requests.","explanation":"A limiter can pass simple sequential tests while failing under concurrency or across tenant boundaries.","principle":"Quota verification must cover policy semantics and isolation, not only request counts.","objective":"Establish evidence that the quota policy is actually enforced.","requirements":["Boundary tests.","Concurrency tests.","Isolation tests.","Dependency-failure tests."],"constraints":["Observed behavior must be interpreted according to the exact algorithm and consistency model."],"preconditions":["Quota policy is documented."],"input":"Quota implementation and test workload.","output":"Verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Implementation behavior matches the defined quota semantics within documented consistency limits.","incorrect_code":"assert allowed_requests <= limit","corrected_code":"assert verify_rate_boundary()\nassert verify_concurrent_enforcement()\nassert verify_tenant_isolation()\nassert verify_failure_policy()","failure_mode":"Sequential count test gives false confidence.","expected_error":"Concurrency or isolation violations remain undetected.","cause":"Incomplete verification surface.","root_cause":"Quota treated as a simple counter.","solution":"Verify semantics across all operational dimensions.","edge_case":"Distributed replicas temporarily disagree on quota state.","security_implication":"Verification must establish whether enforcement is strong, approximate, or best-effort.","performance_implication":"Concurrency tests should measure limiter overhead as well as correctness.","test_case":"Run concurrent requests from multiple tenants near quota boundaries.","test_input":"Concurrent workloads for tenants A and B.","expected_test_result":"Observed decisions conform to documented isolation and consistency semantics.","testing_guidance":"Record exact topology and consistency assumptions for each test.","verification_method":"Unit, concurrency, multi-tenant, and failure-injection tests.","verification_guidance":"Do not claim globally exact enforcement unless the underlying coordination semantics establish it.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B076-R001","D35-B076-R003","D35-B076-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B076"],"tests":["D35-B076-R002","D35-B076-R003","D35-B076-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B076-R001","D35-B076-R002","D35-B076-R003","D35-B076-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","Google SRE reliability principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B077-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bulkhead / Isolation Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Resource partitioning","concept":"Partition resource capacity so failure or overload in one workload does not consume all shared capacity.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What is a bulkhead boundary in software architecture?","answer":"A bulkhead boundary assigns separate or bounded resource capacity to workloads so one workload cannot exhaust capacity needed by others.","explanation":"Isolation can be implemented through separate pools, bounded concurrency, queues, processes, or other mechanisms appropriate to the resource.","principle":"Shared resources require explicit isolation when failure domains must remain independent.","objective":"Contain overload and prevent cross-workload resource exhaustion.","requirements":["Defined resource pools or concurrency limits.","Ownership or workload classification.","Exhaustion behavior."],"constraints":["Isolation consumes additional capacity.","The boundary must protect the actual bottleneck, not an irrelevant resource."],"preconditions":["Critical workload classes are identified."],"input":"Workload and resource request.","output":"Admission to an isolated capacity pool or rejection.","expected_behavior":"One workload cannot consume capacity reserved for another workload beyond explicitly defined shared limits.","incorrect_code":"executor = ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=100)\n# all workloads share the same pool","corrected_code":"critical_pool = BoundedExecutor(20)\nbulk_pool = BoundedExecutor(80)\nsubmit_to_pool(workload_class, task)","failure_mode":"All workloads share unbounded or undifferentiated capacity.","expected_error":"One workload starves unrelated workloads.","cause":"No resource isolation.","root_cause":"Capacity treated as universally fungible.","solution":"Partition or bound capacity according to failure domains.","edge_case":"One pool is idle while another is saturated; policy must define whether borrowing is allowed.","security_implication":"Isolation boundaries can limit abuse and cross-tenant resource interference.","performance_implication":"Partitioning can reduce peak utilization if capacity cannot be borrowed.","test_case":"Saturate one workload pool while submitting work to another.","test_input":"Bulk workload consumes its maximum capacity; critical workload arrives.","expected_test_result":"Critical workload retains its defined capacity.","testing_guidance":"Test exhaustion, borrowing, fairness, queue limits, and shutdown.","verification_method":"Controlled concurrency/load testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual resource bottleneck is isolated.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B036-R001","D35-B048-R001","D35-B050-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B077"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Release It!, Bulkheads pattern","Microsoft Azure Architecture Center, Bulkhead pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B077-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bulkhead / Isolation Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Connection-pool isolation","concept":"Isolate scarce connection resources for workloads with different criticality or failure characteristics.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why can connection-pool isolation be more effective than application-level queue isolation?","answer":"If the true bottleneck is a finite database, HTTP, or other connection pool, isolating that pool directly prevents one workload from consuming the resource needed by another.","explanation":"Protecting an upstream queue does not help if all workloads ultimately contend for the same exhausted connection pool.","principle":"Place isolation at the actual constrained resource.","objective":"Protect critical operations from connection-resource exhaustion.","requirements":["Separate or bounded pools.","Pool ownership policy.","Leak and timeout controls."],"constraints":["Separate pools can increase total resource consumption.","Underlying provider may impose aggregate limits."],"preconditions":["Connection usage can be classified by workload."],"input":"Workload-specific connection request.","output":"Connection acquisition or bounded failure.","expected_behavior":"A noisy workload cannot consume the entire pool needed by a protected workload.","incorrect_code":"shared_pool.acquire()  # used by every workload","corrected_code":"pool = critical_pool if workload.is_critical else bulk_pool\nconnection = pool.acquire(timeout=deadline.remaining())","failure_mode":"Critical and bulk workloads exhaust one shared pool.","expected_error":"Critical operations time out despite application-level queue capacity.","cause":"Isolation placed above the actual bottleneck.","root_cause":"Resource dependency graph not considered.","solution":"Partition connection capacity where appropriate.","edge_case":"Provider imposes a single global connection limit.","security_implication":"Tenant or workload pool assignment must be based on trusted context.","performance_implication":"Pool partitioning may reduce overall utilization; tune using measurements rather than assumptions.","test_case":"Exhaust bulk pool and verify critical pool remains available.","test_input":"Bulk requests equal to bulk-pool capacity.","expected_test_result":"Critical connection acquisition remains within its defined capacity.","testing_guidance":"Test leaks, timeouts, provider limits, and shutdown.","verification_method":"Resource exhaustion integration test.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual connection allocation rather than assuming pool configuration equals effective isolation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B048-R001","D35-B077-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B048-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B077"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Release It!, Bulkheads pattern","Microsoft Azure Architecture Center, Bulkhead pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B077-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bulkhead / Isolation Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Bounded concurrency","concept":"Use explicit concurrency limits as a software bulkhead.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How does bounded concurrency provide bulkhead behavior?","answer":"A bounded concurrency controller limits the number of simultaneous operations for a workload, preventing unlimited in-flight work from consuming CPU, connections, memory, or downstream capacity.","explanation":"Concurrency limits are often simpler than separate infrastructure pools and can directly control pressure on a dependency.","principle":"In-flight work is a resource and should be bounded.","objective":"Prevent concurrency-driven resource exhaustion.","requirements":["Maximum concurrent operations.","Admission policy.","Release semantics."],"constraints":["A concurrency limit does not by itself bound queued work.","Every acquired permit must be released reliably."],"preconditions":["The protected resource has a meaningful concurrency capacity."],"input":"Operation request.","output":"Permit or bounded rejection/wait.","expected_behavior":"At most the configured number of operations execute concurrently within the scope.","incorrect_code":"semaphore = None\nrun_all_tasks_concurrently(tasks)","corrected_code":"async with semaphore:\n    await operation()","failure_mode":"Unlimited concurrent work reaches the dependency.","expected_error":"Resource exhaustion or cascading latency.","cause":"No concurrency boundary.","root_cause":"In-flight operations treated as free.","solution":"Bound concurrency and separately bound waiting work.","edge_case":"Cancellation occurs while a permit is held.","security_implication":"Concurrency controls can mitigate abuse but should not replace authentication or authorization.","performance_implication":"Too-small limits can underutilize capacity; tuning requires measurement.","test_case":"Submit more concurrent tasks than the configured limit and track maximum active operations.","test_input":"100 tasks with concurrency limit 10.","expected_test_result":"Maximum active operations does not exceed the configured limit.","testing_guidance":"Test normal completion, exceptions, cancellation, and timeout while holding permits.","verification_method":"Concurrency instrumentation and tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify permits are released on every terminal path.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B036-R001","D35-B047-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B077"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE reliability principles","Release It!, Bulkheads pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B077-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bulkhead / Isolation Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Queue isolation","concept":"Bound waiting work independently from execution capacity.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why is limiting concurrency insufficient without a bounded queue?","answer":"A concurrency limit can protect active resources while an unbounded waiting queue still consumes memory and increases latency indefinitely. A complete bulkhead bounds both active and waiting work.","explanation":"Resource protection requires controlling the full admission path, not just currently executing operations.","principle":"Bound both in-flight and waiting work.","objective":"Prevent hidden queue growth from defeating resource isolation.","requirements":["Concurrency bound.","Queue bound.","Overflow policy."],"constraints":["Rejecting work can reduce availability but may be safer than unbounded memory growth.","Queue policy must match business semantics."],"preconditions":["Work can be admitted or rejected before execution."],"input":"Incoming workload.","output":"Accepted into bounded queue or rejected.","expected_behavior":"The total resource commitment remains bounded.","incorrect_code":"executor.submit(task)  # unlimited pending tasks","corrected_code":"if queue.full():\n    raise CapacityExceeded()\nqueue.put_nowait(task)","failure_mode":"Pending tasks grow without bound.","expected_error":"Memory exhaustion and unbounded latency.","cause":"Only active concurrency was bounded.","root_cause":"Waiting work was not treated as a resource.","solution":"Bound queue depth and define overflow semantics.","edge_case":"Queue fills during downstream outage.","security_implication":"Attackers can exploit unbounded queues for resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Bounded queues expose overload earlier and make latency behavior more predictable.","test_case":"Disable downstream processing and submit tasks until capacity is exhausted.","test_input":"Sustained workload with no consumer progress.","expected_test_result":"Admission stops at the defined total capacity.","testing_guidance":"Measure queue depth, rejection rate, memory, and recovery after downstream restoration.","verification_method":"Overload and resource-exhaustion test.","verification_guidance":"Verify there is no secondary unbounded buffer outside the controlled queue.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B036-R001","D35-B050-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B036-R001"],"requires":["D35-B050-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B077"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE reliability principles","Release It!, Bulkheads pattern"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B077-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bulkhead / Isolation Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Isolation verification","concept":"Verify that overload in one resource domain cannot exhaust another protected domain.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should bulkhead isolation be verified?","answer":"Saturate one workload or resource domain while exercising another and measure active capacity, queue depth, latency, failures, and recovery behavior for the protected domain.","explanation":"Configuration alone does not prove isolation. Controlled exhaustion tests reveal hidden shared bottlenecks.","principle":"Isolation must be demonstrated under the failure mode it is designed to contain.","objective":"Establish evidence of failure-domain containment.","requirements":["Controlled overload.","Resource instrumentation.","Protected workload assertions."],"constraints":["Test topology must match production-relevant resource sharing.","Do not infer production capacity from an unrepresentative test environment."],"preconditions":["Isolation boundaries are instrumented."],"input":"Overload workload and protected workload.","output":"Isolation test evidence.","expected_behavior":"Protected workload retains its defined capacity or follows documented degraded behavior.","incorrect_code":"assert bulk_queue_size <= 100","corrected_code":"saturate(bulk_workload)\nresult = run(protected_workload)\nassert protected_capacity_within_policy(result)\nassert shared_resource_exhaustion_not_observed()","failure_mode":"Test checks only configuration values.","expected_error":"Hidden shared bottleneck remains undetected.","cause":"No overload scenario.","root_cause":"Isolation considered static configuration instead of dynamic behavior.","solution":"Exercise the actual failure domain under controlled load.","edge_case":"Both workloads legitimately depend on a global shared limit.","security_implication":"Isolation tests should include cross-tenant resource exhaustion attempts.","performance_implication":"Record measured latency and capacity rather than making unverified performance claims.","test_case":"Saturate one pool and verify another protected pool's behavior.","test_input":"Bulk workload at configured maximum plus critical workload.","expected_test_result":"Critical workload remains within its defined capacity policy.","testing_guidance":"Include recovery after overload ends and verify queues drain without permanent starvation.","verification_method":"Controlled load/failure-injection test.","verification_guidance":"Label measurements as measured only for the actual test environment.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B077-R001","D35-B077-R002","D35-B077-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B077"],"tests":["D35-B077-R001","D35-B077-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B077-R001","D35-B077-R002","D35-B077-R003","D35-B077-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Release It!, Bulkheads pattern","Google SRE reliability principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B078-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Validation & Integrity Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Invariant validation","concept":"Validate data against structural and domain integrity rules at the correct boundary.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should an implementation distinguish structural validation from domain integrity validation?","answer":"Perform structural validation at the input boundary and domain invariant validation within the domain/application boundary where the necessary business context exists.","explanation":"A boundary can validate shape, types, and required fields, but it may not have enough context to determine whether a value violates a domain invariant.","principle":"Validate each rule where the required knowledge and authority exist.","objective":"Prevent invalid data while avoiding misplaced business rules.","requirements":["Structural schema validation.","Domain invariant enforcement.","Clear validation ownership."],"constraints":["Do not duplicate domain rules in every transport adapter.","Do not accept structurally valid data as automatically domain-valid."],"preconditions":["Domain invariants are explicitly defined."],"input":"External or internal data.","output":"Validated data or classified validation failure.","expected_behavior":"Structural and semantic invalidity are rejected at their appropriate boundaries.","incorrect_code":"def api_handler(body):\n    if body['quantity'] > inventory_count:\n        raise ValueError('not enough inventory')","corrected_code":"def api_handler(body):\n    request = validate_schema(body)\n    return order_service.place(request)\n\nclass Order:\n    def reserve(self, available):\n        if self.quantity > available:\n            raise InsufficientInventory()","failure_mode":"Domain invariant is embedded in one transport adapter.","expected_error":"Other entry points can bypass the rule.","cause":"Validation ownership is incorrect.","root_cause":"Structural and domain validation were conflated.","solution":"Place structural validation at the boundary and invariant enforcement in domain/application logic.","edge_case":"Multiple commands modify the same invariant concurrently; validation must combine with concurrency control.","security_implication":"Never rely solely on client-side validation.","performance_implication":"Early structural rejection avoids unnecessary application work.","test_case":"Submit structurally valid data that violates a domain invariant through two different entry points.","test_input":"Quantity exceeds available inventory.","expected_test_result":"Both entry points eventually enforce the same domain invariant.","testing_guidance":"Test API, background-job, and internal-call paths separately.","verification_method":"Boundary contract and domain invariant tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify the invariant is enforced independently of transport.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DOMAIN_VALIDATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B021-R001","D35-B005-R001","D35-B013-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B021-R001","D35-B005-R001"],"requires":["D35-B013-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B078"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Domain-Driven Design principles","Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B078-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Validation & Integrity Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Atomic integrity enforcement","concept":"Enforce data integrity atomically when concurrent operations can invalidate prior validation.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why is validation before a write insufficient when concurrent operations can change the underlying state?","answer":"A value validated against current state can become invalid before the write. Integrity must therefore be enforced atomically through transactions, constraints, optimistic concurrency, or another mechanism appropriate to the invariant.","explanation":"A check-then-act sequence creates a race between validation and mutation.","principle":"If an invariant spans mutable state, validation and mutation require compatible atomicity.","objective":"Prevent race-condition-driven integrity violations.","requirements":["Defined invariant.","Concurrency strategy.","Atomic mutation or conflict detection."],"constraints":["Application checks alone cannot guarantee cross-transaction invariants.","Database constraints may be required for certain invariants."],"preconditions":["Concurrent writes are possible."],"input":"Candidate state mutation.","output":"Committed mutation or conflict/failure.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent invalid mutations cannot both silently succeed.","incorrect_code":"if balance >= amount:\n    balance -= amount\n    save(balance)","corrected_code":"with transaction():\n    account = load_for_update(account_id)\n    if account.balance < amount:\n        raise InsufficientFunds()\n    account.balance -= amount\n    save(account)","failure_mode":"Two concurrent operations both pass the pre-check.","expected_error":"Invariant violation such as negative balance.","cause":"Check and mutation are not atomic.","root_cause":"Concurrency semantics absent from integrity enforcement.","solution":"Use appropriate locking, optimistic concurrency, serializable transaction, or database constraint.","edge_case":"Deadlock or optimistic conflict requires retry according to safe transaction semantics.","security_implication":"Integrity failures can become authorization or financial correctness issues.","performance_implication":"Stronger consistency may reduce concurrency; choose based on invariant requirements and measure actual impact.","test_case":"Execute two concurrent mutations against the same invariant boundary.","test_input":"Two withdrawals against one account with insufficient combined funds.","expected_test_result":"At most one succeeds if the invariant permits only one.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic concurrency tests and inspect committed final state.","verification_method":"Concurrency and integrity tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify the chosen mechanism actually covers the invariant's full data scope.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B037-R001","D35-B011-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B037-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B078"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B078-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Validation & Integrity Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Canonical representation","concept":"Use canonical representations when logically equivalent values must compare consistently.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why can canonicalization be necessary before integrity comparisons?","answer":"Equivalent representations such as normalized identifiers, units, or case variants can compare differently unless the system defines and applies a canonical representation before enforcing relevant invariants.","explanation":"Canonicalization reduces representation-level ambiguity but must not erase meaningful distinctions.","principle":"Compare semantics, not accidental representation differences.","objective":"Prevent inconsistent integrity behavior caused by multiple equivalent representations.","requirements":["Canonical representation rules.","Normalization ownership.","Tests for equivalent representations."],"constraints":["Canonicalization must preserve semantic distinctions.","Rules must be versioned when changing persisted representations."],"preconditions":["The domain defines which representations are equivalent."],"input":"Non-canonical value.","output":"Canonical value.","expected_behavior":"Equivalent accepted representations map to the same canonical form.","incorrect_code":"if email1 == email2:\n    duplicate = True","corrected_code":"a = canonicalize_identifier(email1)\nb = canonicalize_identifier(email2)\nif a == b:\n    duplicate = True","failure_mode":"Equivalent identifiers are treated as different.","expected_error":"Duplicate or uniqueness integrity failures.","cause":"Representation-level comparison.","root_cause":"No canonicalization contract.","solution":"Define and consistently apply canonical representations where semantically valid.","edge_case":"Provider-specific or Unicode normalization rules can have surprising semantics; canonicalization must be domain-specific.","security_implication":"Canonicalization must occur consistently across validation and authorization paths to avoid parser discrepancies.","performance_implication":"Canonicalization should be deterministic and bounded.","test_case":"Provide equivalent and intentionally distinct identifiers and verify only true equivalents canonicalize together.","test_input":"Identifier variants differing only in defined non-semantic representation.","expected_test_result":"Equivalent values compare equal; semantically distinct values remain distinct.","testing_guidance":"Include Unicode, whitespace, case, unit, and encoding cases only where the domain explicitly permits normalization.","verification_method":"Canonicalization property and boundary tests.","verification_guidance":"Document the exact equivalence relation; never infer broad normalization from a narrow requirement.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOMAIN_SPECIFIC_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B021-R001","D35-B022-R001","D35-B045-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B021-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B078"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Unicode Standard normalization concepts","Domain-specific data integrity principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B078-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Validation & Integrity Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Integrity error handling","concept":"Return typed integrity failures rather than leaking storage-specific errors.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should persistence integrity violations be translated at the application boundary?","answer":"Translate storage-specific constraint errors into stable application-level failures while preserving sufficient diagnostic information internally for operators.","explanation":"A database constraint name or SQL exception is an infrastructure detail. Application callers need a stable semantic failure such as DuplicateResource or ConcurrencyConflict.","principle":"Infrastructure failures should be translated into application semantics at the boundary.","objective":"Prevent persistence implementation details from becoming application contracts.","requirements":["Typed application errors.","Infrastructure error mapping.","Internal diagnostic context."],"constraints":["Mapping must not hide unrelated infrastructure failures.","Do not expose raw database messages to external callers."],"preconditions":["Infrastructure adapter owns persistence errors."],"input":"Storage exception.","output":"Application-level error or propagated unknown failure.","expected_behavior":"Known integrity violations map to documented application semantics.","incorrect_code":"except DatabaseError as exc:\n    raise ValueError(str(exc))","corrected_code":"except UniqueConstraintError:\n    raise DuplicateResource()\nexcept DatabaseError:\n    raise PersistenceUnavailable()","failure_mode":"All database errors are mapped to one generic validation error.","expected_error":"Operators and callers cannot distinguish duplicate data from infrastructure outage.","cause":"Overly broad exception mapping.","root_cause":"No infrastructure-to-application error taxonomy.","solution":"Map only known storage conditions and preserve unknown failures.","edge_case":"Database vendor changes constraint error representation.","security_implication":"Raw database details must not reach external clients.","performance_implication":"Error translation itself should be negligible; excessive exception parsing can become brittle.","test_case":"Simulate unique constraint violation and generic database outage.","test_input":"Two distinct persistence failures.","expected_test_result":"Known violation maps to duplicate-resource; outage maps to persistence-unavailable.","testing_guidance":"Test vendor-specific adapter mapping independently from application error semantics.","verification_method":"Adapter unit tests and application contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify unknown infrastructure errors are not falsely classified.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BOUNDARY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B020-R001","D35-B019-R001","D35-B006-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B020-R001","D35-B019-R001"],"requires":["D35-B006-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B078"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture","OWASP error handling guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B078-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Validation & Integrity Boundary Implementation","subtopic":"Integrity verification","concept":"Verify data integrity at both representation and committed-state levels.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should data integrity implementation be verified?","answer":"Test structural validation, domain invariants, concurrent mutation, canonicalization where applicable, persistence constraints, and error translation as separate but connected verification layers.","explanation":"Integrity can fail at any layer. A single schema test cannot prove committed state remains valid under concurrency.","principle":"Integrity verification follows the complete data lifecycle from input to committed state.","objective":"Detect integrity failures before and after persistence.","requirements":["Boundary tests.","Domain invariant tests.","Concurrency tests.","Persistence constraint tests."],"constraints":["Verification must reflect actual storage and concurrency semantics."],"preconditions":["Integrity rules are explicitly documented."],"input":"Data-integrity implementation and test matrix.","output":"Verification result.","expected_behavior":"Invalid data is rejected and committed state remains within required invariants.","incorrect_code":"def test_integrity(data):\n    assert schema_is_valid(data)","corrected_code":"def test_integrity():\n    assert rejects_invalid_schema()\n    assert rejects_domain_violation()\n    assert handles_concurrent_conflict()\n    assert persistence_constraints_hold()","failure_mode":"Only input shape is verified.","expected_error":"Concurrent or persistence-level integrity defects remain undetected.","cause":"Verification stops before committed state.","root_cause":"Data integrity treated as a serialization problem only.","solution":"Verify the full lifecycle.","edge_case":"Database and application validation disagree.","security_implication":"Integrity verification should include authorization-sensitive state transitions.","performance_implication":"Concurrency tests can be expensive; keep deterministic targeted scenarios in CI and broader tests in dedicated suites.","test_case":"Exercise invalid input, valid input, concurrent mutation, and persistence constraint violation.","test_input":"Four integrity scenarios.","expected_test_result":"Each scenario produces the documented safe outcome.","testing_guidance":"Separate structural, semantic, concurrency, persistence, and translation assertions.","verification_method":"Layered automated verification.","verification_guidance":"Record actual executed environment and database semantics when claiming verified results.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B078-R001","D35-B078-R002","D35-B078-R004","D35-B040-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B040-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B078"],"tests":["D35-B078-R001","D35-B078-R002","D35-B078-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B078-R001","D35-B078-R002","D35-B078-R003","D35-B078-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Domain-Driven Design principles","Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B079-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Migration / Deployment Compatibility Implementation","subtopic":"Expand-and-contract migration","concept":"Implement schema changes in compatibility-preserving stages when old and new application versions may coexist.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a database schema change be implemented when old and new application versions overlap during deployment?","answer":"Use an expand-and-contract sequence: add compatible structures first, deploy code that can operate with both forms, migrate data, switch reads/writes, then remove obsolete structures only after compatibility is no longer required.","explanation":"Rolling deployments can temporarily run multiple application versions. Directly removing or changing a field can break the older version before it is retired.","principle":"Deployment compatibility must be designed across version overlap, not only final state.","objective":"Make schema evolution safe under staged deployment.","requirements":["Backward-compatible expansion.","Compatibility-aware application code.","Migration completion criteria.","Safe contraction."],"constraints":["Old code must remain compatible until retired.","Destructive changes require evidence that no active consumer depends on them."],"preconditions":["Deployment can overlap versions or the compatibility risk is otherwise relevant."],"input":"Existing schema and target schema.","output":"Incrementally migrated compatible system.","expected_behavior":"Each deployment step remains compatible with the versions that can be active simultaneously.","incorrect_code":"ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN legacy_name;","corrected_code":"ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN canonical_name TEXT;\n-- application temporarily reads/writes compatible fields\n-- migrate data\n-- remove legacy field only after old consumers are retired","failure_mode":"Destructive migration breaks old application instances.","expected_error":"Runtime query or serialization failures during rollout.","cause":"Final-state schema deployed before consumers are compatible.","root_cause":"Migration treated as a one-step change.","solution":"Use staged expand, migrate, switch, and contract steps.","edge_case":"Rollback after data has been written only to the new representation.","security_implication":"Migration scripts require controlled privileges and auditability.","performance_implication":"Large data migrations can create lock, I/O, and replication pressure; execution strategy must be measured.","test_case":"Simulate old and new application versions operating against the expanded schema.","test_input":"Version N and N+1 concurrently using the compatibility schema.","expected_test_result":"Both versions operate according to the defined compatibility contract.","testing_guidance":"Test forward deployment, rollback, partial migration, and interrupted migration.","verification_method":"Compatibility integration tests plus migration dry runs.","verification_guidance":"Do not claim zero-downtime unless the actual database, migration, and deployment topology has been tested.","language":"SQL","language_version":"Vendor-specific","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Database-dependent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DEPLOYMENT_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B045-R001","D35-B046-R001","D35-B050-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B045-R001"],"requires":["D35-B046-R001"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B079"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Evolutionary Database Design","Microsoft Architecture Center, deployment and migration patterns"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B079-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Migration / Deployment Compatibility Implementation","subtopic":"Application compatibility matrix","concept":"Represent supported producer-consumer version combinations explicitly.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why should deployment compatibility be represented as a version matrix?","answer":"A compatibility matrix makes supported combinations explicit and testable, especially when multiple application, schema, message, or API versions can coexist.","explanation":"Without a matrix, teams often assume compatibility based on adjacent versions while missing rollback or mixed-version scenarios.","principle":"Compatibility assumptions should be explicit acceptance criteria.","objective":"Prevent untested version combinations from becoming production dependencies.","requirements":["Version identifiers.","Supported combinations.","Breaking-change rules."],"constraints":["The matrix must reflect actual deployment topology.","Unsupported combinations must fail safely or be prevented from deployment."],"preconditions":["Versioned interfaces or deployable components exist."],"input":"Producer, consumer, schema, and API versions.","output":"Compatibility decision.","expected_behavior":"A combination is accepted only if it is explicitly supported and verified.","incorrect_code":"if major_version >= 2:\n    assume_compatible()","corrected_code":"compatibility = matrix.lookup(producer_version, consumer_version, schema_version)\nif not compatibility.supported:\n    raise UnsupportedCombination()","failure_mode":"Compatibility inferred from version numbers alone.","expected_error":"Unexpected runtime incompatibility.","cause":"No explicit compatibility contract.","root_cause":"Version semantics are treated as sufficient evidence.","solution":"Maintain and test a compatibility matrix.","edge_case":"A patch release introduces a behavioral incompatibility despite unchanged major version.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive protocol compatibility must include authentication and authorization semantics.","performance_implication":"Large matrices can be compressed into rules but must preserve equivalent coverage.","test_case":"Evaluate supported and unsupported version combinations.","test_input":"Producer v2, consumer v1; producer v2, consumer v2.","expected_test_result":"Decision follows the documented compatibility matrix.","testing_guidance":"Include rollback and mixed-version combinations.","verification_method":"Compatibility matrix tests.","verification_guidance":"Require evidence for every production-supported combination.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B045-R001","D35-B046-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B045-R001","D35-B046-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B079"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Microsoft Architecture Center, versioning and deployment compatibility guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B079-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Migration / Deployment Compatibility Implementation","subtopic":"Migration resumability","concept":"Make long-running migrations restartable from durable progress when feasible.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a long-running migration handle interruption?","answer":"Use durable migration state or idempotent chunks so an interrupted migration can resume without corrupting already migrated data.","explanation":"Process crashes, deployment interruption, and operational cancellation are normal failure modes for long migrations.","principle":"Long-running changes require recoverable progress.","objective":"Prevent migration failure from requiring unsafe manual reconstruction.","requirements":["Chunked or resumable operation.","Durable progress.","Idempotent transformation where possible."],"constraints":["Checkpoint state must correspond to actual committed progress.","Partial transformations must remain valid."],"preconditions":["Data transformation can be partitioned safely."],"input":"Migration workload and checkpoint state.","output":"Migrated data plus durable progress.","expected_behavior":"Restart resumes from a safe point without duplicating or corrupting transformations.","incorrect_code":"for row in all_rows:\n    transform(row)\n# no checkpoint","corrected_code":"for chunk in chunks:\n    with transaction():\n        transform_chunk(chunk)\n        checkpoint.mark_complete(chunk.id)","failure_mode":"Interrupted migration restarts ambiguously.","expected_error":"Duplicate transformation or inconsistent partial state.","cause":"No durable progress boundary.","root_cause":"Migration assumed to complete in one uninterrupted process.","solution":"Use transactional chunks and durable checkpoints.","edge_case":"Checkpoint is written before the transformed data is committed.","security_implication":"Migration credentials should be least-privileged and separately controlled.","performance_implication":"Chunk size affects lock duration, throughput, and recovery granularity and must be measured.","test_case":"Interrupt migration after several committed chunks and resume.","test_input":"Migration with deterministic chunk IDs.","expected_test_result":"Completed chunks are not corrupted or incorrectly repeated; remaining chunks continue safely.","testing_guidance":"Test interruption at transaction boundaries and during transformation.","verification_method":"Migration recovery test.","verification_guidance":"Inspect final invariants and migration state after restart.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MIGRATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B069-R004","D35-B037-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B037-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["D35-B079"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Evolutionary Database Design","Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B079-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Migration / Deployment Compatibility Implementation","subtopic":"Rollback compatibility","concept":"Treat rollback as a compatibility scenario rather than assuming it is always a reverse deployment.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why can application rollback fail after a schema migration?","answer":"A newer application may write data that an older application cannot interpret. Therefore rollback requires backward data compatibility or a tested reverse-compatible migration strategy.","explanation":"Deployment rollback changes executable code but does not automatically reverse persisted state.","principle":"Code rollback and data rollback are separate operations.","objective":"Ensure recovery paths remain valid after partial deployment.","requirements":["Rollback compatibility analysis.","Backward-readable data representation.","Tested recovery procedure."],"constraints":["Do not assume schema can be safely reversed after writes.","Destructive transformations may make rollback impossible without backups or compensating migration."],"preconditions":["Rollback is part of the operational recovery plan."],"input":"New application version and persisted state.","output":"Safe rollback decision or blocked rollback.","expected_behavior":"Rollback is allowed only when the previous version can safely interpret current persisted data.","incorrect_code":"deploy(v2)\nif unhealthy:\n    deploy(v1)","corrected_code":"if rollback_compatible(schema_state, v1):\n    deploy(v1)\nelse:\n    execute_recovery_migration_or_restore()","failure_mode":"Rollback starts against incompatible data.","expected_error":"Old application fails on new persisted representation.","cause":"Rollback compatibility not tested.","root_cause":"Data state treated as automatically reversible.","solution":"Design rollback as an explicit compatibility path.","edge_case":"V2 partially writes a new 