{"record_id":"D35-B081-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service Discovery & Endpoint Resolution","subtopic":"Resolution abstraction","concept":"Service endpoint resolution should be isolated behind an explicit interface so application code does not depend on discovery-provider details.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should service endpoint resolution be implemented?","answer":"Define a resolver interface that accepts a logical service identity and returns an eligible endpoint set. Keep registry, DNS, static configuration, or platform-specific discovery mechanisms behind the boundary.","explanation":"The boundary separates service identity from endpoint location. Consumers depend on a stable resolution contract while infrastructure can change without changing application-level dependencies.","principle":"Depend on logical service identity, not infrastructure-specific endpoint lookup.","objective":"Provide replaceable and testable endpoint resolution.","requirements":["Logical service identity","Resolver abstraction","Eligible endpoint representation","Explicit resolution failure semantics"],"constraints":["Do not expose registry-specific types to application code","Do not silently return an unusable endpoint"],"preconditions":["A service identity is known","A discovery mechanism or configured endpoint source exists"],"input":"Service identity such as payment-service","output":"A validated set of candidate service endpoints or an explicit resolution failure","expected_behavior":"Resolver returns only endpoints satisfying the resolver's eligibility rules.","incorrect_code":"def call_payment():\n    return requests.get("http://payment-service.internal:8080/payments")","corrected_code":"class EndpointResolver:\n    def resolve(self, service_name: str) -> list[str]:\n        raise NotImplementedError\n\nclass StaticEndpointResolver(EndpointResolver):\n    def init(self, endpoints: dict[str, list[str]]):\n        self.endpoints = endpoints\n\n    def resolve(self, service_name: str) -> list[str]:\n        endpoints = self.endpoints.get(service_name, [])\n        if not endpoints:\n            raise LookupError(f"No endpoint for {service_name}")\n        return list(endpoints)","failure_mode":"Application is coupled directly to a hard-coded endpoint.","expected_error":"LookupError when no eligible endpoint exists.","cause":"Endpoint location is embedded in business-facing code.","root_cause":"Missing architectural boundary around service discovery.","solution":"Introduce a resolver interface and inject it into the service client.","edge_case":"Multiple endpoints exist but all are temporarily ineligible.","security_implication":"Resolver output must not permit unauthorized or untrusted endpoint selection.","performance_implication":"Repeated discovery can add latency; bounded caching may be appropriate without hiding endpoint health changes.","test_case":"Resolve a known service and verify that the returned endpoint set matches configured eligible endpoints.","test_input":{"service":"payment-service"},"expected_test_result":"Only eligible configured endpoints are returned.","testing_guidance":"Test known service, unknown service, empty registry, multiple endpoints, and malformed endpoint metadata.","verification_method":"Unit-test the resolver contract independently from the discovery provider.","verification_guidance":"Verify that application code depends only on the resolver contract and that provider-specific objects do not cross the boundary.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B082-R001","D35-B089-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["service_discovery_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture — separated interfaces and infrastructure boundaries","Kubernetes Documentation — Service discovery concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B081-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service Discovery & Endpoint Resolution","subtopic":"Service identity","concept":"Logical service identity should remain stable while physical endpoint locations may change.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"Why should service identity be separated from endpoint address?","answer":"A logical identity such as a service name allows endpoint locations to change independently of callers. This supports scaling, failover, deployment changes, and infrastructure replacement.","explanation":"Hard-coded hostnames or addresses make topology changes application changes. A stable identity creates an explicit indirection boundary.","principle":"Address the capability by identity and resolve location at the infrastructure boundary.","objective":"Prevent topology details from leaking into application logic.","requirements":["Stable service identifier","Resolver contract","Endpoint validation"],"constraints":["Service identity must not encode ephemeral instance identifiers"],"preconditions":["Caller knows the required service capability"],"input":"Logical service identity","output":"One or more usable endpoint references","expected_behavior":"Changing physical endpoints does not require changes to application business logic.","incorrect_code":"PAYMENT_HOST = "10.20.4.17"","corrected_code":"PAYMENT_SERVICE = "payment-service"\nendpoint = resolver.resolve(PAYMENT_SERVICE)[0]","failure_mode":"Deployment or scaling requires source-code modification.","expected_error":"Configuration or resolution failure rather than silent fallback.","cause":"Physical topology is treated as application configuration.","root_cause":"Missing identity/location separation.","solution":"Represent logical services explicitly and resolve endpoints through infrastructure.","edge_case":"A service has no stable network address during startup.","security_implication":"Logical identity must be mapped only to authorized endpoints.","performance_implication":"Resolution adds an indirection cost; bounded caching can reduce repeated lookups.","test_case":"Change the endpoint associated with a logical service without changing the caller.","test_input":{"service":"catalog-service","old_endpoint":"10.0.0.1:8080","new_endpoint":"10.0.0.2:8080"},"expected_test_result":"Caller continues to resolve the service and uses the new endpoint.","testing_guidance":"Use a fake resolver so endpoint topology can be changed independently of caller tests.","verification_method":"Contract test between caller and resolver.","verification_guidance":"Verify no physical address appears in application-layer source.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"TypeScript compiler","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B081-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B081-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["logical_service_identity"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Kubernetes Documentation — Service abstraction and service discovery"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B081-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service Discovery & Endpoint Resolution","subtopic":"Discovery failure handling","concept":"Discovery failure must be explicit and bounded rather than silently converted into an invalid endpoint or indefinite wait.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should discovery failures be handled?","answer":"Return a typed resolution failure, apply a bounded timeout, and let the caller decide whether to fail, use a known-safe fallback, or enter degraded mode.","explanation":"Discovery is an infrastructure dependency and can fail independently from the target service. Treating failure explicitly prevents misleading downstream errors.","principle":"Fail explicitly at the discovery boundary.","objective":"Contain discovery failures and preserve diagnosability.","requirements":["Timeout","Typed failure","No invalid endpoint fallback","Caller-visible failure state"],"constraints":["No unbounded discovery retry","No fallback to arbitrary endpoints"],"preconditions":["Discovery provider may be unavailable"],"input":"Service identity and resolver request","output":"Endpoint set or resolution failure","expected_behavior":"Unavailable discovery produces a bounded, classifiable failure.","incorrect_code":"def resolve(name):\n    while True:\n        endpoints = registry.lookup(name)\n        if endpoints:\n            return endpoints","corrected_code":"class DiscoveryUnavailable(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef resolve(name, registry, timeout_seconds=2):\n    try:\n        endpoints = registry.lookup(name, timeout=timeout_seconds)\n    except TimeoutError as exc:\n        raise DiscoveryUnavailable(name) from exc\n    if not endpoints:\n        raise LookupError(name)\n    return endpoints","failure_mode":"Caller hangs indefinitely while discovery is unavailable.","expected_error":"DiscoveryUnavailable or LookupError.","cause":"No bounded timeout or explicit failure classification.","root_cause":"Infrastructure failure semantics were omitted from the boundary contract.","solution":"Define bounded resolution behavior and typed failure outcomes.","edge_case":"Registry responds successfully but returns an empty endpoint set.","security_implication":"Never fall back to attacker-controlled or untrusted endpoints when discovery fails.","performance_implication":"Short bounded resolution timeouts prevent worker exhaustion.","test_case":"Simulate discovery timeout.","test_input":{"service":"inventory-service","registry_behavior":"timeout"},"expected_test_result":"Resolution raises a typed discovery failure within the configured timeout.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, empty result, malformed result, and successful resolution independently.","verification_method":"Fault-injection unit test.","verification_guidance":"Measure that the resolver does not block beyond its configured timeout.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B047-R001","D35-B050-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B081-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["discovery_failure_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering — bounded failure and dependency handling principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B081-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service Discovery & Endpoint Resolution","subtopic":"Resolution caching","concept":"Discovery results may be cached for bounded periods, but cache lifetime must not defeat endpoint eligibility or recovery behavior.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should endpoint resolution caching be implemented?","answer":"Cache successful resolutions for a bounded TTL and invalidate or refresh them when the resolver detects stale or unusable endpoints. Do not treat cached endpoints as permanently valid.","explanation":"Caching reduces discovery overhead but can preserve stale topology. TTL and explicit invalidation create a controlled freshness boundary.","principle":"Cache resolution metadata with an explicit freshness policy.","objective":"Balance discovery overhead against endpoint freshness.","requirements":["Bounded TTL","Cache key by logical service identity","Refresh behavior","Failure handling"],"constraints":["No indefinite endpoint caching","Do not cache malformed resolution results"],"preconditions":["A resolver can return endpoint metadata"],"input":"Service identity","output":"Fresh-enough endpoint set","expected_behavior":"Repeated resolution within TTL may use the cached result while expired entries trigger refresh.","incorrect_code":"cache[name] = registry.lookup(name)","corrected_code":"class CachedResolver:\n    def init(self, resolver, ttl_seconds):\n        self.resolver = resolver\n        self.ttl_seconds = ttl_seconds\n        self.cache = {}\n\n    def resolve(self, name, now):\n        item = self.cache.get(name)\n        if item and now - item["time"] < self.ttl_seconds:\n            return item["endpoints"]\n        endpoints = self.resolver.resolve(name)\n        self.cache[name] = {"time": now, "endpoints": endpoints}\n        return endpoints","failure_mode":"Stale endpoints remain indefinitely usable.","expected_error":"Resolution failure after refresh if no eligible endpoint remains.","cause":"Cache has no expiration or invalidation policy.","root_cause":"Freshness requirements were not modeled in the resolution boundary.","solution":"Use bounded TTL and explicit refresh semantics.","edge_case":"Endpoint becomes unhealthy immediately after being cached.","security_implication":"Stale cached endpoints must not bypass current authorization or trust policy.","performance_implication":"Caching can reduce registry load but may temporarily route to stale topology.","test_case":"Resolve once, advance beyond TTL, then verify that the underlying resolver is called again.","test_input":{"service":"search-service","ttl_seconds":30,"elapsed_seconds":31},"expected_test_result":"Cached value is not used after TTL expiry.","testing_guidance":"Test hit, miss, expiration, refresh failure, and invalid cached data.","verification_method":"Deterministic clock-based cache test.","verification_guidance":"Verify cache freshness independently from endpoint health evaluation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B035-R001","D35-B082-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B081-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["resolution_cache"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["RFC 9111 — HTTP caching concepts; Kubernetes Documentation — service discovery"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B081-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Service Discovery & Endpoint Resolution","subtopic":"Resolver contract verification","concept":"A resolver contract should be tested independently of its concrete discovery implementation.","knowledge_type":"testing","question":"How should service discovery resolution be verified?","answer":"Use contract tests that verify identity input, endpoint validity, empty-result semantics, failure classification, and provider independence.","explanation":"Testing only a registry implementation can miss assumptions made by consumers. A resolver contract establishes stable behavior across discovery providers.","principle":"Verify the boundary contract, not only the implementation.","objective":"Prevent provider changes from silently changing caller behavior.","requirements":["Resolver contract tests","Success and failure cases","Endpoint validation"],"constraints":["Tests must not require a production discovery registry"],"preconditions":["Resolver interface is defined"],"input":"Fake discovery provider","output":"Pass/fail contract results","expected_behavior":"Any conforming resolver satisfies the same observable contract.","incorrect_code":"def test_resolver():\n    assert registry.lookup("service")","corrected_code":"def test_resolver_contract(resolver):\n    endpoints = resolver.resolve("service")\n    assert endpoints\n    assert all(":" in endpoint for endpoint in endpoints)","failure_mode":"Provider-specific tests pass while application assumptions remain unverified.","expected_error":"AssertionError for contract violations.","cause":"Tests target infrastructure internals instead of the boundary.","root_cause":"No explicit resolver contract test suite.","solution":"Create reusable contract tests against fake and real resolver implementations.","edge_case":"Provider returns duplicate or malformed endpoints.","security_implication":"Contract tests should ensure endpoint validation does not accept unsafe schemes or malformed authority data.","performance_implication":"Contract tests should be deterministic and avoid external dependency latency.","test_case":"Run the same resolver contract suite against static and discovery-backed implementations.","test_input":{"implementations":["static","registry-backed"]},"expected_test_result":"Both implementations satisfy the same observable contract.","testing_guidance":"Include empty service, duplicate endpoint, malformed endpoint, timeout, and successful resolution.","verification_method":"Contract testing.","verification_guidance":"Record which contract clauses each implementation satisfies.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B081-R001","D35-B040-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B081-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B081-R001"],"verifies":["D35-B081-R001"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture — interface-oriented testing principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B082-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Load Balancing Boundary","subtopic":"Strategy abstraction","concept":"Load balancing should be isolated behind a selection policy so routing strategy can change without changing service clients.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a load-balancing boundary be structured?","answer":"Represent endpoint selection as a strategy interface receiving eligible endpoints and selection context, then return one endpoint or an explicit no-endpoint result.","explanation":"Selection policy is distinct from endpoint discovery. Keeping the two separate allows round-robin, weighted, least-load, or health-aware strategies to evolve independently.","principle":"Separate endpoint discovery from endpoint selection.","objective":"Make routing strategy replaceable and testable.","requirements":["Endpoint selection interface","Eligible endpoint set","Explicit empty-set behavior"],"constraints":["Selection must not invent endpoints","Strategy must operate only on eligible candidates"],"preconditions":["Candidate endpoints have already been resolved"],"input":"Eligible endpoints and selection context","output":"Selected endpoint or explicit failure","expected_behavior":"Selection returns exactly one eligible endpoint when candidates exist.","incorrect_code":"endpoint = random.choice(resolver.resolve(service))","corrected_code":"class Selector:\n    def select(self, endpoints):\n        if not endpoints:\n            raise LookupError("No eligible endpoints")\n        endpoint = endpoints[0]\n        return endpoint","failure_mode":"Routing policy is embedded in callers and cannot be tested independently.","expected_error":"LookupError for an empty candidate set.","cause":"No selection boundary exists.","root_cause":"Discovery and routing responsibilities are coupled.","solution":"Introduce a selection strategy abstraction.","edge_case":"Candidate list contains only unhealthy endpoints.","security_implication":"Selection must preserve endpoint trust and authorization constraints.","performance_implication":"Selection should be bounded in time and avoid expensive per-request global scans where unnecessary.","test_case":"Provide three eligible endpoints and verify the selector returns one of them.","test_input":["a:1","b:1","c:1"],"expected_test_result":"Returned endpoint belongs to the candidate set.","testing_guidance":"Test empty set, one endpoint, multiple endpoints, weighted inputs, and deterministic test strategies.","verification_method":"Strategy unit tests.","verification_guidance":"Assert selected values are always members of the eligible set.","language":"Java","language_version":"21+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 21+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"JDK 21+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B081-R001","D35-B082-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B081-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["load_balancing_strategy"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Kubernetes Documentation — service traffic distribution concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B082-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Load Balancing Boundary","subtopic":"Weighted routing","concept":"Weighted selection distributes requests according to explicit weights while excluding endpoints that are not eligible.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should weighted endpoint selection be implemented safely?","answer":"Validate non-negative weights, exclude ineligible endpoints, define zero-total behavior, and select deterministically when a deterministic test source is injected.","explanation":"Weights are policy data, not arbitrary arithmetic. Invalid weights can cause bias or runtime failures, while zero eligible weight requires explicit semantics.","principle":"Validate routing policy inputs before selection.","objective":"Provide predictable weighted distribution.","requirements":["Validated weights","Eligibility filtering","Explicit zero-weight behavior","Injectable randomness"],"constraints":["No negative weights","Do not select ineligible endpoints"],"preconditions":["Endpoint candidates contain weights"],"input":[{"endpoint":"a","weight":1},{"endpoint":"b","weight":3}],"output":"One selected endpoint","expected_behavior":"Over many independent selections, positive-weight endpoints receive traffic proportional to their configured weights within sampling variation.","incorrect_code":"weights = [-1, 2]\nchoice = random.choices(["a", "b"], weights=weights)[0]","corrected_code":"import random\n\ndef select_weighted(candidates, rng=random):\n    valid = [(e, w) for e, w in candidates if w > 0]\n    if not valid:\n        raise LookupError("No positive-weight endpoint")\n    endpoints, weights = zip(*valid)\n    return rng.choices(endpoints, weights=weights, k=1)[0]","failure_mode":"Invalid weights produce runtime errors or unintended routing.","expected_error":"LookupError when no positive eligible weight exists.","cause":"Weights are consumed without validation.","root_cause":"Routing policy input validation is missing.","solution":"Validate and filter weights before selection.","edge_case":"All endpoints have weight zero.","security_implication":"Routing metadata must not be accepted from untrusted actors without authorization and validation.","performance_implication":"Selection should be O(n) or use a precomputed structure when candidate sets are large.","test_case":"Use deterministic random selection with known weights.","test_input":{"weights":[1,3],"seed":7},"expected_test_result":"Only eligible positive-weight endpoints can be selected.","testing_guidance":"Test negative, zero, fractional, extremely skewed, and single-positive-weight cases.","verification_method":"Property and distribution-oriented tests.","verification_guidance":"Do not claim statistical distribution guarantees from a small test sample.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B082-R001","D35-B082-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B082-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["weighted_endpoint_selection"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python Standard Library Documentation — random.choices semantics"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B082-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Load Balancing Boundary","subtopic":"Health-aware selection","concept":"Endpoint health should constrain the candidate set before load-balancing selection.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should health information interact with load balancing?","answer":"First determine endpoint eligibility from current health state, then apply the load-balancing strategy only to eligible endpoints.","explanation":"Selecting an unhealthy endpoint and relying on transport failure to correct the choice couples health policy to request failure. Filtering first makes the routing rule explicit.","principle":"Eligibility precedes selection.","objective":"Avoid intentionally routing traffic to known-unhealthy endpoints.","requirements":["Health state","Eligibility predicate","Selection strategy"],"constraints":["Health status may be stale","Do not treat a single failed request as definitive health without policy"],"preconditions":["Endpoints have associated health information"],"input":"Endpoints with health state","output":"Selected healthy/eligible endpoint","expected_behavior":"Known-ineligible endpoints are not selected.","incorrect_code":"endpoint = selector.select(all_endpoints)\nif not endpoint.healthy:\n    raise RuntimeError("unhealthy")","corrected_code":"eligible = [e for e in endpoints if e.healthy]\nendpoint = selector.select(eligible)","failure_mode":"Known unhealthy endpoints continue receiving traffic.","expected_error":"LookupError when no endpoint is eligible.","cause":"Health filtering occurs after selection.","root_cause":"Eligibility and selection are ordered incorrectly.","solution":"Filter candidates using the health policy before invoking the selection strategy.","edge_case":"Every endpoint is temporarily unhealthy.","security_implication":"Health status must not be forgeable by unauthorized sources.","performance_implication":"Maintaining current health state can reduce per-request probing but requires freshness management.","test_case":"Mark one of three endpoints unhealthy and verify it is never selected.","test_input":{"endpoints":[{"id":"a","healthy":true},{"id":"b","healthy":false},{"id":"c","healthy":true}]},"expected_test_result":"Selection can return a or c but never b.","testing_guidance":"Test stale health, all-unhealthy, recovery, and health-state races.","verification_method":"Selection property test.","verification_guidance":"Assert every returned endpoint satisfies the eligibility predicate.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.80+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust 1.80+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Rust toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B081-R004","D35-B082-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B082-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["health_aware_selection"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Kubernetes Documentation — readiness and traffic eligibility concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B082-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Load Balancing Boundary","subtopic":"Request affinity","concept":"Affinity should be an explicit routing requirement rather than an accidental consequence of endpoint selection.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"When should request affinity be represented explicitly?","answer":"Use affinity only when the workload requires related requests to reach the same endpoint or partition. Model the affinity key and fallback behavior explicitly.","explanation":"Implicit affinity can create hidden load concentration and complicate failover. Explicit modeling makes the tradeoff visible.","principle":"Treat affinity as policy, not incidental behavior.","objective":"Preserve required state locality without hiding routing consequences.","requirements":["Affinity key","Selection policy","Fallback behavior","Failure handling"],"constraints":["Affinity must not override endpoint eligibility","Fallback must be defined"],"preconditions":["A workload has a documented locality requirement"],"input":"Affinity key and eligible endpoints","output":"Selected endpoint consistent with affinity policy","expected_behavior":"Same key can map consistently while the target remains eligible.","incorrect_code":"sticky_endpoint = last_endpoint_for_user[user_id]","corrected_code":"def select_with_affinity(key, eligible, mapping):\n    preferred = mapping.get(key)\n    if preferred in eligible:\n        return preferred\n    if not eligible:\n        raise LookupError("No eligible endpoint")\n    return eligible[0]","failure_mode":"Affinity sends traffic to an unavailable endpoint.","expected_error":"LookupError when no fallback endpoint exists.","cause":"Stored affinity mapping is treated as authoritative.","root_cause":"Affinity and endpoint eligibility are not separated.","solution":"Use affinity only as a preference constrained by current eligibility.","edge_case":"Preferred endpoint disappears during a request sequence.","security_implication":"Affinity identifiers must not expose or permit unauthorized cross-tenant routing.","performance_implication":"Affinity can improve locality but concentrate traffic and reduce balancing quality.","test_case":"Preferred endpoint becomes ineligible and verify selection falls back safely.","test_input":{"key":"customer-42","preferred":"a","eligible":["b","c"]},"expected_test_result":"Endpoint a is not selected.","testing_guidance":"Test stable affinity, endpoint removal, key collision, and fallback behavior.","verification_method":"Deterministic routing tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify affinity never violates eligibility constraints.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"TypeScript compiler","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B082-R003","D35-B088-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B082-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["explicit_affinity_policy"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Kubernetes Documentation — service traffic and session affinity concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B082-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Load Balancing Boundary","subtopic":"Selection verification","concept":"Load-balancing correctness can be verified through invariants without claiming a specific production traffic distribution.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"What should be verified in a load-balancer implementation?","answer":"Verify selection membership, exclusion of ineligible endpoints, deterministic behavior under deterministic strategy inputs, and explicit empty-set semantics. Distribution behavior should be tested separately and reported as measured only when actually measured.","explanation":"Correctness properties are distinct from performance or traffic-distribution measurements.","principle":"Verify routing invariants separately from measured distribution.","objective":"Prevent invalid endpoint selection and unsupported performance claims.","requirements":["Selection invariants","Eligibility tests","Empty-set behavior"],"constraints":["Do not infer production distribution from unit tests"],"preconditions":["Selection strategy is testable"],"input":"Synthetic eligible endpoint sets","output":"Verified selection outcomes","expected_behavior":"Every selection satisfies the strategy contract.","incorrect_code":"assert selector.select(endpoints) == "endpoint-a"","corrected_code":"selected = selector.select(endpoints)\nassert selected in {e.id for e in endpoints if e.eligible}","failure_mode":"Tests overfit to one implementation result.","expected_error":"AssertionError when a selected endpoint violates eligibility.","cause":"Tests assert incidental ordering rather than contract properties.","root_cause":"No explicit routing invariants.","solution":"Test properties guaranteed by the boundary contract.","edge_case":"Multiple valid selections exist.","security_implication":"Routing tests should include unauthorized/ineligible endpoint cases.","performance_implication":"Distribution and latency require separate measured tests.","test_case":"Run selection repeatedly over a candidate set and verify every result is eligible.","test_input":{"eligible":["a","b","c"],"iterations":100},"expected_test_result":"All selections belong to the eligible set.","testing_guidance":"Separate correctness, statistical distribution, and performance tests.","verification_method":"Property-based or invariant testing.","verification_guidance":"Label statistical observations MEASURED only when an actual test run produced them.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B082-R001","D35-B082-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B082-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B082-R001","D35-B082-R003"],"verifies":["D35-B082-R001","D35-B082-R003"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Property-based testing principles; Kubernetes traffic distribution documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B083-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Session & Stateful Interaction Boundary","subtopic":"Session abstraction","concept":"Session state should be represented behind an explicit boundary when an interaction requires server-associated state.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a session boundary be implemented?","answer":"Define a session identifier and session-store interface, keep session serialization and persistence outside business logic, and make expiration semantics explicit.","explanation":"The boundary prevents session storage details from leaking into application services.","principle":"Separate interaction identity from session storage.","objective":"Make session state replaceable, testable, and explicit.","requirements":["Session identifier","Session store contract","Expiration policy","Serialization rules"],"constraints":["Do not store unvalidated arbitrary objects","Do not make session lifetime implicit"],"preconditions":["The interaction genuinely requires server-associated state"],"input":"Session identifier and state","output":"Stored/retrieved session state","expected_behavior":"State can be loaded, updated, expired, and removed according to the session contract.","incorrect_code":"global_sessions[user_id] = request_state","corrected_code":"class SessionStore:\n    def get(self, session_id):\n        raise NotImplementedError\n    def put(self, session_id, state, expires_at):\n        raise NotImplementedError\n    def delete(self, session_id):\n        raise NotImplementedError","failure_mode":"Session state is coupled to process memory and disappears or diverges across instances.","expected_error":"SessionNotFound when a session is absent or expired.","cause":"No persistence or session boundary exists.","root_cause":"State ownership was left implicit.","solution":"Introduce an explicit session-store abstraction and lifecycle policy.","edge_case":"Two application instances receive requests for the same session.","security_implication":"Session identifiers require protection against fixation, guessing, replay, and unauthorized access.","performance_implication":"External session storage introduces network latency; state size should be bounded.","test_case":"Store and retrieve a session through a fake session store.","test_input":{"session_id":"s-1","state":{"cart_items":2}},"expected_test_result":"Retrieved state equals the stored logical state before expiration.","testing_guidance":"Test create, read, update, expiration, deletion, malformed state, and concurrent update behavior.","verification_method":"Session contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify lifecycle semantics independently of the storage implementation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B083-R002","D35-B083-R004"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["session_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OWASP Session Management guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B083-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Session & Stateful Interaction Boundary","subtopic":"State ownership","concept":"A stateful interaction requires explicit ownership of which component is authoritative for each piece of session state.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"How should session state ownership be defined?","answer":"Document the authoritative owner, permitted readers and writers, lifecycle, and conflict behavior for each state category.","explanation":"Without ownership rules, multiple components can mutate the same session state independently, causing lost updates and inconsistent behavior.","principle":"Every mutable state has an explicit authoritative owner.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous session state mutation.","requirements":["State ownership model","Read/write permissions","Lifecycle","Conflict semantics"],"constraints":["No hidden secondary writers"],"preconditions":["Session state contains mutable information"],"input":"Session state model","output":"Explicit ownership contract","expected_behavior":"Only authorized components can mutate authoritative state.","incorrect_code":"cart = session.get("cart")\ncart.append(item)\nother_service.save_cart(cart)","corrected_code":"class CartService:\n    def add_item(self, session_id, item):\n        state = self.store.get(session_id)\n        updated = self._apply_item(state, item)\n        self.store.put(session_id, updated)","failure_mode":"Independent components overwrite each other's state.","expected_error":"Conflict error where concurrent version checks fail.","cause":"Multiple writers mutate shared session state.","root_cause":"No authoritative state owner.","solution":"Centralize mutation through the owning service or enforce versioned updates.","edge_case":"Two requests update different fields concurrently.","security_implication":"State ownership must enforce tenant and user boundaries.","performance_implication":"Centralized writes may become contention points and should be designed with bounded state.","test_case":"Attempt a session mutation through an unauthorized component.","test_input":{"actor":"read-only-component","operation":"update_session"},"expected_test_result":"Mutation is rejected by the boundary.","testing_guidance":"Test authorized writer, unauthorized writer, concurrent writers, and stale versions.","verification_method":"Contract and authorization tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify every mutation path is traceable to the authoritative owner.","language":"Java","language_version":"21+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 21+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"JDK 21+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B083-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B083-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["session_state_ownership"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Domain-driven design principles; OWASP Session Management guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B083-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Session & Stateful Interaction Boundary","subtopic":"Stateless versus stateful interaction","concept":"State should be retained server-side only when the interaction requires server-associated mutable state or when another explicit architectural reason exists.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"How should stateless and stateful interactions be distinguished?","answer":"Use stateless interactions when each request carries sufficient context and no server-side conversational state is required; use a session boundary when state must persist across requests.","explanation":"Statefulness introduces storage, expiration, concurrency, scaling, and recovery concerns. It should therefore be an explicit requirement.","principle":"Do not introduce server state without a requirement for it.","objective":"Minimize unnecessary shared mutable state.","requirements":["State requirement analysis","Explicit session contract when needed"],"constraints":["Do not equate authentication with arbitrary session state","Do not store unnecessary data"],"preconditions":["Interaction requirements are known"],"input":"Interaction requirements","output":"Stateless or stateful architecture decision","expected_behavior":"Statefulness is selected only where its benefits or requirements justify it.","incorrect_code":"session["everything"] = request.json","corrected_code":"if requires_cross_request_state:\n    session_store.put(session_id, required_state, expires_at)\nelse:\n    process_request(request)","failure_mode":"Unnecessary server state increases coordination and recovery complexity.","expected_error":"Architecture review failure rather than a runtime error.","cause":"State was introduced without a documented requirement.","root_cause":"No statefulness decision boundary.","solution":"Document and enforce the interaction's state requirements.","edge_case":"A request is retried after the original response was lost.","security_implication":"State stored across requests may contain sensitive information and requires appropriate access controls.","performance_implication":"Stateless designs can reduce shared-storage dependency but may increase request payload or token size.","test_case":"Classify an interaction that needs no cross-request mutable state.","test_input":{"requires_cross_request_state":false},"expected_test_result":"The design does not create a server-side session solely for convenience.","testing_guidance":"Test explicit state requirement, state expiration, retry, and concurrent request cases.","verification_method":"Architecture decision test/review.","verification_guidance":"Verify stateful components have a documented ownership and lifecycle contract.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"TypeScript compiler","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D35-B083-R001","D35-B083-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["statefulness_decision"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Roy Fielding, REST architectural constraints; OWASP Session Management guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B083-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Session & Stateful Interaction Boundary","subtopic":"Expiration and concurrency","concept":"Session expiration and concurrent mutation must be explicit because a session can outlive its intended security or business lifetime and can be updated by multiple requests.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should session expiration and concurrency be implemented?","answer":"Store an expiration timestamp and, when concurrent updates matter, a version or equivalent concurrency token. Reject expired or stale updates rather than silently overwriting newer state.","explanation":"Expiration controls lifetime while versioning controls concurrent mutation. They solve different problems and should not be conflated.","principle":"Enforce lifetime and mutation concurrency independently.","objective":"Prevent stale or expired session updates.","requirements":["Expiration timestamp","Concurrency token where needed","Explicit conflict error"],"constraints":["Do not silently revive expired sessions","Do not overwrite newer state with stale state"],"preconditions":["Session state is mutable"],"input":"Session ID, expected version, current time","output":"Updated session or explicit expiration/conflict result","expected_behavior":"Expired sessions are rejected and stale versions cannot overwrite newer state.","incorrect_code":"state = store.get(id)\nstore.put(id, update(state))","corrected_code":"state = store.get(id)\nif state.expires_at <= now:\n    raise SessionExpired()\nif state.version != expected_version:\n    raise SessionConflict()\nstore.put(id, update(state), version=state.version + 1)","failure_mode":"Late or concurrent requests overwrite valid newer state.","expected_error":"SessionExpired or SessionConflict.","cause":"Expiration and concurrency checks are absent.","root_cause":"Session lifecycle was treated as simple key-value storage.","solution":"Add explicit expiration and optimistic version checks.","edge_case":"Expiration occurs between read and write.","security_implication":"Expired credentials or state must not be accepted after their defined lifetime.","performance_implication":"Version checks add minimal metadata overhead while avoiding heavier locking.","test_case":"Two requests read version 4; the first writes version 5; the second attempts to write using version 4.","test_input":{"initial_version":4,"first_write":5,"second_expected_version":4},"expected_test_result":"Second write is rejected as stale.","testing_guidance":"Test expiration boundary, clock skew assumptions, concurrent updates, and deletion.","verification_method":"Deterministic time and concurrency tests.","verification_guidance":"Use an injected clock and controlled store to make lifecycle tests reproducible.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B033-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B083-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["session_expiration","session_concurrency"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OWASP Session Management guidance; optimistic concurrency control principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B083-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Session & Stateful Interaction Boundary","subtopic":"Session integrity testing","concept":"Session tests should verify lifecycle, authorization, expiration, serialization, and concurrency invariants.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"What should a session boundary test suite verify?","answer":"Verify that sessions cannot be accessed across authorization boundaries, expired sessions are rejected, malformed state is handled safely, concurrent stale writes fail, and deletion is effective.","explanation":"Session correctness includes both state behavior and security boundaries.","principle":"Test session lifecycle and security as one boundary contract.","objective":"Detect state-management and session-isolation defects.","requirements":["Lifecycle tests","Authorization tests","Concurrency tests","Malformed-state tests"],"constraints":["Tests must not rely solely on happy-path session retrieval"],"preconditions":["Session boundary exists"],"input":"Synthetic session scenarios","output":"Verification results","expected_behavior":"All session invariants hold under normal and adversarial boundary conditions.","incorrect_code":"def test_session():\n    assert store.get("known-id") is not None","corrected_code":"def test_expired_session_is_rejected(store, clock):\n    store.put("s1", {"x": 1}, expires_at=100)\n    clock.set(101)\n    assert store.get("s1") is None","failure_mode":"Tests verify existence but not lifecycle or security semantics.","expected_error":"AssertionError on lifecycle contract violation.","cause":"Test coverage focuses only on retrieval.","root_cause":"Session invariants were not encoded as tests.","solution":"Build a boundary-level test matrix covering lifecycle, security, and concurrency.","edge_case":"Session data is corrupted or serialized under an older schema.","security_implication":"Cross-user or cross-tenant session access must be explicitly tested as forbidden.","performance_implication":"Large session-state tests should also check bounded storage behavior.","test_case":"Attempt to retrieve a session using an unauthorized tenant context.","test_input":{"session_tenant":"tenant-a","request_tenant":"tenant-b"},"expected_test_result":"Access is denied and no session state is returned.","testing_guidance":"Include fixation, expiration, authorization, concurrent update, malformed serialization, deletion, and replay cases.","verification_method":"Boundary contract and security tests.","verification_guidance":"Require every supported session implementation to pass the same lifecycle contract.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B083-R001","D35-B083-R004","D35-B028-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B083-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B083-R001","D35-B083-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B083-R001","D35-B083-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OWASP Session Management guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B084-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Transaction Context Propagation","subtopic":"Context contract","concept":"A transaction or workflow context propagated across service boundaries should have a stable identifier and explicit lifecycle semantics.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"What should a distributed transaction context contain?","answer":"Use a minimal context containing a correlation/transaction identifier, scope metadata where required, deadline or cancellation information when supported, and lifecycle status. Do not propagate mutable business state indiscriminately.","explanation":"A context identifies and constrains an operation; it should not become a hidden transport for arbitrary application data.","principle":"Propagate bounded control metadata, not uncontrolled mutable state.","objective":"Provide traceable and bounded cross-service operation context.","requirements":["Stable context identifier","Scope","Lifecycle","Optional deadline/cancellation metadata"],"constraints":["Do not place secrets in context","Do not treat context as authoritative business state"],"preconditions":["Multiple services participate in one logical operation"],"input":"Incoming operation context","output":"Validated local context","expected_behavior":"Each service receives a context consistent with the originating operation.","incorrect_code":"context = request.json\nforwarded_request = {**request.json, "internal_state": database.dump()}","corrected_code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass OperationContext:\n    operation_id: str\n    tenant_id: str\n    deadline_ns: int | None = None","failure_mode":"Context becomes an unbounded mutable data channel.","expected_error":"ContextValidationError for missing or invalid required fields.","cause":"No explicit context schema.","root_cause":"Control context and business payload are conflated.","solution":"Define a minimal immutable context contract.","edge_case":"A downstream service receives an expired deadline.","security_implication":"Context must not be trusted as proof of authorization; authorization must be independently enforced.","performance_implication":"Small context objects minimize propagation overhead.","test_case":"Validate a context containing an operation ID and tenant ID.","test_input":{"operation_id":"op-123","tenant_id":"tenant-a"},"expected_test_result":"Context is accepted and represented as an immutable local object.","testing_guidance":"Test missing identifiers, malformed IDs, unauthorized tenant context, expired deadline, and oversized context.","verification_method":"Schema and boundary tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify only declared context fields are propagated.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B075-R001","D35-B075-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["distributed_operation_context"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["W3C Trace Context Recommendation; OpenTelemetry Context documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B084-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Transaction Context Propagation","subtopic":"Context lifecycle","concept":"A propagated context must have a defined creation, validation, derivation, and termination lifecycle.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a distributed operation context be propagated?","answer":"Create the context at the operation boundary, validate it on receipt, derive a local context without mutating the parent representation, and terminate it when the local operation completes.","explanation":"Explicit lifecycle prevents context leakage across unrelated requests and accidental mutation across asynchronous boundaries.","principle":"Context is scoped to an operation lifecycle.","objective":"Prevent stale or cross-request context contamination.","requirements":["Boundary creation","Validation","Scoped propagation","Termination"],"constraints":["No global mutable context","No context reuse across unrelated operations"],"preconditions":["An operation boundary exists"],"input":"Incoming request and optional parent context","output":"Scoped local context","expected_behavior":"Context exists only for the intended operation scope.","incorrect_code":"CURRENT_CONTEXT[""operation_id""] = request.headers.get(""X-Operation-ID"")","corrected_code":"def handle(request):\n    context = parse_context(request)\n    with operation_scope(context):\n        return process(request)","failure_mode":"Concurrent operations overwrite shared context.","expected_error":"Context isolation test failure.","cause":"Context is stored in global mutable state.","root_cause":"Operation scope is not represented explicitly.","solution":"Use request-scoped or task-scoped context management.","edge_case":"Nested operations create child contexts.","security_implication":"A context from one request must never leak into another tenant or user operation.","performance_implication":"Scoped context propagation should avoid expensive copying of large payloads.","test_case":"Run two concurrent logical operations with different IDs and verify isolation.","test_input":{"operations":["op-a","op-b"]},"expected_test_result":"Each operation observes only its own context.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrency, nested scopes, exceptions, cancellation, and context cleanup.","verification_method":"Concurrency isolation testing.","verification_guidance":"Assert context is absent after each operation completes.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B033-R001","D35-B075-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B084-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["context_lifecycle"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry Context documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B084-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Transaction Context Propagation","subtopic":"Propagation boundary","concept":"Cross-service context propagation should use a standardized transport representation rather than application-specific ad hoc headers when a standard exists.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should operation context cross a service boundary?","answer":"Serialize only the required context into a defined transport representation, propagate it through supported client/server middleware, and reconstruct a local context at the receiving boundary.","explanation":"Standardized propagation reduces incompatible conventions and enables interoperable tracing and context handling.","principle":"Use explicit, interoperable propagation formats.","objective":"Preserve operation identity across service boundaries.","requirements":["Transport representation","Sender propagation","Receiver extraction","Validation"],"constraints":["Never propagate secrets by default","Do not trust propagated identity for authorization"],"preconditions":["Both sides support the propagation contract"],"input":"Local operation context","output":"Transport metadata","expected_behavior":"Receiver reconstructs equivalent non-sensitive context metadata.","incorrect_code":"headers[""X-Everything""] = json.dumps(context.dict)","corrected_code":"headers[""traceparent""] = build_traceparent(context)","failure_mode":"Incompatible or unsafe context metadata is propagated.","expected_error":"PropagationValidationError for malformed metadata.","cause":"No standardized propagation contract.","root_cause":"Transport and context semantics were coupled informally.","solution":"Use an explicit propagation format and validation boundary.","edge_case":"Incoming propagation header is malformed.","security_implication":"Trace or correlation context must not be used as an authorization credential.","performance_implication":"Propagation headers add request overhead proportional to metadata size.","test_case":"Send a valid propagation value and reconstruct it at the receiver.","test_input":{"operation_id":"op-9"},"expected_test_result":"Receiver extracts the operation context without adding unauthorized fields.","testing_guidance":"Test absent, valid, malformed, duplicate, oversized, and conflicting propagation metadata.","verification_method":"Client/server propagation contract test.","verification_guidance":"Verify equivalence of allowed fields and rejection of invalid metadata.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STANDARDIZED","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B075-R001","D35-B075-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B084-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["context_propagation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["W3C Trace Context Recommendation; OpenTelemetry Specification"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B084-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Transaction Context Propagation","subtopic":"Cancellation and failure semantics","concept":"A downstream operation should not continue indefinitely after its originating operation has been cancelled or its deadline has expired.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should deadlines and cancellation propagate across services?","answer":"Compute a remaining deadline at each boundary and pass it downstream. On cancellation or deadline expiration, stop or cancel supported work and return an explicit timeout/cancellation result.","explanation":"A distributed operation needs a bounded lifetime. Independent downstream timeouts can exceed the caller's budget and waste resources.","principle":"Propagate the remaining operation budget, not the original unlimited lifetime.","objective":"Contain work within the originating operation's deadline.","requirements":["Deadline representation","Remaining-budget calculation","Cancellation handling"],"constraints":["Do not extend an expired parent deadline","Avoid negative or ambiguous remaining time"],"preconditions":["Caller provides or establishes a deadline"],"input":"Parent deadline and current time","output":"Local remaining timeout","expected_behavior":"Downstream timeout never exceeds the remaining parent budget.","incorrect_code":"downstream_timeout = 30","corrected_code":"remaining = max(0, parent_deadline - clock.now())\nif remaining == 0:\n    raise TimeoutError(""parent deadline expired"")\ndownstream_call(timeout=remaining)","failure_mode":"Downstream work continues after the parent operation has expired.","expected_error":"TimeoutError or CancellationError.","cause":"Each service chooses an independent timeout.","root_cause":"No deadline propagation contract.","solution":"Propagate and enforce remaining operation budget.","edge_case":"Clock differences exist between services.","security_implication":"Cancellation context must not bypass authorization or cleanup requirements.","performance_implication":"Deadline propagation reduces wasted work and queue buildup.","test_case":"Parent deadline has already expired before downstream invocation.","test_input":{"parent_deadline":100,"current_time":101},"expected_test_result":"Downstream call is not initiated.","testing_guidance":"Test no deadline, short deadline, expired deadline, nested calls, and cancellation races.","verification_method":"Deterministic clock and timeout tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify downstream timeout is bounded by remaining parent budget.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"TypeScript runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B047-R001","D35-B084-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B084-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["deadline_propagation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["gRPC Documentation — deadlines; Google SRE principles on bounded work"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B084-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Transaction Context Propagation","subtopic":"Context verification","concept":"Propagation verification must establish context continuity and isolation without assuming that propagated metadata is trustworthy business authorization.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"How can distributed context propagation be verified?","answer":"Test continuity across service boundaries, isolation between concurrent operations, deadline propagation, malformed metadata handling, and cleanup after completion.","explanation":"Context correctness includes both positive propagation and negative isolation properties.","principle":"Verify continuity and non-leakage.","objective":"Detect missing propagation and cross-operation contamination.","requirements":["Multi-hop propagation tests","Isolation tests","Deadline tests","Malformed-input tests"],"constraints":["Do not treat trace continuity as authorization proof"],"preconditions":["At least two simulated service boundaries"],"input":"Synthetic multi-hop request","output":"Propagation verification results","expected_behavior":"Allowed context fields remain consistent across hops while unrelated operations remain isolated.","incorrect_code":"assert service_b.context == service_a.context","corrected_code":"assert service_b.context.operation_id == service_a.context.operation_id\nassert service_b.context.tenant_id == service_a.context.tenant_id","failure_mode":"Tests assert object identity or internal representation rather than contract equivalence.","expected_error":"AssertionError for missing or leaked context.","cause":"Propagation tests are coupled to implementation details.","root_cause":"No explicit context equivalence contract.","solution":"Verify only declared propagation semantics.","edge_case":"An intermediate service intentionally creates a child operation.","security_implication":"Test that propagated tenant or identity metadata cannot cross authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Multi-hop propagation tests should remain deterministic and avoid real external dependencies.","test_case":"Propagate one operation through three simulated services and run another concurrently.","test_input":{"operation_a":"a","operation_b":"b","hops":3},"expected_test_result":"Each hop preserves its operation context and no context crosses between operations.","testing_guidance":"Include synchronous, asynchronous, error, cancellation, and child-operation scenarios.","verification_method":"End-to-end contract verification using in-memory service boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Record continuity, isolation, deadline, and cleanup assertions separately.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STANDARDIZED","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B084-R001","D35-B084-R003","D35-B084-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B084-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B084-R001","D35-B084-R003","D35-B084-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B084-R001","D35-B084-R003","D35-B084-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["W3C Trace Context Recommendation; OpenTelemetry Specification"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B085-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Reconciliation","subtopic":"Reconciliation contract","concept":"Reconciliation compares authoritative and observed representations according to an explicit comparison and repair contract.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a reconciliation boundary be defined?","answer":"Define source-of-truth selection, comparison identity, detectable difference categories, permissible repair actions, and verification criteria.","explanation":"Without a contract, reconciliation can overwrite correct state or repeatedly oscillate between representations.","principle":"Reconcile according to explicit authority and convergence rules.","objective":"Detect and safely resolve state divergence.","requirements":["Authority definition","Identity function","Comparison rules","Repair policy","Verification"],"constraints":["Never overwrite state without an authority rule","Unknown conflicts require explicit handling"],"preconditions":["Two or more representations may diverge"],"input":"Authoritative and observed records","output":"Reconciliation decision and optional repair action","expected_behavior":"Differences are classified before any mutation occurs.","incorrect_code":"if local != remote:\n    local = remote","corrected_code":"def reconcile(authoritative, observed):\n    differences = compare(authoritative, observed)\n    return classify_and_plan(differences)","failure_mode":"A divergent copy overwrites authoritative or newer data.","expected_error":"Conflict requiring manual or policy-specific resolution.","cause":"Comparison is immediately converted into mutation.","root_cause":"No reconciliation decision boundary.","solution":"Separate detection, classification, repair planning, and verification.","edge_case":"Both sources changed independently.","security_implication":"Reconciliation must preserve tenant and authorization boundaries and avoid copying unauthorized fields.","performance_implication":"Large datasets require incremental comparison, checkpoints, or indexed identities.","test_case":"Compare identical records and verify that no repair is planned.","test_input":{"authoritative":{"id":1,"value":"A"},"observed":{"id":1,"value":"A"}},"expected_test_result":"No divergence is detected.","testing_guidance":"Test equal state, one-sided change, two-sided change, missing record, duplicate identity, and malformed data.","verification_method":"Reconciliation contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify detection does not mutate data.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B068-R001","D35-B085-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["reconciliation_contract"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Well-Architected guidance on consistency and distributed systems; distributed systems reconciliation principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B085-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Reconciliation","subtopic":"Drift detection","concept":"Drift detection identifies divergence without necessarily repairing it.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should drift detection be separated from repair?","answer":"Build a read-only comparison step that emits a structured drift finding. A separate repair planner decides whether and how correction is allowed.","explanation":"Separating detection from mutation prevents a detection bug from directly causing destructive state changes.","principle":"Detect first; mutate only through an explicit repair decision.","objective":"Make divergence observable and safely actionable.","requirements":["Stable identity comparison","Structured drift finding","No mutation during detection"],"constraints":["Detection must be side-effect free","Comparison must account for relevant version metadata"],"preconditions":["Comparable representations exist"],"input":"Two state snapshots","output":"Drift findings","expected_behavior":"Detected differences are reported without changing either input.","incorrect_code":"def detect_and_fix(a, b):\n    if a != b:\n        save(b)","corrected_code":"def detect_drift(a, b):\n    return {"drift": a != b, "differences": compare(a, b)}","failure_mode":"Drift scan mutates production state unexpectedly.","expected_error":"Unexpected mutation detected by verification.","cause":"Detection and repair are combined.","root_cause":"No read-only detection boundary.","solution":"Emit findings first and plan repairs separately.","edge_case":"Input changes during comparison.","security_implication":"Drift findings may expose sensitive differences and require access controls.","performance_implication":"Comparison can be expensive; incremental or hash-based detection may help where appropriate.","test_case":"Run drift detection against differing snapshots and verify no write operation occurs.","test_input":{"a":{"version":1},"b":{"version":2}},"expected_test_result":"A drift finding is returned and no mutation occurs.","testing_guidance":"Use a write-tracking fake store to prove detection is side-effect free.","verification_method":"Mutation-free contract test.","verification_guidance":"Assert zero writes during detection.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B085-R001","D35-B085-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B085-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["drift_detection"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed systems reconciliation principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B085-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Reconciliation","subtopic":"Conflict classification","concept":"Not every divergence is a repairable conflict; differences should be classified using version, authority, and business rules.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should reconciliation conflicts be classified?","answer":"Classify differences such as stale replica, authoritative update, concurrent modification, missing entity, duplicate identity, and unknown conflict before selecting a repair action.","explanation":"Classification makes repair policy explicit and prevents generic last-write-wins behavior from being applied where it is unsafe.","principle":"Classify before correcting.","objective":"Prevent unsafe automatic conflict resolution.","requirements":["Version metadata","Authority information","Conflict categories","Unknown category"],"constraints":["Unknown conflicts must not be silently repaired"],"preconditions":["Both representations expose sufficient comparison metadata"],"input":"Two divergent records","output":"Conflict classification","expected_behavior":"Every divergence receives a documented category.","incorrect_code":"return ""remote_wins""","corrected_code":"def classify(local, remote):\n    if remote.version > local.version:\n        return "remote_newer"\n    if local.version > remote.version:\n        return "local_newer"\n    if local.payload != remote.payload:\n        return "concurrent_conflict"\n    return "equal"","failure_mode":"Concurrent edits are incorrectly treated as stale replicas.","expected_error":"UnknownConflict when metadata is insufficient.","cause":"Classification uses only payload difference.","root_cause":"Version and authority semantics are absent.","solution":"Include explicit version and authority metadata in conflict classification.","edge_case":"Versions are equal but payloads differ due to concurrent writes.","security_implication":"Do not automatically copy sensitive fields across trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"Classification should be cheap enough to run before expensive repair workflows.","test_case":"Two records have equal versions but different payloads.","test_input":{"local":{"version":4,"value":"A"},"remote":{"version":4,"value":"B"}},"expected_test_result":"Conflict is classified as concurrent rather than remote-newer.","testing_guidance":"Test every classification category and insufficient-metadata paths.","verification_method":"Decision-table testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify each conflict category maps to exactly the documented repair policy.","language":"Java","language_version":"21+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 21+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"JDK 21+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B085-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B085-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["reconciliation_conflict_classification"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Optimistic concurrency control principles; distributed data consistency literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B085-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Reconciliation","subtopic":"Repair and convergence","concept":"Repair should be an explicit operation with idempotent behavior where possible and a measurable convergence condition.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should reconciliation repair be implemented?","answer":"Apply only an approved repair plan, make the operation idempotent where feasible, record the resulting version, and re-read the state to verify the intended convergence condition.","explanation":"A successful write does not prove convergence. Verification must compare the resulting state against the reconciliation objective.","principle":"Repair is not complete until the target consistency condition is verified.","objective":"Turn detected divergence into verified convergence.","requirements":["Repair plan","Idempotency","Post-repair read","Convergence condition"],"constraints":["No repair without classification/policy","Failed verification must remain visible"],"preconditions":["A repair action has been authorized"],"input":"Repair plan and target state","output":"Repair result and verification outcome","expected_behavior":"After successful repair, the documented convergence condition is satisfied or a failure remains explicit.","incorrect_code":"store.write(target)\nreturn True","corrected_code":"store.write(target)\nactual = store.read(target.id)\nif not converged(actual, target):\n    raise ReconciliationVerificationError(target.id)","failure_mode":"System reports repair success when state remains divergent.","expected_error":"ReconciliationVerificationError.","cause":"Write completion is treated as convergence.","root_cause":"No post-repair verification.","solution":"Read back and evaluate the actual convergence condition.","edge_case":"Repair succeeds locally but another concurrent writer changes state before verification.","security_implication":"Repair operations require authorization and auditability when changing protected data.","performance_implication":"Post-write verification adds reads but prevents silent divergence.","test_case":"Fake store accepts a write but returns a different state on readback.","test_input":{"write_result":"success","readback":{"version":4,"value":"unexpected"}},"expected_test_result":"Verification fails rather than reporting convergence.","testing_guidance":"Test successful repair, rejected repair, idempotent retry, concurrent mutation, and readback failure.","verification_method":"Repair-plus-readback test.","verification_guidance":"Keep repair execution status separate from convergence verification status.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B085-R001","D35-B085-R002","D35-B085-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B085-R001","D35-B085-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["reconciliation_repair"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed systems reconciliation principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B085-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Consistency Reconciliation","subtopic":"Convergence verification","concept":"Reconciliation verification must measure whether the declared consistency condition is actually satisfied after repair.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"How should reconciliation convergence be verified?","answer":"Re-read relevant state after repair, compare it against the target invariant, and distinguish repair execution success from convergence success.","explanation":"A mutation acknowledgment proves only that an operation was accepted or completed; it does not prove that all relevant representations now agree.","principle":"Verify the outcome, not merely the action.","objective":"Prevent false claims of successful reconciliation.","requirements":["Post-repair observation","Convergence predicate","Failure classification"],"constraints":["Do not infer global convergence from one local read unless the architecture defines it as sufficient"],"preconditions":["A convergence target is defined"],"input":"Before state, repair result, after state","output":"Convergence verification result","expected_behavior":"Verification passes only when the defined invariant is satisfied.","incorrect_code":"assert repair_response.status == 200","corrected_code":"before = snapshot()\napply_repair()\nafter = snapshot()\nassert converged(after)\nassert divergence_count(after) < divergence_count(before)","failure_mode":"Repair endpoint success is mistaken for system convergence.","expected_error":"AssertionError or explicit convergence failure.","cause":"Verification checks action response rather than state outcome.","root_cause":"No outcome-level reconciliation verification.","solution":"Define and evaluate convergence predicates.","edge_case":"Distributed state converges asynchronously after the immediate repair response.","security_implication":"Verification reads must respect the same authorization boundaries as repair operations.","performance_implication":"Repeated convergence polling should use bounded intervals and termination conditions.","test_case":"Repair one divergent representation and verify both representations satisfy the declared invariant.","test_input":{"initial_divergence":1,"target_divergence":0},"expected_test_result":"Verification passes only when divergence reaches the target condition.","testing_guidance":"Test immediate convergence, delayed convergence, non-convergence, and verification timeout.","verification_method":"Outcome verification test.","verification_guidance":"Record verification separately from repair execution.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"TypeScript runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B085-R004","D35-B068-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B085-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B085-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B085-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed systems reconciliation principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B086-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Cache Coherence Boundary","subtopic":"Cache ownership","concept":"A cache is a derived representation unless explicitly defined otherwise; the authoritative owner of data must remain clear.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"How should cache ownership be represented?","answer":"Document the authoritative data source, cache key, cacheable representation, freshness policy, invalidation mechanism, and behavior when cache state conflicts with the source.","explanation":"Treating a cache as an independent source of truth can create silent divergence and unsafe writes.","principle":"Cache derived state unless authority is explicitly designed otherwise.","objective":"Prevent cache contents from becoming accidental authority.","requirements":["Source of truth","Cache contract","Freshness policy","Invalidation/read-through policy"],"constraints":["Do not write authoritative business state only to cache","Cache misses must have defined behavior"],"preconditions":["A performance requirement justifies caching"],"input":"Authoritative record","output":"Cache representation","expected_behavior":"Cache state can be rebuilt or invalidated according to the documented policy.","incorrect_code":"cache.set(key, updated_record)\nreturn updated_record","corrected_code":"authoritative.save(updated_record)\ncache.invalidate(key)\nreturn updated_record","failure_mode":"Cache contains data that was never durably persisted to the authoritative store.","expected_error":"CacheConsistencyError in explicitly modeled strict-coherence designs.","cause":"Cache is written as if it were authoritative.","root_cause":"Ownership boundary is undefined.","solution":"Persist authority first and define cache update/invalidation semantics.","edge_case":"Authoritative write succeeds but cache invalidation fails.","security_implication":"Cached sensitive data requires access controls and appropriate expiration.","performance_implication":"Cache invalidation may be asynchronous and create bounded staleness.","test_case":"Update authoritative state and verify stale cache is invalidated according to policy.","test_input":{"key":"product:1","old":"A","new":"B"},"expected_test_result":"Subsequent reads do not incorrectly return the old value beyond the declared consistency policy.","testing_guidance":"Test miss, hit, write, invalidation failure, stale read, and cache rebuild.","verification_method":"Cache contract testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify cache is not the only durable authority unless explicitly designed as such.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B035-R001","D35-B086-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B035-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["cache_ownership"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Well-Architected Framework — caching and data consistency considerations"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B086-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Cache Coherence Boundary","subtopic":"Invalidation propagation","concept":"Distributed cache invalidation requires explicit propagation semantics because multiple cache instances can hold independent stale representations.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should cache invalidation propagate across instances?","answer":"Define an invalidation event or equivalent mechanism carrying a stable cache key and generation/version information. Consumers should invalidate only the relevant entry and tolerate duplicate invalidations.","explanation":"Versioned invalidation can reduce stale resurrection when invalidation messages arrive out of order.","principle":"Invalidate by identity and version, not by implicit timing alone.","objective":"Coordinate cache invalidation across distributed instances.","requirements":["Stable cache key","Invalidation message","Generation/version","Duplicate tolerance"],"constraints":["Invalidation messages may be duplicated or reordered","Do not invalidate unrelated tenants or keys"],"preconditions":["More than one cache instance may serve the same data"],"input":"Cache invalidation event","output":"Updated local cache state","expected_behavior":"Relevant stale entries are removed or superseded.","incorrect_code":"for key in local_cache:\n    local_cache.pop(key)","corrected_code":"def apply_invalidation(event):\n    current = cache.get(event.key)\n    if current and current.generation <= event.generation:\n        cache.delete(event.key)","failure_mode":"Global invalidation causes unnecessary cache loss or an old invalidation resurrects stale state.","expected_error":"InvalidationValidationError for malformed events.","cause":"No key or generation semantics.","root_cause":"Invalidation is treated as a generic cache flush.","solution":"Use scoped, version-aware invalidation events.","edge_case":"Older invalidation arrives after a newer refresh.","security_implication":"Invalidation events must be authenticated and tenant-scoped.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained invalidation reduces cache churn compared with global flushes.","test_case":"Apply generation 5 invalidation followed by generation 4 invalidation.","test_input":{"events":[{"key":"k","generation":5},{"key":"k","generation":4}]},"expected_test_result":"Generation 4 does not undo the state established by generation 5.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate, reordered, missing-key, malformed, and cross-tenant invalidations.","verification_method":"Event-ordering and idempotency tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify invalidation is safe under duplication and reordering.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B086-R001","D35-B074-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B086-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["distributed_cache_invalidation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed caching consistency principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B086-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Cache Coherence Boundary","subtopic":"Version and generation checks","concept":"Generation or version metadata can prevent older cache data from overwriting or superseding newer representations.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why use generation numbers in distributed cache updates?","answer":"A monotonic generation allows a cache to reject updates older than the representation it already holds, reducing stale resurrection from delayed messages or asynchronous refreshes.","explanation":"Distributed delivery can reorder messages. Generation checks turn ordering assumptions into an explicit state rule.","principle":"Accept state transitions only when their generation satisfies the cache contract.","objective":"Prevent stale asynchronous updates.","requirements":["Comparable generation","Current-generation tracking","Explicit acceptance rule"],"constraints":["Generation must be scoped to the correct key and authority","Do not compare unrelated generations"],"preconditions":["Source provides meaningful version or generation metadata"],"input":"Current and incoming cache versions","output":"Accepted or rejected cache update","expected_behavior":"Older generations cannot replace newer generations.","incorrect_code":"cache[key] = incoming_value","corrected_code":"current = cache.get(key)\nif current is None or incoming.generation >= current.generation:\n    cache[key] = incoming","failure_mode":"Delayed old data replaces newer cached data.","expected_error":"No exception required; update should be safely rejected.","cause":"Cache update ignores generation metadata.","root_cause":"Asynchronous ordering was assumed rather than modeled.","solution":"Compare scoped generation values before accepting updates.","edge_case":"Two updates have equal generation but conflicting payloads.","security_implication":"Generation metadata must be bound to the correct authority and key.","performance_implication":"Generation comparisons are inexpensive and can reduce unnecessary refresh churn.","test_case":"Apply generation 10 followed by generation 9.","test_input":{"current_generation":10,"incoming_generation":9},"expected_test_result":"Generation 9 is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Test older, equal, newer, missing, and conflicting generations.","verification_method":"State-transition property test.","verification_guidance":"Verify acceptance rules are deterministic and key-scoped.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.80+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust 1.80+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Rust toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B086-R002","D35-B087-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B086-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["cache_generation_guard"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed systems versioning and monotonic state principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B086-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Cache Coherence Boundary","subtopic":"Stale-entry behavior","concept":"Stale cache behavior must be an explicit product and architecture decision: reject, refresh, serve-with-warning, or fall back according to defined consistency requirements.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"How should stale cache entries be handled?","answer":"Define a maximum acceptable staleness policy and an explicit behavior for stale entries. Strict data may require refresh-before-read, while tolerant data may permit bounded stale reads.","explanation":"There is no universally correct stale-data policy. The correct implementation depends on the data's consistency requirement.","principle":"Make staleness a declared contract.","objective":"Prevent accidental stale-data semantics.","requirements":["Freshness bound","Stale detection","Fallback policy"],"constraints":["Do not silently serve stale sensitive or correctness-critical state"],"preconditions":["Cache stores data with a freshness requirement"],"input":"Cached entry and current time","output":"Fresh value, refreshed value, or explicit stale outcome","expected_behavior":"Behavior follows the declared maximum-staleness policy.","incorrect_code":"return cache.get(key)","corrected_code":"entry = cache.get(key)\nif entry and now - entry.timestamp <= max_staleness:\n    return entry.value\nreturn refresh(key)","failure_mode":"Consumers receive arbitrarily stale information.","expected_error":"StaleDataUnavailable when refresh is required but cannot be completed.","cause":"No freshness contract.","root_cause":"Cache performance policy replaced data-consistency policy.","solution":"Define and enforce maximum staleness.","edge_case":"Refresh fails after entry becomes stale.","security_implication":"Sensitive data should not remain available through cache after its authorized lifetime.","performance_implication":"Strict freshness increases source reads and latency.","test_case":"Advance the clock beyond maximum staleness and verify refresh is attempted.","test_input":{"age_seconds":61,"max_staleness":60},"expected_test_result":"Stale value is not served as fresh.","testing_guidance":"Test boundary timestamps, refresh success, refresh failure, and allowed stale fallback.","verification_method":"Deterministic freshness tests.","verification_guidance":"Use an injected clock and explicit policy values.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"TypeScript runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B035-R002","D35-B086-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B086-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["cache_staleness_policy"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Well-Architected Framework — caching and consistency considerations"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B086-R005","dataset_id":"D35-B086-R005","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Cache Coherence Boundary","subtopic":"Coherence testing","concept":"Cache coherence testing should model delayed, duplicated, reordered, and failed invalidation/update signals.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"How should distributed cache coherence be tested?","answer":"Use controlled multi-cache tests with deterministic event ordering to verify invalidation, generation checks, stale handling, and recovery after source changes.","explanation":"Single-cache happy-path tests cannot expose distributed ordering defects.","principle":"Test cache behavior under message disorder and source/cache divergence.","objective":"Detect stale resurrection and inconsistent cache behavior.","requirements":["Multiple cache instances","Controlled event delivery","Source of truth","Version metadata"],"constraints":["Tests should be deterministic","No production cache dependency"],"preconditions":["Cache coherence contract exists"],"input":"Synthetic cache update/invalidation sequence","output":"Coherence verification result","expected_behavior":"All cache instances converge according to the declared coherence policy.","incorrect_code":"assert cache_a.get(key) == cache_b.get(key)","corrected_code":"deliver(event_1, cache_a)\ndeliver(event_2, cache_b)\nrun_reconciliation()\nassert policy_allows(cache_a.get(key), cache_b.get(key))","failure_mode":"Tests require immediate equality even though the architecture permits bounded eventual consistency.","expected_error":"AssertionError when declared convergence conditions are violated.","cause":"Test does not encode the actual coherence contract.","root_cause":"Consistency policy and test expectations are disconnected.","solution":"Define measurable coherence invariants and test them under controlled disorder.","edge_case":"One cache misses an invalidation event entirely.","security_implication":"Cross-tenant cache contamination must be included in coherence tests.","performance_implication":"Large coherence tests should use focused keys and controlled event streams.","test_case":"Deliver invalidation to one cache before the other and verify eventual policy compliance.","test_input":{"caches":["a","b"],"events":["invalidate-k"]},"expected_test_result":"Both caches satisfy the declared post-convergence policy.","testing_guidance":"Test delayed, duplicate, reordered, lost, and malformed events.","verification_method":"Deterministic distributed cache simulation.","verification_guidance":"Separate temporary inconsistency from permanent policy violation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B086-R002","D35-B086-R003","D35-B086-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B086-R001","D35-B086-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B086-R002","D35-B086-R003","D35-B086-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B086-R002","D35-B086-R003","D35-B086-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed caching consistency principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B087-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Lock-Free & Atomic State Update","subtopic":"Atomic state transition","concept":"An atomic state update changes a value as one indivisible logical operation relative to competing updates.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What should an atomic state update guarantee?","answer":"The update must have a clearly defined linearization point or equivalent atomicity guarantee, so competing operations cannot observe or create an invalid intermediate state.","explanation":"Atomicity is about the indivisibility of the state transition, not merely the absence of explicit locks.","principle":"Define the state transition as one atomic operation.","objective":"Prevent partial concurrent updates.","requirements":["Atomic operation","Valid state invariant","Concurrency-safe storage primitive"],"constraints":["Do not implement read-modify-write with separate unsynchronized operations when atomicity is required"],"preconditions":["State can be represented by an atomic or transactional primitive"],"input":"Current state and update operation","output":"New state","expected_behavior":"Concurrent operations cannot produce a state outside the declared transition rules.","incorrect_code":"value = shared.value\nvalue += 1\nshared.value = value","corrected_code":"def increment(shared):\n    return shared.compare_and_swap(lambda value: value + 1)","failure_mode":"Concurrent read-modify-write operations lose updates.","expected_error":"Concurrency invariant failure rather than necessarily a runtime exception.","cause":"Read and write are separate synchronization events.","root_cause":"Atomicity requirement was not represented by the implementation primitive.","solution":"Use a native atomic/CAS primitive or transactional mechanism appropriate to the runtime.","edge_case":"Update function is retried after CAS failure.","security_implication":"Atomic updates must not bypass authorization checks associated with the state.","performance_implication":"Lock-free primitives can reduce contention in suitable workloads but may increase retry under contention.","test_case":"Run concurrent increments against a shared counter and verify the final state equals the number of successful operations.","test_input":{"initial":0,"operations":100},"expected_test_result":"Final value reflects every successfully committed increment according to the primitive's contract.","testing_guidance":"Test contention, repeated CAS failure, overflow, cancellation, and invariant-preserving updates.","verification_method":"Concurrency stress/property test.","verification_guidance":"Use actual execution before labeling concurrency behavior MEASURED.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++20","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++20+","dependencies":["C++ standard library"],"execution_requirements":"C++20 compiler with threading support","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STANDARDIZED","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B087-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["atomic_state_update"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["C++ standard library atomics documentation; C++ memory model"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B087-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Lock-Free & Atomic State Update","subtopic":"Compare-and-swap","concept":"Compare-and-swap updates state only if the observed version/value still matches the expected value.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How does compare-and-swap prevent lost updates?","answer":"A CAS operation compares the current value against an expected value and commits the replacement only when they match; otherwise the caller retries using the newly observed state or reports contention.","explanation":"CAS makes the read expectation part of the update operation, preventing a stale reader from overwriting a newer value.","principle":"Condition the write on the state actually observed.","objective":"Implement optimistic atomic updates.","requirements":["Expected value/version","Atomic compare-and-swap","Retry or conflict policy"],"constraints":["Retry count must be bounded where contention can persist","Update function must be safe to recompute"],"preconditions":["Storage provides CAS or equivalent conditional update"],"input":"Expected version and desired update","output":"Committed update or contention failure","expected_behavior":"Stale expected versions cannot commit.","incorrect_code":"store.write(id, new_value)","corrected_code":"if store.compare_and_swap(id, expected_version, new_value):\n    return True\nraise ConflictError(id)","failure_mode":"A stale writer overwrites a newer state.","expected_error":"ConflictError.","cause":"Write does not include expected version.","root_cause":"No conditional update primitive.","solution":"Use CAS or a conditional versioned update.","edge_case":"Many writers repeatedly fail CAS.","security_implication":"CAS does not replace authorization; authorization must be evaluated for the attempted mutation.","performance_implication":"High contention can cause retries and CPU consumption; bounded retries or escalation may be necessary.","test_case":"Two writers use the same expected version; only one can commit.","test_input":{"expected_version":7,"writers":2},"expected_test_result":"At most one writer commits version 8 using the same expected version.","testing_guidance":"Test one writer, two writers, high contention, stale retry, and bounded retry exhaustion.","verification_method":"Concurrent conditional-update test.","verification_guidance":"Verify no two conflicting updates both report success against the same expected version.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.80+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust 1.80+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Rust toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STANDARDIZED","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B087-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B087-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["compare_and_swap_update"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Rust Standard Library Atomic Documentation; C++ memory model concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B087-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Lock-Free & Atomic State Update","subtopic":"Version guards","concept":"Version guards prevent stale operations from applying after another operation has advanced the state.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should version guards be applied to state updates?","answer":"Include the expected version in the update condition and increment the version as part of the successful state transition.","explanation":"A version guard turns stale-write prevention into an explicit invariant.","principle":"A successful update must consume exactly the version it expected.","objective":"Prevent stale writes and lost updates.","requirements":["Monotonic version","Conditional update","Atomic version increment"],"constraints":["Version comparison must be scoped to one entity/state stream","Version wraparound must be considered if fixed-width storage is used"],"preconditions":["State has a meaningful version field"],"input":"Entity ID, expected version, update","output":"Success with next version or conflict","expected_behavior":"Update succeeds only when current version equals expected version.","incorrect_code":"entity.value = update\nentity.version += 1","corrected_code":"updated = store.update_if_version(id, expected_version, update)\nif not updated:\n    raise ConflictError(id)","failure_mode":"Two writers both increment from the same stale version.","expected_error":"ConflictError for stale writer.","cause":"Version check and version increment are not atomic.","root_cause":"Concurrency guard is implemented as separate operations.","solution":"Perform version comparison and update atomically.","edge_case":"Version metadata is missing from a legacy record.","security_implication":"Version checks must not expose unauthorized record existence through detailed conflict responses.","performance_implication":"Conditional updates can avoid heavyweight locks but may retry under contention.","test_case":"Attempt update with expected version lower than current version.","test_input":{"current_version":9,"expected_version":8},"expected_test_result":"Update is rejected and current state remains unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Test equal, lower, higher, missing, and concurrently changed versions.","verification_method":"Conditional update contract test.","verification_guidance":"Verify failed version checks have no state mutation.","language":"Java","language_version":"21+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 21+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"JDK 21+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B013-R001","D35-B087-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B087-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["version_guard"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Optimistic concurrency control principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B087-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Lock-Free & Atomic State Update","subtopic":"Invariant-preserving updates","concept":"An atomic primitive prevents interleaving of the update but does not automatically guarantee that the resulting state satisfies a complex business invariant.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"Why is atomicity insufficient for complex invariants?","answer":"Atomicity guarantees indivisibility of a primitive operation, while complex invariants may require multiple values or records to change consistently. Such invariants need a suitable transactional or synchronization mechanism.","explanation":"A CAS on one field cannot automatically guarantee a relation between several independent fields.","principle":"Choose synchronization semantics based on the invariant scope.","objective":"Avoid incorrectly applying lock-free primitives to multi-object invariants.","requirements":["Defined invariant scope","Appropriate atomic/transactional mechanism","Conflict handling"],"constraints":["Do not claim single-variable atomicity protects multi-variable invariants"],"preconditions":["Invariant spans multiple state elements"],"input":"State transition and invariant","output":"Appropriate synchronization design","expected_behavior":"Chosen mechanism can guarantee the complete invariant.","incorrect_code":"balance.compare_and_swap(old_balance, new_balance)\nledger_total = new_balance","corrected_code":"with transaction:\n    update_balance_and_ledger()\n    assert invariant_holds()","failure_mode":"Individual atomic updates succeed while combined state becomes inconsistent.","expected_error":"InvariantViolation during verification.","cause":"Synchronization scope is smaller than invariant scope.","root_cause":"Atomic primitive was mistaken for general transactionality.","solution":"Use a mechanism that covers all state participating in the invariant.","edge_case":"One state element is external and cannot participate in the same transaction.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive invariants require stronger guarantees when partial state could authorize an action.","performance_implication":"Broader transactions can increase contention; the invariant must determine the necessary scope.","test_case":"Update two related values concurrently and verify their required relationship remains true.","test_input":{"invariant":"a+b==total"},"expected_test_result":"Invariant remains true after every committed operation.","testing_guidance":"Use concurrent invariant tests and failure injection between individual operations.","verification_method":"Invariant and concurrency verification.","verification_guidance":"Test both successful and interrupted transitions.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B011-R001","D35-B087-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B087-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["invariant_scoped_synchronization"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Database transaction and concurrency-control principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B087-R005","dataset_id":"D35-B087-R005","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Lock-Free & Atomic State Update","subtopic":"Concurrent verification","concept":"Concurrent state-update tests should verify safety properties such as no lost update, no invalid state, and bounded retry behavior.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"How should lock-free or atomic updates be verified?","answer":"Run controlled concurrent scenarios and verify state invariants, successful-operation counts, stale-write rejection, and retry termination. Do not infer production throughput without measurement.","explanation":"Concurrency correctness is primarily about safety and progress properties; throughput is a separate empirical concern.","principle":"Verify concurrency invariants before performance characteristics.","objective":"Detect races and incorrect conditional updates.","requirements":["Concurrent test harness","State invariant","Conflict tracking","Bounded retries"],"constraints":["Tests must not assume scheduler ordering","Performance claims require actual measurements"],"preconditions":["Atomic or conditional update primitive exists"],"input":"Concurrent operations","output":"Final state and operation outcomes","expected_behavior":"Final state satisfies the declared invariant and conflicting stale operations are handled according to policy.","incorrect_code":"assert final_value == expected\n# without verifying rejected writes","corrected_code":"assert invariant_holds(final_state)\nassert successful_writes + rejected_conflicts == attempted_writes","failure_mode":"Tests pass despite silently losing or double-counting operations.","expected_error":"AssertionError for invariant or accounting mismatch.","cause":"Test observes only final value.","root_cause":"Concurrency outcomes are not individually tracked.","solution":"Track operation outcomes and assert safety invariants.","edge_case":"A retry loop never converges under sustained contention.","security_implication":"Concurrency defects can cause authorization or accounting state corruption and require security-sensitive tests where applicable.","performance_implication":"Stress tests can reveal contention but results must be labeled MEASURED only for actual runs.","test_case":"Execute many concurrent conditional updates with controlled contention.","test_input":{"workers":8,"operations_per_worker":100},"expected_test_result":"Every committed update satisfies the state invariant and conflicts are explicitly accounted for.","testing_guidance":"Run race detection where supported, contention tests, retry exhaustion, and cancellation tests.","verification_method":"Concurrent invariant testing and race analysis.","verification_guidance":"Record actual execution results separately from test design.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++20","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++20+","dependencies":["C++ standard library"],"execution_requirements":"C++20 compiler and threading support","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B087-R001","D35-B087-R002","D35-B087-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B087-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B087-R001","D35-B087-R002","D35-B087-R003","D35-B087-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B087-R001","D35-B087-R002","D35-B087-R003","D35-B087-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["C++ concurrency and memory-model documentation; concurrency testing principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B088-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Partitioning & Sharding Boundary","subtopic":"Partition-key abstraction","concept":"Partitioning should be driven by an explicit key function that deterministically maps records to partitions according to documented ownership rules.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a partition-key boundary be implemented?","answer":"Define a stable key extractor and partition-mapping function. Keep partitioning logic separate from business entities so placement strategy can evolve.","explanation":"A partition key determines locality, load distribution, and routing. Making it explicit allows it to be tested independently.","principle":"Partition placement is an explicit infrastructure policy.","objective":"Provide deterministic and testable data placement.","requirements":["Stable key extractor","Partition mapping","Defined missing-key behavior"],"constraints":["Key must be stable for the required lifetime","Do not silently use mutable fields that can change placement"],"preconditions":["Records have a suitable partitioning identity"],"input":"Record","output":"Partition identifier","expected_behavior":"Equivalent records map to the same partition under the same partitioning configuration.","incorrect_code":"partition = hash(record.name) % count","corrected_code":"def partition_for(record_id: str, partition_count: int) -> int:\n    if partition_count <= 0:\n        raise ValueError("partition_count must be positive")\n    return stable_hash(record_id) % partition_count","failure_mode":"Mutable or runtime-dependent fields cause records to move unexpectedly.","expected_error":"ValueError for invalid partition configuration.","cause":"Partition key is implicit or unstable.","root_cause":"Placement policy is not modeled as a boundary.","solution":"Define a stable key function and mapping contract.","edge_case":"Partition count changes and modulo mapping moves most keys.","security_implication":"Partition keys must not permit cross-tenant access; tenant scope may need to be included in identity.","performance_implication":"Poor key distribution can create hot partitions.","test_case":"Map the same stable ID repeatedly under the same partition configuration.","test_input":{"record_id":"customer-42","partition_count":16},"expected_test_result":"The same partition identifier is returned each time.","testing_guidance":"Test empty IDs, Unicode IDs, partition-count changes, and skewed keys.","verification_method":"Deterministic mapping tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify mapping determinism independently from storage.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B088-R002","D35-B088-R003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["partition_key_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Apache Kafka Documentation — partitioning by record key; distributed data partitioning principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B088-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Partitioning & Sharding Boundary","subtopic":"Deterministic placement","concept":"Deterministic partitioning preserves locality when the same key and partitioning configuration are used.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why is deterministic partition placement important?","answer":"Deterministic placement lets a system route related records consistently to the same partition, which supports locality, ordering within a partition, and efficient ownership decisions.","explanation":"If placement changes unpredictably, consumers cannot rely on partition-local semantics and data movement becomes difficult to reason about.","principle":"Same key plus same placement configuration yields predictable ownership.","objective":"Preserve partition-local semantics.","requirements":["Stable hashing/mapping","Consistent key extraction","Partition identity"],"constraints":["Determinism does not imply stable placement across partition-count changes unless the mapping algorithm supports it"],"preconditions":["Partition mapping algorithm is defined"],"input":"Key and partition configuration","output":"Partition ID","expected_behavior":"Repeated mapping is stable under unchanged configuration.","incorrect_code":"partition = hash(key) % partitions","corrected_code":"partition = stable_partition(key, partitioning_configuration)","failure_mode":"Language/runtime hash randomization changes placement across process runs.","expected_error":"PlacementVerificationError in a contract test.","cause":"Unstable runtime hash is used as persistent partition identity.","root_cause":"No explicit stable hashing requirement.","solution":"Use a documented stable partitioning function where persistent determinism is required.","edge_case":"Partition configuration changes.","security_implication":"Stable identifiers must not leak sensitive data through partition names or logs.","performance_implication":"Hashing cost should be bounded; distribution quality matters more than micro-optimizing the hash without evidence.","test_case":"Map a fixed set of keys twice using the same configuration.","test_input":{"keys":["a","b","c"],"partitions":8},"expected_test_result":"Both mappings are identical.","testing_guidance":"Test across process boundaries when the language runtime hash is not guaranteed stable.","verification_method":"Cross-process determinism test.","verification_guidance":"Do not confuse deterministic placement with zero data movement during resharding.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.80+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust 1.80+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Rust toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B088-R001","D35-B088-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B088-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["deterministic_partitioning"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Apache Kafka Documentation — partition assignment concepts; distributed hashing principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B088-R003","dataset_id":"D35-B088-R003","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Partitioning & Sharding Boundary","subtopic":"Hot-partition handling","concept":"A partitioning strategy can be deterministic yet operationally poor when a small number of keys receive disproportionate traffic or data.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"How should hot partitions be handled?","answer":"Detect skew using measured key and partition load, then apply an explicit mitigation such as better key design, key salting where semantics permit, or repartitioning.","explanation":"Hot partitions are a workload-distribution problem and cannot be solved safely by blindly changing the partition function.","principle":"Mitigate measured skew according to workload semantics.","objective":"Prevent concentrated partition load.","requirements":["Partition-level telemetry","Skew detection","Mitigation policy"],"constraints":["Do not split a key if ordering or atomicity requires single-partition ownership","Do not claim skew without measurement"],"preconditions":["Partition load can be observed"],"input":"Measured partition load distribution","output":"Skew finding and mitigation plan","expected_behavior":"A hot partition is identified using explicit thresholds or statistical criteria.","incorrect_code":"if partition_load > 80:\n    split_key_randomly()","corrected_code":"if measured_skew_exceeds_threshold(loads):\n    return propose_key_or_partition_strategy_change(loads)","failure_mode":"Random splitting breaks ordering or entity-locality guarantees.","expected_error":"PartitioningPolicyError when requested mitigation violates workload semantics.","cause":"Load balancing is optimized without considering key semantics.","root_cause":"Partitioning policy lacks explicit workload constraints.","solution":"Measure skew and choose a semantics-preserving mitigation.","edge_case":"One customer legitimately generates most traffic.","security_implication":"Tenant-specific partitioning must preserve isolation while preventing one tenant from monopolizing shared resources.","performance_implication":"Hot partitions can dominate latency and throughput even when average utilization appears acceptable.","test_case":"Generate a synthetic skewed key distribution and detect the overloaded partition.","test_input":{"distribution":{"p0":900,"p1":10,"p2":10}},"expected_test_result":"Skew is detected according to the configured detection rule.","testing_guidance":"Use actual measurements for workload claims; test detection logic with synthetic deterministic data.","verification_method":"Skew-detection test.","verification_guidance":"Label workload results MEASURED only when the workload was actually executed.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B042-R001","D35-B088-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B088-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["hot_partition_detection"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Apache Kafka Documentation — partitioning and load distribution concepts"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B088-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Partitioning & Sharding Boundary","subtopic":"Rebalancing boundary","concept":"Partition rebalancing changes ownership or placement and therefore requires an explicit migration and availability contract.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"What belongs in a partition rebalancing boundary?","answer":"Model ownership transition, data movement, routing changes, progress tracking, failure recovery, and completion verification separately from ordinary partition lookup.","explanation":"Changing placement can expose missing or duplicated data if routing and migration are updated inconsistently.","principle":"Treat rebalancing as a stateful migration, not a simple configuration change.","objective":"Make partition movement safe and observable.","requirements":["Ownership state","Migration progress","Routing transition","Recovery","Verification"],"constraints":["Do not switch routing before required data availability is established","Rollback semantics must be defined"],"preconditions":["A partitioning change is required"],"input":"Old and target partition assignments","output":"Migration plan and ownership state","expected_behavior":"Records remain accessible throughout the defined migration lifecycle.","incorrect_code":"partition_count += 1\nrestart_all_services()","corrected_code":"plan = create_rebalance_plan(old_config, new_config)\nexecute_data_migration(plan)\nverify_migration(plan)\nswitch_routing(plan)","failure_mode":"Routing changes before target data is ready.","expected_error":"RebalanceVerificationError.","cause":"Placement and migration are treated as one atomic configuration edit.","root_cause":"No rebalancing lifecycle boundary.","solution":"Plan, migrate, verify, then switch ownership/routing.","edge_case":"Migration stops halfway through a partition.","security_implication":"Migration must preserve tenant and access-control boundaries.","performance_implication":"Rebalancing consumes network, storage, and compute capacity and may affect normal traffic.","test_case":"Interrupt migration after a checkpoint and resume it.","test_input":{"partition":"p3","checkpoint":"record-5000"},"expected_test_result":"Resume continues from a valid checkpoint without corrupting or duplicating logical records.","testing_guidance":"Test interruption, retry, rollback, partial target availability, and final verification.","verification_method":"Migration lifecycle integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify source/target record counts and logical identity according to the migration contract.","language":"Java","language_version":"21+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 21+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"JDK 21+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B069-R001","D35-B079-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B088-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["partition_rebalancing"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Apache Kafka Documentation — partition reassignment concepts; distributed data migration principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B088-R005","dataset_id":"D35-B088-R005","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Data Partitioning & Sharding Boundary","subtopic":"Partition correctness testing","concept":"Partition tests should verify deterministic placement, complete ownership, isolation, and migration correctness.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"How should a sharding boundary be tested?","answer":"Test key-to-partition determinism, valid partition ranges, tenant/key isolation, skew detection, and migration behavior under interruption.","explanation":"Partitioning correctness is broader than whether a hash function returns an integer.","principle":"Verify placement, ownership, and migration invariants.","objective":"Detect partitioning defects before they affect distributed state.","requirements":["Mapping tests","Ownership tests","Migration tests","Skew tests"],"constraints":["No production data required for unit/contract tests"],"preconditions":["Partition contract is documented"],"input":"Synthetic records and partition configurations","output":"Verification results","expected_behavior":"All records have exactly one valid logical owner under the current contract.","incorrect_code":"assert partition_for(key) >= 0","corrected_code":"partition = partition_for(key, count)\nassert 0 <= partition < count\nassert owner_for(key) == partition","failure_mode":"Tests allow invalid or ambiguous ownership.","expected_error":"AssertionError for invalid placement.","cause":"Tests cover only numeric output.","root_cause":"Ownership invariant is not encoded.","solution":"Test complete placement and ownership properties.","edge_case":"Partition count changes during migration.","security_implication":"Include cross-tenant key-collision and ownership-isolation tests.","performance_implication":"Large synthetic distributions can test skew logic but should not be presented as production benchmarks.","test_case":"Map and validate a synthetic dataset across all partitions.","test_input":{"records":1000,"partitions":10},"expected_test_result":"Every record maps to exactly one valid partition under the configured mapping.","testing_guidance":"Test empty dataset, one partition, many partitions, skew, migration interruption, and duplicate keys.","verification_method":"Property and migration tests.","verification_guidance":"Use actual execution to validate concrete implementation behavior.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B088-R001","D35-B088-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B088-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B088-R001","D35-B088-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B088-R001","D35-B088-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed data partitioning principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B089-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Replication Boundary","subtopic":"Primary/replica abstraction","concept":"Replication should expose explicit roles and consistency semantics rather than leaking storage topology into application services.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"How should a primary/replica boundary be represented?","answer":"Define interfaces for authoritative writes and replica reads, along with explicit consistency expectations and behavior when replicas are unavailable or stale.","explanation":"Application code needs to know whether it requires authoritative state or can tolerate a replica view.","principle":"Make replication roles explicit at the data-access boundary.","objective":"Prevent accidental reads from an unsuitable replica.","requirements":["Role abstraction","Read consistency contract","Write authority","Failure behavior"],"constraints":["Replica must not be treated as authoritative without explicit design","Read routing must honor consistency requirements"],"preconditions":["Data is replicated across storage nodes"],"input":"Read/write operation and consistency requirement","output":"Authoritative or replica-backed result","expected_behavior":"Writes use the authoritative role and reads follow the requested consistency policy.","incorrect_code":"db = random.choice(all_databases)\ndb.execute(query)","corrected_code":"class DataStore:\n    def write(self, command):\n        return self.primary.write(command)\n\n    def read(self, query, consistency):\n        return self.router.read(query, consistency)","failure_mode":"Writes or consistency-sensitive reads are routed to replicas incorrectly.","expected_error":"ConsistencyUnavailable when required consistency cannot be satisfied.","cause":"Replication roles are hidden from the application boundary.","root_cause":"No explicit replication abstraction.","solution":"Represent primary and replica responsibilities through a data-access contract.","edge_case":"Primary is unavailable while replicas are readable but stale.","security_implication":"Replica access must preserve the same authorization and tenant constraints as primary access.","performance_implication":"Replica reads can distribute read load but introduce replication lag.","test_case":"Issue a write and verify the write path targets only the authoritative role.","test_input":{"operation":"write","target_roles":["primary","replica-1"]},"expected_test_result":"Only the primary write path is eligible.","testing_guidance":"Test write routing, replica read, consistency-sensitive read, and role failure.","verification_method":"Role-routing contract test.","verification_guidance":"Verify no write-capable application path bypasses the authority boundary.","language":"Java","language_version":"21+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 21+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"JDK 21+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B068-R001","D35-B089-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["replication_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Well-Architected Framework — reliability and database replication considerations"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B089-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Replication Boundary","subtopic":"Replication lag","concept":"Replica lag must be observable when it can affect correctness decisions.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should replica lag influence read routing?","answer":"Expose or derive a bounded freshness signal and route consistency-sensitive reads to an authoritative source when the replica cannot satisfy the required freshness.","explanation":"A replica may be available and responsive while still lacking a recent write.","principle":"Availability does not imply freshness.","objective":"Prevent stale replicas from violating read consistency requirements.","requirements":["Lag/freshness signal","Read consistency requirement","Fallback routing"],"constraints":["Do not claim exact freshness without reliable measurement","Lag metrics must be scoped to the relevant replication stream"],"preconditions":["Replication lag can occur"],"input":"Read request and replica freshness metadata","output":"Eligible read target","expected_behavior":"Replica is used only when its freshness satisfies the request's policy.","incorrect_code":"return replica.read(query)","corrected_code":"if replica.fresh_enough(required_version):\n    return replica.read(query)\nreturn primary.read(query)","failure_mode":"Read-after-write request returns stale data.","expected_error":"ConsistencyUnavailable if authoritative fallback is also unavailable.","cause":"Replica freshness is ignored.","root_cause":"Read routing has no consistency-aware policy.","solution":"Route according to explicit freshness/consistency requirements.","edge_case":"Freshness metadata itself is delayed.","security_implication":"Do not expose sensitive replication topology through client-visible errors.","performance_implication":"Primary fallback can increase load during lag events.","test_case":"Replica is one version behind the required version.","test_input":{"required_version":12,"replica_version":11},"expected_test_result":"Replica is rejected for the consistency-sensitive read.","testing_guidance":"Test exact match, lagging, unknown freshness, primary failure, and relaxed consistency reads.","verification_method":"Consistency routing tests.","verification_guidance":"Use deterministic version metadata rather than claiming real-world lag values without measurement.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B068-R002","D35-B089-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B089-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["replica_freshness_routing"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Well-Architected Framework — distributed data consistency considerations"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B089-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Replication Boundary","subtopic":"Read routing","concept":"Read routing should select a replica only when its consistency level satisfies the caller's requirement.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should consistency-aware read routing be implemented?","answer":"Represent the required consistency level explicitly and filter eligible replicas before selection. If none qualify, use the documented authoritative fallback or return an explicit failure.","explanation":"A generic round-robin reader can violate application semantics when some reads require current state.","principle":"Consistency requirement precedes replica selection.","objective":"Make read routing semantically safe.","requirements":["Consistency requirement","Replica eligibility","Fallback policy"],"constraints":["Do not silently downgrade consistency","Replica health and freshness both matter"],"preconditions":["At least one authoritative or replicated read source exists"],"input":"Query and consistency requirement","output":"Read target","expected_behavior":"Selected target satisfies both health and consistency constraints.","incorrect_code":"replica = replicas[next_index()]\nreturn replica.query(query)","corrected_code":"eligible = [r for r in replicas if r.healthy and r.satisfies(consistency)]\nif not eligible:\n    return primary.query(query)\nreturn select(eligible).query(query)","failure_mode":"A healthy but stale replica serves a consistency-sensitive query.","expected_error":"ConsistencyUnavailable if policy forbids fallback and no eligible source exists.","cause":"Routing considers availability only.","root_cause":"Consistency was not part of endpoint eligibility.","solution":"Include consistency in the routing contract.","edge_case":"No replica satisfies strict consistency but relaxed consistency would be acceptable for another request.","security_implication":"Consistency routing must not allow a stale source to reveal data that has been revoked from the authoritative state.","performance_implication":"Strict reads can increase primary load.","test_case":"Two replicas are healthy; only one satisfies the required version.","test_input":{"required_version":20,"replicas":[{"id":"r1","version":19},{"id":"r2","version":20}]},"expected_test_result":"r2 is eligible and selected; r1 is excluded.","testing_guidance":"Test strong, eventual, unknown, and fallback consistency modes.","verification_method":"Eligibility and routing contract tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify routing never silently downgrades the requested consistency.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"TypeScript runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B068-R001","D35-B089-R002"],"depends_on":["D35-B089-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["consistency_aware_read_routing"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Well-Architected Framework — distributed data consistency considerations"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B089-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Replication Boundary","subtopic":"Failover","concept":"Failover changes authority and therefore requires explicit role transition, fencing or ownership protection where applicable, and verification before normal writes resume.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"What should a replication failover boundary guarantee?","answer":"A failover process should establish a new authoritative role, prevent unsafe concurrent writers where the system requires single authority, route traffic to the new authority, and verify readiness before accepting normal writes.","explanation":"Simply detecting primary failure and redirecting clients can create split-brain or stale-authority writes.","principle":"Failover is an authority transition, not merely endpoint redirection.","objective":"Prevent concurrent authorities during failover.","requirements":["Failure detection","Authority transition","Fencing/ownership mechanism where required","Readiness verification"],"constraints":["Do not assume failure detection is perfect","Recovery of the old primary must not create a second authority"],"preconditions":["Replication system supports a defined failover model"],"input":"Failure signal and replica state","output":"New authority state or failover failure","expected_behavior":"At most the architecture-defined number of authoritative writers are active.","incorrect_code":"if primary_down:\n    route_writes_to(replica)\n    return","corrected_code":"candidate = choose_failover_candidate()\nverify_replica_state(candidate)\nfence_old_authority_if_required()\npromote(candidate)\nverify_authority(candidate)\nroute_writes_to(candidate)","failure_mode":"Old and new primaries both accept writes.","expected_error":"FailoverVerificationError.","cause":"Authority transition is not coordinated.","root_cause":"Failover was modeled as network routing only.","solution":"Separate detection, promotion, fencing, routing, and verification.","edge_case":"Old primary becomes reachable after promotion.","security_implication":"Authority credentials and promotion controls require strict authorization.","performance_implication":"Failover may temporarily increase latency or reduce capacity while roles transition.","test_case":"Simulate primary loss and verify the designated candidate becomes authoritative only after readiness checks.","test_input":{"primary":"p1","candidate":"r1","candidate_lag":0},"expected_test_result":"Writes route to r1 only after the failover contract is satisfied.","testing_guidance":"Test stale candidate, split-brain simulation, promotion failure, old-primary return, and rollback.","verification_method":"Failover state-machine integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify authority ownership explicitly rather than inferring it from network reachability.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B081-R003","D35-B089-R005"],"depends_on":["D35-B089-R001","D35-B089-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["replication_failover"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Well-Architected Framework — reliability and failover principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B089-R005","dataset_id":"D35-B089-R005","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Replication Boundary","subtopic":"Replication verification","concept":"Replication verification should distinguish transport success, replica freshness, data correctness, and authority correctness.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"How should replication be verified?","answer":"Verify that required records reach replicas, freshness meets policy, reads route correctly, failover preserves authority rules, and recovered nodes rejoin without becoming an unauthorized writer.","explanation":"A replication link being connected does not prove that application-level consistency or authority semantics are correct.","principle":"Verify replicated outcomes and authority state separately.","objective":"Detect silent replication and failover correctness defects.","requirements":["Replication health checks","Freshness verification","Authority checks","Recovery tests"],"constraints":["Do not equate transport connectivity with data correctness","Do not claim zero lag without measurement"],"preconditions":["Replication topology is testable"],"input":"Synthetic write/read/failover sequence","output":"Replication verification report","expected_behavior":"All declared replication invariants are satisfied.","incorrect_code":"assert replica.connection.is_open()","corrected_code":"write_record(record)\nwait_until_policy_allows_read(replica, record.version)\nassert replica.read(record.id).version >= record.version","failure_mode":"Tests pass while replica remains stale.","expected_error":"AssertionError or explicit freshness verification failure.","cause":"Verification checks transport rather than state.","root_cause":"No outcome-level replication contract.","solution":"Verify state arrival, freshness, routing, and authority separately.","edge_case":"Replication is eventually consistent and arrival is delayed.","security_implication":"Recovered replicas must not serve unauthorized stale data or become an unintended writer.","performance_implication":"Replication verification can add reads and monitoring load.","test_case":"Write a record, observe its replica version, then perform a consistency-sensitive read.","test_input":{"record_version":15,"replica_initial_version":14},"expected_test_result":"Strict read waits for or routes away from the stale replica according to policy.","testing_guidance":"Test normal replication, lag, lost update, failover, rejoin, and split-brain scenarios.","verification_method":"Integration and failure-injection testing.","verification_guidance":"Record actual lag measurements only from executed tests.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B089-R001","D35-B089-R002","D35-B089-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B089-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B089-R001","D35-B089-R002","D35-B089-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B089-R001","D35-B089-R002","D35-B089-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["AWS Well-Architected Framework — reliability, replication, and failover principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B090-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Failure Detection & Recovery","subtopic":"Failure detector abstraction","concept":"Failure detection should expose a bounded observation contract rather than pretending that an unavailable component can always be classified as definitively failed.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"How should a distributed failure detector be abstracted?","answer":"Define observations such as reachable, unhealthy, suspected, or confirmed according to explicit evidence and timing rules. Keep detector implementation separate from recovery action.","explanation":"In distributed systems, temporary unreachability can be indistinguishable from failure without additional evidence. A detector should therefore expose uncertainty explicitly.","principle":"Detect and classify failure evidence before triggering recovery.","objective":"Prevent false-positive failure actions.","requirements":["Observation model","Timeouts","Evidence thresholds","Explicit uncertainty"],"constraints":["Do not equate timeout with proof of permanent failure","Recovery actions belong outside the detector"],"preconditions":["A dependency can become unreachable or unhealthy"],"input":"Health observations","output":"Failure state/observation","expected_behavior":"Detector reports the state supported by its evidence and policy.","incorrect_code":"if timeout:\n    mark_dead(node)","corrected_code":"observation = detector.observe(node)\nif observation.state == "suspected":\n    record_suspect(node, observation)\nreturn observation","failure_mode":"Transient network delay immediately triggers destructive recovery.","expected_error":"DetectorContractError when observation evidence is invalid.","cause":"Failure and suspicion are treated as identical.","root_cause":"No explicit failure-state model.","solution":"Represent suspicion and confirmation separately.","edge_case":"Network partition makes a healthy node unreachable from one observer.","security_implication":"Failure signals should not alone grant authority to destructive actions.","performance_implication":"Frequent health probes consume network and compute resources.","test_case":"Simulate a single probe timeout and verify the detector returns the configured suspicion state rather than unconditional failure.","test_input":{"probe":"timeout","previous_state":"healthy"},"expected_test_result":"State follows the detector policy and does not exceed its evidence semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout, successful recovery, repeated failure, probe errors, and partition scenarios.","verification_method":"Failure-detector state-machine tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify detector state transitions independently from recovery execution.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B089-R004","D35-B090-R002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["failure_detector_boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Designing Data-Intensive Applications — failure detection uncertainty; distributed systems failure detector literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B090-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Failure Detection & Recovery","subtopic":"Heartbeats and leases","concept":"Heartbeats provide liveness evidence while leases provide time-bounded ownership or validity semantics; they should not be conflated.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What is the implementation difference between a heartbeat and a lease?","answer":"A heartbeat is an observation message indicating that a component is responsive. A lease grants validity or ownership until an expiration condition, usually requiring renewal. A heartbeat alone does not grant ownership.","explanation":"Conflating the two can cause a node that is merely responsive to be treated as authoritative or a missed heartbeat to revoke authority without a valid lease contract.","principle":"Separate liveness evidence from authority validity.","objective":"Prevent incorrect ownership or recovery decisions.","requirements":["Heartbeat observation","Lease state where ownership is required","Expiration semantics"],"constraints":["Lease expiration requires reliable time assumptions and an explicit safety model","Heartbeats must not be treated as authorization"],"preconditions":["The system requires liveness or time-bounded ownership"],"input":"Heartbeat/lease messages","output":"Observation and/or lease state","expected_behavior":"Heartbeat updates liveness evidence; lease state changes only according to lease rules.","incorrect_code":"if heartbeat_received:\n    current_owner = sender","corrected_code":"if heartbeat_received:\n    detector.record_liveness(sender)\nif lease_valid(sender, now):\n    allow_owner_operation(sender)","failure_mode":"Any responsive node can claim ownership.","expected_error":"LeaseOwnershipError when an operation is attempted without a valid lease.","cause":"Liveness and authority are represented by one flag.","root_cause":"Failure detection and coordination semantics are conflated.","solution":"Separate heartbeat observation from lease ownership validation.","edge_case":"A delayed heartbeat arrives after lease expiration.","security_implication":"Lease tokens must be protected from forgery and replay.","performance_implication":"Heartbeat frequency and lease duration affect detection speed and system overhead.","test_case":"Receive a heartbeat after a lease has expired and verify it does not restore ownership automatically.","test_input":{"lease_expired":true,"heartbeat_received":true},"expected_test_result":"Liveness may be recorded, but ownership remains invalid until a valid lease is established.","testing_guidance":"Test renewal before expiration, after expiration, duplicate heartbeat, delayed messages, and clock-boundary cases.","verification_method":"Lease state-machine tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify heartbeat processing cannot bypass lease validation.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.22+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Go toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B067-R001","D35-B090-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B090-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["heartbeat_lease_separation"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Distributed coordination and lease design principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B090-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Failure Detection & Recovery","subtopic":"Suspect versus confirmed failure","concept":"Recovery policy should distinguish suspicion from confirmed failure and define the evidence required for escalation.","knowledge_type":"architecture_implementation","question":"Why should suspected and confirmed failure be separate states?","answer":"A suspected failure may result from transient latency, partition, or probe error, while confirmed failure requires stronger evidence according to the system's policy. Separate states prevent premature recovery actions.","explanation":"Distributed observations are incomplete. Explicit uncertainty is safer than binary health classification when recovery actions have side effects.","principle":"Escalate recovery authority with evidence.","objective":"Reduce false-positive recovery actions.","requirements":["State model","Evidence threshold","Escalation policy","Recovery action mapping"],"constraints":["Do not invent universal failure thresholds","Thresholds depend on workload and failure model"],"preconditions":["Failure has operational consequences"],"input":"Sequence of health observations","output":"Failure classification","expected_behavior":"Classification advances only when configured evidence conditions are met.","incorrect_code":"if missed_heartbeat:\n    recover_node()","corrected_code":"state = detector.update(observation)\nif state == CONFIRMED_FAILED:\n    recovery.plan(node)","failure_mode":"One transient missed signal triggers recovery.","expected_error":"UnexpectedRecoveryAction in a test harness.","cause":"Recovery is coupled directly to one observation.","root_cause":"No explicit uncertainty state.","solution":"Introduce suspicion and confirmation states with policy-driven transitions.","edge_case":"Observations oscillate between healthy and suspected.","security_implication":"High-impact recovery must require stronger evidence and authorization where appropriate.","performance_implication":"Longer confirmation windows can delay recovery while reducing false positives.","test_case":"Feed one timeout followed by a successful heartbeat.","test_input":["timeout","success"],"expected_test_result":"Node does not enter confirmed failure solely from the first timeout.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated failures, recovery signals, flapping, partition-like behavior, and threshold boundaries.","verification_method":"State-machine testing.","verification_guidance":"Verify each transition has an explicit evidence rule.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 5.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"TypeScript runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B090-R001","D35-B090-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B090-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["failure_classification_state_machine"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Designing Data-Intensive Applications — distributed failure detection uncertainty"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B090-R004","dataset_id":"D35-B090-R004","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Failure Detection & Recovery","subtopic":"Recovery and rejoin","concept":"Recovery must include safe rejoin semantics so a recovered component does not immediately reintroduce stale or conflicting state.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should a recovered distributed component rejoin safely?","answer":"A rejoining component should first establish its current role, synchronize required state, pass health/readiness checks, and only then receive normal traffic or authority.","explanation":"A process becoming reachable again does not prove that its state is current or that it is safe to serve requests.","principle":"Reachability precedes synchronization; synchronization precedes readiness; readiness precedes traffic.","objective":"Prevent stale recovered components from corrupting normal operation.","requirements":["Rejoin state","Synchronization","Readiness verification","Traffic admission policy"],"constraints":["Do not route normal traffic immediately on process startup","Old authority must not regain write access accidentally"],"preconditions":["A component can leave and later rejoin the cluster"],"input":"Recovered component state","output":"Admitted or rejected rejoin status","expected_behavior":"Traffic admission occurs only after required synchronization and readiness conditions are satisfied.","incorrect_code":"on_process_restart:\n    add_to_load_balancer(node)","corrected_code":"state = REJOINING\nsynchronize_state(node)\nverify_readiness(node)\nstate = READY\nadmit_traffic(node)","failure_mode":"Stale recovered node serves outdated state.","expected_error":"RejoinVerificationError.","cause":"Process availability is treated as readiness.","root_cause":"Recovery lifecycle lacks synchronization and admission states.","solution":"Use explicit rejoin, synchronize, verify, and admit phases.","edge_case":"Synchronization cannot complete because the source is unavailable.","security_implication":"Rejoining identity and credentials must be revalidated; stale authorization state must not be trusted.","performance_implication":"State synchronization consumes resources and can temporarily reduce cluster capacity.","test_case":"Restart a node with stale state and verify it cannot serve normal traffic until synchronization completes.","test_input":{"node_state":"stale","sync_complete":false},"expected_test_result":"Node remains excluded from normal traffic.","testing_guidance":"Test clean restart, stale restart, failed synchronization, readiness failure, and repeated rejoin.","verification_method":"Recovery lifecycle integration test.","verification_guidance":"Verify traffic admission is gated by explicit readiness state.","language":"Java","language_version":"21+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 21+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"JDK 21+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B039-R001","D35-B089-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B090-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["safe_rejoin"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Kubernetes Documentation — readiness and lifecycle concepts; distributed systems recovery principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B090-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Failure Detection & Recovery","subtopic":"Failure and recovery verification","concept":"Failure-recovery verification must test detection, classification, action gating, recovery, rejoin, and final system invariants as separate stages.","knowledge_type":"testing_verification","question":"How should distributed failure recovery be verified?","answer":"Inject controlled failures and verify detector state, recovery authorization, resulting system state, recovered-node synchronization, and traffic admission. Distinguish detection from successful recovery.","explanation":"A detector can correctly identify failure while recovery still fails; conversely, recovery may occur after an incorrectly classified failure. These outcomes require separate verification.","principle":"Verify each lifecycle transition and the final invariant.","objective":"Prevent false confidence from partial recovery success.","requirements":["Failure injection","State transition assertions","Recovery verification","Rejoin verification"],"constraints":["Do not equate process restart with recovery","Do not claim resilience without executing failure scenarios"],"preconditions":["Recovery lifecycle is explicitly modeled"],"input":"Controlled failure scenario","output":"Lifecycle verification report","expected_behavior":"Failure detection, recovery, and rejoin satisfy their respective contracts.","incorrect_code":"stop(node)\nstart(node)\nassert node.is_running()","corrected_code":"inject_failure(node)\nassert detector.state(node) == CONFIRMED_FAILED\nexecute_recovery(node)\nverify_authority()\nverify_state_sync(node)\nassert admission_state(node) == READY","failure_mode":"A restarted process is considered recovered despite stale state or incorrect authority.","expected_error":"AssertionError or explicit recovery verification failure.","cause":"Verification checks process availability only.","root_cause":"No lifecycle-level recovery contract.","solution":"Verify detection, authority, state synchronization, readiness, and admission separately.","edge_case":"Failure occurs during recovery itself.","security_implication":"High-impact recovery paths should require authorization and produce audit evidence.","performance_implication":"Failure-injection tests can be resource-intensive; scope them to representative failure modes.","test_case":"Simulate component failure, recovery, stale state, synchronization, and final admission.","test_input":{"failure":"node_unreachable","recovery":"restart","initial_rejoin_state":"stale"},"expected_test_result":"Node is not admitted until the recovery contract is fully satisfied.","testing_guidance":"Cover timeout, partition, repeated failure, recovery failure, stale rejoin, authority conflict, and successful convergence.","verification_method":"Failure-injection integration test.","verification_guidance":"Record each lifecycle transition and distinguish NOT_EXECUTED, EXECUTED_FAILED, and EXECUTED_PASSED outcomes from design-time expectations.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B090-R001","D35-B090-R003","D35-B090-R004"],"depends_on":["D35-B090-R001","D35-B090-R003","D35-B090-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D35-B090-R001","D35-B090-R003","D35-B090-R004"],"verifies":["D35-B090-R001","D35-B090-R003","D35-B090-R004"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering — failure testing and recovery principles; Kubernetes lifecycle/readiness documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D35-B091-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Health & Dependency State Implementation","subtopic":"Health-state abstraction","concept":"A distributed component health boundary represents observed dependency state separately from business state and exposes normalized health information to callers.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should distributed dependency health be represented in implementation?","answer":"Define a typed health model with explicit states, dependency identity, observation time, and diagnostic details; keep health observation separate from business logic.","explanation":"A health boundary prevents callers from depending directly on provider-specific health APIs and allows dependency state to be evaluated consistently.","principle":"Health observation is an infrastructure concern and must not mutate business state implicitly.","objective":"Provide a stable implementation boundary for dependency health.","requirements":["Typed health state","Dependency identity","Observation metadata","Provider-independent contract"],"constraints":["Health observations may become stale","Health checks must not create uncontrolled load"],"preconditions":["A dependency identity exists","A health observation mechanism is available"],"input":"Dependency identifier and health observation","output":"Normalized dependency health state","expected_behavior":"The implementation returns an explicit health state and preserves observation metadata.","incorrect_code":"def healthy(client):\n    return client.ping() == 'ok'","corrected_code":"class HealthState:\n    def init(self, status, dependency, observed_at):\n        self.status = status\n        self.dependency = dependency\n        self.observed_at = observed_at","failure_mode":"Provider-specific health logic leaks into application code.","expected_error":"Health state cannot be normalized consistently.","cause":"No health abstraction exists.","root_cause":"Infrastructure dependency state is coupled directly to provider APIs.","solution":"Introduce a normalized health-state boundary.","edge_case":"A dependency returns an unknown or malformed health state.","security_implication":"Health details should not expose secrets or sensitive infrastructure information.","performance_implication":"Health polling should be bounded and preferably reused where freshness requirements permit.","test_case":"Normalize healthy, unhealthy, degraded, and unknown observations.","test_input":"Four valid and invalid health observations.","expected_test_result":"Each observation maps deterministically to the defined health state.","testing_guidance":"Test provider mapping and malformed responses independently.","verification_method":"Static inspection plus unit tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify state mapping, metadata preservation, and absence of business-state mutation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D35-B039-R001","D35-B090-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Health provider adapter"],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B039-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Distributed dependency health boundary"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Kubernetes Health Check documentation; OpenTelemetry semantic conventions","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B091-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Health & Dependency State Implementation","subtopic":"Dependency aggregation","concept":"A component may depend on multiple services whose individual states must be aggregated without hiding the underlying evidence.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should multiple dependency health states be aggregated?","answer":"Represent each dependency independently and derive an aggregate state using explicit deterministic rules rather than replacing individual evidence with one boolean.","explanation":"A single healthy/unhealthy flag loses diagnostic information and can incorrectly hide partial degradation.","principle":"Aggregate state must remain derived from independently observable dependency states.","objective":"Implement deterministic multi-dependency health aggregation.","requirements":["Independent dependency records","Explicit aggregation policy","Deterministic result"],"constraints":["Dependencies can fail independently","Unknown state must not automatically equal healthy"],"preconditions":["Dependency health records are available"],"input":"Collection of dependency health states","output":"Per-dependency state plus aggregate state","expected_behavior":"The aggregate state reflects the configured policy while preserving each dependency's state.","incorrect_code":"return all(dep.healthy for dep in dependencies)","corrected_code":"def aggregate(states):\n    if any(s == 'unhealthy' for s in states):\n        return 'unhealthy'\n    if any(s == 'degraded' for s in states):\n        return 'degraded'\n    if any(s == 'unknown' for s in states):\n        return 'unknown'\n    return 'healthy'","failure_mode":"Partial failure is incorrectly reported as complete health.","expected_error":"Aggregate health contradicts dependency evidence.","cause":"Boolean aggregation discards state distinctions.","root_cause":"Health policy is not explicitly