{"record_id":"D35-B091-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Health & Dependency State Implementation","subtopic":"Staleness handling","concept":"Health observations require freshness metadata and explicit stale-state behavior.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should stale health observations be handled?","answer":"Attach timestamps or freshness intervals to observations and classify expired observations as stale or unknown rather than treating them as current.","explanation":"Distributed health information becomes inaccurate when observation age exceeds its acceptable freshness window.","principle":"Observed health is valid only within its defined freshness policy.","objective":"Prevent stale dependency observations from being mistaken for current health.","requirements":["Observation timestamp","Freshness policy","Stale-state handling"],"constraints":["Clock differences may exist","Freshness thresholds are domain-specific"],"preconditions":["Health observation contains observation time"],"input":"Health observation and current time","output":"Freshness classification","expected_behavior":"Expired observations are explicitly identified as stale.","incorrect_code":"return cached_health","corrected_code":"if now - cached.observed_at > max_age:\n    return 'stale'","failure_mode":"Old healthy state masks current failure.","expected_error":"Stale observation accepted as current.","cause":"No freshness validation.","root_cause":"Health state lacks temporal semantics.","solution":"Add freshness metadata and explicit stale handling.","edge_case":"Clock skew makes an observation appear to originate in the future.","security_implication":"Untrusted timestamps should not bypass health freshness controls.","performance_implication":"Freshness evaluation is inexpensive and should occur before expensive active checks.","test_case":"Evaluate fresh, expired, and future-dated observations.","test_input":"Observations at multiple ages.","expected_test_result":"Freshness classification follows configured policy.","testing_guidance":"Inject a deterministic clock.","verification_method":"Unit tests with controlled timestamps.","verification_guidance":"Verify stale observations cannot silently become healthy.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Python 3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D35-B033-R001","D35-B086-R003"],"depends_on":["D35-B091-R001"],"requires":["Clock boundary","Freshness policy"],"extends":[],"refines":["D35-B033-R001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Health freshness policy"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Distributed-systems failure-detection practice; time-bound cache validity principles","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B091-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Health & Dependency State Implementation","subtopic":"Health state transitions","concept":"Health state changes should be explicit and observable rather than silently overwritten.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should health transitions be implemented?","answer":"Represent health as a state transition model and record meaningful transitions with timestamps and causes.","explanation":"Explicit transitions prevent flapping and make operational behavior auditable.","principle":"A health state is a temporal state machine, not merely a boolean.","objective":"Make health transitions deterministic and diagnosable.","requirements":["Defined states","Transition policy","Transition metadata"],"constraints":["Noisy dependencies can flap","Transition thresholds may be asymmetric"],"preconditions":["Current health state exists"],"input":"Current state and new observation","output":"Accepted new state and transition metadata","expected_behavior":"Only valid transitions are accepted and significant transitions can be inspected.","incorrect_code":"state = observation.status","corrected_code":"if transition_allowed(state, observation.status):\n    state = observation.status","failure_mode":"Transient failures cause uncontrolled state flapping.","expected_error":"Invalid or unstable state transition accepted.","cause":"No transition policy.","root_cause":"Health state treated as raw observation.","solution":"Introduce explicit transition rules and optional hysteresis.","edge_case":"Rapid alternating healthy/unhealthy observations.","security_implication":"Health-transition records should be protected from unauthorized modification.","performance_implication":"Transition evaluation should remain lightweight.","test_case":"Feed alternating health observations.","test_input":"healthy → unhealthy → healthy repeated rapidly.","expected_test_result":"Confi