{"record_id":"D35-B092-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Leader Election & Leadership Lease Implementation","subtopic":"Fencing","concept":"Leadership loss may require fencing so an old leader cannot continue applying effects after another participant acquires leadership.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"Why is fencing important in leader-election implementations?","answer":"Use an authority token, generation, or equivalent fencing mechanism that downstream operations can validate before accepting leader-owned effects.","explanation":"Stopping a local process is not always immediate; fencing protects against stale leaders that still execute after lease loss.","principle":"Leadership ownership must be enforceable at the effect boundary when stale execution is possible.","objective":"Prevent stale leaders from producing conflicting effects.","requirements":["Monotonic authority identifier","Effect-side validation where needed","Leadership loss handling"],"constraints":["Not every system supports fencing","External systems may require an adapter"],"preconditions":["Leader-only side effects exist"],"input":"Leadership token and requested effect","output":"Accepted or rejected effect","expected_behavior":"Effects carrying stale authority are rejected.","incorrect_code":"if leader:\n    database.write(value)","corrected_code":"database.write(value, fencing_token=lease.generation)","failure_mode":"Old leader writes after replacement leader starts.","expected_error":"Stale leader effect accepted.","cause":"No downstream authority validation.","root_cause":"Leadership is trusted only locally.","solution":"Introduce fencing at the critical effect boundary.","edge_case":"Old leader resumes after prolonged partition.","security_implication":"Fencing tokens should be unforgeable or integrity-protected.","performance_implication":"Fencing adds validation overhead to protected operations.","test_case":"Use an old and new