{"record_id":"D35-B095-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Remote Procedure Call (RPC) Contract Implementation","subtopic":"RPC interface","concept":"An RPC boundary defines method identity, request schema, response schema, error semantics, timeout behavior, and compatibility expectations.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What belongs in an RPC implementation contract?","answer":"Define method identity, typed request and response contracts, errors, deadlines, authentication context, and version compatibility.","explanation":"A network call is not equivalent to a local function call because transport failure and partial completion are possible.","principle":"RPC contracts must model distributed failure explicitly.","objective":"Implement an explicit RPC service boundary.","requirements":["Request schema","Response schema","Error model","Deadline semantics","Compatibility policy"],"constraints":["Network calls can fail after server-side execution","Clients and servers may differ in version"],"preconditions":["RPC transport exists"],"input":"RPC request","output":"Response or typed failure","expected_behavior":"Calls produce documented responses or explicit transport/application errors.","incorrect_code":"result = remote.do(data)","corrected_code":"result = remote.call('Do', request, deadline=deadline)","failure_mode":"Transport and application errors become indistinguishable.","expected_error":"Ambiguous RPC failure semantics.","cause":"RPC modeled as local function invocation.","root_cause":"Distributed boundary semantics omitted.","solution":"Define explicit request, response, error, and deadline contracts.","edge_case":"Server completes operation but response is lost.","security_implication":"RPC endpoints require authentication and authorization appropriate to the operation.","performance_implication":"Serialization, network latency, and connection reuse affect RPC cost.","test_case":"Call with valid reque