{"record_id":"D35-B095-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Remote Procedure Call (RPC) Contract Implementation","subtopic":"RPC idempotency","concept":"RPC methods whose requests may be retried require explicit idempotency semantics to avoid duplicate effects.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should RPC idempotency be represented?","answer":"Declare whether an RPC is safe to retry and, for retryable commands, use an idempotency key or equivalent operation identity where required.","explanation":"A lost response does not prove the server did not execute the operation.","principle":"Retry safety must be a contract property, not a client assumption.","objective":"Prevent duplicate effects across RPC retries.","requirements":["Method retry classification","Operation identity where needed","Server-side deduplication for retry-sensitive commands"],"constraints":["Not all operations can be made idempotent"],"preconditions":["RPC method semantics are known"],"input":"RPC command and operation identity","output":"Single logical effect or explicit rejection","expected_behavior":"Retrying a retry-safe command does not create unintended duplicate effects.","incorrect_code":"retry(lambda: remote.create_order(order))","corrected_code":"retry(lambda: remote.create_order(order, idempotency_key=key))","failure_mode":"A completed command is executed again after response loss.","expected_error":"Duplicate side effect.","cause":"Client retries without operation identity.","root_cause":"Idempotency semantics absent from RPC contract.","solution":"Declare retry safety and use operation identity for commands requiring deduplication.","edge_case":"First request succeeds but acknowledgement is lost.","security_implication":"Idempotency keys must be scoped and protected from unauthorized reuse.","performance_implication":"Server-side deduplication requires storage and lookup overhead.","test_case":"Drop first response and retry t