{"record_id":"D35-B097-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Checkpoint / Resume State Implementation","subtopic":"Atomic checkpoint persistence","concept":"Checkpoint persistence must avoid publishing incomplete checkpoint state.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should checkpoint writes be made durable and atomic?","answer":"Write checkpoints using an atomic persistence mechanism so readers observe either the previous complete checkpoint or the new complete checkpoint.","explanation":"A partial checkpoint can make recovery impossible or cause incorrect replay.","principle":"Recovery metadata must itself be crash-safe.","objective":"Prevent corrupted checkpoint state.","requirements":["Atomic write","Durability policy","Integrity validation"],"constraints":["Durability guarantees depend on storage technology"],"preconditions":["Checkpoint store supports atomic persistence or transactional semantics"],"input":"Checkpoint object","output":"Durable checkpoint version","expected_behavior":"A crash during checkpoint persistence cannot expose partial logical state.","incorrect_code":"file.write(json.dumps(cp))","corrected_code":"write_temp(cp)\nfsync_temp()\nos.replace(temp, target)","failure_mode":"Crash leaves malformed checkpoint.","expected_error":"Checkpoint parse or integrity failure.","cause":"Direct overwrite.","root_cause":"Persistence boundary lacks atomicity.","solution":"Use atomic replace or transactional persistence.","edge_case":"Process crashes after rename but before higher-level acknowledgement.","security_implication":"Checkpoint permissions must prevent unauthorized modification.","performance_implication":"Durability operations such as fsync can be expensive and should be policy-driven.","test_case":"Inject crash points during persistence.","test_input":"Checkpoint write at multiple failure points.","expected_test_result":"Recovery finds a complete valid checkpoint or prior valid checkpoint.