{"record_id":"D35-B098-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Runtime Topology & Dependency Graph Implementation","subtopic":"Dependency impact traversal","concept":"A runtime dependency graph can support bounded impact analysis when traversals distinguish direct from transitive dependencies.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How can runtime topology support dependency impact analysis?","answer":"Traverse typed dependency edges from an affected component while recording path and relationship type, with explicit bounds and cycle handling.","explanation":"Unbounded graph traversal can overstate impact or loop through cyclic dependencies.","principle":"Impact analysis must be graph-aware, bounded, and relationship-sensitive.","objective":"Implement safe runtime dependency traversal.","requirements":["Typed edges","Visited tracking","Traversal bounds","Path evidence"],"constraints":["Graphs may contain cycles","Indirect dependencies may not imply equal impact"],"preconditions":["Topology graph is available"],"input":"Affected component","output":"Bounded impact set with paths","expected_behavior":"Traversal returns reachable dependencies within configured rules without infinite loops.","incorrect_code":"def walk(node):\n    for child in graph[node]:\n        walk(child)","corrected_code":"def walk(node, seen=None):\n    seen = seen or set()\n    if node in seen:\n        return\n    seen.add(node)\n    for child in graph[node]:\n        walk(child, seen)","failure_mode":"Cycle causes infinite traversal.","expected_error":"Non-terminating impact analysis.","cause":"No visited set.","root_cause":"Graph cycles ignored.","solution":"Track visited nodes and define traversal semantics.","edge_case":"A dependency path returns to an ancestor.","security_implication":"Impact data may reveal sensitive architecture.","performance_implication":"Use bounded traversal and avoid repeated path expansion.","test_case":"Traverse a cy