{"record_id":"D35-B099-R001","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Operational Control / Administrative Command Boundary","subtopic":"Administrative command model","concept":"Operational control commands require an explicit command boundary that distinguishes administrative intent from ordinary application requests.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should administrative commands be modeled?","answer":"Define typed administrative commands with actor identity, target scope, command intent, authorization requirements, and execution policy.","explanation":"Administrative operations often have broader effects and require stronger governance than normal business operations.","principle":"Administrative authority must be explicit, scoped, and auditable.","objective":"Create a safe operational-control boundary.","requirements":["Typed command","Actor context","Target scope","Authorization policy","Audit metadata"],"constraints":["Administrative commands can have high impact"],"preconditions":["Administrative identity and authorization system exist"],"input":"Administrative command","output":"Authorized command request","expected_behavior":"Command intent and scope are explicit before execution.","incorrect_code":"admin.run(raw_command)","corrected_code":"admin.execute(AdminCommand(actor, action, target, scope))","failure_mode":"Unstructured administrative input reaches execution.","expected_error":"Ambiguous or unauthorized administrative command.","cause":"No typed control boundary.","root_cause":"Operational control treated as generic input.","solution":"Define explicit command contract and governance.","edge_case":"Command targets multiple tenants or environments.","security_implication":"Administrative commands require strong authentication, authorization, audit, and least privilege.","performance_implication":"Authorization and audit overhead should be measured but not bypassed for speed.","test_case":"