{"record_id":"D35-B099-R002","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Operational Control / Administrative Command Boundary","subtopic":"Scope enforcement","concept":"Operational commands must be constrained to an explicit target scope and must not infer broader scope from caller intent.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should administrative command scope be enforced?","answer":"Validate the requested scope against the actor's authorized scope and require explicit target selection for every affected resource boundary.","explanation":"Implicit scope expansion is a common source of administrative mistakes and security failures.","principle":"Authorization must cover the exact resource scope of the command.","objective":"Prevent accidental or unauthorized scope expansion.","requirements":["Explicit target scope","Scope authorization check","Tenant/environment boundary"],"constraints":["Hierarchical scopes require explicit inheritance rules"],"preconditions":["Actor has an authorization context"],"input":"Command scope and actor scope","output":"Allowed or rejected command","expected_behavior":"Commands outside authorized scope are rejected before execution.","incorrect_code":"if user.is_admin:\n    execute_all(command)","corrected_code":"authorize(actor, command.action, command.target_scope)\nexecute(command)","failure_mode":"Broad administrator role bypasses target-level authorization.","expected_error":"Unauthorized scope access.","cause":"Role-only authorization.","root_cause":"Resource scope omitted from authorization decision.","solution":"Authorize action plus target scope.","edge_case":"Command spans resources in multiple tenants.","security_implication":"Cross-tenant scope must be explicitly denied unless a documented system-level authority exists.","performance_implication":"Scope checks should use indexed authorization data.","test_case":"Attempt commands across authorized and unauthorized scopes