{"record_id":"D35-B099-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Operational Control / Administrative Command Boundary","subtopic":"Approval and execution separation","concept":"High-impact administrative commands should separate authorization/approval from execution so the decision is explicit before an effect occurs.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"How should high-impact administrative commands separate approval from execution?","answer":"Represent command authorization or approval as a distinct state and require the execution boundary to verify current approval before performing the effect.","explanation":"Combining approval and execution in one opaque function makes governance difficult to inspect and test.","principle":"Permission to request an action and permission to execute its effect are distinct states where governance requires approval.","objective":"Implement governed administrative execution.","requirements":["Approval state","Actor identity","Command identity","Execution-time authorization check"],"constraints":["Approval may expire","Command content must remain bound to the approval"],"preconditions":["Approval policy exists"],"input":"Administrative command and approval","output":"Authorized execution request","expected_behavior":"Only the exact approved command and scope can execute.","incorrect_code":"if approved:\n    execute(command)","corrected_code":"approval = approval_store.get(command.id)\nverify_approval_binding(approval, command)\nexecute(command)","failure_mode":"Approval for one command is reused for another.","expected_error":"Approval binding mismatch.","cause":"Approval checked only as boolean.","root_cause":"Approval lacks command identity and scope binding.","solution":"Bind approval to immutable command identity, scope, and policy context.","edge_case":"Command changes after approval.","security_implication":"Approval records require integrity protection and auditability.","performance_implication":"Approval lookup should be efficient without weakening verification.","test_case":"Modify a command after approval.","test_input":"Approved command followed by changed target.","expected_test_result":"Execution is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Test approval expiry and replay.","verification_method":"Governance integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify approval-to-command binding.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"None","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":"Approval and audit stores","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_GOVERNANCE_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D35-B027-R001","D35-B043-R001","D35-B099-R001"],"depends_on":["D35-B099-R001"],"requires":["Approval policy"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":["Governed administrative execution"],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Separation-of-duties and approval-control principles","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D35-B099-R004","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Operational Control / Administrative Command Boundary","subtopic":"Command auditability","concept":"Administrative command lifecycle should produce immutable or tamper-evident audit records containing actor, target, decision, and outcome.","knowledge_type":"implementation","question":"What must be recorded for an administrative command?","answer":"Record command identity, actor, scope, requested action, authorization or approval decision, execution result, timestamps, and relevant evidence references.","explanation":"Administrative controls require traceability from request through decision and outcome.","principle":"Operational control must be reconstructable from audit evidence.","objective":"Implement complete administrative command auditability.","requirements":["Command identity","Actor","Scope","Decision","Outcome","Evidence references"],"constraints":["Audit records must not contain unnecessary secrets"],"preconditions":["Audit infrastructure exists"],"input":"Administrative command lifecycle","output":"Audit trail","expected_behavior":"Each significant lifecycle transition produces an attributable record.","incorrect_code":"logger.info('admin action')","corrected_code":"audit.record(command_id=cmd.id, actor=actor.id, scope=cmd.scope, outcome=outcome)","failure_mode":"Audit trail cannot identify what was actually authorized.","expected_error":"Incomplete administrative evidence.","cause":"Generic logging used instead of structured audit.","root_cause":"Administrative traceability not modeled.","solution":"Use dedicated structured audit records.","edge_case":"Execution fails after authorization.","security_implication":"Audit records require integrity and restricted access.","performance_implication":"Audit persistence should be designed so failures do not silently erase accountability.","test_case":"Execute successful, rejected, and failed comma