{"record_id":"D35-B099-R005","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Distributed Operational Control / Administrative Command Boundary","subtopic":"Administrative control verification","concept":"Administrative control verification must prove authorization, scope, approval binding, auditability, and failure behavior.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should administrative command boundaries be verified?","answer":"Test authorized and unauthorized commands, scope violations, approval tampering, replay, execution failure, and audit reconstruction.","explanation":"Administrative safety depends on the complete lifecycle, not merely the authorization function.","principle":"Governance controls must be verified at the execution boundary.","objective":"Verify safe administrative control behavior.","requirements":["Authorization tests","Scope tests","Approval tests","Audit tests","Failure tests"],"constraints":["Use non-production test targets"],"preconditions":["Administrative boundary exists"],"input":"Controlled administrative command scenarios","output":"Verification evidence","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized or altered commands never reach protected execution.","incorrect_code":"assert authorize(admin, command)","corrected_code":"assert execution_result(command) == 'REJECTED' for unauthorized_case","failure_mode":"Authorization is tested but execution bypass is not.","expected_error":"Control bypass remains undetected.","cause":"Verification stops at policy evaluation.","root_cause":"Execution boundary not tested.","solution":"Test from request through execution and audit.","edge_case":"Authorized command becomes unauthorized due to scope change.","security_implication":"Tests must explicitly cover privilege escalation and cross-tenant access.","performance_implication":"Security verification should not be disabled for performance tests.","test_case":"Run administrative control matrix.","test_input":"Valid, invalid, ta