{"record_id":"D35-B100-R003","dataset_id":"D35","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"End-to-End Architecture Implementation Verification","subtopic":"Dependency-direction verification","concept":"Architecture implementation verification should prove that dependency direction remains consistent with the intended architecture.","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How can dependency-direction violations be detected automatically?","answer":"Build a dependency graph from implementation artifacts and assert that dependencies follow the architecture's allowed direction rules.","explanation":"Architecture can degrade gradually when concrete implementations begin depending on higher-level policies or unrelated layers.","principle":"Architectural dependency direction should be machine-checkable where practical.","objective":"Prevent architectural erosion through automated dependency checks.","requirements":["Layer/component rules","Dependency extraction","Violation detection","Regression test"],"constraints":["Dynamic language dependencies may require conservative analysis","Some legitimate exceptions need explicit declarations"],"preconditions":["Allowed dependency rules exist"],"input":"Repository dependency graph","output":"Violation set","expected_behavior":"Forbidden dependency edges are detected and reported with source locations.","incorrect_code":"domain.imports.infrastructure","corrected_code":"domain depends on stable port; infrastructure implements port","failure_mode":"Layer inversion enters code unnoticed.","expected_error":"Architecture dependency violation.","cause":"No automated dependency guard.","root_cause":"Architecture rules remain documentation-only.","solution":"Encode allowed dependency directions as testable rules.","edge_case":"Generated code introduces legitimate dependency edges.","security_implication":"Dependency violations can expose privileged infrastructure to inappropriate layers.","performance_implication":"Static